window.IELTS_DATA.items.push(...[{"id":"writing-task-2-001","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Are advertising methods unethical?","prompt":"Some of the methods used in advertising are unethical and unacceptable in today's society. To what extent do you agree with this view?","answer":"Advertising is indispensable to a market economy because it informs consumers and allows firms to compete. Nevertheless, I largely agree that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a significant proportion of</strong> modern advertising is ethically unacceptable, particularly when it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">conceals commercial intent</strong> or targets people who cannot make fully informed choices.\n\nThe first concern is that digital advertising can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">blur the boundary between persuasion and manipulation</strong>. Online platforms collect extensive behavioural data and use it to predict when an individual is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">most receptive to</strong> a message. Consumers may therefore see personalised claims repeatedly without understanding why they have been selected. This practice can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exploit informational asymmetry</strong>: advertisers know far more about the audience than the audience knows about the campaign. The problem is not merely that people are persuaded to buy unnecessary goods, but that their attention and personal data are used without meaningful consent.\n\nA second ethical problem arises when advertising is directed at children, people in financial difficulty or those with health-related anxieties. Such groups may be less able to assess exaggerated promises or long-term consequences. Promotions for high-interest credit, gambling or unproven health products can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disproportionately affect vulnerable consumers</strong>, even when the wording is technically legal. In these cases, commercial freedom should not outweigh <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the duty to prevent foreseeable harm</strong>.\n\nThis does not mean that all advertising is objectionable. Clear product information, verifiable comparisons and creative brand communication can help consumers make better decisions. However, these practices must be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">subject to meaningful oversight</strong>, including transparent data rules, strict protection for minors and penalties for misleading claims.\n\nIn conclusion, advertising remains legitimate, but methods based on covert tracking, manipulation or exploitation are ethically unacceptable. Strong regulation is therefore necessary to preserve both <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consumer autonomy</strong> and public trust.","phrases":[{"en":"a significant proportion of","context":"Advertising is indispensable to a market economy because it informs consumers and allows firms to compete. Nevertheless, I largely agree that a significant proportion of modern advertising is ethically unacceptable, particularly when it conceals commercial intent or targets people who cannot make fully informed choices."},{"en":"conceals commercial intent","context":"Advertising is indispensable to a market economy because it informs consumers and allows firms to compete. Nevertheless, I largely agree that a significant proportion of modern advertising is ethically unacceptable, particularly when it conceals commercial intent or targets people who cannot make fully informed choices."},{"en":"blur the boundary between persuasion and manipulation","context":"The first concern is that digital advertising can blur the boundary between persuasion and manipulation. Online platforms collect extensive behavioural data and use it to predict when an individual is most receptive to a message. Consumers may therefore see personalised claims repeatedly without understanding why they have been selected. This practice can exploit informational asymmetry: advertisers know far more about the audience than the audience knows about the campaign. The problem is not merely that people are persuaded to buy unnecessary goods, but that their attention and personal data are used without meaningful consent."},{"en":"most receptive to","context":"The first concern is that digital advertising can blur the boundary between persuasion and manipulation. Online platforms collect extensive behavioural data and use it to predict when an individual is most receptive to a message. Consumers may therefore see personalised claims repeatedly without understanding why they have been selected. This practice can exploit informational asymmetry: advertisers know far more about the audience than the audience knows about the campaign. The problem is not merely that people are persuaded to buy unnecessary goods, but that their attention and personal data are used without meaningful consent."},{"en":"exploit informational asymmetry","context":"The first concern is that digital advertising can blur the boundary between persuasion and manipulation. Online platforms collect extensive behavioural data and use it to predict when an individual is most receptive to a message. Consumers may therefore see personalised claims repeatedly without understanding why they have been selected. This practice can exploit informational asymmetry: advertisers know far more about the audience than the audience knows about the campaign. The problem is not merely that people are persuaded to buy unnecessary goods, but that their attention and personal data are used without meaningful consent."},{"en":"disproportionately affect vulnerable consumers","context":"A second ethical problem arises when advertising is directed at children, people in financial difficulty or those with health-related anxieties. Such groups may be less able to assess exaggerated promises or long-term consequences. Promotions for high-interest credit, gambling or unproven health products can disproportionately affect vulnerable consumers, even when the wording is technically legal. In these cases, commercial freedom should not outweigh the duty to prevent foreseeable harm."},{"en":"the duty to prevent foreseeable harm","context":"A second ethical problem arises when advertising is directed at children, people in financial difficulty or those with health-related anxieties. Such groups may be less able to assess exaggerated promises or long-term consequences. Promotions for high-interest credit, gambling or unproven health products can disproportionately affect vulnerable consumers, even when the wording is technically legal. In these cases, commercial freedom should not outweigh the duty to prevent foreseeable harm."},{"en":"subject to meaningful oversight","context":"This does not mean that all advertising is objectionable. Clear product information, verifiable comparisons and creative brand communication can help consumers make better decisions. However, these practices must be subject to meaningful oversight, including transparent data rules, strict protection for minors and penalties for misleading claims."},{"en":"consumer autonomy","context":"In conclusion, advertising remains legitimate, but methods based on covert tracking, manipulation or exploitation are ethically unacceptable. Strong regulation is therefore necessary to preserve both consumer autonomy and public trust."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#001","summary":"Are unethical advertising methods acceptable?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-101","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Does advertising promote quantity over quality?","prompt":"Advertising encourages consumers to buy in quantity rather than in quality. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that advertising persuades people to buy as much as possible rather than to buy well. I largely agree with this view, although the criticism applies to some kinds of advertising far more than to others.\n\nMost advertising is designed to increase the volume of sales, and its methods reflect that aim. Campaigns emphasise price reductions, multi-buy offers and limited-time deals, all of which encourage people to purchase more than they intended. Fashion and technology advertising works differently but towards the same end, by presenting last year&#x27;s perfectly functional clothes or phones as embarrassing, so that consumers replace items that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nowhere near worn out</strong>. The result is visible in the quantity of clothing, electronics and food that households throw away every year.\n\nAdvertising also tends to avoid the information that would allow a genuine judgement of quality. Very few advertisements mention <strong class=\"key-phrase\">durability, repairability or running costs</strong>, because these do not encourage frequent repurchase, and attention is directed instead towards image, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">celebrity endorsement</strong> and lifestyle.\n\nI would accept that this is not universal. Advertising for cars, insurance and household appliances often does compare specifications, safety ratings and reliability, because buyers of expensive items research them carefully. Competition in these markets has arguably raised standards, since a manufacturer with a poor reliability record <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot conceal it</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that most advertising promotes consumption rather than careful choice, and that this contributes to considerable waste. The exception is expensive goods, where consumers demand real information and advertisers are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">obliged to provide it</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"nowhere near worn out","context":"Most advertising is designed to increase the volume of sales, and its methods reflect that aim. Campaigns emphasise price reductions, multi-buy offers and limited-time deals, all of which encourage people to purchase more than they intended. Fashion and technology advertising works differently but towards the same end, by presenting last year's perfectly functional clothes or phones as embarrassing, so that consumers replace items that are nowhere near worn out. The result is visible in the quantity of clothing, electronics and food that households throw away every year."},{"en":"durability, repairability or running costs","context":"Advertising also tends to avoid the information that would allow a genuine judgement of quality. Very few advertisements mention durability, repairability or running costs, because these do not encourage frequent repurchase, and attention is directed instead towards image, celebrity endorsement and lifestyle."},{"en":"celebrity endorsement","context":"Advertising also tends to avoid the information that would allow a genuine judgement of quality. Very few advertisements mention durability, repairability or running costs, because these do not encourage frequent repurchase, and attention is directed instead towards image, celebrity endorsement and lifestyle."},{"en":"cannot conceal it","context":"I would accept that this is not universal. Advertising for cars, insurance and household appliances often does compare specifications, safety ratings and reliability, because buyers of expensive items research them carefully. Competition in these markets has arguably raised standards, since a manufacturer with a poor reliability record cannot conceal it."},{"en":"obliged to provide it","context":"In conclusion, I agree that most advertising promotes consumption rather than careful choice, and that this contributes to considerable waste. The exception is expensive goods, where consumers demand real information and advertisers are obliged to provide it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#002","summary":"Does advertising promote quantity over quality?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Second-hand clothing among the young","prompt":"In recent years, there has been a rise in the popularity of second-hand clothing amongst the younger generation. Why is this happening? Do you think it's a positive or negative development?","answer":"Second-hand clothing has become increasingly popular among young people. The main reasons are lower prices, environmental concern and the desire to find distinctive items, and social media has made it easier than ever for young people to discover, buy and resell them. I see this as a largely positive development.\n\nUsed clothes are often much cheaper than new products, which matters to students and young workers with limited budgets, and online platforms have made buying and selling simple. Second-hand shops also offer unusual designs that are not available in ordinary chain stores, allowing buyers to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build a personal style at a lower cost</strong>, and the trade gives charities and small local businesses a useful source of income.\n\nThe environmental benefit is equally important. Producing new clothes uses water, energy and raw materials, while fast fashion encourages people to discard items quickly. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Reusing garments keeps them in circulation for longer</strong> and reduces waste, and although second-hand shopping cannot solve every problem in the fashion industry on its own, it can meaningfully <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce demand for disposable clothing</strong>.\n\nThere are minor concerns about quality, hygiene and misleading online descriptions. These are manageable rather than serious. Clear photographs and descriptions, honest condition ratings and simple washing routines address most of them, and sellers who build a reliable reputation attract repeat buyers. For most shoppers the drawbacks are a small price to pay for the savings and originality on offer.\n\nIn conclusion, the growth of second-hand fashion is positive because it makes clothing more affordable, supports individual style and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">encourages more responsible consumption</strong>.","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","phrases":[{"en":"build a personal style at a lower cost","context":"Used clothes are often much cheaper than new products, which matters to students and young workers with limited budgets, and online platforms have made buying and selling simple. Second-hand shops also offer unusual designs that are not available in ordinary chain stores, allowing buyers to build a personal style at a lower cost, and the trade gives charities and small local businesses a useful source of income."},{"en":"Reusing garments keeps them in circulation for longer","context":"The environmental benefit is equally important. Producing new clothes uses water, energy and raw materials, while fast fashion encourages people to discard items quickly. Reusing garments keeps them in circulation for longer and reduces waste, and although second-hand shopping cannot solve every problem in the fashion industry on its own, it can meaningfully reduce demand for disposable clothing."},{"en":"reduce demand for disposable clothing","context":"The environmental benefit is equally important. Producing new clothes uses water, energy and raw materials, while fast fashion encourages people to discard items quickly. Reusing garments keeps them in circulation for longer and reduces waste, and although second-hand shopping cannot solve every problem in the fashion industry on its own, it can meaningfully reduce demand for disposable clothing."},{"en":"encourages more responsible consumption","context":"In conclusion, the growth of second-hand fashion is positive because it makes clothing more affordable, supports individual style and encourages more responsible consumption."}],"subtype":"positive","summary":"The rise of second-hand clothing among younger people","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-102","_displayNo":"#003"},{"id":"writing-task-2-104","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Does more advertising do more good than harm?","prompt":"Do the positive effects outweigh negative effects, when advertising gains in popularity?","answer":"Advertising has become almost impossible to avoid, and its growth is more often treated as a problem than as a benefit. In my view, although some of the criticism is deserved, the advantages of widespread advertising <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outweigh its drawbacks</strong>.\n\nThe clearest benefit is information. A consumer who has never heard of a product cannot buy it, and without advertising people would be limited to the few brands sold in their nearest shop. Comparison of prices, features and guarantees is what allows a buyer to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make an informed choice</strong>, and public prices also force sellers to compete rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quietly charge more than their rivals</strong>. The frequent claim that advertising simply manipulates people is hard to sustain when most new products fail despite large campaigns.\n\nAdvertising also pays for a great deal that the public enjoys without noticing. Commercial television, radio, most news websites and nearly all free online services are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">funded by advertisers rather than by their audiences</strong>, and sport at every level depends on sponsorship. Without that income, either these services disappear or the public pays for them directly. By moving goods quickly, advertising also supports the steady production and employment on which economic development depends.\n\nThe strongest objection is that advertising persuades people to buy things they do not need, and that some of it rests on misleading claims about what a product will do. This is a real cost, but a manageable one. Most countries now regulate advertising directly, requiring evidence for health claims and banning tobacco promotion outright, and audiences are far more sceptical than they were: reviews written by other customers now influence a purchase more than the campaign that preceded it.\n\nIn conclusion, advertising does encourage spending, and it is not the neutral public service its defenders describe. Its growth nevertheless brings better information, sharper competition and the funding of services that audiences would otherwise pay for themselves, while regulation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deals with its worst excesses</strong>. On balance, the positive effects are the greater.","phrases":[{"en":"outweigh its drawbacks","context":"Advertising has become almost impossible to avoid, and its growth is more often treated as a problem than as a benefit. In my view, although some of the criticism is deserved, the advantages of widespread advertising outweigh its drawbacks."},{"en":"make an informed choice","context":"The clearest benefit is information. A consumer who has never heard of a product cannot buy it, and without advertising people would be limited to the few brands sold in their nearest shop. Comparison of prices, features and guarantees is what allows a buyer to make an informed choice, and public prices also force sellers to compete rather than quietly charge more than their rivals. The frequent claim that advertising simply manipulates people is hard to sustain when most new products fail despite large campaigns."},{"en":"quietly charge more than their rivals","context":"The clearest benefit is information. A consumer who has never heard of a product cannot buy it, and without advertising people would be limited to the few brands sold in their nearest shop. Comparison of prices, features and guarantees is what allows a buyer to make an informed choice, and public prices also force sellers to compete rather than quietly charge more than their rivals. The frequent claim that advertising simply manipulates people is hard to sustain when most new products fail despite large campaigns."},{"en":"funded by advertisers rather than by their audiences","context":"Advertising also pays for a great deal that the public enjoys without noticing. Commercial television, radio, most news websites and nearly all free online services are funded by advertisers rather than by their audiences, and sport at every level depends on sponsorship. Without that income, either these services disappear or the public pays for them directly. By moving goods quickly, advertising also supports the steady production and employment on which economic development depends."},{"en":"deals with its worst excesses","context":"In conclusion, advertising does encourage spending, and it is not the neutral public service its defenders describe. Its growth nevertheless brings better information, sharper competition and the funding of services that audiences would otherwise pay for themselves, while regulation deals with its worst excesses. On balance, the positive effects are the greater."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#004","summary":"Do the positive effects outweigh negative effects, when advertising gains…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-111","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Advertising aimed at children","prompt":"A great deal of advertising is directed at children. Parents argue that children are misled by it, while advertisers claim that it is a useful source of information. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Children are exposed to an enormous amount of advertising, and there is disagreement about whether this is harmful. Parents generally regard it as manipulation, while advertisers describe it as information. In my opinion the parents&#x27; concerns are better founded.\n\nAdvertisers do have a case. Children are consumers, and advertisements tell them what exists, what it costs and where it can be bought. Campaigns for milk, dental care, cycle helmets and road safety have all improved children&#x27;s habits, and older children are far more sceptical than adults assume: most teenagers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recognise exaggeration</strong> and dismiss it.\n\nThe objection, however, is that young children cannot. Below the age of about eight, children do not reliably understand that an advertisement is trying to sell them something, and they treat its claims as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">straightforward statements of fact</strong>. This makes it unreasonable to describe such advertising as information. Much of it also promotes products that are harmful in quantity, particularly sugary drinks, confectionery and fast food, and the rise in childhood obesity has followed it closely. A further problem is the pressure it places on families, since children who are told repeatedly that a toy or a brand of clothing is essential will demand it, and low-income parents are left choosing between <strong class=\"key-phrase\">debt and their child&#x27;s disappointment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I accept that advertising can inform older children, but the youngest are simply unable to evaluate what they are being shown. Advertising aimed at the under-tens, and all advertising for unhealthy food, should therefore be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tightly restricted</strong> rather than left to the industry to regulate itself.","phrases":[{"en":"recognise exaggeration","context":"Advertisers do have a case. Children are consumers, and advertisements tell them what exists, what it costs and where it can be bought. Campaigns for milk, dental care, cycle helmets and road safety have all improved children's habits, and older children are far more sceptical than adults assume: most teenagers recognise exaggeration and dismiss it."},{"en":"straightforward statements of fact","context":"The objection, however, is that young children cannot. Below the age of about eight, children do not reliably understand that an advertisement is trying to sell them something, and they treat its claims as straightforward statements of fact. This makes it unreasonable to describe such advertising as information. Much of it also promotes products that are harmful in quantity, particularly sugary drinks, confectionery and fast food, and the rise in childhood obesity has followed it closely. A further problem is the pressure it places on families, since children who are told repeatedly that a toy or a brand of clothing is essential will demand it, and low-income parents are left choosing between debt and their child's disappointment."},{"en":"debt and their child's disappointment","context":"The objection, however, is that young children cannot. Below the age of about eight, children do not reliably understand that an advertisement is trying to sell them something, and they treat its claims as straightforward statements of fact. This makes it unreasonable to describe such advertising as information. Much of it also promotes products that are harmful in quantity, particularly sugary drinks, confectionery and fast food, and the rise in childhood obesity has followed it closely. A further problem is the pressure it places on families, since children who are told repeatedly that a toy or a brand of clothing is essential will demand it, and low-income parents are left choosing between debt and their child's disappointment."},{"en":"tightly restricted","context":"In conclusion, I accept that advertising can inform older children, but the youngest are simply unable to evaluate what they are being shown. Advertising aimed at the under-tens, and all advertising for unhealthy food, should therefore be tightly restricted rather than left to the industry to regulate itself."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#005","summary":"Advertising aimed at children"},{"id":"writing-task-2-147","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Should we follow fashion?","prompt":"Fashion is difficult to follow, and some people argue that we should not follow it but should instead wear what we like and feel comfortable in. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people argue that following fashion is pointless and that individuals should simply wear what they find comfortable and attractive. I largely agree with this view, although fashion serves purposes that its critics tend to overlook.\n\nThe case against following fashion is strong. Trends change deliberately and frequently, precisely so that clothes which are still perfectly wearable <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appear out of date</strong>, and consumers who attempt to keep up spend substantial sums replacing them. The environmental consequence is severe, since the clothing industry is among the largest producers of textile waste and industrial water pollution, and much of what is bought is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worn only a handful of times</strong>. Following trends closely also produces a kind of uniformity that is, ironically, the very opposite of the individuality it claims to express.\n\nThere is a social cost as well. Young people in particular judge one another by clothing, and children from poorer families can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">excluded for wearing the wrong brand</strong>, which is one of the main arguments for school uniforms.\n\nNevertheless, clothing is not purely functional. What people wear communicates something about their personality, profession and mood, and taking care over appearance can genuinely improve confidence. Certain occasions, such as interviews and formal events, also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry expectations</strong> that it would be naive to ignore entirely.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that people should dress according to their own taste and comfort rather than following each new trend. Clothing does communicate meaning, but that is an argument for choosing deliberately rather than for following fashion.","phrases":[{"en":"appear out of date","context":"The case against following fashion is strong. Trends change deliberately and frequently, precisely so that clothes which are still perfectly wearable appear out of date, and consumers who attempt to keep up spend substantial sums replacing them. The environmental consequence is severe, since the clothing industry is among the largest producers of textile waste and industrial water pollution, and much of what is bought is worn only a handful of times. Following trends closely also produces a kind of uniformity that is, ironically, the very opposite of the individuality it claims to express."},{"en":"worn only a handful of times","context":"The case against following fashion is strong. Trends change deliberately and frequently, precisely so that clothes which are still perfectly wearable appear out of date, and consumers who attempt to keep up spend substantial sums replacing them. The environmental consequence is severe, since the clothing industry is among the largest producers of textile waste and industrial water pollution, and much of what is bought is worn only a handful of times. Following trends closely also produces a kind of uniformity that is, ironically, the very opposite of the individuality it claims to express."},{"en":"excluded for wearing the wrong brand","context":"There is a social cost as well. Young people in particular judge one another by clothing, and children from poorer families can be excluded for wearing the wrong brand, which is one of the main arguments for school uniforms."},{"en":"carry expectations","context":"Nevertheless, clothing is not purely functional. What people wear communicates something about their personality, profession and mood, and taking care over appearance can genuinely improve confidence. Certain occasions, such as interviews and formal events, also carry expectations that it would be naive to ignore entirely."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#006","summary":"Should we follow fashion?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-148","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Do high sales reflect advertising or real need?","prompt":"The high sales of popular consumer goods reflect the power of advertising rather than the real needs of the society in which they are sold. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that the popularity of consumer goods demonstrates the power of advertising rather than any genuine social need. I largely agree with this view, although advertising cannot create demand entirely on its own.\n\nThe evidence for the argument is easy to find. Many of the products that sell in the greatest quantities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meet no need that previously existed</strong>. Bottled water is the clearest example, since it is sold at hundreds of times the price of tap water of comparable quality, and its market was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">created almost entirely by marketing</strong>. The annual replacement of phones and clothing follows the same pattern: the previous model still works perfectly, but advertising has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">redefined it as embarrassing</strong>. Enormous sums are involved, and companies would not continue spending them year after year if they did not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">measurably shift behaviour</strong>.\n\nAdvertising is also particularly effective where a product is difficult to judge. With cosmetics, supplements and branded clothing, buyers cannot easily assess quality, so image determines the decision, which is why these industries advertise most heavily.\n\nNevertheless, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the claim can be pushed too far</strong>. Advertising cannot sustain a product that people dislike, and expensive campaigns for genuinely poor products fail regularly. Consumers also buy a great many things, such as washing machines and medicines, simply because they solve real problems, and these would sell with or without promotion.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that advertising rather than necessity explains much of what sells in large quantities. It works, however, by directing existing desires rather than by inventing them from nothing.","phrases":[{"en":"meet no need that previously existed","context":"The evidence for the argument is easy to find. Many of the products that sell in the greatest quantities meet no need that previously existed. Bottled water is the clearest example, since it is sold at hundreds of times the price of tap water of comparable quality, and its market was created almost entirely by marketing. The annual replacement of phones and clothing follows the same pattern: the previous model still works perfectly, but advertising has redefined it as embarrassing. Enormous sums are involved, and companies would not continue spending them year after year if they did not measurably shift behaviour."},{"en":"created almost entirely by marketing","context":"The evidence for the argument is easy to find. Many of the products that sell in the greatest quantities meet no need that previously existed. Bottled water is the clearest example, since it is sold at hundreds of times the price of tap water of comparable quality, and its market was created almost entirely by marketing. The annual replacement of phones and clothing follows the same pattern: the previous model still works perfectly, but advertising has redefined it as embarrassing. Enormous sums are involved, and companies would not continue spending them year after year if they did not measurably shift behaviour."},{"en":"redefined it as embarrassing","context":"The evidence for the argument is easy to find. Many of the products that sell in the greatest quantities meet no need that previously existed. Bottled water is the clearest example, since it is sold at hundreds of times the price of tap water of comparable quality, and its market was created almost entirely by marketing. The annual replacement of phones and clothing follows the same pattern: the previous model still works perfectly, but advertising has redefined it as embarrassing. Enormous sums are involved, and companies would not continue spending them year after year if they did not measurably shift behaviour."},{"en":"measurably shift behaviour","context":"The evidence for the argument is easy to find. Many of the products that sell in the greatest quantities meet no need that previously existed. Bottled water is the clearest example, since it is sold at hundreds of times the price of tap water of comparable quality, and its market was created almost entirely by marketing. The annual replacement of phones and clothing follows the same pattern: the previous model still works perfectly, but advertising has redefined it as embarrassing. Enormous sums are involved, and companies would not continue spending them year after year if they did not measurably shift behaviour."},{"en":"the claim can be pushed too far","context":"Nevertheless, the claim can be pushed too far. Advertising cannot sustain a product that people dislike, and expensive campaigns for genuinely poor products fail regularly. Consumers also buy a great many things, such as washing machines and medicines, simply because they solve real problems, and these would sell with or without promotion."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#007","summary":"Do high sales reflect advertising or real need?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-151","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Does advertising make everyone the same?","prompt":"Some people argue that advertising is discouraging people from being different individuals, and makes people to be or seem to be the same. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Advertising often encourages people to buy the same popular products, but it does not completely remove individuality. I partly agree that advertising promotes conformity, especially through global brands and social media. However, consumers still interpret messages differently and can use products to express personal preferences.\n\nThe pressure to conform is real. Companies repeat images of the ideal home, body or lifestyle <strong class=\"key-phrase\">until these standards appear normal</strong>. Influencers then display the same clothes, phones and beauty products to millions of followers. Young consumers may purchase these items <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mainly to avoid feeling excluded</strong>. In this way, advertising can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn social approval into a sales tool</strong> and narrow the range of choices that seem acceptable.\n\nNevertheless, people are not passive. Income, age, culture and personal values shape how they respond to a campaign. The same advertisement may attract one person and annoy another. Digital markets also make specialised products easier to find, so individuals can choose independent designers, niche music or goods linked to particular interests. Advertising may influence the options people notice, but it rarely determines every final decision.\n\nPlatforms and regulators can also require clear labels for paid promotion, helping audiences <strong class=\"key-phrase\">separate personal recommendation from commercial persuasion</strong>. It also discourages hidden commercial influence in everyday online content.\n\nIn conclusion, advertising contributes to similar tastes by promoting a limited set of highly visible lifestyles. Yet it cannot erase personal judgement or cultural difference. The best response is not to blame advertising for all conformity, but to improve media literacy and give consumers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a wider and more transparent range of choices</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"until these standards appear normal","context":"The pressure to conform is real. Companies repeat images of the ideal home, body or lifestyle until these standards appear normal. Influencers then display the same clothes, phones and beauty products to millions of followers. Young consumers may purchase these items mainly to avoid feeling excluded. In this way, advertising can turn social approval into a sales tool and narrow the range of choices that seem acceptable."},{"en":"mainly to avoid feeling excluded","context":"The pressure to conform is real. Companies repeat images of the ideal home, body or lifestyle until these standards appear normal. Influencers then display the same clothes, phones and beauty products to millions of followers. Young consumers may purchase these items mainly to avoid feeling excluded. In this way, advertising can turn social approval into a sales tool and narrow the range of choices that seem acceptable."},{"en":"turn social approval into a sales tool","context":"The pressure to conform is real. Companies repeat images of the ideal home, body or lifestyle until these standards appear normal. Influencers then display the same clothes, phones and beauty products to millions of followers. Young consumers may purchase these items mainly to avoid feeling excluded. In this way, advertising can turn social approval into a sales tool and narrow the range of choices that seem acceptable."},{"en":"separate personal recommendation from commercial persuasion","context":"Platforms and regulators can also require clear labels for paid promotion, helping audiences separate personal recommendation from commercial persuasion. It also discourages hidden commercial influence in everyday online content."},{"en":"a wider and more transparent range of choices","context":"In conclusion, advertising contributes to similar tastes by promoting a limited set of highly visible lifestyles. Yet it cannot erase personal judgement or cultural difference. The best response is not to blame advertising for all conformity, but to improve media literacy and give consumers a wider and more transparent range of choices."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#008","summary":"People argue that advertising is discouraging people from being different…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2206","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Is advertising unnecessary?","prompt":"If a product is good and it meets people's needs, people will buy it. So advertising is unnecessary and is no more than a form of entertainment. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people claim that a genuinely good product will sell itself, and that advertising is therefore pointless and serves only to amuse us. I disagree with this view, because advertising performs an essential informational role in a competitive market.\n\nThe argument that quality alone is enough ignores a simple problem: consumers cannot buy what they do not know exists. Thousands of new products appear every year, and without advertising a small company producing an excellent item would have no way of reaching the public, while established brands would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep their customers by default</strong>. Advertising is what allows a new competitor to challenge a dominant firm, and this competition is what <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drives prices down and quality up</strong>.\n\nAdvertising also carries information that buyers genuinely need, such as price changes, safety warnings, new features and where a product can be obtained. Public information campaigns work in exactly the same way. Government advertising on smoking, road safety, vaccination and energy conservation has changed behaviour <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on a large scale</strong>, and nobody would describe these campaigns as entertainment.\n\nI would accept that some advertising is little more than amusement, and that a proportion of it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manipulative or misleading</strong>. Advertisements aimed at young children, or those encouraging unhealthy eating and unnecessary debt, deserve criticism and firm regulation. However, the existence of bad advertising is an argument for controlling it, not for concluding that the whole activity is unnecessary.\n\nIn conclusion, I strongly disagree that good products sell themselves. Advertising informs consumers, allows new businesses to compete and supports campaigns that benefit the public, even though it clearly needs to be regulated.","phrases":[{"en":"keep their customers by default","context":"The argument that quality alone is enough ignores a simple problem: consumers cannot buy what they do not know exists. Thousands of new products appear every year, and without advertising a small company producing an excellent item would have no way of reaching the public, while established brands would keep their customers by default. Advertising is what allows a new competitor to challenge a dominant firm, and this competition is what drives prices down and quality up."},{"en":"drives prices down and quality up","context":"The argument that quality alone is enough ignores a simple problem: consumers cannot buy what they do not know exists. Thousands of new products appear every year, and without advertising a small company producing an excellent item would have no way of reaching the public, while established brands would keep their customers by default. Advertising is what allows a new competitor to challenge a dominant firm, and this competition is what drives prices down and quality up."},{"en":"on a large scale","context":"Advertising also carries information that buyers genuinely need, such as price changes, safety warnings, new features and where a product can be obtained. Public information campaigns work in exactly the same way. Government advertising on smoking, road safety, vaccination and energy conservation has changed behaviour on a large scale, and nobody would describe these campaigns as entertainment."},{"en":"manipulative or misleading","context":"I would accept that some advertising is little more than amusement, and that a proportion of it is manipulative or misleading. Advertisements aimed at young children, or those encouraging unhealthy eating and unnecessary debt, deserve criticism and firm regulation. However, the existence of bad advertising is an argument for controlling it, not for concluding that the whole activity is unnecessary."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#009","summary":"Is advertising unnecessary?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2237","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Do people simply copy each other?","prompt":"The tendency of human beings to copy one another is shown in the popularity of fashions in clothes and other consumer goods. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often claimed that fashion proves how readily people imitate each other. I agree that imitation is a real force behind it, but I think the claim describes only part of what fashion is doing.\n\nThe evidence for copying is hard to deny. Trends spread outwards from a small group and are adopted almost simultaneously by millions who never made an independent judgement about whether a garment suited them, and the same pattern governs phones, cars and kitchens. This behaviour has an obvious explanation: belonging to a group has always been safer than standing outside it, so people read what others wear as a signal of which group they belong to and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dress to match it</strong>. Advertising works precisely because it exploits this.\n\nWhat the claim misses is that imitation is rarely blind. People copy selectively, choosing the styles of groups they wish to join and deliberately avoiding those they do not, which is why teenagers reject their parents&#x27; clothing rather than copying the nearest available model. Fashion is therefore a language for saying who one is, and using a shared language is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not the same as having no ideas</strong>.\n\nThere is a further limitation. Much of what looks like imitation is simply what is available, since a shopper can only choose from what shops have decided to stock that season. When a person buys the coat that everyone is wearing, the explanation may be limited choice rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a desire to fit in</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that fashion reveals a genuine tendency to imitate, since the speed at which trends spread cannot be explained any other way. It also reveals people using shared symbols to express difference, which makes fashion a more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberate act than the statement suggests</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"dress to match it","context":"The evidence for copying is hard to deny. Trends spread outwards from a small group and are adopted almost simultaneously by millions who never made an independent judgement about whether a garment suited them, and the same pattern governs phones, cars and kitchens. This behaviour has an obvious explanation: belonging to a group has always been safer than standing outside it, so people read what others wear as a signal of which group they belong to and dress to match it. Advertising works precisely because it exploits this."},{"en":"not the same as having no ideas","context":"What the claim misses is that imitation is rarely blind. People copy selectively, choosing the styles of groups they wish to join and deliberately avoiding those they do not, which is why teenagers reject their parents' clothing rather than copying the nearest available model. Fashion is therefore a language for saying who one is, and using a shared language is not the same as having no ideas."},{"en":"a desire to fit in","context":"There is a further limitation. Much of what looks like imitation is simply what is available, since a shopper can only choose from what shops have decided to stock that season. When a person buys the coat that everyone is wearing, the explanation may be limited choice rather than a desire to fit in."},{"en":"deliberate act than the statement suggests","context":"In conclusion, I agree that fashion reveals a genuine tendency to imitate, since the speed at which trends spread cannot be explained any other way. It also reveals people using shared symbols to express difference, which makes fashion a more deliberate act than the statement suggests."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#010","summary":"Whether the popularity of fashion shows a human tendency to imitate"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2242","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Are shoppers really unaffected by adverts?","prompt":"Some people find advertisements amusing or annoying and they are not influenced by them when they shop. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Many shoppers insist that advertising has no effect on them and that they buy purely on price and quality. I disagree, because this belief rests on a mistaken idea of how advertising is designed to work.\n\nThe claim assumes that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">influence means persuasion a person would notice</strong>. In fact most advertising aims at familiarity rather than argument. Repeated exposure <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes a brand name feel safe</strong>, and a shopper facing two similar products under time pressure reaches for the one that seems familiar without ever forming a conscious opinion about it. This is precisely why companies spend on sponsorship and logos that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make no claim about the product at all</strong>.\n\nThe spending itself is the strongest evidence against the claim. Firms competing hard on cost would not commit billions annually to something that failed, and they measure the return closely enough to know when a campaign works. If consumers were genuinely unmoved, the industry would have collapsed long ago.\n\nI would accept a limited version of the argument. Advertising rarely makes anyone buy something they have no use for, and it is far less effective for large purchases such as a house or a car, where buyers research carefully and compare figures. Its real power lies in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deciding between near-identical options</strong>, which describes most everyday shopping.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree with the statement. Finding an advertisement irritating does not prevent it from working, since it operates through recognition rather than argument, and the people most confident that they are immune are often the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">least likely to notice its effect</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"influence means persuasion a person would notice","context":"The claim assumes that influence means persuasion a person would notice. In fact most advertising aims at familiarity rather than argument. Repeated exposure makes a brand name feel safe, and a shopper facing two similar products under time pressure reaches for the one that seems familiar without ever forming a conscious opinion about it. This is precisely why companies spend on sponsorship and logos that make no claim about the product at all."},{"en":"makes a brand name feel safe","context":"The claim assumes that influence means persuasion a person would notice. In fact most advertising aims at familiarity rather than argument. Repeated exposure makes a brand name feel safe, and a shopper facing two similar products under time pressure reaches for the one that seems familiar without ever forming a conscious opinion about it. This is precisely why companies spend on sponsorship and logos that make no claim about the product at all."},{"en":"make no claim about the product at all","context":"The claim assumes that influence means persuasion a person would notice. In fact most advertising aims at familiarity rather than argument. Repeated exposure makes a brand name feel safe, and a shopper facing two similar products under time pressure reaches for the one that seems familiar without ever forming a conscious opinion about it. This is precisely why companies spend on sponsorship and logos that make no claim about the product at all."},{"en":"deciding between near-identical options","context":"I would accept a limited version of the argument. Advertising rarely makes anyone buy something they have no use for, and it is far less effective for large purchases such as a house or a car, where buyers research carefully and compare figures. Its real power lies in deciding between near-identical options, which describes most everyday shopping."},{"en":"least likely to notice its effect","context":"In conclusion, I disagree with the statement. Finding an advertisement irritating does not prevent it from working, since it operates through recognition rather than argument, and the people most confident that they are immune are often the least likely to notice its effect."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#011","summary":"Whether people are genuinely uninfluenced by advertising"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2253","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Why advertising persuades less","prompt":"Research indicates that nowadays some consumers are much less influenced by advertising than in the past. What do you think are the reasons for this? Do you think this is a positive or negative development?","answer":"Advertising appears to persuade less effectively than it did a generation ago, despite budgets that have never been larger. The explanation lies in how much more information buyers now have, and in my view the change is positive.\n\nThe main reason is that consumers can check claims. Before a purchase, a shopper can read hundreds of reviews from people who actually own the product, and a manufacturer&#x27;s assurance that something is durable carries little weight against a thousand accounts of it breaking. Advertising has lost its old advantage of being the only available information. Sheer volume matters too: people who see several thousand advertisements a day stop noticing them, and the growth of ad-blocking and subscription services shows how far audiences will go to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoid being marketed to</strong>. Trust has fallen alongside attention, since decades of exaggeration, and the discovery that endorsements are frequently paid for, have taught buyers to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treat advertising as an interested party</strong> rather than as a source of facts.\n\nI regard this as a healthy development. When purchases are decided by evidence rather than by the size of a marketing budget, small firms with genuinely better products can compete against established brands that once won simply by buying more attention. Households also waste less on goods that fail to match their description.\n\nThere is a cost worth noting. Advertising funds newspapers and free services, and as its effectiveness falls, that funding weakens, which has already damaged local journalism in several countries.\n\nIn conclusion, consumers are harder to persuade because they can now verify claims and have learned to distrust the ones they cannot. This is positive overall, since it rewards firms that compete on quality rather than on volume of advertising.","phrases":[{"en":"avoid being marketed to","context":"The main reason is that consumers can check claims. Before a purchase, a shopper can read hundreds of reviews from people who actually own the product, and a manufacturer's assurance that something is durable carries little weight against a thousand accounts of it breaking. Advertising has lost its old advantage of being the only available information. Sheer volume matters too: people who see several thousand advertisements a day stop noticing them, and the growth of ad-blocking and subscription services shows how far audiences will go to avoid being marketed to. Trust has fallen alongside attention, since decades of exaggeration, and the discovery that endorsements are frequently paid for, have taught buyers to treat advertising as an interested party rather than as a source of facts."},{"en":"treat advertising as an interested party","context":"The main reason is that consumers can check claims. Before a purchase, a shopper can read hundreds of reviews from people who actually own the product, and a manufacturer's assurance that something is durable carries little weight against a thousand accounts of it breaking. Advertising has lost its old advantage of being the only available information. Sheer volume matters too: people who see several thousand advertisements a day stop noticing them, and the growth of ad-blocking and subscription services shows how far audiences will go to avoid being marketed to. Trust has fallen alongside attention, since decades of exaggeration, and the discovery that endorsements are frequently paid for, have taught buyers to treat advertising as an interested party rather than as a source of facts."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#012","summary":"Why consumers are less influenced by advertising than in the past"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2317","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"Does age decide how we shop?","prompt":"People's shopping habits depend more on the age group they belong to than on any other factor. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Marketing departments divide customers by age before anything else, and the differences between a teenager and a pensioner in a shop are obvious. I disagree that age is the strongest influence, because income and circumstance explain far more.\n\nAge clearly does shape behaviour. Younger buyers grew up with phones and expect to order at midnight and return items free, while older customers often prefer to handle goods and speak to a person. Each generation also carries the habits of the economy it grew up in: people who remember shortages repair things, and those raised in abundance replace them. Age further decides what is actually needed, since a student and a grandparent are not shopping for the same life.\n\nIncome is a stronger predictor, however. Two thirty-year-olds, one earning three times the other, will differ far more in what they buy, where and how often than a rich thirty-year-old and a rich sixty-year-old. Money determines whether a person can buy quality that lasts or must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep replacing the cheapest version</strong>, and whether they can wait for a sale or must buy on credit today. Poverty produces recognisable habits at every age.\n\nOther factors also outrank age. Having a first child changes spending more sharply than any birthday. Where people live decides what is available, and culture, education and values explain why one shopper checks where a product came from and another checks only the price. Age itself is often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">standing in for these things</strong> rather than causing the behaviour.\n\nIn conclusion, age influences shopping in visible ways and is a useful rough guide. Wealth, family stage and location shape what people actually buy more powerfully, so treating age as the main factor <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mistakes a convenient label for a real cause</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"keep replacing the cheapest version","context":"Income is a stronger predictor, however. Two thirty-year-olds, one earning three times the other, will differ far more in what they buy, where and how often than a rich thirty-year-old and a rich sixty-year-old. Money determines whether a person can buy quality that lasts or must keep replacing the cheapest version, and whether they can wait for a sale or must buy on credit today. Poverty produces recognisable habits at every age."},{"en":"standing in for these things","context":"Other factors also outrank age. Having a first child changes spending more sharply than any birthday. Where people live decides what is available, and culture, education and values explain why one shopper checks where a product came from and another checks only the price. Age itself is often standing in for these things rather than causing the behaviour."},{"en":"mistakes a convenient label for a real cause","context":"In conclusion, age influences shopping in visible ways and is a useful rough guide. Wealth, family stage and location shape what people actually buy more powerfully, so treating age as the main factor mistakes a convenient label for a real cause."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#013","summary":"Whether age matters more than other factors in shaping shopping habits"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2379","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Advertising & Consumerism","title":"A car, a television and a fridge for everyone","prompt":"It has been suggested that everyone in the world wants to own a car, a television and a fridge. Do the disadvantages of such a development outweigh the advantages?","answer":"Rising incomes in developing regions have brought hundreds of millions of households their first vehicle and their first refrigerator. I believe the disadvantages of universal ownership outweigh the advantages, though the benefits to individual families are genuine and should not be dismissed.\n\nThose benefits are easy to underestimate from a wealthy country. A refrigerator prevents food poisoning and waste, allows a family to buy in quantity and cheaply, and removes the daily journey to market that usually falls to women. A car brings work, hospitals and schools within reach of rural households, and a television carries news, education and language into homes that had neither. For the families concerned these are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improvements in health and opportunity rather than luxuries</strong>.\n\nThe difficulty appears at scale. Extending current levels of ownership to everybody would require materials and energy far beyond what the planet can supply, and cars are the clearest case: cities designed around them consume enormous space, produce most urban air pollution and become slower as ownership rises, so the promised freedom <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disappears in the traffic it creates</strong>. Manufacturing and running billions more appliances would add emissions at a point when they must be falling.\n\nThere is also a social cost. Products are now built to be replaced rather than repaired, so ownership generates a stream of waste, and much of it is exported to poorer countries. Households borrow to buy what they are told they need, and a definition of a good life measured by possessions leaves people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perpetually short of the next item</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, these goods have improved the lives of families who obtained them, and refusing them to others would be indefensible. Universal ownership on the present model cannot be supplied, so the aim should be the services these things provide rather than the objects themselves, through public transport, durable goods and shared infrastructure.","phrases":[{"en":"improvements in health and opportunity rather than luxuries","context":"Those benefits are easy to underestimate from a wealthy country. A refrigerator prevents food poisoning and waste, allows a family to buy in quantity and cheaply, and removes the daily journey to market that usually falls to women. A car brings work, hospitals and schools within reach of rural households, and a television carries news, education and language into homes that had neither. For the families concerned these are improvements in health and opportunity rather than luxuries."},{"en":"disappears in the traffic it creates","context":"The difficulty appears at scale. Extending current levels of ownership to everybody would require materials and energy far beyond what the planet can supply, and cars are the clearest case: cities designed around them consume enormous space, produce most urban air pollution and become slower as ownership rises, so the promised freedom disappears in the traffic it creates. Manufacturing and running billions more appliances would add emissions at a point when they must be falling."},{"en":"perpetually short of the next item","context":"There is also a social cost. Products are now built to be replaced rather than repaired, so ownership generates a stream of waste, and much of it is exported to poorer countries. Households borrow to buy what they are told they need, and a definition of a good life measured by possessions leaves people perpetually short of the next item."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#014","summary":"Whether the drawbacks of universal consumer ownership outweigh the benefits"},{"id":"writing-task-2-002","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Ageing","title":"Living longer, and the problems it brings","prompt":"In the developed world, average life expectancy is increasing. What problems will this cause for individuals and society? Suggest some measures that could be taken to reduce the impact of ageing populations.","answer":"It is true that people in industrialised nations can expect to live longer than ever before. Although there will undoubtedly be some negative consequences of this trend, societies can take steps to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mitigate these potential problems</strong>.\n\nAs people live longer and the populations of developed countries grow older, several related problems can be anticipated. The main issue is that there will obviously be more people of retirement age who will be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eligible to receive a pension</strong>. The proportion of younger, working adults will be smaller, and governments will therefore receive less money in taxes in relation to the size of the population. In other words, an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ageing population</strong> will mean <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a greater tax burden for working adults</strong>. Further pressures will include a rise in the demand for healthcare, and the fact young adults will increasingly have to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look after their elderly relatives</strong>.\n\nThere are several actions that governments could take to solve the problems described above. Firstly, a simple solution would be to increase the retirement age for working adults, perhaps from 65 to 70. Nowadays, people of this age tend to be healthy enough to continue a productive working life. A second measure would be for governments to encourage immigration in order to increase the number of working adults who pay taxes. Finally, money from national budgets will need to be taken from other areas and spent on vital healthcare, accommodation and transport facilities for the rising numbers of older citizens.\n\nIn conclusion, various measures can be taken to tackle the problems that are certain to arise as the populations of countries grow older.","phrases":[{"en":"mitigate these potential problems","context":"It is true that people in industrialised nations can expect to live longer than ever before. Although there will undoubtedly be some negative consequences of this trend, societies can take steps to mitigate these potential problems."},{"en":"eligible to receive a pension","context":"As people live longer and the populations of developed countries grow older, several related problems can be anticipated. The main issue is that there will obviously be more people of retirement age who will be eligible to receive a pension. The proportion of younger, working adults will be smaller, and governments will therefore receive less money in taxes in relation to the size of the population. In other words, an ageing population will mean a greater tax burden for working adults. Further pressures will include a rise in the demand for healthcare, and the fact young adults will increasingly have to look after their elderly relatives."},{"en":"ageing population","context":"As people live longer and the populations of developed countries grow older, several related problems can be anticipated. The main issue is that there will obviously be more people of retirement age who will be eligible to receive a pension. The proportion of younger, working adults will be smaller, and governments will therefore receive less money in taxes in relation to the size of the population. In other words, an ageing population will mean a greater tax burden for working adults. Further pressures will include a rise in the demand for healthcare, and the fact young adults will increasingly have to look after their elderly relatives."},{"en":"a greater tax burden for working adults","context":"As people live longer and the populations of developed countries grow older, several related problems can be anticipated. The main issue is that there will obviously be more people of retirement age who will be eligible to receive a pension. The proportion of younger, working adults will be smaller, and governments will therefore receive less money in taxes in relation to the size of the population. In other words, an ageing population will mean a greater tax burden for working adults. Further pressures will include a rise in the demand for healthcare, and the fact young adults will increasingly have to look after their elderly relatives."},{"en":"look after their elderly relatives","context":"As people live longer and the populations of developed countries grow older, several related problems can be anticipated. The main issue is that there will obviously be more people of retirement age who will be eligible to receive a pension. The proportion of younger, working adults will be smaller, and governments will therefore receive less money in taxes in relation to the size of the population. In other words, an ageing population will mean a greater tax burden for working adults. Further pressures will include a rise in the demand for healthcare, and the fact young adults will increasingly have to look after their elderly relatives."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#015","summary":"Ageing populations: problems and responses"},{"id":"writing-task-2-158","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Ageing","title":"An ageing population","prompt":"In many countries, the proportion of older people is increasing steadily. Is this a positive or a negative development for society?","answer":"In most developed countries, and increasingly in developing ones, the proportion of citizens over sixty-five is rising steadily. Although this trend reflects genuine progress, I believe its overall effect on society is negative unless governments respond to it seriously.\n\nThere are reasons to view the change positively. An ageing population is the direct result of better healthcare, safer workplaces and improved nutrition, so it is in one sense <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an achievement rather than a problem</strong>. Older citizens also contribute a great deal that never appears in economic statistics: they care for grandchildren, which allows younger parents to work, and they make up the majority of volunteers in charities, hospitals and community organisations.\n\nThe difficulties, however, are substantial. Pensions and healthcare are funded largely by taxes paid by working adults, so as the number of retired people grows while the workforce shrinks, each worker must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry a heavier burden</strong>. Demand for hospital beds, care homes and treatment for chronic illness rises sharply, and these are the most expensive services a state provides. Younger families face a further pressure, since many now support elderly relatives while raising children of their own.\n\nGovernments can reduce these problems by raising the retirement age, encouraging immigration and investing in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preventive healthcare</strong>, but none of these measures is politically easy and few countries have acted decisively.\n\nIn conclusion, an ageing population is evidence of social success, yet the financial and practical strain it creates is severe. On balance I regard it as a negative development, and one that requires <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far more urgent attention</strong> than it currently receives.","phrases":[{"en":"an achievement rather than a problem","context":"There are reasons to view the change positively. An ageing population is the direct result of better healthcare, safer workplaces and improved nutrition, so it is in one sense an achievement rather than a problem. Older citizens also contribute a great deal that never appears in economic statistics: they care for grandchildren, which allows younger parents to work, and they make up the majority of volunteers in charities, hospitals and community organisations."},{"en":"carry a heavier burden","context":"The difficulties, however, are substantial. Pensions and healthcare are funded largely by taxes paid by working adults, so as the number of retired people grows while the workforce shrinks, each worker must carry a heavier burden. Demand for hospital beds, care homes and treatment for chronic illness rises sharply, and these are the most expensive services a state provides. Younger families face a further pressure, since many now support elderly relatives while raising children of their own."},{"en":"preventive healthcare","context":"Governments can reduce these problems by raising the retirement age, encouraging immigration and investing in preventive healthcare, but none of these measures is politically easy and few countries have acted decisively."},{"en":"far more urgent attention","context":"In conclusion, an ageing population is evidence of social success, yet the financial and practical strain it creates is severe. On balance I regard it as a negative development, and one that requires far more urgent attention than it currently receives."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","_displayNo":"#016","summary":"An ageing population"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2297","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Ageing","title":"Is growing old worse than it used to be?","prompt":"Some people say that in the modern world, getting old is entirely bad. Others believe that old people's lives nowadays are much better than in the past. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Old age is described either as a period of neglect in a society that values youth or as the most comfortable it has ever been. My own view is that the second description is closer to the truth in material terms, while the first identifies something real about status.\n\nThe pessimistic account has evidence behind it. Older people once held authority because they carried knowledge nobody else had, and in a world where skills change every few years that authority has gone; an experienced worker is now more likely to be regarded as expensive than as valuable. Families have dispersed, so the daily contact that once made age bearable has been replaced by occasional visits, and loneliness among the elderly is now common enough to appear in health statistics. Pensions are under pressure, and a longer life is not obviously a gift if the money runs out first.\n\nThe optimistic account rests on comparison. Life expectancy has risen by decades, and those years are healthier: hips are replaced, cataracts removed and heart conditions managed, so people remain active at seventy in ways their grandparents were not at fifty. State pensions and public health care, which barely existed a century ago, mean far fewer older people live in poverty. Technology keeps distant families in contact, and travel, study and second careers are now normal after retirement rather than eccentric.\n\nWeighing these, the material improvement is beyond argument, and few would exchange today&#x27;s old age for that of a hundred years ago. What has genuinely worsened is position: the elderly are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comfortable but consulted less</strong>, and a society that treats them as a cost rather than a resource wastes them.\n\nIn conclusion, growing old today is healthier, longer and more secure than at any earlier time, so calling it entirely bad is wrong. The loss of respect and daily company is real, and that, rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the condition of the body</strong>, is what most needs repairing.","phrases":[{"en":"comfortable but consulted less","context":"Weighing these, the material improvement is beyond argument, and few would exchange today's old age for that of a hundred years ago. What has genuinely worsened is position: the elderly are comfortable but consulted less, and a society that treats them as a cost rather than a resource wastes them."},{"en":"the condition of the body","context":"In conclusion, growing old today is healthier, longer and more secure than at any earlier time, so calling it entirely bad is wrong. The loss of respect and daily company is real, and that, rather than the condition of the body, is what most needs repairing."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#017","summary":"Whether old age today is entirely bad or better than in the past"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2372","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Ageing","title":"Who should pay for retirement?","prompt":"Some people believe governments should provide financial support for retired people, while others think individuals should save for their own retirement. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Populations are ageing quickly, and the question of who pays for the years after work is now urgent in most developed countries. Both positions contain something necessary, and my own view is that the two have to be combined rather than chosen between.\n\nThe argument for personal saving is partly arithmetic. State pensions are paid by current workers, and as the number of retired people rises relative to workers, that arrangement becomes a promise the next generation cannot afford to keep. Private saving also gives individuals control, since money invested over forty years grows substantially, and people who save for themselves can retire when and how they choose. There is a fairness argument too, in that somebody who spent everything they earned has a weaker claim on those who did not.\n\nThe case for state support answers with what happens in practice. Many people cannot save at all: low wages, insecure work and long periods of caring for children or relatives leave nothing spare, and the individuals in that position are precisely those who will need help. Private saving is also risky, because a market collapse in the year before retirement can destroy decades of contributions, and nobody knows how long they will live. A society that leaves the old to their own resources ends up paying for them anyway through hospitals and emergency housing, at greater cost and after more suffering.\n\nThe systems that work best combine the two. A basic state pension guarantees that nobody falls into poverty, while workplace and private schemes, ideally with automatic enrolment, provide the additional income. Enrolling employees by default has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raised saving dramatically without compelling anybody</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, private saving is essential because state pensions alone will become unaffordable. It cannot stand on its own, so a guaranteed public minimum with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compulsory or automatic saving above it</strong> is the arrangement most likely to work.","phrases":[{"en":"raised saving dramatically without compelling anybody","context":"The systems that work best combine the two. A basic state pension guarantees that nobody falls into poverty, while workplace and private schemes, ideally with automatic enrolment, provide the additional income. Enrolling employees by default has raised saving dramatically without compelling anybody."},{"en":"compulsory or automatic saving above it","context":"In conclusion, private saving is essential because state pensions alone will become unaffordable. It cannot stand on its own, so a guaranteed public minimum with compulsory or automatic saving above it is the arrangement most likely to work."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#018","summary":"Whether the state or the individual should fund old age"},{"id":"writing-task-2-003","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Is protecting wild animals a waste of resources?","prompt":"Wild animals have no place in the 21st century, so protecting them is a waste of resources. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people argue that it is pointless to spend money on the protection of wild animals because we humans have no need for them. I completely disagree with this point of view.\n\nIn my opinion, it is absurd to argue that wild animals have no place in the 21st century. I do not believe that planet Earth exists only for the benefit of humans, and there is nothing special about this particular century that means that we suddenly have the right to allow or encourage <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the extinction of any species</strong>. There is also no compelling reason why we should let animals die out. We do not need to exploit or destroy every last square metre of land in order to feed or accommodate the world&#x27;s population. There is plenty of room for us to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exist side by side</strong> with wild animals, and this should be our aim.\n\nI also disagree with the idea that protecting animals is a waste of resources. It is usually the protection of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">natural habitats</strong> that ensures the survival of wild animals, and most scientists agree that these habitats are also crucial for human survival. For example, rainforests produce oxygen, absorb carbon dioxide and stabilise the Earth&#x27;s climate. If we destroyed these areas, the costs of managing the resulting changes to our planet would far outweigh the costs of conservation. By protecting wild animals and their habitats, we maintain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the natural balance of all life on Earth</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, we have no right to decide whether or not wild animals should exist, and I believe that we should do everything we can to protect them.","phrases":[{"en":"the extinction of any species","context":"In my opinion, it is absurd to argue that wild animals have no place in the 21st century. I do not believe that planet Earth exists only for the benefit of humans, and there is nothing special about this particular century that means that we suddenly have the right to allow or encourage the extinction of any species. There is also no compelling reason why we should let animals die out. We do not need to exploit or destroy every last square metre of land in order to feed or accommodate the world's population. There is plenty of room for us to exist side by side with wild animals, and this should be our aim."},{"en":"exist side by side","context":"In my opinion, it is absurd to argue that wild animals have no place in the 21st century. I do not believe that planet Earth exists only for the benefit of humans, and there is nothing special about this particular century that means that we suddenly have the right to allow or encourage the extinction of any species. There is also no compelling reason why we should let animals die out. We do not need to exploit or destroy every last square metre of land in order to feed or accommodate the world's population. There is plenty of room for us to exist side by side with wild animals, and this should be our aim."},{"en":"natural habitats","context":"I also disagree with the idea that protecting animals is a waste of resources. It is usually the protection of natural habitats that ensures the survival of wild animals, and most scientists agree that these habitats are also crucial for human survival. For example, rainforests produce oxygen, absorb carbon dioxide and stabilise the Earth's climate. If we destroyed these areas, the costs of managing the resulting changes to our planet would far outweigh the costs of conservation. By protecting wild animals and their habitats, we maintain the natural balance of all life on Earth."},{"en":"the natural balance of all life on Earth","context":"I also disagree with the idea that protecting animals is a waste of resources. It is usually the protection of natural habitats that ensures the survival of wild animals, and most scientists agree that these habitats are also crucial for human survival. For example, rainforests produce oxygen, absorb carbon dioxide and stabilise the Earth's climate. If we destroyed these areas, the costs of managing the resulting changes to our planet would far outweigh the costs of conservation. By protecting wild animals and their habitats, we maintain the natural balance of all life on Earth."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#019","summary":"Protecting wild animals"},{"id":"writing-task-2-004","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Animal experiments for medicine and product safety","prompt":"Nowadays animal experiments are widely used to develop new medicines and to test the safety of other products. Some people argue that these experiments should be banned because it is morally wrong to cause animals to suffer, while others are in favour of them because of their benefits to humanity. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"It is true that medicines and other products are routinely tested on animals before they are cleared for human use. While I tend towards the viewpoint that animal testing is morally wrong, I would have to support a limited amount of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">animal experimentation</strong> for the development of medicines.\n\nOn the one hand, there are clear ethical arguments against animal experimentation. To use a common example of this practice, laboratory mice may be given an illness so that the effectiveness of a new drug can be measured. Opponents of such research argue that humans have no right to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">subject animals to this kind of trauma</strong>, and that the lives of all creatures should be respected. They believe that the benefits to humans do not justify the suffering caused, and that scientists should use alternative methods of research.\n\nOn the other hand, reliable alternatives to animal experimentation may not always be available. Supporters of the use of animals in medical research believe that a certain amount of suffering on the part of mice or rats can be justified if human lives are saved. They argue that opponents of such research might feel differently if a member of their own families needed a medical treatment that had been developed through the use of animal experimentation. Personally, I agree with the banning of animal testing for non-medical products, but I feel that it may be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a necessary evil</strong> where new drugs and medical procedures are concerned.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that it would be wrong to ban testing on animals for vital medical research until equally effective alternatives have been developed.","phrases":[{"en":"animal experimentation","context":"It is true that medicines and other products are routinely tested on animals before they are cleared for human use. While I tend towards the viewpoint that animal testing is morally wrong, I would have to support a limited amount of animal experimentation for the development of medicines."},{"en":"subject animals to this kind of trauma","context":"On the one hand, there are clear ethical arguments against animal experimentation. To use a common example of this practice, laboratory mice may be given an illness so that the effectiveness of a new drug can be measured. Opponents of such research argue that humans have no right to subject animals to this kind of trauma, and that the lives of all creatures should be respected. They believe that the benefits to humans do not justify the suffering caused, and that scientists should use alternative methods of research."},{"en":"a necessary evil","context":"On the other hand, reliable alternatives to animal experimentation may not always be available. Supporters of the use of animals in medical research believe that a certain amount of suffering on the part of mice or rats can be justified if human lives are saved. They argue that opponents of such research might feel differently if a member of their own families needed a medical treatment that had been developed through the use of animal experimentation. Personally, I agree with the banning of animal testing for non-medical products, but I feel that it may be a necessary evil where new drugs and medical procedures are concerned."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#020","summary":"The ethics of animal experimentation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-005","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Animals in captivity","prompt":"Some people feel that it is always wrong to keep animals in captivity, for instance in zoos. Other people say that there are benefits for the animals and for humans. Discuss both sides of this debate, and give your personal view.","answer":"Zoos continue to be controversial, even now that they have evolved into ‘animal parks’ or similar facilities. Most people love animals, and there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strong views on both sides</strong>. People opposed to zoos highlight the unnatural way in which animals live in such places. For example, captive animals do not need to hunt or raise offspring, and this means that they are not living as nature intended.\n\nThe anti-zoo lobby also argue that captivity damages animals psychologically, even when enclosures are spacious and close to a natural habitat. Stereotypic behaviour such as repetitive pacing, self-harm or feather-plucking is well documented in captive elephants, big cats and primates, and is rarely seen in the wild. Critics add that zoos contribute little to conservation in practice, since protecting habitat allows far larger, genuinely wild populations to survive than any enclosure can hold.\n\nThose who support zoos reply that, for many species, a zoo enclosure is safer than the wild itself. The Arabian oryx, for instance, was hunted to extinction in its natural range by the 1970s and survives today only because zoos had bred it in captivity; those animals were later reintroduced into protected desert reserves. Modern zoos increasingly operate as one part of a wider conservation system, combining breeding programmes, veterinary research and habitat protection to build up populations of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">endangered species</strong> that would otherwise disappear entirely.\n\nOverall, I feel that zoos provide a valuable service to society and the animal world, as they help to protect many species. This outweighs their possible disadvantages, as long as they are properly staffed and maintained.","phrases":[{"en":"strong views on both sides","context":"Zoos continue to be controversial, even now that they have evolved into ‘animal parks’ or similar facilities. Most people love animals, and there are strong views on both sides. People opposed to zoos highlight the unnatural way in which animals live in such places. For example, captive animals do not need to hunt or raise offspring, and this means that they are not living as nature intended."},{"en":"endangered species","context":"Those who support zoos reply that, for many species, a zoo enclosure is safer than the wild itself. The Arabian oryx, for instance, was hunted to extinction in its natural range by the 1970s and survives today only because zoos had bred it in captivity; those animals were later reintroduced into protected desert reserves. Modern zoos increasingly operate as one part of a wider conservation system, combining breeding programmes, veterinary research and habitat protection to build up populations of endangered species that would otherwise disappear entirely."}],"subtype":"Discussion","_displayNo":"#021","summary":"People feel that it is always wrong to keep animals in captivity, for…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-006","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Why conservation has failed","prompt":"Far too little has been done to prevent animals and plants from dying out, although people have been aware of the problem for a long time. Why have people failed to improve this situation? What could be done about it?","answer":"Although the loss of plant and animal species has been recognised for decades, remarkably little has been achieved. In my view this failure has two main causes, and there are practical steps that could improve the situation.\n\nThe first reason is economic. The activities that destroy habitats, such as logging, mining, intensive farming and industrial fishing, are extremely profitable and employ millions of people. A government that restricts them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">faces immediate opposition from companies and workers</strong>, whereas the benefits of conservation are distant and difficult to measure. The second reason is that responsibility is shared. Wildlife does not respect borders, so a country that protects a forest gains little if its neighbour clears one, and international agreements are usually voluntary and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rarely enforced</strong>. No individual government therefore feels the loss directly, and there is little pressure from voters, most of whom now live in cities and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never see the decline for themselves</strong>.\n\nThe most effective solution would be to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make destruction expensive</strong>. Taxing timber, palm oil and fish that are produced unsustainably, and refusing to import them, would change commercial behaviour far faster than appeals to conscience. Wealthy countries should also pay poorer ones directly to maintain rainforests, since it is unreasonable to expect a low-income nation to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forgo income for a global benefit</strong> that everybody else will share. Protected areas need proper funding for rangers and monitoring as well, because reserves that exist only on paper achieve nothing.\n\nIn conclusion, conservation has failed mainly because destruction is profitable and responsibility is divided. Progress therefore depends on financial penalties for those who damage habitats, and on genuine payments to the countries that agree to protect them.","phrases":[{"en":"faces immediate opposition from companies and workers","context":"The first reason is economic. The activities that destroy habitats, such as logging, mining, intensive farming and industrial fishing, are extremely profitable and employ millions of people. A government that restricts them faces immediate opposition from companies and workers, whereas the benefits of conservation are distant and difficult to measure. The second reason is that responsibility is shared. Wildlife does not respect borders, so a country that protects a forest gains little if its neighbour clears one, and international agreements are usually voluntary and rarely enforced. No individual government therefore feels the loss directly, and there is little pressure from voters, most of whom now live in cities and never see the decline for themselves."},{"en":"rarely enforced","context":"The first reason is economic. The activities that destroy habitats, such as logging, mining, intensive farming and industrial fishing, are extremely profitable and employ millions of people. A government that restricts them faces immediate opposition from companies and workers, whereas the benefits of conservation are distant and difficult to measure. The second reason is that responsibility is shared. Wildlife does not respect borders, so a country that protects a forest gains little if its neighbour clears one, and international agreements are usually voluntary and rarely enforced. No individual government therefore feels the loss directly, and there is little pressure from voters, most of whom now live in cities and never see the decline for themselves."},{"en":"never see the decline for themselves","context":"The first reason is economic. The activities that destroy habitats, such as logging, mining, intensive farming and industrial fishing, are extremely profitable and employ millions of people. A government that restricts them faces immediate opposition from companies and workers, whereas the benefits of conservation are distant and difficult to measure. The second reason is that responsibility is shared. Wildlife does not respect borders, so a country that protects a forest gains little if its neighbour clears one, and international agreements are usually voluntary and rarely enforced. No individual government therefore feels the loss directly, and there is little pressure from voters, most of whom now live in cities and never see the decline for themselves."},{"en":"make destruction expensive","context":"The most effective solution would be to make destruction expensive. Taxing timber, palm oil and fish that are produced unsustainably, and refusing to import them, would change commercial behaviour far faster than appeals to conscience. Wealthy countries should also pay poorer ones directly to maintain rainforests, since it is unreasonable to expect a low-income nation to forgo income for a global benefit that everybody else will share. Protected areas need proper funding for rangers and monitoring as well, because reserves that exist only on paper achieve nothing."},{"en":"forgo income for a global benefit","context":"The most effective solution would be to make destruction expensive. Taxing timber, palm oil and fish that are produced unsustainably, and refusing to import them, would change commercial behaviour far faster than appeals to conscience. Wealthy countries should also pay poorer ones directly to maintain rainforests, since it is unreasonable to expect a low-income nation to forgo income for a global benefit that everybody else will share. Protected areas need proper funding for rangers and monitoring as well, because reserves that exist only on paper achieve nothing."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Solutions","_displayNo":"#022","summary":"Why conservation has failed"},{"id":"writing-task-2-007","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Wildlife protection or helping the poor?","prompt":"Some people believe that money and time should be spent on protecting wild animals, while others argue that this money should be spent on populations living in the poorest areas of the world. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people argue that money spent protecting wild animals would be better used helping the world&#x27;s poorest communities. In my opinion this presents a false choice, since the two aims usually depend on each other.\n\nThe case for prioritising people is emotionally powerful. Millions still lack clean water, basic healthcare and enough food, and it is difficult to justify spending on nature reserves while children die of preventable diseases. Human suffering is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediate and measurable</strong>, whereas the benefits of conservation are gradual and often invisible.\n\nHowever, this argument misunderstands where poverty comes from. The poorest communities are usually those most directly dependent on the natural environment: they fish, farm and gather fuel from it. When a forest is cleared or a river polluted, it is these families who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lose their livelihoods first</strong>, so environmental destruction is itself a major cause of poverty rather than a separate concern. Protecting a wetland that supplies fish and controls flooding is therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a form of poverty reduction</strong>.\n\nThere is also a practical point about scale. The sums spent on conservation worldwide are extremely small compared with military budgets, so redirecting them would make very little difference to global poverty. Conservation can also generate income directly, since wildlife tourism in countries such as Kenya and Costa Rica employs large numbers of local people.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that we must choose between wildlife and the poor. The most effective programmes protect habitats while paying the communities that live in them, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">serves both purposes at once</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"immediate and measurable","context":"The case for prioritising people is emotionally powerful. Millions still lack clean water, basic healthcare and enough food, and it is difficult to justify spending on nature reserves while children die of preventable diseases. Human suffering is immediate and measurable, whereas the benefits of conservation are gradual and often invisible."},{"en":"lose their livelihoods first","context":"However, this argument misunderstands where poverty comes from. The poorest communities are usually those most directly dependent on the natural environment: they fish, farm and gather fuel from it. When a forest is cleared or a river polluted, it is these families who lose their livelihoods first, so environmental destruction is itself a major cause of poverty rather than a separate concern. Protecting a wetland that supplies fish and controls flooding is therefore a form of poverty reduction."},{"en":"a form of poverty reduction","context":"However, this argument misunderstands where poverty comes from. The poorest communities are usually those most directly dependent on the natural environment: they fish, farm and gather fuel from it. When a forest is cleared or a river polluted, it is these families who lose their livelihoods first, so environmental destruction is itself a major cause of poverty rather than a separate concern. Protecting a wetland that supplies fish and controls flooding is therefore a form of poverty reduction."},{"en":"serves both purposes at once","context":"In conclusion, I disagree that we must choose between wildlife and the poor. The most effective programmes protect habitats while paying the communities that live in them, which serves both purposes at once."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#023","summary":"Wildlife protection or helping the poor?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-008","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Animal experimentation","prompt":"Some people strongly oppose animal experimentation, believing that it causes suffering to animals, while others consider it necessary. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Animal experimentation divides opinion sharply. Some people regard it as an unacceptable source of suffering, while others consider it necessary for medical research. In my view, animal studies should be permitted only when there is a strong <strong class=\"key-phrase\">scientific justification</strong> and no <strong class=\"key-phrase\">validated alternative</strong> can answer the same question.\n\nThe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ethical objection</strong> deserves serious consideration. Laboratory procedures can cause pain, distress and confinement, and animals cannot consent to these experiences. Critics also question whether every experiment produces useful evidence. A treatment that appears safe in one species may behave differently in humans. Researchers should therefore explain why the proposed study is relevant rather than assume that using animals automatically makes a product safe.\n\nSupporters argue that some research questions involve interactions between organs and biological systems that remain difficult to reproduce fully. Animal studies have contributed to medical knowledge, and carefully justified work may still provide information that other methods cannot yet supply. For example, understanding how a treatment affects several body systems may require more than an isolated cell experiment. This is a conditional argument for research, however, not a defence of every existing practice.\n\nThe appropriate response is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strict assessment</strong> and continued investment in alternatives. Human cell models, organ-based laboratory systems and computer methods should replace animal procedures wherever they can provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reliable evidence</strong>. Where animal research remains justified, the number of animals and the suffering involved should be minimised, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independent reviewers</strong> should examine the expected benefits.\n\nIn conclusion, neither unrestricted experimentation nor an immediate ban on all animal studies is convincing. A responsible approach requires scientific necessity, effective oversight and a clear commitment to replacing animal use whenever reliable alternatives become available.","phrases":[{"en":"scientific justification","context":"Animal experimentation divides opinion sharply. Some people regard it as an unacceptable source of suffering, while others consider it necessary for medical research. In my view, animal studies should be permitted only when there is a strong scientific justification and no validated alternative can answer the same question."},{"en":"validated alternative","context":"Animal experimentation divides opinion sharply. Some people regard it as an unacceptable source of suffering, while others consider it necessary for medical research. In my view, animal studies should be permitted only when there is a strong scientific justification and no validated alternative can answer the same question."},{"en":"ethical objection","context":"The ethical objection deserves serious consideration. Laboratory procedures can cause pain, distress and confinement, and animals cannot consent to these experiences. Critics also question whether every experiment produces useful evidence. A treatment that appears safe in one species may behave differently in humans. Researchers should therefore explain why the proposed study is relevant rather than assume that using animals automatically makes a product safe."},{"en":"strict assessment","context":"The appropriate response is strict assessment and continued investment in alternatives. Human cell models, organ-based laboratory systems and computer methods should replace animal procedures wherever they can provide reliable evidence. Where animal research remains justified, the number of animals and the suffering involved should be minimised, and independent reviewers should examine the expected benefits."},{"en":"reliable evidence","context":"The appropriate response is strict assessment and continued investment in alternatives. Human cell models, organ-based laboratory systems and computer methods should replace animal procedures wherever they can provide reliable evidence. Where animal research remains justified, the number of animals and the suffering involved should be minimised, and independent reviewers should examine the expected benefits."},{"en":"independent reviewers","context":"The appropriate response is strict assessment and continued investment in alternatives. Human cell models, organ-based laboratory systems and computer methods should replace animal procedures wherever they can provide reliable evidence. Where animal research remains justified, the number of animals and the suffering involved should be minimised, and independent reviewers should examine the expected benefits."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#024","summary":"Animal experimentation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-103","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"The advantages and disadvantages of keeping pets","prompt":"Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of keeping pets, and give your own opinion.","answer":"Keeping animals in the home is common in most societies, but opinions differ about whether it is a good idea. In my view the benefits usually outweigh the drawbacks, provided the owner is genuinely able to look after the animal.\n\nThe advantages are mainly emotional and social. Pets provide company, which matters enormously to elderly people living alone and to those recovering from illness, and there is good evidence that owning a dog lowers stress and encourages daily exercise. For children, caring for an animal is one of the few opportunities they have to take real responsibility: feeding, cleaning and exercising a pet teaches routine, patience and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an early understanding of illness and death</strong>.\n\nThere are, however, genuine disadvantages. Animals are expensive, since food, vaccinations and veterinary treatment cost a considerable amount over a lifetime, and many owners <strong class=\"key-phrase\">underestimate this</strong>. They also restrict freedom, because a dog cannot be left alone for long and travel becomes complicated. The more serious problem is that pets are frequently acquired <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on impulse</strong> and then abandoned, which is why animal shelters in most cities are full, and certain breeds present a real danger to children if they are poorly trained.\n\nIn my opinion these objections are arguments for responsible ownership rather than against pets in general. Anyone who cannot afford veterinary care, or who is out of the house all day, should not take an animal on.\n\nIn conclusion, pets bring significant benefits to health, companionship and children&#x27;s development, and the disadvantages arise mainly when people acquire an animal <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without considering the commitment involved</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"an early understanding of illness and death","context":"The advantages are mainly emotional and social. Pets provide company, which matters enormously to elderly people living alone and to those recovering from illness, and there is good evidence that owning a dog lowers stress and encourages daily exercise. For children, caring for an animal is one of the few opportunities they have to take real responsibility: feeding, cleaning and exercising a pet teaches routine, patience and an early understanding of illness and death."},{"en":"underestimate this","context":"There are, however, genuine disadvantages. Animals are expensive, since food, vaccinations and veterinary treatment cost a considerable amount over a lifetime, and many owners underestimate this. They also restrict freedom, because a dog cannot be left alone for long and travel becomes complicated. The more serious problem is that pets are frequently acquired on impulse and then abandoned, which is why animal shelters in most cities are full, and certain breeds present a real danger to children if they are poorly trained."},{"en":"on impulse","context":"There are, however, genuine disadvantages. Animals are expensive, since food, vaccinations and veterinary treatment cost a considerable amount over a lifetime, and many owners underestimate this. They also restrict freedom, because a dog cannot be left alone for long and travel becomes complicated. The more serious problem is that pets are frequently acquired on impulse and then abandoned, which is why animal shelters in most cities are full, and certain breeds present a real danger to children if they are poorly trained."},{"en":"without considering the commitment involved","context":"In conclusion, pets bring significant benefits to health, companionship and children's development, and the disadvantages arise mainly when people acquire an animal without considering the commitment involved."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","_displayNo":"#025","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of keeping pets"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2209","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Preventing the extinction of species","prompt":"It is a natural process for animal species to become extinct. There is no reason why people should try to prevent this from happening. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes suggested that because species have always died out, there is no point in trying to prevent extinctions today. I strongly disagree with this argument, because the extinctions now taking place are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">neither natural in origin nor natural in speed</strong>.\n\nThe premise itself is misleading. It is true that species disappeared long before humans existed, but the current rate of loss is estimated to be tens or even hundreds of times higher than the natural background rate. This is not the result of gradual evolutionary change; it is caused directly by deforestation, industrial fishing, pollution, hunting and climate change. Describing a process that we ourselves have created as &quot;natural&quot; simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoids responsibility for it</strong>.\n\nThere are also strong practical reasons to act. Ecosystems depend on the relationships between species, and removing one can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trigger a chain of further collapses</strong>. The decline of bees and other pollinating insects, for example, threatens a large proportion of the world&#x27;s food crops. A great many medicines were originally derived from wild plants and animals, so every species lost may represent a treatment that will now never be found.\n\nFinally, there is a moral dimension. Humans have acquired the power to destroy other forms of life on a global scale, and with that power comes an obligation. Future generations are entitled to inherit a world that still contains elephants, tigers and coral reefs, and we have no right to decide otherwise on their behalf.\n\nIn conclusion, extinction caused by human activity is not a natural process, and I believe we have both a practical and a moral duty to prevent it.","phrases":[{"en":"neither natural in origin nor natural in speed","context":"It is sometimes suggested that because species have always died out, there is no point in trying to prevent extinctions today. I strongly disagree with this argument, because the extinctions now taking place are neither natural in origin nor natural in speed."},{"en":"avoids responsibility for it","context":"The premise itself is misleading. It is true that species disappeared long before humans existed, but the current rate of loss is estimated to be tens or even hundreds of times higher than the natural background rate. This is not the result of gradual evolutionary change; it is caused directly by deforestation, industrial fishing, pollution, hunting and climate change. Describing a process that we ourselves have created as \"natural\" simply avoids responsibility for it."},{"en":"trigger a chain of further collapses","context":"There are also strong practical reasons to act. Ecosystems depend on the relationships between species, and removing one can trigger a chain of further collapses. The decline of bees and other pollinating insects, for example, threatens a large proportion of the world's food crops. A great many medicines were originally derived from wild plants and animals, so every species lost may represent a treatment that will now never be found."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#026","summary":"Preventing the extinction of species"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2292","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Is losing species the biggest problem?","prompt":"Some people say that the main environmental problem of our time is the loss of particular species of plants and animals. Others say there are more important environmental problems. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Environmental attention is divided between the disappearance of wildlife and problems such as warming, pollution and water shortage. Both sides describe something serious, and my own view is that the argument is less a ranking than a question of what causes what.\n\nThe case for treating species loss as central rests on permanence. A polluted river can be cleaned within a decade, but an extinct animal is gone for ever, and current rates of loss are far above anything in the geological record. Species also perform work that human beings depend on without paying for: pollination of crops, decomposition of waste and the regulation of pests. Losing enough of them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weakens the systems that produce our food</strong>, and since we understand very little about how these connections operate, each loss carries unknown consequences.\n\nThe opposing view argues from immediacy. Air pollution kills millions of people every year, unsafe water kills hundreds of thousands of children, and climate change is already producing failed harvests and displacement. These affect human beings now, and where budgets are limited it is difficult to justify spending on a rare bird rather than on clean water. There is also a practical objection: a species protected on land that is drying out is not really protected.\n\nMy own position is that the second group of problems is more urgent while the first is more final, and they are not separate. Warming, pollution and habitat clearance are what is driving species loss, so extinction is largely a symptom, and treating the causes protects wildlife more effectively than any programme aimed at individual animals. That does not make conservation optional, since some species will disappear before the causes are fixed.\n\nIn conclusion, the disappearance of species is the least reversible of our environmental problems. Because it is driven by climate change, pollution and land use, addressing those <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is also the most effective form of conservation</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"weakens the systems that produce our food","context":"The case for treating species loss as central rests on permanence. A polluted river can be cleaned within a decade, but an extinct animal is gone for ever, and current rates of loss are far above anything in the geological record. Species also perform work that human beings depend on without paying for: pollination of crops, decomposition of waste and the regulation of pests. Losing enough of them weakens the systems that produce our food, and since we understand very little about how these connections operate, each loss carries unknown consequences."},{"en":"is also the most effective form of conservation","context":"In conclusion, the disappearance of species is the least reversible of our environmental problems. Because it is driven by climate change, pollution and land use, addressing those is also the most effective form of conservation."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#027","summary":"Whether the loss of plants and animals outranks other environmental problems"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2312","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Do we still need animals for food and products?","prompt":"In the modern world, it is no longer necessary to use animals as food or for other products such as medicines and clothing. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Plant-based meat, synthetic fabrics and laboratory-grown materials have made it possible to live comfortably without animal products. I partly agree with the statement, since it is broadly true of food and clothing in wealthy countries, but it does not yet hold for medicine or for much of the world.\n\nFood is where the claim is strongest. Nutritionists accept that a planned vegetarian diet supplies everything the body needs, and the environmental argument is powerful: livestock occupies a vast share of farmland and produces a large share of greenhouse gases, so the same land feeds far more people when it grows crops. Clothing is similar, because warm and durable alternatives to wool and leather already exist.\n\nMedicine is a different matter. Insulin, vaccines and many cancer treatments were developed using animals, and no regulator anywhere will approve a new drug tested only on cells and computer models. That will change as those methods improve, but it has not changed yet, and pretending otherwise would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cost lives that could have been saved</strong>.\n\nThe claim also assumes conditions that do not exist everywhere. Herders on land too dry for crops have no alternative to their animals, and in many regions meat, milk and eggs remain the cheapest reliable source of protein for children. Substitutes are expensive and often unavailable, so what is a free choice in a rich city is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a matter of survival elsewhere</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, a person in a developed country can reasonably give up animal food and clothing today, and there are strong environmental reasons to do so. Calling animal use unnecessary everywhere ignores both medical reality and the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">communities whose diet depends on livestock</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"cost lives that could have been saved","context":"Medicine is a different matter. Insulin, vaccines and many cancer treatments were developed using animals, and no regulator anywhere will approve a new drug tested only on cells and computer models. That will change as those methods improve, but it has not changed yet, and pretending otherwise would cost lives that could have been saved."},{"en":"a matter of survival elsewhere","context":"The claim also assumes conditions that do not exist everywhere. Herders on land too dry for crops have no alternative to their animals, and in many regions meat, milk and eggs remain the cheapest reliable source of protein for children. Substitutes are expensive and often unavailable, so what is a free choice in a rich city is a matter of survival elsewhere."},{"en":"communities whose diet depends on livestock","context":"In conclusion, a person in a developed country can reasonably give up animal food and clothing today, and there are strong environmental reasons to do so. Calling animal use unnecessary everywhere ignores both medical reality and the communities whose diet depends on livestock."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#028","summary":"Whether using animals for food, medicine and clothing is still necessary"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2335","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Protect every species or only some?","prompt":"Some people believe that all wild animals should be protected. Others say that only a few wild animals should be protected. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Conservation budgets are small and the number of threatened species is enormous, which forces an uncomfortable question about where the money goes. Both positions here are defensible, and my own view is that protecting habitats resolves most of the disagreement.\n\nThe case for protecting everything rests on how ecosystems actually work. Species are connected in ways that are rarely obvious until something is removed: insects pollinate crops, predators keep grazing animals from stripping grassland, and unremarkable plants hold soil in place. Choosing which creatures matter also assumes a knowledge we do not have, since medicines have repeatedly come from organisms nobody valued. There is a moral argument as well, in that a species which took millions of years to appear should not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disappear for the sake of a short-term convenience</strong>.\n\nSelective protection answers with arithmetic. There is not enough money to save everything, and spreading it thinly saves nothing, so it is better to concentrate on species whose loss would collapse an ecosystem or that can realistically be recovered. Some animals are already beyond saving in the wild, and funds spent on them are taken from cases where the same money would work. Conservation also competes with schools and hospitals, which cannot always lose.\n\nMy own position is that the choice is largely a false one, because animals are rarely saved individually. Protecting a forest or a reef preserves thousands of species at once, most of which have never been catalogued, and doing so is cheaper than any programme aimed at a single animal. Well-known species still deserve attention, since they attract the public support that funds everything else.\n\nIn conclusion, complete protection is beyond any budget and rigid selection risks losing species we do not yet understand. Concentrating on habitats protects the greatest number for the least money, and that is where the effort belongs.","phrases":[{"en":"disappear for the sake of a short-term convenience","context":"The case for protecting everything rests on how ecosystems actually work. Species are connected in ways that are rarely obvious until something is removed: insects pollinate crops, predators keep grazing animals from stripping grassland, and unremarkable plants hold soil in place. Choosing which creatures matter also assumes a knowledge we do not have, since medicines have repeatedly come from organisms nobody valued. There is a moral argument as well, in that a species which took millions of years to appear should not disappear for the sake of a short-term convenience."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#029","summary":"Whether conservation should cover all wild animals or a selected few"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2363","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Animals & Wildlife","title":"Is it too late to save species?","prompt":"It is true that some human activities have negative effects on plant and animal species. Some people say it is too late to address this problem, while others argue that effective measures can still be taken. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Species are disappearing at a rate not seen for millions of years, and opinions divide between resignation and the belief that action still works. Both readings have evidence behind them, and my own view is that the pessimism describes the past while the optimism describes what remains.\n\nThe pessimistic case is not unreasonable. Extinction is permanent, so everything already lost is beyond recovery, and the causes are still accelerating: forests are cleared, oceans warm and habitats fragment faster than protection can be arranged. Climate change adds a difficulty that conservation alone cannot answer, since a protected reserve is useless if the climate inside it no longer suits the species it was built for. Political attention is also short, and governments that promise targets routinely miss them.\n\nThe opposing view rests on cases where intervention has worked. Whale populations have recovered since commercial hunting was banned, several birds of prey returned after pesticides were restricted, and protected marine areas refill with fish within a decade. Even severely reduced populations have been rebuilt, which shows that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decline is not the same thing as inevitability</strong>. Renewable energy and improved farming are reducing the pressure at its source.\n\nMy own position is that the argument confuses two claims. It is genuinely too late to save what has already gone, and too late to prevent all future loss. It is not too late for the vast majority of species still living, and each degree of warming avoided and each forest protected changes how much is lost rather than whether anything is.\n\nIn conclusion, some damage is irreversible and pretending otherwise helps nobody. Treating the whole problem as hopeless is a mistake, because the evidence shows recovery wherever pressure is removed, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">giving up guarantees the outcome that is feared</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"decline is not the same thing as inevitability","context":"The opposing view rests on cases where intervention has worked. Whale populations have recovered since commercial hunting was banned, several birds of prey returned after pesticides were restricted, and protected marine areas refill with fish within a decade. Even severely reduced populations have been rebuilt, which shows that decline is not the same thing as inevitability. Renewable energy and improved farming are reducing the pressure at its source."},{"en":"giving up guarantees the outcome that is feared","context":"In conclusion, some damage is irreversible and pretending otherwise helps nobody. Treating the whole problem as hopeless is a mistake, because the evidence shows recovery wherever pressure is removed, and giving up guarantees the outcome that is feared."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#030","summary":"Whether the damage to plants and animals can still be reversed"},{"id":"writing-task-2-009","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Should the state fund artists?","prompt":"Some people think that governments should give financial support to creative artists such as painters and musicians. Others believe that creative artists should be funded by alternative sources. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about the funding of creative artists. While some people disagree with the idea of government support for artists, I believe that money for art projects should come from both governments and other sources.\n\nSome art projects definitely require help from the state. In the UK, there are many works of art in public spaces, such as streets or squares in city centres. In Liverpool, for example, there are several new statues and sculptures in the docks area of the city, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">which has been redeveloped recently</strong>. These artworks <strong class=\"key-phrase\">represent culture, heritage and history</strong>. They serve to educate people about the city, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">act as landmarks</strong> or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">talking points for visitors and tourists</strong>. Governments and local councils should pay creative artists to produce this kind of art, because without their funding our cities would be much less interesting and attractive.\n\nOn the other hand, I can understand the arguments against government funding for art. The main reason for this view is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">governments have more important concerns</strong>. For example, state budgets need to be spent on education, healthcare, infrastructure and security, among other areas. These public services are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vital for a country to function properly</strong>, whereas the work of creative artists, even in public places, is a luxury. Another reason for this opinion is that artists do a job like any other professional, and they should therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">earn their own money by selling their work</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, there are good reasons why artists should rely on alternative sources of financial support, but in my opinion government help is sometimes necessary.","phrases":[{"en":"which has been redeveloped recently","context":"Some art projects definitely require help from the state. In the UK, there are many works of art in public spaces, such as streets or squares in city centres. In Liverpool, for example, there are several new statues and sculptures in the docks area of the city, which has been redeveloped recently. These artworks represent culture, heritage and history. They serve to educate people about the city, and act as landmarks or talking points for visitors and tourists. Governments and local councils should pay creative artists to produce this kind of art, because without their funding our cities would be much less interesting and attractive."},{"en":"represent culture, heritage and history","context":"Some art projects definitely require help from the state. In the UK, there are many works of art in public spaces, such as streets or squares in city centres. In Liverpool, for example, there are several new statues and sculptures in the docks area of the city, which has been redeveloped recently. These artworks represent culture, heritage and history. They serve to educate people about the city, and act as landmarks or talking points for visitors and tourists. Governments and local councils should pay creative artists to produce this kind of art, because without their funding our cities would be much less interesting and attractive."},{"en":"act as landmarks","context":"Some art projects definitely require help from the state. In the UK, there are many works of art in public spaces, such as streets or squares in city centres. In Liverpool, for example, there are several new statues and sculptures in the docks area of the city, which has been redeveloped recently. These artworks represent culture, heritage and history. They serve to educate people about the city, and act as landmarks or talking points for visitors and tourists. Governments and local councils should pay creative artists to produce this kind of art, because without their funding our cities would be much less interesting and attractive."},{"en":"talking points for visitors and tourists","context":"Some art projects definitely require help from the state. In the UK, there are many works of art in public spaces, such as streets or squares in city centres. In Liverpool, for example, there are several new statues and sculptures in the docks area of the city, which has been redeveloped recently. These artworks represent culture, heritage and history. They serve to educate people about the city, and act as landmarks or talking points for visitors and tourists. Governments and local councils should pay creative artists to produce this kind of art, because without their funding our cities would be much less interesting and attractive."},{"en":"governments have more important concerns","context":"On the other hand, I can understand the arguments against government funding for art. The main reason for this view is that governments have more important concerns. For example, state budgets need to be spent on education, healthcare, infrastructure and security, among other areas. These public services are vital for a country to function properly, whereas the work of creative artists, even in public places, is a luxury. Another reason for this opinion is that artists do a job like any other professional, and they should therefore earn their own money by selling their work."},{"en":"vital for a country to function properly","context":"On the other hand, I can understand the arguments against government funding for art. The main reason for this view is that governments have more important concerns. For example, state budgets need to be spent on education, healthcare, infrastructure and security, among other areas. These public services are vital for a country to function properly, whereas the work of creative artists, even in public places, is a luxury. Another reason for this opinion is that artists do a job like any other professional, and they should therefore earn their own money by selling their work."},{"en":"earn their own money by selling their work","context":"On the other hand, I can understand the arguments against government funding for art. The main reason for this view is that governments have more important concerns. For example, state budgets need to be spent on education, healthcare, infrastructure and security, among other areas. These public services are vital for a country to function properly, whereas the work of creative artists, even in public places, is a luxury. Another reason for this opinion is that artists do a job like any other professional, and they should therefore earn their own money by selling their work."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#031","summary":"Public funding for creative artists"},{"id":"writing-task-2-033","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Freedom of expression for artists","prompt":"Creative artists should be given freedom to express their ideas through words, pictures, music or films. Some people nevertheless think that the government should restrict artists’ freedom of expression. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Artists need freedom to explore difficult ideas, and governments should not control art simply because it is unpopular or uncomfortable. However, I do not believe that artistic freedom should be completely unlimited. Restrictions are justified when a work clearly encourages violence, hatred or serious harm.\n\nThe strongest argument for freedom is that art helps society examine ideas that ordinary public debate may avoid. A film, novel or painting can question accepted beliefs, expose injustice and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give a voice to</strong> people who are rarely heard: Orwell&#x27;s Animal Farm and Nineteen Eighty-Four, for example, were banned for decades in the communist states their allegories targeted, precisely because fiction can say plainly what direct political speech cannot risk saying. If artists fear punishment whenever they challenge authority, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">creative work becomes safe but shallow</strong>. Society then loses an important source of critical reflection and cultural progress.\n\nNevertheless, freedom of expression does not remove responsibility. A work that directly incites violence against a group, deliberately exploits children or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">presents criminal behaviour as an instruction manual</strong> can cause real harm. In such cases, a narrow legal limit is reasonable. The key is that restrictions should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">clearly defined and independently reviewed</strong>. Governments should not ban work merely because it criticises politicians, religion or social customs.\n\nIn practice, many disputes can be handled without banning a work. Age classifications, content warnings and restricted exhibition spaces allow adults to make informed choices while protecting children. This is usually preferable to censorship because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">responds to risk in a proportionate way</strong> and leaves room for serious discussion of controversial ideas.\n\nIn conclusion, artists should enjoy broad freedom because challenging art is valuable to an open society. Yet this freedom should stop where there is a clear and serious risk of harm. A system based on precise laws and independent courts would protect creative freedom without ignoring public safety.","phrases":[{"en":"give a voice to","context":"The strongest argument for freedom is that art helps society examine ideas that ordinary public debate may avoid. A film, novel or painting can question accepted beliefs, expose injustice and give a voice to people who are rarely heard: Orwell's Animal Farm and Nineteen Eighty-Four, for example, were banned for decades in the communist states their allegories targeted, precisely because fiction can say plainly what direct political speech cannot risk saying. If artists fear punishment whenever they challenge authority, creative work becomes safe but shallow. Society then loses an important source of critical reflection and cultural progress."},{"en":"creative work becomes safe but shallow","context":"The strongest argument for freedom is that art helps society examine ideas that ordinary public debate may avoid. A film, novel or painting can question accepted beliefs, expose injustice and give a voice to people who are rarely heard: Orwell's Animal Farm and Nineteen Eighty-Four, for example, were banned for decades in the communist states their allegories targeted, precisely because fiction can say plainly what direct political speech cannot risk saying. If artists fear punishment whenever they challenge authority, creative work becomes safe but shallow. Society then loses an important source of critical reflection and cultural progress."},{"en":"presents criminal behaviour as an instruction manual","context":"Nevertheless, freedom of expression does not remove responsibility. A work that directly incites violence against a group, deliberately exploits children or presents criminal behaviour as an instruction manual can cause real harm. In such cases, a narrow legal limit is reasonable. The key is that restrictions should be clearly defined and independently reviewed. Governments should not ban work merely because it criticises politicians, religion or social customs."},{"en":"clearly defined and independently reviewed","context":"Nevertheless, freedom of expression does not remove responsibility. A work that directly incites violence against a group, deliberately exploits children or presents criminal behaviour as an instruction manual can cause real harm. In such cases, a narrow legal limit is reasonable. The key is that restrictions should be clearly defined and independently reviewed. Governments should not ban work merely because it criticises politicians, religion or social customs."},{"en":"responds to risk in a proportionate way","context":"In practice, many disputes can be handled without banning a work. Age classifications, content warnings and restricted exhibition spaces allow adults to make informed choices while protecting children. This is usually preferable to censorship because it responds to risk in a proportionate way and leaves room for serious discussion of controversial ideas."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#032","summary":"Creative artists should be given freedom to express their ideas through…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-041","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Public money and the arts","prompt":"Some people argue that governments should spend money on public services and facilities, but not on the arts. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that public money should be reserved for essential services such as healthcare, education and transport, and that the arts should receive none of it. I disagree with this view, because the arts deliver benefits that easily justify a modest share of public spending.\n\nThe argument for concentrating on services is understandable. Hospitals, schools and roads are used by everybody and meet needs that nobody can do without, whereas theatres, galleries and orchestras are attended mainly by a minority. When waiting lists are long and classrooms are overcrowded, subsidising an opera company is difficult to defend.\n\nHowever, this reasoning <strong class=\"key-phrase\">underestimates what the arts contribute</strong>. Museums and galleries are among the largest tourist attractions in most countries, and the visitors they bring support hotels, restaurants and transport, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">generating far more in taxation</strong> than the original subsidy costs. The arts are also an important part of school education, since drama, music and design build creativity and confidence in children who struggle with conventional subjects. Without public funding most of this would disappear, because commercial sponsors <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support only what is already popular</strong>.\n\nThere is a further point about scale. Arts budgets typically represent <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fraction of one per cent</strong> of national spending, so removing them would make no measurable difference to healthcare while destroying institutions that took generations to build.\n\nIn conclusion, essential services must always come first, but I disagree that the arts deserve nothing at all. They <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repay their cost</strong> economically and educationally, and the sums involved are very small.","phrases":[{"en":"underestimates what the arts contribute","context":"However, this reasoning underestimates what the arts contribute. Museums and galleries are among the largest tourist attractions in most countries, and the visitors they bring support hotels, restaurants and transport, generating far more in taxation than the original subsidy costs. The arts are also an important part of school education, since drama, music and design build creativity and confidence in children who struggle with conventional subjects. Without public funding most of this would disappear, because commercial sponsors support only what is already popular."},{"en":"generating far more in taxation","context":"However, this reasoning underestimates what the arts contribute. Museums and galleries are among the largest tourist attractions in most countries, and the visitors they bring support hotels, restaurants and transport, generating far more in taxation than the original subsidy costs. The arts are also an important part of school education, since drama, music and design build creativity and confidence in children who struggle with conventional subjects. Without public funding most of this would disappear, because commercial sponsors support only what is already popular."},{"en":"support only what is already popular","context":"However, this reasoning underestimates what the arts contribute. Museums and galleries are among the largest tourist attractions in most countries, and the visitors they bring support hotels, restaurants and transport, generating far more in taxation than the original subsidy costs. The arts are also an important part of school education, since drama, music and design build creativity and confidence in children who struggle with conventional subjects. Without public funding most of this would disappear, because commercial sponsors support only what is already popular."},{"en":"a fraction of one per cent","context":"There is a further point about scale. Arts budgets typically represent a fraction of one per cent of national spending, so removing them would make no measurable difference to healthcare while destroying institutions that took generations to build."},{"en":"repay their cost","context":"In conclusion, essential services must always come first, but I disagree that the arts deserve nothing at all. They repay their cost economically and educationally, and the sums involved are very small."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#033","summary":"Public money and the arts"},{"id":"writing-task-2-137","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Museums: to entertain or to educate?","prompt":"Some people think that museums should be enjoyable places to entertain people, while others believe that the purpose of museums is to educate. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about the role and function of museums. In my opinion, museums can and should be both entertaining and educational.\n\nOn the one hand, it can be argued that the main role of a museum is to entertain. Museums are tourist attractions, and their aim is to exhibit a collection of interesting objects that many people will want to see. The average visitor may become bored if he or she has to read or listen to too much educational content, so museums often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put more of an emphasis on</strong> enjoyment rather than learning. This type of museum is designed to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visually spectacular</strong>, and may have interactive activities or even games as part of its exhibitions.\n\nOn the other hand, some people argue that museums should focus on education. The aim of any exhibition should be to teach visitors something that they did not previously know. Usually this means that the history behind the museum&#x27;s exhibits needs to be explained, and this can be done in various ways. Some museums employ professional guides to talk to their visitors, while other museums offer headsets so that visitors can listen to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">detailed commentary about the exhibition</strong>. In this way, museums can play an important role in teaching people about history, culture, science and many other aspects of life.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that a good museum should be able to offer an interesting, enjoyable and educational experience so that people can have fun and learn something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the same time</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"put more of an emphasis on","context":"On the one hand, it can be argued that the main role of a museum is to entertain. Museums are tourist attractions, and their aim is to exhibit a collection of interesting objects that many people will want to see. The average visitor may become bored if he or she has to read or listen to too much educational content, so museums often put more of an emphasis on enjoyment rather than learning. This type of museum is designed to be visually spectacular, and may have interactive activities or even games as part of its exhibitions."},{"en":"visually spectacular","context":"On the one hand, it can be argued that the main role of a museum is to entertain. Museums are tourist attractions, and their aim is to exhibit a collection of interesting objects that many people will want to see. The average visitor may become bored if he or she has to read or listen to too much educational content, so museums often put more of an emphasis on enjoyment rather than learning. This type of museum is designed to be visually spectacular, and may have interactive activities or even games as part of its exhibitions."},{"en":"detailed commentary about the exhibition","context":"On the other hand, some people argue that museums should focus on education. The aim of any exhibition should be to teach visitors something that they did not previously know. Usually this means that the history behind the museum's exhibits needs to be explained, and this can be done in various ways. Some museums employ professional guides to talk to their visitors, while other museums offer headsets so that visitors can listen to detailed commentary about the exhibition. In this way, museums can play an important role in teaching people about history, culture, science and many other aspects of life."},{"en":"at the same time","context":"In conclusion, it seems to me that a good museum should be able to offer an interesting, enjoyable and educational experience so that people can have fun and learn something at the same time."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#034","summary":"Museums: education or entertainment"},{"id":"writing-task-2-138","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Why we need music, and whose music matters","prompt":"There are many different types of music in the world today. Why do we need music? Is the traditional music of a country more important than the international music that is heard everywhere nowadays?","answer":"It is true that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a rich variety of musical styles</strong> can be found around the world. Music is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a vital part of</strong> all human cultures for a range of reasons, and I would argue that traditional music is more important than modern, international music.\n\nMusic is something that accompanies all of us throughout our lives. As children, we are taught songs by our parents and teachers as a means of learning language, or simply as a form of enjoyment. Children delight in singing with others, and it would appear that the act of singing in a group creates a connection between participants, regardless of their age. Later in life, people&#x27;s musical preferences develop, and we come to see our favourite songs as part of our life stories. Music both expresses and arouses emotions in a way that words alone cannot. In short, it is difficult to imagine life without it.\n\nIn my opinion, traditional music should be valued over the international music that has become so popular. International pop music is often catchy and fun, but it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">essentially a commercial product</strong> that is marketed and sold by business people. Traditional music, by contrast, expresses the culture, customs and history of a country. Traditional styles, such as folk songs and ceremonial music, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">connect us to the past</strong> and form part of our cultural identity. It would be a real pity if pop music <strong class=\"key-phrase\">became so predominant that these national styles disappeared</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, music is a necessary part of human existence, and I believe that traditional music should be given more importance than international music.","phrases":[{"en":"a rich variety of musical styles","context":"It is true that a rich variety of musical styles can be found around the world. Music is a vital part of all human cultures for a range of reasons, and I would argue that traditional music is more important than modern, international music."},{"en":"a vital part of","context":"It is true that a rich variety of musical styles can be found around the world. Music is a vital part of all human cultures for a range of reasons, and I would argue that traditional music is more important than modern, international music."},{"en":"essentially a commercial product","context":"In my opinion, traditional music should be valued over the international music that has become so popular. International pop music is often catchy and fun, but it is essentially a commercial product that is marketed and sold by business people. Traditional music, by contrast, expresses the culture, customs and history of a country. Traditional styles, such as folk songs and ceremonial music, connect us to the past and form part of our cultural identity. It would be a real pity if pop music became so predominant that these national styles disappeared."},{"en":"connect us to the past","context":"In my opinion, traditional music should be valued over the international music that has become so popular. International pop music is often catchy and fun, but it is essentially a commercial product that is marketed and sold by business people. Traditional music, by contrast, expresses the culture, customs and history of a country. Traditional styles, such as folk songs and ceremonial music, connect us to the past and form part of our cultural identity. It would be a real pity if pop music became so predominant that these national styles disappeared."},{"en":"became so predominant that these national styles disappeared","context":"In my opinion, traditional music should be valued over the international music that has become so popular. International pop music is often catchy and fun, but it is essentially a commercial product that is marketed and sold by business people. Traditional music, by contrast, expresses the culture, customs and history of a country. Traditional styles, such as folk songs and ceremonial music, connect us to the past and form part of our cultural identity. It would be a real pity if pop music became so predominant that these national styles disappeared."}],"subtype":"Two-part question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#035","summary":"The value of traditional music"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2240","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"National or international collections?","prompt":"Museums and art galleries should concentrate on works that show the history and culture of their own country rather than the works of other parts of the world. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Museums must decide how much of their limited space to give to their own country and how much to the wider world. I disagree with the view that they should concentrate on national material, because a collection confined to one country teaches its own history badly.\n\nThe reason is that no national story makes sense in isolation. A country&#x27;s art, food, religion and language are the product of trade, migration and conquest, so a gallery that shows only local painting cannot explain where its own painters found their techniques. Displaying a local textile beside the imported dyes that coloured it tells visitors far more about their own past than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the object standing alone</strong>.\n\nInternational collections also serve the people least able to travel. A family that will never fly to Athens or Kyoto can stand in front of objects from those places in an afternoon, and for schoolchildren this is often the first encounter with a culture they have only heard described. Removing that would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrow the horizons of exactly those visitors</strong> who depend on museums most.\n\nI accept the strongest argument on the other side. Many national collections were assembled during colonial rule, and objects taken without consent should be returned, which will inevitably reduce foreign holdings in some countries. That is an argument about ownership rather than about principle, and museums can continue to show the world through loans, exchanges and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">jointly curated exhibitions</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree with concentrating on national works. A country understands itself better when its own objects are shown beside those that influenced them, and the alternative produces a museum that is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">patriotic but not informative</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the object standing alone","context":"The reason is that no national story makes sense in isolation. A country's art, food, religion and language are the product of trade, migration and conquest, so a gallery that shows only local painting cannot explain where its own painters found their techniques. Displaying a local textile beside the imported dyes that coloured it tells visitors far more about their own past than the object standing alone."},{"en":"narrow the horizons of exactly those visitors","context":"International collections also serve the people least able to travel. A family that will never fly to Athens or Kyoto can stand in front of objects from those places in an afternoon, and for schoolchildren this is often the first encounter with a culture they have only heard described. Removing that would narrow the horizons of exactly those visitors who depend on museums most."},{"en":"jointly curated exhibitions","context":"I accept the strongest argument on the other side. Many national collections were assembled during colonial rule, and objects taken without consent should be returned, which will inevitably reduce foreign holdings in some countries. That is an argument about ownership rather than about principle, and museums can continue to show the world through loans, exchanges and jointly curated exhibitions."},{"en":"patriotic but not informative","context":"In conclusion, I disagree with concentrating on national works. A country understands itself better when its own objects are shown beside those that influenced them, and the alternative produces a museum that is patriotic but not informative."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#036","summary":"Whether museums should focus on their own country's history and culture"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2252","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Returning historical objects","prompt":"Historical objects in museums all over the world should be returned to their country of origin. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Many of the most famous objects in European museums were removed from countries under colonial rule, and pressure to return them has grown. I largely agree that they should go back, though I would not apply the principle to every object without exception.\n\nThe central argument is about how the objects were obtained. A sculpture taken during a military occupation, from a people who had no power to refuse, was not acquired in any meaningful sense, and continuing to display it treats that history as settled. Many of these objects were also religious or ceremonial rather than decorative, so removing them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stripped them of their purpose</strong> as well as their setting. Returning them acknowledges a wrong in the only way that costs anything.\n\nThere is a practical argument too. Museums hold far more than they can exhibit, and objects that sit in storage for decades in one country would be central exhibits in another, seen by the people whose ancestors made them and studied by scholars who understand the context. Access is currently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reversed for the communities most concerned</strong>, who must travel abroad to see their own heritage.\n\nI would accept two limits. Where a country genuinely cannot yet protect an object from war or theft, delay is reasonable, provided it is a delay rather than a refusal. Objects bought openly and fairly also stand in a different position from those seized, and treating the two <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as though they were identical</strong> weakens the case.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that historical objects should generally be returned, because ownership acquired by force is not ownership. The argument is strongest for objects taken without consent, and it can be met while keeping the world&#x27;s collections connected through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-term loans in both directions</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"stripped them of their purpose","context":"The central argument is about how the objects were obtained. A sculpture taken during a military occupation, from a people who had no power to refuse, was not acquired in any meaningful sense, and continuing to display it treats that history as settled. Many of these objects were also religious or ceremonial rather than decorative, so removing them stripped them of their purpose as well as their setting. Returning them acknowledges a wrong in the only way that costs anything."},{"en":"reversed for the communities most concerned","context":"There is a practical argument too. Museums hold far more than they can exhibit, and objects that sit in storage for decades in one country would be central exhibits in another, seen by the people whose ancestors made them and studied by scholars who understand the context. Access is currently reversed for the communities most concerned, who must travel abroad to see their own heritage."},{"en":"as though they were identical","context":"I would accept two limits. Where a country genuinely cannot yet protect an object from war or theft, delay is reasonable, provided it is a delay rather than a refusal. Objects bought openly and fairly also stand in a different position from those seized, and treating the two as though they were identical weakens the case."},{"en":"long-term loans in both directions","context":"In conclusion, I agree that historical objects should generally be returned, because ownership acquired by force is not ownership. The argument is strongest for objects taken without consent, and it can be met while keeping the world's collections connected through long-term loans in both directions."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#037","summary":"Whether museum objects should be returned to their country of origin"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2266","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Public art in towns and cities","prompt":"The government should spend money on putting more works of art such as paintings and statues in towns and cities to make them more attractive. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Public sculpture and murals are often dismissed as decoration that a council cannot justify while other services are cut. I agree that governments should fund such work, provided the money is spent in ways that residents actually value.\n\nThe first argument is that appearance affects behaviour. Neglected streets invite further neglect, whereas a well-maintained square with something worth looking at is used more, and places that are used are safer. Several cities have found that commissioning murals on walls that were repeatedly defaced <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stopped the vandalism altogether</strong>, because a wall that clearly belongs to somebody is treated differently.\n\nPublic art is also the only art that reaches everybody. Entering a gallery requires knowing that one is welcome there, and many people never do, whereas a sculpture in a station is encountered by commuters who would never buy a ticket to see it. For children in areas with no cultural provision, this may be the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">only art they meet outside a screen</strong>. There is an economic return as well, since distinctive public works draw visitors and give a place an identity that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no shopping centre can supply</strong>.\n\nI would attach one condition. Money is wasted when a council commissions an expensive piece that residents dislike and were never consulted about, and such projects damage the case for all the others. Funding should follow consultation, and should include local artists and maintenance rather than a single purchase.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that public money should pay for art in public spaces, because it improves how places are treated and reaches people galleries never do. The spending is justified only where the work is chosen <strong class=\"key-phrase\">with the community rather than for it</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"stopped the vandalism altogether","context":"The first argument is that appearance affects behaviour. Neglected streets invite further neglect, whereas a well-maintained square with something worth looking at is used more, and places that are used are safer. Several cities have found that commissioning murals on walls that were repeatedly defaced stopped the vandalism altogether, because a wall that clearly belongs to somebody is treated differently."},{"en":"only art they meet outside a screen","context":"Public art is also the only art that reaches everybody. Entering a gallery requires knowing that one is welcome there, and many people never do, whereas a sculpture in a station is encountered by commuters who would never buy a ticket to see it. For children in areas with no cultural provision, this may be the only art they meet outside a screen. There is an economic return as well, since distinctive public works draw visitors and give a place an identity that no shopping centre can supply."},{"en":"no shopping centre can supply","context":"Public art is also the only art that reaches everybody. Entering a gallery requires knowing that one is welcome there, and many people never do, whereas a sculpture in a station is encountered by commuters who would never buy a ticket to see it. For children in areas with no cultural provision, this may be the only art they meet outside a screen. There is an economic return as well, since distinctive public works draw visitors and give a place an identity that no shopping centre can supply."},{"en":"with the community rather than for it","context":"In conclusion, I agree that public money should pay for art in public spaces, because it improves how places are treated and reaches people galleries never do. The spending is justified only where the work is chosen with the community rather than for it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#038","summary":"Spending public money on art in towns and cities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2276","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Can anyone create art?","prompt":"Some people say that every human being can create art, while others think that only people born with the ability can create art. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Painting, music and writing are often described either as skills anyone can pick up or as gifts a person is simply born with. I think both descriptions capture something true, but the first is closer to how art actually works.\n\nThe case for natural ability is easy to see in the exceptional. Some children draw accurately or hold a tune before anyone teaches them, and a few adults produce work that decades of training would not explain. There is probably a real difference in how such people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">notice detail and hold ideas in mind</strong>. Anyone who has watched an ordinary student and a gifted one receive the same lesson knows that the results are not equal.\n\nThe wider view is more convincing, though, because it describes the great majority. Drawing is largely a matter of learning to look, and people who claim they cannot draw usually mean that they stopped at eight years old. Skills that appear innate turn out, when examined, to rest on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">years of quiet practice</strong> that nobody saw. Art also covers far more than technical display: cooking, gardening, photographs of a family and songs sung badly at home are all acts of making, and they are open to everyone.\n\nThe distinction matters because of what each belief produces. Treating art as a gift gives most people permission to never try, which is why so many adults say they have no creative ability at all. Treating it as a practice invites them in, and the result is not a nation of masters but a population that makes things rather than only consuming them.\n\nIn conclusion, exceptional talent is genuine and rare, and it explains the few. Everybody else can still make art of real value through attention and practice, so the belief that creation belongs to the gifted few is both false and discouraging.","phrases":[{"en":"notice detail and hold ideas in mind","context":"The case for natural ability is easy to see in the exceptional. Some children draw accurately or hold a tune before anyone teaches them, and a few adults produce work that decades of training would not explain. There is probably a real difference in how such people notice detail and hold ideas in mind. Anyone who has watched an ordinary student and a gifted one receive the same lesson knows that the results are not equal."},{"en":"years of quiet practice","context":"The wider view is more convincing, though, because it describes the great majority. Drawing is largely a matter of learning to look, and people who claim they cannot draw usually mean that they stopped at eight years old. Skills that appear innate turn out, when examined, to rest on years of quiet practice that nobody saw. Art also covers far more than technical display: cooking, gardening, photographs of a family and songs sung badly at home are all acts of making, and they are open to everyone."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#039","summary":"Whether art is open to everyone or only to the naturally gifted"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2354","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Arts & Museums","title":"Live performance or the screen at home?","prompt":"In today's world, it is no longer necessary to attend live performances such as shows or concerts, because watching them at home on television or a computer is a better option. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"High-quality recordings of concerts and plays are now available for a fraction of the price of a ticket. I disagree that this makes attendance unnecessary, although watching at home is genuinely better in several respects.\n\nThe advantages of the screen are considerable. Cost is the largest: a family can watch an opera for the price of a coffee, while seats would be beyond them entirely. Recordings also remove distance, so people in small towns and those with disabilities or young children gain access to performances they could never reach. The view is frequently better than from a real seat, since cameras show the performer&#x27;s hands and face, and a recording can be paused, repeated and kept. For learning an instrument or studying a play, that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ability to watch the same moment again</strong> is invaluable.\n\nWhat the screen removes, however, is the reason live performance exists. An audience changes a performance: musicians respond to it, actors adjust their timing to laughter, and no two nights are identical, which is why recordings of the same work differ so much. There is also a physical dimension that no equipment reproduces, since sound in a hall arrives through the body as well as the ears. Watching among strangers who are all absorbed in the same thing at the same moment produces a shared experience that a living room cannot.\n\nAttendance matters for the art form too. Ticket income supports musicians, technicians and venues, and an audience that only ever watches at home removes the money that pays for the performances it is watching.\n\nIn conclusion, home viewing has made performance far more accessible and is often the more practical choice. It reproduces the content of an event rather than the event itself, so live attendance <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remains worth the cost and the journey</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"ability to watch the same moment again","context":"The advantages of the screen are considerable. Cost is the largest: a family can watch an opera for the price of a coffee, while seats would be beyond them entirely. Recordings also remove distance, so people in small towns and those with disabilities or young children gain access to performances they could never reach. The view is frequently better than from a real seat, since cameras show the performer's hands and face, and a recording can be paused, repeated and kept. For learning an instrument or studying a play, that ability to watch the same moment again is invaluable."},{"en":"remains worth the cost and the journey","context":"In conclusion, home viewing has made performance far more accessible and is often the more practical choice. It reproduces the content of an event rather than the event itself, so live attendance remains worth the cost and the journey."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#040","summary":"Whether watching shows at home has made live attendance unnecessary"},{"id":"writing-task-2-010","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Business & Companies","title":"Do businesses have social responsibilities?","prompt":"As well as making money, businesses also have social responsibilities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Businesses have always aimed to make a profit, but it is becoming increasingly common to hear people talk about the social obligations that companies have. I completely agree with the idea that businesses should do more for society than simply make money.\n\nOn the one hand, I accept that businesses must make money in order to survive in a competitive world. It seems logical that the priority of any company should be to cover its running costs, such as employees’ wages and payments for buildings and utilities. On top of these costs, companies also need to invest in improvements and innovations if they wish to remain successful. If a company is unable to pay its bills or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meet the changing needs of customers</strong>, any concerns about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social responsibilities become irrelevant</strong>. In other words, a company can only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a positive contribution to</strong> society if it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in good financial health</strong>.\n\nOn the other hand, companies should not be run with the sole aim of maximising profit; they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a wider role to play in</strong> society. One social obligation that owners and managers have is to treat their employees well, rather than exploiting them. For example, they could pay a “living wage” to ensure that workers have a good quality of life. I also like the idea that businesses could use a proportion of their profits to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support local charities</strong>, environmental projects or education initiatives. Finally, instead of trying to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">minimise their tax payments by using accounting loopholes</strong>, I believe that company bosses should be happy to contribute to society through the tax system.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that companies should place as much importance on their social responsibilities as they do on their financial objectives.","phrases":[{"en":"meet the changing needs of customers","context":"On the one hand, I accept that businesses must make money in order to survive in a competitive world. It seems logical that the priority of any company should be to cover its running costs, such as employees’ wages and payments for buildings and utilities. On top of these costs, companies also need to invest in improvements and innovations if they wish to remain successful. If a company is unable to pay its bills or meet the changing needs of customers, any concerns about social responsibilities become irrelevant. In other words, a company can only make a positive contribution to society if it is in good financial health."},{"en":"social responsibilities become irrelevant","context":"On the one hand, I accept that businesses must make money in order to survive in a competitive world. It seems logical that the priority of any company should be to cover its running costs, such as employees’ wages and payments for buildings and utilities. On top of these costs, companies also need to invest in improvements and innovations if they wish to remain successful. If a company is unable to pay its bills or meet the changing needs of customers, any concerns about social responsibilities become irrelevant. In other words, a company can only make a positive contribution to society if it is in good financial health."},{"en":"make a positive contribution to","context":"On the one hand, I accept that businesses must make money in order to survive in a competitive world. It seems logical that the priority of any company should be to cover its running costs, such as employees’ wages and payments for buildings and utilities. On top of these costs, companies also need to invest in improvements and innovations if they wish to remain successful. If a company is unable to pay its bills or meet the changing needs of customers, any concerns about social responsibilities become irrelevant. In other words, a company can only make a positive contribution to society if it is in good financial health."},{"en":"in good financial health","context":"On the one hand, I accept that businesses must make money in order to survive in a competitive world. It seems logical that the priority of any company should be to cover its running costs, such as employees’ wages and payments for buildings and utilities. On top of these costs, companies also need to invest in improvements and innovations if they wish to remain successful. If a company is unable to pay its bills or meet the changing needs of customers, any concerns about social responsibilities become irrelevant. In other words, a company can only make a positive contribution to society if it is in good financial health."},{"en":"have a wider role to play in","context":"On the other hand, companies should not be run with the sole aim of maximising profit; they have a wider role to play in society. One social obligation that owners and managers have is to treat their employees well, rather than exploiting them. For example, they could pay a “living wage” to ensure that workers have a good quality of life. I also like the idea that businesses could use a proportion of their profits to support local charities, environmental projects or education initiatives. Finally, instead of trying to minimise their tax payments by using accounting loopholes, I believe that company bosses should be happy to contribute to society through the tax system."},{"en":"support local charities","context":"On the other hand, companies should not be run with the sole aim of maximising profit; they have a wider role to play in society. One social obligation that owners and managers have is to treat their employees well, rather than exploiting them. For example, they could pay a “living wage” to ensure that workers have a good quality of life. I also like the idea that businesses could use a proportion of their profits to support local charities, environmental projects or education initiatives. Finally, instead of trying to minimise their tax payments by using accounting loopholes, I believe that company bosses should be happy to contribute to society through the tax system."},{"en":"minimise their tax payments by using accounting loopholes","context":"On the other hand, companies should not be run with the sole aim of maximising profit; they have a wider role to play in society. One social obligation that owners and managers have is to treat their employees well, rather than exploiting them. For example, they could pay a “living wage” to ensure that workers have a good quality of life. I also like the idea that businesses could use a proportion of their profits to support local charities, environmental projects or education initiatives. Finally, instead of trying to minimise their tax payments by using accounting loopholes, I believe that company bosses should be happy to contribute to society through the tax system."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#041","summary":"Corporate social responsibility"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Multinationals in developing countries","prompt":"Multinational companies are becoming increasingly common in developing countries. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this?","answer":"Multinational companies can bring investment, technology and employment to developing countries, but they may also exploit weak regulation. Their overall impact is positive only when governments negotiate fair conditions and enforce labour and environmental standards.\n\nThe main advantage is economic opportunity. A large international firm can create thousands of direct and indirect jobs, train local workers and connect domestic suppliers to global markets. Competition may also improve product quality and introduce new management methods. These effects can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise productivity across the wider economy</strong>, particularly when knowledge and management practices spread from the foreign firm to local businesses in the same sector.\n\nThe risks are equally real. Some companies move production to countries where wages are low and regulation is weak. Profits may leave the country, while local communities carry the cost of pollution or unsafe work. Governments may also offer excessive tax concessions in the hope of attracting investment.\n\nHost countries can limit the disadvantages through enforceable rules. Foreign firms should meet the same labour, tax and environmental standards as domestic companies, major contracts should include training for local staff, and local suppliers should be given a fair chance to join international production networks rather than being limited to low-paid and insecure work. When regulation is credible, multinational investment can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build local capability instead of creating dependence</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, multinational companies are neither automatically beneficial nor harmful. Developing countries gain most when foreign investment supports local skills and suppliers rather than operating as an isolated low-cost industry. Strong institutions are needed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn private investment into public benefit</strong>.","topic":"Business & Companies","phrases":[{"en":"raise productivity across the wider economy","context":"The main advantage is economic opportunity. A large international firm can create thousands of direct and indirect jobs, train local workers and connect domestic suppliers to global markets. Competition may also improve product quality and introduce new management methods. These effects can raise productivity across the wider economy, particularly when knowledge and management practices spread from the foreign firm to local businesses in the same sector."},{"en":"build local capability instead of creating dependence","context":"Host countries can limit the disadvantages through enforceable rules. Foreign firms should meet the same labour, tax and environmental standards as domestic companies, major contracts should include training for local staff, and local suppliers should be given a fair chance to join international production networks rather than being limited to low-paid and insecure work. When regulation is credible, multinational investment can build local capability instead of creating dependence."},{"en":"turn private investment into public benefit","context":"In conclusion, multinational companies are neither automatically beneficial nor harmful. Developing countries gain most when foreign investment supports local skills and suppliers rather than operating as an isolated low-cost industry. Strong institutions are needed to turn private investment into public benefit."}],"subtype":"advdis","summary":"Multinational companies in developing countries","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-109","_displayNo":"#042"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2371","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Business & Companies","title":"Should big firms provide local facilities?","prompt":"All large companies should provide sports and social facilities for the local community. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Large employers draw workers, traffic and land from the places they operate in, which raises the question of what they should give back. I partly agree that they should fund local facilities, but requiring every large company to build them would be the wrong way to arrange it.\n\nThere is a solid argument for the obligation. Big firms benefit from local infrastructure that the public paid for, and they impose costs through congestion and pressure on housing, so contributing to the community is closer to settling a bill than to charity. They also have the resources: a sports hall costs a large corporation very little and may be beyond a small town entirely. The benefits return to the company as well, since healthier employees take fewer sick days, and firms with a good local reputation find it easier to recruit and to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">obtain planning permission</strong>.\n\nThe objection is that companies are poor providers of public services. Their core purpose is to produce goods and employ people, and facilities built by a firm depend on its profits, so a downturn closes the swimming pool the town has come to rely on. Provision would also be arbitrary, since districts with a large employer would gain while poorer areas with none <strong class=\"key-phrase\">receive nothing at all</strong>. Companies naturally build what suits their own staff rather than what a community needs, and control over local amenities gives a private firm influence that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no elected body has granted it</strong>.\n\nThe better arrangement is financial rather than physical. Companies should pay fair taxes and, where a development imposes real costs, contribute to a fund that the local authority spends according to need. Sponsorship and opening existing facilities to residents are welcome additions.\n\nIn conclusion, large companies owe something to the places that host them, and many give generously. Requiring each to build facilities produces uneven and unreliable provision, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">paying towards services that the community decides on</strong> serves everyone better.","phrases":[{"en":"obtain planning permission","context":"There is a solid argument for the obligation. Big firms benefit from local infrastructure that the public paid for, and they impose costs through congestion and pressure on housing, so contributing to the community is closer to settling a bill than to charity. They also have the resources: a sports hall costs a large corporation very little and may be beyond a small town entirely. The benefits return to the company as well, since healthier employees take fewer sick days, and firms with a good local reputation find it easier to recruit and to obtain planning permission."},{"en":"receive nothing at all","context":"The objection is that companies are poor providers of public services. Their core purpose is to produce goods and employ people, and facilities built by a firm depend on its profits, so a downturn closes the swimming pool the town has come to rely on. Provision would also be arbitrary, since districts with a large employer would gain while poorer areas with none receive nothing at all. Companies naturally build what suits their own staff rather than what a community needs, and control over local amenities gives a private firm influence that no elected body has granted it."},{"en":"no elected body has granted it","context":"The objection is that companies are poor providers of public services. Their core purpose is to produce goods and employ people, and facilities built by a firm depend on its profits, so a downturn closes the swimming pool the town has come to rely on. Provision would also be arbitrary, since districts with a large employer would gain while poorer areas with none receive nothing at all. Companies naturally build what suits their own staff rather than what a community needs, and control over local amenities gives a private firm influence that no elected body has granted it."},{"en":"paying towards services that the community decides on","context":"In conclusion, large companies owe something to the places that host them, and many give generously. Requiring each to build facilities produces uneven and unreliable provision, so paying towards services that the community decides on serves everyone better."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#043","summary":"Whether large companies ought to build sports and social facilities for the community"},{"id":"writing-task-2-069","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"How much should adults control children?","prompt":"Children are often required to obey the rules set by their parents and teachers. Some people worry that too much control will not prepare them well for adult life. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Children are generally expected to obey their parents and teachers, though some people worry that strict control leaves them unprepared for adult life. In my opinion both discipline and gradually increasing freedom are necessary, and the balance should shift as a child grows older.\n\nThose who defend firm rules make a reasonable case. Young children cannot assess risk, and rules about traffic, strangers and bedtimes protect them from consequences they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot foresee</strong>. Clear boundaries also provide security, since a child who knows what is expected feels safer than one left to guess. Schools cannot function without discipline either, because a disruptive minority can prevent an entire class from learning and teachers cannot negotiate every instruction.\n\nThe opposing concern is equally serious. A young person who has never made a decision has never learned how, and such students often struggle badly at university, where nobody supervises their work. Excessive control also damages the relationship itself: teenagers who are forbidden everything tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">conceal what they do rather than obey</strong>, so parents lose the influence they were trying to protect.\n\nIn my view the answer is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a gradual transfer of responsibility</strong>. A ten-year-old needs firm rules; a sixteen-year-old needs to be allowed to choose subjects, manage money and make mistakes that carry a limited cost, while parents intervene only where safety is genuinely at stake.\n\nIn conclusion, obedience matters most in early childhood, but adolescents need practice in deciding for themselves. A family that never relaxes its control has simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">postponed the difficulty</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"cannot foresee","context":"Those who defend firm rules make a reasonable case. Young children cannot assess risk, and rules about traffic, strangers and bedtimes protect them from consequences they cannot foresee. Clear boundaries also provide security, since a child who knows what is expected feels safer than one left to guess. Schools cannot function without discipline either, because a disruptive minority can prevent an entire class from learning and teachers cannot negotiate every instruction."},{"en":"conceal what they do rather than obey","context":"The opposing concern is equally serious. A young person who has never made a decision has never learned how, and such students often struggle badly at university, where nobody supervises their work. Excessive control also damages the relationship itself: teenagers who are forbidden everything tend to conceal what they do rather than obey, so parents lose the influence they were trying to protect."},{"en":"a gradual transfer of responsibility","context":"In my view the answer is a gradual transfer of responsibility. A ten-year-old needs firm rules; a sixteen-year-old needs to be allowed to choose subjects, manage money and make mistakes that carry a limited cost, while parents intervene only where safety is genuinely at stake."},{"en":"postponed the difficulty","context":"In conclusion, obedience matters most in early childhood, but adolescents need practice in deciding for themselves. A family that never relaxes its control has simply postponed the difficulty."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#044","summary":"How much should adults control children?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-092","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Growing up in a city or the countryside","prompt":"Some people believe that a child's character is strongly influenced by whether they grow up in a city or in the countryside. Discuss the effects of both environments and give your own opinion.","answer":"Where a child spends their early years inevitably shapes the kind of adult they become. Cities and the countryside offer very different experiences, and in my view each produces particular strengths rather than one being clearly better.\n\nChildren raised in cities grow up surrounded by variety. They meet people of different nationalities, religions and social backgrounds every day, which tends to make them tolerant and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quick to adapt</strong>. Museums, libraries, sports clubs and specialised schools are within easy reach, so a child with an unusual interest can pursue it seriously. The disadvantages are equally real: urban children have far less contact with nature, less physical freedom because streets are dangerous, and they often grow up in a competitive atmosphere that produces anxiety.\n\nA rural childhood offers the opposite balance. Children can move around freely and spend much of their time outdoors, which benefits both their health and their understanding of the natural world. Because rural communities are small, children usually know their neighbours and are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">given practical responsibilities early</strong>, and this tends to produce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independence and a strong sense of belonging</strong>. The cost is limited exposure: fewer facilities, fewer examples of possible careers, and much less contact with people whose lives differ from their own, which can make later adjustment to a city difficult.\n\nIn my opinion neither environment is superior. A rural upbringing tends to build self-reliance, while a city develops adaptability and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">breadth of outlook</strong>, and what matters far more in both cases is the quality of a child&#x27;s family and schooling.\n\nIn conclusion, place shapes character in different ways, but it is not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the decisive influence</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"quick to adapt","context":"Children raised in cities grow up surrounded by variety. They meet people of different nationalities, religions and social backgrounds every day, which tends to make them tolerant and quick to adapt. Museums, libraries, sports clubs and specialised schools are within easy reach, so a child with an unusual interest can pursue it seriously. The disadvantages are equally real: urban children have far less contact with nature, less physical freedom because streets are dangerous, and they often grow up in a competitive atmosphere that produces anxiety."},{"en":"given practical responsibilities early","context":"A rural childhood offers the opposite balance. Children can move around freely and spend much of their time outdoors, which benefits both their health and their understanding of the natural world. Because rural communities are small, children usually know their neighbours and are given practical responsibilities early, and this tends to produce independence and a strong sense of belonging. The cost is limited exposure: fewer facilities, fewer examples of possible careers, and much less contact with people whose lives differ from their own, which can make later adjustment to a city difficult."},{"en":"independence and a strong sense of belonging","context":"A rural childhood offers the opposite balance. Children can move around freely and spend much of their time outdoors, which benefits both their health and their understanding of the natural world. Because rural communities are small, children usually know their neighbours and are given practical responsibilities early, and this tends to produce independence and a strong sense of belonging. The cost is limited exposure: fewer facilities, fewer examples of possible careers, and much less contact with people whose lives differ from their own, which can make later adjustment to a city difficult."},{"en":"breadth of outlook","context":"In my opinion neither environment is superior. A rural upbringing tends to build self-reliance, while a city develops adaptability and breadth of outlook, and what matters far more in both cases is the quality of a child's family and schooling."},{"en":"the decisive influence","context":"In conclusion, place shapes character in different ways, but it is not the decisive influence."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#045","summary":"Growing up in a city or the countryside"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Couples who choose not to have children","prompt":"There is an increasing trend around the world of married couples deciding not to have children. Discuss the advantages and disadvantages for couples who decide to do this.","answer":"More married couples are choosing not to have children. This decision can give adults greater freedom and financial security, but it may also create personal and social disadvantages. On balance, the effects depend on the couple&#x27;s circumstances and the scale of the trend.\n\nFor individuals, remaining child-free can reduce financial pressure and allow more time for careers, relationships and personal interests. Raising a child requires substantial money, energy and long-term responsibility. Some people also decide that they cannot provide the stable home they would want for a family. In such cases, choosing not to become parents is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a responsible personal decision</strong> rather than a selfish one.\n\nThe disadvantages are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less predictable</strong>. Some couples may later regret the decision or feel isolated in old age, although having children does not guarantee support. At a national level, a very low birth rate can shrink the future workforce and increase the cost of pensions and healthcare. Governments may then need immigration, later retirement or stronger family support policies to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintain a balanced population</strong>.\n\nThe scale of the trend also matters. A modest number of child-free couples has little effect on society, but the pattern becomes serious once it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turns into a generational norm</strong>: South Korea&#x27;s fertility rate, now among the lowest in the world, has already forced schools to close and left some regions with more retirees than working adults. This does not justify pressuring couples into parenthood, but it does mean governments cannot simply hope the trend reverses on its own; the more realistic response is policy that makes parenthood <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine choice rather than a financial penalty</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, couples should be free to decide whether parenthood suits them. The personal advantages can be considerable, but widespread childlessness may create demographic challenges. Policy should make raising children more affordable without pressuring people into parenthood.","topic":"Children & Family","phrases":[{"en":"a responsible personal decision","context":"For individuals, remaining child-free can reduce financial pressure and allow more time for careers, relationships and personal interests. Raising a child requires substantial money, energy and long-term responsibility. Some people also decide that they cannot provide the stable home they would want for a family. In such cases, choosing not to become parents is a responsible personal decision rather than a selfish one."},{"en":"less predictable","context":"The disadvantages are less predictable. Some couples may later regret the decision or feel isolated in old age, although having children does not guarantee support. At a national level, a very low birth rate can shrink the future workforce and increase the cost of pensions and healthcare. Governments may then need immigration, later retirement or stronger family support policies to maintain a balanced population."},{"en":"maintain a balanced population","context":"The disadvantages are less predictable. Some couples may later regret the decision or feel isolated in old age, although having children does not guarantee support. At a national level, a very low birth rate can shrink the future workforce and increase the cost of pensions and healthcare. Governments may then need immigration, later retirement or stronger family support policies to maintain a balanced population."},{"en":"turns into a generational norm","context":"The scale of the trend also matters. A modest number of child-free couples has little effect on society, but the pattern becomes serious once it turns into a generational norm: South Korea's fertility rate, now among the lowest in the world, has already forced schools to close and left some regions with more retirees than working adults. This does not justify pressuring couples into parenthood, but it does mean governments cannot simply hope the trend reverses on its own; the more realistic response is policy that makes parenthood a genuine choice rather than a financial penalty."},{"en":"a genuine choice rather than a financial penalty","context":"The scale of the trend also matters. A modest number of child-free couples has little effect on society, but the pattern becomes serious once it turns into a generational norm: South Korea's fertility rate, now among the lowest in the world, has already forced schools to close and left some regions with more retirees than working adults. This does not justify pressuring couples into parenthood, but it does mean governments cannot simply hope the trend reverses on its own; the more realistic response is policy that makes parenthood a genuine choice rather than a financial penalty."}],"subtype":"advdis","summary":"Couples who decide not to have children","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-098","_displayNo":"#046"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Children spending less time with their families","prompt":"In modern times, children are spending less time with their families and more time with their friends. Why has this change occurred? Do you think parents should force their children to spend more time at home?","answer":"Many children now spend less time with their families and more time with friends. This change is partly caused by longer school days, digital communication and parents’ working hours. It can support independence, but excessive distance from family may weaken guidance and emotional security.\n\nSeveral things have caused this change. Parents in many families now work longer or less predictable hours, so the house is empty for much of the afternoon, while school, homework and organised activities keep children out until the evening. Smartphones have removed the old limits on contact as well: a teenager can remain inside a group conversation all evening without leaving their bedroom, which was impossible when the telephone stood in the hall. Adolescence is also the stage at which young people naturally look outwards, and time with friends is how they develop <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of identity</strong> outside the home and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build confidence and social independence</strong>.\n\nHowever, family contact remains essential. Parents usually provide long-term support, set boundaries and notice changes in behaviour that friends may miss. If children spend most of their free time online or away from home, family members may stop discussing school, relationships and personal worries. This can leave young people more exposed to peer pressure and unreliable advice.\n\nParents should respond by protecting regular family time rather than imposing constant control. Shared meals, weekend activities and limits on late-night socialising can maintain connection while respecting a teenager&#x27;s growing independence. Forced isolation from friends is likely to create resentment, whereas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reliable routines and open conversation</strong> are more likely to bring children home willingly.\n\nIn conclusion, children now spend more time with friends because household schedules have changed and because constant contact has become effortless, and this is a normal part of growing up. Forcing them to stay at home would be counterproductive; parents do far better to make the household somewhere worth returning to, which is how families <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maintain close relationships as children become more independent</strong>.","topic":"Children & Family","phrases":[{"en":"a sense of identity","context":"Several things have caused this change. Parents in many families now work longer or less predictable hours, so the house is empty for much of the afternoon, while school, homework and organised activities keep children out until the evening. Smartphones have removed the old limits on contact as well: a teenager can remain inside a group conversation all evening without leaving their bedroom, which was impossible when the telephone stood in the hall. Adolescence is also the stage at which young people naturally look outwards, and time with friends is how they develop a sense of identity outside the home and build confidence and social independence."},{"en":"build confidence and social independence","context":"Several things have caused this change. Parents in many families now work longer or less predictable hours, so the house is empty for much of the afternoon, while school, homework and organised activities keep children out until the evening. Smartphones have removed the old limits on contact as well: a teenager can remain inside a group conversation all evening without leaving their bedroom, which was impossible when the telephone stood in the hall. Adolescence is also the stage at which young people naturally look outwards, and time with friends is how they develop a sense of identity outside the home and build confidence and social independence."},{"en":"reliable routines and open conversation","context":"Parents should respond by protecting regular family time rather than imposing constant control. Shared meals, weekend activities and limits on late-night socialising can maintain connection while respecting a teenager's growing independence. Forced isolation from friends is likely to create resentment, whereas reliable routines and open conversation are more likely to bring children home willingly."},{"en":"maintain close relationships as children become more independent","context":"In conclusion, children now spend more time with friends because household schedules have changed and because constant contact has become effortless, and this is a normal part of growing up. Forcing them to stay at home would be counterproductive; parents do far better to make the household somewhere worth returning to, which is how families maintain close relationships as children become more independent."}],"subtype":"two-part","summary":"Why children spend less time with their families","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-099","_displayNo":"#047"},{"id":"writing-task-2-143","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Young children and video games","prompt":"Many people today are worried about young children using video games. What problems might these games cause, for children and society as a whole? How could these problems be reduced?","answer":"The almost universal availability of video games among children raises problems that deserve serious attention, alongside some reasonably straightforward ways of managing them. Chief among the risks are the games&#x27; content, the amount of time children spend playing, and the physical toll of long, sedentary sessions.\n\nThe most serious concern is the level of violence in many games, which feature soldiers, criminals and killers as central, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">often glamorised, characters</strong>; a child who spends hours controlling such figures can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">come to see aggression as normal</strong>, or even admirable. A related danger is obsessive use, where some children prefer a screen to real friendships, gradually becoming isolated and socially awkward as a result; surveys in several countries have linked more than three hours of daily gaming with poorer concentration at school, suggesting the effect is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">measurable rather than merely anecdotal</strong>. Even moderate but prolonged play takes a physical toll, contributing to eye strain, disrupted sleep and poor posture that can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">difficult to correct once established</strong>.\n\nThese problems point to some fairly direct remedies. Stricter content regulation, with games reviewed by an independent body in the same way that films are classified, would keep the most violent material away from young players. To tackle the problem of obsessive use, schools could offer counselling and practical advice on healthy screen habits, while safety guidance printed on packaging, or built into the games themselves, would keep young players aware of the physical risks of long sessions.\n\nTo sum up, this is a problem that has both social and physical effects, and the solution will require combined action by manufacturers, schools and authorities.","phrases":[{"en":"often glamorised, characters","context":"The most serious concern is the level of violence in many games, which feature soldiers, criminals and killers as central, often glamorised, characters; a child who spends hours controlling such figures can come to see aggression as normal, or even admirable. A related danger is obsessive use, where some children prefer a screen to real friendships, gradually becoming isolated and socially awkward as a result; surveys in several countries have linked more than three hours of daily gaming with poorer concentration at school, suggesting the effect is measurable rather than merely anecdotal. Even moderate but prolonged play takes a physical toll, contributing to eye strain, disrupted sleep and poor posture that can be difficult to correct once established."},{"en":"come to see aggression as normal","context":"The most serious concern is the level of violence in many games, which feature soldiers, criminals and killers as central, often glamorised, characters; a child who spends hours controlling such figures can come to see aggression as normal, or even admirable. A related danger is obsessive use, where some children prefer a screen to real friendships, gradually becoming isolated and socially awkward as a result; surveys in several countries have linked more than three hours of daily gaming with poorer concentration at school, suggesting the effect is measurable rather than merely anecdotal. Even moderate but prolonged play takes a physical toll, contributing to eye strain, disrupted sleep and poor posture that can be difficult to correct once established."},{"en":"measurable rather than merely anecdotal","context":"The most serious concern is the level of violence in many games, which feature soldiers, criminals and killers as central, often glamorised, characters; a child who spends hours controlling such figures can come to see aggression as normal, or even admirable. A related danger is obsessive use, where some children prefer a screen to real friendships, gradually becoming isolated and socially awkward as a result; surveys in several countries have linked more than three hours of daily gaming with poorer concentration at school, suggesting the effect is measurable rather than merely anecdotal. Even moderate but prolonged play takes a physical toll, contributing to eye strain, disrupted sleep and poor posture that can be difficult to correct once established."},{"en":"difficult to correct once established","context":"The most serious concern is the level of violence in many games, which feature soldiers, criminals and killers as central, often glamorised, characters; a child who spends hours controlling such figures can come to see aggression as normal, or even admirable. A related danger is obsessive use, where some children prefer a screen to real friendships, gradually becoming isolated and socially awkward as a result; surveys in several countries have linked more than three hours of daily gaming with poorer concentration at school, suggesting the effect is measurable rather than merely anecdotal. Even moderate but prolonged play takes a physical toll, contributing to eye strain, disrupted sleep and poor posture that can be difficult to correct once established."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#048","summary":"People today are worried about young children using video games."},{"id":"writing-task-2-144","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Does growing up poor build resilience?","prompt":"Children who grow up in families short of money are better prepared to deal with the problems of adult life than children brought up by wealthy parents. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that children who grow up without money handle adult difficulties better than those raised in wealthy families. I partly agree, since financial hardship can teach resilience, but I believe the claim is exaggerated and overlooks the serious disadvantages of poverty.\n\nThere is something in the argument. Children in poorer households usually take on responsibility earlier, whether by working part-time, caring for younger siblings or managing limited resources, and they learn that difficulties must be solved rather than paid away. They may also develop a stronger motivation to succeed, since nothing is guaranteed and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no family safety net exists behind them</strong>. Conversely, a young adult who has never been refused anything may be poorly equipped for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the first serious setback</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">this romanticises poverty considerably</strong>. Growing up short of money frequently means poor nutrition, overcrowded housing, weaker schools and parents too exhausted by work to provide attention. Persistent financial stress in childhood is associated with anxiety and lower educational attainment, and these are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">handicaps rather than preparation</strong>. Wealthy families, meanwhile, supply advantages that are genuinely useful in adult life: good health, strong schooling and professional contacts.\n\nIn my view what actually builds resilience is being given responsibility and being allowed to fail, and this depends on parenting rather than on income. A wealthy child who is expected to work for what they receive develops <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exactly the same capacity</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, hardship can produce resourcefulness, but I disagree that poverty is a good preparation for adult life. Its disadvantages generally outweigh whatever character it builds.","phrases":[{"en":"no family safety net exists behind them","context":"There is something in the argument. Children in poorer households usually take on responsibility earlier, whether by working part-time, caring for younger siblings or managing limited resources, and they learn that difficulties must be solved rather than paid away. They may also develop a stronger motivation to succeed, since nothing is guaranteed and no family safety net exists behind them. Conversely, a young adult who has never been refused anything may be poorly equipped for the first serious setback."},{"en":"the first serious setback","context":"There is something in the argument. Children in poorer households usually take on responsibility earlier, whether by working part-time, caring for younger siblings or managing limited resources, and they learn that difficulties must be solved rather than paid away. They may also develop a stronger motivation to succeed, since nothing is guaranteed and no family safety net exists behind them. Conversely, a young adult who has never been refused anything may be poorly equipped for the first serious setback."},{"en":"this romanticises poverty considerably","context":"Nevertheless, this romanticises poverty considerably. Growing up short of money frequently means poor nutrition, overcrowded housing, weaker schools and parents too exhausted by work to provide attention. Persistent financial stress in childhood is associated with anxiety and lower educational attainment, and these are handicaps rather than preparation. Wealthy families, meanwhile, supply advantages that are genuinely useful in adult life: good health, strong schooling and professional contacts."},{"en":"handicaps rather than preparation","context":"Nevertheless, this romanticises poverty considerably. Growing up short of money frequently means poor nutrition, overcrowded housing, weaker schools and parents too exhausted by work to provide attention. Persistent financial stress in childhood is associated with anxiety and lower educational attainment, and these are handicaps rather than preparation. Wealthy families, meanwhile, supply advantages that are genuinely useful in adult life: good health, strong schooling and professional contacts."},{"en":"exactly the same capacity","context":"In my view what actually builds resilience is being given responsibility and being allowed to fail, and this depends on parenting rather than on income. A wealthy child who is expected to work for what they receive develops exactly the same capacity."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#049","summary":"Does growing up poor build resilience?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-149","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Nature or nurture?","prompt":"Opinions differ as to whether nature or nurture plays the more important role in the development of personality. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People disagree about whether personality is determined mainly by inherited characteristics or by upbringing and environment. In my opinion both matter, but the evidence suggests that environment has the greater influence over how a person actually behaves.\n\nThe argument for nature is not weak. Studies of identical twins raised in separate families have repeatedly found striking similarities in temperament, and any parent with more than one child will confirm that babies differ from birth in how anxious, sociable or persistent they are. Nobody chooses to be naturally shy or naturally energetic, and these tendencies <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remain recognisable throughout life</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, inherited temperament <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sets a range rather than a fixed outcome</strong>. A naturally shy child who is encouraged to speak in class and never mocked for hesitating usually becomes a reserved but confident adult; the same child, criticised for shyness, may become genuinely withdrawn. Language, values, ambition and attitudes to work are also learned almost entirely from family and society, which is why children raised in different countries develop such different assumptions about what is normal.\n\nThe most convincing explanation is that the two interact continuously rather than competing. Inherited characteristics influence how a child is treated, since an outgoing baby receives more attention and a difficult one receives more correction, and that treatment then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reinforces the original tendency</strong>. Separating the two influences is therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost impossible in practice</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, personality begins with inherited temperament, but I believe upbringing determines far more of the final result. This matters practically, because it means that good parenting and good schooling can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely change how a child&#x27;s life turns out</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"remain recognisable throughout life","context":"The argument for nature is not weak. Studies of identical twins raised in separate families have repeatedly found striking similarities in temperament, and any parent with more than one child will confirm that babies differ from birth in how anxious, sociable or persistent they are. Nobody chooses to be naturally shy or naturally energetic, and these tendencies remain recognisable throughout life."},{"en":"sets a range rather than a fixed outcome","context":"Nevertheless, inherited temperament sets a range rather than a fixed outcome. A naturally shy child who is encouraged to speak in class and never mocked for hesitating usually becomes a reserved but confident adult; the same child, criticised for shyness, may become genuinely withdrawn. Language, values, ambition and attitudes to work are also learned almost entirely from family and society, which is why children raised in different countries develop such different assumptions about what is normal."},{"en":"reinforces the original tendency","context":"The most convincing explanation is that the two interact continuously rather than competing. Inherited characteristics influence how a child is treated, since an outgoing baby receives more attention and a difficult one receives more correction, and that treatment then reinforces the original tendency. Separating the two influences is therefore almost impossible in practice."},{"en":"almost impossible in practice","context":"The most convincing explanation is that the two interact continuously rather than competing. Inherited characteristics influence how a child is treated, since an outgoing baby receives more attention and a difficult one receives more correction, and that treatment then reinforces the original tendency. Separating the two influences is therefore almost impossible in practice."},{"en":"genuinely change how a child's life turns out","context":"In conclusion, personality begins with inherited temperament, but I believe upbringing determines far more of the final result. This matters practically, because it means that good parenting and good schooling can genuinely change how a child's life turns out."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#050","summary":"Nature or nurture?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-154","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Should children learn to compete or to cooperate?","prompt":"Some people think that children should learn to compete, but others think that they should be taught to cooperate with others. What's your opinion?","answer":"Competition can motivate children, while cooperation teaches them to work with others. In my view, schools should develop both qualities, but cooperation deserves greater emphasis because most adult achievements depend on shared effort rather than individual victory.\n\nHealthy competition can encourage pupils to practise, set goals and measure progress. A race, debate or academic challenge may show a child what focused effort can achieve. Problems arise when success is defined only as defeating classmates. Constant ranking can make weaker pupils withdraw, encourage cheating and create the belief that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">another person&#x27;s progress is a threat</strong>.\n\nCooperative tasks develop a broader set of skills. When pupils plan a presentation or solve a practical problem in a group, they must listen, explain ideas, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">divide responsibilities and manage disagreement</strong>. These abilities are essential in universities and workplaces. Cooperation also allows children with different strengths to contribute, which can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn individual ability into a stronger collective result</strong>.\n\nTeachers should also assess both the final result and each pupil&#x27;s contribution. Otherwise, one confident child may control the task while quieter members receive little practice. Clear roles, peer feedback and rotating leadership make cooperation fairer and more educational. It also prevents group work from becoming <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reward only for confident speakers</strong>.\n\nSchools do not need to remove competition entirely. They can compare teams, reward personal improvement and ensure that every child has a meaningful role. In conclusion, competition is useful when it remains fair and limited, but cooperation should form the foundation of classroom life. It prepares children to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">succeed without treating others as obstacles</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"another person's progress is a threat","context":"Healthy competition can encourage pupils to practise, set goals and measure progress. A race, debate or academic challenge may show a child what focused effort can achieve. Problems arise when success is defined only as defeating classmates. Constant ranking can make weaker pupils withdraw, encourage cheating and create the belief that another person's progress is a threat."},{"en":"divide responsibilities and manage disagreement","context":"Cooperative tasks develop a broader set of skills. When pupils plan a presentation or solve a practical problem in a group, they must listen, explain ideas, divide responsibilities and manage disagreement. These abilities are essential in universities and workplaces. Cooperation also allows children with different strengths to contribute, which can turn individual ability into a stronger collective result."},{"en":"turn individual ability into a stronger collective result","context":"Cooperative tasks develop a broader set of skills. When pupils plan a presentation or solve a practical problem in a group, they must listen, explain ideas, divide responsibilities and manage disagreement. These abilities are essential in universities and workplaces. Cooperation also allows children with different strengths to contribute, which can turn individual ability into a stronger collective result."},{"en":"a reward only for confident speakers","context":"Teachers should also assess both the final result and each pupil's contribution. Otherwise, one confident child may control the task while quieter members receive little practice. Clear roles, peer feedback and rotating leadership make cooperation fairer and more educational. It also prevents group work from becoming a reward only for confident speakers."},{"en":"succeed without treating others as obstacles","context":"Schools do not need to remove competition entirely. They can compare teams, reward personal improvement and ensure that every child has a meaningful role. In conclusion, competition is useful when it remains fair and limited, but cooperation should form the foundation of classroom life. It prepares children to succeed without treating others as obstacles."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#051","summary":"People think that children should learn to compete, but others think that…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2199","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Letting children make mistakes","prompt":"Some people believe that children should have the freedom to make mistakes, while others argue that adults should prevent them from doing so. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People disagree about how far adults should intervene when a child is about to do something unwise. Some believe that mistakes are a necessary part of growing up, while others think that adults have a duty to prevent them. In my opinion, both positions are partly right, and the answer depends on how serious the mistake would be.\n\nThose who defend the freedom to make mistakes argue that experience teaches more effectively than instruction. A child who is repeatedly told what to do learns to obey, but a child who fails and then works out why has learned something permanent. Making mistakes also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">builds resilience</strong>. Young people who have never been allowed to fail often struggle at university or in their first job, simply because they have no experience of dealing with disappointment.\n\nOn the other hand, adults have far more knowledge than children and can see consequences that a young person cannot. A ten-year-old cannot reasonably be expected to judge the risk of crossing a busy road, meeting a stranger online or experimenting with drugs. In these situations, allowing a child to &quot;learn from experience&quot; is not education but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">negligence</strong>.\n\nIn my view, the sensible approach is to distinguish between mistakes that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recoverable</strong> and those that are not. Children should be free to choose the wrong subject, spend their pocket money badly or lose a match through poor tactics, because the cost is low and the lesson is valuable. Adults should intervene firmly whenever a decision could damage a child&#x27;s health, safety or future.\n\nIn conclusion, mistakes are an essential part of learning, but adults must still protect children from errors that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be undone</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"builds resilience","context":"Those who defend the freedom to make mistakes argue that experience teaches more effectively than instruction. A child who is repeatedly told what to do learns to obey, but a child who fails and then works out why has learned something permanent. Making mistakes also builds resilience. Young people who have never been allowed to fail often struggle at university or in their first job, simply because they have no experience of dealing with disappointment."},{"en":"negligence","context":"On the other hand, adults have far more knowledge than children and can see consequences that a young person cannot. A ten-year-old cannot reasonably be expected to judge the risk of crossing a busy road, meeting a stranger online or experimenting with drugs. In these situations, allowing a child to \"learn from experience\" is not education but negligence."},{"en":"recoverable","context":"In my view, the sensible approach is to distinguish between mistakes that are recoverable and those that are not. Children should be free to choose the wrong subject, spend their pocket money badly or lose a match through poor tactics, because the cost is low and the lesson is valuable. Adults should intervene firmly whenever a decision could damage a child's health, safety or future."},{"en":"cannot be undone","context":"In conclusion, mistakes are an essential part of learning, but adults must still protect children from errors that cannot be undone."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#052","summary":"Letting children make mistakes"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2231","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Family or the world outside?","prompt":"Some people think that family has the most powerful influence on a child's development, while others believe that other factors such as friends, television and music have a stronger effect. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Children are shaped both by the household they grow up in and by the friends, screens and music that surround them. In my view the family exerts the deeper influence, but its power is concentrated in the early years and declines sharply afterwards.\n\nThe argument for the family rests on timing. Before a child meets a single classmate, parents have already established how language is used at home, whether reading is normal, how conflict is handled and whether adults can be trusted. These patterns are laid down when the brain is most receptive, and they become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the standard against which everything later is measured</strong>. A child who has watched a parent apologise properly carries that model into every relationship, largely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without ever noticing it</strong>.\n\nThe opposing view has force during adolescence. Teenagers spend most of their waking hours among people their own age, and the desire not to be excluded is powerful enough to change how they dress, speak and study. Whether a fifteen-year-old&#x27;s friends treat schoolwork as worth doing predicts their results more reliably than almost anything a parent says. Music and screens matter here too, because they supply the images of adulthood that young people measure themselves against.\n\nMy own position is that these influences are not equal partners. Parents largely determine which friends a child meets, through where the family lives and which school it chooses, so much of the peer effect is itself <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an indirect family decision</strong>. Children with a secure home also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">resist peer pressure better</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, outside influences dominate the teenage years and should not be dismissed. The family nonetheless matters more, because it builds the foundation on which those later influences <strong class=\"key-phrase\">either take hold or do not</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the standard against which everything later is measured","context":"The argument for the family rests on timing. Before a child meets a single classmate, parents have already established how language is used at home, whether reading is normal, how conflict is handled and whether adults can be trusted. These patterns are laid down when the brain is most receptive, and they become the standard against which everything later is measured. A child who has watched a parent apologise properly carries that model into every relationship, largely without ever noticing it."},{"en":"without ever noticing it","context":"The argument for the family rests on timing. Before a child meets a single classmate, parents have already established how language is used at home, whether reading is normal, how conflict is handled and whether adults can be trusted. These patterns are laid down when the brain is most receptive, and they become the standard against which everything later is measured. A child who has watched a parent apologise properly carries that model into every relationship, largely without ever noticing it."},{"en":"an indirect family decision","context":"My own position is that these influences are not equal partners. Parents largely determine which friends a child meets, through where the family lives and which school it chooses, so much of the peer effect is itself an indirect family decision. Children with a secure home also resist peer pressure better."},{"en":"resist peer pressure better","context":"My own position is that these influences are not equal partners. Parents largely determine which friends a child meets, through where the family lives and which school it chooses, so much of the peer effect is itself an indirect family decision. Children with a secure home also resist peer pressure better."},{"en":"either take hold or do not","context":"In conclusion, outside influences dominate the teenage years and should not be dismissed. The family nonetheless matters more, because it builds the foundation on which those later influences either take hold or do not."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#053","summary":"Family influence compared with influences from outside the home"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2248","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Pressure to succeed","prompt":"Parents are putting a lot of pressure on their children to succeed. What are the reasons for this? Is it a positive or negative development?","answer":"Children in many countries now face expectations that would have been unusual a generation ago, with private tutoring beginning in primary school. The reasons are largely economic, and although the intention is protective, I consider the trend negative.\n\nThe main cause is competition for a shrinking number of secure positions. Where a degree from a particular university determines access to stable work, parents conclude that a child who is not ahead at fifteen has already lost, and this belief spreads: once other families hire tutors, refusing to do so feels like <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accepting a disadvantage on a child&#x27;s behalf</strong>. Smaller families reinforce the pattern, since parents with one child concentrate on that child all the ambition previously spread across four.\n\nSome pressure is undoubtedly useful. Children rarely choose difficult work unprompted, and adults who were expected to try often say later that they are grateful. High expectations communicate belief, and a child who is asked for nothing may reasonably conclude that little is thought of them.\n\nThe harm comes from the intensity now common. Adolescent anxiety and sleep deprivation have risen alongside academic pressure, and children who succeed under it frequently arrive at university unable to work without instruction, having never chosen anything themselves. There is a further cost that is easy to miss: a child pushed towards medicine or law may abandon the subject they were actually good at, so the family <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trades genuine talent for a safer label</strong>. Praise conditional on results also teaches that affection <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has to be earned</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the pressure comes from real competition for secure work, and encouragement is valuable. The current intensity is nonetheless a negative development, because it produces anxious young adults who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">achieve well without knowing what they want</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"accepting a disadvantage on a child's behalf","context":"The main cause is competition for a shrinking number of secure positions. Where a degree from a particular university determines access to stable work, parents conclude that a child who is not ahead at fifteen has already lost, and this belief spreads: once other families hire tutors, refusing to do so feels like accepting a disadvantage on a child's behalf. Smaller families reinforce the pattern, since parents with one child concentrate on that child all the ambition previously spread across four."},{"en":"trades genuine talent for a safer label","context":"The harm comes from the intensity now common. Adolescent anxiety and sleep deprivation have risen alongside academic pressure, and children who succeed under it frequently arrive at university unable to work without instruction, having never chosen anything themselves. There is a further cost that is easy to miss: a child pushed towards medicine or law may abandon the subject they were actually good at, so the family trades genuine talent for a safer label. Praise conditional on results also teaches that affection has to be earned."},{"en":"has to be earned","context":"The harm comes from the intensity now common. Adolescent anxiety and sleep deprivation have risen alongside academic pressure, and children who succeed under it frequently arrive at university unable to work without instruction, having never chosen anything themselves. There is a further cost that is easy to miss: a child pushed towards medicine or law may abandon the subject they were actually good at, so the family trades genuine talent for a safer label. Praise conditional on results also teaches that affection has to be earned."},{"en":"achieve well without knowing what they want","context":"In conclusion, the pressure comes from real competition for secure work, and encouragement is valuable. The current intensity is nonetheless a negative development, because it produces anxious young adults who achieve well without knowing what they want."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#054","summary":"Why parents pressure their children to succeed"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2249","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Is television bad for children?","prompt":"Some people think watching TV is bad for children while others think that watching TV has more beneficial effects on children. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Few household habits divide parents as sharply as television. Some regard it as damaging while others see it as a source of learning, and my own view is that the argument is really about quantity and content rather than about the screen itself.\n\nThe case against television rests on what it replaces. Hours spent watching are hours not spent reading, playing outside or talking to adults, and language develops through conversation, which a broadcast cannot supply because it never responds to the child. Heavy viewing is associated with poorer sleep and lower fitness, and programmes made for adults expose children to violence and to advertising for food they should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be eating less of</strong>.\n\nThose who defend television point to what good programmes achieve. Well-designed children&#x27;s series have measurably improved vocabulary and number skills, particularly among children whose parents read to them little. Documentaries also show things a child could never otherwise see, from the deep ocean to the inside of a volcano, and shared viewing gives a family something to talk about together.\n\nMy position is that both sides describe real effects of different practices. Two hours of unsupervised viewing is not the same activity as thirty minutes of a nature programme watched with a parent who asks questions afterwards. The evidence on harm concerns quantity and unsuitable content, while the evidence on benefit concerns careful selection, so the sensible response is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">limit the hours and choose the programmes</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, television is neither harmful nor beneficial in itself. What matters is how much a child watches, what they watch and whether an adult watches with them, which makes this a question of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">parental judgement rather than prohibition</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"be eating less of","context":"The case against television rests on what it replaces. Hours spent watching are hours not spent reading, playing outside or talking to adults, and language develops through conversation, which a broadcast cannot supply because it never responds to the child. Heavy viewing is associated with poorer sleep and lower fitness, and programmes made for adults expose children to violence and to advertising for food they should be eating less of."},{"en":"limit the hours and choose the programmes","context":"My position is that both sides describe real effects of different practices. Two hours of unsupervised viewing is not the same activity as thirty minutes of a nature programme watched with a parent who asks questions afterwards. The evidence on harm concerns quantity and unsuitable content, while the evidence on benefit concerns careful selection, so the sensible response is to limit the hours and choose the programmes."},{"en":"parental judgement rather than prohibition","context":"In conclusion, television is neither harmful nor beneficial in itself. What matters is how much a child watches, what they watch and whether an adult watches with them, which makes this a question of parental judgement rather than prohibition."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#055","summary":"Harmful and beneficial effects of television on children"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2259","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Starting a family later","prompt":"In some countries today, many people are having their first child at an older age. What are the reasons for this trend? Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"In many wealthy countries the average age at which women have a first child has risen by several years within a generation. The causes are mainly economic and educational, and in my view the advantages of the trend slightly outweigh its costs.\n\nThe principal reason is that the years once used for starting a family are now spent in education and early careers. A woman completing a degree and establishing herself professionally is unlikely to be ready for a child before her late twenties, and taking leave at the point when a career is being built carries a lasting penalty in pay. Housing reinforces this: where deposits require years of saving, couples <strong class=\"key-phrase\">postpone children until they have space for them</strong>. Reliable contraception has made this delay a genuine choice rather than an accident.\n\nThe advantages are real. Older parents are typically more financially secure, so a child grows up with fewer of the pressures that money problems create, and parents who have already travelled and worked are less likely to feel that a child <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cost them the life they wanted</strong>. Greater maturity also shows in parenting, and children of older parents perform slightly better at school on average.\n\nThe disadvantages are chiefly biological. Fertility declines with age, so some couples who delay discover that conceiving is difficult and face expensive treatment, and the risk of certain complications rises. Older parents also have less energy for young children and are more likely to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caring for their own parents</strong> at the same time.\n\nIn conclusion, the trend is driven by longer education and the cost of housing. On balance the benefits are greater, provided couples understand that delaying <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrows the window rather than closing it later</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"postpone children until they have space for them","context":"The principal reason is that the years once used for starting a family are now spent in education and early careers. A woman completing a degree and establishing herself professionally is unlikely to be ready for a child before her late twenties, and taking leave at the point when a career is being built carries a lasting penalty in pay. Housing reinforces this: where deposits require years of saving, couples postpone children until they have space for them. Reliable contraception has made this delay a genuine choice rather than an accident."},{"en":"cost them the life they wanted","context":"The advantages are real. Older parents are typically more financially secure, so a child grows up with fewer of the pressures that money problems create, and parents who have already travelled and worked are less likely to feel that a child cost them the life they wanted. Greater maturity also shows in parenting, and children of older parents perform slightly better at school on average."},{"en":"caring for their own parents","context":"The disadvantages are chiefly biological. Fertility declines with age, so some couples who delay discover that conceiving is difficult and face expensive treatment, and the risk of certain complications rises. Older parents also have less energy for young children and are more likely to be caring for their own parents at the same time."},{"en":"narrows the window rather than closing it later","context":"In conclusion, the trend is driven by longer education and the cost of housing. On balance the benefits are greater, provided couples understand that delaying narrows the window rather than closing it later."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Advantages outweigh disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#056","summary":"Why people are having their first child at an older age"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2261","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"The effects of peer pressure","prompt":"Young people are often influenced in their behaviour and attitudes by others of the same age. This is called peer pressure. Do the disadvantages of peer pressure outweigh the advantages?","answer":"Young people are strongly affected by the expectations of others their own age, and this influence is usually described as a danger. In my view the disadvantages do outweigh the benefits during adolescence, though the effect is not as one-sided as it is often presented.\n\nThe harm is easiest to see in behaviour that carries real risk. Smoking, drinking and dangerous driving almost always begin in a group rather than alone, because a teenager who refuses <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fears exclusion more than the risk itself</strong>, and the part of the brain that weighs consequences is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">still developing at that age</strong>. The pressure also narrows what young people allow themselves to be: a boy who enjoys music or a girl who is good at mathematics may abandon the subject rather than be seen as different, and the cost of that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is paid for years afterwards</strong>.\n\nThe positive side is genuine but weaker. The same mechanism works in the opposite direction when the group values effort: a student whose friends intend to go to university is far more likely to apply, and teams and clubs use exactly this force to build commitment. Peers also teach social skills that adults cannot, since learning to negotiate and apologise happens among equals rather than under adult supervision.\n\nThe imbalance lies in what each side risks. A beneficial influence produces better habits, while a harmful one can produce an addiction, a criminal record or a fatal accident, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the consequences are not symmetrical</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, peer pressure can encourage effort and teaches young people how to live among others. Its drawbacks are nonetheless greater, because the damage it causes is often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">difficult to undo later</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"fears exclusion more than the risk itself","context":"The harm is easiest to see in behaviour that carries real risk. Smoking, drinking and dangerous driving almost always begin in a group rather than alone, because a teenager who refuses fears exclusion more than the risk itself, and the part of the brain that weighs consequences is still developing at that age. The pressure also narrows what young people allow themselves to be: a boy who enjoys music or a girl who is good at mathematics may abandon the subject rather than be seen as different, and the cost of that is paid for years afterwards."},{"en":"still developing at that age","context":"The harm is easiest to see in behaviour that carries real risk. Smoking, drinking and dangerous driving almost always begin in a group rather than alone, because a teenager who refuses fears exclusion more than the risk itself, and the part of the brain that weighs consequences is still developing at that age. The pressure also narrows what young people allow themselves to be: a boy who enjoys music or a girl who is good at mathematics may abandon the subject rather than be seen as different, and the cost of that is paid for years afterwards."},{"en":"is paid for years afterwards","context":"The harm is easiest to see in behaviour that carries real risk. Smoking, drinking and dangerous driving almost always begin in a group rather than alone, because a teenager who refuses fears exclusion more than the risk itself, and the part of the brain that weighs consequences is still developing at that age. The pressure also narrows what young people allow themselves to be: a boy who enjoys music or a girl who is good at mathematics may abandon the subject rather than be seen as different, and the cost of that is paid for years afterwards."},{"en":"the consequences are not symmetrical","context":"The imbalance lies in what each side risks. A beneficial influence produces better habits, while a harmful one can produce an addiction, a criminal record or a fatal accident, so the consequences are not symmetrical."},{"en":"difficult to undo later","context":"In conclusion, peer pressure can encourage effort and teaches young people how to live among others. Its drawbacks are nonetheless greater, because the damage it causes is often difficult to undo later."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#057","summary":"Whether the drawbacks of peer pressure outweigh its benefits"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2275","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Limiting screens and encouraging books","prompt":"Some people say that parents should place restrictions on the hours their children spend watching television and playing computer games, and should encourage them to spend this time reading books instead. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Most children would happily spend every free hour in front of a screen, and many now do. I agree that parents should set limits, though I think the second half of the suggestion, swapping those hours directly for books, works only if it is handled with some care.\n\nLimits are justified because screen time crowds out everything else. A child absorbed in a game is not sleeping, moving, talking to anyone or being bored, and boredom is where children invent things. Games and streaming services are also built to hold attention: episodes play automatically and rewards arrive on a schedule, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stopping requires more willpower than a child has</strong>. Setting an hour is not a punishment but a substitute for self-control the child has not yet developed.\n\nReading is a reasonable thing to steer them towards. It builds vocabulary and concentration in a way no other activity matches, and a child who reads fluently by ten finds every school subject easier. Sitting with a difficult page also teaches patience, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the reward comes slowly</strong> rather than every few seconds.\n\nThe plan fails, however, when reading is imposed as the price of the screen being switched off. Forced reading teaches children that books are a chore, which is the opposite of the intention. What works is making books available and attractive, letting children choose whatever they enjoy, comics included, and having parents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">read visibly themselves</strong>. Nor should the freed hours all go to reading; sport and unstructured play deserve a share.\n\nIn conclusion, parents are right to cap the hours their children spend on screens, since those devices are designed to be hard to put down. Books should be offered generously rather than enforced, because a child who is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made to read stops wanting to</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"stopping requires more willpower than a child has","context":"Limits are justified because screen time crowds out everything else. A child absorbed in a game is not sleeping, moving, talking to anyone or being bored, and boredom is where children invent things. Games and streaming services are also built to hold attention: episodes play automatically and rewards arrive on a schedule, so stopping requires more willpower than a child has. Setting an hour is not a punishment but a substitute for self-control the child has not yet developed."},{"en":"the reward comes slowly","context":"Reading is a reasonable thing to steer them towards. It builds vocabulary and concentration in a way no other activity matches, and a child who reads fluently by ten finds every school subject easier. Sitting with a difficult page also teaches patience, because the reward comes slowly rather than every few seconds."},{"en":"read visibly themselves","context":"The plan fails, however, when reading is imposed as the price of the screen being switched off. Forced reading teaches children that books are a chore, which is the opposite of the intention. What works is making books available and attractive, letting children choose whatever they enjoy, comics included, and having parents read visibly themselves. Nor should the freed hours all go to reading; sport and unstructured play deserve a share."},{"en":"made to read stops wanting to","context":"In conclusion, parents are right to cap the hours their children spend on screens, since those devices are designed to be hard to put down. Books should be offered generously rather than enforced, because a child who is made to read stops wanting to."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#058","summary":"Whether parents should restrict screen time and push children towards reading"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2289","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"What children watch, or how long?","prompt":"Some people believe that what children watch on television influences their behaviour. Others say that the amount of time spent watching television is what matters. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Parents worried about television face two different warnings: one about the programmes and one about the hours. Both concerns have evidence behind them, and my own view is that duration is the greater problem for most children.\n\nThe argument about content is well established. Young children imitate what they see, and studies going back decades show that those exposed to repeated aggression on screen behave more aggressively soon afterwards, particularly when the violence is rewarded. Content also teaches expectations, since programmes carry assumptions about how families work, what bodies should look like and what wealth means, and a child absorbs these long before they can question them. Advertising works on the same principle, which is why several countries restrict it during children&#x27;s programmes.\n\nThe argument about time addresses something more basic. An hour spent watching is an hour not spent running, reading, talking or inventing a game, and those activities build language, coordination and social skill during years when the effects are lasting. Heavy viewing is associated with poorer sleep, lower reading ability and weight problems regardless of what was on the screen, so even excellent programmes displace something the child needs more. Long passive viewing also trains a child to expect entertainment to arrive without effort.\n\nMy own position is that content matters most for the youngest children and time matters most overall. A four-year-old cannot distinguish a cartoon from instruction, so what is shown is critical, but by school age children understand that television is invented, while the hours continue to take the same amount from their day.\n\nIn conclusion, both what children watch and how long they watch shape their behaviour, and parents should attend to each. Limiting the hours is the more useful rule, because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protects the sleep, activity and conversation</strong> that everything else depends on.","phrases":[{"en":"protects the sleep, activity and conversation","context":"In conclusion, both what children watch and how long they watch shape their behaviour, and parents should attend to each. Limiting the hours is the more useful rule, because it protects the sleep, activity and conversation that everything else depends on."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#059","summary":"Whether content or viewing time has the greater effect on children's behaviour"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2342","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Giving children more freedom","prompt":"In many parts of the world, children are given more freedom than in the past. Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"Children today are consulted about decisions their grandparents would simply have been told about, and physical punishment has largely disappeared. I regard the change as positive overall, though one part of it has gone wrong.\n\nThe gains are considerable. Children who are allowed to express opinions and disagree respectfully grow into adults who can argue, negotiate and resist pressure, which is exactly what modern work and citizenship demand. Freedom to choose subjects, hobbies and friends lets a child discover ability that a strict family would never have noticed. The decline of harsh discipline matters too, since fear teaches obedience while a child is watched and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">teaches nothing about why a rule exists</strong>. Homes are also warmer places, and children who feel able to tell their parents about a problem are far safer than those who cannot.\n\nThere are genuine costs. Freedom without limits leaves children without the structure they need, and constant negotiation over bedtimes and meals exhausts everyone. Some parents have confused respect with equality, so children make decisions they are not equipped to make, and a child who has never been refused anything <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meets adult life without any tolerance for disappointment</strong>.\n\nThe stranger contradiction is that freedom has increased in one direction and collapsed in another. Children today have more say at home but far less liberty outside it: fewer walk to school or play unsupervised than at any point in the past century. That is the loss that matters, because independence is learned by being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trusted to manage something without an adult present</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the shift towards listening to children and away from harsh discipline is clearly positive. It works only where freedom comes with firm limits, and it remains incomplete while children are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">given a voice indoors and no independence outside</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"teaches nothing about why a rule exists","context":"The gains are considerable. Children who are allowed to express opinions and disagree respectfully grow into adults who can argue, negotiate and resist pressure, which is exactly what modern work and citizenship demand. Freedom to choose subjects, hobbies and friends lets a child discover ability that a strict family would never have noticed. The decline of harsh discipline matters too, since fear teaches obedience while a child is watched and teaches nothing about why a rule exists. Homes are also warmer places, and children who feel able to tell their parents about a problem are far safer than those who cannot."},{"en":"meets adult life without any tolerance for disappointment","context":"There are genuine costs. Freedom without limits leaves children without the structure they need, and constant negotiation over bedtimes and meals exhausts everyone. Some parents have confused respect with equality, so children make decisions they are not equipped to make, and a child who has never been refused anything meets adult life without any tolerance for disappointment."},{"en":"trusted to manage something without an adult present","context":"The stranger contradiction is that freedom has increased in one direction and collapsed in another. Children today have more say at home but far less liberty outside it: fewer walk to school or play unsupervised than at any point in the past century. That is the loss that matters, because independence is learned by being trusted to manage something without an adult present."},{"en":"given a voice indoors and no independence outside","context":"In conclusion, the shift towards listening to children and away from harsh discipline is clearly positive. It works only where freedom comes with firm limits, and it remains incomplete while children are given a voice indoors and no independence outside."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#060","summary":"Whether the greater freedom children have today is a good thing"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2351","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Families that move abroad for work","prompt":"Nowadays, many parents work abroad and take their families with them. Do the benefits of this trend for families outweigh the drawbacks?","answer":"Skilled workers now move across borders routinely, and many take partners and children with them rather than leaving them behind. On balance I believe the benefits outweigh the drawbacks, largely because the alternative is usually separation.\n\nThe financial gain is the reason such moves are made. Higher salaries abroad lift a family&#x27;s standard of living immediately and often fund education or property at home. The professional benefit matters too, since experience in a larger market advances a career in a way that years of local work would not.\n\nThe advantages for children may be greater still. They grow up bilingual without effort, attend schools that are frequently better resourced, and learn early that people can live differently without either side being wrong, which is an understanding that is difficult to teach any other way. Access to healthcare and safety can also be decisive for a family leaving a country in difficulty.\n\nThe drawbacks are real. Children lose grandparents, cousins and the friendships they had built, and repeated moves leave some with no strong attachment anywhere. Language and cultural gaps can isolate them in the first years, and a gap frequently opens between children who adapt quickly and parents who do not. The accompanying partner often suffers most, giving up a career for a visa that does not permit work, which strains the marriage. Elderly relatives left behind are a permanent worry. These costs are heavy but largely manageable, and they compare with a worse alternative: the parent who works abroad alone. Families divided for years suffer far more, since children grow up knowing a parent through a screen.\n\nIn conclusion, moving abroad together costs a family its roots and often one partner&#x27;s career. Higher income, better schooling and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeping the family in one place</strong> outweigh those losses, particularly when the alternative is long separation.","phrases":[{"en":"keeping the family in one place","context":"In conclusion, moving abroad together costs a family its roots and often one partner's career. Higher income, better schooling and keeping the family in one place outweigh those losses, particularly when the alternative is long separation."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#061","summary":"Whether working overseas with the family brings more benefits than drawbacks"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2369","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"The end of the extended family","prompt":"In many nations, people in huge cities live alone or in small family units instead of in large, extended family groups. Is this a positive or negative trend?","answer":"Households in large cities have shrunk from several generations to two people or one. The change brings genuine freedom, but I regard it as negative overall because of what it removes from the young and the old at the same time.\n\nThe advantages are easy to identify. Living apart from an extended family allows people to choose their work, their partner and their beliefs without constant supervision, and it has freed women in particular from obligations that were assumed rather than chosen. Small households reduce friction, since disagreements between generations about money, religion and child-rearing are among the commonest sources of unhappiness. Practically, small units also move easily, which matters where work requires it, and a couple can accept a job in another city without negotiating with a dozen relatives.\n\nThe losses are heavier than they first appear. Extended families provided childcare, so young parents now pay for what was once shared, and the cost keeps many from having children at all. They also cared for the elderly, and their absence has produced widespread loneliness among older people and a growing bill for residential care that states struggle to meet. Children lose the daily presence of grandparents, from whom they learn a longer view of life. A small household is fragile too, because illness, redundancy or divorce leaves nobody to absorb the shock the way a larger group could.\n\nThere is a wider social effect. Cities of solitary households consume more housing, energy and goods per person, and loneliness has become common enough among young adults to appear in health statistics.\n\nIn conclusion, smaller households have given people freedom that most would not surrender. The trend is nonetheless negative, because it has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transferred care from families to money</strong>, and many households cannot afford the exchange.","phrases":[{"en":"transferred care from families to money","context":"In conclusion, smaller households have given people freedom that most would not surrender. The trend is nonetheless negative, because it has transferred care from families to money, and many households cannot afford the exchange."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#062","summary":"Whether living alone or in small units rather than extended families is good"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2377","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Children & Family","title":"Compulsory parenting classes","prompt":"Some people believe that the best way to ensure a happy family life for children is to make mothers and fathers take part in parenting courses every year. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Raising a child is the most demanding task most adults undertake, and almost nobody receives any training for it. I agree that parenting courses help, but I disagree that compulsory annual attendance is the best way to secure a happy family life.\n\nGood courses do work. Parents learn that a two-year-old&#x27;s tantrums are normal development rather than defiance, which alone prevents a great deal of anger, and they are given alternatives to shouting and hitting that actually change a child&#x27;s behaviour rather than only stopping it. Programmes for parents of difficult children have measurably reduced conflict, and courses also connect isolated parents with others facing the same week, which is often the greater benefit.\n\nCompulsion is the problem. Attendance is not the same as learning, and adults required to sit in a room every year will do so resentfully while ignoring the content, particularly if they feel their competence is being questioned. The practical obstacles are severe: parents working shifts or two jobs cannot attend, and enforcing it would need childcare, time off and an inspectorate that no country would fund. Annual repetition also treats parenting as a licence to be renewed rather than a skill, and a family in real difficulty needs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained help rather than a yearly session</strong>.\n\nThere is a deeper objection. Most unhappiness in families comes from poverty, overcrowding, insecure work and untreated illness, not from ignorance of technique. A parent working two jobs knows perfectly well that they should read to their child; what they lack is the hour. Courses cannot teach a household out of the conditions it lives in.\n\nIn conclusion, parenting education is valuable and should be free, welcoming and widely available, particularly around a first birth. Making it compulsory every year would produce attendance rather than better parenting, and the money would achieve more spent on the pressures families are under.","phrases":[{"en":"sustained help rather than a yearly session","context":"Compulsion is the problem. Attendance is not the same as learning, and adults required to sit in a room every year will do so resentfully while ignoring the content, particularly if they feel their competence is being questioned. The practical obstacles are severe: parents working shifts or two jobs cannot attend, and enforcing it would need childcare, time off and an inspectorate that no country would fund. Annual repetition also treats parenting as a licence to be renewed rather than a skill, and a family in real difficulty needs sustained help rather than a yearly session."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#063","summary":"Whether requiring parents to attend annual parenting courses would help children"},{"id":"writing-task-2-011","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"The difficulties of city life","prompt":"More and more people are migrating to cities in search of a better life, but city life can be extremely difficult. Explain some of the difficulties of living in a city. How can governments make urban life better for everyone?","answer":"Cities are often seen as places of opportunity, but there are also some major drawbacks of living in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a large metropolis</strong>. In my opinion, governments could do much more to improve city life for the average inhabitant.\n\nThe main problem for anyone who hopes to migrate to a large city is that the cost of living is likely to be much higher than it is in a small town or village. Inhabitants of cities have to pay higher prices for housing, transport, and even food. Another issue is that urban areas tend to suffer from social problems such as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">high crime and poverty rates</strong> in comparison with rural areas. Air quality in cities is also often poor, due to pollution from traffic, and the streets and public transport systems are usually overcrowded. As a result, city life can be unhealthy and stressful.\n\nHowever, there are various steps that governments could take to tackle these problems. Firstly, they could invest money in the building of affordable or social housing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce the cost of living</strong>. Secondly, politicians have the power to ban vehicles from city centres and promote the use of cleaner public transport, which would help to reduce both air pollution and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traffic congestion</strong>. In London, for example, the introduction of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a congestion charge</strong> for drivers has helped to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curb the traffic problem</strong>. A third option would be to develop provincial towns and rural areas, by moving industry and jobs to those regions, in order to reduce the pressure on major cities.\n\nIn conclusion, governments could certainly implement a range of measures to enhance the quality of life for all city residents.","phrases":[{"en":"a large metropolis","context":"Cities are often seen as places of opportunity, but there are also some major drawbacks of living in a large metropolis. In my opinion, governments could do much more to improve city life for the average inhabitant."},{"en":"high crime and poverty rates","context":"The main problem for anyone who hopes to migrate to a large city is that the cost of living is likely to be much higher than it is in a small town or village. Inhabitants of cities have to pay higher prices for housing, transport, and even food. Another issue is that urban areas tend to suffer from social problems such as high crime and poverty rates in comparison with rural areas. Air quality in cities is also often poor, due to pollution from traffic, and the streets and public transport systems are usually overcrowded. As a result, city life can be unhealthy and stressful."},{"en":"reduce the cost of living","context":"However, there are various steps that governments could take to tackle these problems. Firstly, they could invest money in the building of affordable or social housing to reduce the cost of living. Secondly, politicians have the power to ban vehicles from city centres and promote the use of cleaner public transport, which would help to reduce both air pollution and traffic congestion. In London, for example, the introduction of a congestion charge for drivers has helped to curb the traffic problem. A third option would be to develop provincial towns and rural areas, by moving industry and jobs to those regions, in order to reduce the pressure on major cities."},{"en":"traffic congestion","context":"However, there are various steps that governments could take to tackle these problems. Firstly, they could invest money in the building of affordable or social housing to reduce the cost of living. Secondly, politicians have the power to ban vehicles from city centres and promote the use of cleaner public transport, which would help to reduce both air pollution and traffic congestion. In London, for example, the introduction of a congestion charge for drivers has helped to curb the traffic problem. A third option would be to develop provincial towns and rural areas, by moving industry and jobs to those regions, in order to reduce the pressure on major cities."},{"en":"a congestion charge","context":"However, there are various steps that governments could take to tackle these problems. Firstly, they could invest money in the building of affordable or social housing to reduce the cost of living. Secondly, politicians have the power to ban vehicles from city centres and promote the use of cleaner public transport, which would help to reduce both air pollution and traffic congestion. In London, for example, the introduction of a congestion charge for drivers has helped to curb the traffic problem. A third option would be to develop provincial towns and rural areas, by moving industry and jobs to those regions, in order to reduce the pressure on major cities."},{"en":"curb the traffic problem","context":"However, there are various steps that governments could take to tackle these problems. Firstly, they could invest money in the building of affordable or social housing to reduce the cost of living. Secondly, politicians have the power to ban vehicles from city centres and promote the use of cleaner public transport, which would help to reduce both air pollution and traffic congestion. In London, for example, the introduction of a congestion charge for drivers has helped to curb the traffic problem. A third option would be to develop provincial towns and rural areas, by moving industry and jobs to those regions, in order to reduce the pressure on major cities."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#064","summary":"Urban living challenges and solutions"},{"id":"writing-task-2-012","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Moving from the countryside to the city","prompt":"Many countries today are experiencing high levels of migration from rural areas to cities. What are the causes of this trend, and what effects does it have on the existing city dwellers? Give reasons for your answer, and provide ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"Migration from the countryside to cities is certainly a topical issue, as so many nations are seeing this pattern today. There are three main causes, and two key impacts, which I will outline now.\n\nThe main cause is probably the lack of employment available to rural people. Rural industries, ranging from agriculture to food production and animal breeding, have all been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rationalised and made far more efficient</strong> in recent years, meaning that fewer people are needed to staff them. At the same time, the growth of employment opportunities in the cities means that people, especially youngsters, are tempted to move there by higher wages and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the prospect of reliable work</strong>. Finally, many countries still have a weaker infrastructure in rural areas, especially in terms of transport and economic activity. For example, if a country dweller is unable to find public transport to get to work locally, that person will inevitably consider moving to a city where the infrastructure is far better established.\n\nRegarding the effects felt by the urban residents, perhaps the main impact is on accommodation. An increased urban population drives up the cost of buying or renting a home, leading to possible hardship for people who had assumed that prices would remain stable. We can see this clearly in major conurbations such as London, Sao Paolo or Delhi, where property prices are extremely high and continue to rise. The other main effect on the existing city population is the vastly increased competition for job opportunities, especially at a less skilled level. For instance, drivers, shop workers and hospitality staff find themselves competing for work with new arrivals who are often prepared to work for less. This can lead to a reduction in wages and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consequent decline in standard of living</strong>, especially when combined with the increased cost of housing.\n\nTo sum up, the causes of this trend are primarily to do with employment, and the effects are felt in terms of wages and accommodation.","phrases":[{"en":"rationalised and made far more efficient","context":"The main cause is probably the lack of employment available to rural people. Rural industries, ranging from agriculture to food production and animal breeding, have all been rationalised and made far more efficient in recent years, meaning that fewer people are needed to staff them. At the same time, the growth of employment opportunities in the cities means that people, especially youngsters, are tempted to move there by higher wages and the prospect of reliable work. Finally, many countries still have a weaker infrastructure in rural areas, especially in terms of transport and economic activity. For example, if a country dweller is unable to find public transport to get to work locally, that person will inevitably consider moving to a city where the infrastructure is far better established."},{"en":"the prospect of reliable work","context":"The main cause is probably the lack of employment available to rural people. Rural industries, ranging from agriculture to food production and animal breeding, have all been rationalised and made far more efficient in recent years, meaning that fewer people are needed to staff them. At the same time, the growth of employment opportunities in the cities means that people, especially youngsters, are tempted to move there by higher wages and the prospect of reliable work. Finally, many countries still have a weaker infrastructure in rural areas, especially in terms of transport and economic activity. For example, if a country dweller is unable to find public transport to get to work locally, that person will inevitably consider moving to a city where the infrastructure is far better established."},{"en":"consequent decline in standard of living","context":"Regarding the effects felt by the urban residents, perhaps the main impact is on accommodation. An increased urban population drives up the cost of buying or renting a home, leading to possible hardship for people who had assumed that prices would remain stable. We can see this clearly in major conurbations such as London, Sao Paolo or Delhi, where property prices are extremely high and continue to rise. The other main effect on the existing city population is the vastly increased competition for job opportunities, especially at a less skilled level. For instance, drivers, shop workers and hospitality staff find themselves competing for work with new arrivals who are often prepared to work for less. This can lead to a reduction in wages and consequent decline in standard of living, especially when combined with the increased cost of housing."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#065","summary":"Countries today are experiencing high levels of migration from rural areas…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-013","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Overcrowded cities and what to do about them","prompt":"Overpopulation of urban areas has led to numerous problems. Identify one or two serious ones and suggest ways that governments and individuals can tackle these problems.","answer":"Rapid urban population growth can generate economic opportunity, but when housing and infrastructure fail to expand at the same pace, cities become less liveable. The most serious consequences are insecure housing and overloaded transport, both of which require action from governments as well as residents.\n\nA shortage of affordable homes forces low-income households into overcrowded or informal accommodation. Such housing may lack ventilation, sanitation or legal protection, exposing residents to illness and sudden eviction. At the same time, excessive demand for road space and public transport produces long commutes, air pollution and lost productivity. These pressures are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mutually reinforcing</strong>: families pushed to distant suburbs often spend more time and money travelling to work, and this ends up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deepening existing social inequality</strong>.\n\nGovernments should first increase the supply of well-connected, affordable housing rather than simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moving poorer residents to the urban fringe</strong>. Planning rules can require new developments to include lower-cost units, while public investment should extend water, schools and clinics alongside housing construction. Cities also need high-capacity public transport <strong class=\"key-phrase\">integrated with land-use planning</strong>. Reliable rail and bus networks allow denser neighbourhoods to function without proportional growth in private car use.\n\nIndividuals cannot solve structural shortages, but they can contribute. Residents can use public transport, share vehicles and support local planning proposals that increase housing density near employment centres. Community organisations can also identify unsafe properties, assist tenants and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold local authorities accountable for implementation</strong>. These actions are most effective when citizens have access to transparent data and formal channels for participation.\n\nIn conclusion, urban overpopulation becomes damaging when housing and transport systems are neglected. Governments must lead through coordinated investment and planning, while individuals can reduce pressure on infrastructure and demand that public commitments are fulfilled.","phrases":[{"en":"mutually reinforcing","context":"A shortage of affordable homes forces low-income households into overcrowded or informal accommodation. Such housing may lack ventilation, sanitation or legal protection, exposing residents to illness and sudden eviction. At the same time, excessive demand for road space and public transport produces long commutes, air pollution and lost productivity. These pressures are mutually reinforcing: families pushed to distant suburbs often spend more time and money travelling to work, and this ends up deepening existing social inequality."},{"en":"deepening existing social inequality","context":"A shortage of affordable homes forces low-income households into overcrowded or informal accommodation. Such housing may lack ventilation, sanitation or legal protection, exposing residents to illness and sudden eviction. At the same time, excessive demand for road space and public transport produces long commutes, air pollution and lost productivity. These pressures are mutually reinforcing: families pushed to distant suburbs often spend more time and money travelling to work, and this ends up deepening existing social inequality."},{"en":"moving poorer residents to the urban fringe","context":"Governments should first increase the supply of well-connected, affordable housing rather than simply moving poorer residents to the urban fringe. Planning rules can require new developments to include lower-cost units, while public investment should extend water, schools and clinics alongside housing construction. Cities also need high-capacity public transport integrated with land-use planning. Reliable rail and bus networks allow denser neighbourhoods to function without proportional growth in private car use."},{"en":"integrated with land-use planning","context":"Governments should first increase the supply of well-connected, affordable housing rather than simply moving poorer residents to the urban fringe. Planning rules can require new developments to include lower-cost units, while public investment should extend water, schools and clinics alongside housing construction. Cities also need high-capacity public transport integrated with land-use planning. Reliable rail and bus networks allow denser neighbourhoods to function without proportional growth in private car use."},{"en":"hold local authorities accountable for implementation","context":"Individuals cannot solve structural shortages, but they can contribute. Residents can use public transport, share vehicles and support local planning proposals that increase housing density near employment centres. Community organisations can also identify unsafe properties, assist tenants and hold local authorities accountable for implementation. These actions are most effective when citizens have access to transparent data and formal channels for participation."}],"subtype":"Problems and solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#066","summary":"Urban overpopulation: problems and solutions"},{"id":"writing-task-2-037","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Should a building be useful or beautiful?","prompt":"It is more important for a building to serve its purposes than to look beautiful. Architects do not need to worry about whether it is a real work of art. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"A building must first be safe and useful, but this does not mean that appearance is unimportant. I disagree with the idea that architects should ignore beauty. Good architecture combines function, cost and visual quality rather than treating them as separate goals.\n\nPractical requirements are clearly essential. A hospital needs efficient routes for patients and staff, a school needs suitable classrooms, and housing must be safe, affordable and easy to maintain. A beautiful design that creates dark rooms, poor access or excessive construction costs <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has failed its users</strong>. Architects must therefore begin with the needs of the people who use the building.\n\nNevertheless, appearance also affects daily life. Well-designed streets and public buildings can make people feel proud of their neighbourhood and encourage them to care for shared spaces. Natural light, balanced proportions and appropriate materials may also improve comfort without adding unreasonable cost. In addition, distinctive buildings can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strengthen a city&#x27;s identity</strong> and attract visitors or investment. Beauty is not merely decoration when it contributes to wellbeing and a sense of place.\n\nThe best results come from integrating both aims from the start. Simple, durable materials can be attractive, and an efficient layout can still have character. For this reason, design competitions and planning rules should reward buildings that remain useful for decades, use resources efficiently and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contribute positively to the public realm</strong>; such standards show that visual quality can be achieved without sacrificing affordability or function.\n\nIn conclusion, function should have priority where safety or affordability is at stake, but architects should also seek <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a practical balance between usefulness and beauty</strong>. A building serves society best when it works well and improves its surroundings.","phrases":[{"en":"has failed its users","context":"Practical requirements are clearly essential. A hospital needs efficient routes for patients and staff, a school needs suitable classrooms, and housing must be safe, affordable and easy to maintain. A beautiful design that creates dark rooms, poor access or excessive construction costs has failed its users. Architects must therefore begin with the needs of the people who use the building."},{"en":"strengthen a city's identity","context":"Nevertheless, appearance also affects daily life. Well-designed streets and public buildings can make people feel proud of their neighbourhood and encourage them to care for shared spaces. Natural light, balanced proportions and appropriate materials may also improve comfort without adding unreasonable cost. In addition, distinctive buildings can strengthen a city's identity and attract visitors or investment. Beauty is not merely decoration when it contributes to wellbeing and a sense of place."},{"en":"contribute positively to the public realm","context":"The best results come from integrating both aims from the start. Simple, durable materials can be attractive, and an efficient layout can still have character. For this reason, design competitions and planning rules should reward buildings that remain useful for decades, use resources efficiently and contribute positively to the public realm; such standards show that visual quality can be achieved without sacrificing affordability or function."},{"en":"a practical balance between usefulness and beauty","context":"In conclusion, function should have priority where safety or affordability is at stake, but architects should also seek a practical balance between usefulness and beauty. A building serves society best when it works well and improves its surroundings."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#067","summary":"It is more important for a building to serve its purposes than to look…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-043","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Should new buildings copy traditional styles?","prompt":"Some people believe that new buildings should be built in traditional styles. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that new buildings should be constructed in the traditional style of the country in which they are built. I disagree with this view, because although historic architecture is certainly worth protecting, imitating it indefinitely would leave cities with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no character of their own</strong>.\n\nThe argument for traditional design is understandable. A city whose buildings share a consistent style has a visual identity that visitors recognise immediately, and a modern glass tower inserted into a row of old houses can genuinely spoil a street. Traditional materials such as stone, brick and timber also age well, whereas much of the concrete used in the 1960s has deteriorated badly.\n\nNevertheless, this approach has serious weaknesses. Every style that we now call traditional was modern when it was built, and the districts we admire are valued precisely because each generation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">added something of its own</strong>. A rule requiring imitation would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">freeze a city at an arbitrary date</strong> and produce buildings that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">neither genuinely old nor honestly new</strong>. There is also a practical objection: traditional construction depends on skills and materials that are now scarce and expensive, so it could never be applied to housing estates, hospitals or schools.\n\nModern buildings can also do things that older ones cannot. Contemporary design allows far better insulation, natural light and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">access for disabled people</strong>, and these matter more to the people who use a building every day than its exterior does.\n\nIn conclusion, historic buildings should certainly be preserved, but I disagree that new ones must copy them. New architecture should respect the scale and materials of its surroundings rather than reproduce the past.","phrases":[{"en":"no character of their own","context":"It is sometimes argued that new buildings should be constructed in the traditional style of the country in which they are built. I disagree with this view, because although historic architecture is certainly worth protecting, imitating it indefinitely would leave cities with no character of their own."},{"en":"added something of its own","context":"Nevertheless, this approach has serious weaknesses. Every style that we now call traditional was modern when it was built, and the districts we admire are valued precisely because each generation added something of its own. A rule requiring imitation would freeze a city at an arbitrary date and produce buildings that are neither genuinely old nor honestly new. There is also a practical objection: traditional construction depends on skills and materials that are now scarce and expensive, so it could never be applied to housing estates, hospitals or schools."},{"en":"freeze a city at an arbitrary date","context":"Nevertheless, this approach has serious weaknesses. Every style that we now call traditional was modern when it was built, and the districts we admire are valued precisely because each generation added something of its own. A rule requiring imitation would freeze a city at an arbitrary date and produce buildings that are neither genuinely old nor honestly new. There is also a practical objection: traditional construction depends on skills and materials that are now scarce and expensive, so it could never be applied to housing estates, hospitals or schools."},{"en":"neither genuinely old nor honestly new","context":"Nevertheless, this approach has serious weaknesses. Every style that we now call traditional was modern when it was built, and the districts we admire are valued precisely because each generation added something of its own. A rule requiring imitation would freeze a city at an arbitrary date and produce buildings that are neither genuinely old nor honestly new. There is also a practical objection: traditional construction depends on skills and materials that are now scarce and expensive, so it could never be applied to housing estates, hospitals or schools."},{"en":"access for disabled people","context":"Modern buildings can also do things that older ones cannot. Contemporary design allows far better insulation, natural light and access for disabled people, and these matter more to the people who use a building every day than its exterior does."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#068","summary":"Should new buildings copy traditional styles?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-108","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"The growth of large cities","prompt":"Cities around the world are growing rapidly. What are the causes of this trend, and what consequences might it have?","answer":"Cities in almost every country are expanding rapidly, and in several regions the population of a single urban area now exceeds that of a small nation. This growth has clear causes, and its consequences are both beneficial and damaging.\n\nThe principal cause is economic. Employment is concentrated in cities, wages are higher, and rural work has declined as farming has become mechanised, so millions move in search of a living. Cities also offer services the countryside cannot provide: universities, specialist hospitals and better schools. A further factor is that urban populations grow from within, since migrants are typically young adults who then have families of their own, so growth continues even when new arrivals slow.\n\nSome consequences are positive. Concentrating people makes public transport, water supply and healthcare <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far cheaper to provide per person</strong>, and cities generate the majority of national income and tax revenue. The variety of people living closely together also produces <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the exchange of ideas</strong> on which new industries depend.\n\nThe problems, however, are severe. Housing cannot be built quickly enough, so prices rise and the poorest end up in overcrowded or informal settlements. Roads congest, air quality deteriorates, and demand for water and electricity <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outstrips supply</strong>. Rural areas suffer the opposite difficulty, losing the young workers who would have supported them.\n\nIn conclusion, urban growth is driven mainly by the concentration of work and services, and it is unlikely to reverse. It brings genuine efficiency, but without serious investment in housing and public transport the costs will steadily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outweigh the advantages</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"far cheaper to provide per person","context":"Some consequences are positive. Concentrating people makes public transport, water supply and healthcare far cheaper to provide per person, and cities generate the majority of national income and tax revenue. The variety of people living closely together also produces the exchange of ideas on which new industries depend."},{"en":"the exchange of ideas","context":"Some consequences are positive. Concentrating people makes public transport, water supply and healthcare far cheaper to provide per person, and cities generate the majority of national income and tax revenue. The variety of people living closely together also produces the exchange of ideas on which new industries depend."},{"en":"outstrips supply","context":"The problems, however, are severe. Housing cannot be built quickly enough, so prices rise and the poorest end up in overcrowded or informal settlements. Roads congest, air quality deteriorates, and demand for water and electricity outstrips supply. Rural areas suffer the opposite difficulty, losing the young workers who would have supported them."},{"en":"outweigh the advantages","context":"In conclusion, urban growth is driven mainly by the concentration of work and services, and it is unlikely to reverse. It brings genuine efficiency, but without serious investment in housing and public transport the costs will steadily outweigh the advantages."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#069","summary":"The growth of large cities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-155","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Knocking down old buildings","prompt":"People think that old buildings should be knocked down and give way to the new buildings. Do you agree or disagree? How important are old buildings to us?","answer":"Some old buildings should be protected, while others should be replaced when they are unsafe or no longer useful. I therefore disagree with the idea that age alone is a reason either to preserve or demolish a structure. Decisions should be based on historical value, condition and the needs of the surrounding community.\n\nImportant old buildings connect people with the history of a place. A traditional market, theatre or government building may show how earlier generations lived and what materials or techniques they used. Such structures can strengthen local identity, support tourism and provide architects with practical examples of earlier design. Once a unique building is destroyed, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">its cultural value cannot be recreated</strong> by a modern copy.\n\nNevertheless, preservation has limits. Some buildings are structurally dangerous, extremely expensive to maintain or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">poorly suited to modern needs</strong>. Keeping every ageing structure can prevent the construction of housing, schools or transport links in areas where land is scarce. In these cases, demolition may be justified after an independent assessment. Useful parts of the original building, such as a façade or significant interior features, can sometimes be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">incorporated into a new development</strong>.\n\nThe best policy is a clear classification system. Buildings of genuine architectural or historical importance should receive legal protection and realistic maintenance funding. Ordinary structures should be assessed more flexibly, with public consultation and a full comparison of renovation and replacement costs. This creates a balance between heritage and present-day needs.\n\nIn conclusion, old buildings are important when they carry distinctive cultural, historical or design value. Others may reasonably give way to safer and more useful development, provided that the decision is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">based on evidence rather than short-term profit</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"its cultural value cannot be recreated","context":"Important old buildings connect people with the history of a place. A traditional market, theatre or government building may show how earlier generations lived and what materials or techniques they used. Such structures can strengthen local identity, support tourism and provide architects with practical examples of earlier design. Once a unique building is destroyed, its cultural value cannot be recreated by a modern copy."},{"en":"poorly suited to modern needs","context":"Nevertheless, preservation has limits. Some buildings are structurally dangerous, extremely expensive to maintain or poorly suited to modern needs. Keeping every ageing structure can prevent the construction of housing, schools or transport links in areas where land is scarce. In these cases, demolition may be justified after an independent assessment. Useful parts of the original building, such as a façade or significant interior features, can sometimes be incorporated into a new development."},{"en":"incorporated into a new development","context":"Nevertheless, preservation has limits. Some buildings are structurally dangerous, extremely expensive to maintain or poorly suited to modern needs. Keeping every ageing structure can prevent the construction of housing, schools or transport links in areas where land is scarce. In these cases, demolition may be justified after an independent assessment. Useful parts of the original building, such as a façade or significant interior features, can sometimes be incorporated into a new development."},{"en":"based on evidence rather than short-term profit","context":"In conclusion, old buildings are important when they carry distinctive cultural, historical or design value. Others may reasonably give way to safer and more useful development, provided that the decision is based on evidence rather than short-term profit."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#070","summary":"People think that old buildings should be knocked down and give way to the…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2198","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Preserving historic buildings","prompt":"Some people think that certain old buildings that are historically important in cities or the countryside should be preserved. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people argue that old buildings of historical significance, whether in urban or rural areas, should be protected rather than demolished. I completely agree with this view, for both cultural and economic reasons.\n\nThe main argument for preservation is that historic buildings are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a physical record of the past</strong>. Written history can be rewritten or forgotten, but a medieval church or a colonial town hall shows later generations exactly how earlier societies lived, built and worshipped. Once such a building is knocked down, that evidence is lost permanently, and no reconstruction can genuinely replace it. Countries that cleared their old quarters during periods of rapid development now find that their cities <strong class=\"key-phrase\">look almost identical to every other modern city</strong> in the world.\n\nPreservation also makes economic sense. Historic architecture attracts enormous numbers of visitors, and tourism supports hotels, restaurants, transport and thousands of local jobs. Cities such as Prague and Kyoto owe much of their income to buildings that could easily have been replaced by offices. Old structures can also be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adapted rather than replaced</strong>: former factories and warehouses are now used as museums, apartments and workspaces, which is often cheaper and more environmentally friendly than demolition and new construction.\n\nThe obvious objection is cost, since restoration requires specialist skills and materials. However, this expense is small compared with the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">permanent loss of a country&#x27;s heritage</strong>, and much of it can be recovered through ticket sales and tourist spending.\n\nIn conclusion, I strongly agree that historically important buildings should be preserved, because they connect people with their past and bring lasting economic benefits.","phrases":[{"en":"a physical record of the past","context":"The main argument for preservation is that historic buildings are a physical record of the past. Written history can be rewritten or forgotten, but a medieval church or a colonial town hall shows later generations exactly how earlier societies lived, built and worshipped. Once such a building is knocked down, that evidence is lost permanently, and no reconstruction can genuinely replace it. Countries that cleared their old quarters during periods of rapid development now find that their cities look almost identical to every other modern city in the world."},{"en":"look almost identical to every other modern city","context":"The main argument for preservation is that historic buildings are a physical record of the past. Written history can be rewritten or forgotten, but a medieval church or a colonial town hall shows later generations exactly how earlier societies lived, built and worshipped. Once such a building is knocked down, that evidence is lost permanently, and no reconstruction can genuinely replace it. Countries that cleared their old quarters during periods of rapid development now find that their cities look almost identical to every other modern city in the world."},{"en":"adapted rather than replaced","context":"Preservation also makes economic sense. Historic architecture attracts enormous numbers of visitors, and tourism supports hotels, restaurants, transport and thousands of local jobs. Cities such as Prague and Kyoto owe much of their income to buildings that could easily have been replaced by offices. Old structures can also be adapted rather than replaced: former factories and warehouses are now used as museums, apartments and workspaces, which is often cheaper and more environmentally friendly than demolition and new construction."},{"en":"permanent loss of a country's heritage","context":"The obvious objection is cost, since restoration requires specialist skills and materials. However, this expense is small compared with the permanent loss of a country's heritage, and much of it can be recovered through ticket sales and tourist spending."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#071","summary":"Preserving historic buildings"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2203","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Public spaces in towns and cities","prompt":"It is important for all towns and cities to have large public spaces such as squares and parks. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?","answer":"It is often argued that every town and city should provide substantial open areas such as parks and public squares. I completely agree with this view, because such spaces improve residents&#x27; health, strengthen communities and make urban environments far more liveable.\n\nThe health benefits are the most obvious. City dwellers spend most of the day indoors and seated, and a nearby park is the cheapest and most accessible place to walk, run, cycle or play sport. Free access matters enormously, since low-income families cannot afford gym memberships. Green space also improves air quality and lowers summer temperatures, which is increasingly important as cities grow hotter, and there is now considerable evidence that regular contact with nature reduces stress and anxiety.\n\nPublic spaces also perform a social function that no private facility can replace. A square or park is one of the very few places where people of different ages, incomes and backgrounds meet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without having to spend money</strong>. Markets, festivals, concerts and public celebrations all require open ground, and communities that have such places tend to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more cohesive</strong> and less isolated.\n\nThe usual objection is that land in city centres is extremely expensive and could be used for housing or offices. This is a genuine difficulty, but it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">short-sighted</strong>. Property near parks consistently commands higher prices, and cities famous for their open spaces, such as London and New York, have found that these areas <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repay their cost many times over</strong> through tourism, health savings and residential appeal.\n\nIn conclusion, I strongly agree that large public spaces are essential, and planners should protect them even when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the pressure to build is intense</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"without having to spend money","context":"Public spaces also perform a social function that no private facility can replace. A square or park is one of the very few places where people of different ages, incomes and backgrounds meet without having to spend money. Markets, festivals, concerts and public celebrations all require open ground, and communities that have such places tend to be more cohesive and less isolated."},{"en":"more cohesive","context":"Public spaces also perform a social function that no private facility can replace. A square or park is one of the very few places where people of different ages, incomes and backgrounds meet without having to spend money. Markets, festivals, concerts and public celebrations all require open ground, and communities that have such places tend to be more cohesive and less isolated."},{"en":"short-sighted","context":"The usual objection is that land in city centres is extremely expensive and could be used for housing or offices. This is a genuine difficulty, but it is short-sighted. Property near parks consistently commands higher prices, and cities famous for their open spaces, such as London and New York, have found that these areas repay their cost many times over through tourism, health savings and residential appeal."},{"en":"repay their cost many times over","context":"The usual objection is that land in city centres is extremely expensive and could be used for housing or offices. This is a genuine difficulty, but it is short-sighted. Property near parks consistently commands higher prices, and cities famous for their open spaces, such as London and New York, have found that these areas repay their cost many times over through tourism, health savings and residential appeal."},{"en":"the pressure to build is intense","context":"In conclusion, I strongly agree that large public spaces are essential, and planners should protect them even when the pressure to build is intense."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#072","summary":"Public spaces in towns and cities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2247","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Building outwards or upwards","prompt":"Some people think that it is best to live in a horizontal city while others think of a vertical city. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"As populations grow, cities must decide whether to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spread outwards</strong> in low buildings or upwards in towers. Both models have committed supporters, and my own view is that the vertical approach is the more responsible one for large cities.\n\nThose who prefer low-rise living point to what happens at ground level. Streets of houses two or three storeys high keep front doors, gardens and pavements close together, so neighbours meet and children can play outside within sight of a window. Such districts are also easier to adapt, since a family can extend a house as it grows. Towers, by contrast, can leave residents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">isolated behind a lift door</strong>, with no space of their own outdoors.\n\nThe argument for building upwards is a matter of arithmetic. Housing the same population across a wider area means longer journeys, more roads, more pipes and more cable, all of which cost money and produce emissions, and it consumes farmland at the edges. Density does the opposite: where enough people live within walking distance, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shops, clinics and a bus route become viable</strong>, and a resident may not need a car at all. Compact cities consistently record lower emissions per person than sprawling ones.\n\nMy view is that the objections to towers describe bad design rather than height itself. Buildings with generous shared gardens, ground-floor shops and well-maintained lifts produce sociable neighbourhoods, whereas towers surrounded by car parks fail for reasons that have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nothing to do</strong> with the storeys.\n\nIn conclusion, low-rise districts are pleasant but cannot house growing populations without consuming the countryside. Building upwards is preferable, provided cities invest in the shared spaces that make density <strong class=\"key-phrase\">liveable rather than merely efficient</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"spread outwards","context":"As populations grow, cities must decide whether to spread outwards in low buildings or upwards in towers. Both models have committed supporters, and my own view is that the vertical approach is the more responsible one for large cities."},{"en":"isolated behind a lift door","context":"Those who prefer low-rise living point to what happens at ground level. Streets of houses two or three storeys high keep front doors, gardens and pavements close together, so neighbours meet and children can play outside within sight of a window. Such districts are also easier to adapt, since a family can extend a house as it grows. Towers, by contrast, can leave residents isolated behind a lift door, with no space of their own outdoors."},{"en":"shops, clinics and a bus route become viable","context":"The argument for building upwards is a matter of arithmetic. Housing the same population across a wider area means longer journeys, more roads, more pipes and more cable, all of which cost money and produce emissions, and it consumes farmland at the edges. Density does the opposite: where enough people live within walking distance, shops, clinics and a bus route become viable, and a resident may not need a car at all. Compact cities consistently record lower emissions per person than sprawling ones."},{"en":"nothing to do","context":"My view is that the objections to towers describe bad design rather than height itself. Buildings with generous shared gardens, ground-floor shops and well-maintained lifts produce sociable neighbourhoods, whereas towers surrounded by car parks fail for reasons that have nothing to do with the storeys."},{"en":"liveable rather than merely efficient","context":"In conclusion, low-rise districts are pleasant but cannot house growing populations without consuming the countryside. Building upwards is preferable, provided cities invest in the shared spaces that make density liveable rather than merely efficient."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#073","summary":"Low-rise cities compared with high-rise cities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2313","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Moving companies out of the cities","prompt":"Traffic and housing problems could be solved by moving large companies, factories and their employees to the countryside. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Congested roads and unaffordable housing are the standard complaints of every large city, and moving employers out of them sounds like a direct cure. I agree that relocation would ease both problems, but I disagree that it would solve them, and it would create new difficulties of its own.\n\nThe logic behind the idea is sound as far as it goes. Traffic and housing pressure are both consequences of too many people needing to be in one place, so removing a large employer removes thousands of daily journeys and thousands of competing tenants at once. Rural areas gain from the same movement, since a factory brings wages, customers for local shops and a reason for young people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay in the town they grew up in</strong>.\n\nThe plan is weaker than it looks, however. Firms cluster in cities because they need suppliers, specialists, airports and each other, and a company that moves alone loses those advantages and often loses staff, since employees have partners with careers and children in school. Rural roads and railways are also thin, so a relocated workforce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drives where it used to take a train</strong>, and the traffic reappears in a less efficient form. Housing pressure tends to follow the jobs rather than disappear, which is how expensive commuter towns are created.\n\nMore effective measures work on the city itself: building densely near stations, taxing empty property, improving public transport and allowing people to work from home for part of the week. These reduce the need to travel without dismantling what makes a city productive.\n\nIn conclusion, moving some employers out of crowded cities is a reasonable part of a wider plan, particularly for factories that need space rather than proximity. On its own it would relocate the problems rather than remove them.","phrases":[{"en":"stay in the town they grew up in","context":"The logic behind the idea is sound as far as it goes. Traffic and housing pressure are both consequences of too many people needing to be in one place, so removing a large employer removes thousands of daily journeys and thousands of competing tenants at once. Rural areas gain from the same movement, since a factory brings wages, customers for local shops and a reason for young people to stay in the town they grew up in."},{"en":"drives where it used to take a train","context":"The plan is weaker than it looks, however. Firms cluster in cities because they need suppliers, specialists, airports and each other, and a company that moves alone loses those advantages and often loses staff, since employees have partners with careers and children in school. Rural roads and railways are also thin, so a relocated workforce drives where it used to take a train, and the traffic reappears in a less efficient form. Housing pressure tends to follow the jobs rather than disappear, which is how expensive commuter towns are created."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#074","summary":"Whether relocating firms to the countryside would solve traffic and housing problems"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2332","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Trees or houses on urban land?","prompt":"Some people say it is more important to plant trees in the open spaces in towns and cities than to build more housing. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Cities with a housing shortage and a heat problem must decide what their remaining open land is for. I disagree that planting should take priority, because homes meet a need that nothing else can substitute, though the two aims are less opposed than the question suggests.\n\nTrees earn their place. They lower street temperatures by several degrees in summer, which now prevents deaths in cities that were not built for the heat they experience. They absorb rainfall that would otherwise flood drains, filter pollution and reduce noise, and access to greenery has a measurable effect on mental health and recovery from illness. A city without them is harder to live in and more expensive to run.\n\nHousing, however, addresses something more urgent. People priced out of a city do not simply live elsewhere pleasantly; they commute for hours, share overcrowded flats or become homeless, and a shortage pushes rents up for everyone. Overcrowding damages health and children&#x27;s schooling directly. A person without a home gains nothing from the park they cannot afford to live beside, and where the shortage is severe the claim of housing is stronger.\n\nThe real error is treating the choice as unavoidable. Cities waste enormous quantities of land on car parking, empty offices and derelict industrial sites, and building on those instead protects parks entirely. Density also helps: flats with courtyards and street trees house far more people per hectare than low houses with private gardens, so it is possible to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">add homes and greenery at the same time</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, trees are essential to a liveable city and should never be treated as decoration. Where a genuine housing shortage exists, homes come first, and the answer is to build on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">land that is already wasted</strong> rather than on the parks.","phrases":[{"en":"add homes and greenery at the same time","context":"The real error is treating the choice as unavoidable. Cities waste enormous quantities of land on car parking, empty offices and derelict industrial sites, and building on those instead protects parks entirely. Density also helps: flats with courtyards and street trees house far more people per hectare than low houses with private gardens, so it is possible to add homes and greenery at the same time."},{"en":"land that is already wasted","context":"In conclusion, trees are essential to a liveable city and should never be treated as decoration. Where a genuine housing shortage exists, homes come first, and the answer is to build on land that is already wasted rather than on the parks."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#075","summary":"Whether open urban space should be planted rather than built on"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2352","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Turning parks into vegetable gardens","prompt":"In some cities, public parks and open spaces are being turned into gardens where local residents can grow their own fruit and vegetables. Do the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Allotments and community plots have reappeared in cities where land is scarce and food is expensive. I believe the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, provided the gardens take only part of the open space available.\n\nThe clearest benefit is what growing food does for the people doing it. Gardening is steady physical activity that continues into old age, and it produces fresh vegetables at a cost that matters to households on low incomes. The mental effect is well documented, since working with plants reduces stress and gives structure to the days of the retired and the unemployed. Community plots also bring neighbours into contact who would otherwise never speak, and shared work builds the kind of familiarity that makes a street feel safe.\n\nThere are wider gains. Children who have grown a carrot understand where food comes from and eat vegetables more willingly. Food grown a few streets away needs no transport or packaging, and gardens absorb rainwater and support insects that a mown lawn does not.\n\nThe objections deserve attention. A garden is not a park: it excludes the football, running and casual sitting that open space allows, and plots are usually allocated to a limited number of households, so land that belonged to everybody now belongs to a few. Gardens also require commitment, and an abandoned plot quickly becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">waste ground that nobody wants to look at</strong>. Cities that are short of green space cannot afford to convert much of it. The balance therefore depends on scale. Converting a corner of a large park serves both purposes, while converting the whole of a district&#x27;s only open space does not.\n\nIn conclusion, community gardens improve health, food access and neighbourly contact at very low cost. They deserve support wherever they take a share of the open space rather than replacing it.","phrases":[{"en":"waste ground that nobody wants to look at","context":"The objections deserve attention. A garden is not a park: it excludes the football, running and casual sitting that open space allows, and plots are usually allocated to a limited number of households, so land that belonged to everybody now belongs to a few. Gardens also require commitment, and an abandoned plot quickly becomes waste ground that nobody wants to look at. Cities that are short of green space cannot afford to convert much of it. The balance therefore depends on scale. Converting a corner of a large park serves both purposes, while converting the whole of a district's only open space does not."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#076","summary":"Whether converting urban open space into community gardens is worthwhile"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2359","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Cities & Urban Life","title":"Separating homes, shops and offices","prompt":"In many urban areas, planners tend to place shops, schools, offices and homes in separate zones. Do the benefits of this outweigh the drawbacks?","answer":"Twentieth-century planning divided cities into districts with a single purpose, and most new suburbs are still built that way. I believe the drawbacks now outweigh the benefits, though the practice began for reasons that made sense.\n\nSeparation was introduced to protect people from industry. Housing built beside factories meant noise, smoke and danger, and moving homes away from that was a genuine improvement in health. Zoning also allows infrastructure to be planned efficiently, since roads and drainage can be sized for what each district actually does, and it gives residential areas quiet evenings and predictable property values. Shopping centres and business parks benefit from clustering, because customers and suppliers find everything in one place.\n\nThe costs have grown as cities have spread. Separating uses guarantees travel: every trip to work, to school or to buy milk becomes a journey, usually by car, which produces the congestion and pollution that planners then attempt to solve with wider roads. Anyone without a car — the young, the old, the poor — is stranded in a district that contains nothing they need. Single-use areas are also lifeless for most of the day, since office districts empty at six and suburbs empty at eight, and streets with nobody on them feel unsafe. Small businesses suffer too, because a corner shop cannot exist where no shops are permitted.\n\nMixed neighbourhoods answer most of this. Where flats sit above shops and offices stand near homes, daily needs are within walking distance, streets stay occupied throughout the day, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">children can reach a school without being driven</strong>. Modern industry is quiet enough for this to be safe.\n\nIn conclusion, zoning improved conditions when cities were polluted by heavy industry, and some separation remains sensible. It now forces unnecessary travel and empties streets, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mixing uses within a neighbourhood</strong> is the better principle for cities being built today.","phrases":[{"en":"children can reach a school without being driven","context":"Mixed neighbourhoods answer most of this. Where flats sit above shops and offices stand near homes, daily needs are within walking distance, streets stay occupied throughout the day, and children can reach a school without being driven. Modern industry is quiet enough for this to be safe."},{"en":"mixing uses within a neighbourhood","context":"In conclusion, zoning improved conditions when cities were polluted by heavy industry, and some separation remains sensible. It now forces unnecessary travel and empties streets, so mixing uses within a neighbourhood is the better principle for cities being built today."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#077","summary":"Whether dividing a city into separate zones brings more benefits than drawbacks"},{"id":"writing-task-2-016","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Ex-prisoners talking to teenagers about crime","prompt":"Some people who have been in prison become good citizens later, and it is often argued that these are the best people to talk to teenagers about the dangers of committing a crime. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is true that ex-prisoners can become normal, productive members of society. I completely agree with the idea that allowing such people to speak to teenagers about their experiences is the best way to discourage them from breaking the law.\n\nIn my opinion, teenagers are more likely to accept advice from someone who can speak from experience. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Reformed offenders</strong> can tell young people about how they became involved in crime, the dangers of a criminal lifestyle, and what life in prison is really like. They can also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">dispel any ideas</strong> that teenagers may have about criminals leading glamorous lives. While adolescents are often indifferent to the guidance given by older people, I imagine that most of them would be extremely keen to hear the stories of an ex-offender. The vivid and perhaps shocking nature of these stories is likely to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a powerful impact</strong>.\n\nThe alternatives to using reformed criminals to educate teenagers about crime would be much less effective. One option would be for police officers to visit schools and talk to young people. This could be useful in terms of informing teens about what happens to lawbreakers when they are caught, but young people are often reluctant to take advice from figures of authority. A second option would be for school teachers to speak to their students about crime, but I doubt that students would see teachers as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">credible sources of information</strong> about this topic. Finally, educational films might be informative, but there would be no opportunity for young people to interact and ask questions.\n\nIn conclusion, I fully support the view that people who have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turned their lives around</strong> after serving a prison sentence could help to deter teenagers from committing crimes.","phrases":[{"en":"Reformed offenders","context":"In my opinion, teenagers are more likely to accept advice from someone who can speak from experience. Reformed offenders can tell young people about how they became involved in crime, the dangers of a criminal lifestyle, and what life in prison is really like. They can also dispel any ideas that teenagers may have about criminals leading glamorous lives. While adolescents are often indifferent to the guidance given by older people, I imagine that most of them would be extremely keen to hear the stories of an ex-offender. The vivid and perhaps shocking nature of these stories is likely to have a powerful impact."},{"en":"dispel any ideas","context":"In my opinion, teenagers are more likely to accept advice from someone who can speak from experience. Reformed offenders can tell young people about how they became involved in crime, the dangers of a criminal lifestyle, and what life in prison is really like. They can also dispel any ideas that teenagers may have about criminals leading glamorous lives. While adolescents are often indifferent to the guidance given by older people, I imagine that most of them would be extremely keen to hear the stories of an ex-offender. The vivid and perhaps shocking nature of these stories is likely to have a powerful impact."},{"en":"have a powerful impact","context":"In my opinion, teenagers are more likely to accept advice from someone who can speak from experience. Reformed offenders can tell young people about how they became involved in crime, the dangers of a criminal lifestyle, and what life in prison is really like. They can also dispel any ideas that teenagers may have about criminals leading glamorous lives. While adolescents are often indifferent to the guidance given by older people, I imagine that most of them would be extremely keen to hear the stories of an ex-offender. The vivid and perhaps shocking nature of these stories is likely to have a powerful impact."},{"en":"credible sources of information","context":"The alternatives to using reformed criminals to educate teenagers about crime would be much less effective. One option would be for police officers to visit schools and talk to young people. This could be useful in terms of informing teens about what happens to lawbreakers when they are caught, but young people are often reluctant to take advice from figures of authority. A second option would be for school teachers to speak to their students about crime, but I doubt that students would see teachers as credible sources of information about this topic. Finally, educational films might be informative, but there would be no opportunity for young people to interact and ask questions."},{"en":"turned their lives around","context":"In conclusion, I fully support the view that people who have turned their lives around after serving a prison sentence could help to deter teenagers from committing crimes."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#078","summary":"Ex-prisoners educating teenagers"},{"id":"writing-task-2-017","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Identity theft and its victims","prompt":"In many countries, financial crime involving identity theft is increasing. What are the causes of this trend, and what effect does identity theft have on the victims involved? Give reasons for your answer, and provide ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"There is no doubt that identity fraud is a great concern, especially in western countries where people are most at risk. I think there are two main causes, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a number of damaging effects</strong>.\n\nThe major cause is probably the almost universal use of digital technology to store financial data and undertake financial transactions. It is surprisingly easy for criminals to obtain personal information about a possible victim, and then to use this information to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impersonate the target</strong>. Because bank accounts and loans can be applied for digitally, without the need for face-to-face contact, the criminals are able to apply for financial products remotely, often before the victim is even aware of the attack. The second key cause is, I believe, the increasing probability of ‘traditional’ crimes (such as armed robbery, mugging or burglary) being detected. When criminals see the widespread use of CCTV, DNA profiling and fingerprinting, they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less inclined to indulge in such old-fashioned crimes</strong>. Rather, they prefer the lower risk and more profitable use of data-based crimes. The effects on the unfortunate victims can be quite devastating.\n\nFirstly, there is the loss of money from bank accounts or by being connected to a fraudulent loan. In some cases, this can take years for the victim to pay back or resolve with the banks and the authorities. Secondly, there is the damage to their credit rating, which means they may find it impossible to obtain legitimate credit in future. In extreme cases, people&#x27;s employment prospects can even be damaged as well, as they find themselves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">profiled as a financial risk</strong>.\n\nOverall, the presence of technology and the decline of ‘traditional’ crimes are the key causes behind this trend. The effects on the victims involve long-term financial hardship in many instances.","phrases":[{"en":"a number of damaging effects","context":"There is no doubt that identity fraud is a great concern, especially in western countries where people are most at risk. I think there are two main causes, and a number of damaging effects."},{"en":"impersonate the target","context":"The major cause is probably the almost universal use of digital technology to store financial data and undertake financial transactions. It is surprisingly easy for criminals to obtain personal information about a possible victim, and then to use this information to impersonate the target. Because bank accounts and loans can be applied for digitally, without the need for face-to-face contact, the criminals are able to apply for financial products remotely, often before the victim is even aware of the attack. The second key cause is, I believe, the increasing probability of ‘traditional’ crimes (such as armed robbery, mugging or burglary) being detected. When criminals see the widespread use of CCTV, DNA profiling and fingerprinting, they are less inclined to indulge in such old-fashioned crimes. Rather, they prefer the lower risk and more profitable use of data-based crimes. The effects on the unfortunate victims can be quite devastating."},{"en":"less inclined to indulge in such old-fashioned crimes","context":"The major cause is probably the almost universal use of digital technology to store financial data and undertake financial transactions. It is surprisingly easy for criminals to obtain personal information about a possible victim, and then to use this information to impersonate the target. Because bank accounts and loans can be applied for digitally, without the need for face-to-face contact, the criminals are able to apply for financial products remotely, often before the victim is even aware of the attack. The second key cause is, I believe, the increasing probability of ‘traditional’ crimes (such as armed robbery, mugging or burglary) being detected. When criminals see the widespread use of CCTV, DNA profiling and fingerprinting, they are less inclined to indulge in such old-fashioned crimes. Rather, they prefer the lower risk and more profitable use of data-based crimes. The effects on the unfortunate victims can be quite devastating."},{"en":"profiled as a financial risk","context":"Firstly, there is the loss of money from bank accounts or by being connected to a fraudulent loan. In some cases, this can take years for the victim to pay back or resolve with the banks and the authorities. Secondly, there is the damage to their credit rating, which means they may find it impossible to obtain legitimate credit in future. In extreme cases, people's employment prospects can even be damaged as well, as they find themselves profiled as a financial risk."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#079","summary":"Financial crime involving identity theft is increasing."},{"id":"writing-task-2-018","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Do police patrols reduce crime?","prompt":"Having police officers patrolling the streets is often considered an essential way to reduce crime. How effective do you think police street patrols are? What other ways of reducing crime can you propose? Give reasons for your answer, and provide ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"We are all keen to reduce crime wherever possible, and the role of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visible policing</strong> is a hotly debated part of this. Personally, I feel that street patrols are highly effective, although there are several <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supplementary methods</strong> too.\n\nRegarding the effectiveness of patrols, evidence suggests that an increase in police on the streets leads directly to a drop in crime, especially crimes of violence and robbery. A good example is the experience of New York when it adopted the ‘zero tolerance’ policy in the 1990s, a high-profile policing tactic which has been extended successfully to many other cities worldwide. When compared to other tactics that have been tried, ranging from citizen patrols to CCTV, there is little doubt that assertive police patrolling is the most effective resource we have.\n\nPatrols work best, however, when the courts and prisons back them up with coordinated action. An offender who is arrested but then <strong class=\"key-phrase\">given a lenient sentence</strong> has little reason to stop, so consistently deterrent sentencing and strict prison regimes matter almost as much as the patrols themselves.\n\nDetection matters just as much as prevention, so police forces also need well-funded detective and forensic departments to ensure that crimes which do occur are actually solved. Ordinary citizens and businesses have a part to play too: something as simple as better locks and outdoor lighting measurably reduces theft and burglary, and a street that looks cared for is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a less attractive target in the first place</strong>.\n\nAll in all, I feel that visible police patrols have been proved to be the most effective way to reduce common crimes. Their effectiveness can be maximised by supporting action from elsewhere in the police and justice system, and by private citizens doing their part as well.","phrases":[{"en":"visible policing","context":"We are all keen to reduce crime wherever possible, and the role of visible policing is a hotly debated part of this. Personally, I feel that street patrols are highly effective, although there are several supplementary methods too."},{"en":"supplementary methods","context":"We are all keen to reduce crime wherever possible, and the role of visible policing is a hotly debated part of this. Personally, I feel that street patrols are highly effective, although there are several supplementary methods too."},{"en":"given a lenient sentence","context":"Patrols work best, however, when the courts and prisons back them up with coordinated action. An offender who is arrested but then given a lenient sentence has little reason to stop, so consistently deterrent sentencing and strict prison regimes matter almost as much as the patrols themselves."},{"en":"a less attractive target in the first place","context":"Detection matters just as much as prevention, so police forces also need well-funded detective and forensic departments to ensure that crimes which do occur are actually solved. Ordinary citizens and businesses have a part to play too: something as simple as better locks and outdoor lighting measurably reduces theft and burglary, and a street that looks cared for is a less attractive target in the first place."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#080","summary":"Having police officers patrolling the streets is often considered an…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-019","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Is prison the only effective punishment?","prompt":"“Prison is the only truly effective form of punishment, because it separates criminals from society.” To what extent do you support this view?","answer":"There is no doubt that we need to find effective forms of punishment for criminals, and also find ways to protect citizens from the threat of crime. I personally feel that prison is only one of a range of options we should use, and is not always the most effective.\n\nFirstly, I do not accept that all criminals need to be separated from society. Juvenile and minor offenders can be punished through fines, community service or other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">non-custodial means</strong>. This is cheaper than prison, and prevents the damage to the criminal&#x27;s family which in itself could generate further social problems.\n\nThere is a second benefit. By keeping these small-scale criminals in society, we can try to rehabilitate them, for example through training or education programmes. This will help to reduce re-offending in future. Besides this, if offenders are believed to be a threat to their fellow citizens, there are ways of containing them such as electronic tagging, curfews or regular reporting to the police. Such methods have been shown to be effective in reducing criminal behaviour and are themselves quite a strong punishment, as they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">restrict an offender&#x27;s freedom greatly</strong>.\n\nFinally, we should remember that prisons are to some extent ‘<strong class=\"key-phrase\">schools of crime</strong>’ and in many cases prisoners end their jail terms as more capable and determined criminals than when they entered. Of course, it is true that in certain cases we need to both punish criminals and isolate them from other people. I recognise that violent or dangerous people need to be imprisoned – yet I believe this applies to a small minority of instances.\n\nTo conclude, I feel that prison is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a last resort</strong> in cases where there is no alternative. This does not make it the only effective punishment, but rather the most severe among a variety of possibilities.","phrases":[{"en":"non-custodial means","context":"Firstly, I do not accept that all criminals need to be separated from society. Juvenile and minor offenders can be punished through fines, community service or other non-custodial means. This is cheaper than prison, and prevents the damage to the criminal's family which in itself could generate further social problems."},{"en":"restrict an offender's freedom greatly","context":"There is a second benefit. By keeping these small-scale criminals in society, we can try to rehabilitate them, for example through training or education programmes. This will help to reduce re-offending in future. Besides this, if offenders are believed to be a threat to their fellow citizens, there are ways of containing them such as electronic tagging, curfews or regular reporting to the police. Such methods have been shown to be effective in reducing criminal behaviour and are themselves quite a strong punishment, as they restrict an offender's freedom greatly."},{"en":"schools of crime","context":"Finally, we should remember that prisons are to some extent ‘schools of crime’ and in many cases prisoners end their jail terms as more capable and determined criminals than when they entered. Of course, it is true that in certain cases we need to both punish criminals and isolate them from other people. I recognise that violent or dangerous people need to be imprisoned – yet I believe this applies to a small minority of instances."},{"en":"a last resort","context":"To conclude, I feel that prison is a last resort in cases where there is no alternative. This does not make it the only effective punishment, but rather the most severe among a variety of possibilities."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#081","summary":"“Prison is the only truly effective form of punishment, because it…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-020","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Is corporal punishment ever acceptable?","prompt":"Although it is generally prohibited, corporal punishment persists in many families. Do you think corporal punishment is an acceptable way to regulate children's behaviour?","answer":"Corporal punishment is still used in some families because it can stop bad behaviour immediately. However, I do not believe that hitting a child is an acceptable form of discipline. It may produce short-term obedience, but it creates serious physical, emotional and educational risks.\n\nThe first problem is that physical punishment can easily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">blur the line between discipline and abuse</strong>. A parent who is angry or tired may use more force than intended, and a child has little power to protect himself or herself. Even when no visible injury occurs, repeated punishment can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cause lasting psychological harm</strong>. Children may become anxious, secretive or resentful, rather than learning why their behaviour was wrong.\n\nA second concern is the lesson that physical punishment teaches. When adults solve conflict by hitting, they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">model aggression as a legitimate response</strong> to frustration. Children may then copy this behaviour with siblings or classmates. Fear can also damage trust between parents and children, making honest communication less likely precisely when young people need guidance.\n\nParents still need effective ways to control unacceptable behaviour. The better approach is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">set clear and consistent boundaries</strong>, explain the consequences of breaking them, and use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proportionate measures such as loss of privileges</strong> or additional responsibilities. Praise for good behaviour is equally important because it shows children what they should do, not merely what they should avoid.\n\nIn conclusion, corporal punishment is neither necessary nor defensible. Non-violent forms of discipline can correct behaviour while preserving a child&#x27;s dignity, safety and relationship with the family.","phrases":[{"en":"blur the line between discipline and abuse","context":"The first problem is that physical punishment can easily blur the line between discipline and abuse. A parent who is angry or tired may use more force than intended, and a child has little power to protect himself or herself. Even when no visible injury occurs, repeated punishment can cause lasting psychological harm. Children may become anxious, secretive or resentful, rather than learning why their behaviour was wrong."},{"en":"cause lasting psychological harm","context":"The first problem is that physical punishment can easily blur the line between discipline and abuse. A parent who is angry or tired may use more force than intended, and a child has little power to protect himself or herself. Even when no visible injury occurs, repeated punishment can cause lasting psychological harm. Children may become anxious, secretive or resentful, rather than learning why their behaviour was wrong."},{"en":"model aggression as a legitimate response","context":"A second concern is the lesson that physical punishment teaches. When adults solve conflict by hitting, they model aggression as a legitimate response to frustration. Children may then copy this behaviour with siblings or classmates. Fear can also damage trust between parents and children, making honest communication less likely precisely when young people need guidance."},{"en":"set clear and consistent boundaries","context":"Parents still need effective ways to control unacceptable behaviour. The better approach is to set clear and consistent boundaries, explain the consequences of breaking them, and use proportionate measures such as loss of privileges or additional responsibilities. Praise for good behaviour is equally important because it shows children what they should do, not merely what they should avoid."},{"en":"proportionate measures such as loss of privileges","context":"Parents still need effective ways to control unacceptable behaviour. The better approach is to set clear and consistent boundaries, explain the consequences of breaking them, and use proportionate measures such as loss of privileges or additional responsibilities. Praise for good behaviour is equally important because it shows children what they should do, not merely what they should avoid."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#082","summary":"Although it is generally prohibited, corporal punishment persists in many…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-021","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"At what age is a child responsible?","prompt":"Do you think that parents should be punished if their five-year-old child commits a crime? From what age should children be held responsible for their own behaviour?","answer":"Parents have a major influence on a young child, but punishing them automatically for every offence committed by a five-year-old would be unfair. At that age, children have only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a limited understanding of law and consequences</strong>. In my view, parents should be held accountable only when clear neglect or encouragement of wrongdoing can be proved.\n\nA five-year-old normally copies behaviour rather than forming a deliberate criminal intention. Parents are responsible for supervision, basic discipline and the examples they set at home. If a child repeatedly harms others because adults have ignored serious warning signs, allowed access to dangerous objects or actively encouraged aggression, the parents may reasonably face penalties or compulsory support programmes. This approach recognises <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a duty of reasonable supervision</strong> without treating every childhood mistake as parental misconduct.\n\nHowever, responsibility should gradually move from the parent to the child as judgement develops. A single fixed age cannot reflect every case, but the law can use age bands. Children under about ten should usually receive protection, education and family intervention rather than punishment. Older children can be held increasingly responsible when they understand that an act is wrong and can foresee its consequences. For serious offences, courts should consider maturity, intention and personal circumstances instead of relying only on age.\n\nIn conclusion, parents should not be punished simply because their young child commits an offence. Penalties are justified only where there is evidence of serious neglect or deliberate encouragement. Children themselves should assume <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increasing responsibility as their judgement matures</strong>, with rehabilitation remaining the main aim of the youth justice system.","phrases":[{"en":"a limited understanding of law and consequences","context":"Parents have a major influence on a young child, but punishing them automatically for every offence committed by a five-year-old would be unfair. At that age, children have only a limited understanding of law and consequences. In my view, parents should be held accountable only when clear neglect or encouragement of wrongdoing can be proved."},{"en":"a duty of reasonable supervision","context":"A five-year-old normally copies behaviour rather than forming a deliberate criminal intention. Parents are responsible for supervision, basic discipline and the examples they set at home. If a child repeatedly harms others because adults have ignored serious warning signs, allowed access to dangerous objects or actively encouraged aggression, the parents may reasonably face penalties or compulsory support programmes. This approach recognises a duty of reasonable supervision without treating every childhood mistake as parental misconduct."},{"en":"increasing responsibility as their judgement matures","context":"In conclusion, parents should not be punished simply because their young child commits an offence. Penalties are justified only where there is evidence of serious neglect or deliberate encouragement. Children themselves should assume increasing responsibility as their judgement matures, with rehabilitation remaining the main aim of the youth justice system."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#083","summary":"Do you think that parents should be punished if their five-year-old child…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-022","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Why crime among young people is rising","prompt":"In recent years, the proportion of crimes committed by young people has risen. What are the causes of this problem, and what solutions can you suggest?","answer":"Youth crime usually has several causes rather than one simple explanation. In my view, the most important factors are unstable family life, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repeated exposure to violence</strong> and a lack of realistic opportunities. Effective solutions must therefore combine early support with education and employment.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">The breakdown of stable family relationships</strong> can leave young people without consistent supervision or clear boundaries. Parents who work long hours may have little time to notice changes in behaviour, while conflict or abuse at home can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">normalise aggression</strong>. A second factor is repeated exposure to violence through neighbourhood crime, social media or certain forms of entertainment. This does not automatically turn a teenager into an offender, but it can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce sensitivity to harm</strong> and make risky behaviour appear ordinary. Finally, some young people see <strong class=\"key-phrase\">few legal routes to status or income</strong>, especially when they struggle at school and cannot find work.\n\nThe most effective response is early intervention. Schools should identify persistent absence, aggression and learning difficulties before they develop into more serious problems. Counselling and family-support services can help parents establish routines and manage conflict. After-school programmes are also valuable because sport, music and supervised study give teenagers a structured alternative to spending time on the street.\n\nGovernments and employers should also provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">realistic routes into education and employment</strong>. Paid apprenticeships, vocational courses and entry-level jobs can give young people both practical skills and a reason to plan for the future. Serious offences still require proportionate punishment, but detention alone is unlikely to prevent reoffending unless it includes education and rehabilitation.\n\nIn conclusion, youth crime is driven mainly by weak support, exposure to violence and limited opportunity. Prevention is most successful when families, schools and employers act before criminal behaviour becomes established.","phrases":[{"en":"repeated exposure to violence","context":"Youth crime usually has several causes rather than one simple explanation. In my view, the most important factors are unstable family life, repeated exposure to violence and a lack of realistic opportunities. Effective solutions must therefore combine early support with education and employment."},{"en":"The breakdown of stable family relationships","context":"The breakdown of stable family relationships can leave young people without consistent supervision or clear boundaries. Parents who work long hours may have little time to notice changes in behaviour, while conflict or abuse at home can normalise aggression. A second factor is repeated exposure to violence through neighbourhood crime, social media or certain forms of entertainment. This does not automatically turn a teenager into an offender, but it can reduce sensitivity to harm and make risky behaviour appear ordinary. Finally, some young people see few legal routes to status or income, especially when they struggle at school and cannot find work."},{"en":"normalise aggression","context":"The breakdown of stable family relationships can leave young people without consistent supervision or clear boundaries. Parents who work long hours may have little time to notice changes in behaviour, while conflict or abuse at home can normalise aggression. A second factor is repeated exposure to violence through neighbourhood crime, social media or certain forms of entertainment. This does not automatically turn a teenager into an offender, but it can reduce sensitivity to harm and make risky behaviour appear ordinary. Finally, some young people see few legal routes to status or income, especially when they struggle at school and cannot find work."},{"en":"reduce sensitivity to harm","context":"The breakdown of stable family relationships can leave young people without consistent supervision or clear boundaries. Parents who work long hours may have little time to notice changes in behaviour, while conflict or abuse at home can normalise aggression. A second factor is repeated exposure to violence through neighbourhood crime, social media or certain forms of entertainment. This does not automatically turn a teenager into an offender, but it can reduce sensitivity to harm and make risky behaviour appear ordinary. Finally, some young people see few legal routes to status or income, especially when they struggle at school and cannot find work."},{"en":"few legal routes to status or income","context":"The breakdown of stable family relationships can leave young people without consistent supervision or clear boundaries. Parents who work long hours may have little time to notice changes in behaviour, while conflict or abuse at home can normalise aggression. A second factor is repeated exposure to violence through neighbourhood crime, social media or certain forms of entertainment. This does not automatically turn a teenager into an offender, but it can reduce sensitivity to harm and make risky behaviour appear ordinary. Finally, some young people see few legal routes to status or income, especially when they struggle at school and cannot find work."},{"en":"realistic routes into education and employment","context":"Governments and employers should also provide realistic routes into education and employment. Paid apprenticeships, vocational courses and entry-level jobs can give young people both practical skills and a reason to plan for the future. Serious offences still require proportionate punishment, but detention alone is unlikely to prevent reoffending unless it includes education and rehabilitation."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#084","summary":"In recent years, the proportion of the crimes committed by youngsters is on…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-023","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Punishment and road safety","prompt":"The only way to improve road safety is to impose severe punishments for driving offences. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that harsh penalties for driving offences are the only effective means of making roads safer. I agree that punishment plays an important part, but I disagree that it is the only solution, since several other measures work at least as well.\n\nSevere penalties certainly influence behaviour. Most dangerous driving is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a calculated risk</strong> rather than an accident, and a driver who expects to lose their licence for speeding or drink-driving is far less likely to do either. Countries that introduced heavy fines and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">automatic disqualification for drink-driving</strong> recorded immediate falls in fatalities. It is worth noting, however, that what deters people is the likelihood of being caught rather than the size of the penalty, which is why cameras and roadside testing matter more than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the length of a sentence</strong>.\n\nOther approaches have been equally effective. Road design prevents accidents rather than punishing them, and measures such as roundabouts, separated cycle lanes, lower urban speed limits and crash barriers have reduced deaths substantially. Vehicle technology has contributed as well, since seatbelts, airbags and automatic braking save lives <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regardless of how a driver behaves</strong>. Education plays a part too, particularly for young men, who are involved in a disproportionate share of serious crashes and who often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overestimate their own competence behind the wheel</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that offenders should face real consequences, and that penalties for drink-driving in particular should be severe. I disagree, however, that punishment is the only route to safer roads, since consistent enforcement, safer design and improved vehicles are equally necessary.","phrases":[{"en":"a calculated risk","context":"Severe penalties certainly influence behaviour. Most dangerous driving is a calculated risk rather than an accident, and a driver who expects to lose their licence for speeding or drink-driving is far less likely to do either. Countries that introduced heavy fines and automatic disqualification for drink-driving recorded immediate falls in fatalities. It is worth noting, however, that what deters people is the likelihood of being caught rather than the size of the penalty, which is why cameras and roadside testing matter more than the length of a sentence."},{"en":"automatic disqualification for drink-driving","context":"Severe penalties certainly influence behaviour. Most dangerous driving is a calculated risk rather than an accident, and a driver who expects to lose their licence for speeding or drink-driving is far less likely to do either. Countries that introduced heavy fines and automatic disqualification for drink-driving recorded immediate falls in fatalities. It is worth noting, however, that what deters people is the likelihood of being caught rather than the size of the penalty, which is why cameras and roadside testing matter more than the length of a sentence."},{"en":"the length of a sentence","context":"Severe penalties certainly influence behaviour. Most dangerous driving is a calculated risk rather than an accident, and a driver who expects to lose their licence for speeding or drink-driving is far less likely to do either. Countries that introduced heavy fines and automatic disqualification for drink-driving recorded immediate falls in fatalities. It is worth noting, however, that what deters people is the likelihood of being caught rather than the size of the penalty, which is why cameras and roadside testing matter more than the length of a sentence."},{"en":"regardless of how a driver behaves","context":"Other approaches have been equally effective. Road design prevents accidents rather than punishing them, and measures such as roundabouts, separated cycle lanes, lower urban speed limits and crash barriers have reduced deaths substantially. Vehicle technology has contributed as well, since seatbelts, airbags and automatic braking save lives regardless of how a driver behaves. Education plays a part too, particularly for young men, who are involved in a disproportionate share of serious crashes and who often overestimate their own competence behind the wheel."},{"en":"overestimate their own competence behind the wheel","context":"Other approaches have been equally effective. Road design prevents accidents rather than punishing them, and measures such as roundabouts, separated cycle lanes, lower urban speed limits and crash barriers have reduced deaths substantially. Vehicle technology has contributed as well, since seatbelts, airbags and automatic braking save lives regardless of how a driver behaves. Education plays a part too, particularly for young men, who are involved in a disproportionate share of serious crashes and who often overestimate their own competence behind the wheel."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#085","summary":"Punishment and road safety"},{"id":"writing-task-2-024","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Can crime be prevented?","prompt":"Many people are afraid to leave their homes because of crime. Some believe that more can be done to prevent crime, while others think that little can be done. What is your opinion?","answer":"Rising crime has made many people reluctant to leave their homes, and there is disagreement about whether anything can realistically be done about it. In my opinion <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the pessimistic view is mistaken</strong>, because the evidence from countries that have successfully reduced crime is fairly clear.\n\nThe most effective measures address the causes rather than the offence itself. Most crime is committed by young men with poor education, no employment and often an addiction problem, so programmes that keep teenagers in school, provide training and treat addiction reduce offending far more than longer sentences do. Countries that invested in these areas have seen crime fall substantially over the past twenty years, while those relying on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imprisonment alone</strong> have not.\n\nPractical prevention also works. Better street lighting, secure locks, alarm systems and visible policing all reduce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opportunistic crime</strong>, which is the majority of it. Vehicle theft has almost disappeared in many countries simply because manufacturers improved the locks and immobilisers, and that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">required no change in anyone&#x27;s behaviour at all</strong>.\n\nIndividuals can contribute too, although I would be careful here. Taking sensible precautions is reasonable, but the suggestion that victims are negligent, or that people should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">confront criminals themselves</strong>, is both unfair and dangerous. Reporting offences and cooperating with the police is the useful contribution an ordinary citizen can make.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that little can be done about crime. The figures fall reliably when governments tackle poverty and addiction, when opportunities to offend are designed out of everyday life, and when the public is willing to report what it sees.","phrases":[{"en":"the pessimistic view is mistaken","context":"Rising crime has made many people reluctant to leave their homes, and there is disagreement about whether anything can realistically be done about it. In my opinion the pessimistic view is mistaken, because the evidence from countries that have successfully reduced crime is fairly clear."},{"en":"imprisonment alone","context":"The most effective measures address the causes rather than the offence itself. Most crime is committed by young men with poor education, no employment and often an addiction problem, so programmes that keep teenagers in school, provide training and treat addiction reduce offending far more than longer sentences do. Countries that invested in these areas have seen crime fall substantially over the past twenty years, while those relying on imprisonment alone have not."},{"en":"opportunistic crime","context":"Practical prevention also works. Better street lighting, secure locks, alarm systems and visible policing all reduce opportunistic crime, which is the majority of it. Vehicle theft has almost disappeared in many countries simply because manufacturers improved the locks and immobilisers, and that required no change in anyone's behaviour at all."},{"en":"required no change in anyone's behaviour at all","context":"Practical prevention also works. Better street lighting, secure locks, alarm systems and visible policing all reduce opportunistic crime, which is the majority of it. Vehicle theft has almost disappeared in many countries simply because manufacturers improved the locks and immobilisers, and that required no change in anyone's behaviour at all."},{"en":"confront criminals themselves","context":"Individuals can contribute too, although I would be careful here. Taking sensible precautions is reasonable, but the suggestion that victims are negligent, or that people should confront criminals themselves, is both unfair and dangerous. Reporting offences and cooperating with the police is the useful contribution an ordinary citizen can make."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#086","summary":"Can crime be prevented?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-026","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Should the police carry guns?","prompt":"Police in Britain do not carry guns. Some people believe that unarmed police are unable to protect citizens, but some suggest that arming the police will lead to a surge in violence in society. Discuss those two views.","answer":"Whether police officers should routinely carry guns depends on the level of risk in a particular society. Supporters argue that firearms allow officers to confront dangerous criminals, while opponents fear that routine arming would increase violence. I believe that most officers should remain unarmed, although specially trained units should have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strictly regulated access to firearms</strong>.\n\nThe main argument for arming the police is protection. Officers may face criminals who already possess guns, and an unarmed response can place both the police and the public at risk. The visible possibility of an armed response may also deter some offenders. In areas where gun crime is common, it would be unrealistic to expect officers to deal with every incident without suitable protection.\n\nHowever, routine arming can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise the risk of fatal mistakes</strong>. Police officers often make decisions in seconds, sometimes in poor light or under intense pressure. A weapon can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">escalate otherwise manageable encounters</strong> and turn a misunderstanding into a death. Widespread firearms may also change the relationship between the police and the public, making ordinary citizens feel that they are dealing with a military force rather than a civil service.\n\nA balanced policy is therefore preferable. Ordinary patrol officers can remain unarmed, while specialist teams receive advanced training and respond to incidents involving firearms or terrorism. Clear rules should govern when weapons may be used, and every shooting should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">subject to independent investigation</strong>. Officers also need strong training in negotiation, restraint and de-escalation.\n\nIn conclusion, armed police are necessary in exceptional high-risk situations, but routine arming would create avoidable dangers. Limited access, specialist training and strict accountability offer better protection than giving every officer a gun.","phrases":[{"en":"strictly regulated access to firearms","context":"Whether police officers should routinely carry guns depends on the level of risk in a particular society. Supporters argue that firearms allow officers to confront dangerous criminals, while opponents fear that routine arming would increase violence. I believe that most officers should remain unarmed, although specially trained units should have strictly regulated access to firearms."},{"en":"raise the risk of fatal mistakes","context":"However, routine arming can raise the risk of fatal mistakes. Police officers often make decisions in seconds, sometimes in poor light or under intense pressure. A weapon can escalate otherwise manageable encounters and turn a misunderstanding into a death. Widespread firearms may also change the relationship between the police and the public, making ordinary citizens feel that they are dealing with a military force rather than a civil service."},{"en":"escalate otherwise manageable encounters","context":"However, routine arming can raise the risk of fatal mistakes. Police officers often make decisions in seconds, sometimes in poor light or under intense pressure. A weapon can escalate otherwise manageable encounters and turn a misunderstanding into a death. Widespread firearms may also change the relationship between the police and the public, making ordinary citizens feel that they are dealing with a military force rather than a civil service."},{"en":"subject to independent investigation","context":"A balanced policy is therefore preferable. Ordinary patrol officers can remain unarmed, while specialist teams receive advanced training and respond to incidents involving firearms or terrorism. Clear rules should govern when weapons may be used, and every shooting should be subject to independent investigation. Officers also need strong training in negotiation, restraint and de-escalation."}],"subtype":"Problems / Solutions","_displayNo":"#087","summary":"Police in Britain do not carry guns."},{"id":"writing-task-2-027","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Are criminals born or made?","prompt":"Some scientists believe that a child's future criminal behaviour can be predicted from their behaviour at a very young age. To what extent is crime a product of human nature, and can children be prevented from growing up to be criminals?","answer":"Research suggesting that difficult behaviour in very young children predicts later offending has led some people to conclude that criminals are born rather than made. In my opinion inherited temperament plays a real but limited part, and the environment in which a child grows up matters far more.\n\nThere is some evidence for the biological argument. Traits such as impulsiveness, aggression and an <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inability to feel guilt</strong> appear early, are partly inherited, and are found much more often among repeat offenders than in the general population. It would be dishonest to pretend that every child begins life with the same disposition.\n\nHowever, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a tendency is not a destiny</strong>. The great majority of impulsive or aggressive three-year-olds never commit a crime, and offending <strong class=\"key-phrase\">correlates far more strongly with</strong> poverty, neglect, family breakdown, poor schooling and drug addiction than with temperament. This is why crime rates differ so sharply between neighbourhoods and between countries, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">differences that genetics cannot possibly explain</strong>.\n\nIt follows that prevention is realistic. Programmes that support struggling parents, identify learning difficulties early, keep teenagers in education and provide activities in deprived areas have repeatedly been shown to reduce later offending, and they cost <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a small fraction of what imprisonment costs</strong>. A child with a difficult temperament who receives consistent guidance usually learns to control it.\n\nIn conclusion, human nature contributes to criminal behaviour but does not determine it. Since circumstances are what turn a difficult child into an offender, I believe most criminality can be prevented by acting early enough.","phrases":[{"en":"inability to feel guilt","context":"There is some evidence for the biological argument. Traits such as impulsiveness, aggression and an inability to feel guilt appear early, are partly inherited, and are found much more often among repeat offenders than in the general population. It would be dishonest to pretend that every child begins life with the same disposition."},{"en":"a tendency is not a destiny","context":"However, a tendency is not a destiny. The great majority of impulsive or aggressive three-year-olds never commit a crime, and offending correlates far more strongly with poverty, neglect, family breakdown, poor schooling and drug addiction than with temperament. This is why crime rates differ so sharply between neighbourhoods and between countries, differences that genetics cannot possibly explain."},{"en":"correlates far more strongly with","context":"However, a tendency is not a destiny. The great majority of impulsive or aggressive three-year-olds never commit a crime, and offending correlates far more strongly with poverty, neglect, family breakdown, poor schooling and drug addiction than with temperament. This is why crime rates differ so sharply between neighbourhoods and between countries, differences that genetics cannot possibly explain."},{"en":"differences that genetics cannot possibly explain","context":"However, a tendency is not a destiny. The great majority of impulsive or aggressive three-year-olds never commit a crime, and offending correlates far more strongly with poverty, neglect, family breakdown, poor schooling and drug addiction than with temperament. This is why crime rates differ so sharply between neighbourhoods and between countries, differences that genetics cannot possibly explain."},{"en":"a small fraction of what imprisonment costs","context":"It follows that prevention is realistic. Programmes that support struggling parents, identify learning difficulties early, keep teenagers in education and provide activities in deprived areas have repeatedly been shown to reduce later offending, and they cost a small fraction of what imprisonment costs. A child with a difficult temperament who receives consistent guidance usually learns to control it."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#088","summary":"Are criminals born or made?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-028","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Security measures in large cities","prompt":"Large cities are introducing increasingly extensive security measures in order to reduce crime. Do the benefits of this outweigh the drawbacks?","answer":"Cities around the world now rely on street cameras, facial recognition, data monitoring and larger police patrols to control crime. In my view these measures bring real benefits, but the drawbacks are serious enough to require strict limits.\n\nThe advantages are straightforward. Surveillance deters offenders, because most crimes are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">committed opportunistically</strong> and the likelihood of being recorded changes that calculation. Cameras also supply the evidence that secures convictions, and they have proved particularly valuable in investigating violent attacks, where witnesses are frightened, absent or simply unreliable. Ordinary residents benefit too: people are far more willing to use public transport or walk home at night in areas they believe are monitored, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps city centres alive after dark</strong>.\n\nThe problems, however, are significant. Constant monitoring of law-abiding citizens is an intrusion into private life, and the data collected can easily be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">misused</strong>, whether by governments tracking political opponents or by criminals who obtain it through hacking. Facial recognition systems are also known to make more errors with certain ethnic groups, which risks wrongful arrest. There is a further practical objection: much of this technology <strong class=\"key-phrase\">displaces crime</strong> into unmonitored areas rather than preventing it, and the same money might reduce offending more effectively if it were spent on education and drug treatment.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe the benefits are greater, since these measures genuinely reduce crime and allow ordinary people to move around their own city without fear. They are only acceptable, however, where the data is tightly regulated, held for a short period and open to independent inspection.","phrases":[{"en":"committed opportunistically","context":"The advantages are straightforward. Surveillance deters offenders, because most crimes are committed opportunistically and the likelihood of being recorded changes that calculation. Cameras also supply the evidence that secures convictions, and they have proved particularly valuable in investigating violent attacks, where witnesses are frightened, absent or simply unreliable. Ordinary residents benefit too: people are far more willing to use public transport or walk home at night in areas they believe are monitored, which keeps city centres alive after dark."},{"en":"keeps city centres alive after dark","context":"The advantages are straightforward. Surveillance deters offenders, because most crimes are committed opportunistically and the likelihood of being recorded changes that calculation. Cameras also supply the evidence that secures convictions, and they have proved particularly valuable in investigating violent attacks, where witnesses are frightened, absent or simply unreliable. Ordinary residents benefit too: people are far more willing to use public transport or walk home at night in areas they believe are monitored, which keeps city centres alive after dark."},{"en":"misused","context":"The problems, however, are significant. Constant monitoring of law-abiding citizens is an intrusion into private life, and the data collected can easily be misused, whether by governments tracking political opponents or by criminals who obtain it through hacking. Facial recognition systems are also known to make more errors with certain ethnic groups, which risks wrongful arrest. There is a further practical objection: much of this technology displaces crime into unmonitored areas rather than preventing it, and the same money might reduce offending more effectively if it were spent on education and drug treatment."},{"en":"displaces crime","context":"The problems, however, are significant. Constant monitoring of law-abiding citizens is an intrusion into private life, and the data collected can easily be misused, whether by governments tracking political opponents or by criminals who obtain it through hacking. Facial recognition systems are also known to make more errors with certain ethnic groups, which risks wrongful arrest. There is a further practical objection: much of this technology displaces crime into unmonitored areas rather than preventing it, and the same money might reduce offending more effectively if it were spent on education and drug treatment."}],"subtype":"Benefits & Drawbacks","_displayNo":"#089","summary":"Security measures in large cities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-059","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Prison or rehabilitation?","prompt":"Some people believe that criminals should be sent to prison, while others think they should receive education and job training instead. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"When someone is convicted of a crime, society must decide how to protect the public while reducing the likelihood of further offending. Some people favour imprisonment, whereas others prefer education and job training. In my opinion, these approaches should be combined according to the seriousness of the offence and the individual&#x27;s circumstances.\n\nThe strongest argument for prison is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">public protection</strong>. Someone who poses a serious danger may need to be detained, and a substantial offence should have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meaningful consequences</strong>. Imprisonment can also demonstrate that the law recognises the harm suffered by victims. However, the need for punishment does not by itself explain what should happen during a sentence or how a person should prepare for release.\n\nEducation and training address a different part of the problem. Some offenders face difficulties with literacy, qualifications or finding <strong class=\"key-phrase\">legitimate employment</strong>. If these barriers remain unchanged, release may return them to the same circumstances that contributed to their offending. Teaching reading, supporting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">recognised qualifications</strong> and helping people develop practical work skills can make lawful employment more realistic. The value of a programme should be judged by its quality and results rather than by the assumption that any classroom activity will prevent crime.\n\nI would therefore reserve imprisonment for cases where the seriousness of the offence or the risk to others justifies it. Suitable lower-risk offenders could receive <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supervised community sentences</strong> with relevant support. In prison, education should be accompanied by planning for housing, treatment where needed and employment after release. These measures do not excuse the original offence; they aim to prevent another victim being harmed.\n\nIn conclusion, prison and rehabilitation serve different but compatible purposes. A fair system should impose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">proportionate consequences</strong> while giving offenders a realistic opportunity to return to society without committing further crimes.","phrases":[{"en":"public protection","context":"The strongest argument for prison is public protection. Someone who poses a serious danger may need to be detained, and a substantial offence should have meaningful consequences. Imprisonment can also demonstrate that the law recognises the harm suffered by victims. However, the need for punishment does not by itself explain what should happen during a sentence or how a person should prepare for release."},{"en":"meaningful consequences","context":"The strongest argument for prison is public protection. Someone who poses a serious danger may need to be detained, and a substantial offence should have meaningful consequences. Imprisonment can also demonstrate that the law recognises the harm suffered by victims. However, the need for punishment does not by itself explain what should happen during a sentence or how a person should prepare for release."},{"en":"legitimate employment","context":"Education and training address a different part of the problem. Some offenders face difficulties with literacy, qualifications or finding legitimate employment. If these barriers remain unchanged, release may return them to the same circumstances that contributed to their offending. Teaching reading, supporting recognised qualifications and helping people develop practical work skills can make lawful employment more realistic. The value of a programme should be judged by its quality and results rather than by the assumption that any classroom activity will prevent crime."},{"en":"recognised qualifications","context":"Education and training address a different part of the problem. Some offenders face difficulties with literacy, qualifications or finding legitimate employment. If these barriers remain unchanged, release may return them to the same circumstances that contributed to their offending. Teaching reading, supporting recognised qualifications and helping people develop practical work skills can make lawful employment more realistic. The value of a programme should be judged by its quality and results rather than by the assumption that any classroom activity will prevent crime."},{"en":"supervised community sentences","context":"I would therefore reserve imprisonment for cases where the seriousness of the offence or the risk to others justifies it. Suitable lower-risk offenders could receive supervised community sentences with relevant support. In prison, education should be accompanied by planning for housing, treatment where needed and employment after release. These measures do not excuse the original offence; they aim to prevent another victim being harmed."},{"en":"proportionate consequences","context":"In conclusion, prison and rehabilitation serve different but compatible purposes. A fair system should impose proportionate consequences while giving offenders a realistic opportunity to return to society without committing further crimes."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#090","summary":"Prison or rehabilitation?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Crime is falling, so why do people feel unsafe?","prompt":"In some countries, even though the rates of serious crimes are decreasing, people feel less safe than ever before. What do you think are the causes of this problem and what measures could be taken to solve it?","answer":"People can feel less safe even when serious crime is falling. This apparent contradiction is often caused by intensive news coverage, online rumours and visible signs of disorder. Governments should respond with accurate information as well as effective policing.\n\nModern media reports violent incidents repeatedly because they attract attention. A crime that once appeared in a local newspaper may now circulate nationally through video and social media. As a result, people judge risk by the examples they remember rather than by long-term statistics. This can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">create a distorted sense of danger</strong>, since a single alarming clip can reach millions of people who never encounter the actual location.\n\nA second cause is what people actually see around them. Broken street lighting, graffiti, abandoned buildings, litter and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">low-level antisocial behaviour</strong> all signal that a place is not looked after and that nobody is in charge of it. None of this appears in serious-crime statistics, yet it is what residents encounter every day, and a street that feels neglected is read as a dangerous one long before anything happens there.\n\nThe response should address both causes. Local authorities can repair lighting, clear graffiti quickly and maintain parks and estates, since a well-kept area is judged safer and is in fact easier to police. The same reasoning applies to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">visible community policing</strong>: officers who are known by name in a neighbourhood reassure residents in a way that patrol cars cannot. Police forces should also publish local crime figures in plain language, showing the trend over several years alongside the month&#x27;s incidents, because accessible data is the surest way to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce the gap between perceived and actual risk</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, falling crime rates do not automatically produce a feeling of safety. Public confidence depends on the condition of neighbourhoods and on information people can trust, and a combination of visible local action and honest communication is the most effective response.","topic":"Crime & Punishment","phrases":[{"en":"create a distorted sense of danger","context":"Modern media reports violent incidents repeatedly because they attract attention. A crime that once appeared in a local newspaper may now circulate nationally through video and social media. As a result, people judge risk by the examples they remember rather than by long-term statistics. This can create a distorted sense of danger, since a single alarming clip can reach millions of people who never encounter the actual location."},{"en":"low-level antisocial behaviour","context":"A second cause is what people actually see around them. Broken street lighting, graffiti, abandoned buildings, litter and low-level antisocial behaviour all signal that a place is not looked after and that nobody is in charge of it. None of this appears in serious-crime statistics, yet it is what residents encounter every day, and a street that feels neglected is read as a dangerous one long before anything happens there."},{"en":"visible community policing","context":"The response should address both causes. Local authorities can repair lighting, clear graffiti quickly and maintain parks and estates, since a well-kept area is judged safer and is in fact easier to police. The same reasoning applies to visible community policing: officers who are known by name in a neighbourhood reassure residents in a way that patrol cars cannot. Police forces should also publish local crime figures in plain language, showing the trend over several years alongside the month's incidents, because accessible data is the surest way to reduce the gap between perceived and actual risk."},{"en":"reduce the gap between perceived and actual risk","context":"The response should address both causes. Local authorities can repair lighting, clear graffiti quickly and maintain parks and estates, since a well-kept area is judged safer and is in fact easier to police. The same reasoning applies to visible community policing: officers who are known by name in a neighbourhood reassure residents in a way that patrol cars cannot. Police forces should also publish local crime figures in plain language, showing the trend over several years alongside the month's incidents, because accessible data is the surest way to reduce the gap between perceived and actual risk."}],"subtype":"problem","summary":"Why people feel less safe even as serious crime falls","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-107","_displayNo":"#091"},{"id":"writing-task-2-167","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Cybercrime and what can be done about it","prompt":"Many people today are worried about ‘cybercrime’ such as hacking and identity theft. What problems does ‘cybercrime’ cause, and what solutions can you suggest for ordinary people and businesses to take? Give reasons for your answer, and provide ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"Cybercrime is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cause of widespread concern</strong> today, as so many people use technology to store and transmit sensitive data. There are a number of problems that people suffer as a result of these crimes, but also some straightforward measures we can take to protect ourselves. Perhaps the worst problem stemming from this is the financial impact. If someone has his or her bank account emptied, or has false credit lines created under their name, they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">potentially liable for large sums of money</strong>. Even if they can avoid the consequences, their bank or finance company has to cover the losses, which can damage their performance.\n\nThese losses do not stay contained, either: once stolen money enters a criminal network, it can just as easily fund drug trafficking or terrorism as ordinary theft, and by the time it has passed through several accounts there is no realistic way to trace where it has gone. Victims rarely emerge unscathed even when a bank eventually refunds the loss. Months of disputed transactions, replacement documents and the fear of it happening again take a genuine toll on people who did nothing to invite it.\n\nThe most effective response starts with individuals. Strong, unique passwords and the simple habit of shredding old bank statements <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deny criminals the raw material they need</strong> in the first place. Public education matters just as much, since scammers deliberately target the elderly and the very young; campaigns that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise awareness of the risks</strong> in schools and community centres can close off an easy source of victims before a crime even happens. The greatest responsibility, though, lies with banks and technology firms, whose fraud-detection systems and closer cooperation with the police can stop an attack before a customer notices anything is wrong.\n\nOverall, the problems caused are both financial and social, and the solutions should involve coordinated action by individuals, corporations and the state.","phrases":[{"en":"a cause of widespread concern","context":"Cybercrime is a cause of widespread concern today, as so many people use technology to store and transmit sensitive data. There are a number of problems that people suffer as a result of these crimes, but also some straightforward measures we can take to protect ourselves. Perhaps the worst problem stemming from this is the financial impact. If someone has his or her bank account emptied, or has false credit lines created under their name, they are potentially liable for large sums of money. Even if they can avoid the consequences, their bank or finance company has to cover the losses, which can damage their performance."},{"en":"potentially liable for large sums of money","context":"Cybercrime is a cause of widespread concern today, as so many people use technology to store and transmit sensitive data. There are a number of problems that people suffer as a result of these crimes, but also some straightforward measures we can take to protect ourselves. Perhaps the worst problem stemming from this is the financial impact. If someone has his or her bank account emptied, or has false credit lines created under their name, they are potentially liable for large sums of money. Even if they can avoid the consequences, their bank or finance company has to cover the losses, which can damage their performance."},{"en":"deny criminals the raw material they need","context":"The most effective response starts with individuals. Strong, unique passwords and the simple habit of shredding old bank statements deny criminals the raw material they need in the first place. Public education matters just as much, since scammers deliberately target the elderly and the very young; campaigns that raise awareness of the risks in schools and community centres can close off an easy source of victims before a crime even happens. The greatest responsibility, though, lies with banks and technology firms, whose fraud-detection systems and closer cooperation with the police can stop an attack before a customer notices anything is wrong."},{"en":"raise awareness of the risks","context":"The most effective response starts with individuals. Strong, unique passwords and the simple habit of shredding old bank statements deny criminals the raw material they need in the first place. Public education matters just as much, since scammers deliberately target the elderly and the very young; campaigns that raise awareness of the risks in schools and community centres can close off an easy source of victims before a crime even happens. The greatest responsibility, though, lies with banks and technology firms, whose fraud-detection systems and closer cooperation with the police can stop an attack before a customer notices anything is wrong."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#092","summary":"People today are worried about ‘cybercrime’ such as hacking and identity…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2205","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Will technology reduce crime?","prompt":"Crime rates are likely to decline due to the advancements in technology, which will help to prevent and solve crimes in an easier way. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often predicted that advances in technology will make crime easier to prevent and to solve, and that offending will therefore fall. I accept that technology has made certain traditional crimes far more difficult, but I disagree with the wider claim, because the same developments have created entirely new forms of criminal activity.\n\nThere is no doubt that technology assists the police. Street cameras, number-plate recognition and mobile phone records make it much harder to commit a burglary or a robbery without being identified, and DNA analysis has allowed investigators to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">solve cases once considered hopeless</strong>. Banks now use automated systems that detect unusual transactions instantly. Rates of car theft and street crime have indeed fallen sharply in many countries over the past twenty years, and better security technology is a major reason for this.\n\nHowever, criminals adopt new technology just as quickly as the authorities do. Fraud, identity theft and online scams have grown enormously, and in several countries these offences now outnumber all traditional crimes combined. Such crimes are extremely difficult to investigate, because the offender may be in another country, may be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hidden behind encryption</strong>, and may target thousands of victims at once. Overall crime figures have not fallen; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the crime has simply moved</strong>.\n\nIt is also worth remembering that technology does not address the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">underlying causes of crime</strong>, which are usually poverty, addiction, poor education and inequality. Cameras may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">displace offending</strong> rather than prevent it.\n\nIn conclusion, technology has clearly reduced some categories of crime, but I do not believe it will produce a general decline in crime rates.","phrases":[{"en":"solve cases once considered hopeless","context":"There is no doubt that technology assists the police. Street cameras, number-plate recognition and mobile phone records make it much harder to commit a burglary or a robbery without being identified, and DNA analysis has allowed investigators to solve cases once considered hopeless. Banks now use automated systems that detect unusual transactions instantly. Rates of car theft and street crime have indeed fallen sharply in many countries over the past twenty years, and better security technology is a major reason for this."},{"en":"hidden behind encryption","context":"However, criminals adopt new technology just as quickly as the authorities do. Fraud, identity theft and online scams have grown enormously, and in several countries these offences now outnumber all traditional crimes combined. Such crimes are extremely difficult to investigate, because the offender may be in another country, may be hidden behind encryption, and may target thousands of victims at once. Overall crime figures have not fallen; the crime has simply moved."},{"en":"the crime has simply moved","context":"However, criminals adopt new technology just as quickly as the authorities do. Fraud, identity theft and online scams have grown enormously, and in several countries these offences now outnumber all traditional crimes combined. Such crimes are extremely difficult to investigate, because the offender may be in another country, may be hidden behind encryption, and may target thousands of victims at once. Overall crime figures have not fallen; the crime has simply moved."},{"en":"underlying causes of crime","context":"It is also worth remembering that technology does not address the underlying causes of crime, which are usually poverty, addiction, poor education and inequality. Cameras may displace offending rather than prevent it."},{"en":"displace offending","context":"It is also worth remembering that technology does not address the underlying causes of crime, which are usually poverty, addiction, poor education and inequality. Cameras may displace offending rather than prevent it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#093","summary":"Will technology reduce crime?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2229","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Why released prisoners reoffend","prompt":"Many criminals commit further crimes as soon as they are released from prison. What do you think are the causes of this? What possible solutions can you suggest?","answer":"In many countries around half of those released from prison are convicted again within two years. The causes lie in what happens on release rather than in the sentence itself, and they can be addressed by treating release as a process rather than an event.\n\nThe first cause is that prisoners leave with nothing. A person released with a small discharge grant, no address and no job has to solve housing, income and identity documents in the same week, and returning to crime is often the only route they know to money. A criminal record then closes the legitimate routes: employers reject applicants who declare a conviction, so the harder someone tries to find lawful work, the more <strong class=\"key-phrase\">firmly the door is closed</strong>.\n\nA second cause is that prison rarely changes the conditions that produced the offence. Most prisoners entered with addiction, untreated mental illness or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">literacy below the level of an eleven-year-old</strong>, and a sentence spent locked in a cell for twenty hours a day <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves all of that untouched</strong>. Released into the same streets and the same company, they face exactly the situation that led to conviction.\n\nThe solutions follow. Housing should be arranged before the release date rather than after it, since almost nothing else can be sustained without an address. Treatment for addiction and teaching of basic literacy and a trade should occupy the sentence, because a released prisoner who can read and hold a job has something to lose. Limiting how long employers may consider minor convictions would also remove a needless obstacle.\n\nIn conclusion, reoffending is driven by release without housing, work or treatment. Prisons that prepare people for the outside during the sentence, rather than simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opening the gate on the final day</strong>, achieve markedly lower rates.","phrases":[{"en":"firmly the door is closed","context":"The first cause is that prisoners leave with nothing. A person released with a small discharge grant, no address and no job has to solve housing, income and identity documents in the same week, and returning to crime is often the only route they know to money. A criminal record then closes the legitimate routes: employers reject applicants who declare a conviction, so the harder someone tries to find lawful work, the more firmly the door is closed."},{"en":"literacy below the level of an eleven-year-old","context":"A second cause is that prison rarely changes the conditions that produced the offence. Most prisoners entered with addiction, untreated mental illness or literacy below the level of an eleven-year-old, and a sentence spent locked in a cell for twenty hours a day leaves all of that untouched. Released into the same streets and the same company, they face exactly the situation that led to conviction."},{"en":"leaves all of that untouched","context":"A second cause is that prison rarely changes the conditions that produced the offence. Most prisoners entered with addiction, untreated mental illness or literacy below the level of an eleven-year-old, and a sentence spent locked in a cell for twenty hours a day leaves all of that untouched. Released into the same streets and the same company, they face exactly the situation that led to conviction."},{"en":"opening the gate on the final day","context":"In conclusion, reoffending is driven by release without housing, work or treatment. Prisons that prepare people for the outside during the sentence, rather than simply opening the gate on the final day, achieve markedly lower rates."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#094","summary":"Causes of reoffending after release from prison and possible solutions"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2281","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Should young offenders be treated as adults?","prompt":"Some people believe that young people who commit serious crimes should be punished in the same way as adults. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"When a teenager commits a violent crime, the demand that age should make no difference is understandable. I disagree with it, however, because the reasons we punish adults as we do apply only partly to the young.\n\nThe argument for equal treatment rests on the harm done. A victim is no less injured because the attacker was sixteen, and a system that hands down light sentences for serious offences looks unjust to the family involved. There is also a fear that lenient courts will be exploited, with older criminals <strong class=\"key-phrase\">using teenagers to carry out the work</strong>. These points deserve a serious answer rather than dismissal.\n\nThe answer is that responsibility depends on judgement, and judgement is not complete at sixteen. The parts of the brain governing impulse and consequence continue developing into the mid-twenties, which is why young people offend in groups, on impulse and with little thought of what follows. Most also stop: the great majority of teenage offenders commit no crimes as adults, so a long sentence punishes a person who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">would have grown out of it anyway</strong>. Prison makes that worse, since a young man placed among experienced criminals learns from them and leaves with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no qualifications and a permanent record</strong>.\n\nThis is not an argument for doing nothing. Serious crimes should carry serious consequences, including detention where the public must be protected. The difference is that a young offender&#x27;s sentence should be built around education, treatment and supervision, and courts should retain the power to punish the most extreme cases severely.\n\nIn conclusion, treating children exactly as adults ignores what is known about how they think and how they change. Firm but separate treatment, aimed at stopping the next offence rather than matching the last one, protects the public more effectively.","phrases":[{"en":"using teenagers to carry out the work","context":"The argument for equal treatment rests on the harm done. A victim is no less injured because the attacker was sixteen, and a system that hands down light sentences for serious offences looks unjust to the family involved. There is also a fear that lenient courts will be exploited, with older criminals using teenagers to carry out the work. These points deserve a serious answer rather than dismissal."},{"en":"would have grown out of it anyway","context":"The answer is that responsibility depends on judgement, and judgement is not complete at sixteen. The parts of the brain governing impulse and consequence continue developing into the mid-twenties, which is why young people offend in groups, on impulse and with little thought of what follows. Most also stop: the great majority of teenage offenders commit no crimes as adults, so a long sentence punishes a person who would have grown out of it anyway. Prison makes that worse, since a young man placed among experienced criminals learns from them and leaves with no qualifications and a permanent record."},{"en":"no qualifications and a permanent record","context":"The answer is that responsibility depends on judgement, and judgement is not complete at sixteen. The parts of the brain governing impulse and consequence continue developing into the mid-twenties, which is why young people offend in groups, on impulse and with little thought of what follows. Most also stop: the great majority of teenage offenders commit no crimes as adults, so a long sentence punishes a person who would have grown out of it anyway. Prison makes that worse, since a young man placed among experienced criminals learns from them and leaves with no qualifications and a permanent record."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#095","summary":"Whether young people who commit serious crimes deserve adult punishment"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2353","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Crime & Punishment","title":"Prison or community service?","prompt":"Some people believe that all offenders should be sent to prison, while others argue that there are more effective alternatives for those who commit minor crimes, such as community service. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Prisons in most countries hold more people than they were built for, and a large share of those people are serving short sentences for minor offences. My own view is that imprisonment should be reserved for the dangerous, with community penalties used for the rest.\n\nThe argument for imprisoning everyone rests on consistency and deterrence. If minor offences bring no serious consequence, the law appears optional, and victims of theft or vandalism reasonably feel that a few hours of unpaid work does not answer what was done to them. Prison also removes the offender from the streets, which is the one thing that stops the behaviour <strong class=\"key-phrase\">for as long as the sentence lasts</strong>.\n\nThe case for alternatives is supported by what happens afterwards. Short sentences are long enough to cost a person their job, their housing and often their family, and short enough to teach them nothing, so they leave with fewer reasons to stay honest than when they arrived. Prison also functions as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an education in crime supplied at public expense</strong>, since a first-time offender spends months among experienced ones. Reoffending rates after community sentences are consistently lower, and the cost is a fraction of a prison place.\n\nCommunity penalties also do something imprisonment cannot: they keep the offender working and paying tax, allow treatment for addiction or mental illness where that is the real cause, and let the debt be repaid visibly to the neighbourhood that was harmed. The essential condition is that they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supervised strictly enough to be taken seriously</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, prison remains necessary for violent and repeat offenders, and no system can do without it. For minor crimes it is expensive, damaging and largely ineffective, so penalties that keep people in work and under supervision protect the public more successfully.","phrases":[{"en":"for as long as the sentence lasts","context":"The argument for imprisoning everyone rests on consistency and deterrence. If minor offences bring no serious consequence, the law appears optional, and victims of theft or vandalism reasonably feel that a few hours of unpaid work does not answer what was done to them. Prison also removes the offender from the streets, which is the one thing that stops the behaviour for as long as the sentence lasts."},{"en":"an education in crime supplied at public expense","context":"The case for alternatives is supported by what happens afterwards. Short sentences are long enough to cost a person their job, their housing and often their family, and short enough to teach them nothing, so they leave with fewer reasons to stay honest than when they arrived. Prison also functions as an education in crime supplied at public expense, since a first-time offender spends months among experienced ones. Reoffending rates after community sentences are consistently lower, and the cost is a fraction of a prison place."},{"en":"supervised strictly enough to be taken seriously","context":"Community penalties also do something imprisonment cannot: they keep the offender working and paying tax, allow treatment for addiction or mental illness where that is the real cause, and let the debt be repaid visibly to the neighbourhood that was harmed. The essential condition is that they are supervised strictly enough to be taken seriously."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#096","summary":"Whether every offender should be imprisoned or minor crimes handled differently"},{"id":"writing-task-2-029","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Will technology destroy traditional cultures?","prompt":"It is inevitable that traditional cultures will be lost as technology develops. Technology and traditional cultures are incompatible. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this view?","answer":"Some people believe that technological developments lead to the loss of traditional cultures. I partly agree with this assertion; while it may be true in the case of some societies, others seem to be unaffected by technology and the modern world.\n\nOn the one hand, the advances in technology that have driven industrialisation in developed countries have certainly contributed to the disappearance of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">traditional ways of life</strong>. For example, in pre-industrial Britain, generations of families grew up in the same small village communities. These communities had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a strong sense of identity</strong>, due to their shared customs and beliefs. However, developments in transport, communications and manufacturing led to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the dispersal of families</strong> and village communities as people moved to the cities in search of work. Nowadays most British villages are inhabited by commuters, many of whom do not know their closest neighbours.\n\nOn the other hand, in some parts of the world traditional cultures still thrive. There are tribes in the Amazon Rainforest, for example, that have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completely untouched by the technological developments</strong> of the developed world. These tribal communities continue to hunt and gather food from the forest, and traditional skills are passed on to children by parents and elders. Other traditional cultures, such as farming communities in parts of Africa, are embracing communications technologies. Mobile phones give farmers access to information, from weather predictions to market prices, which helps them to prosper and therefore supports their culture.\n\nIn conclusion, many traditional ways of life have been lost as a result of advances in technology, but other traditional communities have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">survived and even flourished</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"traditional ways of life","context":"On the one hand, the advances in technology that have driven industrialisation in developed countries have certainly contributed to the disappearance of traditional ways of life. For example, in pre-industrial Britain, generations of families grew up in the same small village communities. These communities had a strong sense of identity, due to their shared customs and beliefs. However, developments in transport, communications and manufacturing led to the dispersal of families and village communities as people moved to the cities in search of work. Nowadays most British villages are inhabited by commuters, many of whom do not know their closest neighbours."},{"en":"a strong sense of identity","context":"On the one hand, the advances in technology that have driven industrialisation in developed countries have certainly contributed to the disappearance of traditional ways of life. For example, in pre-industrial Britain, generations of families grew up in the same small village communities. These communities had a strong sense of identity, due to their shared customs and beliefs. However, developments in transport, communications and manufacturing led to the dispersal of families and village communities as people moved to the cities in search of work. Nowadays most British villages are inhabited by commuters, many of whom do not know their closest neighbours."},{"en":"the dispersal of families","context":"On the one hand, the advances in technology that have driven industrialisation in developed countries have certainly contributed to the disappearance of traditional ways of life. For example, in pre-industrial Britain, generations of families grew up in the same small village communities. These communities had a strong sense of identity, due to their shared customs and beliefs. However, developments in transport, communications and manufacturing led to the dispersal of families and village communities as people moved to the cities in search of work. Nowadays most British villages are inhabited by commuters, many of whom do not know their closest neighbours."},{"en":"completely untouched by the technological developments","context":"On the other hand, in some parts of the world traditional cultures still thrive. There are tribes in the Amazon Rainforest, for example, that have been completely untouched by the technological developments of the developed world. These tribal communities continue to hunt and gather food from the forest, and traditional skills are passed on to children by parents and elders. Other traditional cultures, such as farming communities in parts of Africa, are embracing communications technologies. Mobile phones give farmers access to information, from weather predictions to market prices, which helps them to prosper and therefore supports their culture."},{"en":"survived and even flourished","context":"In conclusion, many traditional ways of life have been lost as a result of advances in technology, but other traditional communities have survived and even flourished."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#097","summary":"Technology and traditional cultures"},{"id":"writing-task-2-036","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Does travel build understanding?","prompt":"Some people believe that increased travel between countries helps people to learn about different cultures. Others argue that it increases tension between people from different countries. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"International travel has increased enormously, and opinions differ about its effect on relations between nations. Some argue that it builds understanding, while others believe it creates friction. In my view the outcome depends almost entirely on how people travel.\n\nThere is good reason to think travel promotes understanding. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Prejudice usually survives on ignorance</strong>, and meeting people directly tends to dismantle it: a visitor who is helped by strangers in a country they had been taught to fear finds <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the stereotype difficult to maintain</strong>. Travel also creates practical connections through trade, study and friendship, and countries whose citizens visit each other frequently are historically <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less likely to go to war</strong>.\n\nThe opposite case is not without foundation. Mass tourism can breed resentment when visitors ignore local customs, push up house prices and treat a community as an attraction rather than a home, which is why residents of cities such as Barcelona and Venice have protested. Tourists who stay inside resorts and never meet local people learn nothing, and encounters limited to buying and selling can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reinforce prejudice rather than reduce it</strong>.\n\nMy own view is that the difference lies in the depth of contact. A traveller who learns some of the language, eats where local people eat and stays long enough to form relationships almost always returns with more understanding. Someone who spends a week beside a hotel pool does not.\n\nIn conclusion, travel has real potential to reduce hostility, but only where visitors engage seriously with the places they visit.","phrases":[{"en":"Prejudice usually survives on ignorance","context":"There is good reason to think travel promotes understanding. Prejudice usually survives on ignorance, and meeting people directly tends to dismantle it: a visitor who is helped by strangers in a country they had been taught to fear finds the stereotype difficult to maintain. Travel also creates practical connections through trade, study and friendship, and countries whose citizens visit each other frequently are historically less likely to go to war."},{"en":"the stereotype difficult to maintain","context":"There is good reason to think travel promotes understanding. Prejudice usually survives on ignorance, and meeting people directly tends to dismantle it: a visitor who is helped by strangers in a country they had been taught to fear finds the stereotype difficult to maintain. Travel also creates practical connections through trade, study and friendship, and countries whose citizens visit each other frequently are historically less likely to go to war."},{"en":"less likely to go to war","context":"There is good reason to think travel promotes understanding. Prejudice usually survives on ignorance, and meeting people directly tends to dismantle it: a visitor who is helped by strangers in a country they had been taught to fear finds the stereotype difficult to maintain. Travel also creates practical connections through trade, study and friendship, and countries whose citizens visit each other frequently are historically less likely to go to war."},{"en":"reinforce prejudice rather than reduce it","context":"The opposite case is not without foundation. Mass tourism can breed resentment when visitors ignore local customs, push up house prices and treat a community as an attraction rather than a home, which is why residents of cities such as Barcelona and Venice have protested. Tourists who stay inside resorts and never meet local people learn nothing, and encounters limited to buying and selling can reinforce prejudice rather than reduce it."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#098","summary":"Does travel build understanding?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-039","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Should immigrants adopt the local culture?","prompt":"Some people argue that immigrants should adopt the local culture when they move to a new country. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Moving to another country requires immigrants to understand local customs, but it should not require them to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">abandon their own culture</strong>. I partly agree that newcomers need to adapt. They should respect the law and learn the language, while remaining free to preserve harmless traditions and community ties.\n\nAdaptation is essential for practical reasons. A person who can communicate in the local language is more likely to find work, use public services and build relationships outside an immigrant community. Understanding everyday conventions also prevents avoidable conflict. For example, expectations about punctuality, personal space or workplace communication may differ greatly between countries. Learning these norms helps newcomers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">participate fully in public life</strong>.\n\nHowever, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">integration is not the same as cultural surrender</strong>. Family traditions, food, music and religious practice can give immigrants stability during a difficult transition. Minority organisations may also provide language support, childcare and advice for new arrivals. These communities become harmful only when they isolate members from the wider society or reject basic legal principles. In most cases, they can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preserve identity while supporting integration</strong>.\n\nThe host country has responsibilities as well. Schools, employers and public institutions should make participation possible and respond firmly to discrimination. Integration works best when newcomers are expected to contribute but are not treated as inferior for having a different background.\n\nIn conclusion, immigrants should adopt the language, laws and civic expectations of their new country. They should not be required to erase their heritage. A successful multicultural society creates a shared public culture with room for private difference.","phrases":[{"en":"abandon their own culture","context":"Moving to another country requires immigrants to understand local customs, but it should not require them to abandon their own culture. I partly agree that newcomers need to adapt. They should respect the law and learn the language, while remaining free to preserve harmless traditions and community ties."},{"en":"participate fully in public life","context":"Adaptation is essential for practical reasons. A person who can communicate in the local language is more likely to find work, use public services and build relationships outside an immigrant community. Understanding everyday conventions also prevents avoidable conflict. For example, expectations about punctuality, personal space or workplace communication may differ greatly between countries. Learning these norms helps newcomers participate fully in public life."},{"en":"integration is not the same as cultural surrender","context":"However, integration is not the same as cultural surrender. Family traditions, food, music and religious practice can give immigrants stability during a difficult transition. Minority organisations may also provide language support, childcare and advice for new arrivals. These communities become harmful only when they isolate members from the wider society or reject basic legal principles. In most cases, they can preserve identity while supporting integration."},{"en":"preserve identity while supporting integration","context":"However, integration is not the same as cultural surrender. Family traditions, food, music and religious practice can give immigrants stability during a difficult transition. Minority organisations may also provide language support, childcare and advice for new arrivals. These communities become harmful only when they isolate members from the wider society or reject basic legal principles. In most cases, they can preserve identity while supporting integration."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#099","summary":"People argue that immigrants should adopt the local culture when"},{"id":"writing-task-2-042","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Tourism and cultural preservation","prompt":"Some people believe that culture will be ruined if it is used to earn tourism revenue, but others consider that tourism is the only way of protecting a culture. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Tourism now provides much of the income that pays for cultural preservation, and opinion is divided over whether this protects a culture or gradually destroys it. In my view it does both, and which effect dominates depends almost entirely on how the tourism is managed.\n\nThe argument that tourism preserves culture is a strong one. Historic buildings, temples and traditional crafts are expensive to maintain, and without visitor income many would simply have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">left to decay</strong>. Tourism also gives young people a reason to learn traditional skills that would otherwise disappear, because those skills now provide a living. Kyoto and Luang Prabang are both places where the money visitors bring pays directly for conservation work.\n\nThe opposite case is equally clear. When a ceremony that once had religious meaning is performed four times a day for coach parties, something genuine is lost even though the performance continues. Traditional dress and festivals get simplified into whatever photographs well, and local people can end up as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">performers in their own town</strong>. Housing costs also rise until residents move out, leaving a centre that looks authentic but is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inhabited by nobody who belongs there</strong>.\n\nMy own view is that the distinction lies in control. Where local communities own the businesses and decide what is shown, tourism tends to strengthen a culture. Where outside companies run it and residents are merely employed, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the culture becomes a product</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, tourism can either preserve or damage a culture, and the deciding factor is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">who makes the decisions</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"left to decay","context":"The argument that tourism preserves culture is a strong one. Historic buildings, temples and traditional crafts are expensive to maintain, and without visitor income many would simply have been left to decay. Tourism also gives young people a reason to learn traditional skills that would otherwise disappear, because those skills now provide a living. Kyoto and Luang Prabang are both places where the money visitors bring pays directly for conservation work."},{"en":"performers in their own town","context":"The opposite case is equally clear. When a ceremony that once had religious meaning is performed four times a day for coach parties, something genuine is lost even though the performance continues. Traditional dress and festivals get simplified into whatever photographs well, and local people can end up as performers in their own town. Housing costs also rise until residents move out, leaving a centre that looks authentic but is inhabited by nobody who belongs there."},{"en":"inhabited by nobody who belongs there","context":"The opposite case is equally clear. When a ceremony that once had religious meaning is performed four times a day for coach parties, something genuine is lost even though the performance continues. Traditional dress and festivals get simplified into whatever photographs well, and local people can end up as performers in their own town. Housing costs also rise until residents move out, leaving a centre that looks authentic but is inhabited by nobody who belongs there."},{"en":"the culture becomes a product","context":"My own view is that the distinction lies in control. Where local communities own the businesses and decide what is shown, tourism tends to strengthen a culture. Where outside companies run it and residents are merely employed, the culture becomes a product."},{"en":"who makes the decisions","context":"In conclusion, tourism can either preserve or damage a culture, and the deciding factor is who makes the decisions."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#100","summary":"Tourism and cultural preservation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-044","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Traditional dress and national identity","prompt":"People in many countries no longer wear their traditional costume. Some people argue that they will therefore forget their history and traditions. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"In most countries, traditional dress is now worn only at weddings, festivals and official ceremonies. Some people fear that this means a nation&#x27;s history and customs will gradually be forgotten. I disagree with this view, because culture is preserved by far more than clothing.\n\nIt is easy to see why the change has happened. Traditional garments were designed for a way of life that no longer exists. They are often expensive, difficult to clean and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impractical for</strong> commuting, office work or factory shifts, whereas modern clothing is cheap, comfortable and suitable for almost any activity. People have therefore abandoned traditional dress for practical reasons, not because they have lost interest in their heritage.\n\nMore importantly, a nation&#x27;s history is carried by things that are much harder to lose than a style of dress. Language, food, religion, music, festivals, architecture and family customs all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transmit culture from one generation to the next</strong>, and none of them depends on what people wear to work. Vietnam and Japan are good examples: everyday clothing in both countries is almost entirely Western, yet their languages, cuisines and traditional celebrations remain firmly intact.\n\nI would accept that traditional costume has real value and should not disappear completely. Governments and schools can protect it by teaching children its history, wearing it on national holidays and supporting the craftspeople who still make it. Wearing it occasionally, however, is enough to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep the knowledge alive</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the decline of traditional dress <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflects practical convenience rather than cultural loss</strong>, and I do not believe it will cause people to forget their history.","phrases":[{"en":"impractical for","context":"It is easy to see why the change has happened. Traditional garments were designed for a way of life that no longer exists. They are often expensive, difficult to clean and impractical for commuting, office work or factory shifts, whereas modern clothing is cheap, comfortable and suitable for almost any activity. People have therefore abandoned traditional dress for practical reasons, not because they have lost interest in their heritage."},{"en":"transmit culture from one generation to the next","context":"More importantly, a nation's history is carried by things that are much harder to lose than a style of dress. Language, food, religion, music, festivals, architecture and family customs all transmit culture from one generation to the next, and none of them depends on what people wear to work. Vietnam and Japan are good examples: everyday clothing in both countries is almost entirely Western, yet their languages, cuisines and traditional celebrations remain firmly intact."},{"en":"keep the knowledge alive","context":"I would accept that traditional costume has real value and should not disappear completely. Governments and schools can protect it by teaching children its history, wearing it on national holidays and supporting the craftspeople who still make it. Wearing it occasionally, however, is enough to keep the knowledge alive."},{"en":"reflects practical convenience rather than cultural loss","context":"In conclusion, the decline of traditional dress reflects practical convenience rather than cultural loss, and I do not believe it will cause people to forget their history."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#101","summary":"Traditional dress and national identity"},{"id":"writing-task-2-046","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Does contact between countries erode identity?","prompt":"There is a disagreement on the impact of increased business and culture contact between countries on a country's identity. What is your opinion?","answer":"Closer business and cultural contact between countries can weaken certain traditions, but it does not inevitably destroy national identity. In my view, international contact can strengthen rather than erase national identity when governments and communities protect what is distinctive and allow culture to develop naturally.\n\nThe main risk is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cultural homogenisation</strong>. Global companies sell similar products in many countries, international media promote the same celebrities and younger people often adopt foreign fashions or expressions. Under commercial pressure, small local businesses, minority languages and traditional crafts may struggle to survive. If these trends continue without support, visible differences between societies can gradually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become less pronounced</strong>.\n\nHowever, contact with other countries can also increase interest in local culture. Tourism creates demand for regional food, music, architecture and festivals. International audiences may give artists and small producers new markets, while digital platforms allow communities to document and share traditions that were previously known only locally. Exposure to other cultures can even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make people more conscious of their own identity</strong> because comparison shows them what is distinctive about it.\n\nThe outcome therefore depends on policy and public choice. Schools can teach local history and language, governments can support cultural institutions, and planning rules can protect important buildings and neighbourhoods. At the same time, identity should not be treated as something fixed. Cultures have always borrowed ideas from one another, and selective change can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep traditions relevant to younger generations</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, international business and cultural exchange create some pressure towards uniformity, but they also provide resources and audiences for local culture. Countries can preserve distinctive traditions while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">engaging with the world on their own terms</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"cultural homogenisation","context":"The main risk is cultural homogenisation. Global companies sell similar products in many countries, international media promote the same celebrities and younger people often adopt foreign fashions or expressions. Under commercial pressure, small local businesses, minority languages and traditional crafts may struggle to survive. If these trends continue without support, visible differences between societies can gradually become less pronounced."},{"en":"become less pronounced","context":"The main risk is cultural homogenisation. Global companies sell similar products in many countries, international media promote the same celebrities and younger people often adopt foreign fashions or expressions. Under commercial pressure, small local businesses, minority languages and traditional crafts may struggle to survive. If these trends continue without support, visible differences between societies can gradually become less pronounced."},{"en":"make people more conscious of their own identity","context":"However, contact with other countries can also increase interest in local culture. Tourism creates demand for regional food, music, architecture and festivals. International audiences may give artists and small producers new markets, while digital platforms allow communities to document and share traditions that were previously known only locally. Exposure to other cultures can even make people more conscious of their own identity because comparison shows them what is distinctive about it."},{"en":"keep traditions relevant to younger generations","context":"The outcome therefore depends on policy and public choice. Schools can teach local history and language, governments can support cultural institutions, and planning rules can protect important buildings and neighbourhoods. At the same time, identity should not be treated as something fixed. Cultures have always borrowed ideas from one another, and selective change can keep traditions relevant to younger generations."},{"en":"engaging with the world on their own terms","context":"In conclusion, international business and cultural exchange create some pressure towards uniformity, but they also provide resources and audiences for local culture. Countries can preserve distinctive traditions while engaging with the world on their own terms."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#102","summary":"There is a disagreement on the impact of increased business and culture…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-100","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Have festivals lost their meaning?","prompt":"Most people have forgotten the meaning behind traditional or religious festivals; during festival periods, people nowadays only want to enjoy themselves. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?","answer":"Some people argue that we no longer remember <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the original meaning of</strong> festivals, and that most of us treat them as opportunities to have fun. While I agree that enjoyment seems to be the priority during festival times, I do not agree that people have forgotten what these festivals mean.\n\nOn the one hand, religious and traditional festivals have certainly become times for celebration. In the UK, Christmas is a good example of a festival period when people are most concerned with shopping, giving and receiving presents, decorating their homes and enjoying traditional meals with their families. Most people look forward to Christmas as a holiday period, rather than a time to practise religion. Similar behaviour can be seen during non-religious festivals, such as Bonfire Night. People associate this occasion with making fires, watching firework displays, and perhaps going to large events in local parks; in other words, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enjoyment is people&#x27;s primary goal</strong>.\n\nHowever, I disagree with the idea that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the underlying meaning of</strong> such festivals has been forgotten. In UK primary schools, children learn in detail about the religious reasons for celebrating Christmas, Easter and a variety of festivals in other religions. For example, in late December, children sing Christmas songs which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a religious content</strong>, and they may even perform nativity plays telling the story of Jesus’ birth. Families also play a role in passing knowledge of religious festivals’ deeper significance on to the next generation. The same is true for festivals that have a historical background, such as Bonfire Night or Halloween, in the sense that people generally learn the stories behind these occasions at an early age.\n\nIn conclusion, although people mainly want to enjoy themselves during festivals, I believe that they are still aware of the reasons for these celebrations.","phrases":[{"en":"the original meaning of","context":"Some people argue that we no longer remember the original meaning of festivals, and that most of us treat them as opportunities to have fun. While I agree that enjoyment seems to be the priority during festival times, I do not agree that people have forgotten what these festivals mean."},{"en":"enjoyment is people's primary goal","context":"On the one hand, religious and traditional festivals have certainly become times for celebration. In the UK, Christmas is a good example of a festival period when people are most concerned with shopping, giving and receiving presents, decorating their homes and enjoying traditional meals with their families. Most people look forward to Christmas as a holiday period, rather than a time to practise religion. Similar behaviour can be seen during non-religious festivals, such as Bonfire Night. People associate this occasion with making fires, watching firework displays, and perhaps going to large events in local parks; in other words, enjoyment is people's primary goal."},{"en":"the underlying meaning of","context":"However, I disagree with the idea that the underlying meaning of such festivals has been forgotten. In UK primary schools, children learn in detail about the religious reasons for celebrating Christmas, Easter and a variety of festivals in other religions. For example, in late December, children sing Christmas songs which have a religious content, and they may even perform nativity plays telling the story of Jesus’ birth. Families also play a role in passing knowledge of religious festivals’ deeper significance on to the next generation. The same is true for festivals that have a historical background, such as Bonfire Night or Halloween, in the sense that people generally learn the stories behind these occasions at an early age."},{"en":"have a religious content","context":"However, I disagree with the idea that the underlying meaning of such festivals has been forgotten. In UK primary schools, children learn in detail about the religious reasons for celebrating Christmas, Easter and a variety of festivals in other religions. For example, in late December, children sing Christmas songs which have a religious content, and they may even perform nativity plays telling the story of Jesus’ birth. Families also play a role in passing knowledge of religious festivals’ deeper significance on to the next generation. The same is true for festivals that have a historical background, such as Bonfire Night or Halloween, in the sense that people generally learn the stories behind these occasions at an early age."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#103","summary":"The meaning of traditional festivals"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2197","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Giving money instead of gifts","prompt":"In more and more countries, people give money on special occasions rather than personally giving gifts. Why might this be the case? Is it a positive or negative development?","answer":"In many countries it has become normal to give money at weddings, birthdays and religious festivals instead of choosing a present. There are two main reasons for this change, and although it has one obvious drawback, I believe it is a largely positive development.\n\nThe main reason is convenience. People today work long hours and often live far from their relatives, so they simply do not have time to visit shops and select something appropriate, whereas sending money through a banking app takes seconds. A second reason is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the fear of choosing badly</strong>. Modern consumers already own a great deal, and tastes in clothes, technology and decoration are highly personal. Almost everyone has received a present that they never used, and giving cash <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoids this waste entirely</strong>.\n\nI regard the trend as positive, mainly because of what it means for the person receiving the gift. Money allows the recipient to buy exactly what he or she needs, which matters enormously to a young couple furnishing a home or a student paying tuition fees. Nothing is thrown away, and nothing has to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">politely stored in a cupboard</strong>. It is true that a cash transfer can feel impersonal, and that the effort of choosing a present is itself <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sign of affection</strong>. However, this is easily solved by adding a handwritten card or by handing over the money in person.\n\nIn conclusion, people give money because it is quick and reliable, and because they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no longer trust their own judgement</strong>. Despite lacking the personal touch of a carefully chosen present, it is in my view a positive development.","phrases":[{"en":"the fear of choosing badly","context":"The main reason is convenience. People today work long hours and often live far from their relatives, so they simply do not have time to visit shops and select something appropriate, whereas sending money through a banking app takes seconds. A second reason is the fear of choosing badly. Modern consumers already own a great deal, and tastes in clothes, technology and decoration are highly personal. Almost everyone has received a present that they never used, and giving cash avoids this waste entirely."},{"en":"avoids this waste entirely","context":"The main reason is convenience. People today work long hours and often live far from their relatives, so they simply do not have time to visit shops and select something appropriate, whereas sending money through a banking app takes seconds. A second reason is the fear of choosing badly. Modern consumers already own a great deal, and tastes in clothes, technology and decoration are highly personal. Almost everyone has received a present that they never used, and giving cash avoids this waste entirely."},{"en":"politely stored in a cupboard","context":"I regard the trend as positive, mainly because of what it means for the person receiving the gift. Money allows the recipient to buy exactly what he or she needs, which matters enormously to a young couple furnishing a home or a student paying tuition fees. Nothing is thrown away, and nothing has to be politely stored in a cupboard. It is true that a cash transfer can feel impersonal, and that the effort of choosing a present is itself a sign of affection. However, this is easily solved by adding a handwritten card or by handing over the money in person."},{"en":"a sign of affection","context":"I regard the trend as positive, mainly because of what it means for the person receiving the gift. Money allows the recipient to buy exactly what he or she needs, which matters enormously to a young couple furnishing a home or a student paying tuition fees. Nothing is thrown away, and nothing has to be politely stored in a cupboard. It is true that a cash transfer can feel impersonal, and that the effort of choosing a present is itself a sign of affection. However, this is easily solved by adding a handwritten card or by handing over the money in person."},{"en":"no longer trust their own judgement","context":"In conclusion, people give money because it is quick and reliable, and because they no longer trust their own judgement. Despite lacking the personal touch of a carefully chosen present, it is in my view a positive development."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#104","summary":"Giving money instead of gifts"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2228","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Cities that look alike","prompt":"In the past, people used to travel abroad to look for many differences from their home country. Nowadays, cities throughout the world are becoming more and more similar. What are the reasons? Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"A traveller arriving in a large city today is likely to find the same coffee chains, the same clothing shops and the same glass towers as at home. This convergence has clear commercial causes, and in my view its disadvantages outweigh its benefits.\n\nThe main reason is the reach of international business. A successful retailer opens in fifty countries with an identical shopfront, because a recognisable brand is precisely what customers are paying for. Construction has followed the same path: the materials, engineering methods and even the architects behind a modern office tower are international, so a new district in Jakarta is designed by much the same process as one in Frankfurt. Local authorities encourage this, since attracting a known company or a famous architect is treated as evidence that a city has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arrived on the world stage</strong>.\n\nThere are advantages. Familiarity lowers the barrier to travel for people who find unfamiliar places intimidating, and international standards have brought better plumbing, safer buildings and more reliable transport to cities that previously lacked them.\n\nThe losses are heavier, however. Distinctive local architecture, markets and trades are what gave a place its character, and once a historic quarter is replaced by a shopping centre it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be recreated</strong>. Tourism suffers directly, because visitors travel to see what they cannot see at home, and a city that looks like everywhere else gives them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no particular reason to come</strong>. Local businesses also lose the ground on which they competed.\n\nIn conclusion, cities are converging because commerce and construction now operate globally. On balance the disadvantages are greater, since the practical gains could be achieved without demolishing what made each city <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth visiting in the first place</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"arrived on the world stage","context":"The main reason is the reach of international business. A successful retailer opens in fifty countries with an identical shopfront, because a recognisable brand is precisely what customers are paying for. Construction has followed the same path: the materials, engineering methods and even the architects behind a modern office tower are international, so a new district in Jakarta is designed by much the same process as one in Frankfurt. Local authorities encourage this, since attracting a known company or a famous architect is treated as evidence that a city has arrived on the world stage."},{"en":"cannot be recreated","context":"The losses are heavier, however. Distinctive local architecture, markets and trades are what gave a place its character, and once a historic quarter is replaced by a shopping centre it cannot be recreated. Tourism suffers directly, because visitors travel to see what they cannot see at home, and a city that looks like everywhere else gives them no particular reason to come. Local businesses also lose the ground on which they competed."},{"en":"no particular reason to come","context":"The losses are heavier, however. Distinctive local architecture, markets and trades are what gave a place its character, and once a historic quarter is replaced by a shopping centre it cannot be recreated. Tourism suffers directly, because visitors travel to see what they cannot see at home, and a city that looks like everywhere else gives them no particular reason to come. Local businesses also lose the ground on which they competed."},{"en":"worth visiting in the first place","context":"In conclusion, cities are converging because commerce and construction now operate globally. On balance the disadvantages are greater, since the practical gains could be achieved without demolishing what made each city worth visiting in the first place."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Advantages outweigh disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#105","summary":"Why cities around the world are becoming more similar"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2232","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Culture & Traditions","title":"Is family history worth researching?","prompt":"In many parts of the world, people do research on their family history. Some people believe that finding out about previous generations is a useful thing to do. However, others believe that it is better to focus on present and future generations. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Tracing ancestors has become a widespread hobby, helped by digitised records and inexpensive testing. Some regard it as valuable self-knowledge while others see it as time better spent on the living, and I believe both positions hold in different circumstances.\n\nThe case for researching the past begins with identity. Knowing that a great-grandmother crossed an ocean alone at nineteen, or that a family survived a famine, gives a person a sense of where their own situation sits in a longer story, which can be steadying during difficult periods. There are practical returns as well. Family records reveal inherited conditions such as heart disease or certain cancers, and a doctor who knows that history can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">screen for it far earlier</strong>. For communities separated by migration or conflict, this research is also the only way of recovering what was lost.\n\nThose who disagree point out that the past cannot be changed while the present can. Hours spent in archives are hours not spent with a grandparent who is still alive and whose memories will disappear with them, and there is something uncomfortable about a person who is proud of an ancestor&#x27;s achievements while having <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contributed nothing of their own</strong>.\n\nMy view is that the objection applies to the extreme case rather than to the activity. Most people research a few evenings a year, which displaces very little, and the results often bring living relatives back into contact rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drawing attention away from them</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, family history is worth pursuing for the medical and personal understanding it offers, provided it does not become a substitute for present relationships. The healthiest approach treats it as a way of understanding the family that is still here.","phrases":[{"en":"screen for it far earlier","context":"The case for researching the past begins with identity. Knowing that a great-grandmother crossed an ocean alone at nineteen, or that a family survived a famine, gives a person a sense of where their own situation sits in a longer story, which can be steadying during difficult periods. There are practical returns as well. Family records reveal inherited conditions such as heart disease or certain cancers, and a doctor who knows that history can screen for it far earlier. For communities separated by migration or conflict, this research is also the only way of recovering what was lost."},{"en":"contributed nothing of their own","context":"Those who disagree point out that the past cannot be changed while the present can. Hours spent in archives are hours not spent with a grandparent who is still alive and whose memories will disappear with them, and there is something uncomfortable about a person who is proud of an ancestor's achievements while having contributed nothing of their own."},{"en":"drawing attention away from them","context":"My view is that the objection applies to the extreme case rather than to the activity. Most people research a few evenings a year, which displaces very little, and the results often bring living relatives back into contact rather than drawing attention away from them."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#106","summary":"Researching previous generations compared with focusing on the present"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Putting economic growth above everything else","prompt":"In many countries, the government prioritises economic growth above all other concerns. Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of this.","answer":"Economic growth is necessary for jobs, public revenue and better living standards, but it should not be the government&#x27;s only priority. A country can become richer while its environment, health system and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social trust deteriorate</strong>. Governments should therefore pursue growth alongside broader measures of progress.\n\nThe benefits of a growing economy are clear. Successful businesses create employment, wages rise and the state collects more tax. This money can fund schools, hospitals and infrastructure. In poorer countries, sustained growth can also reduce extreme poverty and give families access to basic services. For these reasons, governments cannot simply ignore economic performance.\n\nHowever, growth can produce serious costs if it depends on pollution, unsafe work or extreme inequality. Higher national income means little to people who cannot afford housing or healthcare. Governments should evaluate whether growth improves ordinary lives, not only whether production increases. Measures such as life expectancy, educational outcomes, air quality and income distribution provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fuller picture of national progress</strong>.\n\nThe central question is how the gains are distributed. When productivity rises across an economy, ordinary households usually see it in wages as well as taxes; but when growth is driven by a handful of highly automated or extractive industries, profits can rise sharply while employment and average pay barely move, as has happened in some oil-exporting and heavily automated economies. Governments therefore need environmental limits and social investment so that higher output <strong class=\"key-phrase\">translates into a broader improvement in living standards</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, economic growth should remain an important objective, but not the objective that overrides every other concern. The best policy is growth that is environmentally sustainable and widely shared, so that higher output translates into better lives.","topic":"Economy & Money","phrases":[{"en":"social trust deteriorate","context":"Economic growth is necessary for jobs, public revenue and better living standards, but it should not be the government's only priority. A country can become richer while its environment, health system and social trust deteriorate. Governments should therefore pursue growth alongside broader measures of progress."},{"en":"a fuller picture of national progress","context":"However, growth can produce serious costs if it depends on pollution, unsafe work or extreme inequality. Higher national income means little to people who cannot afford housing or healthcare. Governments should evaluate whether growth improves ordinary lives, not only whether production increases. Measures such as life expectancy, educational outcomes, air quality and income distribution provide a fuller picture of national progress."},{"en":"translates into a broader improvement in living standards","context":"The central question is how the gains are distributed. When productivity rises across an economy, ordinary households usually see it in wages as well as taxes; but when growth is driven by a handful of highly automated or extractive industries, profits can rise sharply while employment and average pay barely move, as has happened in some oil-exporting and heavily automated economies. Governments therefore need environmental limits and social investment so that higher output translates into a broader improvement in living standards."}],"subtype":"advdis","summary":"Governments that put economic growth above all other concerns","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-106","_displayNo":"#107"},{"id":"writing-task-2-142","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Saving money or spending it","prompt":"Some people think that it is best to save money, for example in a bank or savings scheme. Other people feel that money should be spent whenever it is available. Discuss these views and reach an opinion on this debate. Give reasons for your answer, and support your essay with ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"The question of whether to save or spend money is very topical today, as so many people are struggling with financial worries. There are valid arguments on both sides, but I believe that putting money aside whenever possible is the wiser course.\n\nOn the one hand, it is often said that spending money is one way to relax and enjoy life, for instance by shopping or taking holidays, and there is certainly some truth in this. We also have little choice but to spend a large proportion of our salary on day to day expenses such as food, housing and transport, so there is surprisingly little left to save anyway. Spending on products and services also boosts the economy and creates employment, which benefits everyone.\n\nOn the other hand, there are real dangers in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reckless spending</strong>, particularly on impulse purchases financed by credit. Advertising and, more recently, buy-now-pay-later schemes make it very easy to commit future income without noticing, and young people who have never had to manage a budget are especially vulnerable to this.\n\nThe stronger argument for saving, however, is security. Without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reserve of funds for emergencies</strong>, a single period of unemployment or illness leaves people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">putting themselves at risk of hardship</strong> or even bankruptcy, which is why financial advisers commonly suggest keeping three months of expenses in reserve. Saving also allows people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accumulate wealth</strong> over a lifetime, so that their children inherit money or property which will increase their security and standard of living.\n\nOverall, I tend to agree that it is wiser to save money wherever possible, for reasons which benefit both individuals today and future generations.","phrases":[{"en":"reckless spending","context":"On the other hand, there are real dangers in reckless spending, particularly on impulse purchases financed by credit. Advertising and, more recently, buy-now-pay-later schemes make it very easy to commit future income without noticing, and young people who have never had to manage a budget are especially vulnerable to this."},{"en":"a reserve of funds for emergencies","context":"The stronger argument for saving, however, is security. Without a reserve of funds for emergencies, a single period of unemployment or illness leaves people putting themselves at risk of hardship or even bankruptcy, which is why financial advisers commonly suggest keeping three months of expenses in reserve. Saving also allows people to accumulate wealth over a lifetime, so that their children inherit money or property which will increase their security and standard of living."},{"en":"putting themselves at risk of hardship","context":"The stronger argument for saving, however, is security. Without a reserve of funds for emergencies, a single period of unemployment or illness leaves people putting themselves at risk of hardship or even bankruptcy, which is why financial advisers commonly suggest keeping three months of expenses in reserve. Saving also allows people to accumulate wealth over a lifetime, so that their children inherit money or property which will increase their security and standard of living."},{"en":"accumulate wealth","context":"The stronger argument for saving, however, is security. Without a reserve of funds for emergencies, a single period of unemployment or illness leaves people putting themselves at risk of hardship or even bankruptcy, which is why financial advisers commonly suggest keeping three months of expenses in reserve. Saving also allows people to accumulate wealth over a lifetime, so that their children inherit money or property which will increase their security and standard of living."}],"subtype":"Discussion","_displayNo":"#108","summary":"People think that it is best to save money, for example in a bank or…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2208","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Wealth and satisfaction in rich countries","prompt":"Some experts believe that when a country is already rich, any additional increase in economic wealth does not make its citizens any more satisfied. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some economists argue that once a nation has reached a comfortable standard of living, further increases in national wealth do little to make its people happier. I largely agree with this position, although I would add that how the additional wealth is used matters a great deal.\n\nThe evidence for the argument is fairly strong. Once basic needs such as food, housing, healthcare and education have been met, extra income tends to be spent on goods that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">provide only temporary satisfaction</strong>. People quickly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become accustomed to</strong> a larger house or a newer car, and then begin comparing themselves with those who have more, so the sense of improvement disappears. Several wealthy countries, including Japan and the United States, have seen national income rise substantially over recent decades with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no corresponding rise in reported life satisfaction</strong>.\n\nThe reason is that the things which make life genuinely satisfying are not usually purchased. Secure relationships, good health, meaningful work, free time and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of belonging</strong> determine well-being far more than income does. Indeed, the pursuit of higher earnings often damages precisely these things, because it demands longer hours and greater stress, leaving people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wealthier but more exhausted and more isolated</strong> than before.\n\nI would qualify this argument in one respect. Additional national wealth can still improve people&#x27;s lives if it is deliberately directed towards reducing inequality, shortening working hours or improving public healthcare. What fails to increase satisfaction is simply raising average consumption.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that greater economic wealth does little for the citizens of an already prosperous country, unless that wealth is used to improve the conditions in which people actually live.","phrases":[{"en":"provide only temporary satisfaction","context":"The evidence for the argument is fairly strong. Once basic needs such as food, housing, healthcare and education have been met, extra income tends to be spent on goods that provide only temporary satisfaction. People quickly become accustomed to a larger house or a newer car, and then begin comparing themselves with those who have more, so the sense of improvement disappears. Several wealthy countries, including Japan and the United States, have seen national income rise substantially over recent decades with no corresponding rise in reported life satisfaction."},{"en":"become accustomed to","context":"The evidence for the argument is fairly strong. Once basic needs such as food, housing, healthcare and education have been met, extra income tends to be spent on goods that provide only temporary satisfaction. People quickly become accustomed to a larger house or a newer car, and then begin comparing themselves with those who have more, so the sense of improvement disappears. Several wealthy countries, including Japan and the United States, have seen national income rise substantially over recent decades with no corresponding rise in reported life satisfaction."},{"en":"no corresponding rise in reported life satisfaction","context":"The evidence for the argument is fairly strong. Once basic needs such as food, housing, healthcare and education have been met, extra income tends to be spent on goods that provide only temporary satisfaction. People quickly become accustomed to a larger house or a newer car, and then begin comparing themselves with those who have more, so the sense of improvement disappears. Several wealthy countries, including Japan and the United States, have seen national income rise substantially over recent decades with no corresponding rise in reported life satisfaction."},{"en":"a sense of belonging","context":"The reason is that the things which make life genuinely satisfying are not usually purchased. Secure relationships, good health, meaningful work, free time and a sense of belonging determine well-being far more than income does. Indeed, the pursuit of higher earnings often damages precisely these things, because it demands longer hours and greater stress, leaving people wealthier but more exhausted and more isolated than before."},{"en":"wealthier but more exhausted and more isolated","context":"The reason is that the things which make life genuinely satisfying are not usually purchased. Secure relationships, good health, meaningful work, free time and a sense of belonging determine well-being far more than income does. Indeed, the pursuit of higher earnings often damages precisely these things, because it demands longer hours and greater stress, leaving people wealthier but more exhausted and more isolated than before."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#109","summary":"Wealth and satisfaction in rich countries"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2221","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Are credit cards worth the risk?","prompt":"Nowadays it is easy to apply for and be given a credit card. However, some people experience problems when they are not able to pay their debt back. In your opinion, do the advantages of credit cards outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Credit cards are now issued almost automatically to anyone in regular employment, and while some borrowers fall into debt they cannot repay, I believe the benefits still outweigh the harm for most users.\n\nThe central advantage is that a card separates when money is needed from when it arrives. A boiler that fails in January cannot wait until payday, and a household without savings would otherwise have to borrow from a lender charging far more. Used this way, a card is a cheap form of short-term insurance rather than a way of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">living beyond one&#x27;s means</strong>.\n\nCards also offer protection that cash does not. If goods are never delivered or a company collapses, the card provider is jointly liable and the money is returned, whereas cash handed over is simply gone. Paying by card leaves a record too, which makes tracking spending straightforward and gives young adults a credit history, without which renting a flat or obtaining a mortgage becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unnecessarily difficult</strong>.\n\nThe danger is genuine and should not be minimised. Interest of twenty per cent or more compounds quickly, and because the minimum repayment is deliberately small, a borrower who pays only that amount can service a debt for years while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">barely reducing the balance</strong>. Lenders have not always been careful about whom they approve, and those with the least income are pushed hardest.\n\nIn conclusion, the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, since a card used sensibly is a convenience and a safeguard. The harm comes not from the card itself but from careless lending and from borrowers who have never been taught how interest works, and both can be addressed by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stricter affordability checks</strong> and basic financial education in schools.","phrases":[{"en":"living beyond one's means","context":"The central advantage is that a card separates when money is needed from when it arrives. A boiler that fails in January cannot wait until payday, and a household without savings would otherwise have to borrow from a lender charging far more. Used this way, a card is a cheap form of short-term insurance rather than a way of living beyond one's means."},{"en":"unnecessarily difficult","context":"Cards also offer protection that cash does not. If goods are never delivered or a company collapses, the card provider is jointly liable and the money is returned, whereas cash handed over is simply gone. Paying by card leaves a record too, which makes tracking spending straightforward and gives young adults a credit history, without which renting a flat or obtaining a mortgage becomes unnecessarily difficult."},{"en":"barely reducing the balance","context":"The danger is genuine and should not be minimised. Interest of twenty per cent or more compounds quickly, and because the minimum repayment is deliberately small, a borrower who pays only that amount can service a debt for years while barely reducing the balance. Lenders have not always been careful about whom they approve, and those with the least income are pushed hardest."},{"en":"stricter affordability checks","context":"In conclusion, the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, since a card used sensibly is a convenience and a safeguard. The harm comes not from the card itself but from careless lending and from borrowers who have never been taught how interest works, and both can be addressed by stricter affordability checks and basic financial education in schools."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#110","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of easy access to credit cards"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2296","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Development and the values it costs","prompt":"Many countries aim to improve living standards through economic development, but some important social values are lost as a result. Do the advantages of economic development outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Rapid development transforms what a country can offer its citizens and also changes how they live together. I believe the advantages outweigh the losses, though the losses are real and are felt most by those who did not choose the change.\n\nThe gains are measurable and enormous. Development brings clean water, electricity, vaccination and schooling, and the result is visible in child mortality that falls by more than half within a generation. It offers choice as well: young people can work outside farming, women can earn independently, and families are no longer confined to the village they were born in. Governments also gain the revenue that funds hospitals, pensions and courts, and it is worth remembering that the values romanticised in poor societies came with early death and rigid hierarchy.\n\nThe costs are nonetheless genuine. Extended families disperse as work moves to cities, so the care that grandparents and neighbours once provided is replaced by services people must buy. Communities that shared work and celebrations become collections of households with little contact. Time is measured differently, since hospitality and obligation give way to schedules, and a competitive economy encourages people to compare rather than to help. Traditional crafts, dialects and festivals fade because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nobody has the hours to maintain them</strong>.\n\nThe important question is whether the losses are unavoidable, and largely they are not. Some countries have developed while protecting rest days, family time, language and public space, whereas others allowed the market to decide everything. What destroys social values is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the speed and the absence of any policy</strong> rather than prosperity itself.\n\nIn conclusion, development delivers health, education and freedom that no traditional society provided, and few who have gained them wish to return. The social costs deserve attention, so governments should pursue growth while deliberately protecting the time and places that hold communities together.","phrases":[{"en":"nobody has the hours to maintain them","context":"The costs are nonetheless genuine. Extended families disperse as work moves to cities, so the care that grandparents and neighbours once provided is replaced by services people must buy. Communities that shared work and celebrations become collections of households with little contact. Time is measured differently, since hospitality and obligation give way to schedules, and a competitive economy encourages people to compare rather than to help. Traditional crafts, dialects and festivals fade because nobody has the hours to maintain them."},{"en":"the speed and the absence of any policy","context":"The important question is whether the losses are unavoidable, and largely they are not. Some countries have developed while protecting rest days, family time, language and public space, whereas others allowed the market to decide everything. What destroys social values is usually the speed and the absence of any policy rather than prosperity itself."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#111","summary":"Whether economic development is worth the social values that are lost"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2344","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Is wealth mainly a chance to help others?","prompt":"Some people think that the most important thing about being rich is that it gives a person the opportunity to help other people. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Wealthy individuals are frequently praised for their donations, and the idea that this is the point of having money is an attractive one. I disagree that it is the most important thing about being rich, although generosity is one of the better uses of a fortune.\n\nThe claim has obvious appeal. Money makes help possible on a scale that goodwill alone cannot reach: a single donor can fund a hospital wing or eliminate a disease from a region, and private foundations have paid for vaccination programmes that governments delayed. Giving also appears to benefit the giver, since people who support others report more satisfaction than those who spend the same amount on themselves, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a purpose beyond accumulation</strong> is what many wealthy people say they were missing.\n\nWhat the statement understates is the first and most personal thing wealth provides, which is security. Money removes the fear that dominates poorer lives: illness does not become catastrophe, a broken car does not cost the job, and children can be educated properly. More than that, it buys time and choice, so a person can leave bad work, refuse bad treatment and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decide how their own days are spent</strong>. Anyone who has lacked money knows that this, rather than the chance to donate, is what having it means.\n\nThere is also reason for caution about the charitable version. Reliance on private generosity leaves essential services depending on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">whatever a donor happens to find interesting</strong>, and it is no substitute for a fair tax system. Some giving is closer to reputation management than to help.\n\nIn conclusion, wealth genuinely allows people to relieve suffering, and those who use it that way deserve credit. Its greatest significance is nonetheless the freedom and security it gives the person who holds it, and treating charity as the main point flatters the rich more than it helps the poor.","phrases":[{"en":"a purpose beyond accumulation","context":"The claim has obvious appeal. Money makes help possible on a scale that goodwill alone cannot reach: a single donor can fund a hospital wing or eliminate a disease from a region, and private foundations have paid for vaccination programmes that governments delayed. Giving also appears to benefit the giver, since people who support others report more satisfaction than those who spend the same amount on themselves, and a purpose beyond accumulation is what many wealthy people say they were missing."},{"en":"decide how their own days are spent","context":"What the statement understates is the first and most personal thing wealth provides, which is security. Money removes the fear that dominates poorer lives: illness does not become catastrophe, a broken car does not cost the job, and children can be educated properly. More than that, it buys time and choice, so a person can leave bad work, refuse bad treatment and decide how their own days are spent. Anyone who has lacked money knows that this, rather than the chance to donate, is what having it means."},{"en":"whatever a donor happens to find interesting","context":"There is also reason for caution about the charitable version. Reliance on private generosity leaves essential services depending on whatever a donor happens to find interesting, and it is no substitute for a fair tax system. Some giving is closer to reputation management than to help."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#112","summary":"Whether the best thing about being rich is the ability to help other people"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2347","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Economy & Money","title":"Growth against the environment","prompt":"Some people believe that economic growth is essential for ending global poverty and hunger, while others argue that it damages the environment. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Growth has lifted more people out of poverty than any other force and has also driven the emissions now changing the climate. Both observations are correct, and my own view is that the argument mistakes the amount of growth for the kind of growth.\n\nThe case for growth is supported by the record. Hundreds of millions of people left extreme poverty within a single generation as economies in Asia expanded, and it was industry and trade rather than aid that did it. Growth also pays for the things that end hunger permanently: clinics, roads, irrigation and schools all require tax revenue, and a government of a poor country <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot redistribute what has not been produced</strong>. Telling such countries to stop developing, having industrialised freely ourselves, is difficult to defend.\n\nThe environmental objection is equally grounded. Rising output has meant rising emissions, cleared forests and exhausted fisheries, and the planet cannot supply every person with the consumption levels of a wealthy country. There is also a fairness point, in that the poorest nations contributed least to the damage and are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suffering the worst of the consequences first</strong>.\n\nThe way out is that growth and emissions are separable. Several developed economies have expanded while their emissions fell, because energy came from wind and solar rather than coal and because services grew faster than heavy industry. Renewable power is now cheaper than fossil fuel in most places, so poorer countries can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">develop without repeating our route</strong> if the finance and technology reach them.\n\nIn conclusion, growth remains the most reliable route out of poverty and should not be abandoned. What has to end is growth measured by consumption of materials, and wealthy nations owe poorer ones the investment that makes cleaner development affordable.","phrases":[{"en":"cannot redistribute what has not been produced","context":"The case for growth is supported by the record. Hundreds of millions of people left extreme poverty within a single generation as economies in Asia expanded, and it was industry and trade rather than aid that did it. Growth also pays for the things that end hunger permanently: clinics, roads, irrigation and schools all require tax revenue, and a government of a poor country cannot redistribute what has not been produced. Telling such countries to stop developing, having industrialised freely ourselves, is difficult to defend."},{"en":"suffering the worst of the consequences first","context":"The environmental objection is equally grounded. Rising output has meant rising emissions, cleared forests and exhausted fisheries, and the planet cannot supply every person with the consumption levels of a wealthy country. There is also a fairness point, in that the poorest nations contributed least to the damage and are suffering the worst of the consequences first."},{"en":"develop without repeating our route","context":"The way out is that growth and emissions are separable. Several developed economies have expanded while their emissions fell, because energy came from wind and solar rather than coal and because services grew faster than heavy industry. Renewable power is now cheaper than fossil fuel in most places, so poorer countries can develop without repeating our route if the finance and technology reach them."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#113","summary":"Whether economic growth is the cure for poverty or a threat to the planet"},{"id":"writing-task-2-050","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Study what you like, or what is useful?","prompt":"Some people think that all university students should study whatever they like. Others believe that they should only be allowed to study subjects that will be useful in the future, such as those related to science and technology. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about how much choice students should have with regard to what they can study at university. While some argue that it would be better for students to be forced into certain key subject areas, I believe that everyone should be able to study the course of their choice.\n\nThere are various reasons why people believe that universities should only offer subjects that will be useful in the future. They may assert that university courses like medicine, engineering and information technology are more likely to be beneficial than certain art degrees. From a personal perspective, it can be argued that these courses <strong class=\"key-phrase\">provide more job opportunities</strong>, career progression, better salaries, and therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an improved quality of life</strong> for students who take them. On the societal level, by forcing people to choose particular university subjects, governments can ensure that any knowledge and skill gaps in the economy are covered. Finally, a focus on technology in higher education could lead to new inventions, economic growth, and greater future prosperity.\n\nIn spite of these arguments, I believe that university students should be free to choose their <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preferred areas of study</strong>. In my opinion, society will benefit more if our students are passionate about what they are learning. Besides, nobody can really predict which areas of knowledge will be most useful to society in the future, and it may be that employers begin to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">value creative thinking skills</strong> above practical or technical skills. If this were the case, perhaps we would need more students of art, history and philosophy than of science or technology.\n\nIn conclusion, although it might seem sensible for universities to focus only on the most useful subjects, I personally prefer the current system in which people have the right to study whatever they like.","phrases":[{"en":"provide more job opportunities","context":"There are various reasons why people believe that universities should only offer subjects that will be useful in the future. They may assert that university courses like medicine, engineering and information technology are more likely to be beneficial than certain art degrees. From a personal perspective, it can be argued that these courses provide more job opportunities, career progression, better salaries, and therefore an improved quality of life for students who take them. On the societal level, by forcing people to choose particular university subjects, governments can ensure that any knowledge and skill gaps in the economy are covered. Finally, a focus on technology in higher education could lead to new inventions, economic growth, and greater future prosperity."},{"en":"an improved quality of life","context":"There are various reasons why people believe that universities should only offer subjects that will be useful in the future. They may assert that university courses like medicine, engineering and information technology are more likely to be beneficial than certain art degrees. From a personal perspective, it can be argued that these courses provide more job opportunities, career progression, better salaries, and therefore an improved quality of life for students who take them. On the societal level, by forcing people to choose particular university subjects, governments can ensure that any knowledge and skill gaps in the economy are covered. Finally, a focus on technology in higher education could lead to new inventions, economic growth, and greater future prosperity."},{"en":"preferred areas of study","context":"In spite of these arguments, I believe that university students should be free to choose their preferred areas of study. In my opinion, society will benefit more if our students are passionate about what they are learning. Besides, nobody can really predict which areas of knowledge will be most useful to society in the future, and it may be that employers begin to value creative thinking skills above practical or technical skills. If this were the case, perhaps we would need more students of art, history and philosophy than of science or technology."},{"en":"value creative thinking skills","context":"In spite of these arguments, I believe that university students should be free to choose their preferred areas of study. In my opinion, society will benefit more if our students are passionate about what they are learning. Besides, nobody can really predict which areas of knowledge will be most useful to society in the future, and it may be that employers begin to value creative thinking skills above practical or technical skills. If this were the case, perhaps we would need more students of art, history and philosophy than of science or technology."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#114","summary":"Freedom to choose university subjects"},{"id":"writing-task-2-051","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Equal numbers of men and women in every subject","prompt":"Universities should accept equal numbers of male and female students in every subject. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"In my opinion, men and women should have the same educational opportunities. However, I do not agree with the idea of accepting equal proportions of each gender in every university subject.\n\nHaving the same number of men and women on all degree courses is simply unrealistic. Student numbers on any course depend on the applications that the institution receives. If a university decided to fill courses with equal numbers of males and females, it would need enough <strong class=\"key-phrase\">applicants of each gender</strong>. In reality, many courses are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more popular with one gender</strong> than the other, and it would not be practical to aim for equal proportions. For example, nursing courses tend to attract more female applicants, and it would be difficult to fill these courses if fifty per cent of the places needed to go to males.\n\nApart from the practical concerns expressed above, I also believe that it would be unfair to base <strong class=\"key-phrase\">admission to university courses</strong> on gender. Universities should continue to select the best candidates for each course according to their qualifications. In this way, both men and women <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have the same opportunities</strong>, and applicants know that they will be successful if they work hard to achieve good grades at school. If a female student is the best candidate for a place on a course, it is surely wrong to reject her in favour of a male student with lower grades or fewer qualifications.\n\nIn conclusion, the selection of university students should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">based on merit</strong>, and it would be both impractical and unfair to change to a selection procedure based on gender.","phrases":[{"en":"applicants of each gender","context":"Having the same number of men and women on all degree courses is simply unrealistic. Student numbers on any course depend on the applications that the institution receives. If a university decided to fill courses with equal numbers of males and females, it would need enough applicants of each gender. In reality, many courses are more popular with one gender than the other, and it would not be practical to aim for equal proportions. For example, nursing courses tend to attract more female applicants, and it would be difficult to fill these courses if fifty per cent of the places needed to go to males."},{"en":"more popular with one gender","context":"Having the same number of men and women on all degree courses is simply unrealistic. Student numbers on any course depend on the applications that the institution receives. If a university decided to fill courses with equal numbers of males and females, it would need enough applicants of each gender. In reality, many courses are more popular with one gender than the other, and it would not be practical to aim for equal proportions. For example, nursing courses tend to attract more female applicants, and it would be difficult to fill these courses if fifty per cent of the places needed to go to males."},{"en":"admission to university courses","context":"Apart from the practical concerns expressed above, I also believe that it would be unfair to base admission to university courses on gender. Universities should continue to select the best candidates for each course according to their qualifications. In this way, both men and women have the same opportunities, and applicants know that they will be successful if they work hard to achieve good grades at school. If a female student is the best candidate for a place on a course, it is surely wrong to reject her in favour of a male student with lower grades or fewer qualifications."},{"en":"have the same opportunities","context":"Apart from the practical concerns expressed above, I also believe that it would be unfair to base admission to university courses on gender. Universities should continue to select the best candidates for each course according to their qualifications. In this way, both men and women have the same opportunities, and applicants know that they will be successful if they work hard to achieve good grades at school. If a female student is the best candidate for a place on a course, it is surely wrong to reject her in favour of a male student with lower grades or fewer qualifications."},{"en":"based on merit","context":"In conclusion, the selection of university students should be based on merit, and it would be both impractical and unfair to change to a selection procedure based on gender."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#115","summary":"Gender quotas in university admissions"},{"id":"writing-task-2-052","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Is homework worth setting?","prompt":"Some people believe that school children should not be given homework by their teachers, whereas others argue that homework plays an important role in the education of children. Discuss both of these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People&#x27;s opinions differ as to whether or not school children should be given homework. While there are some strong arguments against the setting of homework, I still believe that it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a necessary aspect of education</strong>.\n\nThere are several reasons why people might argue that homework is an unnecessary burden on children. The strongest is the evidence that it does nothing to improve educational outcomes: Finland sets its school children almost no homework, yet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistently outperforms countries where homework is standard practice</strong> in international league tables. Even where the evidence is less clear-cut, the practical case remains: a school day of six or seven hours already <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves little energy for further study</strong>, and a child who is genuinely tired does not learn much from an extra hour of exercises. The time saved is not wasted either, since play and family time support the kind of brain development that formal lessons cannot provide on their own.\n\nIn spite of the above arguments, I support the view that homework has an important role to play in the schooling of children. The main benefit of homework is that it encourages independent learning and problem solving, as children are challenged to work through tasks alone and at their own pace. In doing so, students must apply the knowledge that they have learned in the classroom. For example, by doing mathematics exercises at home, students <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consolidate their understanding of the concepts</strong> taught by their teacher at school. In my view, it is important for children to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">develop an independent study habit</strong> because this prepares them to work alone as adults.\n\nIn conclusion, homework certainly has its drawbacks, but I believe that the benefits outweigh them in the long term.","phrases":[{"en":"a necessary aspect of education","context":"People's opinions differ as to whether or not school children should be given homework. While there are some strong arguments against the setting of homework, I still believe that it is a necessary aspect of education."},{"en":"consistently outperforms countries where homework is standard practice","context":"There are several reasons why people might argue that homework is an unnecessary burden on children. The strongest is the evidence that it does nothing to improve educational outcomes: Finland sets its school children almost no homework, yet consistently outperforms countries where homework is standard practice in international league tables. Even where the evidence is less clear-cut, the practical case remains: a school day of six or seven hours already leaves little energy for further study, and a child who is genuinely tired does not learn much from an extra hour of exercises. The time saved is not wasted either, since play and family time support the kind of brain development that formal lessons cannot provide on their own."},{"en":"leaves little energy for further study","context":"There are several reasons why people might argue that homework is an unnecessary burden on children. The strongest is the evidence that it does nothing to improve educational outcomes: Finland sets its school children almost no homework, yet consistently outperforms countries where homework is standard practice in international league tables. Even where the evidence is less clear-cut, the practical case remains: a school day of six or seven hours already leaves little energy for further study, and a child who is genuinely tired does not learn much from an extra hour of exercises. The time saved is not wasted either, since play and family time support the kind of brain development that formal lessons cannot provide on their own."},{"en":"consolidate their understanding of the concepts","context":"In spite of the above arguments, I support the view that homework has an important role to play in the schooling of children. The main benefit of homework is that it encourages independent learning and problem solving, as children are challenged to work through tasks alone and at their own pace. In doing so, students must apply the knowledge that they have learned in the classroom. For example, by doing mathematics exercises at home, students consolidate their understanding of the concepts taught by their teacher at school. In my view, it is important for children to develop an independent study habit because this prepares them to work alone as adults."},{"en":"develop an independent study habit","context":"In spite of the above arguments, I support the view that homework has an important role to play in the schooling of children. The main benefit of homework is that it encourages independent learning and problem solving, as children are challenged to work through tasks alone and at their own pace. In doing so, students must apply the knowledge that they have learned in the classroom. For example, by doing mathematics exercises at home, students consolidate their understanding of the concepts taught by their teacher at school. In my view, it is important for children to develop an independent study habit because this prepares them to work alone as adults."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#116","summary":"Homework in children's education"},{"id":"writing-task-2-053","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"University or straight into work?","prompt":"Some people believe that studying at university or college is the best route to a successful career, while others believe that it is better to get a job straight after school.","answer":"When they finish school, teenagers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">face the dilemma</strong> of whether to get a job or continue their education. While there are some benefits to getting a job straight after school, I would argue that it is better to go to college or university.\n\nThe option to start work straight after school is attractive for several reasons. Many young people want to start earning money as soon as possible, so that they can become independent and eventually afford their own house or start a family. There is also a genuine career advantage: an eighteen-year-old who joins a retail company or begins a trade apprenticeship as an electrician or plumber can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">gain real experience and learn practical skills</strong> on the job. By the time a same-age graduate finally enters the workplace, several years and a considerable debt later, the early starter may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">already have been promoted into a supervisory role</strong>.\n\nOn the other hand, I believe that it is more beneficial for students to continue their studies. Firstly, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">academic qualifications</strong> are required in many professions. For example, it is impossible to become a doctor, teacher or lawyer without having the relevant degree. As a result, university graduates have access to more and better job opportunities, and they tend to earn higher salaries than those with fewer qualifications. Secondly, the job market is becoming <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increasingly competitive</strong>, and sometimes there are hundreds of applicants for one position in a company. Young people who do not have qualifications from a university or college will not be able to compete.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that students are more likely to be successful in their careers if they continue their studies beyond school level.","phrases":[{"en":"face the dilemma","context":"When they finish school, teenagers face the dilemma of whether to get a job or continue their education. While there are some benefits to getting a job straight after school, I would argue that it is better to go to college or university."},{"en":"gain real experience and learn practical skills","context":"The option to start work straight after school is attractive for several reasons. Many young people want to start earning money as soon as possible, so that they can become independent and eventually afford their own house or start a family. There is also a genuine career advantage: an eighteen-year-old who joins a retail company or begins a trade apprenticeship as an electrician or plumber can gain real experience and learn practical skills on the job. By the time a same-age graduate finally enters the workplace, several years and a considerable debt later, the early starter may already have been promoted into a supervisory role."},{"en":"already have been promoted into a supervisory role","context":"The option to start work straight after school is attractive for several reasons. Many young people want to start earning money as soon as possible, so that they can become independent and eventually afford their own house or start a family. There is also a genuine career advantage: an eighteen-year-old who joins a retail company or begins a trade apprenticeship as an electrician or plumber can gain real experience and learn practical skills on the job. By the time a same-age graduate finally enters the workplace, several years and a considerable debt later, the early starter may already have been promoted into a supervisory role."},{"en":"academic qualifications","context":"On the other hand, I believe that it is more beneficial for students to continue their studies. Firstly, academic qualifications are required in many professions. For example, it is impossible to become a doctor, teacher or lawyer without having the relevant degree. As a result, university graduates have access to more and better job opportunities, and they tend to earn higher salaries than those with fewer qualifications. Secondly, the job market is becoming increasingly competitive, and sometimes there are hundreds of applicants for one position in a company. Young people who do not have qualifications from a university or college will not be able to compete."},{"en":"increasingly competitive","context":"On the other hand, I believe that it is more beneficial for students to continue their studies. Firstly, academic qualifications are required in many professions. For example, it is impossible to become a doctor, teacher or lawyer without having the relevant degree. As a result, university graduates have access to more and better job opportunities, and they tend to earn higher salaries than those with fewer qualifications. Secondly, the job market is becoming increasingly competitive, and sometimes there are hundreds of applicants for one position in a company. Young people who do not have qualifications from a university or college will not be able to compete."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#117","summary":"People believe that studying at university or college is the best route to…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-054","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Private school fees and paying tax for state schools","prompt":"Families who send their children to private schools should not be required to pay taxes that support the state education system. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?","answer":"Some people believe that parents of children who attend private schools should not need to contribute to state schools through taxes. Personally, I completely disagree with this view.\n\nFor a variety of reasons, it would be wrong to reduce taxes for families who pay for private education. Firstly, it would be difficult to calculate the correct amount of tax reduction for these families, and staff would be required to manage this complex process. Secondly, we all pay a certain amount of tax for public services that we may not use. For example, most people are fortunate enough not to have to call the police or fire brigade at any time in their lives, but they would not expect a tax reduction for this. Finally, if wealthy families were given a tax discount for sending their children to private schools, we might have a situation where poorer people pay higher taxes than the rich.\n\nIn my opinion, we should all be happy to pay our share of the money that supports public schools. It is beneficial for all members of society to have a high quality education system with equal opportunities for all young people. This will result in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a well-educated workforce</strong>, and in turn a more productive and prosperous nation. Parents of children in private schools may also see the advantages of this in their own lives. For example, a company owner will need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well qualified and competent staff</strong>, and a well-funded education system can provide such employees.\n\nIn conclusion, I do not believe that any <strong class=\"key-phrase\">financial concessions</strong> should be made for people who choose private education.","phrases":[{"en":"a well-educated workforce","context":"In my opinion, we should all be happy to pay our share of the money that supports public schools. It is beneficial for all members of society to have a high quality education system with equal opportunities for all young people. This will result in a well-educated workforce, and in turn a more productive and prosperous nation. Parents of children in private schools may also see the advantages of this in their own lives. For example, a company owner will need well qualified and competent staff, and a well-funded education system can provide such employees."},{"en":"well qualified and competent staff","context":"In my opinion, we should all be happy to pay our share of the money that supports public schools. It is beneficial for all members of society to have a high quality education system with equal opportunities for all young people. This will result in a well-educated workforce, and in turn a more productive and prosperous nation. Parents of children in private schools may also see the advantages of this in their own lives. For example, a company owner will need well qualified and competent staff, and a well-funded education system can provide such employees."},{"en":"financial concessions","context":"In conclusion, I do not believe that any financial concessions should be made for people who choose private education."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#118","summary":"Private schools and education taxes"},{"id":"writing-task-2-055","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Is a gap year a good idea?","prompt":"Some people believe that a gap year between school and university is a good idea, while others disagree strongly. Consider both sides of this debate and present your own opinion.","answer":"Gap years, in which school leavers delay university for a year to travel or volunteer, have become increasingly common, and opinion is divided about whether they are worth the disruption. In my opinion the benefits usually justify a gap year provided it has a clear purpose, though the arguments against it are real.\n\nThe case against is mainly practical. A year of flights, accommodation and living costs is a real burden that often falls on the family or has to be borrowed against. Travelling for months can also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">blunt academic momentum</strong>, making it harder to return to essay deadlines and exam discipline than for a student who has come straight from school.\n\nSafety is a further concern, particularly for young people travelling independently for the first time. Without the structure of a school trip or a university term, a gap-year traveller is more exposed to medical emergencies, transport accidents and petty crime, often somewhere language barriers make help harder to find.\n\nOn the other hand, supporters of gap years say that the skills learned outweigh the disadvantages. They highlight the independence and assertiveness that a student will acquire, in areas such as time management and interpersonal skills. They also say that these skills lead to greater career success later in life, which cancels out the costs involved. A student who spends six months teaching English or volunteering on a conservation project, for instance, returns with organisational experience and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a self-reliance that few school leavers have</strong>, and this is precisely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what stands out on an application</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, my own feeling is that a year off can indeed be a useful activity, with the experience leading to improved skills that are invaluable in the future. Of course, this is provided that the year is planned carefully and used for something <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely worthwhile</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"blunt academic momentum","context":"The case against is mainly practical. A year of flights, accommodation and living costs is a real burden that often falls on the family or has to be borrowed against. Travelling for months can also blunt academic momentum, making it harder to return to essay deadlines and exam discipline than for a student who has come straight from school."},{"en":"a self-reliance that few school leavers have","context":"On the other hand, supporters of gap years say that the skills learned outweigh the disadvantages. They highlight the independence and assertiveness that a student will acquire, in areas such as time management and interpersonal skills. They also say that these skills lead to greater career success later in life, which cancels out the costs involved. A student who spends six months teaching English or volunteering on a conservation project, for instance, returns with organisational experience and a self-reliance that few school leavers have, and this is precisely what stands out on an application."},{"en":"what stands out on an application","context":"On the other hand, supporters of gap years say that the skills learned outweigh the disadvantages. They highlight the independence and assertiveness that a student will acquire, in areas such as time management and interpersonal skills. They also say that these skills lead to greater career success later in life, which cancels out the costs involved. A student who spends six months teaching English or volunteering on a conservation project, for instance, returns with organisational experience and a self-reliance that few school leavers have, and this is precisely what stands out on an application."},{"en":"genuinely worthwhile","context":"In conclusion, my own feeling is that a year off can indeed be a useful activity, with the experience leading to improved skills that are invaluable in the future. Of course, this is provided that the year is planned carefully and used for something genuinely worthwhile."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#119","summary":"People believe that a gap year between school and university is a good…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-056","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should sport be compulsory at school?","prompt":"Some people believe that sport is an essential part of school life for children, while others feel it should be purely optional. Discuss these opposing views and give your own opinion.","answer":"The place of sport in the school curriculum is a subject of real disagreement, and there are reasonable arguments on both sides, although in my view the case for making it compulsory is the stronger one. On the one hand, some people feel that sport uses up time which could be better spent on academic subjects such as exam preparation.\n\nCritics also argue that it is unwise to force children into activities they have no interest in. A pupil who dreads every games lesson learns little from it, and some youngsters are simply not sporty and would benefit more from extra study. A third concern is the conduct of professional sport itself: doping and corruption scandals have become common in cycling and athletics, and some parents feel that these set a poor example to young people.\n\nOn the other hand, the clearest benefit of sport is its contribution to children&#x27;s physical development at the stage when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">habits are formed</strong>. Children who never exercise at school are far less likely to do so as adults, and obesity among young people has risen sharply in countries where physical education has been cut. Poor fitness also affects concentration, so the time given to sport is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">partly repaid in the classroom</strong>.\n\nSupporters of school sport also emphasise the value of team games such as football or hockey, in which children must accept instruction, depend on others and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put a shared aim above their own performance</strong>. A related argument is that a competitive spirit, so important today, is best formed early: learning to lose a match and return to training the following week <strong class=\"key-phrase\">teaches a resilience that examinations alone rarely develop</strong>.\n\nOverall, I believe that sport should be compulsory for school children, because the positive effect on their mental and physical development outweighs the objections. Schools should, however, offer a range wide enough for every child to find something they enjoy, and should teach fair play as firmly as they teach the game itself.","phrases":[{"en":"habits are formed","context":"On the other hand, the clearest benefit of sport is its contribution to children's physical development at the stage when habits are formed. Children who never exercise at school are far less likely to do so as adults, and obesity among young people has risen sharply in countries where physical education has been cut. Poor fitness also affects concentration, so the time given to sport is partly repaid in the classroom."},{"en":"partly repaid in the classroom","context":"On the other hand, the clearest benefit of sport is its contribution to children's physical development at the stage when habits are formed. Children who never exercise at school are far less likely to do so as adults, and obesity among young people has risen sharply in countries where physical education has been cut. Poor fitness also affects concentration, so the time given to sport is partly repaid in the classroom."},{"en":"put a shared aim above their own performance","context":"Supporters of school sport also emphasise the value of team games such as football or hockey, in which children must accept instruction, depend on others and put a shared aim above their own performance. A related argument is that a competitive spirit, so important today, is best formed early: learning to lose a match and return to training the following week teaches a resilience that examinations alone rarely develop."},{"en":"teaches a resilience that examinations alone rarely develop","context":"Supporters of school sport also emphasise the value of team games such as football or hockey, in which children must accept instruction, depend on others and put a shared aim above their own performance. A related argument is that a competitive spirit, so important today, is best formed early: learning to lose a match and return to training the following week teaches a resilience that examinations alone rarely develop."}],"subtype":"Discussion","_displayNo":"#120","summary":"People believe that sport is an essential part of school life for children, …"},{"id":"writing-task-2-057","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Will computers replace teachers?","prompt":"Computers are now widely used in education, and some people think that teachers will no longer play an important role in the classroom. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"As computers have become central to education, some people predict that teachers will no longer play an important role in the classroom. I disagree with this view, because technology changes what teachers do rather than removing the need for them.\n\nThe argument for the prediction is understandable. A well-designed program can explain a concept as clearly as most teachers, repeat it indefinitely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without impatience</strong>, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adjust its difficulty to each student</strong>. Online courses give learners in remote areas access to material that would otherwise be unavailable, and software marks tests instantly, which frees a great deal of time.\n\nNevertheless, these advantages address only part of what education involves. Most students, particularly younger ones, do not study consistently without someone who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">notices when they have stopped trying</strong> and asks why. A teacher recognises that a child is struggling because of problems at home rather than because the material is difficult, and no program can make that judgement. Motivation is also personal: pupils work hard for teachers they respect and are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reluctant to disappoint</strong>, and no program can generate that.\n\nTeaching also involves skills that software cannot assess. Discussion, argument and cooperation are learned from other people, and a computer cannot tell a student that their reasoning is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">superficially plausible but wrong</strong>. Experience with remote learning demonstrated this clearly, since results fell sharply and the weakest students suffered most.\n\nIn conclusion, computers are valuable tools that have changed how teachers work, but I disagree that they will make teachers unimportant. The human elements of education have become more important, not less.","phrases":[{"en":"without impatience","context":"The argument for the prediction is understandable. A well-designed program can explain a concept as clearly as most teachers, repeat it indefinitely without impatience, and adjust its difficulty to each student. Online courses give learners in remote areas access to material that would otherwise be unavailable, and software marks tests instantly, which frees a great deal of time."},{"en":"adjust its difficulty to each student","context":"The argument for the prediction is understandable. A well-designed program can explain a concept as clearly as most teachers, repeat it indefinitely without impatience, and adjust its difficulty to each student. Online courses give learners in remote areas access to material that would otherwise be unavailable, and software marks tests instantly, which frees a great deal of time."},{"en":"notices when they have stopped trying","context":"Nevertheless, these advantages address only part of what education involves. Most students, particularly younger ones, do not study consistently without someone who notices when they have stopped trying and asks why. A teacher recognises that a child is struggling because of problems at home rather than because the material is difficult, and no program can make that judgement. Motivation is also personal: pupils work hard for teachers they respect and are reluctant to disappoint, and no program can generate that."},{"en":"reluctant to disappoint","context":"Nevertheless, these advantages address only part of what education involves. Most students, particularly younger ones, do not study consistently without someone who notices when they have stopped trying and asks why. A teacher recognises that a child is struggling because of problems at home rather than because the material is difficult, and no program can make that judgement. Motivation is also personal: pupils work hard for teachers they respect and are reluctant to disappoint, and no program can generate that."},{"en":"superficially plausible but wrong","context":"Teaching also involves skills that software cannot assess. Discussion, argument and cooperation are learned from other people, and a computer cannot tell a student that their reasoning is superficially plausible but wrong. Experience with remote learning demonstrated this clearly, since results fell sharply and the weakest students suffered most."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#121","summary":"Will computers replace teachers?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-058","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"The advantages and disadvantages of studying abroad","prompt":"Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of studying abroad.","answer":"Increasing numbers of students now complete part of their education in another country. In my view the experience offers substantial benefits, although the difficulties involved are greater than most students expect before they go.\n\nThe clearest advantage is language. Living somewhere the language is spoken constantly produces a fluency that classroom study rarely achieves, because everyday necessities such as shopping, banking and asking directions force students to use it. Cultural understanding develops in the same way: a student who has actually lived in a country learns how people there think and behave, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rather than learning about it second-hand</strong>.\n\nThe second benefit is independence. Students abroad have to manage accommodation, money, cooking and bureaucracy without their families, often in a second language. Most find the first few months genuinely difficult, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the confidence that comes from having coped</strong> is lasting, and employers recognise it immediately. The international friendships formed along the way are valuable too, both personally and later in a career.\n\nThe drawbacks are real, however. Studying abroad is expensive, which restricts it to families who can afford it or students who win scholarships. Loneliness is the more serious problem: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">homesickness affects almost everyone</strong> at some stage, and a student who is struggling has no family nearby and may not yet have formed close friendships. A minority never adjust and return home early.\n\nIn conclusion, studying abroad develops language ability, cultural understanding and self-reliance far more effectively than studying at home ever could. Students should nevertheless <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prepare for a difficult first term</strong> rather than expecting the whole experience to be straightforwardly enjoyable.","phrases":[{"en":"rather than learning about it second-hand","context":"The clearest advantage is language. Living somewhere the language is spoken constantly produces a fluency that classroom study rarely achieves, because everyday necessities such as shopping, banking and asking directions force students to use it. Cultural understanding develops in the same way: a student who has actually lived in a country learns how people there think and behave, rather than learning about it second-hand."},{"en":"the confidence that comes from having coped","context":"The second benefit is independence. Students abroad have to manage accommodation, money, cooking and bureaucracy without their families, often in a second language. Most find the first few months genuinely difficult, but the confidence that comes from having coped is lasting, and employers recognise it immediately. The international friendships formed along the way are valuable too, both personally and later in a career."},{"en":"homesickness affects almost everyone","context":"The drawbacks are real, however. Studying abroad is expensive, which restricts it to families who can afford it or students who win scholarships. Loneliness is the more serious problem: homesickness affects almost everyone at some stage, and a student who is struggling has no family nearby and may not yet have formed close friendships. A minority never adjust and return home early."},{"en":"prepare for a difficult first term","context":"In conclusion, studying abroad develops language ability, cultural understanding and self-reliance far more effectively than studying at home ever could. Students should nevertheless prepare for a difficult first term rather than expecting the whole experience to be straightforwardly enjoyable."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","_displayNo":"#122","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of studying abroad"},{"id":"writing-task-2-060","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Pushing children to study hard","prompt":"Some people believe that children should be pushed to study hard from a young age. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that children should be pushed to work hard academically from an early age. I agree that high expectations benefit young people, but I believe that excessive pressure does more harm than good.\n\nThere is a strong case for expecting effort. Skills such as reading, mathematics and a foreign language are far easier to acquire when young, and a child who falls behind early rarely catches up. Habits also form early: a student who learns to work steadily, meet deadlines and persist through difficulty acquires something more valuable than the subject content itself. Left entirely to themselves, most children would choose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediate entertainment over long-term benefit</strong>, so a degree of adult direction is clearly necessary.\n\nThe difficulty is that pressure has a point beyond which it becomes destructive. In several countries where competition for university places is extreme, rates of anxiety and sleep deprivation among teenagers are among the highest in the world. Excessive pressure also damages learning itself, because a student who studies only to avoid punishment <strong class=\"key-phrase\">memorises material and forgets it immediately</strong>, and often develops <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lasting dislike of the subject</strong>. Children additionally need time for play, sport and friendship, and these are not luxuries: they are where social skills and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">emotional resilience</strong> develop.\n\nIn my opinion the distinction lies <strong class=\"key-phrase\">between encouragement and coercion</strong>. Parents should set clear expectations and take an interest in progress. They should not threaten, compare a child unfavourably with siblings, or fill every evening with extra classes.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that children should be expected to work hard, but pressure must be kept within limits that protect their health and their interest in learning.","phrases":[{"en":"immediate entertainment over long-term benefit","context":"There is a strong case for expecting effort. Skills such as reading, mathematics and a foreign language are far easier to acquire when young, and a child who falls behind early rarely catches up. Habits also form early: a student who learns to work steadily, meet deadlines and persist through difficulty acquires something more valuable than the subject content itself. Left entirely to themselves, most children would choose immediate entertainment over long-term benefit, so a degree of adult direction is clearly necessary."},{"en":"memorises material and forgets it immediately","context":"The difficulty is that pressure has a point beyond which it becomes destructive. In several countries where competition for university places is extreme, rates of anxiety and sleep deprivation among teenagers are among the highest in the world. Excessive pressure also damages learning itself, because a student who studies only to avoid punishment memorises material and forgets it immediately, and often develops a lasting dislike of the subject. Children additionally need time for play, sport and friendship, and these are not luxuries: they are where social skills and emotional resilience develop."},{"en":"a lasting dislike of the subject","context":"The difficulty is that pressure has a point beyond which it becomes destructive. In several countries where competition for university places is extreme, rates of anxiety and sleep deprivation among teenagers are among the highest in the world. Excessive pressure also damages learning itself, because a student who studies only to avoid punishment memorises material and forgets it immediately, and often develops a lasting dislike of the subject. Children additionally need time for play, sport and friendship, and these are not luxuries: they are where social skills and emotional resilience develop."},{"en":"emotional resilience","context":"The difficulty is that pressure has a point beyond which it becomes destructive. In several countries where competition for university places is extreme, rates of anxiety and sleep deprivation among teenagers are among the highest in the world. Excessive pressure also damages learning itself, because a student who studies only to avoid punishment memorises material and forgets it immediately, and often develops a lasting dislike of the subject. Children additionally need time for play, sport and friendship, and these are not luxuries: they are where social skills and emotional resilience develop."},{"en":"between encouragement and coercion","context":"In my opinion the distinction lies between encouragement and coercion. Parents should set clear expectations and take an interest in progress. They should not threaten, compare a child unfavourably with siblings, or fill every evening with extra classes."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#123","summary":"Pushing children to study hard"},{"id":"writing-task-2-061","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Teaching international news at school","prompt":"Some people think that international news should be taught as a school subject. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes proposed that international news should be taught as a formal subject in secondary schools. I agree with this suggestion, provided that it is taught as a way of understanding the world rather than as a series of facts to be memorised.\n\nThe main argument in favour is that young people now encounter international events constantly but without any framework for interpreting them. Social media presents war, migration and economic crisis <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in fragments</strong>, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stripped of history and often deliberately distorted</strong>. A student who has been taught how a conflict arose, and who is used to comparing how different countries report the same event, is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">considerably harder to mislead</strong>.\n\nThere is also a practical justification. Economies are now interconnected, and decisions taken abroad affect employment, prices and the environment at home. Young people will vote on questions of trade, immigration and climate policy, and they cannot do so sensibly without some knowledge of the wider world. Employers increasingly value the same awareness, since many graduates work with colleagues in other countries.\n\nThe obvious objection is that the curriculum is already crowded and that news <strong class=\"key-phrase\">becomes outdated immediately</strong>. This is a fair point, and it suggests that the subject should focus on skills rather than events: how to identify a reliable source, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how to recognise bias</strong>, and how a country&#x27;s history shapes its behaviour.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that international news deserves a place in secondary education, but it should be taught as media literacy rather than as a list of events.","phrases":[{"en":"in fragments","context":"The main argument in favour is that young people now encounter international events constantly but without any framework for interpreting them. Social media presents war, migration and economic crisis in fragments, stripped of history and often deliberately distorted. A student who has been taught how a conflict arose, and who is used to comparing how different countries report the same event, is considerably harder to mislead."},{"en":"stripped of history and often deliberately distorted","context":"The main argument in favour is that young people now encounter international events constantly but without any framework for interpreting them. Social media presents war, migration and economic crisis in fragments, stripped of history and often deliberately distorted. A student who has been taught how a conflict arose, and who is used to comparing how different countries report the same event, is considerably harder to mislead."},{"en":"considerably harder to mislead","context":"The main argument in favour is that young people now encounter international events constantly but without any framework for interpreting them. Social media presents war, migration and economic crisis in fragments, stripped of history and often deliberately distorted. A student who has been taught how a conflict arose, and who is used to comparing how different countries report the same event, is considerably harder to mislead."},{"en":"becomes outdated immediately","context":"The obvious objection is that the curriculum is already crowded and that news becomes outdated immediately. This is a fair point, and it suggests that the subject should focus on skills rather than events: how to identify a reliable source, how to recognise bias, and how a country's history shapes its behaviour."},{"en":"how to recognise bias","context":"The obvious objection is that the curriculum is already crowded and that news becomes outdated immediately. This is a fair point, and it suggests that the subject should focus on skills rather than events: how to identify a reliable source, how to recognise bias, and how a country's history shapes its behaviour."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#124","summary":"Teaching international news at school"},{"id":"writing-task-2-062","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Education as a lifelong process","prompt":"In the past, education was a short period of training, but today people treat it as a lifelong process. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often observed that education is no longer confined to childhood and early adulthood, but continues throughout a person&#x27;s working life. I agree that this change has taken place, and I believe it is both necessary and beneficial.\n\nThe main reason is that knowledge and technology now change far faster than they once did. A person who trained as an accountant or a mechanic forty years ago could expect the skills learned at twenty to remain useful until retirement. Today, software, regulations and equipment are replaced every few years, and entire occupations disappear. An employee who stops learning becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unemployable long before</strong> reaching pension age, so continuous training is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simply a condition of remaining in work</strong>.\n\nA second reason is that access to learning has become far easier. Online courses, professional qualifications and evening classes allow an adult to study while working, which was rarely possible in the past. Employers increasingly fund this training themselves, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retraining existing staff</strong> is cheaper than recruiting new ones.\n\nThere are benefits beyond employment. Adults who continue to study tend to be more confident and more adaptable, and there is evidence that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained mental activity</strong> helps to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protect memory in later life</strong>. Many people also return to education for subjects they were unable to pursue when young, which is a genuine improvement in quality of life.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that education has become a lifelong process. This is an unavoidable consequence of rapid technological change, but it also gives adults opportunities that earlier generations never had.","phrases":[{"en":"unemployable long before","context":"The main reason is that knowledge and technology now change far faster than they once did. A person who trained as an accountant or a mechanic forty years ago could expect the skills learned at twenty to remain useful until retirement. Today, software, regulations and equipment are replaced every few years, and entire occupations disappear. An employee who stops learning becomes unemployable long before reaching pension age, so continuous training is simply a condition of remaining in work."},{"en":"simply a condition of remaining in work","context":"The main reason is that knowledge and technology now change far faster than they once did. A person who trained as an accountant or a mechanic forty years ago could expect the skills learned at twenty to remain useful until retirement. Today, software, regulations and equipment are replaced every few years, and entire occupations disappear. An employee who stops learning becomes unemployable long before reaching pension age, so continuous training is simply a condition of remaining in work."},{"en":"retraining existing staff","context":"A second reason is that access to learning has become far easier. Online courses, professional qualifications and evening classes allow an adult to study while working, which was rarely possible in the past. Employers increasingly fund this training themselves, since retraining existing staff is cheaper than recruiting new ones."},{"en":"sustained mental activity","context":"There are benefits beyond employment. Adults who continue to study tend to be more confident and more adaptable, and there is evidence that sustained mental activity helps to protect memory in later life. Many people also return to education for subjects they were unable to pursue when young, which is a genuine improvement in quality of life."},{"en":"protect memory in later life","context":"There are benefits beyond employment. Adults who continue to study tend to be more confident and more adaptable, and there is evidence that sustained mental activity helps to protect memory in later life. Many people also return to education for subjects they were unable to pursue when young, which is a genuine improvement in quality of life."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#125","summary":"Education as a lifelong process"},{"id":"writing-task-2-063","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Women in male-dominated university subjects","prompt":"Women remain underrepresented in some university subjects. Some people argue that this imbalance can never be eliminated. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Women remain a small minority in subjects such as engineering, physics and computer science, and some people argue that this imbalance can never be removed. I disagree, because the evidence suggests that it results from social influences rather than from any fixed difference in ability.\n\nThose who believe the gap is permanent usually claim that men and women are naturally drawn to different fields. This argument does not survive comparison between countries and periods. Women now outnumber men in medicine, law and veterinary science, all of which were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overwhelmingly male</strong> within living memory, and the proportion of female computing graduates in several Asian countries is far higher than in Western Europe. A preference that varies so widely cannot be biological.\n\nThe real causes are social, and they begin early. Children are given different toys and praised for different achievements, and by secondary school many girls have absorbed the idea that mathematics and technology are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not meant for them</strong>. Science is frequently taught by men, careers advice reflects the same assumptions, and university departments where women are visibly outnumbered can feel unwelcoming, which discourages those who do enrol from continuing.\n\nBecause the causes are social, they can be changed. Countries that have introduced early exposure to coding, recruited female science teachers, publicised women working in these fields and reformed workplace conditions have all seen female enrolment <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rise steadily</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that this imbalance is permanent. It reflects expectations formed in childhood, and expectations can be altered by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberate policy</strong> in schools and universities.","phrases":[{"en":"overwhelmingly male","context":"Those who believe the gap is permanent usually claim that men and women are naturally drawn to different fields. This argument does not survive comparison between countries and periods. Women now outnumber men in medicine, law and veterinary science, all of which were overwhelmingly male within living memory, and the proportion of female computing graduates in several Asian countries is far higher than in Western Europe. A preference that varies so widely cannot be biological."},{"en":"not meant for them","context":"The real causes are social, and they begin early. Children are given different toys and praised for different achievements, and by secondary school many girls have absorbed the idea that mathematics and technology are not meant for them. Science is frequently taught by men, careers advice reflects the same assumptions, and university departments where women are visibly outnumbered can feel unwelcoming, which discourages those who do enrol from continuing."},{"en":"rise steadily","context":"Because the causes are social, they can be changed. Countries that have introduced early exposure to coding, recruited female science teachers, publicised women working in these fields and reformed workplace conditions have all seen female enrolment rise steadily."},{"en":"deliberate policy","context":"In conclusion, I disagree that this imbalance is permanent. It reflects expectations formed in childhood, and expectations can be altered by deliberate policy in schools and universities."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#126","summary":"Women in male-dominated university subjects"},{"id":"writing-task-2-065","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Are schools still necessary?","prompt":"Some people argue that schools have become unnecessary because children can study at home using the Internet. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Since a great deal of educational material is now freely available online, some people argue that schools are no longer necessary. I strongly disagree, because schools provide far more than the transmission of information.\n\nThe argument for home study has some foundation. Online courses allow a student to progress at their own speed, to repeat difficult explanations and to study subjects their local school does not offer. For children who are seriously ill, live in remote areas or have been badly bullied, this can be genuinely valuable, and in some cases it is the only practical option available.\n\nNevertheless, information is only one part of education. Most children do not study consistently without structure and supervision, and the experience of remote teaching demonstrated this clearly: attainment fell in almost every country, and the children who suffered most were those from poorer homes with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no quiet space and no reliable device</strong>. Removing schools would therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">widen inequality dramatically</strong>.\n\nSchools also teach what cannot be learned alone. Cooperating with people one has not chosen, resolving disagreements, presenting an argument and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accepting correction</strong> are social skills acquired through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">daily practice with others</strong>. A child educated entirely at home may be academically competent but unprepared for a workplace. Schools additionally serve a protective function, since teachers are frequently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the first adults to notice neglect</strong> or abuse at home.\n\nIn conclusion, online resources are a valuable addition to education, but I disagree that they could replace schools. The social, protective and structural roles that schools perform have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no realistic online equivalent</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"no quiet space and no reliable device","context":"Nevertheless, information is only one part of education. Most children do not study consistently without structure and supervision, and the experience of remote teaching demonstrated this clearly: attainment fell in almost every country, and the children who suffered most were those from poorer homes with no quiet space and no reliable device. Removing schools would therefore widen inequality dramatically."},{"en":"widen inequality dramatically","context":"Nevertheless, information is only one part of education. Most children do not study consistently without structure and supervision, and the experience of remote teaching demonstrated this clearly: attainment fell in almost every country, and the children who suffered most were those from poorer homes with no quiet space and no reliable device. Removing schools would therefore widen inequality dramatically."},{"en":"accepting correction","context":"Schools also teach what cannot be learned alone. Cooperating with people one has not chosen, resolving disagreements, presenting an argument and accepting correction are social skills acquired through daily practice with others. A child educated entirely at home may be academically competent but unprepared for a workplace. Schools additionally serve a protective function, since teachers are frequently the first adults to notice neglect or abuse at home."},{"en":"daily practice with others","context":"Schools also teach what cannot be learned alone. Cooperating with people one has not chosen, resolving disagreements, presenting an argument and accepting correction are social skills acquired through daily practice with others. A child educated entirely at home may be academically competent but unprepared for a workplace. Schools additionally serve a protective function, since teachers are frequently the first adults to notice neglect or abuse at home."},{"en":"the first adults to notice neglect","context":"Schools also teach what cannot be learned alone. Cooperating with people one has not chosen, resolving disagreements, presenting an argument and accepting correction are social skills acquired through daily practice with others. A child educated entirely at home may be academically competent but unprepared for a workplace. Schools additionally serve a protective function, since teachers are frequently the first adults to notice neglect or abuse at home."},{"en":"no realistic online equivalent","context":"In conclusion, online resources are a valuable addition to education, but I disagree that they could replace schools. The social, protective and structural roles that schools perform have no realistic online equivalent."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#127","summary":"Are schools still necessary?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-066","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should students evaluate their teachers?","prompt":"Some people believe that high school students should be encouraged to evaluate and criticise their teachers in order to improve the quality of education. Others think this would result in a loss of respect and discipline in the classroom. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people believe that allowing secondary students to evaluate their teachers would raise standards, while others fear it would undermine respect and discipline. In my opinion student feedback is valuable, but only if it is collected carefully and used appropriately.\n\nThe argument in favour is that students observe teaching more closely than anyone else. An inspector visits a classroom once every few years, whereas pupils sit through every lesson and know precisely who explains clearly, who marks work promptly and who does not prepare. Without their views, a weak teacher may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">continue unnoticed for decades</strong>. Being consulted also encourages students to take their own learning more seriously, since it treats them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as participants rather than as passive recipients</strong>.\n\nThe objection is equally reasonable. Adolescents may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reward teachers who are entertaining or lenient</strong> and criticise those who set demanding work, so the teachers most likely to raise attainment could be penalised. There is also evidence from universities that such evaluations reflect prejudice about a teacher&#x27;s gender, age or accent rather than teaching quality. If feedback is public, or affects pay directly, the authority a teacher needs to maintain order may be damaged.\n\nThe sensible approach is therefore a compromise. Feedback should be anonymous, gathered through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">specific questions about clarity and fairness</strong> rather than general popularity, and given to the teacher and head of department as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one source of evidence among several</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I support consulting students, since they possess information nobody else has. It should inform professional judgement, however, rather than function as a public verdict on individual teachers.","phrases":[{"en":"continue unnoticed for decades","context":"The argument in favour is that students observe teaching more closely than anyone else. An inspector visits a classroom once every few years, whereas pupils sit through every lesson and know precisely who explains clearly, who marks work promptly and who does not prepare. Without their views, a weak teacher may continue unnoticed for decades. Being consulted also encourages students to take their own learning more seriously, since it treats them as participants rather than as passive recipients."},{"en":"as participants rather than as passive recipients","context":"The argument in favour is that students observe teaching more closely than anyone else. An inspector visits a classroom once every few years, whereas pupils sit through every lesson and know precisely who explains clearly, who marks work promptly and who does not prepare. Without their views, a weak teacher may continue unnoticed for decades. Being consulted also encourages students to take their own learning more seriously, since it treats them as participants rather than as passive recipients."},{"en":"reward teachers who are entertaining or lenient","context":"The objection is equally reasonable. Adolescents may reward teachers who are entertaining or lenient and criticise those who set demanding work, so the teachers most likely to raise attainment could be penalised. There is also evidence from universities that such evaluations reflect prejudice about a teacher's gender, age or accent rather than teaching quality. If feedback is public, or affects pay directly, the authority a teacher needs to maintain order may be damaged."},{"en":"specific questions about clarity and fairness","context":"The sensible approach is therefore a compromise. Feedback should be anonymous, gathered through specific questions about clarity and fairness rather than general popularity, and given to the teacher and head of department as one source of evidence among several."},{"en":"one source of evidence among several","context":"The sensible approach is therefore a compromise. Feedback should be anonymous, gathered through specific questions about clarity and fairness rather than general popularity, and given to the teacher and head of department as one source of evidence among several."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#128","summary":"Should students evaluate their teachers?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-067","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Broad study or specialisation?","prompt":"It is argued that university students should study a full range of subjects instead of specialising in one particular area. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that university students should study a wide range of subjects rather than concentrating on a single field. I partly agree: some breadth is valuable, but depth is what a degree exists to provide.\n\nThe case for breadth is reasonable. Employers increasingly want graduates who can do several things, since most jobs now combine tasks that once belonged to separate roles — a marketing position may require statistics, design and writing. Studying different subjects also develops flexible thinking, because each discipline approaches problems differently, and a student who has encountered <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more than one way of reasoning</strong> adapts more easily when their field changes.\n\nNevertheless, a degree spread across many subjects risks producing graduates who know a little about everything and are qualified for nothing. Serious understanding of any discipline takes years, and the value of a graduate lies precisely in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">having gone further into a subject</strong> than a person could manage alone. Nobody would want a doctor or a structural engineer whose training had been broad but shallow. Employers also value the qualification because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">it certifies depth</strong>; a degree guaranteeing only general familiarity would tell them very little.\n\nThe sensible arrangement, in my view, is the one many universities already use: a specialised main subject supported by a smaller number of optional courses outside it. That gives students genuine expertise while ensuring they can write clearly, handle data and understand something of a second field.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that students benefit from studying beyond their own discipline, but this should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supplement specialisation rather than replace it</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"more than one way of reasoning","context":"The case for breadth is reasonable. Employers increasingly want graduates who can do several things, since most jobs now combine tasks that once belonged to separate roles — a marketing position may require statistics, design and writing. Studying different subjects also develops flexible thinking, because each discipline approaches problems differently, and a student who has encountered more than one way of reasoning adapts more easily when their field changes."},{"en":"having gone further into a subject","context":"Nevertheless, a degree spread across many subjects risks producing graduates who know a little about everything and are qualified for nothing. Serious understanding of any discipline takes years, and the value of a graduate lies precisely in having gone further into a subject than a person could manage alone. Nobody would want a doctor or a structural engineer whose training had been broad but shallow. Employers also value the qualification because it certifies depth; a degree guaranteeing only general familiarity would tell them very little."},{"en":"it certifies depth","context":"Nevertheless, a degree spread across many subjects risks producing graduates who know a little about everything and are qualified for nothing. Serious understanding of any discipline takes years, and the value of a graduate lies precisely in having gone further into a subject than a person could manage alone. Nobody would want a doctor or a structural engineer whose training had been broad but shallow. Employers also value the qualification because it certifies depth; a degree guaranteeing only general familiarity would tell them very little."},{"en":"supplement specialisation rather than replace it","context":"In conclusion, I agree that students benefit from studying beyond their own discipline, but this should supplement specialisation rather than replace it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#129","summary":"Broad study or specialisation?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-068","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"What education should consist of","prompt":"It is generally believed that education is of vital importance to individual development and to the well-being of societies. What should education consist of in order to fulfil both of these functions?","answer":"Education is important both for personal development and for the well-being of society. In my opinion, a system that serves these purposes must combine a strong foundation in knowledge with independent thinking, practical skills and the ability to cooperate with others.\n\nThe first requirement is an effective academic and vocational foundation. Literacy and numeracy help people understand information, manage everyday tasks and continue learning. Scientific knowledge also supports informed decisions about the world. Alongside academic study, students should have access to respected <strong class=\"key-phrase\">vocational routes</strong> that prepare them for useful work. Training an electrician or a technician can benefit both the learner and a community that needs those skills. Neither route should be treated as a substitute for basic educational quality.\n\nA second requirement is the ability to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evaluate ideas</strong>. Easy access to information does not guarantee that the information is accurate or relevant. Students need opportunities to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">examine evidence</strong>, recognise weak arguments and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">revise a conclusion</strong> when new facts emerge. For example, comparing two explanations of the same issue can be more valuable than memorising a single approved response. Knowledge and reasoning should support each other, since critical thinking is difficult without enough understanding of the subject.\n\nEducation should also develop <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social responsibility</strong> and personal independence. Group projects can teach cooperation, while discussion allows students to practise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disagreeing respectfully</strong>. Teachers&#x27; behaviour matters too: fairness and honesty are more convincing when students see them demonstrated. Explicit lessons about citizenship can reinforce these experiences, rather than being expected to produce good judgement on their own.\n\nIn conclusion, education should help people earn a living, understand their choices and participate constructively in society. This requires <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a balanced curriculum</strong> in which useful knowledge, careful reasoning and responsible behaviour develop together.","phrases":[{"en":"vocational routes","context":"The first requirement is an effective academic and vocational foundation. Literacy and numeracy help people understand information, manage everyday tasks and continue learning. Scientific knowledge also supports informed decisions about the world. Alongside academic study, students should have access to respected vocational routes that prepare them for useful work. Training an electrician or a technician can benefit both the learner and a community that needs those skills. Neither route should be treated as a substitute for basic educational quality."},{"en":"evaluate ideas","context":"A second requirement is the ability to evaluate ideas. Easy access to information does not guarantee that the information is accurate or relevant. Students need opportunities to examine evidence, recognise weak arguments and revise a conclusion when new facts emerge. For example, comparing two explanations of the same issue can be more valuable than memorising a single approved response. Knowledge and reasoning should support each other, since critical thinking is difficult without enough understanding of the subject."},{"en":"examine evidence","context":"A second requirement is the ability to evaluate ideas. Easy access to information does not guarantee that the information is accurate or relevant. Students need opportunities to examine evidence, recognise weak arguments and revise a conclusion when new facts emerge. For example, comparing two explanations of the same issue can be more valuable than memorising a single approved response. Knowledge and reasoning should support each other, since critical thinking is difficult without enough understanding of the subject."},{"en":"revise a conclusion","context":"A second requirement is the ability to evaluate ideas. Easy access to information does not guarantee that the information is accurate or relevant. Students need opportunities to examine evidence, recognise weak arguments and revise a conclusion when new facts emerge. For example, comparing two explanations of the same issue can be more valuable than memorising a single approved response. Knowledge and reasoning should support each other, since critical thinking is difficult without enough understanding of the subject."},{"en":"social responsibility","context":"Education should also develop social responsibility and personal independence. Group projects can teach cooperation, while discussion allows students to practise disagreeing respectfully. Teachers' behaviour matters too: fairness and honesty are more convincing when students see them demonstrated. Explicit lessons about citizenship can reinforce these experiences, rather than being expected to produce good judgement on their own."},{"en":"disagreeing respectfully","context":"Education should also develop social responsibility and personal independence. Group projects can teach cooperation, while discussion allows students to practise disagreeing respectfully. Teachers' behaviour matters too: fairness and honesty are more convincing when students see them demonstrated. Explicit lessons about citizenship can reinforce these experiences, rather than being expected to produce good judgement on their own."},{"en":"a balanced curriculum","context":"In conclusion, education should help people earn a living, understand their choices and participate constructively in society. This requires a balanced curriculum in which useful knowledge, careful reasoning and responsible behaviour develop together."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#130","summary":"What education should consist of"},{"id":"writing-task-2-070","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should students pay the full cost of a degree?","prompt":"It is widely accepted that people with post-school qualifications earn higher salaries than those who are less educated. University students should therefore pay the full cost of obtaining their degree. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Graduates generally earn more than people who leave school at eighteen, and some argue that students should therefore meet the entire cost of their degrees. I disagree, because although graduates do gain personally, society gains at least as much and should pay a share.\n\nThe argument for full fees is not unreasonable. A degree is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a private investment that raises lifetime earnings</strong> substantially, and it seems unfair to ask a taxpayer who never attended university to fund someone who will eventually earn considerably more. Public budgets are also limited, and money spent on universities cannot be spent on schools or hospitals.\n\nThe difficulty is that education produces benefits far beyond the individual. Doctors, engineers, teachers and nurses are all trained at universities, and no country can function without them, whatever the private earnings of the graduates concerned. Higher education also produces the research on which new industries depend. Since the public receives these benefits, it is reasonable for the public to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contribute to the cost</strong>.\n\nFull fees would also change who studies. Several degrees of real social value, such as nursing and teaching, are among the worst paid, so students facing enormous debts would avoid <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exactly the professions a country needs most</strong>. Students from low-income families are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most sensitive to debt</strong>, and they are the ones most likely to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deterred altogether</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that students should bear the full cost. A shared system, in which graduates repay a proportion once they are earning and the state funds the remainder, distributes the burden according to who actually benefits.","phrases":[{"en":"a private investment that raises lifetime earnings","context":"The argument for full fees is not unreasonable. A degree is a private investment that raises lifetime earnings substantially, and it seems unfair to ask a taxpayer who never attended university to fund someone who will eventually earn considerably more. Public budgets are also limited, and money spent on universities cannot be spent on schools or hospitals."},{"en":"contribute to the cost","context":"The difficulty is that education produces benefits far beyond the individual. Doctors, engineers, teachers and nurses are all trained at universities, and no country can function without them, whatever the private earnings of the graduates concerned. Higher education also produces the research on which new industries depend. Since the public receives these benefits, it is reasonable for the public to contribute to the cost."},{"en":"exactly the professions a country needs most","context":"Full fees would also change who studies. Several degrees of real social value, such as nursing and teaching, are among the worst paid, so students facing enormous debts would avoid exactly the professions a country needs most. Students from low-income families are the most sensitive to debt, and they are the ones most likely to be deterred altogether."},{"en":"the most sensitive to debt","context":"Full fees would also change who studies. Several degrees of real social value, such as nursing and teaching, are among the worst paid, so students facing enormous debts would avoid exactly the professions a country needs most. Students from low-income families are the most sensitive to debt, and they are the ones most likely to be deterred altogether."},{"en":"deterred altogether","context":"Full fees would also change who studies. Several degrees of real social value, such as nursing and teaching, are among the worst paid, so students facing enormous debts would avoid exactly the professions a country needs most. Students from low-income families are the most sensitive to debt, and they are the ones most likely to be deterred altogether."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#131","summary":"Should students pay the full cost of a degree?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Who should teach children to behave well?","prompt":"Some people think that parents should teach their children how to be good members of society. Others, however, believe that school is the best place to learn this. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Children learn social behaviour from both their families and their schools. Parents have the strongest influence in the early years, while schools give children daily opportunities to practise cooperation and responsibility. I therefore believe that both share this duty.\n\nParents shape a child&#x27;s first ideas about honesty, respect and self-control. Children observe how adults speak to others, keep promises and deal with disagreement. Advice is useful, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">example is more powerful</strong>. A home in which adults act responsibly gives children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear moral foundation</strong> before they enter wider society.\n\nSchools then expand this learning. In class, children must wait their turn, follow common rules and work with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">people from different backgrounds</strong>. Teachers can discuss bullying, fairness and civic responsibility, while group projects allow students to put these ideas into practice. Schools are particularly important when children do not receive consistent guidance at home, but they cannot <strong class=\"key-phrase\">replace parental example</strong>.\n\nCooperation between parents and teachers is especially important when a child behaves badly. If the two sides send opposite messages, rules lose their force. Regular communication, clear consequences and shared expectations help adults identify whether a problem begins at home, at school or online. This creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a consistent framework for social development</strong>. This shared approach is especially valuable during adolescence, when peer pressure becomes stronger and rules are more likely to be questioned.\n\nIn conclusion, parents should introduce the values that make someone a good member of society, and schools should reinforce them through teaching and shared experience. The most effective approach is close cooperation between home and school, so that children receive <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistent expectations in different settings</strong>.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"example is more powerful","context":"Parents shape a child's first ideas about honesty, respect and self-control. Children observe how adults speak to others, keep promises and deal with disagreement. Advice is useful, but example is more powerful. A home in which adults act responsibly gives children a clear moral foundation before they enter wider society."},{"en":"a clear moral foundation","context":"Parents shape a child's first ideas about honesty, respect and self-control. Children observe how adults speak to others, keep promises and deal with disagreement. Advice is useful, but example is more powerful. A home in which adults act responsibly gives children a clear moral foundation before they enter wider society."},{"en":"people from different backgrounds","context":"Schools then expand this learning. In class, children must wait their turn, follow common rules and work with people from different backgrounds. Teachers can discuss bullying, fairness and civic responsibility, while group projects allow students to put these ideas into practice. Schools are particularly important when children do not receive consistent guidance at home, but they cannot replace parental example."},{"en":"replace parental example","context":"Schools then expand this learning. In class, children must wait their turn, follow common rules and work with people from different backgrounds. Teachers can discuss bullying, fairness and civic responsibility, while group projects allow students to put these ideas into practice. Schools are particularly important when children do not receive consistent guidance at home, but they cannot replace parental example."},{"en":"a consistent framework for social development","context":"Cooperation between parents and teachers is especially important when a child behaves badly. If the two sides send opposite messages, rules lose their force. Regular communication, clear consequences and shared expectations help adults identify whether a problem begins at home, at school or online. This creates a consistent framework for social development. This shared approach is especially valuable during adolescence, when peer pressure becomes stronger and rules are more likely to be questioned."},{"en":"consistent expectations in different settings","context":"In conclusion, parents should introduce the values that make someone a good member of society, and schools should reinforce them through teaching and shared experience. The most effective approach is close cooperation between home and school, so that children receive consistent expectations in different settings."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Whether parents or schools should teach children to behave well","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-071","_displayNo":"#132"},{"id":"writing-task-2-072","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Boarding school or day school?","prompt":"Some people argue that it is good for children to go to a boarding school away from their families. Others believe that children should live at home and attend a day school. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some parents send their children to boarding school, while others believe children should remain at home and attend a local day school. In my opinion a day school is the better choice for most children, although boarding suits particular circumstances.\n\nThe advantages of boarding are real. Pupils live in a structured environment where study time is supervised, and facilities are usually excellent because everything is concentrated on one site. Living away from home also develops independence: children learn to manage their own routines, resolve disagreements and cope <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without a parent solving every problem</strong>, which many find valuable when they later go to university.\n\nNevertheless, the disadvantages are significant for younger children. Family relationships depend on daily contact, and a child who sees their parents only during holidays may never develop the closeness that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">provides security in difficult moments</strong>. Boarding schools are also enclosed communities, and a pupil who is unhappy, homesick or bullied <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot escape at the end of the day</strong>. There is a further concern that such schools are socially narrow, since almost everyone comes from a similar background.\n\nIn my view boarding makes sense where families live abroad, move frequently or are too far from a suitable school, and it works far better for teenagers than for young children. Where a good local school exists, however, there is little reason to send a child away.\n\nIn conclusion, boarding schools build independence, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the emotional cost is high</strong>, and I believe most children benefit more from living with their families.","phrases":[{"en":"without a parent solving every problem","context":"The advantages of boarding are real. Pupils live in a structured environment where study time is supervised, and facilities are usually excellent because everything is concentrated on one site. Living away from home also develops independence: children learn to manage their own routines, resolve disagreements and cope without a parent solving every problem, which many find valuable when they later go to university."},{"en":"provides security in difficult moments","context":"Nevertheless, the disadvantages are significant for younger children. Family relationships depend on daily contact, and a child who sees their parents only during holidays may never develop the closeness that provides security in difficult moments. Boarding schools are also enclosed communities, and a pupil who is unhappy, homesick or bullied cannot escape at the end of the day. There is a further concern that such schools are socially narrow, since almost everyone comes from a similar background."},{"en":"cannot escape at the end of the day","context":"Nevertheless, the disadvantages are significant for younger children. Family relationships depend on daily contact, and a child who sees their parents only during holidays may never develop the closeness that provides security in difficult moments. Boarding schools are also enclosed communities, and a pupil who is unhappy, homesick or bullied cannot escape at the end of the day. There is a further concern that such schools are socially narrow, since almost everyone comes from a similar background."},{"en":"the emotional cost is high","context":"In conclusion, boarding schools build independence, but the emotional cost is high, and I believe most children benefit more from living with their families."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#133","summary":"Boarding school or day school?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-074","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"A year of work experience before university","prompt":"Some people believe that students who graduate from high school should spend a year gaining work experience before going to university. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes suggested that school leavers should spend a year gaining work experience before beginning a university course. I agree with this proposal, provided that the year is genuinely structured rather than simply a delay.\n\nThe strongest argument concerns the choice of course. Most students select a degree at seventeen, with almost no knowledge of what the corresponding profession actually involves, and a significant proportion later change subject or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">abandon their studies altogether</strong>. A year in a hospital, an office or a laboratory gives <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a far more realistic impression</strong>, and a student who confirms their interest in this way studies with much greater purpose.\n\nWork experience also develops qualities that school does not. Handling money, dealing with difficult customers, arriving punctually and working with colleagues of different ages teach responsibility in a way that examinations cannot. Students who have supported themselves financially also tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manage their time more carefully</strong> at university, having learned <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what an hour of work is actually worth</strong>.\n\nThere are objections worth acknowledging. Some students <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lose academic momentum</strong> and find it hard to return to studying, and a year of unpaid work is easier for wealthy families to arrange than for poor ones, which risks creating a further advantage for those who already have one.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that a year of work experience benefits most school leavers, since it improves their choice of course and their maturity. It should, however, be organised and paid, so that it is available to every student rather than only to those who can afford it.","phrases":[{"en":"abandon their studies altogether","context":"The strongest argument concerns the choice of course. Most students select a degree at seventeen, with almost no knowledge of what the corresponding profession actually involves, and a significant proportion later change subject or abandon their studies altogether. A year in a hospital, an office or a laboratory gives a far more realistic impression, and a student who confirms their interest in this way studies with much greater purpose."},{"en":"a far more realistic impression","context":"The strongest argument concerns the choice of course. Most students select a degree at seventeen, with almost no knowledge of what the corresponding profession actually involves, and a significant proportion later change subject or abandon their studies altogether. A year in a hospital, an office or a laboratory gives a far more realistic impression, and a student who confirms their interest in this way studies with much greater purpose."},{"en":"manage their time more carefully","context":"Work experience also develops qualities that school does not. Handling money, dealing with difficult customers, arriving punctually and working with colleagues of different ages teach responsibility in a way that examinations cannot. Students who have supported themselves financially also tend to manage their time more carefully at university, having learned what an hour of work is actually worth."},{"en":"what an hour of work is actually worth","context":"Work experience also develops qualities that school does not. Handling money, dealing with difficult customers, arriving punctually and working with colleagues of different ages teach responsibility in a way that examinations cannot. Students who have supported themselves financially also tend to manage their time more carefully at university, having learned what an hour of work is actually worth."},{"en":"lose academic momentum","context":"There are objections worth acknowledging. Some students lose academic momentum and find it hard to return to studying, and a year of unpaid work is easier for wealthy families to arrange than for poor ones, which risks creating a further advantage for those who already have one."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#134","summary":"A year of work experience before university"},{"id":"writing-task-2-075","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Is distance learning as good as attending university?","prompt":"Many people join distance-learning programmes and study at home, but some people think that this cannot bring the same benefit as attending college or university. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Distance learning has expanded rapidly, and there is disagreement about whether it can match a conventional university education. In my opinion it is genuinely effective for some learners and some subjects, but for most students it does not replace attending in person.\n\nThe weaknesses are well established. Studying alone demands <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a level of self-discipline</strong> that many people, particularly younger students, have not yet developed. Without fixed classes, deadlines and a tutor who notices when work stops arriving, a significant proportion simply fall behind, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">completion rates for online courses</strong> are considerably lower than for campus programmes. The experience of remote teaching during the pandemic demonstrated this clearly, and the students who suffered most were those already struggling.\n\nDistance learning also removes a great deal that has nothing to do with the syllabus. Arguing with other students, working in a group, using a laboratory and being able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ask a lecturer a question immediately</strong> are all part of how understanding develops, and they are difficult to reproduce through a screen. Certain subjects, such as medicine and engineering, cannot be taught this way at all.\n\nThere are, however, real advantages. For working adults, parents and people in remote areas, distance learning is often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only realistic option</strong>, and a motivated student can achieve a great deal through it. It is also far cheaper, and for someone who simply needs a specific qualification while continuing to work, it is an entirely sensible route.\n\nIn conclusion, distance learning is genuinely valuable for disciplined and self-motivated learners, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opens doors that would otherwise stay closed</strong>. For most young students, however, I do not believe it delivers the same benefit as attending in person.","phrases":[{"en":"a level of self-discipline","context":"The weaknesses are well established. Studying alone demands a level of self-discipline that many people, particularly younger students, have not yet developed. Without fixed classes, deadlines and a tutor who notices when work stops arriving, a significant proportion simply fall behind, and completion rates for online courses are considerably lower than for campus programmes. The experience of remote teaching during the pandemic demonstrated this clearly, and the students who suffered most were those already struggling."},{"en":"completion rates for online courses","context":"The weaknesses are well established. Studying alone demands a level of self-discipline that many people, particularly younger students, have not yet developed. Without fixed classes, deadlines and a tutor who notices when work stops arriving, a significant proportion simply fall behind, and completion rates for online courses are considerably lower than for campus programmes. The experience of remote teaching during the pandemic demonstrated this clearly, and the students who suffered most were those already struggling."},{"en":"ask a lecturer a question immediately","context":"Distance learning also removes a great deal that has nothing to do with the syllabus. Arguing with other students, working in a group, using a laboratory and being able to ask a lecturer a question immediately are all part of how understanding develops, and they are difficult to reproduce through a screen. Certain subjects, such as medicine and engineering, cannot be taught this way at all."},{"en":"the only realistic option","context":"There are, however, real advantages. For working adults, parents and people in remote areas, distance learning is often the only realistic option, and a motivated student can achieve a great deal through it. It is also far cheaper, and for someone who simply needs a specific qualification while continuing to work, it is an entirely sensible route."},{"en":"opens doors that would otherwise stay closed","context":"In conclusion, distance learning is genuinely valuable for disciplined and self-motivated learners, and it opens doors that would otherwise stay closed. For most young students, however, I do not believe it delivers the same benefit as attending in person."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#135","summary":"Is distance learning as good as attending university?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-076","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Private secondary schools","prompt":"Some people think that students benefit greatly from attending private secondary schools. Others believe that private schools have a negative effect on young people and on society as a whole. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Private secondary schools attract strong opinions. Some believe they give pupils an outstanding education, while others argue that they harm both young people and society. In my view they do benefit their own students, but at a real cost to everyone else.\n\nThe advantages for those who attend are difficult to deny. Private schools have smaller classes, better laboratories and sports facilities, and they can pay more to attract experienced teachers. They also offer activities that state schools cannot afford, such as music tuition, drama and foreign exchanges. Examination results reflect this, and their pupils are consistently more likely to reach the leading universities and the best-paid professions.\n\nThe objections, however, are serious. When wealthy families remove their children from the state system, they also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove the parents most able to raise money</strong>, question standards and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">put pressure on politicians</strong>, and state schools decline as a result. The wider effect is on social mobility, since a child&#x27;s future comes to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on parental income rather than on ability</strong>. In several countries a small number of private schools supply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a disproportionate share</strong> of judges, senior officials and politicians, which means those who make decisions have very little experience of ordinary life.\n\nIn my opinion the solution is not to abolish these schools, which would simply push wealthy families abroad, but to fund state education properly so that the gap in quality closes.\n\nIn conclusion, private schools serve their own pupils well but deepen inequality, and I believe the fairer response is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise standards in state schools</strong> rather than accept a divided system.","phrases":[{"en":"remove the parents most able to raise money","context":"The objections, however, are serious. When wealthy families remove their children from the state system, they also remove the parents most able to raise money, question standards and put pressure on politicians, and state schools decline as a result. The wider effect is on social mobility, since a child's future comes to depend on parental income rather than on ability. In several countries a small number of private schools supply a disproportionate share of judges, senior officials and politicians, which means those who make decisions have very little experience of ordinary life."},{"en":"put pressure on politicians","context":"The objections, however, are serious. When wealthy families remove their children from the state system, they also remove the parents most able to raise money, question standards and put pressure on politicians, and state schools decline as a result. The wider effect is on social mobility, since a child's future comes to depend on parental income rather than on ability. In several countries a small number of private schools supply a disproportionate share of judges, senior officials and politicians, which means those who make decisions have very little experience of ordinary life."},{"en":"depend on parental income rather than on ability","context":"The objections, however, are serious. When wealthy families remove their children from the state system, they also remove the parents most able to raise money, question standards and put pressure on politicians, and state schools decline as a result. The wider effect is on social mobility, since a child's future comes to depend on parental income rather than on ability. In several countries a small number of private schools supply a disproportionate share of judges, senior officials and politicians, which means those who make decisions have very little experience of ordinary life."},{"en":"a disproportionate share","context":"The objections, however, are serious. When wealthy families remove their children from the state system, they also remove the parents most able to raise money, question standards and put pressure on politicians, and state schools decline as a result. The wider effect is on social mobility, since a child's future comes to depend on parental income rather than on ability. In several countries a small number of private schools supply a disproportionate share of judges, senior officials and politicians, which means those who make decisions have very little experience of ordinary life."},{"en":"raise standards in state schools","context":"In conclusion, private schools serve their own pupils well but deepen inequality, and I believe the fairer response is to raise standards in state schools rather than accept a divided system."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#136","summary":"Private secondary schools"},{"id":"writing-task-2-077","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should teachers teach right and wrong?","prompt":"Some people think that teachers should be responsible for teaching students to judge right and wrong and to behave well. Others say that teachers should only teach academic subjects. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People disagree about how wide a teacher&#x27;s responsibilities should be. Some believe that schools must teach children right from wrong, while others argue that teachers should concentrate on academic subjects alone. In my opinion, moral guidance cannot realistically be separated from teaching.\n\nThose who favour a narrow academic role make a reasonable point. Teachers are trained in mathematics, science or languages, not in ethics, and class sizes are already large. Every hour spent on behaviour is an hour <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not spent on the curriculum</strong>, and examination results are what determine a child&#x27;s future opportunities. There is also the question of whose values would be taught, since families differ in religion, politics and culture, and many parents would resent a school <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imposing beliefs</strong> they do not share.\n\nThose on the other side reply that children spend a large part of their waking lives at school, and that behaviour is learned there whether teachers intend it or not. A classroom cannot function unless pupils are honest, punctual and respectful, so teachers are obliged to deal with bullying, cheating and rudeness every day. Some families also provide very little guidance at home, and for those children school may be the only place where fair treatment is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consistently modelled</strong>.\n\nMy own view is closer to the second position. Teachers should not deliver lessons in religion or politics, but they should insist on a small number of principles that almost everyone accepts: honesty, respect for others and responsibility for one&#x27;s own actions. These are taught through example and consistent rules rather than through separate lessons.\n\nIn conclusion, academic instruction must remain the priority, but basic moral standards are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unavoidable part</strong> of a teacher&#x27;s work.","phrases":[{"en":"not spent on the curriculum","context":"Those who favour a narrow academic role make a reasonable point. Teachers are trained in mathematics, science or languages, not in ethics, and class sizes are already large. Every hour spent on behaviour is an hour not spent on the curriculum, and examination results are what determine a child's future opportunities. There is also the question of whose values would be taught, since families differ in religion, politics and culture, and many parents would resent a school imposing beliefs they do not share."},{"en":"imposing beliefs","context":"Those who favour a narrow academic role make a reasonable point. Teachers are trained in mathematics, science or languages, not in ethics, and class sizes are already large. Every hour spent on behaviour is an hour not spent on the curriculum, and examination results are what determine a child's future opportunities. There is also the question of whose values would be taught, since families differ in religion, politics and culture, and many parents would resent a school imposing beliefs they do not share."},{"en":"consistently modelled","context":"Those on the other side reply that children spend a large part of their waking lives at school, and that behaviour is learned there whether teachers intend it or not. A classroom cannot function unless pupils are honest, punctual and respectful, so teachers are obliged to deal with bullying, cheating and rudeness every day. Some families also provide very little guidance at home, and for those children school may be the only place where fair treatment is consistently modelled."},{"en":"an unavoidable part","context":"In conclusion, academic instruction must remain the priority, but basic moral standards are an unavoidable part of a teacher's work."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#137","summary":"Should teachers teach right and wrong?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-080","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should schools drop music and sport?","prompt":"Schools should teach children the academic subjects that are most relevant to their future careers, so other subjects such as music and sport are not important. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that schools should concentrate on the academic subjects that lead directly to employment, and that music and sport are therefore of little value. I disagree with this view, because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">defines the purpose of education far too narrowly</strong>.\n\nThe argument does contain something reasonable. School hours are limited, and literacy, numeracy and scientific understanding determine what a young person can realistically do for the rest of their life. Employers rarely ask whether a candidate played in an orchestra, and a school that produced excellent musicians but weak readers would be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">failing in its central duty</strong>.\n\nHowever, this reasoning misunderstands how the subjects relate to one another. Physical activity improves concentration, sleep and mood, so pupils who exercise regularly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perform better academically rather than worse</strong>; removing sport would damage the very results it is meant to protect. Music has a similar effect, since learning an instrument demands sustained daily practice and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trains exactly the patience</strong> that difficult academic work requires.\n\nThere is also a serious objection to the premise itself. Nobody can predict which careers will exist in twenty years, and the qualities employers consistently name — creativity, teamwork, discipline and the ability to perform under pressure — are developed at least as much on a sports field or in a rehearsal as in a classroom. For some pupils, music or sport is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only part of school they enjoy</strong>, and it is often what keeps them attending at all.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that music and sport are unimportant. They support academic achievement and develop qualities that no examination measures.","phrases":[{"en":"defines the purpose of education far too narrowly","context":"It is sometimes argued that schools should concentrate on the academic subjects that lead directly to employment, and that music and sport are therefore of little value. I disagree with this view, because it defines the purpose of education far too narrowly."},{"en":"failing in its central duty","context":"The argument does contain something reasonable. School hours are limited, and literacy, numeracy and scientific understanding determine what a young person can realistically do for the rest of their life. Employers rarely ask whether a candidate played in an orchestra, and a school that produced excellent musicians but weak readers would be failing in its central duty."},{"en":"perform better academically rather than worse","context":"However, this reasoning misunderstands how the subjects relate to one another. Physical activity improves concentration, sleep and mood, so pupils who exercise regularly perform better academically rather than worse; removing sport would damage the very results it is meant to protect. Music has a similar effect, since learning an instrument demands sustained daily practice and trains exactly the patience that difficult academic work requires."},{"en":"trains exactly the patience","context":"However, this reasoning misunderstands how the subjects relate to one another. Physical activity improves concentration, sleep and mood, so pupils who exercise regularly perform better academically rather than worse; removing sport would damage the very results it is meant to protect. Music has a similar effect, since learning an instrument demands sustained daily practice and trains exactly the patience that difficult academic work requires."},{"en":"the only part of school they enjoy","context":"There is also a serious objection to the premise itself. Nobody can predict which careers will exist in twenty years, and the qualities employers consistently name — creativity, teamwork, discipline and the ability to perform under pressure — are developed at least as much on a sports field or in a rehearsal as in a classroom. For some pupils, music or sport is the only part of school they enjoy, and it is often what keeps them attending at all."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#138","summary":"Should schools drop music and sport?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Separate schools for boys and girls","prompt":"Some people think that it is better to educate boys and girls in separate schools. Others, however, believe that boys and girls benefit more from attending mixed schools. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people believe that boys and girls learn better in separate schools, while others prefer mixed education. Although single-sex schools may reduce certain distractions, I believe mixed schools prepare students more effectively for adult life.\n\nSupporters of separate schools argue that teachers can adapt lessons to different learning patterns and that students may feel less pressure to perform for the opposite sex. In some subjects, girls or boys may also participate more confidently when they are not worried about stereotypes. These schools can therefore create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a focused academic environment</strong> for some learners.\n\nHowever, the adult world is mixed, and schools should help young people communicate naturally with everyone. In a mixed classroom, students learn to cooperate, challenge stereotypes and judge classmates by ability rather than gender. Problems such as distraction or teasing should be managed through good discipline, not by separating students. Regular contact also helps young people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">develop mutual respect and social confidence</strong>.\n\nIt is also difficult to prove that gender separation itself produces better results. School quality, class size, teacher skill and family support often matter more. A well-run mixed school can address different learning needs through flexible teaching, while avoiding the assumption that all boys or all girls learn in the same way. This is a more evidence-based approach.\n\nIn conclusion, single-sex education can work well in particular cases, but its advantages are not strong enough to justify broad separation. Mixed schools offer both academic learning and realistic social experience, making them better suited to preparing students for study, work and public life.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"a focused academic environment","context":"Supporters of separate schools argue that teachers can adapt lessons to different learning patterns and that students may feel less pressure to perform for the opposite sex. In some subjects, girls or boys may also participate more confidently when they are not worried about stereotypes. These schools can therefore create a focused academic environment for some learners."},{"en":"develop mutual respect and social confidence","context":"However, the adult world is mixed, and schools should help young people communicate naturally with everyone. In a mixed classroom, students learn to cooperate, challenge stereotypes and judge classmates by ability rather than gender. Problems such as distraction or teasing should be managed through good discipline, not by separating students. Regular contact also helps young people develop mutual respect and social confidence."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Separate schools for boys and girls compared with mixed schools","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-081","_displayNo":"#139"},{"id":"writing-task-2-082","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Employable skills or knowledge for its own sake?","prompt":"Nowadays, some universities teach graduates practical skills that help them find employment, while others believe that the main function of university is to pursue knowledge for its own sake. What is your opinion?","answer":"Universities have two important roles: they develop knowledge and prepare students for adult working life. In my view, these aims are not in conflict. A strong university education should combine academic understanding with practical skills.\n\nKnowledge for its own sake remains valuable. Subjects such as history, mathematics and philosophy train students to analyse evidence, question assumptions and build logical arguments. These abilities are useful far beyond one job. They also allow universities to conduct research that may have no immediate commercial use but can later produce major social benefits. Reducing higher education to short-term job training would therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">narrow the purpose of university</strong>.\n\nAt the same time, graduates need to enter a competitive labour market. Universities should teach students how to communicate clearly, work in teams, solve unfamiliar problems and use relevant technology. Internships, project work and careers advice can help students <strong class=\"key-phrase\">connect theory with practice</strong>. This does not mean that employers should dictate the entire curriculum; rather, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical experience should support serious academic study</strong>.\n\nPractical preparation already works well in fields such as medicine and engineering, where students spend years on hospital wards or in industrial placements <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without losing scientific rigour</strong>. Some countries extend this idea at an institutional level, running applied-science universities alongside research universities so that students who want a profession quickly and those who want an academic career can choose the right kind of course. Expanding this sort of structured choice, rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">diluting every degree equally</strong>, would let more graduates leave with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">skills that transfer between different careers</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, universities should not choose between knowledge and employability. Their best contribution is to give students a deep understanding of a subject while also developing skills that can be transferred to different careers. This balanced approach would produce graduates who are both intellectually independent and work-ready.","phrases":[{"en":"narrow the purpose of university","context":"Knowledge for its own sake remains valuable. Subjects such as history, mathematics and philosophy train students to analyse evidence, question assumptions and build logical arguments. These abilities are useful far beyond one job. They also allow universities to conduct research that may have no immediate commercial use but can later produce major social benefits. Reducing higher education to short-term job training would therefore narrow the purpose of university."},{"en":"connect theory with practice","context":"At the same time, graduates need to enter a competitive labour market. Universities should teach students how to communicate clearly, work in teams, solve unfamiliar problems and use relevant technology. Internships, project work and careers advice can help students connect theory with practice. This does not mean that employers should dictate the entire curriculum; rather, practical experience should support serious academic study."},{"en":"practical experience should support serious academic study","context":"At the same time, graduates need to enter a competitive labour market. Universities should teach students how to communicate clearly, work in teams, solve unfamiliar problems and use relevant technology. Internships, project work and careers advice can help students connect theory with practice. This does not mean that employers should dictate the entire curriculum; rather, practical experience should support serious academic study."},{"en":"without losing scientific rigour","context":"Practical preparation already works well in fields such as medicine and engineering, where students spend years on hospital wards or in industrial placements without losing scientific rigour. Some countries extend this idea at an institutional level, running applied-science universities alongside research universities so that students who want a profession quickly and those who want an academic career can choose the right kind of course. Expanding this sort of structured choice, rather than diluting every degree equally, would let more graduates leave with skills that transfer between different careers."},{"en":"diluting every degree equally","context":"Practical preparation already works well in fields such as medicine and engineering, where students spend years on hospital wards or in industrial placements without losing scientific rigour. Some countries extend this idea at an institutional level, running applied-science universities alongside research universities so that students who want a profession quickly and those who want an academic career can choose the right kind of course. Expanding this sort of structured choice, rather than diluting every degree equally, would let more graduates leave with skills that transfer between different careers."},{"en":"skills that transfer between different careers","context":"Practical preparation already works well in fields such as medicine and engineering, where students spend years on hospital wards or in industrial placements without losing scientific rigour. Some countries extend this idea at an institutional level, running applied-science universities alongside research universities so that students who want a profession quickly and those who want an academic career can choose the right kind of course. Expanding this sort of structured choice, rather than diluting every degree equally, would let more graduates leave with skills that transfer between different careers."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#140","summary":"Some universities offer graduate students skills that assist them to find…"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Is television useful for education?","prompt":"Some people say that television is useful for education, while others say it is useful only for entertainment. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Television can educate and entertain, and these functions often overlap. In my view, its educational value is significant, but most viewers use it mainly for relaxation. The quality of the programme matters more than the medium itself.\n\nEducational television can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make difficult subjects easy to grasp</strong>. A documentary can take a class to the deep ocean or inside a volcano, and film shot in slow motion can show a chemical reaction that no school laboratory could safely run. News programmes also improve public knowledge when they explain an issue carefully rather than simply report the headline. For children and adults with limited access to books or courses, television can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bring useful knowledge into the home</strong> at no extra cost.\n\nAt the same time, entertainment is the main reason many people switch on the television. Drama, sport and comedy provide a simple way to rest after work. This is not necessarily negative, because good entertainment can reflect social problems, introduce new cultures and encourage discussion. The problem begins when viewers watch passively for many hours or when channels <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on sensational content to hold attention</strong>.\n\nWhat television does well, though, is not what a book or a classroom does. It can show a process, a place or a period in a way that makes it feel real, but it moves at its own pace and cannot be questioned, so viewers tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep the impression and lose the detail</strong>. This is why the strongest programmes are supported by reliable information elsewhere — a website, a booklet or a lesson that follows — which is what allows a broadcast to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">engaging without becoming superficial</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, television is neither purely educational nor purely entertaining, and the two functions are not easily separated. What decides its value is what is broadcast and how it is watched, and the most useful change would be for viewers to choose programmes deliberately rather than leave the set running.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"make difficult subjects easy to grasp","context":"Educational television can make difficult subjects easy to grasp. A documentary can take a class to the deep ocean or inside a volcano, and film shot in slow motion can show a chemical reaction that no school laboratory could safely run. News programmes also improve public knowledge when they explain an issue carefully rather than simply report the headline. For children and adults with limited access to books or courses, television can bring useful knowledge into the home at no extra cost."},{"en":"bring useful knowledge into the home","context":"Educational television can make difficult subjects easy to grasp. A documentary can take a class to the deep ocean or inside a volcano, and film shot in slow motion can show a chemical reaction that no school laboratory could safely run. News programmes also improve public knowledge when they explain an issue carefully rather than simply report the headline. For children and adults with limited access to books or courses, television can bring useful knowledge into the home at no extra cost."},{"en":"rely on sensational content to hold attention","context":"At the same time, entertainment is the main reason many people switch on the television. Drama, sport and comedy provide a simple way to rest after work. This is not necessarily negative, because good entertainment can reflect social problems, introduce new cultures and encourage discussion. The problem begins when viewers watch passively for many hours or when channels rely on sensational content to hold attention."},{"en":"keep the impression and lose the detail","context":"What television does well, though, is not what a book or a classroom does. It can show a process, a place or a period in a way that makes it feel real, but it moves at its own pace and cannot be questioned, so viewers tend to keep the impression and lose the detail. This is why the strongest programmes are supported by reliable information elsewhere — a website, a booklet or a lesson that follows — which is what allows a broadcast to be engaging without becoming superficial."},{"en":"engaging without becoming superficial","context":"What television does well, though, is not what a book or a classroom does. It can show a process, a place or a period in a way that makes it feel real, but it moves at its own pace and cannot be questioned, so viewers tend to keep the impression and lose the detail. This is why the strongest programmes are supported by reliable information elsewhere — a website, a booklet or a lesson that follows — which is what allows a broadcast to be engaging without becoming superficial."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Whether television is useful for education or only for entertainment","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-083","_displayNo":"#141"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Music, art and drama against other subjects","prompt":"Some say that music, art and drama are as important as other school subjects, especially at the primary level. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Music, art and drama deserve an important place in primary education. They should not replace literacy, mathematics or science, but they support children&#x27;s development in ways that academic subjects alone cannot.\n\nCreative subjects help young children <strong class=\"key-phrase\">express ideas before they have the vocabulary</strong> to explain them fully. Drawing, singing and acting also develop concentration, coordination and confidence. A child who performs in a short play must listen to others, remember a sequence and manage nervousness. These are transferable skills that support learning across the curriculum.\n\nThe arts also make school more inclusive. Some pupils who struggle with written tests may show strong visual, musical or practical ability. Giving these children a chance to succeed can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve motivation and attendance</strong>. In addition, creative activities introduce students to their own culture and to unfamiliar traditions, helping them develop <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curiosity and cultural awareness</strong>.\n\nThere is also a longer-term argument about talent. Creative industries such as design, film and digital media now employ a significant share of the workforce, and ability in these fields often first appears at primary age. A pupil who never draws, performs or composes may simply never discover a talent worth developing, much as a footballer would never be discovered without ever being given a ball. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Spotting this kind of talent early</strong> is arguably as valuable as anything taught in a standard lesson, provided timetables protect enough regular time for these subjects rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treating them as an occasional extra</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, primary schools should treat the arts as a core part of a balanced education, while still protecting enough time for basic academic skills. The aim is not to train every child as an artist, but to use creative work to build confident, observant and cooperative learners.","topic":"Education","phrases":[{"en":"express ideas before they have the vocabulary","context":"Creative subjects help young children express ideas before they have the vocabulary to explain them fully. Drawing, singing and acting also develop concentration, coordination and confidence. A child who performs in a short play must listen to others, remember a sequence and manage nervousness. These are transferable skills that support learning across the curriculum."},{"en":"improve motivation and attendance","context":"The arts also make school more inclusive. Some pupils who struggle with written tests may show strong visual, musical or practical ability. Giving these children a chance to succeed can improve motivation and attendance. In addition, creative activities introduce students to their own culture and to unfamiliar traditions, helping them develop curiosity and cultural awareness."},{"en":"curiosity and cultural awareness","context":"The arts also make school more inclusive. Some pupils who struggle with written tests may show strong visual, musical or practical ability. Giving these children a chance to succeed can improve motivation and attendance. In addition, creative activities introduce students to their own culture and to unfamiliar traditions, helping them develop curiosity and cultural awareness."},{"en":"Spotting this kind of talent early","context":"There is also a longer-term argument about talent. Creative industries such as design, film and digital media now employ a significant share of the workforce, and ability in these fields often first appears at primary age. A pupil who never draws, performs or composes may simply never discover a talent worth developing, much as a footballer would never be discovered without ever being given a ball. Spotting this kind of talent early is arguably as valuable as anything taught in a standard lesson, provided timetables protect enough regular time for these subjects rather than treating them as an occasional extra."},{"en":"treating them as an occasional extra","context":"There is also a longer-term argument about talent. Creative industries such as design, film and digital media now employ a significant share of the workforce, and ability in these fields often first appears at primary age. A pupil who never draws, performs or composes may simply never discover a talent worth developing, much as a footballer would never be discovered without ever being given a ball. Spotting this kind of talent early is arguably as valuable as anything taught in a standard lesson, provided timetables protect enough regular time for these subjects rather than treating them as an occasional extra."}],"subtype":"opinion","summary":"Whether music, art and drama matter as much as other school subjects","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-084","_displayNo":"#142"},{"id":"writing-task-2-085","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"University education","prompt":"Some people believe the aim of university education is to help graduates get better jobs. Others believe there are much wider benefits of university education for both individuals and society. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"The purpose of university education is often debated. Some regard it primarily as professional preparation, whereas others emphasise its broader intellectual and social value. Although employability is an essential outcome, I believe universities serve society best when they combine vocational relevance with the development of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independent judgement</strong>.\n\nThose who prioritise employment have a strong practical case. Higher education requires substantial investment from students, families and governments, so graduates reasonably expect <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a measurable return on that investment</strong>. Degree programmes can provide specialist knowledge, professional accreditation and access to occupations that would otherwise remain closed. In fields such as medicine, engineering and law, universities also protect the public by ensuring that entrants meet rigorous standards. From this perspective, a course that ignores labour-market realities may leave students heavily indebted but poorly equipped for work.\n\nHowever, reducing university to job training would overlook its wider contribution. Students learn to evaluate evidence, challenge assumptions and communicate across cultural or disciplinary boundaries. These capacities help individuals <strong class=\"key-phrase\">navigate complex public and private decisions</strong> long after specific technical knowledge becomes outdated. Universities also generate research, sustain cultural institutions and create spaces in which contested ideas can be examined rather than merely repeated. A society with citizens who can reason critically is better able to resist misinformation and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold powerful institutions accountable</strong>.\n\nIn my view, the two purposes are not mutually exclusive. Programmes should maintain academic depth while incorporating placements, collaborative projects and transferable skills. This approach would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bridge the gap between scholarship and professional practice</strong> without turning every subject into narrow occupational training.\n\nIn conclusion, better employment is a legitimate aim of university education, but it is not the only one. The most valuable institutions prepare graduates both to earn a living and to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">participate intelligently in civic life</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"independent judgement","context":"The purpose of university education is often debated. Some regard it primarily as professional preparation, whereas others emphasise its broader intellectual and social value. Although employability is an essential outcome, I believe universities serve society best when they combine vocational relevance with the development of independent judgement."},{"en":"a measurable return on that investment","context":"Those who prioritise employment have a strong practical case. Higher education requires substantial investment from students, families and governments, so graduates reasonably expect a measurable return on that investment. Degree programmes can provide specialist knowledge, professional accreditation and access to occupations that would otherwise remain closed. In fields such as medicine, engineering and law, universities also protect the public by ensuring that entrants meet rigorous standards. From this perspective, a course that ignores labour-market realities may leave students heavily indebted but poorly equipped for work."},{"en":"navigate complex public and private decisions","context":"However, reducing university to job training would overlook its wider contribution. Students learn to evaluate evidence, challenge assumptions and communicate across cultural or disciplinary boundaries. These capacities help individuals navigate complex public and private decisions long after specific technical knowledge becomes outdated. Universities also generate research, sustain cultural institutions and create spaces in which contested ideas can be examined rather than merely repeated. A society with citizens who can reason critically is better able to resist misinformation and hold powerful institutions accountable."},{"en":"hold powerful institutions accountable","context":"However, reducing university to job training would overlook its wider contribution. Students learn to evaluate evidence, challenge assumptions and communicate across cultural or disciplinary boundaries. These capacities help individuals navigate complex public and private decisions long after specific technical knowledge becomes outdated. Universities also generate research, sustain cultural institutions and create spaces in which contested ideas can be examined rather than merely repeated. A society with citizens who can reason critically is better able to resist misinformation and hold powerful institutions accountable."},{"en":"bridge the gap between scholarship and professional practice","context":"In my view, the two purposes are not mutually exclusive. Programmes should maintain academic depth while incorporating placements, collaborative projects and transferable skills. This approach would bridge the gap between scholarship and professional practice without turning every subject into narrow occupational training."},{"en":"participate intelligently in civic life","context":"In conclusion, better employment is a legitimate aim of university education, but it is not the only one. The most valuable institutions prepare graduates both to earn a living and to participate intelligently in civic life."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#143","summary":"The wider aims of university education"},{"id":"writing-task-2-086","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Why school leavers cannot find work","prompt":"Today, more school leavers are unable to find jobs. Discuss the causes of rising unemployment among young adults and suggest any solutions.","answer":"Rising unemployment among school leavers is caused mainly by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a lack of practical experience</strong> and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a mismatch between education and labour-market needs</strong>. The problem can be reduced if schools, employers and governments share responsibility for the transition from study to work.\n\nMany young applicants leave school with theoretical knowledge but little evidence that they can use it in a workplace. Employers often prefer experienced candidates because training a beginner takes time and money. At the same time, some students specialise in areas with limited demand, while sectors such as construction, healthcare and technology struggle to recruit people with the right technical skills. Unrealistic expectations can add to the problem when graduates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reject entry-level opportunities</strong> because the pay, status or location is not ideal.\n\nEducation should respond more closely to actual employment patterns. Schools and colleges can work with local employers to update courses, provide work placements and teach practical skills such as communication, problem-solving and digital literacy. Realistic career guidance is also essential so that students understand salary levels, recruitment requirements and possible training routes before they leave education.\n\nEmployers should create more paid apprenticeships and genuine junior positions. Governments can support this by offering temporary tax incentives for firms that train young workers, while requiring clear standards so that subsidies do not become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a source of cheap labour</strong>. Short vocational programmes can also help unemployed graduates move into sectors with staff shortages.\n\nIn conclusion, youth unemployment is not simply the result of a weak economy. It also reflects limited experience, unsuitable training and poor information. Better coordination between education and employment would give school leavers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a more realistic route into stable work</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a lack of practical experience","context":"Rising unemployment among school leavers is caused mainly by a lack of practical experience and a mismatch between education and labour-market needs. The problem can be reduced if schools, employers and governments share responsibility for the transition from study to work."},{"en":"a mismatch between education and labour-market needs","context":"Rising unemployment among school leavers is caused mainly by a lack of practical experience and a mismatch between education and labour-market needs. The problem can be reduced if schools, employers and governments share responsibility for the transition from study to work."},{"en":"reject entry-level opportunities","context":"Many young applicants leave school with theoretical knowledge but little evidence that they can use it in a workplace. Employers often prefer experienced candidates because training a beginner takes time and money. At the same time, some students specialise in areas with limited demand, while sectors such as construction, healthcare and technology struggle to recruit people with the right technical skills. Unrealistic expectations can add to the problem when graduates reject entry-level opportunities because the pay, status or location is not ideal."},{"en":"a source of cheap labour","context":"Employers should create more paid apprenticeships and genuine junior positions. Governments can support this by offering temporary tax incentives for firms that train young workers, while requiring clear standards so that subsidies do not become a source of cheap labour. Short vocational programmes can also help unemployed graduates move into sectors with staff shortages."},{"en":"a more realistic route into stable work","context":"In conclusion, youth unemployment is not simply the result of a weak economy. It also reflects limited experience, unsuitable training and poor information. Better coordination between education and employment would give school leavers a more realistic route into stable work."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#144","summary":"Today, more school leavers are unable to find jobs."},{"id":"writing-task-2-094","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should libraries buy digital media instead of books?","prompt":"Some people believe that public libraries should spend their money on digital media, such as software, films and online resources, rather than on books. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that public libraries should spend their budgets on digital material rather than on books. I disagree with this view, because the two serve different purposes and a library that abandoned books would lose its central function.\n\nThere are reasonable arguments for digital investment. Many people, particularly younger users, now read on screens, and a single subscription can give an entire community access to thousands of titles and academic journals at once. Libraries also serve those who cannot afford computers or reliable internet at home, so providing machines, software and training addresses <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine form of exclusion</strong>.\n\nHowever, replacing books would be a mistake. Printed books remain the cheapest and most durable way of preserving information: a library copy lasts decades, requires no licence renewal and cannot be withdrawn by a company that has changed its terms. Digital material, by contrast, is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rented rather than owned</strong>, and libraries have already lost access to titles when publishers altered their agreements. There is also evidence that readers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retain more from print than from screens</strong>, which matters particularly for children learning to read.\n\nThe stronger argument is that this is a false choice. A well-run library does both, and the more important question is what proportion of the budget each deserves, which depends on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the community the library serves</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, digital resources deserve a place in modern libraries, but I disagree that they should replace books, which remain the more reliable and lasting form of access to knowledge.","phrases":[{"en":"a genuine form of exclusion","context":"There are reasonable arguments for digital investment. Many people, particularly younger users, now read on screens, and a single subscription can give an entire community access to thousands of titles and academic journals at once. Libraries also serve those who cannot afford computers or reliable internet at home, so providing machines, software and training addresses a genuine form of exclusion."},{"en":"rented rather than owned","context":"However, replacing books would be a mistake. Printed books remain the cheapest and most durable way of preserving information: a library copy lasts decades, requires no licence renewal and cannot be withdrawn by a company that has changed its terms. Digital material, by contrast, is usually rented rather than owned, and libraries have already lost access to titles when publishers altered their agreements. There is also evidence that readers retain more from print than from screens, which matters particularly for children learning to read."},{"en":"retain more from print than from screens","context":"However, replacing books would be a mistake. Printed books remain the cheapest and most durable way of preserving information: a library copy lasts decades, requires no licence renewal and cannot be withdrawn by a company that has changed its terms. Digital material, by contrast, is usually rented rather than owned, and libraries have already lost access to titles when publishers altered their agreements. There is also evidence that readers retain more from print than from screens, which matters particularly for children learning to read."},{"en":"the community the library serves","context":"The stronger argument is that this is a false choice. A well-run library does both, and the more important question is what proportion of the budget each deserves, which depends on the community the library serves."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#145","summary":"Should libraries buy digital media instead of books?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-139","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Universities offering courses online","prompt":"Some universities now offer their courses on the Internet so that people can study online. Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"It is true that online courses are becoming a common feature of university education. Although there are some drawbacks of Internet-based learning, I would argue that there are far more benefits.\n\nThe main drawback of the trend towards online university courses is that there is less direct interaction. Students may not have the opportunity to engage face-to-face with their teachers, and will instead have to rely on written forms of communication. Similarly, students who study online do not come into direct contact with each other, and this could have a negative impact on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peer support, discussion and exchange of ideas</strong>. For example, whereas students on traditional courses can attend seminars and even discuss their subjects over coffee after lessons, online learners are restricted to chatting through website forum areas. These learners may also lack the motivation and element of competition that face-to-face group work brings.\n\nDespite the negatives mentioned above, I believe that online university courses are a positive development for various reasons. Firstly, they allow learners to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">study in a flexible way</strong>, meaning that they can work whenever and wherever is convenient, and they can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cover the material at their own pace</strong>. Secondly, the cost of a university education can be greatly reduced, while revenues for institutions may increase as more students can be taught.\n\nFinally, online learning offers open access to anybody who is willing to study, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regardless of age, location, ability and background</strong>. For example, my uncle, who is 65 years old, has recently enrolled on an online MBA course in a different country, which would have been impossible in the days before Internet-based education.\n\nIn conclusion, while I recognise the possible disadvantages of online learning, I consider it to be a positive development overall.","phrases":[{"en":"peer support, discussion and exchange of ideas","context":"The main drawback of the trend towards online university courses is that there is less direct interaction. Students may not have the opportunity to engage face-to-face with their teachers, and will instead have to rely on written forms of communication. Similarly, students who study online do not come into direct contact with each other, and this could have a negative impact on peer support, discussion and exchange of ideas. For example, whereas students on traditional courses can attend seminars and even discuss their subjects over coffee after lessons, online learners are restricted to chatting through website forum areas. These learners may also lack the motivation and element of competition that face-to-face group work brings."},{"en":"study in a flexible way","context":"Despite the negatives mentioned above, I believe that online university courses are a positive development for various reasons. Firstly, they allow learners to study in a flexible way, meaning that they can work whenever and wherever is convenient, and they can cover the material at their own pace. Secondly, the cost of a university education can be greatly reduced, while revenues for institutions may increase as more students can be taught."},{"en":"cover the material at their own pace","context":"Despite the negatives mentioned above, I believe that online university courses are a positive development for various reasons. Firstly, they allow learners to study in a flexible way, meaning that they can work whenever and wherever is convenient, and they can cover the material at their own pace. Secondly, the cost of a university education can be greatly reduced, while revenues for institutions may increase as more students can be taught."},{"en":"regardless of age, location, ability and background","context":"Finally, online learning offers open access to anybody who is willing to study, regardless of age, location, ability and background. For example, my uncle, who is 65 years old, has recently enrolled on an online MBA course in a different country, which would have been impossible in the days before Internet-based education."}],"subtype":"Positive / Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#146","summary":"Online university courses"},{"id":"writing-task-2-152","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Is studying history useful?","prompt":"Some people argue that history is of little or no use to us. Others believe that studying history brings many benefits. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people regard the study of history as an interesting but essentially useless subject, while others believe it offers real benefits. In my opinion history is valuable, although the way it is often taught obscures this.\n\nThe case against history is largely practical. School and university time is limited, and many argue that it would be better spent on subjects leading directly to employment, such as mathematics, science and computing. Critics also question its reliability, since accounts are written by the winners and are frequently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewritten to suit</strong> whichever government is in power. There is a further objection: circumstances change so completely that the experience of previous centuries offers little guidance for today&#x27;s problems.\n\nThose who defend the subject reply that history explains the present. Current conflicts over borders, religion and national identity are almost impossible to understand without knowing how they arose, and voters who lack this background are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easily misled</strong> by politicians who invent a convenient past. History also develops a genuinely transferable skill, since students must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weigh conflicting evidence</strong> and judge which sources to trust, which is exactly what law, journalism and management require.\n\nMy own view is that history is worth studying, but its value depends on the approach. Memorising the dates of battles achieves very little. Examining why a decision was taken, what alternatives existed and what followed teaches judgement.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that history is useless. Properly taught, it explains why societies are as they are and trains people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evaluate evidence for themselves</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"rewritten to suit","context":"The case against history is largely practical. School and university time is limited, and many argue that it would be better spent on subjects leading directly to employment, such as mathematics, science and computing. Critics also question its reliability, since accounts are written by the winners and are frequently rewritten to suit whichever government is in power. There is a further objection: circumstances change so completely that the experience of previous centuries offers little guidance for today's problems."},{"en":"easily misled","context":"Those who defend the subject reply that history explains the present. Current conflicts over borders, religion and national identity are almost impossible to understand without knowing how they arose, and voters who lack this background are easily misled by politicians who invent a convenient past. History also develops a genuinely transferable skill, since students must weigh conflicting evidence and judge which sources to trust, which is exactly what law, journalism and management require."},{"en":"weigh conflicting evidence","context":"Those who defend the subject reply that history explains the present. Current conflicts over borders, religion and national identity are almost impossible to understand without knowing how they arose, and voters who lack this background are easily misled by politicians who invent a convenient past. History also develops a genuinely transferable skill, since students must weigh conflicting evidence and judge which sources to trust, which is exactly what law, journalism and management require."},{"en":"evaluate evidence for themselves","context":"In conclusion, I disagree that history is useless. Properly taught, it explains why societies are as they are and trains people to evaluate evidence for themselves."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#147","summary":"Is studying history useful?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2195","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Study time versus physical activity","prompt":"Parents should encourage children to spend less time studying and more time doing physical activities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that children would benefit from doing more exercise and less schoolwork. I agree that many children are far too inactive, but in my view the time for sport should come from their leisure hours rather than from their studies.\n\nThere are clear reasons why children need more physical activity. Rates of childhood obesity have risen sharply in most developed countries, and doctors link this trend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increasingly sedentary lifestyles</strong>. Regular exercise also improves concentration and mood, which means that an active child is likely to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learn more efficiently, not less</strong>. A child who plays football for an hour after school will probably return to homework <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in a far better state of mind</strong> than one who has spent the afternoon in front of a screen.\n\nHowever, I do not believe that study time is the right thing to sacrifice. School hours are already limited, and the skills that children acquire in class, such as literacy, numeracy and scientific reasoning, determine their opportunities for the rest of their lives. If parents reduce study time, weaker students in particular will fall behind, and this is difficult to correct later. The real problem is not homework but the number of hours that young people spend on phones, computers and television. Cutting an hour of screen time each day would free enough time for sport <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at no academic cost</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I strongly support the idea that children should be more physically active, but I disagree that this must involve studying less. Parents should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">target passive entertainment instead</strong>, and treat exercise as a normal part of a balanced daily routine.","phrases":[{"en":"increasingly sedentary lifestyles","context":"There are clear reasons why children need more physical activity. Rates of childhood obesity have risen sharply in most developed countries, and doctors link this trend to increasingly sedentary lifestyles. Regular exercise also improves concentration and mood, which means that an active child is likely to learn more efficiently, not less. A child who plays football for an hour after school will probably return to homework in a far better state of mind than one who has spent the afternoon in front of a screen."},{"en":"learn more efficiently, not less","context":"There are clear reasons why children need more physical activity. Rates of childhood obesity have risen sharply in most developed countries, and doctors link this trend to increasingly sedentary lifestyles. Regular exercise also improves concentration and mood, which means that an active child is likely to learn more efficiently, not less. A child who plays football for an hour after school will probably return to homework in a far better state of mind than one who has spent the afternoon in front of a screen."},{"en":"in a far better state of mind","context":"There are clear reasons why children need more physical activity. Rates of childhood obesity have risen sharply in most developed countries, and doctors link this trend to increasingly sedentary lifestyles. Regular exercise also improves concentration and mood, which means that an active child is likely to learn more efficiently, not less. A child who plays football for an hour after school will probably return to homework in a far better state of mind than one who has spent the afternoon in front of a screen."},{"en":"at no academic cost","context":"However, I do not believe that study time is the right thing to sacrifice. School hours are already limited, and the skills that children acquire in class, such as literacy, numeracy and scientific reasoning, determine their opportunities for the rest of their lives. If parents reduce study time, weaker students in particular will fall behind, and this is difficult to correct later. The real problem is not homework but the number of hours that young people spend on phones, computers and television. Cutting an hour of screen time each day would free enough time for sport at no academic cost."},{"en":"target passive entertainment instead","context":"In conclusion, I strongly support the idea that children should be more physically active, but I disagree that this must involve studying less. Parents should target passive entertainment instead, and treat exercise as a normal part of a balanced daily routine."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#148","summary":"Study time versus physical activity"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2196","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Free university education for all","prompt":"Some people believe that everyone has a right to have access to university education and the governments should make it free for all students no matter what financial background they have. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people believe that a university place is a basic right, and that governments should therefore fund it for every student regardless of income. While I agree that money should never prevent a talented student from studying, I do not think that free education for all is the best use of public funds.\n\nThe argument for free tuition is a strong one. When students have to pay high fees, those from poorer families are either excluded altogether or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forced into debt that takes decades to repay</strong>. This <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wastes talent</strong>, because a bright child from a low-income household may be far more capable than a wealthy one who can simply afford the fees. A country that educates its most able citizens also benefits economically, since graduates pay higher taxes and fill skilled positions in medicine, engineering and teaching.\n\nHowever, universal free education creates serious problems. Government budgets are limited, and money spent on universities cannot be spent on hospitals, schools or transport. It is also worth remembering that a degree brings <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a substantial private reward</strong>: graduates typically earn far more over a lifetime than those who leave school at eighteen. It seems unfair to ask a factory worker who never attended university to pay, through taxation, for the education of someone who will eventually earn twice as much.\n\nIn conclusion, access to university should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend on ability rather than wealth</strong>, but rather than making courses free for everyone, governments should target their support at students who genuinely cannot afford to pay, through full scholarships and living grants.","phrases":[{"en":"forced into debt that takes decades to repay","context":"The argument for free tuition is a strong one. When students have to pay high fees, those from poorer families are either excluded altogether or forced into debt that takes decades to repay. This wastes talent, because a bright child from a low-income household may be far more capable than a wealthy one who can simply afford the fees. A country that educates its most able citizens also benefits economically, since graduates pay higher taxes and fill skilled positions in medicine, engineering and teaching."},{"en":"wastes talent","context":"The argument for free tuition is a strong one. When students have to pay high fees, those from poorer families are either excluded altogether or forced into debt that takes decades to repay. This wastes talent, because a bright child from a low-income household may be far more capable than a wealthy one who can simply afford the fees. A country that educates its most able citizens also benefits economically, since graduates pay higher taxes and fill skilled positions in medicine, engineering and teaching."},{"en":"a substantial private reward","context":"However, universal free education creates serious problems. Government budgets are limited, and money spent on universities cannot be spent on hospitals, schools or transport. It is also worth remembering that a degree brings a substantial private reward: graduates typically earn far more over a lifetime than those who leave school at eighteen. It seems unfair to ask a factory worker who never attended university to pay, through taxation, for the education of someone who will eventually earn twice as much."},{"en":"depend on ability rather than wealth","context":"In conclusion, access to university should depend on ability rather than wealth, but rather than making courses free for everyone, governments should target their support at students who genuinely cannot afford to pay, through full scholarships and living grants."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#149","summary":"Free university education for all"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2213","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Team sports and cooperation","prompt":"The best way to teach children to cooperate is through team sports at school. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often claimed that school team sports are the most effective method of teaching children to work together. I agree that sport teaches cooperation extremely well, but I do not accept that it is the best method for every child.\n\nThe strengths of team sport are clear. In a game of football or basketball, cooperation is not an abstract idea to be explained in a classroom; it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediately and visibly necessary</strong>. A player who refuses to pass will lose, and the consequences are obvious to everyone within seconds. Children also learn to accept different roles, to trust others to do their part, to support a teammate who has made a mistake and to deal with defeat as a group. These lessons are memorable precisely because they are physical and emotional rather than theoretical.\n\nHowever, sport suits some children far better than others. A child who is unathletic, physically small or simply uninterested may spend years being chosen last and blamed for defeats, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the lesson learned is exclusion rather than cooperation</strong>. Team sports can also encourage the wrong attitudes, since aggressive competition and pressure to win sometimes produce selfish behaviour instead.\n\nIn my opinion, schools should therefore use a range of methods. Group projects in science, drama productions, orchestras, debating teams and school newspapers all require pupils to plan together, divide responsibilities and depend on one another, and they engage children whose talents are not physical.\n\nIn conclusion, team sports are a valuable way of teaching cooperation, but they work best <strong class=\"key-phrase\">alongside other collaborative activities</strong> rather than as the only approach.","phrases":[{"en":"immediately and visibly necessary","context":"The strengths of team sport are clear. In a game of football or basketball, cooperation is not an abstract idea to be explained in a classroom; it is immediately and visibly necessary. A player who refuses to pass will lose, and the consequences are obvious to everyone within seconds. Children also learn to accept different roles, to trust others to do their part, to support a teammate who has made a mistake and to deal with defeat as a group. These lessons are memorable precisely because they are physical and emotional rather than theoretical."},{"en":"the lesson learned is exclusion rather than cooperation","context":"However, sport suits some children far better than others. A child who is unathletic, physically small or simply uninterested may spend years being chosen last and blamed for defeats, and the lesson learned is exclusion rather than cooperation. Team sports can also encourage the wrong attitudes, since aggressive competition and pressure to win sometimes produce selfish behaviour instead."},{"en":"alongside other collaborative activities","context":"In conclusion, team sports are a valuable way of teaching cooperation, but they work best alongside other collaborative activities rather than as the only approach."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#150","summary":"Team sports and cooperation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2220","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Are libraries still necessary?","prompt":"Students today can access information online, so libraries are no longer necessary. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Since almost every text a student needs can now be opened on a laptop, some people conclude that library buildings have outlived their purpose. I disagree, because this argument mistakes a library for a store of books when its real value lies elsewhere.\n\nThe first thing a library provides is a place to work. Many students have nowhere at home that is quiet, warm and free of interruption, particularly those sharing a room or living with young children, and a desk in a reading room is the only environment in which sustained study is possible for them. Removing that would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disadvantage precisely the students</strong> who already have the least support.\n\nLibraries also solve the problem the internet creates. Searching online produces thousands of results of wildly varying reliability, and a first-year student rarely knows which to trust. Librarians teach people how to judge a source, how to search a specialist database and how to reference properly, and universities pay for subscriptions to journals that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">locked behind expensive paywalls</strong> and cannot simply be found through a search engine.\n\nI accept that the traditional function of lending printed books has genuinely declined, and that libraries which do nothing else look increasingly redundant. The response, though, has been to change rather than to close: many now offer computers, study rooms, language help and community classes, and their visitor numbers have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">risen rather than fallen</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I strongly disagree that libraries are unnecessary. Online access has removed one of their functions while making the others more valuable, because a student who can reach everything needs help more than ever in deciding what is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worth reading at all</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"disadvantage precisely the students","context":"The first thing a library provides is a place to work. Many students have nowhere at home that is quiet, warm and free of interruption, particularly those sharing a room or living with young children, and a desk in a reading room is the only environment in which sustained study is possible for them. Removing that would disadvantage precisely the students who already have the least support."},{"en":"locked behind expensive paywalls","context":"Libraries also solve the problem the internet creates. Searching online produces thousands of results of wildly varying reliability, and a first-year student rarely knows which to trust. Librarians teach people how to judge a source, how to search a specialist database and how to reference properly, and universities pay for subscriptions to journals that are locked behind expensive paywalls and cannot simply be found through a search engine."},{"en":"risen rather than fallen","context":"I accept that the traditional function of lending printed books has genuinely declined, and that libraries which do nothing else look increasingly redundant. The response, though, has been to change rather than to close: many now offer computers, study rooms, language help and community classes, and their visitor numbers have risen rather than fallen."},{"en":"worth reading at all","context":"In conclusion, I strongly disagree that libraries are unnecessary. Online access has removed one of their functions while making the others more valuable, because a student who can reach everything needs help more than ever in deciding what is worth reading at all."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#151","summary":"Whether online access has made libraries unnecessary"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2223","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Why university study feels harder","prompt":"Many students find it is harder to study at university or college compared to when they were at school. Why is this? What are some possible solutions for this problem?","answer":"Many students who did well at school struggle badly in their first year of higher education. The difficulty is usually caused by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the sudden removal of structure</strong> rather than by the subject matter itself, and it can be reduced by teaching students how to work independently.\n\nThe main cause is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supervision disappears overnight</strong>. At school a timetable fills every hour, homework is set and checked weekly, and a teacher notices immediately when a pupil falls behind. At university a student may have twelve contact hours and one deadline in three months, which means nobody will point out that no work has been done until it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far too late to recover</strong>. Reading also changes in kind: instead of a textbook chapter, students are given a list of papers and expected to decide for themselves what matters.\n\nLiving arrangements make this worse. Most first-year students are managing money, cooking and a new social life for the first time, often several hundred miles from anyone who knows them, and the energy this consumes is energy not spent on study. Those who feel isolated tend to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">withdraw rather than ask for help</strong>.\n\nThe solutions follow from the causes. Universities should break long assignments into smaller submitted stages during the first year, so that a struggling student is identified in week four rather than week twenty. Explicit teaching of academic skills — how to read a paper, plan an essay and reference sources — should be part of the timetable rather than left to chance. Assigning each new student a named tutor and a second-year mentor also gives them somebody obvious to approach.\n\nIn conclusion, the difficulty comes from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independence arriving all at once</strong>, and the remedy is to introduce it gradually while making support easy to reach.","phrases":[{"en":"the sudden removal of structure","context":"Many students who did well at school struggle badly in their first year of higher education. The difficulty is usually caused by the sudden removal of structure rather than by the subject matter itself, and it can be reduced by teaching students how to work independently."},{"en":"supervision disappears overnight","context":"The main cause is that supervision disappears overnight. At school a timetable fills every hour, homework is set and checked weekly, and a teacher notices immediately when a pupil falls behind. At university a student may have twelve contact hours and one deadline in three months, which means nobody will point out that no work has been done until it is far too late to recover. Reading also changes in kind: instead of a textbook chapter, students are given a list of papers and expected to decide for themselves what matters."},{"en":"far too late to recover","context":"The main cause is that supervision disappears overnight. At school a timetable fills every hour, homework is set and checked weekly, and a teacher notices immediately when a pupil falls behind. At university a student may have twelve contact hours and one deadline in three months, which means nobody will point out that no work has been done until it is far too late to recover. Reading also changes in kind: instead of a textbook chapter, students are given a list of papers and expected to decide for themselves what matters."},{"en":"withdraw rather than ask for help","context":"Living arrangements make this worse. Most first-year students are managing money, cooking and a new social life for the first time, often several hundred miles from anyone who knows them, and the energy this consumes is energy not spent on study. Those who feel isolated tend to withdraw rather than ask for help."},{"en":"independence arriving all at once","context":"In conclusion, the difficulty comes from independence arriving all at once, and the remedy is to introduce it gradually while making support easy to reach."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#152","summary":"Why studying at university is harder than at school"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2234","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"When should school begin?","prompt":"Some people think that children should start school at a very early age, but others believe that children should not go to school until they are older. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"School starting ages vary widely, from four in Britain to seven in much of Scandinavia. The disagreement is genuine, and my own view is that early attendance is valuable provided that what happens in the classroom suits the age of the children in it.\n\nThose in favour of an early start point to the pace of early development. Vocabulary and social behaviour develop fastest before the age of six, and a structured setting exposes children to more language, more books and more negotiation with other children than many homes can provide. The effect is largest for children from disadvantaged families, for whom nursery and early school close a gap that otherwise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">only widens with time</strong>. Early attendance also allows parents to return to work.\n\nThe opposing argument is that formal instruction too early does more harm than good. A four-year-old asked to sit still and form letters may simply learn that school is unpleasant, and countries that begin reading instruction at seven catch up within a few years while reporting fewer children who dislike school. Long days away from home at that age can also be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely tiring for a small child</strong>.\n\nMy position is that this debate confuses starting age with teaching method. The evidence against early schooling concerns formal lessons and testing, not attendance itself, and countries with the best results send children early but spend those first years on play, talk and stories rather than on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">worksheets and assessment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, an early start benefits children, particularly the least advantaged, provided the early years are built around play and language. The mistake is not sending a four-year-old to school but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treating a four-year-old like a pupil of ten</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"only widens with time","context":"Those in favour of an early start point to the pace of early development. Vocabulary and social behaviour develop fastest before the age of six, and a structured setting exposes children to more language, more books and more negotiation with other children than many homes can provide. The effect is largest for children from disadvantaged families, for whom nursery and early school close a gap that otherwise only widens with time. Early attendance also allows parents to return to work."},{"en":"genuinely tiring for a small child","context":"The opposing argument is that formal instruction too early does more harm than good. A four-year-old asked to sit still and form letters may simply learn that school is unpleasant, and countries that begin reading instruction at seven catch up within a few years while reporting fewer children who dislike school. Long days away from home at that age can also be genuinely tiring for a small child."},{"en":"worksheets and assessment","context":"My position is that this debate confuses starting age with teaching method. The evidence against early schooling concerns formal lessons and testing, not attendance itself, and countries with the best results send children early but spend those first years on play, talk and stories rather than on worksheets and assessment."},{"en":"treating a four-year-old like a pupil of ten","context":"In conclusion, an early start benefits children, particularly the least advantaged, provided the early years are built around play and language. The mistake is not sending a four-year-old to school but treating a four-year-old like a pupil of ten."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#153","summary":"Starting school at a very early age compared with starting later"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2236","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Studying in a group or alone","prompt":"Some people think that it is more effective for students to study in a group while others believe that it is better for them to study alone. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Students differ over whether learning is better done together or in private, and both methods have measurable strengths. My own view is that they suit different stages of learning and that the sensible approach is to combine them.\n\nThe argument for group study rests on explanation. A student who has to describe a concept aloud discovers immediately which parts they do not actually understand, since gaps that survive silent reading do not survive a question from someone else. Groups also expose students to methods they would never have found alone: in a mathematics problem there may be three routes to an answer, and hearing the other two deepens understanding of all of them. A fixed meeting time is useful in itself, because it makes the work <strong class=\"key-phrase\">harder to postpone</strong>.\n\nStudying alone has different advantages. Difficult material requires sustained concentration, and a group inevitably interrupts, drifts into conversation or moves at a pace that suits nobody exactly. Working alone also lets a student spend three hours on the one topic that is defeating them rather than the ten minutes a group would allow, and it removes the temptation to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on a stronger classmate</strong> instead of struggling through.\n\nIn my view the two are complementary rather than competing. The most effective pattern is to learn the material alone first, so that everyone arrives with something to contribute, and then to meet in order to test each other and argue about the difficult points. A group whose members have done no preparation simply wastes an afternoon.\n\nIn conclusion, both methods work, but for different purposes. Independent study builds the knowledge and group work <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exposes what is missing from it</strong>, which is why serious students use both.","phrases":[{"en":"harder to postpone","context":"The argument for group study rests on explanation. A student who has to describe a concept aloud discovers immediately which parts they do not actually understand, since gaps that survive silent reading do not survive a question from someone else. Groups also expose students to methods they would never have found alone: in a mathematics problem there may be three routes to an answer, and hearing the other two deepens understanding of all of them. A fixed meeting time is useful in itself, because it makes the work harder to postpone."},{"en":"rely on a stronger classmate","context":"Studying alone has different advantages. Difficult material requires sustained concentration, and a group inevitably interrupts, drifts into conversation or moves at a pace that suits nobody exactly. Working alone also lets a student spend three hours on the one topic that is defeating them rather than the ten minutes a group would allow, and it removes the temptation to rely on a stronger classmate instead of struggling through."},{"en":"exposes what is missing from it","context":"In conclusion, both methods work, but for different purposes. Independent study builds the knowledge and group work exposes what is missing from it, which is why serious students use both."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#154","summary":"Group study compared with studying alone"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2238","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should children be encouraged to watch television?","prompt":"Children can learn effectively by watching television. Therefore, they should be encouraged to watch television regularly at home and at school. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Television can undoubtedly teach children things that a classroom cannot show them. I agree with the first half of this statement but disagree with the conclusion drawn from it, because the value of television depends entirely on what is watched and for how long.\n\nThe learning argument is sound within limits. A documentary can take a child to the deep ocean or inside an erupting volcano, and a chemistry programme can show a reaction that no school laboratory could safely run. Well-made children&#x27;s programmes have measurably improved vocabulary and number skills, particularly among children whose parents read to them little, so for those families television <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closes a gap rather than creating one</strong>.\n\nThe recommendation that follows does not hold, however. Almost none of what children actually watch is educational, and encouraging regular viewing in practice means encouraging entertainment. Television is also passive in a way that matters: a child watching an experiment learns less than a child performing one, because understanding is built by doing something and getting it wrong. Hours in front of a screen additionally <strong class=\"key-phrase\">displace reading, play and conversation</strong>, which are the activities that develop language fastest.\n\nThe school proposal is weaker still. Class time is scarce, and a teacher who can question, correct and adapt to a confused pupil does something no broadcast can. Television belongs in a lesson as an occasional illustration, not as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a substitute for teaching</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, children do learn from good programmes, and those programmes deserve support. The conclusion that viewing should be encouraged regularly is wrong, because it treats television as uniformly educational when the value lies in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a narrow band of what is broadcast</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"closes a gap rather than creating one","context":"The learning argument is sound within limits. A documentary can take a child to the deep ocean or inside an erupting volcano, and a chemistry programme can show a reaction that no school laboratory could safely run. Well-made children's programmes have measurably improved vocabulary and number skills, particularly among children whose parents read to them little, so for those families television closes a gap rather than creating one."},{"en":"displace reading, play and conversation","context":"The recommendation that follows does not hold, however. Almost none of what children actually watch is educational, and encouraging regular viewing in practice means encouraging entertainment. Television is also passive in a way that matters: a child watching an experiment learns less than a child performing one, because understanding is built by doing something and getting it wrong. Hours in front of a screen additionally displace reading, play and conversation, which are the activities that develop language fastest."},{"en":"a substitute for teaching","context":"The school proposal is weaker still. Class time is scarce, and a teacher who can question, correct and adapt to a confused pupil does something no broadcast can. Television belongs in a lesson as an occasional illustration, not as a substitute for teaching."},{"en":"a narrow band of what is broadcast","context":"In conclusion, children do learn from good programmes, and those programmes deserve support. The conclusion that viewing should be encouraged regularly is wrong, because it treats television as uniformly educational when the value lies in a narrow band of what is broadcast."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#155","summary":"Whether children learn effectively from watching television"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2241","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Who should take children to school?","prompt":"Some people say it is the government's responsibility to transport children to school, while others believe parents should transport their children to school. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Getting children to school is a daily problem for millions of families, and there is disagreement over whether the state or the family should solve it. In my view the responsibility belongs primarily to government, though not in every circumstance.\n\nThe argument for public provision follows from compulsory education. If a country requires a child to attend school, it has effectively created the journey and cannot reasonably leave families to manage it alone. The burden otherwise falls hardest where it can least be carried: a single parent working an early shift, or a family without a car in a village with no bus, may find that attendance depends on transport they cannot arrange. A school bus also removes hundreds of individual car journeys, which reduces congestion outside school gates and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cuts emissions in residential streets</strong>.\n\nThose who favour parental responsibility argue that raising a child includes getting them to school, and that public transport is expensive to run and rarely used efficiently. There is a further point in their favour: the walk or drive is often the only unhurried time a parent and child spend together in a working day, and handing it to a bus <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removes something worth keeping</strong>.\n\nMy position is that the answer depends on distance. Where a school is within walking distance, families should manage the journey themselves, and councils should make that safe with crossings and pavements. Where children live several miles away or roads are dangerous, transport is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a condition of attendance</strong> and the state should provide it.\n\nIn conclusion, parents can reasonably be expected to handle short local journeys, but a government that makes schooling compulsory must ensure that no child is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">kept away by the distance</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"cuts emissions in residential streets","context":"The argument for public provision follows from compulsory education. If a country requires a child to attend school, it has effectively created the journey and cannot reasonably leave families to manage it alone. The burden otherwise falls hardest where it can least be carried: a single parent working an early shift, or a family without a car in a village with no bus, may find that attendance depends on transport they cannot arrange. A school bus also removes hundreds of individual car journeys, which reduces congestion outside school gates and cuts emissions in residential streets."},{"en":"removes something worth keeping","context":"Those who favour parental responsibility argue that raising a child includes getting them to school, and that public transport is expensive to run and rarely used efficiently. There is a further point in their favour: the walk or drive is often the only unhurried time a parent and child spend together in a working day, and handing it to a bus removes something worth keeping."},{"en":"a condition of attendance","context":"My position is that the answer depends on distance. Where a school is within walking distance, families should manage the journey themselves, and councils should make that safe with crossings and pavements. Where children live several miles away or roads are dangerous, transport is a condition of attendance and the state should provide it."},{"en":"kept away by the distance","context":"In conclusion, parents can reasonably be expected to handle short local journeys, but a government that makes schooling compulsory must ensure that no child is kept away by the distance."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#156","summary":"Government responsibility for school transport compared with parents'"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2245","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should students only study?","prompt":"Full-time university students spend a lot of time studying. Some say they should do other activities too. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"University students are often advised to concentrate entirely on their degree, since that is what they are paying for. I strongly agree that they should also do other things, because the value of a degree is limited by what a graduate can do besides recall a syllabus.\n\nThe practical reason is that employers rarely choose between candidates on marks alone. When fifty applicants hold the same qualification, the decision turns on evidence that someone has organised a society, held a part-time job or captained a team, because these show that a person can meet a deadline that nobody set for them. A student with excellent results and nothing else has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no way of demonstrating that</strong>.\n\nThere is also a direct effect on the studying itself. Concentration is a finite resource, and a student reading for ten hours absorbs far less in the last three than in the first three. Exercise, sport and time with friends restore attention, which is why students who train regularly often report that they achieve more in fewer hours. Isolation works the other way: the students who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">withdraw into their books</strong> are the ones most likely to become depressed and abandon the course.\n\nI would add one qualification. Activities crowd out study when they become excessive, and a student working thirty hours a week in a bar to pay rent is not developing anything. The argument is for balance rather than for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">filling every spare hour</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree strongly. Other activities improve employability and protect the concentration that studying depends on, so a student who does nothing but read is not even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">maximising their academic results</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"no way of demonstrating that","context":"The practical reason is that employers rarely choose between candidates on marks alone. When fifty applicants hold the same qualification, the decision turns on evidence that someone has organised a society, held a part-time job or captained a team, because these show that a person can meet a deadline that nobody set for them. A student with excellent results and nothing else has no way of demonstrating that."},{"en":"withdraw into their books","context":"There is also a direct effect on the studying itself. Concentration is a finite resource, and a student reading for ten hours absorbs far less in the last three than in the first three. Exercise, sport and time with friends restore attention, which is why students who train regularly often report that they achieve more in fewer hours. Isolation works the other way: the students who withdraw into their books are the ones most likely to become depressed and abandon the course."},{"en":"filling every spare hour","context":"I would add one qualification. Activities crowd out study when they become excessive, and a student working thirty hours a week in a bar to pay rent is not developing anything. The argument is for balance rather than for filling every spare hour."},{"en":"maximising their academic results","context":"In conclusion, I agree strongly. Other activities improve employability and protect the concentration that studying depends on, so a student who does nothing but read is not even maximising their academic results."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#157","summary":"Whether full-time students should spend time on other activities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2254","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Does class size matter in language learning?","prompt":"Some people argue that small classes for language students are beneficial, whereas others say numbers do not matter. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Language schools often advertise small classes as their main advantage, while others insist that the number of students is far less important than how they are taught. In my view size matters more in language teaching than in most other subjects.\n\nThe argument for small groups rests on speaking time. A language is learned by producing it, and in a class of thirty each student may speak for under a minute in an hour, whereas in a group of eight the same lesson gives them ten times as much practice. Correction also becomes possible: a teacher with eight students can hear individual errors and address them, while a teacher with thirty can only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">correct the class as a whole</strong>. Smaller groups are less intimidating too, and learners who are afraid of appearing foolish in front of a large audience often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay silent for an entire term</strong>.\n\nThose who say numbers do not matter point to method. A skilled teacher can divide a large class into pairs so that half the room is speaking at once, and a poor teacher with six students may still lecture at them. Motivation matters more than either: a determined learner who practises daily will progress in any class, while a reluctant one will not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve in a group of four</strong>.\n\nMy own position accepts this second point without accepting its conclusion. Method and motivation are decisive, but a large class limits what even a good teacher can do, because speaking time is divided by the number of people present.\n\nIn conclusion, teaching quality matters most, yet class size sets a ceiling on it. Small groups are worth paying for in language learning specifically, because the subject <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be learned by listening</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"correct the class as a whole","context":"The argument for small groups rests on speaking time. A language is learned by producing it, and in a class of thirty each student may speak for under a minute in an hour, whereas in a group of eight the same lesson gives them ten times as much practice. Correction also becomes possible: a teacher with eight students can hear individual errors and address them, while a teacher with thirty can only correct the class as a whole. Smaller groups are less intimidating too, and learners who are afraid of appearing foolish in front of a large audience often stay silent for an entire term."},{"en":"stay silent for an entire term","context":"The argument for small groups rests on speaking time. A language is learned by producing it, and in a class of thirty each student may speak for under a minute in an hour, whereas in a group of eight the same lesson gives them ten times as much practice. Correction also becomes possible: a teacher with eight students can hear individual errors and address them, while a teacher with thirty can only correct the class as a whole. Smaller groups are less intimidating too, and learners who are afraid of appearing foolish in front of a large audience often stay silent for an entire term."},{"en":"improve in a group of four","context":"Those who say numbers do not matter point to method. A skilled teacher can divide a large class into pairs so that half the room is speaking at once, and a poor teacher with six students may still lecture at them. Motivation matters more than either: a determined learner who practises daily will progress in any class, while a reluctant one will not improve in a group of four."},{"en":"cannot be learned by listening","context":"In conclusion, teaching quality matters most, yet class size sets a ceiling on it. Small groups are worth paying for in language learning specifically, because the subject cannot be learned by listening."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#158","summary":"Whether small classes benefit language students"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2264","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Education or leisure for young people?","prompt":"Too much emphasis is placed on education for young people. Some people believe that governments should spend more money on leisure activities for the youth instead. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that societies concentrate too heavily on schooling and should divert money towards sport, music and youth centres. I disagree with the proposition as stated, though I accept that leisure provision is undervalued and deserves more support than it receives.\n\nEducation cannot reasonably be described as over-emphasised. It is the single strongest predictor of income, health and life expectancy, and a young person who leaves school without qualifications faces a permanent disadvantage that no amount of leisure provision will repair. In economic terms, schools produce the skilled workers a country depends on, and reducing that spending to fund recreation would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trade a long-term asset for a short-term one</strong>.\n\nI would nonetheless agree that leisure has been cut too far in many places. Youth clubs, sports pitches and music tuition have disappeared from exactly the neighbourhoods where families cannot buy private alternatives, and the results are visible: children with nowhere to go after school are more likely to drift into crime, and areas that closed youth services have seen <strong class=\"key-phrase\">antisocial behaviour rise measurably</strong>. Such activities also teach persistence and teamwork, which schools deliver less directly.\n\nThe two are complementary rather than competing. Children who exercise concentrate better, and those who feel that something outside lessons belongs to them attend school more reliably, so leisure spending <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improves educational results rather than diluting them</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that too much emphasis falls on education, since qualifications determine life chances in a way recreation does not. Governments should still restore funding for youth activities, and should do so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as an addition rather than a replacement</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"trade a long-term asset for a short-term one","context":"Education cannot reasonably be described as over-emphasised. It is the single strongest predictor of income, health and life expectancy, and a young person who leaves school without qualifications faces a permanent disadvantage that no amount of leisure provision will repair. In economic terms, schools produce the skilled workers a country depends on, and reducing that spending to fund recreation would trade a long-term asset for a short-term one."},{"en":"antisocial behaviour rise measurably","context":"I would nonetheless agree that leisure has been cut too far in many places. Youth clubs, sports pitches and music tuition have disappeared from exactly the neighbourhoods where families cannot buy private alternatives, and the results are visible: children with nowhere to go after school are more likely to drift into crime, and areas that closed youth services have seen antisocial behaviour rise measurably. Such activities also teach persistence and teamwork, which schools deliver less directly."},{"en":"improves educational results rather than diluting them","context":"The two are complementary rather than competing. Children who exercise concentrate better, and those who feel that something outside lessons belongs to them attend school more reliably, so leisure spending improves educational results rather than diluting them."},{"en":"as an addition rather than a replacement","context":"In conclusion, I disagree that too much emphasis falls on education, since qualifications determine life chances in a way recreation does not. Governments should still restore funding for youth activities, and should do so as an addition rather than a replacement."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#159","summary":"Spending on leisure activities for young people instead of education"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2277","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Adults who cannot read or write","prompt":"Despite better access to education, many adults today still cannot read or write. In what ways are they disadvantaged? What can governments do to help them?","answer":"Schooling has spread widely over the past century, yet a substantial number of adults still cannot read a form or write a sentence. Their difficulties reach well beyond employment, and governments can address them with measures that are neither complicated nor expensive.\n\nThe most obvious disadvantage is economic. Almost every job above manual labour now requires reading instructions, filling in records or using a screen, so illiterate adults are pushed into insecure and poorly paid work, and are the first to be dismissed when it disappears. They also lose money quietly, because they cannot check a contract, a payslip or a loan agreement and must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">trust whoever is holding the paper</strong>.\n\nThe social cost is heavier and less visible. An adult who cannot read struggles to follow a prescription, understand a diagnosis or vote on the basis of anything written, which narrows both health and citizenship. Many arrange their lives around hiding the problem, claiming they have forgotten their glasses or asking a relative to deal with letters, and that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">daily effort to conceal it</strong> produces real shame. Their children suffer too, since nobody reads with them at home.\n\nGovernments can act in three ways. Free classes must be built for adults rather than borrowed from schools: in the evening, near workplaces, in small groups, and taught with the documents learners actually face, such as bank forms and medical letters. Second, employers should be paid to release staff for a few hours a week, which removes the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">choice between wages and lessons</strong>. Third, public information should be offered in spoken and pictorial form, so that people are not excluded while they learn.\n\nIn conclusion, illiteracy limits earnings, health and confidence rather than reading alone. Practical adult classes, workplace support and accessible public information would reduce that disadvantage substantially.","phrases":[{"en":"trust whoever is holding the paper","context":"The most obvious disadvantage is economic. Almost every job above manual labour now requires reading instructions, filling in records or using a screen, so illiterate adults are pushed into insecure and poorly paid work, and are the first to be dismissed when it disappears. They also lose money quietly, because they cannot check a contract, a payslip or a loan agreement and must trust whoever is holding the paper."},{"en":"daily effort to conceal it","context":"The social cost is heavier and less visible. An adult who cannot read struggles to follow a prescription, understand a diagnosis or vote on the basis of anything written, which narrows both health and citizenship. Many arrange their lives around hiding the problem, claiming they have forgotten their glasses or asking a relative to deal with letters, and that daily effort to conceal it produces real shame. Their children suffer too, since nobody reads with them at home."},{"en":"choice between wages and lessons","context":"Governments can act in three ways. Free classes must be built for adults rather than borrowed from schools: in the evening, near workplaces, in small groups, and taught with the documents learners actually face, such as bank forms and medical letters. Second, employers should be paid to release staff for a few hours a week, which removes the choice between wages and lessons. Third, public information should be offered in spoken and pictorial form, so that people are not excluded while they learn."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#160","summary":"The disadvantages faced by illiterate adults and how governments can help"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2279","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"What is school actually for?","prompt":"Some people say that the main purpose of school is to turn children into good citizens and workers, rather than to benefit them as individuals. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"States pay for education out of taxes, so it is natural to ask what they are buying. I partly agree that schools exist to produce capable citizens and workers, but I think the statement sets up a division that does not hold.\n\nThe social purpose is undeniable. No government funds twelve years of teaching out of pure generosity. Schools deliver the literacy and numeracy an economy needs, and they also teach queuing, punctuality, sharing and disagreeing without violence, which is where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the habits of living together</strong> are actually learned. A country whose schools failed at this would find both its workplaces and its politics unmanageable.\n\nThe individual purpose is equally real, however. A child who learns to read gains a private life of books that has nothing to do with any employer. Music, sport and history repay nobody in productivity, yet they are what many adults look back on. Education also lets a child <strong class=\"key-phrase\">discover what they are good at</strong>, which is the difference between a chosen life and an assigned one.\n\nWhat makes the statement misleading is that these aims mostly produce each other. A confident, curious adult is a better employee than an obedient one, and a person who has been encouraged to think for themselves makes a better citizen than someone trained only to comply. The two purposes only separate at the extremes, where a system <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drills children for examinations</strong> and turns out graduates who can pass tests but cannot think.\n\nIn conclusion, schools do serve society, and there is nothing shameful in saying so. Yet they serve it best by developing individuals rather than by shaping them for a role, so the choice the statement offers is a false one.","phrases":[{"en":"the habits of living together","context":"The social purpose is undeniable. No government funds twelve years of teaching out of pure generosity. Schools deliver the literacy and numeracy an economy needs, and they also teach queuing, punctuality, sharing and disagreeing without violence, which is where the habits of living together are actually learned. A country whose schools failed at this would find both its workplaces and its politics unmanageable."},{"en":"discover what they are good at","context":"The individual purpose is equally real, however. A child who learns to read gains a private life of books that has nothing to do with any employer. Music, sport and history repay nobody in productivity, yet they are what many adults look back on. Education also lets a child discover what they are good at, which is the difference between a chosen life and an assigned one."},{"en":"drills children for examinations","context":"What makes the statement misleading is that these aims mostly produce each other. A confident, curious adult is a better employee than an obedient one, and a person who has been encouraged to think for themselves makes a better citizen than someone trained only to comply. The two purposes only separate at the extremes, where a system drills children for examinations and turns out graduates who can pass tests but cannot think."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#161","summary":"Whether schools exist to produce good citizens and workers or to benefit individuals"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2286","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Teaching yourself or learning from a teacher","prompt":"Some people think adults should learn practical skills by themselves, while others believe that learning from a teacher in a classroom is better. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"An adult who wants to cook, code or rewire a lamp can now choose between a video and a course. Both routes work, and my own view is that the choice should depend on the consequences of getting it wrong.\n\nTeaching yourself has clear advantages. It costs almost nothing, fits around work and family, and lets the learner move at their own pace, repeating a difficult step and skipping what they already know. Online material is often of high quality and shows the task from angles a classroom cannot. The independence also has value in itself, since somebody who has learned one skill alone <strong class=\"key-phrase\">knows how to teach themselves the next one</strong>.\n\nA teacher offers something different. The first is correction: a self-taught learner cannot see their own mistakes, and a bad technique practised for months becomes very hard to remove, whereas a teacher notices it within minutes. The second is sequence, because an experienced instructor knows which step must come before another, while a beginner choosing videos <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assembles knowledge with gaps in the middle</strong>. A class also supplies commitment through a fixed time and other people, which is why most people who begin online courses never finish them.\n\nThe distinction that matters is risk. Cooking, drawing or basic gardening can be learned alone, because a mistake costs a meal. Electrical work, driving, using power tools and anything involving other people&#x27;s safety should be taught, since the first serious error may be the last one. Professional qualifications also require supervised practice for the same reason.\n\nIn conclusion, independent learning is cheap, flexible and increasingly effective, and adults should use it freely for skills where errors are harmless. Where safety or a recognised qualification is involved, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an experienced teacher who corrects the fault early</strong> is worth the cost.","phrases":[{"en":"knows how to teach themselves the next one","context":"Teaching yourself has clear advantages. It costs almost nothing, fits around work and family, and lets the learner move at their own pace, repeating a difficult step and skipping what they already know. Online material is often of high quality and shows the task from angles a classroom cannot. The independence also has value in itself, since somebody who has learned one skill alone knows how to teach themselves the next one."},{"en":"assembles knowledge with gaps in the middle","context":"A teacher offers something different. The first is correction: a self-taught learner cannot see their own mistakes, and a bad technique practised for months becomes very hard to remove, whereas a teacher notices it within minutes. The second is sequence, because an experienced instructor knows which step must come before another, while a beginner choosing videos assembles knowledge with gaps in the middle. A class also supplies commitment through a fixed time and other people, which is why most people who begin online courses never finish them."},{"en":"an experienced teacher who corrects the fault early","context":"In conclusion, independent learning is cheap, flexible and increasingly effective, and adults should use it freely for skills where errors are harmless. Where safety or a recognised qualification is involved, an experienced teacher who corrects the fault early is worth the cost."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#162","summary":"Whether adults should learn practical skills alone or in a classroom"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2299","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Educated for society or for yourself?","prompt":"Some people say that the purpose of education is to prepare individuals to be useful to society. Others say its purpose is to help individuals achieve their personal ambitions. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Education consumes a large share of public money and a large share of a person&#x27;s life, so what it is for is a fair question. Both answers describe something real, and my own view is that they are the same aim seen from different sides.\n\nThe social argument is the reason education is funded publicly. Societies need doctors, engineers, teachers and farmers, and a country that produced none of them would not survive whatever its citizens had studied. Education also transmits the shared knowledge, language and rules that hold a community together, and taxpayers who pay for a medical degree may reasonably expect a doctor at the end of it. Where shortages are severe, directing students towards needed fields is defensible.\n\nThe individual argument answers that people are not instruments. A person spends their own years studying, and a system that assigns them to whatever the economy requires ignores what they might become. Education also does things with no social return at all: it lets someone understand music, argue about history or read for pleasure, and those belong to the person rather than to the state. Ambition is also the strongest motivation available, since students working towards something they chose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learn far more than those completing an allocation</strong>.\n\nThe division dissolves on examination. People who pursue their own interests generally become useful, because they work harder at what absorbs them, and most valuable contributions were made by people following curiosity rather than instruction. In the other direction, useful work is satisfying, so a nurse or an engineer usually finds personal meaning in exactly the work society needed. Conflict arises only when a system <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forces everybody down a single route</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, education must supply the skills a society depends on, and it must also let individuals become what they are capable of. These aims support each other, and the failures occur where one of them is pursued as if the other did not matter.","phrases":[{"en":"learn far more than those completing an allocation","context":"The individual argument answers that people are not instruments. A person spends their own years studying, and a system that assigns them to whatever the economy requires ignores what they might become. Education also does things with no social return at all: it lets someone understand music, argue about history or read for pleasure, and those belong to the person rather than to the state. Ambition is also the strongest motivation available, since students working towards something they chose learn far more than those completing an allocation."},{"en":"forces everybody down a single route","context":"The division dissolves on examination. People who pursue their own interests generally become useful, because they work harder at what absorbs them, and most valuable contributions were made by people following curiosity rather than instruction. In the other direction, useful work is satisfying, so a nurse or an engineer usually finds personal meaning in exactly the work society needed. Conflict arises only when a system forces everybody down a single route."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#163","summary":"Whether education should serve society or individual ambition"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2306","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Too few students studying science","prompt":"It is observed that in many countries not enough students are choosing to study science subjects at university. What are the causes, and what will be the effects on society?","answer":"Applications to physics, chemistry and engineering have fallen in many developed countries even as demand for those skills has risen. The causes lie in school experience and in the returns students expect, and the consequences reach further than a shortage of laboratory staff.\n\nSchool is where most of the decision is made. Science taught as facts to be memorised, with little laboratory work and often by teachers trained in another subject, convinces capable students that it is dull and difficult. The subjects are also marked more severely than many alternatives, so students protecting their grades choose the safer route to the same university place. A shortage of specialist teachers makes this self-reinforcing, since fewer science graduates means fewer science teachers.\n\nExpectations about work matter too. A science degree is demanding and often leads to research posts that are insecure and modestly paid, while finance, law and business offer higher salaries for less mathematics. Students also see very few scientists in public life and have little idea what the career actually involves, and girls in particular receive the impression early that the field is not for them.\n\nThe effects on society are serious. Countries without enough engineers and scientists cannot build the energy systems, medical capacity and digital infrastructure their economies depend on, and they end up importing both the technology and the specialists, which is expensive and unreliable. Innovation slows, since new industries form where the expertise already exists. There is also a wider cost to public life: a population with little scientific understanding struggles to judge claims about health, climate or risk, and decisions on those subjects then follow whoever argues most loudly.\n\nIn conclusion, students avoid science because it is taught poorly, graded harshly and rewarded modestly. The result is a country that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depends on expertise it has not produced</strong> and a public less able to evaluate the questions that matter most.","phrases":[{"en":"depends on expertise it has not produced","context":"In conclusion, students avoid science because it is taught poorly, graded harshly and rewarded modestly. The result is a country that depends on expertise it has not produced and a public less able to evaluate the questions that matter most."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Effects","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#164","summary":"Why fewer students choose science at university and the effects on society"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2314","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Should governments fund science above other subjects?","prompt":"Some people think the government should invest more money in teaching science than in other subjects in order for a country to develop and progress. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Science and engineering are visibly linked to prosperity, so governments under pressure often favour them when budgets are set. I partly agree that science deserves generous funding, but I disagree that progress comes from teaching it at the expense of everything else.\n\nThe argument for priority is practical. Laboratories, equipment and qualified teachers cost far more than a history classroom, so equal funding is not equal provision. Countries also face problems that only technical training can address, from clean energy to disease, and firms in most economies report shortages of engineers and data specialists rather than of graduates in general. Investing here shortens the distance between a classroom and a working economy.\n\nThe case against overcorrection is just as strong. A scientific advance becomes useful only when other skills carry it: a vaccine needs communicators to explain it and lawyers to license it, and an invention that nobody trusts is not adopted. Many of the most valuable industries in developed economies, from design to entertainment, rest on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">training that no science budget would cover</strong>. History, languages and ethics also produce the judgement a technological society needs, and a country full of engineers who cannot argue or write builds things without being able to decide whether it should.\n\nThere is a practical objection too. Children pushed into science because that is where the money went perform poorly and leave the field, so the policy fails on its own terms. Progress depends on people working where they are able.\n\nIn conclusion, science teaching is expensive and important, and it is reasonable for governments to fund it well. Treating other subjects as secondary would weaken exactly the skills that turn scientific work into national development.","phrases":[{"en":"training that no science budget would cover","context":"The case against overcorrection is just as strong. A scientific advance becomes useful only when other skills carry it: a vaccine needs communicators to explain it and lawyers to license it, and an invention that nobody trusts is not adopted. Many of the most valuable industries in developed economies, from design to entertainment, rest on training that no science budget would cover. History, languages and ethics also produce the judgement a technological society needs, and a country full of engineers who cannot argue or write builds things without being able to decide whether it should."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#165","summary":"Whether teaching science deserves more public money than other subjects"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2320","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Why girls choose arts and boys choose science","prompt":"In schools and universities, girls tend to choose arts subjects while boys prefer science. What are the reasons for this trend, and do you think it should be changed?","answer":"Physics and engineering lecture halls remain largely male, while literature and languages are largely female. The reasons have little to do with ability, and the pattern should be changed because of what it costs both sexes.\n\nThe influences begin early. Children are handed different toys and different praise: construction sets and puzzles for boys, drawing and stories for girls, and by the age of seven many children already describe science as something boys do. Teachers, without intending it, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ask boys the harder questions in maths</strong> and reward girls for neatness. Textbooks and news coverage supply almost no female engineers, so girls have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nobody obvious to imagine themselves as</strong>. By the time subjects are chosen at sixteen, the decision feels personal but has been shaped for a decade.\n\nTwo further factors matter at that point. Girls who are equally good at both often choose the arts because they expect the sciences to be an uncomfortable environment, and reports of harassment in male-dominated fields confirm this. Boys, meanwhile, avoid languages and literature because those subjects are treated as unmasculine, which is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a loss that receives far less attention</strong>.\n\nThe pattern deserves to be changed for three reasons. It wastes talent a country needs, since shortages of engineers exist alongside capable girls who were steered away. It also limits earnings, because technical work pays more, so the divide quietly widens the pay gap. Products designed by uniform teams fail predictably: crash tests built around male bodies made cars less safe for women.\n\nIn conclusion, the split arises from expectations set in childhood rather than from aptitude. Changing it through early exposure, visible role models and better teaching practice would benefit both the economy and the individuals currently choosing by habit.","phrases":[{"en":"ask boys the harder questions in maths","context":"The influences begin early. Children are handed different toys and different praise: construction sets and puzzles for boys, drawing and stories for girls, and by the age of seven many children already describe science as something boys do. Teachers, without intending it, ask boys the harder questions in maths and reward girls for neatness. Textbooks and news coverage supply almost no female engineers, so girls have nobody obvious to imagine themselves as. By the time subjects are chosen at sixteen, the decision feels personal but has been shaped for a decade."},{"en":"nobody obvious to imagine themselves as","context":"The influences begin early. Children are handed different toys and different praise: construction sets and puzzles for boys, drawing and stories for girls, and by the age of seven many children already describe science as something boys do. Teachers, without intending it, ask boys the harder questions in maths and reward girls for neatness. Textbooks and news coverage supply almost no female engineers, so girls have nobody obvious to imagine themselves as. By the time subjects are chosen at sixteen, the decision feels personal but has been shaped for a decade."},{"en":"a loss that receives far less attention","context":"Two further factors matter at that point. Girls who are equally good at both often choose the arts because they expect the sciences to be an uncomfortable environment, and reports of harassment in male-dominated fields confirm this. Boys, meanwhile, avoid languages and literature because those subjects are treated as unmasculine, which is a loss that receives far less attention."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#166","summary":"The reasons behind subject choice by gender and whether it should change"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2325","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Why children cannot concentrate at school","prompt":"Nowadays, children find it difficult to concentrate or pay attention in school. What are the causes of this problem? What solutions can be suggested?","answer":"Teachers across very different countries report the same change: pupils lose focus within minutes. The causes lie mostly outside the classroom, and the remedies have to begin there as well.\n\nSleep is the first and most underestimated cause. Children go to bed with phones, and lessons that start early meet pupils who are several hours short of what they need, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attention fails before the day begins</strong>. The second cause is the entertainment they are used to. Short videos deliver a new stimulus every few seconds, and a mind trained on that pace finds a twenty-minute explanation intolerably slow. Notifications add constant interruption even when the device is silent.\n\nDiet and movement matter too. Sugary breakfasts produce a rise and then a collapse in energy, and children who once walked to school and played at breaks now sit for most of the day, with break times cut to protect examination results. There are also causes closer to home: worry about money or conflict between parents occupies a child&#x27;s attention completely, and large classes make it easy to drift unnoticed.\n\nPractical solutions exist. Schools can start later for teenagers, which has raised attendance and results wherever it has been tried, and can require phones to be handed in rather than merely silenced. Lessons can be broken into shorter segments with movement between them, and break times and physical education restored. Families can set a fixed hour when devices leave the bedroom, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the strongest single measure is protecting sleep</strong>. Teachers also need small enough groups to notice the child who has quietly stopped listening.\n\nIn conclusion, poor concentration comes from lost sleep, fragmented entertainment and long hours of sitting rather than from any change in children themselves. Later starts, phone-free classrooms and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more movement in the school day</strong> would address it directly.","phrases":[{"en":"attention fails before the day begins","context":"Sleep is the first and most underestimated cause. Children go to bed with phones, and lessons that start early meet pupils who are several hours short of what they need, so attention fails before the day begins. The second cause is the entertainment they are used to. Short videos deliver a new stimulus every few seconds, and a mind trained on that pace finds a twenty-minute explanation intolerably slow. Notifications add constant interruption even when the device is silent."},{"en":"the strongest single measure is protecting sleep","context":"Practical solutions exist. Schools can start later for teenagers, which has raised attendance and results wherever it has been tried, and can require phones to be handed in rather than merely silenced. Lessons can be broken into shorter segments with movement between them, and break times and physical education restored. Families can set a fixed hour when devices leave the bedroom, since the strongest single measure is protecting sleep. Teachers also need small enough groups to notice the child who has quietly stopped listening."},{"en":"more movement in the school day","context":"In conclusion, poor concentration comes from lost sleep, fragmented entertainment and long hours of sitting rather than from any change in children themselves. Later starts, phone-free classrooms and more movement in the school day would address it directly."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#167","summary":"The causes of falling concentration in class and possible remedies"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2326","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"University places for the many","prompt":"It is neither possible nor useful for a country to provide university places for a high proportion of young people. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Half of all school leavers now enter higher education in many countries, and critics argue that this is both unaffordable and pointless. I partly agree: mass expansion has been achieved, so it is clearly possible, but its usefulness depends entirely on how it is done.\n\nThe claim that it cannot be done is the weaker half. Several countries already educate more than half their young adults, and the cost, while large, is smaller than the tax revenue graduates return over a working life. What is genuinely difficult is expanding without diluting: hurried growth produces overcrowded lectures, unqualified teaching and degrees that no employer treats as evidence of anything.\n\nThe usefulness argument carries more weight. Many graduates now work in jobs that never required a degree, which suggests the qualification is being used to sort applicants rather than to prepare them. Students pay years of fees and lost earnings for that sorting, and countries with strong technical training produce skilled workers faster and at lower cost. There is a real shortage of electricians and nurses alongside a surplus of graduates in crowded fields, so the money has been spent on the wrong training.\n\nThe conclusion I draw is not that fewer people should be educated, but that education after school should be broader than a single model. Apprenticeships, technical colleges and short professional qualifications deserve the funding and the respect currently reserved for universities, and school leavers need honest information about where each route leads. A country also benefits from graduates beyond their wages, because an educated population argues better and is harder to mislead.\n\nIn conclusion, providing university places for many young people is possible and has already happened. It is useful only where the teaching is genuine and the alternatives are properly funded, and pushing everyone through the same route <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wastes both money and talent</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"wastes both money and talent","context":"In conclusion, providing university places for many young people is possible and has already happened. It is useful only where the teaching is genuine and the alternatives are properly funded, and pushing everyone through the same route wastes both money and talent."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#168","summary":"Whether it is possible or useful to send a high proportion of young people to university"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2334","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Teachers or parents: who shapes a child more?","prompt":"Once students go to school, teachers have a greater influence on their intellectual and social development than parents. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"School occupies six hours of a child&#x27;s day for a dozen years, so the claim that teachers take over from parents is not unreasonable. I disagree with it, though the balance shifts more than many parents realise.\n\nTeachers clearly hold advantages in the intellectual half of the argument. They are trained to explain, they know the order in which ideas should be introduced, and they can identify a difficulty that a parent would miss entirely. A gifted teacher can turn a child towards a subject for life, and for children whose homes contain few books and no help with study, school is the only place learning happens at all. Socially, too, the classroom supplies what a family cannot: a crowd of equals, with the negotiating, queuing and losing that go with it.\n\nParents nonetheless remain the stronger influence for structural reasons. They are there before school, after it, and long after it has ended, and their effect is cumulative rather than annual. They decide the things that determine whether teaching works: sleep, meals, whether homework is possible, whether reading happens at home, and whether school is spoken about with respect. Children also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">copy behaviour rather than instruction</strong>, and the model they see daily is at home. Values around honesty, money and how people are treated are absorbed there, and a teacher who contradicts them rarely wins.\n\nThe two are not really in competition. Teachers work with what parents send them, and the pupils who advance fastest almost always have both, which is why parental involvement predicts results more reliably than the choice of school.\n\nIn conclusion, teachers have a greater influence on academic knowledge and on how a child behaves among equals. Parents remain more powerful overall, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their influence is constant, early and long</strong>, and the best outcomes come from the two working in the same direction.","phrases":[{"en":"copy behaviour rather than instruction","context":"Parents nonetheless remain the stronger influence for structural reasons. They are there before school, after it, and long after it has ended, and their effect is cumulative rather than annual. They decide the things that determine whether teaching works: sleep, meals, whether homework is possible, whether reading happens at home, and whether school is spoken about with respect. Children also copy behaviour rather than instruction, and the model they see daily is at home. Values around honesty, money and how people are treated are absorbed there, and a teacher who contradicts them rarely wins."},{"en":"their influence is constant, early and long","context":"In conclusion, teachers have a greater influence on academic knowledge and on how a child behaves among equals. Parents remain more powerful overall, because their influence is constant, early and long, and the best outcomes come from the two working in the same direction."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#169","summary":"Whether teachers influence children more than parents once school begins"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2337","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"Vocational training or university?","prompt":"Nations should spend more money on skills and vocational training for practical work rather than on university education. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Countries with plenty of graduates and no electricians are beginning to question where their education budgets go. I largely agree that vocational training deserves a greater share, though not at the cost of weakening universities.\n\nThe practical argument is strong. Economies report persistent shortages of plumbers, welders, nurses and technicians, and those shortages hold up housing, healthcare and energy projects directly. Training for such work is also cheaper and faster: an apprenticeship takes two or three years, costs a fraction of a degree and leads to a job that exists in the town where the student lives. Graduates in oversupplied fields, meanwhile, often end up in work that never required their qualification, having paid heavily for it.\n\nThere is a fairness argument too. Countries that fund universities generously and technical colleges poorly are effectively subsidising the route taken by wealthier families, while the students who choose practical training receive less support for work the country urgently needs. Nations with strong apprenticeship systems show what the alternative looks like: skilled trades are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">respected rather than treated as a fallback</strong>, and youth unemployment is markedly lower.\n\nThe argument has limits, however. Universities produce the doctors, engineers and researchers no apprenticeship can, and they generate the knowledge on which new industries are built. Practical training also dates quickly, so a technician educated narrowly may find the equipment obsolete within a decade, whereas broader study makes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retraining later far easier</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, shifting money towards vocational education is justified by real shortages, lower costs and fairness to students who do not attend university. The aim should be parity between the two routes rather than the replacement of one by the other, since a country needs both.","phrases":[{"en":"respected rather than treated as a fallback","context":"There is a fairness argument too. Countries that fund universities generously and technical colleges poorly are effectively subsidising the route taken by wealthier families, while the students who choose practical training receive less support for work the country urgently needs. Nations with strong apprenticeship systems show what the alternative looks like: skilled trades are respected rather than treated as a fallback, and youth unemployment is markedly lower."},{"en":"retraining later far easier","context":"The argument has limits, however. Universities produce the doctors, engineers and researchers no apprenticeship can, and they generate the knowledge on which new industries are built. Practical training also dates quickly, so a technician educated narrowly may find the equipment obsolete within a decade, whereas broader study makes retraining later far easier."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#170","summary":"Whether public money should favour practical skills training over university education"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2374","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Education","title":"School until eighteen for everyone?","prompt":"Some people think everyone should stay at school until the age of eighteen. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Several countries have raised the leaving age to eighteen, and others are considering it. I agree that everybody should be in education or training until that age, but not that everybody should remain in a school classroom.\n\nThe case for staying is strong. Jobs available to a sixteen-year-old today are few, poorly paid and unstable, and those who leave early earn less throughout their lives and are the first to lose work in a downturn. Two additional years also cover the period when many young people mature enough to take study seriously, and leaving at sixteen closes doors that are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">very difficult to reopen once earning has begun</strong>. There is a social argument as well, since early leavers are more likely to be unemployed or in trouble at eighteen than those who stayed.\n\nThe objection is that compulsion produces resentment. A student who has disliked school for a decade will not improve by being kept in the same building for two more years, and a disruptive class harms everybody in it. Some sixteen-year-olds genuinely learn better through work, and an apprenticeship at that age can lead somewhere a reluctant sixth form will not. Families depending on a young person&#x27;s wage also feel the cost, and forcing attendance without support <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simply moves the problem into a register</strong>.\n\nThe way to resolve this is to broaden what counts. Requiring education or training until eighteen, and including apprenticeships, technical colleges and part-time study alongside work, keeps young people learning without insisting they all learn in the same way. Countries that have done this report better outcomes than those that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raised the age and offered only one route</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, leaving education at sixteen limits a person&#x27;s earnings and choices for life, so a requirement until eighteen is justified. It succeeds only if the requirement is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">training of some kind rather than school itself</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"very difficult to reopen once earning has begun","context":"The case for staying is strong. Jobs available to a sixteen-year-old today are few, poorly paid and unstable, and those who leave early earn less throughout their lives and are the first to lose work in a downturn. Two additional years also cover the period when many young people mature enough to take study seriously, and leaving at sixteen closes doors that are very difficult to reopen once earning has begun. There is a social argument as well, since early leavers are more likely to be unemployed or in trouble at eighteen than those who stayed."},{"en":"simply moves the problem into a register","context":"The objection is that compulsion produces resentment. A student who has disliked school for a decade will not improve by being kept in the same building for two more years, and a disruptive class harms everybody in it. Some sixteen-year-olds genuinely learn better through work, and an apprenticeship at that age can lead somewhere a reluctant sixth form will not. Families depending on a young person's wage also feel the cost, and forcing attendance without support simply moves the problem into a register."},{"en":"raised the age and offered only one route","context":"The way to resolve this is to broaden what counts. Requiring education or training until eighteen, and including apprenticeships, technical colleges and part-time study alongside work, keeps young people learning without insisting they all learn in the same way. Countries that have done this report better outcomes than those that raised the age and offered only one route."},{"en":"training of some kind rather than school itself","context":"In conclusion, leaving education at sixteen limits a person's earnings and choices for life, so a requirement until eighteen is justified. It succeeds only if the requirement is training of some kind rather than school itself."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#171","summary":"Whether all young people should remain in school until they are eighteen"},{"id":"writing-task-2-088","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"How humans damage the environment","prompt":"Explain some of the ways in which humans are damaging the environment. What can governments do to address these problems? What can individual people do?","answer":"Humans are responsible for a variety of environmental problems, but we can also take steps to reduce the damage that we are causing to the planet. Both governments and individuals need to act, since neither group can solve pollution and waste on its own.\n\nTwo of the biggest threats to the environment are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">air pollution and waste</strong>. Gas emissions from factories and exhaust fumes from vehicles lead to global warming, which may have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a devastating effect on</strong> the planet in the future. As the human population increases, we are also producing ever greater quantities of waste, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contaminates the earth and pollutes rivers and oceans</strong>.\n\nGovernments could certainly make more effort to reduce air pollution. They could introduce laws to limit emissions from factories or to force companies to use renewable energy from solar, wind or water power. They could also impose ‘green taxes’ on drivers and airline companies. In this way, people would be encouraged to use public transport and to take fewer flights abroad, therefore reducing emissions.\n\nIndividuals should also take responsibility for the impact they have on the environment. They can insulate their homes and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">switch off appliances that are not in use</strong>, choose products with less packaging, and recycle as much as possible. Most supermarkets now provide reusable bags for shoppers as well as ‘banks’ for recycling glass, plastic and paper in their car parks. By reusing and recycling, we can help to reduce waste.\n\nIn conclusion, both national governments and individuals must <strong class=\"key-phrase\">play their part in looking after the environment</strong> for future generations.","phrases":[{"en":"air pollution and waste","context":"Two of the biggest threats to the environment are air pollution and waste. Gas emissions from factories and exhaust fumes from vehicles lead to global warming, which may have a devastating effect on the planet in the future. As the human population increases, we are also producing ever greater quantities of waste, which contaminates the earth and pollutes rivers and oceans."},{"en":"a devastating effect on","context":"Two of the biggest threats to the environment are air pollution and waste. Gas emissions from factories and exhaust fumes from vehicles lead to global warming, which may have a devastating effect on the planet in the future. As the human population increases, we are also producing ever greater quantities of waste, which contaminates the earth and pollutes rivers and oceans."},{"en":"contaminates the earth and pollutes rivers and oceans","context":"Two of the biggest threats to the environment are air pollution and waste. Gas emissions from factories and exhaust fumes from vehicles lead to global warming, which may have a devastating effect on the planet in the future. As the human population increases, we are also producing ever greater quantities of waste, which contaminates the earth and pollutes rivers and oceans."},{"en":"switch off appliances that are not in use","context":"Individuals should also take responsibility for the impact they have on the environment. They can insulate their homes and switch off appliances that are not in use, choose products with less packaging, and recycle as much as possible. Most supermarkets now provide reusable bags for shoppers as well as ‘banks’ for recycling glass, plastic and paper in their car parks. By reusing and recycling, we can help to reduce waste."},{"en":"play their part in looking after the environment","context":"In conclusion, both national governments and individuals must play their part in looking after the environment for future generations."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#172","summary":"Environmental damage: shared responsibility"},{"id":"writing-task-2-089","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Can the environment only be protected internationally?","prompt":"Environmental problems are serious in many countries. It means that the only possible way to protect the environment is at an international level. To what extent do you agree or disagree on this hypothesis?","answer":"Globally, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">environmental degradation</strong> has been recognised as one of the main challenges that people have to overcome in the 21st century. The rapid deterioration of the environment is believed to impose a severe threat on people&#x27;s survival in the years ahead, if not solved effectively. In addressing this issue, countries worldwide should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">join forces and make a concerted effort</strong>, instead of working individually for inconsistent targets.\n\nThe clearest reason for international cooperation is that environmental harm <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rarely stays inside one border</strong>. Rivers and ocean currents connect countries whether governments intend this or not, so pollution created in one place <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quickly becomes another country&#x27;s problem</strong>. Pesticide run-off from farms beside a shared river, for instance, can poison fishing grounds hundreds of kilometres downstream in a neighbouring state, and carbon released by factories in one country contributes to flooding and drought in others that had no part in producing it. Because no government controls an entire river, ocean or atmosphere alone, protecting shared natural resources is only realistic if neighbouring states act together.\n\nA second benefit is the transfer of money and technology that no country can generate alone. Cleaning a polluted river, replacing coal-fired power stations or protecting a forest all require funding and expertise that many governments simply do not have; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the absence of</strong> outside funding, a government struggling with debt will rarely choose conservation over factories or farmland, however much it might prefer to. This is why funds financed mainly by wealthier, industrialised nations, which produced most of the world&#x27;s historical emissions, matter: they give <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support from the global community</strong> to countries that would otherwise face a straight choice between economic survival and protecting their environment.\n\nTo summarise, cooperation between countries is inevitable in this global campaign, not only because the ecosystem is a unified mechanism but also because international engagement can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make optimal use of</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">financial and human resources</strong>. By collaborating with each other, countries can protect the global environment while using the fewest possible resources.","phrases":[{"en":"environmental degradation","context":"Globally, environmental degradation has been recognised as one of the main challenges that people have to overcome in the 21st century. The rapid deterioration of the environment is believed to impose a severe threat on people's survival in the years ahead, if not solved effectively. In addressing this issue, countries worldwide should join forces and make a concerted effort, instead of working individually for inconsistent targets."},{"en":"join forces and make a concerted effort","context":"Globally, environmental degradation has been recognised as one of the main challenges that people have to overcome in the 21st century. The rapid deterioration of the environment is believed to impose a severe threat on people's survival in the years ahead, if not solved effectively. In addressing this issue, countries worldwide should join forces and make a concerted effort, instead of working individually for inconsistent targets."},{"en":"rarely stays inside one border","context":"The clearest reason for international cooperation is that environmental harm rarely stays inside one border. Rivers and ocean currents connect countries whether governments intend this or not, so pollution created in one place quickly becomes another country's problem. Pesticide run-off from farms beside a shared river, for instance, can poison fishing grounds hundreds of kilometres downstream in a neighbouring state, and carbon released by factories in one country contributes to flooding and drought in others that had no part in producing it. Because no government controls an entire river, ocean or atmosphere alone, protecting shared natural resources is only realistic if neighbouring states act together."},{"en":"quickly becomes another country's problem","context":"The clearest reason for international cooperation is that environmental harm rarely stays inside one border. Rivers and ocean currents connect countries whether governments intend this or not, so pollution created in one place quickly becomes another country's problem. Pesticide run-off from farms beside a shared river, for instance, can poison fishing grounds hundreds of kilometres downstream in a neighbouring state, and carbon released by factories in one country contributes to flooding and drought in others that had no part in producing it. Because no government controls an entire river, ocean or atmosphere alone, protecting shared natural resources is only realistic if neighbouring states act together."},{"en":"in the absence of","context":"A second benefit is the transfer of money and technology that no country can generate alone. Cleaning a polluted river, replacing coal-fired power stations or protecting a forest all require funding and expertise that many governments simply do not have; in the absence of outside funding, a government struggling with debt will rarely choose conservation over factories or farmland, however much it might prefer to. This is why funds financed mainly by wealthier, industrialised nations, which produced most of the world's historical emissions, matter: they give support from the global community to countries that would otherwise face a straight choice between economic survival and protecting their environment."},{"en":"support from the global community","context":"A second benefit is the transfer of money and technology that no country can generate alone. Cleaning a polluted river, replacing coal-fired power stations or protecting a forest all require funding and expertise that many governments simply do not have; in the absence of outside funding, a government struggling with debt will rarely choose conservation over factories or farmland, however much it might prefer to. This is why funds financed mainly by wealthier, industrialised nations, which produced most of the world's historical emissions, matter: they give support from the global community to countries that would otherwise face a straight choice between economic survival and protecting their environment."},{"en":"make optimal use of","context":"To summarise, cooperation between countries is inevitable in this global campaign, not only because the ecosystem is a unified mechanism but also because international engagement can make optimal use of financial and human resources. By collaborating with each other, countries can protect the global environment while using the fewest possible resources."},{"en":"financial and human resources","context":"To summarise, cooperation between countries is inevitable in this global campaign, not only because the ecosystem is a unified mechanism but also because international engagement can make optimal use of financial and human resources. By collaborating with each other, countries can protect the global environment while using the fewest possible resources."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#173","summary":"Environmental problems are serious in many countries."},{"id":"writing-task-2-090","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Environmental damage and living standards","prompt":"Some industrialised countries have serious environmental problems. The damage to the environment is an inevitable result when a country tends to improve the standard of living. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Economic development can place heavy pressure on the environment, but serious damage is not an unavoidable price of a higher standard of living. I disagree with the view that pollution and resource loss must accompany progress. The outcome depends on the energy, transport and industrial policies a country chooses.\n\nRapid growth often increases electricity use, construction, traffic and consumption. If this expansion depends on coal, inefficient factories and weak regulation, air and water quality will deteriorate. Poorer governments may also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prioritise immediate jobs and tax revenue</strong> over long-term environmental protection. These pressures explain why industrialisation has caused severe pollution in many countries.\n\nHowever, development also gives societies the money and technology needed to solve these problems. Cleaner power, efficient public transport and strict emissions standards can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">allow incomes to rise while pollution falls</strong>. Governments can price carbon, protect forests and require companies to pay for the damage they cause. Investment in insulation and renewable energy may even reduce household costs, so environmental policy can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support rather than obstruct prosperity</strong>.\n\nPublic reporting is equally important: when emissions and health effects are measured openly, voters and investors can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reward cleaner choices</strong> and challenge industries that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transfer their costs to society</strong>. This also makes environmental performance easier to compare across sectors and over time.\n\nIn conclusion, environmental harm is a risk of badly managed growth, not an inevitable result of improving living standards. Countries should measure progress through health, resource security and quality of life as well as income. With firm regulation and clean investment, they can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise living standards without repeating past mistakes</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"prioritise immediate jobs and tax revenue","context":"Rapid growth often increases electricity use, construction, traffic and consumption. If this expansion depends on coal, inefficient factories and weak regulation, air and water quality will deteriorate. Poorer governments may also prioritise immediate jobs and tax revenue over long-term environmental protection. These pressures explain why industrialisation has caused severe pollution in many countries."},{"en":"allow incomes to rise while pollution falls","context":"However, development also gives societies the money and technology needed to solve these problems. Cleaner power, efficient public transport and strict emissions standards can allow incomes to rise while pollution falls. Governments can price carbon, protect forests and require companies to pay for the damage they cause. Investment in insulation and renewable energy may even reduce household costs, so environmental policy can support rather than obstruct prosperity."},{"en":"support rather than obstruct prosperity","context":"However, development also gives societies the money and technology needed to solve these problems. Cleaner power, efficient public transport and strict emissions standards can allow incomes to rise while pollution falls. Governments can price carbon, protect forests and require companies to pay for the damage they cause. Investment in insulation and renewable energy may even reduce household costs, so environmental policy can support rather than obstruct prosperity."},{"en":"reward cleaner choices","context":"Public reporting is equally important: when emissions and health effects are measured openly, voters and investors can reward cleaner choices and challenge industries that transfer their costs to society. This also makes environmental performance easier to compare across sectors and over time."},{"en":"transfer their costs to society","context":"Public reporting is equally important: when emissions and health effects are measured openly, voters and investors can reward cleaner choices and challenge industries that transfer their costs to society. This also makes environmental performance easier to compare across sectors and over time."},{"en":"raise living standards without repeating past mistakes","context":"In conclusion, environmental harm is a risk of badly managed growth, not an inevitable result of improving living standards. Countries should measure progress through health, resource security and quality of life as well as income. With firm regulation and clean investment, they can raise living standards without repeating past mistakes."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#174","summary":"Industrialised countries have serious environmental problems."},{"id":"writing-task-2-091","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Raising fuel prices to protect the environment","prompt":"It is said that the best way to solve the world's environmental problems is to increase the price of fuel. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It has been suggested that raising the price of fuel is the most effective way to tackle the world&#x27;s environmental problems. I agree that higher fuel prices would reduce emissions, but I disagree that this measure alone could solve problems of such variety and scale.\n\nThe argument for it is straightforward. Burning petrol, diesel and coal is the largest single source of the greenhouse gases that cause climate change, and price is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most reliable influence on behaviour</strong>. When fuel becomes expensive, people drive less, use public transport, buy smaller cars and insulate their homes, while manufacturers redesign products to use less energy. Countries with high fuel taxes, such as those in northern Europe, consistently record lower emissions per person than countries where fuel is cheap.\n\nThere are, however, two serious objections. The first is fairness. Fuel costs represent a much larger share of a poor family&#x27;s income than of a wealthy one&#x27;s, and a rural worker with no bus service <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has no realistic alternative</strong> to driving. A price rise would therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">punish those least able to change their behaviour</strong>, unless the money raised were returned to them.\n\nThe second is that fuel is only one part of the problem. Deforestation, intensive agriculture, plastic waste, overfishing and habitat loss would continue regardless of what petrol costs, and these require regulation and international agreement rather than pricing.\n\nIn conclusion, higher fuel prices are a useful and probably necessary tool, and they should be introduced gradually so that households can adjust. I disagree, however, that they represent <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the best or only solution</strong> to problems of this range and complexity.","phrases":[{"en":"the most reliable influence on behaviour","context":"The argument for it is straightforward. Burning petrol, diesel and coal is the largest single source of the greenhouse gases that cause climate change, and price is the most reliable influence on behaviour. When fuel becomes expensive, people drive less, use public transport, buy smaller cars and insulate their homes, while manufacturers redesign products to use less energy. Countries with high fuel taxes, such as those in northern Europe, consistently record lower emissions per person than countries where fuel is cheap."},{"en":"has no realistic alternative","context":"There are, however, two serious objections. The first is fairness. Fuel costs represent a much larger share of a poor family's income than of a wealthy one's, and a rural worker with no bus service has no realistic alternative to driving. A price rise would therefore punish those least able to change their behaviour, unless the money raised were returned to them."},{"en":"punish those least able to change their behaviour","context":"There are, however, two serious objections. The first is fairness. Fuel costs represent a much larger share of a poor family's income than of a wealthy one's, and a rural worker with no bus service has no realistic alternative to driving. A price rise would therefore punish those least able to change their behaviour, unless the money raised were returned to them."},{"en":"the best or only solution","context":"In conclusion, higher fuel prices are a useful and probably necessary tool, and they should be introduced gradually so that households can adjust. I disagree, however, that they represent the best or only solution to problems of this range and complexity."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#175","summary":"Raising fuel prices to protect the environment"},{"id":"writing-task-2-093","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Should air travel be restricted?","prompt":"Some people believe that air travel should be restricted because it causes serious pollution and will use up the world's resources. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The environmental impact of air travel has attracted growing attention as more people use planes for work and leisure. Some therefore argue that restrictions should be imposed on flying. There are valid concerns behind this view, although a complete restriction would be neither practical nor necessary.\n\nThe first concern is that aircraft are responsible for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a significant share of</strong> air pollution. Planes consume large quantities of fuel while taking off, landing and taxiing, and they produce emissions at high altitude. Some might suggest that air travel is the only suitable mode of transport for long-distance journeys. However, trains can often provide an effective alternative on shorter routes while using less energy and causing less pollution.\n\nAircraft also create noise pollution. Although the precise impact of noise on daily life is difficult to measure, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">persistent exposure can increase anxiety and annoyance</strong>. For people living near airports or beneath flight paths, this noise can become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a serious and continuing disturbance</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, these problems do not mean that air travel should be heavily restricted. A more balanced response is to make aviation cleaner and more efficient. For example, airlines can replace older engines with cleaner models, improve flight planning and use fuller aircraft so that more passengers are carried with the same amount of fuel. Governments can also support high-speed rail where it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a realistic substitute</strong>.\n\nIn light of these considerations, the environmental damage caused by aviation deserves serious attention. However, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">targeted technological improvements</strong> and sensible transport policies are preferable to broad restrictions on air travel.","phrases":[{"en":"a significant share of","context":"The first concern is that aircraft are responsible for a significant share of air pollution. Planes consume large quantities of fuel while taking off, landing and taxiing, and they produce emissions at high altitude. Some might suggest that air travel is the only suitable mode of transport for long-distance journeys. However, trains can often provide an effective alternative on shorter routes while using less energy and causing less pollution."},{"en":"persistent exposure can increase anxiety and annoyance","context":"Aircraft also create noise pollution. Although the precise impact of noise on daily life is difficult to measure, persistent exposure can increase anxiety and annoyance. For people living near airports or beneath flight paths, this noise can become a serious and continuing disturbance."},{"en":"a serious and continuing disturbance","context":"Aircraft also create noise pollution. Although the precise impact of noise on daily life is difficult to measure, persistent exposure can increase anxiety and annoyance. For people living near airports or beneath flight paths, this noise can become a serious and continuing disturbance."},{"en":"a realistic substitute","context":"Nevertheless, these problems do not mean that air travel should be heavily restricted. A more balanced response is to make aviation cleaner and more efficient. For example, airlines can replace older engines with cleaner models, improve flight planning and use fuller aircraft so that more passengers are carried with the same amount of fuel. Governments can also support high-speed rail where it is a realistic substitute."},{"en":"targeted technological improvements","context":"In light of these considerations, the environmental damage caused by aviation deserves serious attention. However, targeted technological improvements and sensible transport policies are preferable to broad restrictions on air travel."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#176","summary":"People believe that air travel should be restricted because it causes…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-095","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Water shortages","prompt":"Water resources around the world are becoming increasingly scarce. What are the possible reasons for this, and what solutions can you suggest?","answer":"Water shortages affect many regions and result from a combination of environmental pressures and human decisions. In my view, the most useful responses are to improve agricultural water management, reduce unnecessary losses and create fair incentives for conservation.\n\nAgriculture accounts for the largest share of global <strong class=\"key-phrase\">freshwater withdrawals</strong>, so inefficient irrigation can place <strong class=\"key-phrase\">substantial pressure on</strong> supplies. Water may be lost through evaporation or poorly managed distribution, while crops with high water needs are sometimes grown in areas with limited resources. Population growth and expanding cities add demand. Leaking urban pipes can waste treated water before it reaches households, and climate change can make rainfall and drought patterns less predictable. These factors interact rather than operate as separate problems.\n\nOne priority is to help farmers use appropriate methods and crops. Drip irrigation can reduce losses in suitable conditions, but equipment needs maintenance and careful management. Choosing crops suited to local <strong class=\"key-phrase\">water availability</strong> may be equally important. Governments should assess the wider effect, because improving efficiency does not necessarily save water overall if production then expands substantially.\n\nCities also need investment in basic infrastructure. Finding leaks, repairing pipes and monitoring usage can protect existing supplies rather than relying only on new reservoirs. Pricing can support these measures if it rewards efficient use while keeping <strong class=\"key-phrase\">essential household water</strong> affordable. Large commercial users should not have an unlimited incentive to consume a scarce resource, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">low-income families</strong> should not be denied safe water because of their ability to pay.\n\nIn conclusion, scarcity reflects pressure from farming, urban development and changing environmental conditions. A realistic strategy combines better management and infrastructure with policies that protect essential access and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">discourage avoidable waste</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"freshwater withdrawals","context":"Agriculture accounts for the largest share of global freshwater withdrawals, so inefficient irrigation can place substantial pressure on supplies. Water may be lost through evaporation or poorly managed distribution, while crops with high water needs are sometimes grown in areas with limited resources. Population growth and expanding cities add demand. Leaking urban pipes can waste treated water before it reaches households, and climate change can make rainfall and drought patterns less predictable. These factors interact rather than operate as separate problems."},{"en":"substantial pressure on","context":"Agriculture accounts for the largest share of global freshwater withdrawals, so inefficient irrigation can place substantial pressure on supplies. Water may be lost through evaporation or poorly managed distribution, while crops with high water needs are sometimes grown in areas with limited resources. Population growth and expanding cities add demand. Leaking urban pipes can waste treated water before it reaches households, and climate change can make rainfall and drought patterns less predictable. These factors interact rather than operate as separate problems."},{"en":"water availability","context":"One priority is to help farmers use appropriate methods and crops. Drip irrigation can reduce losses in suitable conditions, but equipment needs maintenance and careful management. Choosing crops suited to local water availability may be equally important. Governments should assess the wider effect, because improving efficiency does not necessarily save water overall if production then expands substantially."},{"en":"essential household water","context":"Cities also need investment in basic infrastructure. Finding leaks, repairing pipes and monitoring usage can protect existing supplies rather than relying only on new reservoirs. Pricing can support these measures if it rewards efficient use while keeping essential household water affordable. Large commercial users should not have an unlimited incentive to consume a scarce resource, but low-income families should not be denied safe water because of their ability to pay."},{"en":"low-income families","context":"Cities also need investment in basic infrastructure. Finding leaks, repairing pipes and monitoring usage can protect existing supplies rather than relying only on new reservoirs. Pricing can support these measures if it rewards efficient use while keeping essential household water affordable. Large commercial users should not have an unlimited incentive to consume a scarce resource, but low-income families should not be denied safe water because of their ability to pay."},{"en":"discourage avoidable waste","context":"In conclusion, scarcity reflects pressure from farming, urban development and changing environmental conditions. A realistic strategy combines better management and infrastructure with policies that protect essential access and discourage avoidable waste."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#177","summary":"Water shortages"},{"id":"writing-task-2-153","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"The throw-away society","prompt":"Many people believe that modern society has become a throw-away society, filled with plastic bags and rubbish. What are the causes of this, and what solutions can you suggest?","answer":"It is widely observed that modern societies discard an enormous quantity of packaging, plastic and barely used goods. This problem has clear causes, and it can only be solved by changing the economics of production rather than by appealing to consumers.\n\nThe principal cause is that manufacturing has become extremely cheap. Mass production and low-cost labour mean that a new appliance or garment often costs less than repairing the old one, and repair skills have become correspondingly rare. Manufacturers reinforce this deliberately: products are designed with sealed cases and non-replaceable batteries, and new models are released annually to make functioning items <strong class=\"key-phrase\">appear outdated</strong>. A second cause is packaging, since retailers wrap goods in plastic to protect them in transit and to display them attractively, and almost none of this material is reused.\n\nThe most effective solution would be to make producers responsible for what they sell. If manufacturers were legally obliged to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">collect and recycle their own products</strong>, they would immediately design them to last longer and to be dismantled easily. A second measure is to tax packaging and single-use plastics, since charging for plastic bags has reduced their use dramatically wherever it has been tried. Finally, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a legal right to repair</strong>, requiring spare parts and manuals to be available at reasonable cost, would extend the life of expensive items considerably.\n\nIn conclusion, the throw-away society exists because discarding is cheaper than maintaining. Reversing that calculation through regulation and taxation is far more likely to succeed than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asking individuals to consume less</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"appear outdated","context":"The principal cause is that manufacturing has become extremely cheap. Mass production and low-cost labour mean that a new appliance or garment often costs less than repairing the old one, and repair skills have become correspondingly rare. Manufacturers reinforce this deliberately: products are designed with sealed cases and non-replaceable batteries, and new models are released annually to make functioning items appear outdated. A second cause is packaging, since retailers wrap goods in plastic to protect them in transit and to display them attractively, and almost none of this material is reused."},{"en":"collect and recycle their own products","context":"The most effective solution would be to make producers responsible for what they sell. If manufacturers were legally obliged to collect and recycle their own products, they would immediately design them to last longer and to be dismantled easily. A second measure is to tax packaging and single-use plastics, since charging for plastic bags has reduced their use dramatically wherever it has been tried. Finally, a legal right to repair, requiring spare parts and manuals to be available at reasonable cost, would extend the life of expensive items considerably."},{"en":"a legal right to repair","context":"The most effective solution would be to make producers responsible for what they sell. If manufacturers were legally obliged to collect and recycle their own products, they would immediately design them to last longer and to be dismantled easily. A second measure is to tax packaging and single-use plastics, since charging for plastic bags has reduced their use dramatically wherever it has been tried. Finally, a legal right to repair, requiring spare parts and manuals to be available at reasonable cost, would extend the life of expensive items considerably."},{"en":"asking individuals to consume less","context":"In conclusion, the throw-away society exists because discarding is cheaper than maintaining. Reversing that calculation through regulation and taxation is far more likely to succeed than asking individuals to consume less."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#178","summary":"The throw-away society"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2222","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Who should control fresh water?","prompt":"Some people think that people should be given the right to use fresh water as they like. Others believe governments should strictly control the use of fresh water. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Fresh water is both a basic necessity and a limited resource, and there is disagreement over whether households should use it freely or whether governments should regulate consumption. I believe some control is unavoidable, though it must be designed carefully.\n\nThe argument for free use rests on the idea that water is a right rather than a commodity. People need it to drink, cook and stay clean, and rationing it risks harming the poorest households first, since a family that cannot afford a metered supply may cut back on washing before anything else. There is also a fairness objection: domestic users account for a small fraction of consumption, so restricting them while agriculture and industry continue as before <strong class=\"key-phrase\">places the burden on the wrong people</strong>.\n\nThe case for control is simply that demand can exceed supply. Aquifers in northern India and the American west are being <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pumped faster than rainfall replaces them</strong>, and once a water table falls the damage takes decades to reverse. Where no limits exist, water tends to be used on whatever is most profitable rather than most necessary, which is why arid regions end up <strong class=\"key-phrase\">growing thirsty crops for export</strong>.\n\nMy view is that governments must regulate, but should aim at the largest users rather than at families. Charging industry and irrigation the real cost of water, while guaranteeing every household an affordable basic allowance, cuts consumption without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">threatening anybody&#x27;s health</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, unrestricted use is not sustainable in regions where water is scarce, and the alternative is not rationing showers but pricing large-scale use properly. Handled this way, control protects the resource while leaving ordinary people with what they actually need.","phrases":[{"en":"places the burden on the wrong people","context":"The argument for free use rests on the idea that water is a right rather than a commodity. People need it to drink, cook and stay clean, and rationing it risks harming the poorest households first, since a family that cannot afford a metered supply may cut back on washing before anything else. There is also a fairness objection: domestic users account for a small fraction of consumption, so restricting them while agriculture and industry continue as before places the burden on the wrong people."},{"en":"pumped faster than rainfall replaces them","context":"The case for control is simply that demand can exceed supply. Aquifers in northern India and the American west are being pumped faster than rainfall replaces them, and once a water table falls the damage takes decades to reverse. Where no limits exist, water tends to be used on whatever is most profitable rather than most necessary, which is why arid regions end up growing thirsty crops for export."},{"en":"growing thirsty crops for export","context":"The case for control is simply that demand can exceed supply. Aquifers in northern India and the American west are being pumped faster than rainfall replaces them, and once a water table falls the damage takes decades to reverse. Where no limits exist, water tends to be used on whatever is most profitable rather than most necessary, which is why arid regions end up growing thirsty crops for export."},{"en":"threatening anybody's health","context":"My view is that governments must regulate, but should aim at the largest users rather than at families. Charging industry and irrigation the real cost of water, while guaranteeing every household an affordable basic allowance, cuts consumption without threatening anybody's health."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#179","summary":"Free use of fresh water compared with strict government control"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2243","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Where should recycling be taught?","prompt":"Some people think that children should be taught at school to recycle materials and avoid waste. Other people believe that children should be taught this at home. Discuss both opinions and give your own opinion.","answer":"Environmental habits form early, which raises the question of whether schools or parents should be responsible for teaching them. Both have a part to play, and my own view is that the two do different jobs and neither can be dropped.\n\nThe argument for schools is that they reach every child and can explain the reasoning. A parent can say that a bottle goes in a particular bin, but a teacher can show what happens to plastic that reaches the sea and why some materials can be reprocessed while others cannot, and a child who understands the reason will apply it to situations nobody has covered. Schools also reach children whose families recycle nothing, which is where teaching <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changes the most behaviour</strong>. Pupils frequently take the habit home and press their parents to adopt it.\n\nThe case for the home rests on repetition. Recycling is a daily routine rather than a body of knowledge, and it is learned by sorting rubbish several times a week from the age of three. Children also copy what adults actually do rather than what they are told, so a lesson at school carries little weight in a household where everything <strong class=\"key-phrase\">goes into the same bin</strong>.\n\nIn my view the objection to each is answered by the other. Schools supply the understanding but only a few hours of it; homes supply the daily practice but not always the reason, and sometimes not the example. A child who receives both learns a habit that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">survives leaving home</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, this is not a choice between two options. Schools should explain why waste matters and homes should make sorting normal, since knowledge without routine is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forgotten within a term</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"changes the most behaviour","context":"The argument for schools is that they reach every child and can explain the reasoning. A parent can say that a bottle goes in a particular bin, but a teacher can show what happens to plastic that reaches the sea and why some materials can be reprocessed while others cannot, and a child who understands the reason will apply it to situations nobody has covered. Schools also reach children whose families recycle nothing, which is where teaching changes the most behaviour. Pupils frequently take the habit home and press their parents to adopt it."},{"en":"goes into the same bin","context":"The case for the home rests on repetition. Recycling is a daily routine rather than a body of knowledge, and it is learned by sorting rubbish several times a week from the age of three. Children also copy what adults actually do rather than what they are told, so a lesson at school carries little weight in a household where everything goes into the same bin."},{"en":"survives leaving home","context":"In my view the objection to each is answered by the other. Schools supply the understanding but only a few hours of it; homes supply the daily practice but not always the reason, and sometimes not the example. A child who receives both learns a habit that survives leaving home."},{"en":"forgotten within a term","context":"In conclusion, this is not a choice between two options. Schools should explain why waste matters and homes should make sorting normal, since knowledge without routine is forgotten within a term."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#180","summary":"Teaching children about recycling at school or at home"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2251","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Food and drink in plastic","prompt":"In many countries today, more and more food and drink is sold in plastic containers. Do you think the advantages of this trend outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Almost everything in a modern supermarket arrives wrapped in plastic. The material solves genuine problems, but in my view its disadvantages now outweigh its benefits.\n\nThe advantages are worth stating fairly. Plastic is light, cheap and airtight, so a sealed package keeps meat and salad edible for days longer than an open one, which reduces the amount of food thrown away. Because it weighs so little, transporting goods in plastic burns far less fuel than transporting them in glass, and sealed containers protect food from contamination between the factory and the shelf. In countries without reliable refrigeration, this <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prevents a great deal of illness</strong>.\n\nThe difficulty is what happens afterwards. Food packaging is used for minutes and lasts for centuries, and most of it is not recycled: it is thin, often made of mixed layers that cannot be separated, and contaminated by the food it held. What is collected is frequently exported to countries with weaker regulation, where it is burned or dumped. The result is visible on coastlines and invisible in the food chain, since plastic breaks into fragments that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enter fish and eventually people</strong>.\n\nThe cost also falls in the wrong place. Manufacturers gain the savings while councils and taxpayers pay for collection and disposal, so there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no pressure on producers to use less</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, plastic packaging genuinely reduces food waste and illness, and simply banning it would create new problems. Its disadvantages nonetheless outweigh those gains, because the damage is permanent while the benefit lasts a few days, and this will only change when producers are made responsible for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what happens to the packaging they create</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"prevents a great deal of illness","context":"The advantages are worth stating fairly. Plastic is light, cheap and airtight, so a sealed package keeps meat and salad edible for days longer than an open one, which reduces the amount of food thrown away. Because it weighs so little, transporting goods in plastic burns far less fuel than transporting them in glass, and sealed containers protect food from contamination between the factory and the shelf. In countries without reliable refrigeration, this prevents a great deal of illness."},{"en":"enter fish and eventually people","context":"The difficulty is what happens afterwards. Food packaging is used for minutes and lasts for centuries, and most of it is not recycled: it is thin, often made of mixed layers that cannot be separated, and contaminated by the food it held. What is collected is frequently exported to countries with weaker regulation, where it is burned or dumped. The result is visible on coastlines and invisible in the food chain, since plastic breaks into fragments that enter fish and eventually people."},{"en":"no pressure on producers to use less","context":"The cost also falls in the wrong place. Manufacturers gain the savings while councils and taxpayers pay for collection and disposal, so there is no pressure on producers to use less."},{"en":"what happens to the packaging they create","context":"In conclusion, plastic packaging genuinely reduces food waste and illness, and simply banning it would create new problems. Its disadvantages nonetheless outweigh those gains, because the damage is permanent while the benefit lasts a few days, and this will only change when producers are made responsible for what happens to the packaging they create."}],"subtype":"Advantages outweigh disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#181","summary":"The rise of plastic packaging for food and drink"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2255","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Flights or cars?","prompt":"Long distance flight uses more fuel than cars and brings pollution to the environment. We should discourage non-essential flights rather than limit the use of cars. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Aviation and road transport both damage the atmosphere, and it is argued that policy should target unnecessary flying rather than driving. I partly agree, because flying is harder to make clean, but I do not accept that cars should therefore be left alone.\n\nThe case for concentrating on flights is strong in one respect. A single return flight between Europe and Asia produces more carbon per passenger than a year of typical local driving, and the emissions are released high in the atmosphere, where their warming effect is greater. Aviation fuel is also untaxed in most countries, so tickets are artificially cheap, and the technology to replace kerosene on long routes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does not yet exist at scale</strong>. Discouraging the least necessary journeys is therefore one of the few tools available.\n\nHowever, treating this as an alternative to reducing car use misreads the arithmetic. Road transport produces several times more emissions in total than aviation, simply because so many more people drive so much more often, and a small reduction in a very large number outweighs a large reduction in a small one. Flying is also concentrated among wealthier travellers, so a policy aimed only at flights <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves most of the problem untouched</strong> while appearing decisive.\n\nCars are additionally the easier problem to solve. Electric vehicles, better buses and cycle lanes already work, whereas no equivalent exists for a long-haul aircraft, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">progress is available now</strong> on the road.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that unnecessary flights deserve discouragement, since aviation is difficult to decarbonise and currently undertaxed. Presenting this as a reason to spare cars is mistaken, because driving causes the larger share of the damage and is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the part we already know how to fix</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"does not yet exist at scale","context":"The case for concentrating on flights is strong in one respect. A single return flight between Europe and Asia produces more carbon per passenger than a year of typical local driving, and the emissions are released high in the atmosphere, where their warming effect is greater. Aviation fuel is also untaxed in most countries, so tickets are artificially cheap, and the technology to replace kerosene on long routes does not yet exist at scale. Discouraging the least necessary journeys is therefore one of the few tools available."},{"en":"leaves most of the problem untouched","context":"However, treating this as an alternative to reducing car use misreads the arithmetic. Road transport produces several times more emissions in total than aviation, simply because so many more people drive so much more often, and a small reduction in a very large number outweighs a large reduction in a small one. Flying is also concentrated among wealthier travellers, so a policy aimed only at flights leaves most of the problem untouched while appearing decisive."},{"en":"progress is available now","context":"Cars are additionally the easier problem to solve. Electric vehicles, better buses and cycle lanes already work, whereas no equivalent exists for a long-haul aircraft, so progress is available now on the road."},{"en":"the part we already know how to fix","context":"In conclusion, I agree that unnecessary flights deserve discouragement, since aviation is difficult to decarbonise and currently undertaxed. Presenting this as a reason to spare cars is mistaken, because driving causes the larger share of the damage and is the part we already know how to fix."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#182","summary":"Discouraging non-essential flights rather than limiting car use"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2260","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Drilling in untouched places","prompt":"The increased world demand for oil and gas has made it necessary to locate these sources in remote and untouched natural areas. Do you think the advantages outweigh the disadvantages of damaging these natural areas?","answer":"As accessible reserves are exhausted, energy companies have moved into the Arctic, deep oceans and rainforest. I do not believe the advantages outweigh the damage, although the arguments in favour deserve to be taken seriously.\n\nThe case for extraction rests on need. Modern economies still run on oil and gas, and a country that cannot secure supply faces rising prices that fall hardest on poorer households, who spend the largest share of their income on heating and transport. Domestic production also reduces dependence on unstable exporters, and for developing countries a discovery can fund schools and hospitals that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">would otherwise be unaffordable</strong>.\n\nThe damage, however, is of a different kind from the benefit. These regions are remote precisely because they are difficult to reach, which means that any spill is almost impossible to contain: oil in Arctic ice degrades over decades rather than years, and there is no equipment capable of cleaning it. Extraction also brings roads, pipelines and settlements into ecosystems that had none, so the disturbance <strong class=\"key-phrase\">extends far beyond the drilling site</strong>. Indigenous communities lose the land their way of life depends on.\n\nThe decisive objection is that this is investment in the wrong direction. A new field takes decades to repay its cost, which commits a country to burning fossil fuels long after it has promised to stop, and the same capital spent on renewable generation would deliver energy security <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without any of the damage</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the disadvantages outweigh the advantages. The energy obtained is real but temporary, whereas the harm to fragile environments is effectively permanent, and the money involved would buy more security if it were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">directed at replacing the demand instead</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"would otherwise be unaffordable","context":"The case for extraction rests on need. Modern economies still run on oil and gas, and a country that cannot secure supply faces rising prices that fall hardest on poorer households, who spend the largest share of their income on heating and transport. Domestic production also reduces dependence on unstable exporters, and for developing countries a discovery can fund schools and hospitals that would otherwise be unaffordable."},{"en":"extends far beyond the drilling site","context":"The damage, however, is of a different kind from the benefit. These regions are remote precisely because they are difficult to reach, which means that any spill is almost impossible to contain: oil in Arctic ice degrades over decades rather than years, and there is no equipment capable of cleaning it. Extraction also brings roads, pipelines and settlements into ecosystems that had none, so the disturbance extends far beyond the drilling site. Indigenous communities lose the land their way of life depends on."},{"en":"without any of the damage","context":"The decisive objection is that this is investment in the wrong direction. A new field takes decades to repay its cost, which commits a country to burning fossil fuels long after it has promised to stop, and the same capital spent on renewable generation would deliver energy security without any of the damage."},{"en":"directed at replacing the demand instead","context":"In conclusion, the disadvantages outweigh the advantages. The energy obtained is real but temporary, whereas the harm to fragile environments is effectively permanent, and the money involved would buy more security if it were directed at replacing the demand instead."}],"subtype":"Advantages outweigh disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#183","summary":"Extracting oil and gas from remote natural areas"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2269","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"The cost of making more goods","prompt":"The increase in the production of consumer goods results in damage to the natural environment. What are the causes of this? What can be done to solve this problem?","answer":"Factories now produce more clothing, electronics and furniture than at any point in history, and the environmental cost is visible in emissions, landfill and polluted rivers. The causes lie in how goods are designed and priced, and the solutions must change both.\n\nThe first cause is that products are built to be replaced. Clothing is made cheaply enough to be worn a few times, and appliances are designed so that a failed component cannot be replaced without buying the whole machine, which means each purchase must be manufactured, shipped and eventually discarded. Marketing reinforces this by presenting last year&#x27;s model as embarrassing rather than adequate, so goods are thrown away while still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">perfectly capable of working</strong>.\n\nThe second cause is that prices do not reflect damage. A manufacturer pays for materials and labour but not for the river it pollutes or the carbon it releases, so the cheapest production method is usually the dirtiest and the cost is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carried by the public instead</strong>.\n\nThe solutions follow directly. Making producers legally responsible for their products at the end of life gives them a financial reason to design goods that can be repaired and dismantled, and this has already reduced electronic waste where it has been applied. Pricing pollution through carbon taxes removes the advantage currently enjoyed by dirty production. Requiring spare parts to be available for a fixed number of years would also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make repair a realistic option</strong> again.\n\nIn conclusion, the damage arises because goods are designed for a short life and their true cost is never charged. It can be reduced by making manufacturers responsible for what they produce and by ensuring that the price of an item <strong class=\"key-phrase\">includes the harm it causes</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"perfectly capable of working","context":"The first cause is that products are built to be replaced. Clothing is made cheaply enough to be worn a few times, and appliances are designed so that a failed component cannot be replaced without buying the whole machine, which means each purchase must be manufactured, shipped and eventually discarded. Marketing reinforces this by presenting last year's model as embarrassing rather than adequate, so goods are thrown away while still perfectly capable of working."},{"en":"carried by the public instead","context":"The second cause is that prices do not reflect damage. A manufacturer pays for materials and labour but not for the river it pollutes or the carbon it releases, so the cheapest production method is usually the dirtiest and the cost is carried by the public instead."},{"en":"make repair a realistic option","context":"The solutions follow directly. Making producers legally responsible for their products at the end of life gives them a financial reason to design goods that can be repaired and dismantled, and this has already reduced electronic waste where it has been applied. Pricing pollution through carbon taxes removes the advantage currently enjoyed by dirty production. Requiring spare parts to be available for a fixed number of years would also make repair a realistic option again."},{"en":"includes the harm it causes","context":"In conclusion, the damage arises because goods are designed for a short life and their true cost is never charged. It can be reduced by making manufacturers responsible for what they produce and by ensuring that the price of an item includes the harm it causes."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#184","summary":"Environmental damage caused by rising production of consumer goods"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2283","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Why so little gets recycled","prompt":"In many countries, there is not enough recycling of waste materials such as paper, glass and cans. What are the reasons for this, and what can be done to solve the problem?","answer":"Most households claim to support recycling, yet the proportion of material actually recovered remains low in many countries. The causes are practical and economic rather than a lack of goodwill, and the remedies have to work on both.\n\nConfusion is the first obstacle. Rules differ between districts and change without explanation, packaging is rarely labelled clearly, and a resident who cannot tell whether a container is acceptable puts it in the ordinary bin to be safe. Convenience is the second: where recycling requires washing items, separating them into four containers and carrying them to a distant point, participation falls sharply, particularly among people in flats with no space for the bins.\n\nThe economics matter more than most people realise. Recycled material must compete with new material, and while oil is cheap new plastic is cheaper than recovered plastic, so collected waste sometimes has no buyer. Products are also designed without recovery in mind: mixed plastics, glued laminates and coated cartons <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be separated at any reasonable cost</strong>. Meanwhile landfill and incineration remain the cheap option for local authorities in many places.\n\nEffective measures exist. Deposit schemes on bottles and cans, where a small sum is refunded on return, achieve recovery rates above ninety per cent wherever they operate, because they attach a price to the object. Simple, standardised rules across a country and clear symbols on packaging remove the confusion, and frequent collection from the doorstep removes the effort. The strongest measure is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make producers responsible for the cost of disposal</strong>, which pushes them to redesign packaging rather than leaving councils to deal with it. Charging by the weight of unsorted rubbish also works, provided low-income households are protected.\n\nIn conclusion, recycling rates are low because the system is confusing, inconvenient and often uneconomic. Deposit schemes, uniform rules and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">charging producers for what they create</strong> would change all three.","phrases":[{"en":"cannot be separated at any reasonable cost","context":"The economics matter more than most people realise. Recycled material must compete with new material, and while oil is cheap new plastic is cheaper than recovered plastic, so collected waste sometimes has no buyer. Products are also designed without recovery in mind: mixed plastics, glued laminates and coated cartons cannot be separated at any reasonable cost. Meanwhile landfill and incineration remain the cheap option for local authorities in many places."},{"en":"make producers responsible for the cost of disposal","context":"Effective measures exist. Deposit schemes on bottles and cans, where a small sum is refunded on return, achieve recovery rates above ninety per cent wherever they operate, because they attach a price to the object. Simple, standardised rules across a country and clear symbols on packaging remove the confusion, and frequent collection from the doorstep removes the effort. The strongest measure is to make producers responsible for the cost of disposal, which pushes them to redesign packaging rather than leaving councils to deal with it. Charging by the weight of unsorted rubbish also works, provided low-income households are protected."},{"en":"charging producers for what they create","context":"In conclusion, recycling rates are low because the system is confusing, inconvenient and often uneconomic. Deposit schemes, uniform rules and charging producers for what they create would change all three."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#185","summary":"The reasons for low recycling rates and how they could be raised"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2290","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Prevent climate change or adapt to it?","prompt":"Some people say that instead of preventing climate change we should find a way to live with it. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Adaptation has moved from a fringe idea to a central part of climate policy, and some now argue it should replace the attempt to cut emissions. I disagree with that substitution, although adaptation is necessary and currently underfunded.\n\nThe argument for adapting has a factual basis. A degree of warming is already unavoidable, because emissions released decades ago continue to act, so sea defences, drought-resistant crops and heat-proofed buildings are needed whatever happens next. Adaptation also has the advantage that a country benefits directly from its own spending, unlike emission cuts, whose benefit is shared with everyone including those who do nothing. Given how slowly international agreements progress, protecting yourself is at least within your own control.\n\nThe case against treating it as an alternative is decisive, however. Adaptation has limits that prevention does not: a wall can be built against a metre of sea level rise but not against ten, and no engineering protects a region whose rainfall has stopped. Costs also rise faster than the temperature, so each further degree makes adaptation less affordable, and the ability to pay is unequal, meaning a wealthy country builds defences while poorer ones <strong class=\"key-phrase\">absorb losses they did not cause</strong>. Some consequences cannot be adapted to at all, since a species that cannot move or a glacier feeding a river system <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does not respond to engineering</strong>.\n\nThe two are also not competing for the same money in the way the argument suggests. Preventing further warming reduces how much adaptation will eventually be required, so spending on emissions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lowers the future adaptation bill</strong> rather than diverting funds from it.\n\nIn conclusion, adapting to changes already under way is unavoidable and deserves far more investment than it receives. Abandoning prevention would raise the eventual cost beyond anything adaptation could meet, so both must be pursued together.","phrases":[{"en":"absorb losses they did not cause","context":"The case against treating it as an alternative is decisive, however. Adaptation has limits that prevention does not: a wall can be built against a metre of sea level rise but not against ten, and no engineering protects a region whose rainfall has stopped. Costs also rise faster than the temperature, so each further degree makes adaptation less affordable, and the ability to pay is unequal, meaning a wealthy country builds defences while poorer ones absorb losses they did not cause. Some consequences cannot be adapted to at all, since a species that cannot move or a glacier feeding a river system does not respond to engineering."},{"en":"does not respond to engineering","context":"The case against treating it as an alternative is decisive, however. Adaptation has limits that prevention does not: a wall can be built against a metre of sea level rise but not against ten, and no engineering protects a region whose rainfall has stopped. Costs also rise faster than the temperature, so each further degree makes adaptation less affordable, and the ability to pay is unequal, meaning a wealthy country builds defences while poorer ones absorb losses they did not cause. Some consequences cannot be adapted to at all, since a species that cannot move or a glacier feeding a river system does not respond to engineering."},{"en":"lowers the future adaptation bill","context":"The two are also not competing for the same money in the way the argument suggests. Preventing further warming reduces how much adaptation will eventually be required, so spending on emissions lowers the future adaptation bill rather than diverting funds from it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#186","summary":"Whether we should adapt to a changing climate rather than try to stop it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2302","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Climate change and business","prompt":"Some people think that climate change could have a negative effect on business, while others think it could create more business opportunities. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Companies now treat climate change as a financial question rather than an environmental one, and their conclusions differ sharply. Both readings are accurate for particular industries, and my own view is that the losses will be larger and will fall on those least able to absorb them.\n\nThe case for damage is straightforward. Floods, storms and heat destroy factories, crops and transport links, and insurers in several regions have stopped covering the risk altogether, which makes some businesses impossible to run. Supply chains are disrupted by drought and low rivers, and agriculture, tourism and fishing face conditions their whole business model assumed would not change. Regulation adds a second cost, since carbon pricing and emissions rules make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">existing methods more expensive to continue</strong>.\n\nThe opportunity argument is equally grounded. Any problem of this size creates markets: renewable energy, insulation, batteries, electric transport and efficient farming are among the fastest-growing industries anywhere, and companies that moved early have profited substantially. Adaptation is a market of its own, covering flood defences, cooling, drought-resistant seed and insurance products, and firms that reduce energy use <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cut their costs while reducing emissions</strong>.\n\nThe two views can be reconciled by asking who gains. The opportunities are concentrated in wealthy countries with capital, technology and stable institutions, while the damage falls hardest on poorer regions whose economies depend on agriculture and which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot borrow to rebuild</strong>. Even where new industries grow, they arrive in different places from the ones that decline.\n\nIn conclusion, climate change will destroy some businesses and create others, and the sectors benefiting are already visible. The destruction will be greater in total and unevenly distributed, so treating the situation as an opportunity <strong class=\"key-phrase\">describes the view from the countries least affected</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"existing methods more expensive to continue","context":"The case for damage is straightforward. Floods, storms and heat destroy factories, crops and transport links, and insurers in several regions have stopped covering the risk altogether, which makes some businesses impossible to run. Supply chains are disrupted by drought and low rivers, and agriculture, tourism and fishing face conditions their whole business model assumed would not change. Regulation adds a second cost, since carbon pricing and emissions rules make existing methods more expensive to continue."},{"en":"cut their costs while reducing emissions","context":"The opportunity argument is equally grounded. Any problem of this size creates markets: renewable energy, insulation, batteries, electric transport and efficient farming are among the fastest-growing industries anywhere, and companies that moved early have profited substantially. Adaptation is a market of its own, covering flood defences, cooling, drought-resistant seed and insurance products, and firms that reduce energy use cut their costs while reducing emissions."},{"en":"cannot borrow to rebuild","context":"The two views can be reconciled by asking who gains. The opportunities are concentrated in wealthy countries with capital, technology and stable institutions, while the damage falls hardest on poorer regions whose economies depend on agriculture and which cannot borrow to rebuild. Even where new industries grow, they arrive in different places from the ones that decline."},{"en":"describes the view from the countries least affected","context":"In conclusion, climate change will destroy some businesses and create others, and the sectors benefiting are already visible. The destruction will be greater in total and unevenly distributed, so treating the situation as an opportunity describes the view from the countries least affected."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#187","summary":"Whether a changing climate threatens business or creates opportunities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2315","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Politicians or individuals on the environment?","prompt":"Environmental protection is the responsibility of politicians, not individuals, because individuals can do too little. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Faced with a problem the size of climate change, most people conclude that their own recycling counts for nothing. I largely agree that governments carry the decisive responsibility, but the conclusion that individuals therefore have none is mistaken.\n\nGovernments hold the tools that matter. Only a state can price carbon, set building standards, rewrite planning law or shut down a coal plant, and a single one of those decisions outweighs the lifetime efforts of thousands of careful households. Governments also fix the conditions under which individuals choose: a person cannot cycle where there is no safe route, and cannot avoid a car where nothing else runs. Blaming consumers is often a convenient way of moving attention away from policy, which is why some of the loudest campaigns about personal footprints were funded by the industries responsible.\n\nIndividual behaviour still matters for three reasons. It adds up, since household energy, food and transport account for a large share of national emissions, and no policy reaches those choices directly. It also creates the market that policy needs, because electric cars and plant-based food became cheap only after enough people bought the expensive early versions. Most importantly, individuals are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">voters, employees and shareholders</strong> as well as consumers, and the political will to pass difficult laws only exists where the public has already <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shifted its own expectations</strong>.\n\nThe two are therefore linked rather than opposed. Governments act when the public demands it, and the public demands what it has begun to practise.\n\nIn conclusion, politicians must lead, because the decisions with real weight are theirs alone. Individuals who conclude that nothing they do counts remove the pressure that makes such leadership possible.","phrases":[{"en":"voters, employees and shareholders","context":"Individual behaviour still matters for three reasons. It adds up, since household energy, food and transport account for a large share of national emissions, and no policy reaches those choices directly. It also creates the market that policy needs, because electric cars and plant-based food became cheap only after enough people bought the expensive early versions. Most importantly, individuals are voters, employees and shareholders as well as consumers, and the political will to pass difficult laws only exists where the public has already shifted its own expectations."},{"en":"shifted its own expectations","context":"Individual behaviour still matters for three reasons. It adds up, since household energy, food and transport account for a large share of national emissions, and no policy reaches those choices directly. It also creates the market that policy needs, because electric cars and plant-based food became cheap only after enough people bought the expensive early versions. Most importantly, individuals are voters, employees and shareholders as well as consumers, and the political will to pass difficult laws only exists where the public has already shifted its own expectations."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#188","summary":"Whether environmental protection is the job of politicians rather than individuals"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2338","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Who should reduce packaging?","prompt":"Some people feel that manufacturers and supermarkets have the responsibility to reduce the amount of packaging on goods. Others argue that customers should avoid buying goods with a lot of packaging. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Household bins fill mostly with wrapping rather than with anything that was bought. Responsibility for that can be placed on the companies producing it or on the shoppers accepting it, and although both hold some, the weight falls clearly on the producers.\n\nThe argument for consumer responsibility is straightforward. Companies supply what sells, so a widespread refusal to buy heavily wrapped goods would change shelves quickly, as the reaction to plastic straws showed. Shoppers can also choose loose vegetables, refill containers and larger sizes, and since the purchase is the moment the waste is created, that is where it can be prevented.\n\nThe case against relying on shoppers is stronger. Choice is largely an illusion in a supermarket, because a customer who wants unwrapped rice may find every brand in plastic, and avoiding packaging then means going without the product. Poorer households in particular cannot pay the premium that unpackaged goods usually carry. Consumers also cannot judge what they are buying, since materials are unlabelled and much of what appears recyclable is not, so the choice <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depends on information the shopper does not have</strong>. Placing the duty on individuals is convenient for producers precisely because it moves attention away from the decisions that matter.\n\nManufacturers and retailers hold the real levers. They decide the material, the size and whether a product needs a tray inside a box inside a wrapper, and a single change made centrally removes millions of items at once. They also profit from packaging, which sells the product and extends its life on the shelf, so the cost of disposal falls on somebody who never chose it.\n\nIn conclusion, shoppers should use the influence they have, and their preferences do shift markets. Real reduction requires producers to redesign packaging and governments to make them pay for what they create, because only the producer can change what is on the shelf.","phrases":[{"en":"depends on information the shopper does not have","context":"The case against relying on shoppers is stronger. Choice is largely an illusion in a supermarket, because a customer who wants unwrapped rice may find every brand in plastic, and avoiding packaging then means going without the product. Poorer households in particular cannot pay the premium that unpackaged goods usually carry. Consumers also cannot judge what they are buying, since materials are unlabelled and much of what appears recyclable is not, so the choice depends on information the shopper does not have. Placing the duty on individuals is convenient for producers precisely because it moves attention away from the decisions that matter."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#189","summary":"Whether producers or shoppers are responsible for cutting packaging waste"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2341","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Pollution from transport: whose job?","prompt":"Research has shown that the transport of goods and people is a main source of pollution. Some people believe governments should deal with this, while others believe it is the responsibility of individuals. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Moving people and freight now produces a substantial share of global emissions, and the argument about who should fix it repeats every time fuel prices rise. Both parties have a role, but the decisive one belongs to government.\n\nThe case for individual responsibility is that the journeys are ours. Nobody forces a person to drive a large car to a shop half a mile away, to fly for a weekend break or to order goods separately for next-day delivery. Millions of such decisions add up, and if enough people cycled, combined their trips or flew less, emissions would fall without any law being passed. Consumer preference also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moves companies faster than legislation does</strong>, as the demand for electric cars has shown.\n\nThe limits of that view appear quickly, however. Individual choice depends on what exists: a family cannot take a train where the line was closed, and cannot cycle where the road is dangerous. Freight, which produces much of the pollution, is not chosen by consumers at all. There is also a fairness problem, because the person who gives up a car pays a real cost while everybody else enjoys the cleaner air, so acting alone is a sacrifice rather than a solution.\n\nGovernments hold the tools that change the underlying conditions: fuel and emissions standards, investment in railways and cycle routes, planning rules that stop cities sprawling, and taxes that make polluting transport reflect its true cost. One decision on vehicle standards achieves what millions of careful drivers could not.\n\nIn conclusion, individuals should travel more thoughtfully, and public opinion is what makes difficult policies possible. Responsibility rests mainly with governments, because only they can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build the alternatives that individual choices depend on</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"moves companies faster than legislation does","context":"The case for individual responsibility is that the journeys are ours. Nobody forces a person to drive a large car to a shop half a mile away, to fly for a weekend break or to order goods separately for next-day delivery. Millions of such decisions add up, and if enough people cycled, combined their trips or flew less, emissions would fall without any law being passed. Consumer preference also moves companies faster than legislation does, as the demand for electric cars has shown."},{"en":"build the alternatives that individual choices depend on","context":"In conclusion, individuals should travel more thoughtfully, and public opinion is what makes difficult policies possible. Responsibility rests mainly with governments, because only they can build the alternatives that individual choices depend on."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#190","summary":"Whether governments or individuals should tackle pollution from moving goods and people"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2348","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"Acting together on fossil fuels","prompt":"The international community must take immediate action to ensure that all nations reduce their consumption of fossil fuels such as gas and oil. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Coal, oil and gas still supply most of the world&#x27;s energy, and the emissions from burning them are the principal cause of a warming climate. I agree that immediate international action is necessary, but the word &quot;all&quot; needs qualifying if the effort is to succeed.\n\nThe argument for urgency rests on how the atmosphere works. Carbon dioxide remains for centuries, so today&#x27;s emissions determine conditions long after the power stations have closed, and delay is not neutral: each year of high output makes the eventual reduction steeper and more expensive. The damage is already visible in heatwaves, failed harvests and flooding, and the cost of acting later <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rises faster than the cost of acting now</strong>.\n\nCooperation is essential because the problem ignores borders. A country that reduces its emissions alone gains a fraction of the benefit and carries the whole cost, while its industries move to wherever rules are loosest. Only an international agreement removes that incentive, and only shared finance and technology make the transition possible for countries that cannot pay for it. Agreements also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give companies the certainty they need to invest</strong> in something other than oil.\n\nThe qualification matters, however. Demanding identical cuts from every nation would be neither fair nor effective, since wealthy countries produced most of the accumulated emissions and can afford to move fastest, while a poor country using fossil fuel to supply electricity for the first time is in a different position. Insisting otherwise <strong class=\"key-phrase\">guarantees that no agreement is signed at all</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, immediate and coordinated action on fossil fuels is justified, because the cost of waiting grows and no country can solve this alone. Reductions have to be shared according to wealth and past emissions, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unfair agreement will not be kept</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"rises faster than the cost of acting now","context":"The argument for urgency rests on how the atmosphere works. Carbon dioxide remains for centuries, so today's emissions determine conditions long after the power stations have closed, and delay is not neutral: each year of high output makes the eventual reduction steeper and more expensive. The damage is already visible in heatwaves, failed harvests and flooding, and the cost of acting later rises faster than the cost of acting now."},{"en":"give companies the certainty they need to invest","context":"Cooperation is essential because the problem ignores borders. A country that reduces its emissions alone gains a fraction of the benefit and carries the whole cost, while its industries move to wherever rules are loosest. Only an international agreement removes that incentive, and only shared finance and technology make the transition possible for countries that cannot pay for it. Agreements also give companies the certainty they need to invest in something other than oil."},{"en":"guarantees that no agreement is signed at all","context":"The qualification matters, however. Demanding identical cuts from every nation would be neither fair nor effective, since wealthy countries produced most of the accumulated emissions and can afford to move fastest, while a poor country using fossil fuel to supply electricity for the first time is in a different position. Insisting otherwise guarantees that no agreement is signed at all."},{"en":"an unfair agreement will not be kept","context":"In conclusion, immediate and coordinated action on fossil fuels is justified, because the cost of waiting grows and no country can solve this alone. Reductions have to be shared according to wealth and past emissions, since an unfair agreement will not be kept."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#191","summary":"Whether the world must act immediately to cut fossil fuel use"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2355","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"The shift to wind and solar power","prompt":"While fossil fuels are still the main source of energy for most countries, others have begun to rely on renewable options such as wind and solar power. Is this a positive or negative trend?","answer":"Wind and solar installations have grown from a curiosity to the cheapest new source of electricity in barely two decades. I regard the trend as clearly positive, although the transition brings difficulties that deserve honest treatment.\n\nThe environmental gain is the obvious one. Generating power without burning anything removes both the carbon dioxide driving climate change and the particles that cause respiratory disease in cities, and the health benefit alone justifies the change in many countries. There is an economic argument as well: sunlight and wind are free once the equipment is built, so electricity costs stop rising and falling with the price of imported fuel. That also matters politically, since a country generating its own power <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be pressured by whoever supplies its gas</strong>. Employment follows the investment, and renewable projects now support more workers than coal in several economies.\n\nThe problems are practical rather than fundamental. Wind and sunshine vary, so a grid depending on them needs storage and connections across regions, and building both takes time and money. Manufacturing turbines and batteries requires mining, which carries its own environmental and human cost, and the panels will eventually need recycling. Communities built around coal also lose their industry, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a transition that abandons those towns</strong> deservedly loses public support.\n\nNone of this outweighs the alternative. Fossil fuels carry far larger costs that are simply paid elsewhere, in hospital admissions and in damage from floods and heat, and their price is set by markets no importing country controls.\n\nIn conclusion, moving to wind and solar power lowers emissions, stabilises energy prices and reduces dependence on imports. It is a positive trend provided governments invest in storage and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">plan properly for the workers it displaces</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"cannot be pressured by whoever supplies its gas","context":"The environmental gain is the obvious one. Generating power without burning anything removes both the carbon dioxide driving climate change and the particles that cause respiratory disease in cities, and the health benefit alone justifies the change in many countries. There is an economic argument as well: sunlight and wind are free once the equipment is built, so electricity costs stop rising and falling with the price of imported fuel. That also matters politically, since a country generating its own power cannot be pressured by whoever supplies its gas. Employment follows the investment, and renewable projects now support more workers than coal in several economies."},{"en":"a transition that abandons those towns","context":"The problems are practical rather than fundamental. Wind and sunshine vary, so a grid depending on them needs storage and connections across regions, and building both takes time and money. Manufacturing turbines and batteries requires mining, which carries its own environmental and human cost, and the panels will eventually need recycling. Communities built around coal also lose their industry, and a transition that abandons those towns deservedly loses public support."},{"en":"plan properly for the workers it displaces","context":"In conclusion, moving to wind and solar power lowers emissions, stabilises energy prices and reduces dependence on imports. It is a positive trend provided governments invest in storage and plan properly for the workers it displaces."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#192","summary":"Whether the move from fossil fuels to renewable energy is a positive trend"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2358","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Environment","title":"A global body for the environment?","prompt":"Some people believe that tackling environmental problems should be managed by a global organisation instead of by national governments individually. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Pollution and climate change ignore borders, which makes managing them through separate national policies look inefficient. I partly agree that a global body is needed, but it should coordinate and enforce rather than replace national governments.\n\nThe argument for central management is strong where the problem is genuinely shared. Emissions released anywhere affect everyone, so a country acting alone bears the full cost and receives a fraction of the benefit, which is why so many governments promise action and deliver little. A global authority could set binding limits, prevent industries from relocating to wherever rules are weakest, and stop each country waiting for the others to move first. Oceans and the atmosphere belong to nobody in particular, so only an international body can defend them, and problems such as overfishing show what happens when nothing does.\n\nThe objection is that such an organisation would have no way of acting. Environmental measures require changing taxes, planning law, farming and energy supply, all of which depend on domestic institutions that no international body possesses. Countries also differ enormously in what they can afford and in what caused the damage, so uniform rules would be unjust as well as unworkable. Any authority strong enough to overrule governments would also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lack the accountability that elections provide</strong>, and states would simply refuse to join.\n\nThe workable arrangement is division of labour. An international body should set targets, verify emissions independently, distribute finance to poorer countries and impose consequences for breaking agreements, while national governments choose how to meet the targets in their own conditions. That is roughly what existing agreements attempt, and their weakness is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the absence of any penalty for failure</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, environmental problems are international and require an international institution with real enforcement powers. Delivery must remain national, because only governments can change what happens on the ground.","phrases":[{"en":"lack the accountability that elections provide","context":"The objection is that such an organisation would have no way of acting. Environmental measures require changing taxes, planning law, farming and energy supply, all of which depend on domestic institutions that no international body possesses. Countries also differ enormously in what they can afford and in what caused the damage, so uniform rules would be unjust as well as unworkable. Any authority strong enough to overrule governments would also lack the accountability that elections provide, and states would simply refuse to join."},{"en":"the absence of any penalty for failure","context":"The workable arrangement is division of labour. An international body should set targets, verify emissions independently, distribute finance to poorer countries and impose consequences for breaking agreements, while national governments choose how to meet the targets in their own conditions. That is roughly what existing agreements attempt, and their weakness is the absence of any penalty for failure."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#193","summary":"Whether one international organisation should handle environmental problems"},{"id":"writing-task-2-030","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Laws requiring equal numbers of men and women","prompt":"‘We should introduce laws to make businesses and state services employ equal numbers of male and female workers in every department or area of the company.’ How far do you support this idea? Give reasons for your answer, and support your essay with ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"The issue of equality for men and women at work is still widely debated, despite the extensive legislation that now exists in many countries. Personally, I do not feel that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imposing a numerical target</strong> for gender is a sensible idea, and I will explain why.\n\nFirstly, we have to consider whether equal numbers of men and women really wish to do every job which is available. For example, few women wish to be airline pilots, and only a minority of school teachers are male. If we impose a law on airlines or schools, where will they find the people to fill these posts willingly?\n\nThere is also the huge issue of the existing workforce. Would it be right to dismiss a worker because he or she is of a certain gender, in order to create a statistically perfect balance? The effects would be disastrous for many hard-working people.\n\nFinally, I believe we should find ways to encourage equality of opportunity through support and guidance, rather than top-down legislation. For instance, improved child care facilities and subsidies would probably encourage more women into parts of the workforce which may currently be difficult to enter. I do understand the opposing view, which is that legislation is the quickest and most effective way to achieve gender equality.\n\nNevertheless, I do not believe that a fairer workplace can be created by a change imposed overnight, at the cost of the jobs and prospects of people who are already doing the work. Overall, equality of opportunity is a goal worth pursuing, but it is reached by removing the obstacles that keep women out of certain careers, not by fixing the numbers in advance.","phrases":[{"en":"imposing a numerical target","context":"The issue of equality for men and women at work is still widely debated, despite the extensive legislation that now exists in many countries. Personally, I do not feel that imposing a numerical target for gender is a sensible idea, and I will explain why."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#194","summary":"‘We should introduce laws to make businesses and state services employ…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-096","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Should governments cap the highest salaries?","prompt":"In many countries, a small number of people earn extremely high salaries. Some people believe that this is good for the country, but others think that governments should not allow salaries above a certain level. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about whether governments should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">introduce a maximum wage</strong>. While in some ways it may seem reasonable to allow people to earn as much as companies are willing to pay, I personally believe that employee remuneration should be capped at a certain level.\n\nThere are various reasons why it might be considered beneficial to allow people to be paid extremely high salaries. If companies offer excellent pay packages, they can attract the most talented people in their fields to work for them. For example, technology companies like Google are able to employ the best programmers because of the huge sums that they are willing to pay. These well-paid employees are also likely to be highly motivated to work hard and therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drive their businesses successfully</strong>. In theory, this should result in a thriving economy and increased tax revenues, which means that paying high salaries benefits everyone.\n\nHowever, I agree with those who argue that there should be a maximum wage. By introducing a limit on earnings, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the pay-gap between bosses and employees</strong> can be reduced. Currently, the difference between normal and top salaries is huge, and this can demotivate workers who feel that the situation is unfair. With lower executive salaries, it might become feasible to introduce higher minimum wages, and everybody would be better off. One possible consequence of greater equality could be that poverty and crime rates fall because the general population will experience an improved standard of living.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that it would be better, on balance, for governments to set a limit on the wages of the highest earners in society.","phrases":[{"en":"introduce a maximum wage","context":"People have different views about whether governments should introduce a maximum wage. While in some ways it may seem reasonable to allow people to earn as much as companies are willing to pay, I personally believe that employee remuneration should be capped at a certain level."},{"en":"drive their businesses successfully","context":"There are various reasons why it might be considered beneficial to allow people to be paid extremely high salaries. If companies offer excellent pay packages, they can attract the most talented people in their fields to work for them. For example, technology companies like Google are able to employ the best programmers because of the huge sums that they are willing to pay. These well-paid employees are also likely to be highly motivated to work hard and therefore drive their businesses successfully. In theory, this should result in a thriving economy and increased tax revenues, which means that paying high salaries benefits everyone."},{"en":"the pay-gap between bosses and employees","context":"However, I agree with those who argue that there should be a maximum wage. By introducing a limit on earnings, the pay-gap between bosses and employees can be reduced. Currently, the difference between normal and top salaries is huge, and this can demotivate workers who feel that the situation is unfair. With lower executive salaries, it might become feasible to introduce higher minimum wages, and everybody would be better off. One possible consequence of greater equality could be that poverty and crime rates fall because the general population will experience an improved standard of living."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#195","summary":"Capping extremely high salaries"},{"id":"writing-task-2-097","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Equality and personal achievement","prompt":"In recent years, there has been growing interest in the relationship between equality and personal achievement. Some people believe that individuals can achieve more in egalitarian societies. Others believe that high levels of personal achievement are possible only if individuals are free to succeed or fail according to their individual merits. What is your view of the relationship between equality and personal success?","answer":"In my opinion, an egalitarian society is one in which everyone has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the same rights and the same opportunities</strong>. I completely agree that people can achieve more in this kind of society.\n\nEducation is an important factor with regard to personal success in life. I believe that all children should have access to free schooling, and higher education should be either free or affordable for all those who choose to pursue a university degree. Consider two students of equal ability: one whose parents can pay for tutoring, textbooks and a debt-free degree, and another whose family cannot afford any of this. In a society without free schooling or affordable higher education, it is the first student, not the more talented one, who would be better prepared for the job market. This kind of inequality would ensure the success of some but harm the prospects of others, for reasons that have nothing to do with ability.\n\nI would argue that equal rights and opportunities are not in conflict with people&#x27;s freedom to succeed or fail. In other words, equality does not mean that people lose their motivation to succeed, or that they are not allowed to fail. On the contrary, I believe that most people would feel more motivated to work hard and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reach their potential</strong> if they thought that they lived in a fair society. Those who did not make the same effort would know that they had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wasted their opportunity</strong>. Inequality, on the other hand, would be more likely to demotivate people because they would know that the odds of success were stacked in favour of those from privileged backgrounds.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that there is a positive relationship between equality and personal success.","phrases":[{"en":"the same rights and the same opportunities","context":"In my opinion, an egalitarian society is one in which everyone has the same rights and the same opportunities. I completely agree that people can achieve more in this kind of society."},{"en":"reach their potential","context":"I would argue that equal rights and opportunities are not in conflict with people's freedom to succeed or fail. In other words, equality does not mean that people lose their motivation to succeed, or that they are not allowed to fail. On the contrary, I believe that most people would feel more motivated to work hard and reach their potential if they thought that they lived in a fair society. Those who did not make the same effort would know that they had wasted their opportunity. Inequality, on the other hand, would be more likely to demotivate people because they would know that the odds of success were stacked in favour of those from privileged backgrounds."},{"en":"wasted their opportunity","context":"I would argue that equal rights and opportunities are not in conflict with people's freedom to succeed or fail. In other words, equality does not mean that people lose their motivation to succeed, or that they are not allowed to fail. On the contrary, I believe that most people would feel more motivated to work hard and reach their potential if they thought that they lived in a fair society. Those who did not make the same effort would know that they had wasted their opportunity. Inequality, on the other hand, would be more likely to demotivate people because they would know that the odds of success were stacked in favour of those from privileged backgrounds."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#196","summary":"Equality and personal achievement"},{"id":"writing-task-2-159","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Would women leaders make the world more peaceful?","prompt":"Throughout the history, male leaders often made the society more violent and conflicting. If women governed the world, the world would be more peaceful. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The claim that female leaders would automatically make the world more peaceful is attractive but too simple. Gender may influence personal experience and priorities, yet gender alone is a poor predictor of political behaviour. Decisions about war and peace are shaped more strongly by institutions, national interests and the circumstances a leader faces.\n\nSupporters of the claim argue that women are generally more cooperative and less aggressive than men. They may also bring greater attention to welfare, family security and the human cost of conflict. Broader representation can improve debate because a government that includes people with different experiences is less likely to rely on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a narrow set of assumptions</strong>. For this reason, increasing the number of women in leadership is valuable in its own right.\n\nHowever, a head of government does not act solely according to personality. Leaders operate under <strong class=\"key-phrase\">institutional constraints</strong>, military advice, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">electoral incentives</strong> and international pressure. A female leader may still authorise decisive action when national security is threatened, just as a male leader may choose negotiation and restraint. History contains examples of both patterns. It is therefore unsafe to treat <strong class=\"key-phrase\">peacefulness as a fixed female characteristic</strong> or violence as a fixed male one.\n\nThe better way to reduce conflict is to strengthen accountable institutions. Transparent decision-making, effective diplomacy, independent media and legislative oversight make it harder for any leader to start an unnecessary war. Diverse leadership can support these safeguards, but it cannot replace them.\n\nIn conclusion, the world would benefit from more women in positions of power because representation should be fair and decision-making should include wider perspectives. Nevertheless, peace depends primarily on political systems and incentives, not on the sex of the individual leader.","phrases":[{"en":"a narrow set of assumptions","context":"Supporters of the claim argue that women are generally more cooperative and less aggressive than men. They may also bring greater attention to welfare, family security and the human cost of conflict. Broader representation can improve debate because a government that includes people with different experiences is less likely to rely on a narrow set of assumptions. For this reason, increasing the number of women in leadership is valuable in its own right."},{"en":"institutional constraints","context":"However, a head of government does not act solely according to personality. Leaders operate under institutional constraints, military advice, electoral incentives and international pressure. A female leader may still authorise decisive action when national security is threatened, just as a male leader may choose negotiation and restraint. History contains examples of both patterns. It is therefore unsafe to treat peacefulness as a fixed female characteristic or violence as a fixed male one."},{"en":"electoral incentives","context":"However, a head of government does not act solely according to personality. Leaders operate under institutional constraints, military advice, electoral incentives and international pressure. A female leader may still authorise decisive action when national security is threatened, just as a male leader may choose negotiation and restraint. History contains examples of both patterns. It is therefore unsafe to treat peacefulness as a fixed female characteristic or violence as a fixed male one."},{"en":"peacefulness as a fixed female characteristic","context":"However, a head of government does not act solely according to personality. Leaders operate under institutional constraints, military advice, electoral incentives and international pressure. A female leader may still authorise decisive action when national security is threatened, just as a male leader may choose negotiation and restraint. History contains examples of both patterns. It is therefore unsafe to treat peacefulness as a fixed female characteristic or violence as a fixed male one."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#197","summary":"Throughout the history, male leaders often made the society more violent…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-163","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Women in the army","prompt":"In many countries, women join the army as men do. However, some people argue that the army solely needs males. What is your opinion?","answer":"In the 21st century, women have entered nearly every walk of life. However, the army is still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a male-dominated area</strong> in much of the world. A great many people hold biases against women&#x27;s enrolment in the army. In my view, women can contribute to the army just as men do, even though they face obstacles that men do not.\n\nThe first argument for recruiting women is that they now have far greater access to education. In comparison with the past, when recruitment concentrated on strength and fitness, armies today select heavily on technical and academic ability. Hand-to-hand fighting accounts for only a small part of modern warfare, which is dominated by tanks, armoured vehicles, missiles and aircraft, most of them computerised. In other words, women can perform military tasks as their male counterparts do, provided that they are well-educated and well-trained.\n\nSecondly, women bring qualities that modern military organisations actually need. Much of service life consists of intelligence analysis, logistics, medical support and communications, roles that reward patience and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meticulous attention to detail</strong> rather than physical force. Mixed units are also more effective in conflicts fought among civilians: in Afghanistan, armies deployed female engagement teams because male soldiers could not speak to local women without causing serious offence.\n\nThese strengths do not remove the obstacles that women in uniform still face. The first is social attitudes. Many women have to cope with pressure from their families and friends, because soldiering is still widely seen as a dangerous occupation that is suitable only for men. The second is discrimination and harassment, which remains common in most armies and discourages capable recruits from staying long enough to reach senior rank.\n\nIn conclusion, the presence of women in the army should be accepted and encouraged. Modern soldiering rewards the education and precision that women bring to it, and the prejudice they still meet is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an argument for reforming military culture</strong>, not for excluding them.","phrases":[{"en":"a male-dominated area","context":"In the 21st century, women have entered nearly every walk of life. However, the army is still a male-dominated area in much of the world. A great many people hold biases against women's enrolment in the army. In my view, women can contribute to the army just as men do, even though they face obstacles that men do not."},{"en":"meticulous attention to detail","context":"Secondly, women bring qualities that modern military organisations actually need. Much of service life consists of intelligence analysis, logistics, medical support and communications, roles that reward patience and meticulous attention to detail rather than physical force. Mixed units are also more effective in conflicts fought among civilians: in Afghanistan, armies deployed female engagement teams because male soldiers could not speak to local women without causing serious offence."},{"en":"an argument for reforming military culture","context":"In conclusion, the presence of women in the army should be accepted and encouraged. Modern soldiering rewards the education and precision that women bring to it, and the prejudice they still meet is an argument for reforming military culture, not for excluding them."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#198","summary":"Women join the army as men do."},{"id":"writing-task-2-2370","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Equality","title":"Would a smaller income gap make us happier?","prompt":"Many believe that the best way to ensure a happier society is to reduce the difference in income between the rich and the poor. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Countries with similar levels of wealth report very different levels of satisfaction, and the distribution of income is one of the clearest things separating them. I largely agree that narrowing the gap raises happiness, though calling it the best single measure claims too much.\n\nThe evidence for the connection is substantial. Beyond the point where basic needs are met, additional income adds little to wellbeing, but relative position continues to matter, because people judge their lives by comparison with those around them. Wide gaps therefore produce dissatisfaction at every level except the top. More equal societies also record better health, lower crime and higher social trust, since people are more willing to contribute where they feel treated fairly. Practically, money moved from the wealthy to the poor buys more happiness, as the same sum means far more to a household struggling with rent.\n\nThere are limits to the argument, however. Equality achieved by making everyone poorer helps nobody, and countries that suppressed differences entirely produced neither prosperity nor contentment. What people object to is usually unfairness rather than difference: a gap earned through effort is accepted, while one inherited or extracted is resented. Other factors also weigh heavily, since employment, health, safety and the quality of relationships shape happiness at least as much as the income distribution does.\n\nThe most effective approach is therefore indirect. Rather than redistributing income alone, governments raise satisfaction by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">providing services that everyone uses regardless of income</strong> — schools, health care, transport — which reduces the practical consequences of the gap while allowing differences in earnings to remain.\n\nIn conclusion, reducing the distance between rich and poor genuinely improves health, trust and satisfaction, so the belief is well founded. It works best when it comes from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fair opportunity and strong public services</strong> rather than from levelling incomes for its own sake.","phrases":[{"en":"providing services that everyone uses regardless of income","context":"The most effective approach is therefore indirect. Rather than redistributing income alone, governments raise satisfaction by providing services that everyone uses regardless of income — schools, health care, transport — which reduces the practical consequences of the gap while allowing differences in earnings to remain."},{"en":"fair opportunity and strong public services","context":"In conclusion, reducing the distance between rich and poor genuinely improves health, trust and satisfaction, so the belief is well founded. It works best when it comes from fair opportunity and strong public services rather than from levelling incomes for its own sake."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#199","summary":"Whether reducing the gap between rich and poor is the route to a happier society"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Does fame bring more benefits than problems?","prompt":"Being a celebrity, such as a famous film star or sports personality, brings problems as well as benefits. Do you think that being a celebrity brings more benefits or more problems?","answer":"Celebrity brings wealth, influence and unusual opportunities, but it also removes much of a person&#x27;s privacy. In my view, the benefits can be substantial, yet fame is only positive when the individual has strong support and realistic boundaries.\n\nA successful actor or athlete may earn a high income, travel widely and work on projects that would otherwise be impossible. Public recognition can also give celebrities a platform to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support charities or raise awareness</strong> of important issues. In career terms, fame can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open doors that talent alone may not</strong>.\n\nThe disadvantages are more personal. Famous people are photographed, judged and discussed by strangers, and ordinary mistakes can become public scandals. Their families may also face unwanted attention. Constant pressure to maintain an image can damage mental health, while it becomes difficult to know whether new relationships are genuine. High earnings do not automatically <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compensate for</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the loss of a private life</strong>.\n\nWhat makes the difference is whether the fame rests on the work or on the person. An actor or athlete is judged mainly by performance and can withdraw between films or seasons, so their private life remains largely their own. Someone who is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">famous for being famous</strong> must keep supplying personal material in order to stay visible, which is why reality television and social media have produced so many people who cannot step back without disappearing altogether.\n\nIn conclusion, being a celebrity offers exceptional rewards but also serious costs. I would not describe it as clearly beneficial or harmful. The experience depends on whether the person can control access to their life, manage public pressure and maintain relationships outside the entertainment or sports industry.","topic":"Famous People","phrases":[{"en":"support charities or raise awareness","context":"A successful actor or athlete may earn a high income, travel widely and work on projects that would otherwise be impossible. Public recognition can also give celebrities a platform to support charities or raise awareness of important issues. In career terms, fame can open doors that talent alone may not."},{"en":"open doors that talent alone may not","context":"A successful actor or athlete may earn a high income, travel widely and work on projects that would otherwise be impossible. Public recognition can also give celebrities a platform to support charities or raise awareness of important issues. In career terms, fame can open doors that talent alone may not."},{"en":"compensate for","context":"The disadvantages are more personal. Famous people are photographed, judged and discussed by strangers, and ordinary mistakes can become public scandals. Their families may also face unwanted attention. Constant pressure to maintain an image can damage mental health, while it becomes difficult to know whether new relationships are genuine. High earnings do not automatically compensate for the loss of a private life."},{"en":"the loss of a private life","context":"The disadvantages are more personal. Famous people are photographed, judged and discussed by strangers, and ordinary mistakes can become public scandals. Their families may also face unwanted attention. Constant pressure to maintain an image can damage mental health, while it becomes difficult to know whether new relationships are genuine. High earnings do not automatically compensate for the loss of a private life."},{"en":"famous for being famous","context":"What makes the difference is whether the fame rests on the work or on the person. An actor or athlete is judged mainly by performance and can withdraw between films or seasons, so their private life remains largely their own. Someone who is famous for being famous must keep supplying personal material in order to stay visible, which is why reality television and social media have produced so many people who cannot step back without disappearing altogether."}],"subtype":"two-part","summary":"Whether being a celebrity brings more benefits than problems","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-123","_displayNo":"#200"},{"id":"writing-task-2-134","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Famous People","title":"Famous for glamour rather than achievement","prompt":"Nowadays celebrities are more famous for their glamour and wealth than for their achievements, and this sets a bad example to young people. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?","answer":"It is true that some celebrities are known for their glamorous lifestyles rather than for the work they do. While I agree that these celebrities set a bad example for children, I believe that other famous people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">act as positive role models</strong>.\n\nOn the one hand, many people do achieve fame without really working for it. They may have inherited money from parents, married a famous or wealthy person, or they may have appeared in gossip magazines or on a reality TV programme. A good example would be Paris Hilton, who is rich and famous for the wrong reasons. She spends her time attending parties and nightclubs, and her behaviour promotes the idea that appearance, glamour and media profile are more important than hard work and good character. The message to young people is that success can be achieved easily, and that school work is not necessary.\n\nOn the other hand, there are at least as many celebrities whose accomplishments make them excellent role models for young people. Actors, musicians and sports stars become famous idols because they have worked hard and applied themselves to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">develop real skills and abilities</strong>. They demonstrate great effort, determination and ambition, which is required for someone who wants to be truly successful in their chosen field. An example is the actor and martial artist Jackie Chan, who has become world famous through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">years of practice and hard work</strong>. This kind of self-made celebrity can inspire children to develop their talents through application and perseverance.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that the influence of celebrities on young people can be positive as well as negative.","phrases":[{"en":"act as positive role models","context":"It is true that some celebrities are known for their glamorous lifestyles rather than for the work they do. While I agree that these celebrities set a bad example for children, I believe that other famous people act as positive role models."},{"en":"develop real skills and abilities","context":"On the other hand, there are at least as many celebrities whose accomplishments make them excellent role models for young people. Actors, musicians and sports stars become famous idols because they have worked hard and applied themselves to develop real skills and abilities. They demonstrate great effort, determination and ambition, which is required for someone who wants to be truly successful in their chosen field. An example is the actor and martial artist Jackie Chan, who has become world famous through years of practice and hard work. This kind of self-made celebrity can inspire children to develop their talents through application and perseverance."},{"en":"years of practice and hard work","context":"On the other hand, there are at least as many celebrities whose accomplishments make them excellent role models for young people. Actors, musicians and sports stars become famous idols because they have worked hard and applied themselves to develop real skills and abilities. They demonstrate great effort, determination and ambition, which is required for someone who wants to be truly successful in their chosen field. An example is the actor and martial artist Jackie Chan, who has become world famous through years of practice and hard work. This kind of self-made celebrity can inspire children to develop their talents through application and perseverance."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#201","summary":"Celebrity influence on young people"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2207","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Famous People","title":"Celebrities and media intrusion","prompt":"In some countries, celebrities complain about the way the media publicizes their private lives. Some people say that they should accept it as part of their fame. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Public figures frequently complain that newspapers and websites report on their private lives, while others reply that this is simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the price of being famous</strong>. I disagree with the second view, because fame does not remove a person&#x27;s basic right to privacy.\n\nThose who defend media intrusion argue that celebrities have chosen public life and benefit enormously from it. Actors and musicians rely on publicity to sell films and concert tickets, and many of them actively invite attention by discussing their relationships in interviews or posting details of their homes online. On this argument, it is inconsistent to welcome coverage when it is flattering and object to it when it is not.\n\nI find this reasoning unconvincing. Accepting a job in the public eye is not the same as consenting to be photographed inside one&#x27;s own house or followed while taking children to school. A footballer is paid to play football and an actor to act; neither has agreed to surrender family life. The consequences are also serious, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">constant surveillance</strong> has contributed to depression and breakdown among performers, and the children of famous people have made no such choice at all.\n\nThere is, however, an important exception. Where a politician or a public official behaves dishonestly, and where that behaviour affects how they carry out their duties, reporting it is legitimate journalism rather than intrusion. The proper test is whether a story <strong class=\"key-phrase\">serves the public interest</strong>, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not whether the public happens to be interested</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that celebrities must simply accept media intrusion. Private life should be reported only where there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine public interest</strong> in doing so.","phrases":[{"en":"the price of being famous","context":"Public figures frequently complain that newspapers and websites report on their private lives, while others reply that this is simply the price of being famous. I disagree with the second view, because fame does not remove a person's basic right to privacy."},{"en":"constant surveillance","context":"I find this reasoning unconvincing. Accepting a job in the public eye is not the same as consenting to be photographed inside one's own house or followed while taking children to school. A footballer is paid to play football and an actor to act; neither has agreed to surrender family life. The consequences are also serious, since constant surveillance has contributed to depression and breakdown among performers, and the children of famous people have made no such choice at all."},{"en":"serves the public interest","context":"There is, however, an important exception. Where a politician or a public official behaves dishonestly, and where that behaviour affects how they carry out their duties, reporting it is legitimate journalism rather than intrusion. The proper test is whether a story serves the public interest, not whether the public happens to be interested."},{"en":"not whether the public happens to be interested","context":"There is, however, an important exception. Where a politician or a public official behaves dishonestly, and where that behaviour affects how they carry out their duties, reporting it is legitimate journalism rather than intrusion. The proper test is whether a story serves the public interest, not whether the public happens to be interested."},{"en":"a genuine public interest","context":"In conclusion, I disagree that celebrities must simply accept media intrusion. Private life should be reported only where there is a genuine public interest in doing so."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#202","summary":"Celebrities and media intrusion"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2364","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Famous People","title":"Pop stars instead of national heroes","prompt":"Many young people today know more about international pop and film stars than about famous people in the history of their own country. Why is this? What can be done to increase young people's interest in their national history?","answer":"A teenager who can name a dozen foreign performers may struggle to identify the figures on their own currency. The reasons lie in how information now reaches them, and the remedy is to make history as vivid as the alternative rather than to make it compulsory.\n\nExposure is the main cause. Entertainment is delivered continuously through phones, and the industry producing it is global, well funded and designed to hold attention, so a young person encounters the same faces hundreds of times a week. Celebrities are also present, changing and easy to follow, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a historical figure never posts anything new</strong>. English-language culture dominates the platforms most young people use, and that shifts attention outwards by default.\n\nTeaching plays a part too. History in many schools is delivered as dates and treaties to be memorised for an examination, which is the surest way to make an interesting subject dull. National figures are frequently presented as faultless statues, and adolescents distrust anything presented that way. Families have also stopped passing stories down as they once did, since grandparents live further away.\n\nSeveral measures would help. History taught as argument rather than fact — why a decision was made, what the alternatives were, who suffered — engages students who would never memorise a list. Local history works particularly well, because a child who learns what happened in their own street finds the subject <strong class=\"key-phrase\">attached to somewhere they already know</strong>. Museums, restored sites and school visits do more than any textbook. Beyond the classroom, well-made national drama and documentary reach young audiences on the platforms they actually use, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competing for attention rather than demanding it</strong> is the only realistic approach.\n\nIn conclusion, foreign celebrities dominate because they are marketed constantly while history is taught as examination material. Livelier teaching, local focus and good national storytelling would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give the past a chance to be interesting</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a historical figure never posts anything new","context":"Exposure is the main cause. Entertainment is delivered continuously through phones, and the industry producing it is global, well funded and designed to hold attention, so a young person encounters the same faces hundreds of times a week. Celebrities are also present, changing and easy to follow, while a historical figure never posts anything new. English-language culture dominates the platforms most young people use, and that shifts attention outwards by default."},{"en":"attached to somewhere they already know","context":"Several measures would help. History taught as argument rather than fact — why a decision was made, what the alternatives were, who suffered — engages students who would never memorise a list. Local history works particularly well, because a child who learns what happened in their own street finds the subject attached to somewhere they already know. Museums, restored sites and school visits do more than any textbook. Beyond the classroom, well-made national drama and documentary reach young audiences on the platforms they actually use, and competing for attention rather than demanding it is the only realistic approach."},{"en":"competing for attention rather than demanding it","context":"Several measures would help. History taught as argument rather than fact — why a decision was made, what the alternatives were, who suffered — engages students who would never memorise a list. Local history works particularly well, because a child who learns what happened in their own street finds the subject attached to somewhere they already know. Museums, restored sites and school visits do more than any textbook. Beyond the classroom, well-made national drama and documentary reach young audiences on the platforms they actually use, and competing for attention rather than demanding it is the only realistic approach."},{"en":"give the past a chance to be interesting","context":"In conclusion, foreign celebrities dominate because they are marketed constantly while history is taught as examination material. Livelier teaching, local focus and good national storytelling would give the past a chance to be interesting."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#203","summary":"Why young people know foreign celebrities better than their own history"},{"id":"writing-task-2-048","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Traditional meals replaced by fast food","prompt":"In many countries, traditional home-cooked meals are being replaced by fast food. Why is this happening, and what effects does it have?","answer":"In many countries, meals that were once prepared at home are increasingly bought ready-made from restaurants and takeaways. This change has several practical causes, and in my view its effects are largely negative.\n\nThe principal cause is a shortage of time. In most households every adult now works, often with a long commute, and traditional cooking is slow: preparing a Vietnamese or Italian family meal from raw ingredients can take well over an hour, whereas fast food arrives in minutes. Cost reinforces this. Because international chains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">buy on an enormous scale</strong>, a burger is frequently cheaper than the fresh vegetables, meat and spices required to cook properly, which matters most to low-income families. Advertising is a further factor, since it has made these brands familiar and fashionable among young people.\n\nThe effects on health are the most serious. Fast food is typically high in salt, sugar and saturated fat and low in fibre, and rates of obesity, type 2 diabetes and heart disease have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">risen sharply</strong> in every country where it has become common. There is also a cultural cost. Traditional dishes are passed on by being cooked and shared, so a generation that never learns to prepare them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">will not pass them on</strong> either. The family meal itself is disappearing, and with it one of the few daily occasions when a household sits down together.\n\nIn conclusion, fast food has spread because it is quick, cheap and heavily promoted, and the consequences for public health and for culinary traditions are worrying.","phrases":[{"en":"buy on an enormous scale","context":"The principal cause is a shortage of time. In most households every adult now works, often with a long commute, and traditional cooking is slow: preparing a Vietnamese or Italian family meal from raw ingredients can take well over an hour, whereas fast food arrives in minutes. Cost reinforces this. Because international chains buy on an enormous scale, a burger is frequently cheaper than the fresh vegetables, meat and spices required to cook properly, which matters most to low-income families. Advertising is a further factor, since it has made these brands familiar and fashionable among young people."},{"en":"risen sharply","context":"The effects on health are the most serious. Fast food is typically high in salt, sugar and saturated fat and low in fibre, and rates of obesity, type 2 diabetes and heart disease have risen sharply in every country where it has become common. There is also a cultural cost. Traditional dishes are passed on by being cooked and shared, so a generation that never learns to prepare them will not pass them on either. The family meal itself is disappearing, and with it one of the few daily occasions when a household sits down together."},{"en":"will not pass them on","context":"The effects on health are the most serious. Fast food is typically high in salt, sugar and saturated fat and low in fibre, and rates of obesity, type 2 diabetes and heart disease have risen sharply in every country where it has become common. There is also a cultural cost. Traditional dishes are passed on by being cooked and shared, so a generation that never learns to prepare them will not pass them on either. The family meal itself is disappearing, and with it one of the few daily occasions when a household sits down together."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Effects","_displayNo":"#204","summary":"Traditional meals replaced by fast food"},{"id":"writing-task-2-124","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"The hidden cost of cheap food","prompt":"Food can be produced much more cheaply today because of improved fertilisers and better machinery. However, some of the methods used to achieve this may be dangerous to human health and may have negative effects on local communities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Modern farming has made food dramatically cheaper through chemical fertilisers, pesticides and mechanised production. I agree that these methods carry real risks, both to human health and to the communities in which the food is grown.\n\nThe health concerns are well documented. Pesticide residues remain on fruit and vegetables, and agricultural workers who handle these chemicals directly suffer measurably higher rates of certain cancers and respiratory illness. The routine use of antibiotics in intensive livestock farming is a further danger, since it accelerates the development of resistant bacteria that ordinary medicines can no longer treat. Cheap food is also, on average, less nutritious, because varieties are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">selected for yield and durability in transport</strong> rather than for taste or vitamin content, and much of it is heavily processed before it reaches the shops.\n\nThe effects on local communities are equally serious. Mechanisation means that a farm which once employed fifty people may now need five, and the villages that depended on that work have declined. Chemical fertilisers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">run off into rivers</strong>, damaging fisheries on which other families rely, and repeated intensive cropping <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exhausts the soil</strong>, leaving land <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less productive for the next generation</strong>.\n\nThis does not mean the methods should be abandoned, since they feed a population that traditional farming could not support. It means they need proper regulation: strict limits on chemicals, restrictions on antibiotics and support for farmers who protect their land.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that cheap food carries hidden costs in health and in rural livelihoods. These costs are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rarely reflected in the price</strong>, which is precisely why consumers have so little reason to demand better methods.","phrases":[{"en":"selected for yield and durability in transport","context":"The health concerns are well documented. Pesticide residues remain on fruit and vegetables, and agricultural workers who handle these chemicals directly suffer measurably higher rates of certain cancers and respiratory illness. The routine use of antibiotics in intensive livestock farming is a further danger, since it accelerates the development of resistant bacteria that ordinary medicines can no longer treat. Cheap food is also, on average, less nutritious, because varieties are selected for yield and durability in transport rather than for taste or vitamin content, and much of it is heavily processed before it reaches the shops."},{"en":"run off into rivers","context":"The effects on local communities are equally serious. Mechanisation means that a farm which once employed fifty people may now need five, and the villages that depended on that work have declined. Chemical fertilisers run off into rivers, damaging fisheries on which other families rely, and repeated intensive cropping exhausts the soil, leaving land less productive for the next generation."},{"en":"exhausts the soil","context":"The effects on local communities are equally serious. Mechanisation means that a farm which once employed fifty people may now need five, and the villages that depended on that work have declined. Chemical fertilisers run off into rivers, damaging fisheries on which other families rely, and repeated intensive cropping exhausts the soil, leaving land less productive for the next generation."},{"en":"less productive for the next generation","context":"The effects on local communities are equally serious. Mechanisation means that a farm which once employed fifty people may now need five, and the villages that depended on that work have declined. Chemical fertilisers run off into rivers, damaging fisheries on which other families rely, and repeated intensive cropping exhausts the soil, leaving land less productive for the next generation."},{"en":"rarely reflected in the price","context":"In conclusion, I agree that cheap food carries hidden costs in health and in rural livelihoods. These costs are rarely reflected in the price, which is precisely why consumers have so little reason to demand better methods."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#205","summary":"The hidden cost of cheap food"},{"id":"writing-task-2-127","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Is killing animals for food cruel?","prompt":"Some people think that killing animals for food is cruel, while others claim that animals are a necessary part of the human diet. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people regard the killing of animals for food as fundamentally cruel, while others consider meat a necessary part of the human diet. In my view the objection to cruelty is justified, but it applies to how animals are raised and killed rather than to eating meat itself.\n\nThose who object have a serious case. Most meat now comes from intensive farms where animals spend their lives in confined spaces, unable to move naturally, and are transported long distances before slaughter. Animals of this kind <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demonstrably feel pain and fear</strong>, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">inflicting that on a large scale</strong> for the sake of a cheaper product is difficult to defend. Critics also point out that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the necessity argument is weak in wealthy countries</strong>, where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a varied plant-based diet</strong> is readily available.\n\nThe opposing view rests on biology and culture. Humans have eaten meat throughout their evolution, and it remains the most reliable source of iron, vitamin B12 and complete protein, which matters particularly for children and for people in regions where alternatives are scarce. Livestock also supports the livelihoods of hundreds of millions of small farmers.\n\nMy own position is that killing an animal for food is not in itself wrong, but causing unnecessary suffering is. The practical conclusion is that intensive farming should be reformed rather than that meat should be abandoned.\n\nIn conclusion, I do not accept that eating meat is cruel by definition, but I do believe that the way most meat is currently produced <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be morally justified</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"demonstrably feel pain and fear","context":"Those who object have a serious case. Most meat now comes from intensive farms where animals spend their lives in confined spaces, unable to move naturally, and are transported long distances before slaughter. Animals of this kind demonstrably feel pain and fear, and inflicting that on a large scale for the sake of a cheaper product is difficult to defend. Critics also point out that the necessity argument is weak in wealthy countries, where a varied plant-based diet is readily available."},{"en":"inflicting that on a large scale","context":"Those who object have a serious case. Most meat now comes from intensive farms where animals spend their lives in confined spaces, unable to move naturally, and are transported long distances before slaughter. Animals of this kind demonstrably feel pain and fear, and inflicting that on a large scale for the sake of a cheaper product is difficult to defend. Critics also point out that the necessity argument is weak in wealthy countries, where a varied plant-based diet is readily available."},{"en":"the necessity argument is weak in wealthy countries","context":"Those who object have a serious case. Most meat now comes from intensive farms where animals spend their lives in confined spaces, unable to move naturally, and are transported long distances before slaughter. Animals of this kind demonstrably feel pain and fear, and inflicting that on a large scale for the sake of a cheaper product is difficult to defend. Critics also point out that the necessity argument is weak in wealthy countries, where a varied plant-based diet is readily available."},{"en":"a varied plant-based diet","context":"Those who object have a serious case. Most meat now comes from intensive farms where animals spend their lives in confined spaces, unable to move naturally, and are transported long distances before slaughter. Animals of this kind demonstrably feel pain and fear, and inflicting that on a large scale for the sake of a cheaper product is difficult to defend. Critics also point out that the necessity argument is weak in wealthy countries, where a varied plant-based diet is readily available."},{"en":"cannot be morally justified","context":"In conclusion, I do not accept that eating meat is cruel by definition, but I do believe that the way most meat is currently produced cannot be morally justified."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#206","summary":"Is killing animals for food cruel?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Is a vegetarian diet worth it?","prompt":"In some countries, it is becoming increasingly common for people to adopt a vegetarian diet. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"A vegetarian diet can improve health and reduce environmental pressure, but it requires careful planning. In my view, the advantages generally outweigh the disadvantages for people who have access to a varied diet and reliable nutritional advice.\n\nPlant-based meals are often rich in fibre, fruit and vegetables, and they can contain less saturated fat than diets based heavily on processed meat. Reducing meat consumption may therefore lower the risk of certain chronic diseases. It also decreases demand for land, water and animal feed, which can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce the environmental cost of food production</strong> at a time when agriculture is a major contributor to greenhouse gas emissions.\n\nThe main disadvantage is the possibility of nutritional deficiency. People who remove all animal products need suitable sources of protein, iron, vitamin B12 and other nutrients. This can be difficult for children, pregnant women or low-income households if specialist foods are expensive, and cultural preferences or limited restaurant choices may also make the diet inconvenient. These risks are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manageable rather than decisive</strong>: clear nutritional advice, fortified foods, and restaurants and schools offering affordable, clearly labelled meals can make balanced vegetarian eating <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical rather than restrictive</strong>. The strongest case for vegetarianism is therefore based on a balanced diet rather than the simple removal of meat.\n\nIn conclusion, vegetarianism offers important health and environmental benefits, but it should not be adopted carelessly. Public advice should focus on balanced meals rather than simply telling people to avoid meat. With sensible planning, a vegetarian diet can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">both healthy and sustainable</strong>.","topic":"Food & Diet","phrases":[{"en":"reduce the environmental cost of food production","context":"Plant-based meals are often rich in fibre, fruit and vegetables, and they can contain less saturated fat than diets based heavily on processed meat. Reducing meat consumption may therefore lower the risk of certain chronic diseases. It also decreases demand for land, water and animal feed, which can reduce the environmental cost of food production at a time when agriculture is a major contributor to greenhouse gas emissions."},{"en":"manageable rather than decisive","context":"The main disadvantage is the possibility of nutritional deficiency. People who remove all animal products need suitable sources of protein, iron, vitamin B12 and other nutrients. This can be difficult for children, pregnant women or low-income households if specialist foods are expensive, and cultural preferences or limited restaurant choices may also make the diet inconvenient. These risks are manageable rather than decisive: clear nutritional advice, fortified foods, and restaurants and schools offering affordable, clearly labelled meals can make balanced vegetarian eating practical rather than restrictive. The strongest case for vegetarianism is therefore based on a balanced diet rather than the simple removal of meat."},{"en":"practical rather than restrictive","context":"The main disadvantage is the possibility of nutritional deficiency. People who remove all animal products need suitable sources of protein, iron, vitamin B12 and other nutrients. This can be difficult for children, pregnant women or low-income households if specialist foods are expensive, and cultural preferences or limited restaurant choices may also make the diet inconvenient. These risks are manageable rather than decisive: clear nutritional advice, fortified foods, and restaurants and schools offering affordable, clearly labelled meals can make balanced vegetarian eating practical rather than restrictive. The strongest case for vegetarianism is therefore based on a balanced diet rather than the simple removal of meat."},{"en":"both healthy and sustainable","context":"In conclusion, vegetarianism offers important health and environmental benefits, but it should not be adopted carelessly. Public advice should focus on balanced meals rather than simply telling people to avoid meat. With sensible planning, a vegetarian diet can be both healthy and sustainable."}],"subtype":"outweigh","summary":"Whether the advantages of a vegetarian diet outweigh the disadvantages","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-129","_displayNo":"#207"},{"id":"writing-task-2-181","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Flying fruit and vegetables around the world","prompt":"Aircraft are increasingly used to transport fruit and vegetables to countries where these products are unavailable or out of season. Do the advantages of this development outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Flying fruit and vegetables across long distances gives consumers more choice and provides farmers with access to valuable export markets. However, routine air freight also creates high emissions and may encourage wasteful consumption. In my view, the advantages outweigh the disadvantages only for limited products and circumstances, not as a general food policy.\n\nThe main benefit is a more reliable food supply. Countries with short growing seasons can obtain fresh produce throughout the year, and unusual weather in one region <strong class=\"key-phrase\">does not have to cause an immediate shortage</strong>. Export income can also support farmers in countries whose climate allows them to grow high-value crops efficiently. For consumers, greater variety may improve diet, particularly where certain fruit or vegetables cannot be produced locally.\n\nThe environmental cost is substantial. Aircraft emit far more carbon per tonne of cargo than ships or trains, while refrigeration, packaging and rapid road delivery add further energy use. Air freight can also create the misleading impression that every product should be available in every season. This weakens demand for local crops and may increase food waste when delicate imports spoil before sale. These costs are difficult to justify for ordinary products that could be grown locally or transported more slowly.\n\nA sensible policy would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reserve air transport for urgent shortages</strong>, highly perishable goods or small quantities with clear nutritional or economic value. Retailers should publish transport information and favour sea or rail freight whenever possible. In conclusion, imported produce can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve resilience and consumer choice</strong>, but unrestricted air freight has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an environmental cost that cannot be ignored</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"does not have to cause an immediate shortage","context":"The main benefit is a more reliable food supply. Countries with short growing seasons can obtain fresh produce throughout the year, and unusual weather in one region does not have to cause an immediate shortage. Export income can also support farmers in countries whose climate allows them to grow high-value crops efficiently. For consumers, greater variety may improve diet, particularly where certain fruit or vegetables cannot be produced locally."},{"en":"reserve air transport for urgent shortages","context":"A sensible policy would reserve air transport for urgent shortages, highly perishable goods or small quantities with clear nutritional or economic value. Retailers should publish transport information and favour sea or rail freight whenever possible. In conclusion, imported produce can improve resilience and consumer choice, but unrestricted air freight has an environmental cost that cannot be ignored."},{"en":"improve resilience and consumer choice","context":"A sensible policy would reserve air transport for urgent shortages, highly perishable goods or small quantities with clear nutritional or economic value. Retailers should publish transport information and favour sea or rail freight whenever possible. In conclusion, imported produce can improve resilience and consumer choice, but unrestricted air freight has an environmental cost that cannot be ignored."},{"en":"an environmental cost that cannot be ignored","context":"A sensible policy would reserve air transport for urgent shortages, highly perishable goods or small quantities with clear nutritional or economic value. Retailers should publish transport information and favour sea or rail freight whenever possible. In conclusion, imported produce can improve resilience and consumer choice, but unrestricted air freight has an environmental cost that cannot be ignored."}],"subtype":"Discussion","_displayNo":"#208","summary":"Aircraft have been increasingly used to transport fruit and vegetables to…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2200","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Should everyone be a vegetarian?","prompt":"Some people think everyone should be a vegetarian, because we do not need to eat meat to have a healthy diet. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that people should give up meat altogether, on the grounds that a healthy diet does not require it. While I accept that most of us eat far more meat than we need, I disagree with the idea that everyone should become a vegetarian.\n\nThere are certainly good arguments for eating less meat. Livestock farming uses enormous quantities of land, water and grain, and produces a significant share of global greenhouse gas emissions. There is also strong medical evidence linking high consumption of red and processed meat with heart disease and certain cancers. A diet based mainly on vegetables, grains and pulses is cheaper, and it is perfectly possible to obtain enough protein from beans, nuts and dairy products.\n\nNevertheless, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a universal rule</strong> would be neither practical nor fair. Meat and fish remain the most reliable sources of iron and vitamin B12, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deficiencies are common</strong> among people who remove them without careful planning. This is a particular risk for children, pregnant women and families in poorer countries, where the range of alternative foods is limited. Food is also central to culture, and asking every community to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">abandon dishes that have been prepared for centuries</strong> would be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unreasonable interference</strong> in private life.\n\nIn my opinion, the sensible response is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moderation rather than prohibition</strong>. Governments should educate the public about the health and environmental costs of excessive meat consumption, and make plant-based options widely available, while leaving the final decision to the individual.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that we should all eat less meat, but I disagree that vegetarianism should be required of everyone.","phrases":[{"en":"a universal rule","context":"Nevertheless, a universal rule would be neither practical nor fair. Meat and fish remain the most reliable sources of iron and vitamin B12, and deficiencies are common among people who remove them without careful planning. This is a particular risk for children, pregnant women and families in poorer countries, where the range of alternative foods is limited. Food is also central to culture, and asking every community to abandon dishes that have been prepared for centuries would be an unreasonable interference in private life."},{"en":"deficiencies are common","context":"Nevertheless, a universal rule would be neither practical nor fair. Meat and fish remain the most reliable sources of iron and vitamin B12, and deficiencies are common among people who remove them without careful planning. This is a particular risk for children, pregnant women and families in poorer countries, where the range of alternative foods is limited. Food is also central to culture, and asking every community to abandon dishes that have been prepared for centuries would be an unreasonable interference in private life."},{"en":"abandon dishes that have been prepared for centuries","context":"Nevertheless, a universal rule would be neither practical nor fair. Meat and fish remain the most reliable sources of iron and vitamin B12, and deficiencies are common among people who remove them without careful planning. This is a particular risk for children, pregnant women and families in poorer countries, where the range of alternative foods is limited. Food is also central to culture, and asking every community to abandon dishes that have been prepared for centuries would be an unreasonable interference in private life."},{"en":"an unreasonable interference","context":"Nevertheless, a universal rule would be neither practical nor fair. Meat and fish remain the most reliable sources of iron and vitamin B12, and deficiencies are common among people who remove them without careful planning. This is a particular risk for children, pregnant women and families in poorer countries, where the range of alternative foods is limited. Food is also central to culture, and asking every community to abandon dishes that have been prepared for centuries would be an unreasonable interference in private life."},{"en":"moderation rather than prohibition","context":"In my opinion, the sensible response is moderation rather than prohibition. Governments should educate the public about the health and environmental costs of excessive meat consumption, and make plant-based options widely available, while leaving the final decision to the individual."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#209","summary":"Should everyone be a vegetarian?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2305","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Fast food replacing traditional meals","prompt":"In many countries, traditional foods are being replaced by international fast foods, and this is having a negative effect on families and societies. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Fast-food chains now appear in almost every country, and the meals they sell are displacing dishes that had been cooked for generations. I largely agree that the effect is negative, though the harm is more social than culinary and the causes deserve some sympathy.\n\nThe clearest damage is to health. Fast food is engineered to be cheap and appealing, which in practice means high in sugar, salt and fat and low in fibre, and countries that adopted it rapidly have seen obesity and diabetes follow within a decade. Traditional diets, whatever their origin, were built around vegetables, grains and small quantities of meat, and replacing them changes what a whole population eats within a generation.\n\nThe family effect is subtler and possibly more important. A cooked meal requires shopping, preparation and a time when everybody sits down, and that shared hour is where much of the conversation between parents and children actually takes place. Food bought quickly and eaten separately in front of screens removes it. Cooking knowledge also passes from one generation to the next only by being used, so a household that stops cooking <strong class=\"key-phrase\">loses recipes that no book records</strong>. Socially, uniform food weakens the small distinctions that make places feel different, and money spent in international chains leaves the local economy rather than reaching nearby farmers and family restaurants.\n\nThe trend is not simple ignorance, however. Fast food is genuinely cheaper for a household counting every coin, and quick for parents working long hours, so blaming families for choosing it ignores what pushed them. The remedies are practical: teaching cooking in schools, restricting advertising to children, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making fresh ingredients cheaper than processed meals</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the replacement of traditional food harms health, reduces family contact and drains money from local producers. The response should address the cost and time pressures behind it rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treating it as a failure of willpower</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"loses recipes that no book records","context":"The family effect is subtler and possibly more important. A cooked meal requires shopping, preparation and a time when everybody sits down, and that shared hour is where much of the conversation between parents and children actually takes place. Food bought quickly and eaten separately in front of screens removes it. Cooking knowledge also passes from one generation to the next only by being used, so a household that stops cooking loses recipes that no book records. Socially, uniform food weakens the small distinctions that make places feel different, and money spent in international chains leaves the local economy rather than reaching nearby farmers and family restaurants."},{"en":"making fresh ingredients cheaper than processed meals","context":"The trend is not simple ignorance, however. Fast food is genuinely cheaper for a household counting every coin, and quick for parents working long hours, so blaming families for choosing it ignores what pushed them. The remedies are practical: teaching cooking in schools, restricting advertising to children, and making fresh ingredients cheaper than processed meals."},{"en":"treating it as a failure of willpower","context":"In conclusion, the replacement of traditional food harms health, reduces family contact and drains money from local producers. The response should address the cost and time pressures behind it rather than treating it as a failure of willpower."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#210","summary":"Whether the spread of international fast food harms families and societies"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2308","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Should countries grow all their own food?","prompt":"Countries should try to produce all the food for their population and import as little food as possible. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Recent shortages and rising shipping costs have revived the idea that every country should feed itself. I agree that nations should protect a core level of domestic production, but I disagree with the stronger claim that imports should be minimised.\n\nThe case for growing your own food is a case about risk. A country that buys most of its grain abroad is exposed to any war, export ban or bad harvest elsewhere, and food is the one commodity a government cannot allow to run short. Domestic farming also keeps rural communities alive, protects the knowledge and land needed to expand production in a crisis, and cuts the emissions of moving food across oceans. These are serious arguments, and no state should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depend entirely on somebody else&#x27;s fields</strong>.\n\nTotal self-sufficiency, though, is either impossible or ruinous. Climate and soil decide what will grow: coffee, bananas and rice cannot be produced in northern Europe, and Gulf states have neither the water nor the land. Forcing production against nature means heated glasshouses, heavy irrigation and chemical inputs, so the environmental cost usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exceeds the cost of shipping</strong>. Diets would narrow, prices would rise, and the poorest households would feel it first.\n\nThere is also a hidden danger in relying on one country&#x27;s harvest alone. A single drought or plant disease then affects everything at once, whereas buying from several regions <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spreads the risk across different climates</strong>. Trade is not only cheaper; it is often safer.\n\nIn conclusion, every country should retain enough farming to survive a disruption and to keep its rural economy working. Aiming to grow everything would raise prices, damage the environment and, by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">concentrating the risk at home</strong>, make the food supply less secure rather than more.","phrases":[{"en":"depend entirely on somebody else's fields","context":"The case for growing your own food is a case about risk. A country that buys most of its grain abroad is exposed to any war, export ban or bad harvest elsewhere, and food is the one commodity a government cannot allow to run short. Domestic farming also keeps rural communities alive, protects the knowledge and land needed to expand production in a crisis, and cuts the emissions of moving food across oceans. These are serious arguments, and no state should depend entirely on somebody else's fields."},{"en":"exceeds the cost of shipping","context":"Total self-sufficiency, though, is either impossible or ruinous. Climate and soil decide what will grow: coffee, bananas and rice cannot be produced in northern Europe, and Gulf states have neither the water nor the land. Forcing production against nature means heated glasshouses, heavy irrigation and chemical inputs, so the environmental cost usually exceeds the cost of shipping. Diets would narrow, prices would rise, and the poorest households would feel it first."},{"en":"spreads the risk across different climates","context":"There is also a hidden danger in relying on one country's harvest alone. A single drought or plant disease then affects everything at once, whereas buying from several regions spreads the risk across different climates. Trade is not only cheaper; it is often safer."},{"en":"concentrating the risk at home","context":"In conclusion, every country should retain enough farming to survive a disruption and to keep its rural economy working. Aiming to grow everything would raise prices, damage the environment and, by concentrating the risk at home, make the food supply less secure rather than more."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#211","summary":"Whether nations should aim for food self-sufficiency and minimal imports"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2329","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Why restaurants and shops throw food away","prompt":"In many countries, people throw away a lot of food from restaurants and shops. Why do people waste food in this way? What can be done to reduce the amount of food thrown away?","answer":"A large share of everything grown is discarded before anyone eats it, and much of that loss happens in kitchens and supermarkets rather than in homes. The causes are commercial rather than careless, and the remedies have to change the commercial logic.\n\nShops waste food because empty shelves cost more than wasted stock. A customer who finds no bread may shop elsewhere permanently, so managers order generously and discard the surplus, and that surplus is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cheaper than the risk of disappointing a buyer</strong>. Cosmetic standards add to it: fruit of the wrong shape is rejected before it reaches a shelf. Date labels create more waste still, since &quot;best before&quot; describes quality rather than safety, yet perfectly good food is thrown out on the day it is reached.\n\nRestaurants waste for related reasons. Menus must be available in full until closing, portions have grown far beyond what most people finish, and buffets guarantee that whatever remains is discarded. Kitchens also work at speed, so trimmings that a home cook would use become rubbish.\n\nSeveral measures work. France requires large supermarkets to donate unsold food rather than destroy it, and similar laws elsewhere have raised donations sharply. Clearer labelling, with safety dates separated from quality dates, prevents an enormous amount of unnecessary disposal. Shops can price food down through the day instead of discarding it at closing, sell irregular produce cheaply, and use ordering systems that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">predict demand instead of guessing at it</strong>. Restaurants can offer two portion sizes and make taking leftovers home normal rather than embarrassing.\n\nIn conclusion, commercial food waste arises because throwing food away is currently cheaper and safer for a business than running short. Donation laws, honest date labels and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prices that fall as the day ends</strong> would reverse that calculation and cut the losses substantially.","phrases":[{"en":"cheaper than the risk of disappointing a buyer","context":"Shops waste food because empty shelves cost more than wasted stock. A customer who finds no bread may shop elsewhere permanently, so managers order generously and discard the surplus, and that surplus is cheaper than the risk of disappointing a buyer. Cosmetic standards add to it: fruit of the wrong shape is rejected before it reaches a shelf. Date labels create more waste still, since \"best before\" describes quality rather than safety, yet perfectly good food is thrown out on the day it is reached."},{"en":"predict demand instead of guessing at it","context":"Several measures work. France requires large supermarkets to donate unsold food rather than destroy it, and similar laws elsewhere have raised donations sharply. Clearer labelling, with safety dates separated from quality dates, prevents an enormous amount of unnecessary disposal. Shops can price food down through the day instead of discarding it at closing, sell irregular produce cheaply, and use ordering systems that predict demand instead of guessing at it. Restaurants can offer two portion sizes and make taking leftovers home normal rather than embarrassing."},{"en":"prices that fall as the day ends","context":"In conclusion, commercial food waste arises because throwing food away is currently cheaper and safer for a business than running short. Donation laws, honest date labels and prices that fall as the day ends would reverse that calculation and cut the losses substantially."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#212","summary":"The causes of commercial food waste and how it can be reduced"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2333","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Buying imported food instead of local","prompt":"In many countries, more and more people choose to buy imported food rather than food produced locally. Why do people buy imported food? What could be done to encourage people to buy local food?","answer":"Supermarket shelves now carry fruit from three continents in the middle of winter, and shoppers reach for it readily. The reasons are mostly price and habit, and reversing the trend requires making local food easier to buy rather than lecturing people about buying it.\n\nCost is the first explanation. Large-scale farms abroad, cheaper labour and government subsidies mean imported apples or rice often undercut the same product grown nearby, and for a household on a tight budget that decides the matter. Availability is the second: imports allow strawberries in December and food that simply cannot be grown in the local climate. Quality and consistency play a part too, since importers grade produce carefully while local supply varies with the season. There is also a matter of status, because in many countries foreign brands <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carry an assumption of higher quality</strong> whether or not it is deserved.\n\nSeveral measures would change this. The most effective is price: supporting local growers, and taxing the environmental cost of long-distance transport, would narrow the gap that drives the decision. Distribution matters as much, because farmers often cannot meet supermarket contracts, so investment in cold storage, processing and cooperatives would let them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supply the shelves rather than only the roadside</strong>.\n\nDemand can be built as well. Clear country-of-origin labelling lets people choose deliberately, and public institutions such as schools and hospitals can be required to buy locally, which creates guaranteed orders. Markets, cookery programmes and seasonal campaigns help by making local produce feel like the better option rather than the patriotic one.\n\nIn conclusion, people buy imported food because it is cheaper, always available and often better presented. Encouraging local purchases means closing the price gap, strengthening the supply chain and labelling clearly enough for the choice to be real.","phrases":[{"en":"carry an assumption of higher quality","context":"Cost is the first explanation. Large-scale farms abroad, cheaper labour and government subsidies mean imported apples or rice often undercut the same product grown nearby, and for a household on a tight budget that decides the matter. Availability is the second: imports allow strawberries in December and food that simply cannot be grown in the local climate. Quality and consistency play a part too, since importers grade produce carefully while local supply varies with the season. There is also a matter of status, because in many countries foreign brands carry an assumption of higher quality whether or not it is deserved."},{"en":"supply the shelves rather than only the roadside","context":"Several measures would change this. The most effective is price: supporting local growers, and taxing the environmental cost of long-distance transport, would narrow the gap that drives the decision. Distribution matters as much, because farmers often cannot meet supermarket contracts, so investment in cold storage, processing and cooperatives would let them supply the shelves rather than only the roadside."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#213","summary":"Why shoppers choose imported food and how local produce could be encouraged"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2340","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Who is responsible for what we eat?","prompt":"Some people think the government must ensure that citizens have a healthy diet, while others think this is an individual's own duty. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Obesity and diabetes now cost health systems more than most diseases, which raises the question of whose problem eating badly actually is. Both sides have a point, and my own view is that governments should shape the choices while leaving the choosing to individuals.\n\nThe argument for personal responsibility is a moral one. Adults are entitled to decide what they eat, and a state that dictates diet has crossed into an area where it does not belong. People also know the basic facts already, so the failure is one of will rather than knowledge, and taxes on food fall hardest on the poor while leaving the wealthy free to eat as they please.\n\nThe case for government action is more practical. Individual choice is heavily conditioned: processed food is cheap, filling and advertised constantly, while fresh food is expensive and takes time that people working two jobs do not have. Children cannot choose at all. Diet is also not a private matter once the consequences arrive, since the treatment of preventable illness is paid for by everyone. Governments already regulate food safety without controversy, and nobody argues that adults should judge bacteria for themselves.\n\nThe measures that work support this view. Cutting sugar in drinks through a levy reduced consumption without banning anything, and clearer labelling, limits on advertising to children and proper school meals all change behaviour at scale. These <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adjust the setting rather than the person</strong>, which is both more effective and less intrusive than instructing people what to eat.\n\nIn conclusion, individuals must make their own decisions, and no government should decide what appears on a plate. Governments are nonetheless responsible for the prices, labels and advertising that surround those decisions, because a free choice between bad options is not really free.","phrases":[{"en":"adjust the setting rather than the person","context":"The measures that work support this view. Cutting sugar in drinks through a levy reduced consumption without banning anything, and clearer labelling, limits on advertising to children and proper school meals all change behaviour at scale. These adjust the setting rather than the person, which is both more effective and less intrusive than instructing people what to eat."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#214","summary":"Whether diet is a government duty or a personal one"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2343","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Feeding a growing world","prompt":"Demand for food is increasing worldwide. What is the cause of this? What measures can the international community and governments take to meet this demand?","answer":"The world will need substantially more food within a generation, and the pressure is already visible in prices. The causes are population and prosperity together, and meeting the demand depends less on growing more than on losing less.\n\nPopulation is the obvious cause, since several billion more people will need feeding this century, most of them in regions that already struggle to produce enough. The less obvious cause is rising income. As households become wealthier they eat more meat and dairy, and livestock consumes many times more grain than it returns as food, so a richer world needs far more crops per person. Two further pressures make this harder: farmland is being lost to cities and erosion, and climate change is reducing yields in exactly the regions where demand is growing fastest.\n\nGovernments can act on supply first. Investment in irrigation, storage and rural roads raises output quickly in poorer countries, where much of the harvest is currently lost between the field and the market, and better seed and advice can close the gap between what smallholders grow and what the same land could produce. Research into drought-resistant crops deserves public funding rather than being left to a few companies.\n\nDemand can be managed as well. Reducing waste is the cheapest measure available, since roughly a third of food produced is never eaten, and shifting diets slightly away from meat frees enormous quantities of grain. Internationally, keeping trade open matters, because export bans turn a local shortage into a global one, and emergency reserves prevent panic buying from driving prices beyond the reach of poor households.\n\nIn conclusion, demand is rising because there are more people and because they are eating richer diets. Investment in farming in developing regions, serious action on waste and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open trade with proper reserves</strong> would meet it without clearing more land.","phrases":[{"en":"open trade with proper reserves","context":"In conclusion, demand is rising because there are more people and because they are eating richer diets. Investment in farming in developing regions, serious action on waste and open trade with proper reserves would meet it without clearing more land."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#215","summary":"Why global food demand is rising and how governments can meet it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2360","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Food & Diet","title":"Cheaper vegetables or costlier junk food?","prompt":"Some argue that subsidising fruit and vegetables is a good way for governments to make healthy food cheaper, while others maintain that taxing junk food would work better. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Diet-related illness now consumes a large share of health budgets, and price is one of the few levers a government can pull. Both approaches have evidence behind them, and my own view is that they work properly only together.\n\nSubsidy has the advantage of pushing rather than punishing. Fresh produce is expensive relative to processed food, particularly for households buying calories on a fixed budget, so lowering its price removes a real barrier rather than adding a burden. Schemes that give low-income families vouchers for fruit and vegetables have raised consumption measurably. Politically it is far easier to introduce, since nobody objects to cheaper food, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">helps the poorest households rather than charging them</strong>. It also supports growers.\n\nTaxation has stronger evidence behind it. Levies on sugary drinks have reduced sugar consumption in every country that has tried them, partly because buyers switch and partly because manufacturers reformulate products to avoid the charge, which changes what is on the shelf for everyone. Tax also raises revenue instead of costing it, and that money can fund the subsidy. The principle is familiar from tobacco: where a product imposes costs on the health service, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the price should carry some of that cost</strong>.\n\nThe objections are equally clear. Subsidy alone is expensive and does not stop anybody buying what they were buying already. Taxation alone falls hardest on the poor, who spend more of their income on food and have fewest alternatives, and it looks like punishing people for being unable to afford anything better.\n\nIn conclusion, taxing unhealthy products changes both purchases and recipes, while subsidies make the healthy option genuinely affordable. Used separately each has a serious weakness, so the sensible policy is to tax the harm and spend the revenue on the alternative.","phrases":[{"en":"helps the poorest households rather than charging them","context":"Subsidy has the advantage of pushing rather than punishing. Fresh produce is expensive relative to processed food, particularly for households buying calories on a fixed budget, so lowering its price removes a real barrier rather than adding a burden. Schemes that give low-income families vouchers for fruit and vegetables have raised consumption measurably. Politically it is far easier to introduce, since nobody objects to cheaper food, and it helps the poorest households rather than charging them. It also supports growers."},{"en":"the price should carry some of that cost","context":"Taxation has stronger evidence behind it. Levies on sugary drinks have reduced sugar consumption in every country that has tried them, partly because buyers switch and partly because manufacturers reformulate products to avoid the charge, which changes what is on the shelf for everyone. Tax also raises revenue instead of costing it, and that money can fund the subsidy. The principle is familiar from tobacco: where a product imposes costs on the health service, the price should carry some of that cost."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#216","summary":"Whether subsidising healthy food or taxing unhealthy food works better"},{"id":"writing-task-2-114","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Global Issues","title":"Helping our own country or the whole world?","prompt":"We cannot help everyone in the world that needs help, so we should only be concerned with our own communities and countries. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this statement?","answer":"Some people believe that we should not help people in other countries as long as there are problems in our own society. I disagree with this view because I believe that we should try to help as many people as possible.\n\nOn the one hand, I accept that it is important to help our neighbours and fellow citizens. In most communities there are people who are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impoverished or disadvantaged</strong> in some way. It is possible to find homeless people, for example, in even the wealthiest of cities, and for those who are concerned about this problem, there are usually opportunities to volunteer time or give money to support these people. In the UK, people can help in a variety of ways, from donating clothing to serving free food in a soup kitchen. As <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the problems are on our doorstep</strong>, and there are obvious ways to help, I can understand why some people feel that we should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prioritise local charity</strong>.\n\nAt the same time, I believe that we have an obligation to help those who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">live beyond our national borders</strong>. In some countries the problems that people face are much more serious than those in our own communities, and it is often even easier to help. For example, when children are dying from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curable diseases</strong> in African countries, governments and individuals in richer countries can save lives simply by paying for vaccines that already exist. A small donation to an international charity might have a much greater impact than helping in our local area.\n\nIn conclusion, it is true that we cannot help everyone, but in my opinion national boundaries should not stop us from helping those who are in need.","phrases":[{"en":"impoverished or disadvantaged","context":"On the one hand, I accept that it is important to help our neighbours and fellow citizens. In most communities there are people who are impoverished or disadvantaged in some way. It is possible to find homeless people, for example, in even the wealthiest of cities, and for those who are concerned about this problem, there are usually opportunities to volunteer time or give money to support these people. In the UK, people can help in a variety of ways, from donating clothing to serving free food in a soup kitchen. As the problems are on our doorstep, and there are obvious ways to help, I can understand why some people feel that we should prioritise local charity."},{"en":"the problems are on our doorstep","context":"On the one hand, I accept that it is important to help our neighbours and fellow citizens. In most communities there are people who are impoverished or disadvantaged in some way. It is possible to find homeless people, for example, in even the wealthiest of cities, and for those who are concerned about this problem, there are usually opportunities to volunteer time or give money to support these people. In the UK, people can help in a variety of ways, from donating clothing to serving free food in a soup kitchen. As the problems are on our doorstep, and there are obvious ways to help, I can understand why some people feel that we should prioritise local charity."},{"en":"prioritise local charity","context":"On the one hand, I accept that it is important to help our neighbours and fellow citizens. In most communities there are people who are impoverished or disadvantaged in some way. It is possible to find homeless people, for example, in even the wealthiest of cities, and for those who are concerned about this problem, there are usually opportunities to volunteer time or give money to support these people. In the UK, people can help in a variety of ways, from donating clothing to serving free food in a soup kitchen. As the problems are on our doorstep, and there are obvious ways to help, I can understand why some people feel that we should prioritise local charity."},{"en":"live beyond our national borders","context":"At the same time, I believe that we have an obligation to help those who live beyond our national borders. In some countries the problems that people face are much more serious than those in our own communities, and it is often even easier to help. For example, when children are dying from curable diseases in African countries, governments and individuals in richer countries can save lives simply by paying for vaccines that already exist. A small donation to an international charity might have a much greater impact than helping in our local area."},{"en":"curable diseases","context":"At the same time, I believe that we have an obligation to help those who live beyond our national borders. In some countries the problems that people face are much more serious than those in our own communities, and it is often even easier to help. For example, when children are dying from curable diseases in African countries, governments and individuals in richer countries can save lives simply by paying for vaccines that already exist. A small donation to an international charity might have a much greater impact than helping in our local area."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#217","summary":"Helping locally or helping people abroad"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2257","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Global Issues","title":"Do countries need each other more than ever?","prompt":"As we are facing more and more problems which affect the whole planet, good relationships between different countries are becoming more important than ever before. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The largest problems now facing humanity do not respect borders, which has led to the claim that cooperation between states matters more than at any previous point in history. I agree with this, because the specific threats we face cannot be solved by any country acting alone.\n\nThe clearest case is the atmosphere. Emissions released in one country warm the whole planet, so a state that reduces its own output while others expand theirs achieves nothing except its own economic disadvantage. This is why agreements matter more than national policies: cooperation is not a moral preference here but the only arrangement in which any effort <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produces a result at all</strong>.\n\nDisease works the same way. A virus that appears in one city reaches every continent within weeks, and the speed of detection depends entirely on whether governments share information honestly and quickly. Countries that concealed early outbreaks damaged themselves as much as their neighbours, and vaccine development during the last pandemic depended on laboratories in several countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exchanging results within days</strong>.\n\nThe main counter-argument is that cooperation often fails when interests genuinely conflict, and that agreements are ignored the moment they become expensive. This is a fair description of how international politics frequently works, but it argues for building stronger institutions rather than for abandoning the attempt, since the alternative <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaves every country worse off</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that good relations between countries have never mattered more. Climate change, pandemics and the regulation of new technologies are problems whose causes and effects cross borders, and no government, however powerful, can protect its own citizens from them <strong class=\"key-phrase\">by acting within its own territory</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"produces a result at all","context":"The clearest case is the atmosphere. Emissions released in one country warm the whole planet, so a state that reduces its own output while others expand theirs achieves nothing except its own economic disadvantage. This is why agreements matter more than national policies: cooperation is not a moral preference here but the only arrangement in which any effort produces a result at all."},{"en":"exchanging results within days","context":"Disease works the same way. A virus that appears in one city reaches every continent within weeks, and the speed of detection depends entirely on whether governments share information honestly and quickly. Countries that concealed early outbreaks damaged themselves as much as their neighbours, and vaccine development during the last pandemic depended on laboratories in several countries exchanging results within days."},{"en":"leaves every country worse off","context":"The main counter-argument is that cooperation often fails when interests genuinely conflict, and that agreements are ignored the moment they become expensive. This is a fair description of how international politics frequently works, but it argues for building stronger institutions rather than for abandoning the attempt, since the alternative leaves every country worse off."},{"en":"by acting within its own territory","context":"In conclusion, I agree that good relations between countries have never mattered more. Climate change, pandemics and the regulation of new technologies are problems whose causes and effects cross borders, and no government, however powerful, can protect its own citizens from them by acting within its own territory."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#218","summary":"The growing importance of good relations between countries"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2268","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Global Issues","title":"What is international cooperation for?","prompt":"Some people think the main benefit of international cooperation is in the protection of the environment, while others think that the main benefits are in world business. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Countries cooperate for many reasons, and there is disagreement over whether the greatest return comes from protecting the environment or from trade. Both produce real gains, but in my view the environmental case is the stronger one.\n\nThe commercial argument is easy to demonstrate. Agreements that lower tariffs and standardise regulations have allowed goods to move cheaply, which has raised living standards and lifted hundreds of millions out of poverty in Asia within a generation. Shared technical standards matter just as much: aviation, shipping and telecommunications function only because states agreed on common rules, and a phone bought in one country works in another because of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decisions taken jointly decades ago</strong>.\n\nThe environmental argument rests on a different logic. Trade produces benefits that a country could partly achieve alone through domestic reform, whereas no country can protect its own atmosphere or oceans by itself. When states agreed to phase out the chemicals destroying the ozone layer, the hole began to close; no individual government could have produced that outcome, however determined. This is the defining feature of environmental cooperation: it is not one route to a benefit but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only route there is</strong>.\n\nThe two are also connected rather than separate. Trade agreements now increasingly carry environmental conditions, and the flow of clean technology between countries depends on the same commercial arrangements that move everything else, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">progress in one supports the other</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, cooperation on business has raised incomes on a scale that should not be understated. Environmental cooperation matters more, because a failure to reach agreement there produces damage that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no amount of trade can compensate for</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"decisions taken jointly decades ago","context":"The commercial argument is easy to demonstrate. Agreements that lower tariffs and standardise regulations have allowed goods to move cheaply, which has raised living standards and lifted hundreds of millions out of poverty in Asia within a generation. Shared technical standards matter just as much: aviation, shipping and telecommunications function only because states agreed on common rules, and a phone bought in one country works in another because of decisions taken jointly decades ago."},{"en":"the only route there is","context":"The environmental argument rests on a different logic. Trade produces benefits that a country could partly achieve alone through domestic reform, whereas no country can protect its own atmosphere or oceans by itself. When states agreed to phase out the chemicals destroying the ozone layer, the hole began to close; no individual government could have produced that outcome, however determined. This is the defining feature of environmental cooperation: it is not one route to a benefit but the only route there is."},{"en":"progress in one supports the other","context":"The two are also connected rather than separate. Trade agreements now increasingly carry environmental conditions, and the flow of clean technology between countries depends on the same commercial arrangements that move everything else, so progress in one supports the other."},{"en":"no amount of trade can compensate for","context":"In conclusion, cooperation on business has raised incomes on a scale that should not be understated. Environmental cooperation matters more, because a failure to reach agreement there produces damage that no amount of trade can compensate for."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#219","summary":"The main benefits of international cooperation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2361","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Global Issues","title":"Can any country solve these problems alone?","prompt":"Most of the urgent problems facing the world today can only be solved with international cooperation. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Climate change, pandemics and financial instability are frequently described as problems no government can handle alone. I largely agree, though the claim needs care, since cooperation is essential for some problems and irrelevant to others.\n\nThe strongest evidence comes from problems that cross borders by nature. A virus reaches every continent within weeks, so a country that vaccinates its own population while the disease circulates elsewhere buys only a delay. Emissions behave the same way, and a nation that eliminated its own would still face rising seas. Tax avoidance, organised crime and the regulation of powerful technology companies all fail for the same reason: firms simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move to whichever country asks the fewest questions</strong>. In each case acting alone means bearing the cost while others enjoy the benefit, and that is precisely the situation only an agreement can fix. There is a further argument about capacity. Poorer countries often understand their problems perfectly well and lack the money and expertise to act, so international finance and shared research make the difference between a plan and a result.\n\nThe claim can be overstated, however. Many urgent problems are domestic: housing shortages, failing schools, corruption and inequality are created and solved within a country&#x27;s own institutions, and blaming international conditions can become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a convenient excuse for doing nothing at home</strong>. Cooperation also has a poor record where interests genuinely conflict, since agreements without penalties are frequently signed and ignored.\n\nThe honest position is that cooperation is necessary but not sufficient. It works when countries see a shared benefit and when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">obligations are checked rather than promised</strong>, and it collapses when either is missing.\n\nIn conclusion, the largest threats of this century cross borders and cannot be solved by any government acting alone. Much of the practical work remains national, so the useful formula is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">international agreement with domestic delivery</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"move to whichever country asks the fewest questions","context":"The strongest evidence comes from problems that cross borders by nature. A virus reaches every continent within weeks, so a country that vaccinates its own population while the disease circulates elsewhere buys only a delay. Emissions behave the same way, and a nation that eliminated its own would still face rising seas. Tax avoidance, organised crime and the regulation of powerful technology companies all fail for the same reason: firms simply move to whichever country asks the fewest questions. In each case acting alone means bearing the cost while others enjoy the benefit, and that is precisely the situation only an agreement can fix. There is a further argument about capacity. Poorer countries often understand their problems perfectly well and lack the money and expertise to act, so international finance and shared research make the difference between a plan and a result."},{"en":"a convenient excuse for doing nothing at home","context":"The claim can be overstated, however. Many urgent problems are domestic: housing shortages, failing schools, corruption and inequality are created and solved within a country's own institutions, and blaming international conditions can become a convenient excuse for doing nothing at home. Cooperation also has a poor record where interests genuinely conflict, since agreements without penalties are frequently signed and ignored."},{"en":"obligations are checked rather than promised","context":"The honest position is that cooperation is necessary but not sufficient. It works when countries see a shared benefit and when obligations are checked rather than promised, and it collapses when either is missing."},{"en":"international agreement with domestic delivery","context":"In conclusion, the largest threats of this century cross borders and cannot be solved by any government acting alone. Much of the practical work remains national, so the useful formula is international agreement with domestic delivery."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#220","summary":"Whether the world's urgent problems require international cooperation"},{"id":"writing-task-2-073","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Public money for theatres and stadiums","prompt":"Some people argue that governments should spend money only on medical care and education, and not on theatres or sports stadiums. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that public funds should be restricted to hospitals and schools, and that theatres and sports stadiums should receive nothing. I disagree, because facilities of this kind deliver benefits that healthcare and education cannot provide on their own.\n\nThe strongest argument concerns public health. Obesity, diabetes and heart disease are now among the costliest problems that health services face, and regular exercise is the cheapest way of reducing them. Money spent on swimming pools, athletics tracks and sports halls therefore returns to the health budget <strong class=\"key-phrase\">in the form of illnesses that never develop</strong>. Treating a patient after a heart attack costs far more than providing the facilities that might have prevented it, so this spending is not an alternative to healthcare but part of it.\n\nCultural venues work in a similar way, though less directly. Theatres, concert halls and galleries give people somewhere to go and something to belong to, which matters particularly in small towns where young people otherwise have very little. They also attract visitors, and the money those visitors spend supports local businesses and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">returns to the state in taxation</strong>.\n\nI would accept that priorities matter. When hospitals are short of staff, a city does not need a spectacular new stadium, and such projects should be modest and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely open to the public</strong> rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">built for prestige</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, medical care and education must come first, but I disagree that sport and culture deserve no public money, since both reduce costs elsewhere and improve daily life.","phrases":[{"en":"in the form of illnesses that never develop","context":"The strongest argument concerns public health. Obesity, diabetes and heart disease are now among the costliest problems that health services face, and regular exercise is the cheapest way of reducing them. Money spent on swimming pools, athletics tracks and sports halls therefore returns to the health budget in the form of illnesses that never develop. Treating a patient after a heart attack costs far more than providing the facilities that might have prevented it, so this spending is not an alternative to healthcare but part of it."},{"en":"returns to the state in taxation","context":"Cultural venues work in a similar way, though less directly. Theatres, concert halls and galleries give people somewhere to go and something to belong to, which matters particularly in small towns where young people otherwise have very little. They also attract visitors, and the money those visitors spend supports local businesses and returns to the state in taxation."},{"en":"genuinely open to the public","context":"I would accept that priorities matter. When hospitals are short of staff, a city does not need a spectacular new stadium, and such projects should be modest and genuinely open to the public rather than built for prestige."},{"en":"built for prestige","context":"I would accept that priorities matter. When hospitals are short of staff, a city does not need a spectacular new stadium, and such projects should be modest and genuinely open to the public rather than built for prestige."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#221","summary":"Public money for theatres and stadiums"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Why young people do not vote","prompt":"In some countries, younger people are neglecting their right to vote. What problems does this cause and what are some of the possible solutions?","answer":"When young people do not vote, governments receive <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a less accurate picture of the population&#x27;s priorities</strong>. This can produce policies that favour older voters and weaken confidence in democracy. The best solutions are practical civic education and easier access to voting.\n\nLow youth turnout creates a representation problem. Politicians naturally pay more attention to groups that vote regularly, so issues such as housing costs, entry-level jobs and education may receive less priority. Young adults may then feel ignored, which further reduces participation and creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cycle of political disengagement</strong>.\n\nSeveral measures could improve the situation. Schools should teach how elections and public budgets affect everyday life, rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">presenting politics as a list of institutions</strong>. Registration should be simple, polling information should be written in plain language, and early or postal voting should be widely available so that work or study commitments are not a barrier. Parties also need to select younger candidates and discuss policies in language that is direct and relevant, rather than assuming that young voters are simply uninterested in politics. Youth councils, school mock elections and public debates can also show that political participation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produces visible results in local communities</strong>, which helps make the case for taking part before people reach voting age.\n\nIn conclusion, neglecting the vote reduces the influence of young citizens and makes public policy less balanced. Governments cannot force genuine interest, but they can remove unnecessary barriers and help young people see that voting is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a practical way to protect their interests</strong>.","topic":"Government & Politics","phrases":[{"en":"a less accurate picture of the population's priorities","context":"When young people do not vote, governments receive a less accurate picture of the population's priorities. This can produce policies that favour older voters and weaken confidence in democracy. The best solutions are practical civic education and easier access to voting."},{"en":"a cycle of political disengagement","context":"Low youth turnout creates a representation problem. Politicians naturally pay more attention to groups that vote regularly, so issues such as housing costs, entry-level jobs and education may receive less priority. Young adults may then feel ignored, which further reduces participation and creates a cycle of political disengagement."},{"en":"presenting politics as a list of institutions","context":"Several measures could improve the situation. Schools should teach how elections and public budgets affect everyday life, rather than presenting politics as a list of institutions. Registration should be simple, polling information should be written in plain language, and early or postal voting should be widely available so that work or study commitments are not a barrier. Parties also need to select younger candidates and discuss policies in language that is direct and relevant, rather than assuming that young voters are simply uninterested in politics. Youth councils, school mock elections and public debates can also show that political participation produces visible results in local communities, which helps make the case for taking part before people reach voting age."},{"en":"produces visible results in local communities","context":"Several measures could improve the situation. Schools should teach how elections and public budgets affect everyday life, rather than presenting politics as a list of institutions. Registration should be simple, polling information should be written in plain language, and early or postal voting should be widely available so that work or study commitments are not a barrier. Parties also need to select younger candidates and discuss policies in language that is direct and relevant, rather than assuming that young voters are simply uninterested in politics. Youth councils, school mock elections and public debates can also show that political participation produces visible results in local communities, which helps make the case for taking part before people reach voting age."},{"en":"a practical way to protect their interests","context":"In conclusion, neglecting the vote reduces the influence of young citizens and makes public policy less balanced. Governments cannot force genuine interest, but they can remove unnecessary barriers and help young people see that voting is a practical way to protect their interests."}],"subtype":"problem","summary":"Why younger people are not using their right to vote","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-113","_displayNo":"#222"},{"id":"writing-task-2-115","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Is economic progress the most important goal?","prompt":"Many governments think that economic progress is their most important goal. Some people, however, think that other types of progress are equally important for a country. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about how governments should measure their countries’ progress. While economic progress is of course essential, I agree with those who believe that other measures of progress are just as important.\n\nThere are three key reasons why economic growth is seen as a fundamental goal for countries. Firstly, a healthy economy results in job creation, a high level of employment, and better salaries for all citizens. Secondly, economic progress ensures that more money is available for governments to spend on infrastructure and public services. For example, a government with higher revenues can invest in the country&#x27;s transport network, its education system and its hospitals. Finally, a strong economy can help <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a country&#x27;s standing on the global stage</strong>, in terms of its political influence and trading power.\n\nHowever, I would argue that various other forms of progress are just as significant as the economic factors mentioned above. In particular, we should consider the area of social justice, human rights, equality and democracy itself. For example, the treatment of minority groups is often seen as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reflection of the moral standards</strong> and level of development of a society. Perhaps another key consideration when judging the progress of a modern country should be how well that country protects the natural environment, and whether it is moving towards <strong class=\"key-phrase\">environmental sustainability</strong>. Alternatively, the success of a nation could be measured by looking at the health, well-being and happiness of its residents.\n\nIn conclusion, the economy is obviously <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a key marker of a country&#x27;s success</strong>, but social, environmental and health criteria are equally significant.","phrases":[{"en":"a country's standing on the global stage","context":"There are three key reasons why economic growth is seen as a fundamental goal for countries. Firstly, a healthy economy results in job creation, a high level of employment, and better salaries for all citizens. Secondly, economic progress ensures that more money is available for governments to spend on infrastructure and public services. For example, a government with higher revenues can invest in the country's transport network, its education system and its hospitals. Finally, a strong economy can help a country's standing on the global stage, in terms of its political influence and trading power."},{"en":"a reflection of the moral standards","context":"However, I would argue that various other forms of progress are just as significant as the economic factors mentioned above. In particular, we should consider the area of social justice, human rights, equality and democracy itself. For example, the treatment of minority groups is often seen as a reflection of the moral standards and level of development of a society. Perhaps another key consideration when judging the progress of a modern country should be how well that country protects the natural environment, and whether it is moving towards environmental sustainability. Alternatively, the success of a nation could be measured by looking at the health, well-being and happiness of its residents."},{"en":"environmental sustainability","context":"However, I would argue that various other forms of progress are just as significant as the economic factors mentioned above. In particular, we should consider the area of social justice, human rights, equality and democracy itself. For example, the treatment of minority groups is often seen as a reflection of the moral standards and level of development of a society. Perhaps another key consideration when judging the progress of a modern country should be how well that country protects the natural environment, and whether it is moving towards environmental sustainability. Alternatively, the success of a nation could be measured by looking at the health, well-being and happiness of its residents."},{"en":"a key marker of a country's success","context":"In conclusion, the economy is obviously a key marker of a country's success, but social, environmental and health criteria are equally significant."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#223","summary":"Economic growth versus broader progress"},{"id":"writing-task-2-116","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Is it better to save than to spend?","prompt":"It is better to save money than to spend it. How far do you agree with this statement? Is saving more important than spending in today's world? Give reasons for your answer, and provide ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"With the world in such economic turmoil, many of us face difficult choices in managing our money. Personally, I feel that saving is preferable to high spending, and I will explain why.\n\nThe clearest reason to save is that the future is unpredictable. Saving is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sensible precaution</strong> against unemployment, illness or a sudden repair bill, any of which can appear with almost no warning. A household with funds in reserve can absorb a shock like this without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">borrowing at a punishing rate of interest</strong>; one without any savings is often pushed onto a credit card, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a temporary setback turns into years of debt</strong>.\n\nThis habit of setting money aside also builds a discipline that spending does not. Someone who transfers a fixed share of each pay cheque, even ten per cent, into a separate account is forced to plan ahead rather than spend whatever is left at the end of the month. Treating that transfer as a fixed bill rather than an optional extra is usually enough, over several years, to turn an ordinary salary into a house deposit or a comfortable retirement fund.\n\nThe 2008 financial crisis illustrates what happens when this discipline disappears on a larger scale: banks, governments and households had all borrowed heavily against future income, and when that borrowing could no longer be rolled over, the resulting crash cost millions of people their jobs and homes. Some argue that life is short and money should be enjoyed now rather than hoarded, which is fair advice for everyday spending. Treating saving as optional, however, assumes that good times will continue indefinitely, an assumption the last two decades have repeatedly shown to be unwise.\n\nTo conclude, I believe that saving is the wiser course, not just for financial but also for personal and political reasons. Sensible saving is an invaluable skill and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a buffer against uncertainty</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"a sensible precaution","context":"The clearest reason to save is that the future is unpredictable. Saving is a sensible precaution against unemployment, illness or a sudden repair bill, any of which can appear with almost no warning. A household with funds in reserve can absorb a shock like this without borrowing at a punishing rate of interest; one without any savings is often pushed onto a credit card, and a temporary setback turns into years of debt."},{"en":"borrowing at a punishing rate of interest","context":"The clearest reason to save is that the future is unpredictable. Saving is a sensible precaution against unemployment, illness or a sudden repair bill, any of which can appear with almost no warning. A household with funds in reserve can absorb a shock like this without borrowing at a punishing rate of interest; one without any savings is often pushed onto a credit card, and a temporary setback turns into years of debt."},{"en":"a temporary setback turns into years of debt","context":"The clearest reason to save is that the future is unpredictable. Saving is a sensible precaution against unemployment, illness or a sudden repair bill, any of which can appear with almost no warning. A household with funds in reserve can absorb a shock like this without borrowing at a punishing rate of interest; one without any savings is often pushed onto a credit card, and a temporary setback turns into years of debt."},{"en":"a buffer against uncertainty","context":"To conclude, I believe that saving is the wiser course, not just for financial but also for personal and political reasons. Sensible saving is an invaluable skill and a buffer against uncertainty."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#224","summary":"It is better to save money than to spend it."},{"id":"writing-task-2-117","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Is international aid a moral obligation?","prompt":"The advocates of international aid believe that countries have a moral obligation to help each other, while the opponents consider it unnecessary, because money is misspent by the governments that receive it. Discuss these two points of view and give your opinion.","answer":"Supporters of international aid argue that wealthy countries have a moral duty to assist poorer ones, while critics point to corruption and waste. Both concerns are valid. In my view, aid remains necessary, but it should be targeted, transparent and designed to build local capacity rather than permanent dependence.\n\nThe strongest case for aid arises during emergencies. After war, drought or an earthquake, outside funding can provide food, medicine, clean water and temporary shelter far more quickly than a damaged government can. Longer-term support can also fund vaccination, teacher training and basic infrastructure. These investments save lives and may prevent instability from spreading across borders.\n\nCritics are right that money can be diverted by officials, spent on unsuitable projects or tied to the donor&#x27;s commercial interests. Large transfers made without local oversight may weaken accountability and encourage governments to rely on external finance. The answer, however, is not to abandon assistance. Donors should publish contracts, audit outcomes, work with reliable local organisations and release funds in stages. Wherever possible, aid should transfer skills and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strengthen institutions that can continue the work</strong>.\n\nAid can also affect a government&#x27;s own incentives. When a large share of the budget arrives as foreign transfers, ministers face less pressure to build effective domestic tax collection, since public spending no longer depends entirely on what citizens are willing to pay. Aid that is tied to measurable progress on revenue collection, rather than simply replacing it, is more likely to leave a government financially self-reliant once the programme ends.\n\nIn conclusion, international aid is justified both morally and practically, but its quality matters more than the headline amount. Carefully monitored programmes that respond to local priorities can deliver lasting benefits instead of short-term publicity.","phrases":[{"en":"strengthen institutions that can continue the work","context":"Critics are right that money can be diverted by officials, spent on unsuitable projects or tied to the donor's commercial interests. Large transfers made without local oversight may weaken accountability and encourage governments to rely on external finance. The answer, however, is not to abandon assistance. Donors should publish contracts, audit outcomes, work with reliable local organisations and release funds in stages. Wherever possible, aid should transfer skills and strengthen institutions that can continue the work."}],"subtype":"Discussion","_displayNo":"#225","summary":"Advocates of international aid believe that countries have a moral…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-118","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Can only government solve housing shortages?","prompt":"Only government action can solve housing shortages in big cities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that only government action can solve the housing shortages affecting large cities. I disagree, because although the state has an essential role, the problem cannot be solved without private construction as well.\n\nGovernments do have powers that nobody else possesses. They control planning permission, which is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the real constraint on building</strong>, and they can release public land, invest in the transport that makes outer districts viable, and enforce safety standards. They are also the only body that will house the people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the market ignores</strong>: the disabled, the elderly and families on very low incomes will never be profitable to build for, and without public housing they simply go without.\n\nHowever, the scale of the shortage is beyond what any state budget can fund alone. Major cities need hundreds of thousands of new homes, and building them requires private capital, private construction firms and the speed that competition produces. Countries that relied entirely on state provision generally ended up with too few homes, built slowly and to a uniform design that suited nobody in particular. Restricting the market also tends to reduce supply further, since developers move their investment elsewhere.\n\nThe effective approach combines the two. Government sets the rules, provides land and builds for those the market will not serve, while developers build the remainder under conditions requiring <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a proportion of affordable units</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, government action is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">necessary but not sufficient</strong>. Solving housing shortages requires the state and the private sector to do <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the parts each does better</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the real constraint on building","context":"Governments do have powers that nobody else possesses. They control planning permission, which is usually the real constraint on building, and they can release public land, invest in the transport that makes outer districts viable, and enforce safety standards. They are also the only body that will house the people the market ignores: the disabled, the elderly and families on very low incomes will never be profitable to build for, and without public housing they simply go without."},{"en":"the market ignores","context":"Governments do have powers that nobody else possesses. They control planning permission, which is usually the real constraint on building, and they can release public land, invest in the transport that makes outer districts viable, and enforce safety standards. They are also the only body that will house the people the market ignores: the disabled, the elderly and families on very low incomes will never be profitable to build for, and without public housing they simply go without."},{"en":"a proportion of affordable units","context":"The effective approach combines the two. Government sets the rules, provides land and builds for those the market will not serve, while developers build the remainder under conditions requiring a proportion of affordable units."},{"en":"necessary but not sufficient","context":"In conclusion, government action is necessary but not sufficient. Solving housing shortages requires the state and the private sector to do the parts each does better."},{"en":"the parts each does better","context":"In conclusion, government action is necessary but not sufficient. Solving housing shortages requires the state and the private sector to do the parts each does better."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#226","summary":"Can only government solve housing shortages?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-119","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Should people pay taxes?","prompt":"Some people believe that individuals should keep all the money they earn and should not pay taxes to the state. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people argue that individuals should keep everything they earn and pay nothing to the state. I strongly disagree with this view, because almost everything that allows a modern society to function depends on taxation.\n\nThe most obvious objection is that the services people rely on cannot be produced individually. Roads, police, courts, street lighting, clean water and national defence are used by everyone, and it is impossible to supply them only to those who choose to pay. Without compulsory taxation, each person would have an incentive to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">let others fund them</strong>, and the services would simply cease to exist. Even the business owner who resents paying tax depends on educated employees, functioning roads and a legal system that enforces contracts, none of which the private market would supply on its own.\n\nTaxation also protects people against risks they cannot predict. Serious illness, disability, unemployment and old age can affect anyone, and very few individuals could meet the cost of major surgery or decades of retirement from savings alone. A tax-funded system <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spreads that risk</strong> across the whole population, which is why countries without one see families <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ruined by a single accident</strong>.\n\nI would accept that taxes must be reasonable and spent efficiently, and that waste and corruption justify criticism. That, however, is an argument for better government rather than for abolishing taxation altogether.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree entirely with this proposal. Taxation is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the price of living in an organised society</strong>, and a country that abandoned it would quickly find that almost everyone, including the wealthy, was considerably worse off.","phrases":[{"en":"let others fund them","context":"The most obvious objection is that the services people rely on cannot be produced individually. Roads, police, courts, street lighting, clean water and national defence are used by everyone, and it is impossible to supply them only to those who choose to pay. Without compulsory taxation, each person would have an incentive to let others fund them, and the services would simply cease to exist. Even the business owner who resents paying tax depends on educated employees, functioning roads and a legal system that enforces contracts, none of which the private market would supply on its own."},{"en":"spreads that risk","context":"Taxation also protects people against risks they cannot predict. Serious illness, disability, unemployment and old age can affect anyone, and very few individuals could meet the cost of major surgery or decades of retirement from savings alone. A tax-funded system spreads that risk across the whole population, which is why countries without one see families ruined by a single accident."},{"en":"ruined by a single accident","context":"Taxation also protects people against risks they cannot predict. Serious illness, disability, unemployment and old age can affect anyone, and very few individuals could meet the cost of major surgery or decades of retirement from savings alone. A tax-funded system spreads that risk across the whole population, which is why countries without one see families ruined by a single accident."},{"en":"the price of living in an organised society","context":"In conclusion, I disagree entirely with this proposal. Taxation is the price of living in an organised society, and a country that abandoned it would quickly find that almost everyone, including the wealthy, was considerably worse off."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#227","summary":"Should people pay taxes?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2244","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Government & Politics","title":"Who should pay for schools and hospitals?","prompt":"Some people think that governments should pay for education and health care, while others believe that it is not their responsibility. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Whether the state should fund schools and hospitals is one of the oldest disagreements in politics. I believe governments should pay for both, although the argument for private provision alongside them is stronger than it is usually allowed to be.\n\nThe case for public funding is that these two services <strong class=\"key-phrase\">determine what a person can become</strong>. A child whose family cannot afford school fees loses opportunities permanently, and an untreated illness can end a working life, so leaving both to private purchase means that ability counts for less than the income a person happens to be born into. There is also an efficiency argument that is often overlooked: an educated, healthy population is what produces skilled workers and taxpayers, so the spending <strong class=\"key-phrase\">returns to the treasury over time</strong> rather than disappearing.\n\nThose who disagree point to cost and quality. Health spending rises faster than any government&#x27;s income as populations age, and state monopolies face no competition, which can leave patients waiting months for treatment they would receive privately in a week. Taxing everyone to fund a service that many choose not to use is, on this view, difficult to justify.\n\nMy own position accepts the criticism without accepting the conclusion. Waiting lists are an argument for funding and managing public services properly, not for withdrawing them, and countries that rely on private provision spend more per person while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">covering fewer of their citizens</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, governments should fund education and health care, because both determine life chances and both repay the investment. Private alternatives can reasonably exist for those who want them, provided they operate alongside a public system rather than instead of one.","phrases":[{"en":"determine what a person can become","context":"The case for public funding is that these two services determine what a person can become. A child whose family cannot afford school fees loses opportunities permanently, and an untreated illness can end a working life, so leaving both to private purchase means that ability counts for less than the income a person happens to be born into. There is also an efficiency argument that is often overlooked: an educated, healthy population is what produces skilled workers and taxpayers, so the spending returns to the treasury over time rather than disappearing."},{"en":"returns to the treasury over time","context":"The case for public funding is that these two services determine what a person can become. A child whose family cannot afford school fees loses opportunities permanently, and an untreated illness can end a working life, so leaving both to private purchase means that ability counts for less than the income a person happens to be born into. There is also an efficiency argument that is often overlooked: an educated, healthy population is what produces skilled workers and taxpayers, so the spending returns to the treasury over time rather than disappearing."},{"en":"covering fewer of their citizens","context":"My own position accepts the criticism without accepting the conclusion. Waiting lists are an argument for funding and managing public services properly, not for withdrawing them, and countries that rely on private provision spend more per person while covering fewer of their citizens."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#228","summary":"Government responsibility for education and health care"},{"id":"writing-task-2-112","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"The causes of stress and how to manage it","prompt":"Stress is increasingly common in modern life. What are the main causes of stress, and how can people cope with it?","answer":"Stress is a common problem in modern life, but its causes are not identical for everyone. Work demands, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">financial insecurity</strong> and weak <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social support</strong> can all contribute. In my view, coping requires both practical personal habits and changes to the conditions that create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">persistent pressure</strong>.\n\nWork is an important source of stress when expectations are unclear or workloads are unrealistic. Digital communication can make the problem worse if employees feel they must remain available outside normal hours. Financial worries may reinforce this pressure: someone facing high housing costs or an unexpected bill can find it difficult to switch off. Social isolation is another factor, especially when people have nobody they trust enough to discuss a problem with. Comparing ordinary life with carefully selected images on social media can also create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unrealistic expectations</strong>.\n\nAt the individual level, regular physical activity, adequate sleep and contact with supportive people can help with everyday stress. Setting boundaries around messages and breaking a large task into <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manageable steps</strong> may also reduce the feeling of being constantly overwhelmed. These habits are useful, but they should not be presented as a substitute for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">professional support</strong> when distress is persistent or seriously affects daily life.\n\nEmployers have responsibilities too. Clear priorities, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">realistic deadlines</strong> and predictable working arrangements can remove unnecessary uncertainty. A manager who encourages early discussion of difficulties may prevent a temporary problem from becoming a lasting one. Governments can support access to appropriate services and employment protections, while recognising that personal circumstances differ. Telling individuals simply to become more resilient leaves important causes untouched.\n\nIn conclusion, stress often reflects the interaction of work, money and relationships. Lasting improvement is more likely when helpful individual habits are supported by fair working conditions, social connection and accessible assistance.","phrases":[{"en":"financial insecurity","context":"Stress is a common problem in modern life, but its causes are not identical for everyone. Work demands, financial insecurity and weak social support can all contribute. In my view, coping requires both practical personal habits and changes to the conditions that create persistent pressure."},{"en":"social support","context":"Stress is a common problem in modern life, but its causes are not identical for everyone. Work demands, financial insecurity and weak social support can all contribute. In my view, coping requires both practical personal habits and changes to the conditions that create persistent pressure."},{"en":"persistent pressure","context":"Stress is a common problem in modern life, but its causes are not identical for everyone. Work demands, financial insecurity and weak social support can all contribute. In my view, coping requires both practical personal habits and changes to the conditions that create persistent pressure."},{"en":"unrealistic expectations","context":"Work is an important source of stress when expectations are unclear or workloads are unrealistic. Digital communication can make the problem worse if employees feel they must remain available outside normal hours. Financial worries may reinforce this pressure: someone facing high housing costs or an unexpected bill can find it difficult to switch off. Social isolation is another factor, especially when people have nobody they trust enough to discuss a problem with. Comparing ordinary life with carefully selected images on social media can also create unrealistic expectations."},{"en":"manageable steps","context":"At the individual level, regular physical activity, adequate sleep and contact with supportive people can help with everyday stress. Setting boundaries around messages and breaking a large task into manageable steps may also reduce the feeling of being constantly overwhelmed. These habits are useful, but they should not be presented as a substitute for professional support when distress is persistent or seriously affects daily life."},{"en":"professional support","context":"At the individual level, regular physical activity, adequate sleep and contact with supportive people can help with everyday stress. Setting boundaries around messages and breaking a large task into manageable steps may also reduce the feeling of being constantly overwhelmed. These habits are useful, but they should not be presented as a substitute for professional support when distress is persistent or seriously affects daily life."},{"en":"realistic deadlines","context":"Employers have responsibilities too. Clear priorities, realistic deadlines and predictable working arrangements can remove unnecessary uncertainty. A manager who encourages early discussion of difficulties may prevent a temporary problem from becoming a lasting one. Governments can support access to appropriate services and employment protections, while recognising that personal circumstances differ. Telling individuals simply to become more resilient leaves important causes untouched."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#229","summary":"The causes of stress and how to manage it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-121","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Health and the way we live now","prompt":"In many countries today, there are concerns about the unhealthy lifestyles that people lead. What health issues are linked to modern lifestyles? What answers to these problems can you suggest? Give reasons for your answer, and support your essay with ideas and examples from your own experience. You should write at least 250 words.","answer":"Most people agree that modern lifestyles can be unhealthy, both physically and mentally. I believe there are three main problems, but also some practical steps we can take. Perhaps the largest problem is the issue of obesity, which is growing drastically in most Western countries. With people working at desks and using transport rather than walking, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weight gain seems inevitable</strong>, and being overweight causes various medical issues including heart trouble and strokes.\n\nAnother major problem linked to lifestyle is poor diet, with people who are short of time eating junk food rather than proper nutrients, and resultant health problems range from obesity to high cholesterol and even organ failure. Finally, we must consider the issue of mental health. Modern lifestyles are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">highly pressurised</strong>, with people expected to work at a fast pace for extended periods, and little scope for family life or relaxation, leading to enormous stress and anxiety.\n\nTurning to possible solutions, the biggest step would be to improve the level of education regarding health issues connected to lifestyle, especially obesity. The government, media and schools should work together to raise awareness of the dangers and to promote sports and other positive practices. A further step would be to use legislation to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">curb the consumption of unhealthy foods</strong>, for example by imposing higher sales taxes or by restricting availability in retail outlets. To deal with the psychological impacts, we should also encourage employers to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adopt more flexible working practices</strong>, allowing workers to lead a fuller private life with less stress.\n\nOverall, the key problems of obesity and stress can be addressed through a combination of awareness, targeted legislation and better employment practices.","phrases":[{"en":"weight gain seems inevitable","context":"Most people agree that modern lifestyles can be unhealthy, both physically and mentally. I believe there are three main problems, but also some practical steps we can take. Perhaps the largest problem is the issue of obesity, which is growing drastically in most Western countries. With people working at desks and using transport rather than walking, weight gain seems inevitable, and being overweight causes various medical issues including heart trouble and strokes."},{"en":"highly pressurised","context":"Another major problem linked to lifestyle is poor diet, with people who are short of time eating junk food rather than proper nutrients, and resultant health problems range from obesity to high cholesterol and even organ failure. Finally, we must consider the issue of mental health. Modern lifestyles are highly pressurised, with people expected to work at a fast pace for extended periods, and little scope for family life or relaxation, leading to enormous stress and anxiety."},{"en":"curb the consumption of unhealthy foods","context":"Turning to possible solutions, the biggest step would be to improve the level of education regarding health issues connected to lifestyle, especially obesity. The government, media and schools should work together to raise awareness of the dangers and to promote sports and other positive practices. A further step would be to use legislation to curb the consumption of unhealthy foods, for example by imposing higher sales taxes or by restricting availability in retail outlets. To deal with the psychological impacts, we should also encourage employers to adopt more flexible working practices, allowing workers to lead a fuller private life with less stress."},{"en":"adopt more flexible working practices","context":"Turning to possible solutions, the biggest step would be to improve the level of education regarding health issues connected to lifestyle, especially obesity. The government, media and schools should work together to raise awareness of the dangers and to promote sports and other positive practices. A further step would be to use legislation to curb the consumption of unhealthy foods, for example by imposing higher sales taxes or by restricting availability in retail outlets. To deal with the psychological impacts, we should also encourage employers to adopt more flexible working practices, allowing workers to lead a fuller private life with less stress."}],"subtype":"Problems / Solutions","_displayNo":"#230","summary":"Today, there are concerns about the unhealthy lifestyles that people lead."},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"More sports facilities, or other measures?","prompt":"Some people say that the best way to improve public health is by increasing the number of sports facilities. Others, however, say that this would have little effect on public health and that other measures are required. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Building more sports facilities may encourage some people to exercise, but it is not the best single way to improve public health. I agree with those who argue that broader measures are required because health is shaped by diet, education, income and the local environment.\n\nAdditional gyms, parks and swimming pools can make exercise more convenient. This is particularly useful in neighbourhoods where safe outdoor space is limited. However, facilities have little effect if they are expensive, badly located or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">used mainly by people who are already active</strong>. Access alone does not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">change deeply established habits</strong>.\n\nA more effective strategy would combine affordable exercise with prevention. Schools should teach practical nutrition, cities should provide safe walking and cycling routes, and governments should limit misleading marketing of unhealthy products to children. Basic healthcare should also identify problems such as high blood pressure before they become severe. These policies reach people who may never choose to join a sports centre.\n\nOne reason facilities receive so much funding is political rather than medical. A new swimming pool gives a minister something to open and photograph; a falling blood-pressure average across the population never gets one. Health budgets are too often decided by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">which project looks most impressive</strong>, not by which saves the most lives, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prevention usually loses that contest</strong> despite being the more cost-effective choice.\n\nIn conclusion, sports facilities are a useful part of public-health policy, but they cannot address the full range of causes behind poor health. The greatest improvement will come from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">making healthy choices easy and affordable</strong> in everyday life.","topic":"Health","phrases":[{"en":"used mainly by people who are already active","context":"Additional gyms, parks and swimming pools can make exercise more convenient. This is particularly useful in neighbourhoods where safe outdoor space is limited. However, facilities have little effect if they are expensive, badly located or used mainly by people who are already active. Access alone does not change deeply established habits."},{"en":"change deeply established habits","context":"Additional gyms, parks and swimming pools can make exercise more convenient. This is particularly useful in neighbourhoods where safe outdoor space is limited. However, facilities have little effect if they are expensive, badly located or used mainly by people who are already active. Access alone does not change deeply established habits."},{"en":"which project looks most impressive","context":"One reason facilities receive so much funding is political rather than medical. A new swimming pool gives a minister something to open and photograph; a falling blood-pressure average across the population never gets one. Health budgets are too often decided by which project looks most impressive, not by which saves the most lives, and prevention usually loses that contest despite being the more cost-effective choice."},{"en":"prevention usually loses that contest","context":"One reason facilities receive so much funding is political rather than medical. A new swimming pool gives a minister something to open and photograph; a falling blood-pressure average across the population never gets one. Health budgets are too often decided by which project looks most impressive, not by which saves the most lives, and prevention usually loses that contest despite being the more cost-effective choice."},{"en":"making healthy choices easy and affordable","context":"In conclusion, sports facilities are a useful part of public-health policy, but they cannot address the full range of causes behind poor health. The greatest improvement will come from making healthy choices easy and affordable in everyday life."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Sports facilities compared with other ways of improving public health","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-125","_displayNo":"#231"},{"id":"writing-task-2-130","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Why more children are overweight","prompt":"The percentage of overweight children in western society has increased by almost 20% in the last ten years. Discuss the causes and effects of this disturbing trend.","answer":"The sharp rise in childhood obesity is not the result of a single poor choice. It reflects changes in the food environment, family routines and children&#x27;s daily activity, and its consequences extend well beyond appearance or short-term fitness.\n\nOne major cause is the widespread availability of inexpensive, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">energy-dense food</strong>. Highly processed meals and snacks are convenient for time-pressed households, while sophisticated marketing makes them especially attractive to children. Portion sizes have also increased, meaning that excessive calorie intake can become normal before a child understands nutrition. At the same time, many neighbourhoods and schools provide fewer opportunities for safe, unstructured physical activity. Screen-based entertainment has therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">displaced a substantial amount of active play</strong>, particularly where parents are reluctant to let children play outdoors unsupervised.\n\nThe immediate effects include reduced stamina, sleep problems and a higher likelihood of social stigma. More seriously, obesity during childhood can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">establish patterns that persist into adulthood</strong>. Children who become accustomed to sugary food and sedentary routines may find those habits difficult to reverse later. This increases the risk of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease and joint problems at a younger age than previous generations experienced.\n\nThere are also psychological and social consequences. Overweight children may face bullying or exclusion, which can damage confidence and encourage further withdrawal from sport. Families and health systems then carry a growing financial burden as preventable conditions require long-term treatment. The trend therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">creates a cycle of physical and emotional disadvantage</strong> rather than an isolated medical issue.\n\nIn conclusion, childhood obesity is driven by unhealthy food environments and declining everyday activity. Its effects are cumulative, affecting health, self-esteem and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">public expenditure</strong>. Because the causes are structural as well as personal, the problem should be addressed through coordinated action by families, schools, food producers and governments.","phrases":[{"en":"energy-dense food","context":"One major cause is the widespread availability of inexpensive, energy-dense food. Highly processed meals and snacks are convenient for time-pressed households, while sophisticated marketing makes them especially attractive to children. Portion sizes have also increased, meaning that excessive calorie intake can become normal before a child understands nutrition. At the same time, many neighbourhoods and schools provide fewer opportunities for safe, unstructured physical activity. Screen-based entertainment has therefore displaced a substantial amount of active play, particularly where parents are reluctant to let children play outdoors unsupervised."},{"en":"displaced a substantial amount of active play","context":"One major cause is the widespread availability of inexpensive, energy-dense food. Highly processed meals and snacks are convenient for time-pressed households, while sophisticated marketing makes them especially attractive to children. Portion sizes have also increased, meaning that excessive calorie intake can become normal before a child understands nutrition. At the same time, many neighbourhoods and schools provide fewer opportunities for safe, unstructured physical activity. Screen-based entertainment has therefore displaced a substantial amount of active play, particularly where parents are reluctant to let children play outdoors unsupervised."},{"en":"establish patterns that persist into adulthood","context":"The immediate effects include reduced stamina, sleep problems and a higher likelihood of social stigma. More seriously, obesity during childhood can establish patterns that persist into adulthood. Children who become accustomed to sugary food and sedentary routines may find those habits difficult to reverse later. This increases the risk of type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease and joint problems at a younger age than previous generations experienced."},{"en":"creates a cycle of physical and emotional disadvantage","context":"There are also psychological and social consequences. Overweight children may face bullying or exclusion, which can damage confidence and encourage further withdrawal from sport. Families and health systems then carry a growing financial burden as preventable conditions require long-term treatment. The trend therefore creates a cycle of physical and emotional disadvantage rather than an isolated medical issue."},{"en":"public expenditure","context":"In conclusion, childhood obesity is driven by unhealthy food environments and declining everyday activity. Its effects are cumulative, affecting health, self-esteem and public expenditure. Because the causes are structural as well as personal, the problem should be addressed through coordinated action by families, schools, food producers and governments."}],"subtype":"Causes and effects","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#232","summary":"Why childhood obesity is increasing"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2219","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Preventing illness through policy","prompt":"Some people think that governments should focus on reducing environmental pollution and housing problems to help people to prevent illness and disease. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that the best way to improve public health is not to build more hospitals but to clean the air and fix people&#x27;s homes. I largely agree with this, because the causes of much modern illness lie outside the health service altogether.\n\nThe evidence on air quality is difficult to dismiss. Fine particles from traffic and coal burning penetrate deep into the lungs, and cities with heavy pollution record markedly higher rates of asthma, heart disease and stroke. A government that lowers emissions therefore prevents thousands of admissions that would otherwise have to be treated, which is both cheaper and kinder than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treating the consequences afterwards</strong>.\n\nHousing works in the same way. Damp and cold homes are directly linked to chest infections and childhood asthma, while overcrowding spreads infectious disease and undermines the sleep and study that children need. Insulating an old housing estate is unglamorous work, but it removes a cause of illness permanently rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">managing it year after year</strong>.\n\nI would qualify my agreement in one respect. Prevention cannot replace treatment, because clean air does nothing for a person who has already developed cancer or been injured in an accident, and a government that diverted its entire health budget into housing would leave those patients with nowhere to go. The sensible position is that prevention deserves a far larger share of spending than it usually receives, not that it should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take the place of medical care</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that reducing pollution and improving housing should be central to health policy, since both remove causes of disease rather than symptoms. A country that acts on them will need fewer hospital beds in twenty years, but it will still need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the beds it has today</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"treating the consequences afterwards","context":"The evidence on air quality is difficult to dismiss. Fine particles from traffic and coal burning penetrate deep into the lungs, and cities with heavy pollution record markedly higher rates of asthma, heart disease and stroke. A government that lowers emissions therefore prevents thousands of admissions that would otherwise have to be treated, which is both cheaper and kinder than treating the consequences afterwards."},{"en":"managing it year after year","context":"Housing works in the same way. Damp and cold homes are directly linked to chest infections and childhood asthma, while overcrowding spreads infectious disease and undermines the sleep and study that children need. Insulating an old housing estate is unglamorous work, but it removes a cause of illness permanently rather than managing it year after year."},{"en":"take the place of medical care","context":"I would qualify my agreement in one respect. Prevention cannot replace treatment, because clean air does nothing for a person who has already developed cancer or been injured in an accident, and a government that diverted its entire health budget into housing would leave those patients with nowhere to go. The sensible position is that prevention deserves a far larger share of spending than it usually receives, not that it should take the place of medical care."},{"en":"the beds it has today","context":"In conclusion, I agree that reducing pollution and improving housing should be central to health policy, since both remove causes of disease rather than symptoms. A country that acts on them will need fewer hospital beds in twenty years, but it will still need the beds it has today."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#233","summary":"Tackling pollution and housing in order to prevent illness"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2273","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Should medical care be run for profit?","prompt":"Some people think that good health is very important to every person, so medical services should not be run by profit-making companies. Do the advantages of private health care outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Health is unlike other goods, because somebody who cannot afford treatment is not simply going without a luxury. Private providers do bring speed and investment that state systems often lack, but the disadvantages of running medicine for profit outweigh those gains.\n\nThe advantages are real. Private hospitals compete for patients, so they have a strong reason to shorten waiting times, buy modern equipment and treat people courteously. They also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relieve pressure on the public system</strong>, since every patient who pays for a knee operation frees a state bed for somebody who cannot. Where taxes no longer cover rising costs, private money keeps hospitals building and researching rather than closing wards.\n\nThe central difficulty is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">profit and health point in different directions</strong>. A company earns most from patients who are wealthy and only mildly ill, and least from the elderly, the chronically sick and the poor, who are precisely the people who need care most. The result is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treatment shaped by ability to pay</strong> rather than by medical need. It also encourages unnecessary procedures, because a clinic paid for each scan has an obvious reason to order one.\n\nThere is a further cost to the country as a whole. When wealthier families leave the public system, they stop caring how well it works, and the political pressure to fund it fades. Experienced doctors follow the money as well, so the hospitals that serve the majority lose their best staff. What forms is a two-tier service that is very hard to reverse.\n\nIn conclusion, private medicine delivers efficiency and additional funding, and no country should dismiss those benefits. They are outweighed, though, by a system that allocates care according to wealth and slowly weakens the public provision on which most citizens depend.","phrases":[{"en":"relieve pressure on the public system","context":"The advantages are real. Private hospitals compete for patients, so they have a strong reason to shorten waiting times, buy modern equipment and treat people courteously. They also relieve pressure on the public system, since every patient who pays for a knee operation frees a state bed for somebody who cannot. Where taxes no longer cover rising costs, private money keeps hospitals building and researching rather than closing wards."},{"en":"profit and health point in different directions","context":"The central difficulty is that profit and health point in different directions. A company earns most from patients who are wealthy and only mildly ill, and least from the elderly, the chronically sick and the poor, who are precisely the people who need care most. The result is treatment shaped by ability to pay rather than by medical need. It also encourages unnecessary procedures, because a clinic paid for each scan has an obvious reason to order one."},{"en":"treatment shaped by ability to pay","context":"The central difficulty is that profit and health point in different directions. A company earns most from patients who are wealthy and only mildly ill, and least from the elderly, the chronically sick and the poor, who are precisely the people who need care most. The result is treatment shaped by ability to pay rather than by medical need. It also encourages unnecessary procedures, because a clinic paid for each scan has an obvious reason to order one."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#234","summary":"Whether the advantages of private health care outweigh the disadvantages"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2278","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Healthier in the countryside or the city?","prompt":"Many people believe that it is easier to have a healthy lifestyle in the countryside. Others believe that there are health benefits to living in cities. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"The countryside is usually pictured as clean air and fresh vegetables, and the city as noise, traffic and takeaway food. Both settings carry genuine advantages, and I believe the city has the stronger claim once medical care is taken into account.\n\nRural living clearly helps in some respects. The air is cleaner, which matters enormously for children with asthma and for anyone with a weak heart. Food is often grown nearby and eaten in season. Physical work and long walks build activity into the day rather than requiring a gym, and quieter surroundings and darker nights produce better sleep, which affects almost every measure of health.\n\nCities answer with different strengths. Hospitals, specialists and emergency services are concentrated there, and for a stroke or a difficult birth the distance to a surgeon decides the outcome. Urban residents also have more choice: a supermarket carries fresh food all year, and pools, parks and clubs make exercise easy to sustain. Walking to a station or a shop means many city dwellers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move more than they realise</strong>, while rural life increasingly means driving everywhere. Loneliness, which harms health as much as smoking does, is easier to escape where people are close together.\n\nMy own view is that the countryside offers the better environment and the city the better safety net, and the safety net wins. Clean air cannot compensate for an ambulance that takes fifty minutes, and rural areas now suffer from shortages of doctors and few services for the old. The healthiest arrangement is a city with parks, cycle routes and controlled traffic, since it keeps medical access while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">borrowing what the countryside does well</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, each setting protects health in its own way. Given the weight of medical care and social contact, city living is on balance the healthier option, provided that pollution and inactivity are actively managed.","phrases":[{"en":"move more than they realise","context":"Cities answer with different strengths. Hospitals, specialists and emergency services are concentrated there, and for a stroke or a difficult birth the distance to a surgeon decides the outcome. Urban residents also have more choice: a supermarket carries fresh food all year, and pools, parks and clubs make exercise easy to sustain. Walking to a station or a shop means many city dwellers move more than they realise, while rural life increasingly means driving everywhere. Loneliness, which harms health as much as smoking does, is easier to escape where people are close together."},{"en":"borrowing what the countryside does well","context":"My own view is that the countryside offers the better environment and the city the better safety net, and the safety net wins. Clean air cannot compensate for an ambulance that takes fifty minutes, and rural areas now suffer from shortages of doctors and few services for the old. The healthiest arrangement is a city with parks, cycle routes and controlled traffic, since it keeps medical access while borrowing what the countryside does well."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#235","summary":"Whether rural or urban living is better for health"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2304","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Prevention or treatment?","prompt":"It is more important to spend public money on promoting a healthy lifestyle in order to prevent illness than on treating people who are already ill. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Health budgets everywhere are growing faster than the economies that fund them, which makes the balance between prevention and treatment a practical question rather than a philosophical one. I largely agree that prevention deserves a greater share, though it cannot come at the expense of care for those already ill.\n\nThe argument for prevention is mainly arithmetic. A large proportion of the conditions filling hospitals — type 2 diabetes, heart disease, several cancers, lung disease — is driven by smoking, diet, inactivity and alcohol, and preventing a case costs a fraction of treating one for decades. Measures such as vaccination, clean water, tobacco control and sugar levies have produced the largest gains in life expectancy in history, far exceeding anything achieved by treatment. Prevention also delivers something treatment cannot, since a person who never develops a disease <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps the years rather than surviving them</strong>.\n\nThe opposing consideration is that treatment cannot be reduced. People are ill now, and a government that redirected money from hospitals to campaigns would be abandoning them; illness also arrives through accident, infection and inheritance, none of which any lifestyle prevents. There is a further difficulty in that prevention pays off over decades while ministers are judged over years, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the savings appear long after the spending</strong>.\n\nThe strongest objection concerns what prevention means. Advice and campaigns achieve very little on their own and tend to reach the people already healthy, which widens the gap between rich and poor rather than closing it. What works is structural: taxes, regulation of advertising, safe places to walk and cycle, decent housing and school meals.\n\nIn conclusion, prevention protects more lives per pound than treatment and should receive a larger and protected share of health spending. Treatment must be funded fully alongside it, and prevention must mean <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changing conditions rather than issuing advice</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"keeps the years rather than surviving them","context":"The argument for prevention is mainly arithmetic. A large proportion of the conditions filling hospitals — type 2 diabetes, heart disease, several cancers, lung disease — is driven by smoking, diet, inactivity and alcohol, and preventing a case costs a fraction of treating one for decades. Measures such as vaccination, clean water, tobacco control and sugar levies have produced the largest gains in life expectancy in history, far exceeding anything achieved by treatment. Prevention also delivers something treatment cannot, since a person who never develops a disease keeps the years rather than surviving them."},{"en":"the savings appear long after the spending","context":"The opposing consideration is that treatment cannot be reduced. People are ill now, and a government that redirected money from hospitals to campaigns would be abandoning them; illness also arrives through accident, infection and inheritance, none of which any lifestyle prevents. There is a further difficulty in that prevention pays off over decades while ministers are judged over years, so the savings appear long after the spending."},{"en":"changing conditions rather than issuing advice","context":"In conclusion, prevention protects more lives per pound than treatment and should receive a larger and protected share of health spending. Treatment must be funded fully alongside it, and prevention must mean changing conditions rather than issuing advice."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#236","summary":"Whether public money is better spent preventing illness than treating it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2323","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"The rise of cosmetic surgery","prompt":"An increasing number of people are choosing to have cosmetic surgery in order to improve their appearance. Why are more people choosing to change the way they look? Do you think this is a positive or negative trend?","answer":"Procedures that were once rare and expensive are now advertised beside dental work. Several forces explain the increase, and although the freedom involved is genuine, I regard the trend as largely negative.\n\nThe first reason is exposure. People used to compare themselves with those they met; now they compare themselves with edited images seen hundreds of times a day, and filters have made everyone familiar with a version of their own face that does not exist. Video calls have added the strange experience of watching yourself while talking. The second reason is access: treatments have become cheaper, quicker and less invasive, and clinics offer payment by instalment, so surgery has moved from a decision to a purchase. The third is that appearance carries measurable rewards in hiring and dating, which makes the spending feel rational.\n\nSome outcomes are clearly positive. Reconstruction after burns, accidents or illness restores lives, and an adult who has been troubled by a single feature for decades may gain real confidence. Choosing what to do with your own body is also a reasonable freedom.\n\nThe wider effects are harder to defend. Surgery treats a feeling of inadequacy by changing the body, and the feeling frequently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moves to the next feature instead of disappearing</strong>. Every procedure narrows the range of faces that look normal, which increases the pressure on everyone else, and the age of patients keeps falling. Regulation lags behind demand, so unqualified practitioners operate freely, and complications from cheap treatment abroad now occupy public hospitals.\n\nIn conclusion, cosmetic surgery has spread because ideal images are unavoidable and treatment is easy to buy. It restores some people and helps a few, but it mostly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">solves a problem created by the images themselves</strong>, and for that reason the trend is a negative one.","phrases":[{"en":"moves to the next feature instead of disappearing","context":"The wider effects are harder to defend. Surgery treats a feeling of inadequacy by changing the body, and the feeling frequently moves to the next feature instead of disappearing. Every procedure narrows the range of faces that look normal, which increases the pressure on everyone else, and the age of patients keeps falling. Regulation lags behind demand, so unqualified practitioners operate freely, and complications from cheap treatment abroad now occupy public hospitals."},{"en":"solves a problem created by the images themselves","context":"In conclusion, cosmetic surgery has spread because ideal images are unavoidable and treatment is easy to buy. It restores some people and helps a few, but it mostly solves a problem created by the images themselves, and for that reason the trend is a negative one."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#237","summary":"Why more people alter their appearance surgically and whether this is positive"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2324","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Looking up symptoms instead of seeing a doctor","prompt":"Nowadays, many people use the Internet to get medical advice instead of going to see a doctor. Why is this? Do you think it is a positive or negative development?","answer":"Most people now search their symptoms before deciding whether to book an appointment. The reasons are largely practical, and the development is positive if patients use the Internet to prepare for a consultation rather than to replace one.\n\nThe main cause is difficulty of access. Waiting weeks for an appointment, or paying for one, makes a free answer in ten seconds extremely attractive, and shift workers and parents of small children often cannot attend during opening hours at all. Embarrassment plays a part too, since people will type a question about a rash or a mental health symptom that they would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hesitate to say aloud to a stranger</strong>. There is also a rise in the wish to understand rather than simply obey, and patients arrive wanting to discuss options instead of receiving instructions.\n\nThe benefits are real. Reliable health sites explain conditions clearly and repeatedly, which no appointment allows, and a well-informed patient asks better questions and follows treatment more faithfully. Online guidance also prevents unnecessary visits for minor complaints and, in remote areas, is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only medical information available at all</strong>. Support communities help people with rare conditions find others in the same position.\n\nThe dangers are equally clear. Search results are not sorted by accuracy, and commercial sites sell remedies while pretending to inform. Serious illness is missed when a symptom is explained away, and the opposite error is common too: ordinary tiredness read as something grave produces <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anxiety out of all proportion to the evidence</strong>. Nothing online can examine a patient, which is where diagnosis actually happens.\n\nIn conclusion, people search because care is slow, costly or awkward to obtain, and good information genuinely helps them. The development is positive only where the Internet <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prepares the patient rather than replaces the examination</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"hesitate to say aloud to a stranger","context":"The main cause is difficulty of access. Waiting weeks for an appointment, or paying for one, makes a free answer in ten seconds extremely attractive, and shift workers and parents of small children often cannot attend during opening hours at all. Embarrassment plays a part too, since people will type a question about a rash or a mental health symptom that they would hesitate to say aloud to a stranger. There is also a rise in the wish to understand rather than simply obey, and patients arrive wanting to discuss options instead of receiving instructions."},{"en":"the only medical information available at all","context":"The benefits are real. Reliable health sites explain conditions clearly and repeatedly, which no appointment allows, and a well-informed patient asks better questions and follows treatment more faithfully. Online guidance also prevents unnecessary visits for minor complaints and, in remote areas, is the only medical information available at all. Support communities help people with rare conditions find others in the same position."},{"en":"anxiety out of all proportion to the evidence","context":"The dangers are equally clear. Search results are not sorted by accuracy, and commercial sites sell remedies while pretending to inform. Serious illness is missed when a symptom is explained away, and the opposite error is common too: ordinary tiredness read as something grave produces anxiety out of all proportion to the evidence. Nothing online can examine a patient, which is where diagnosis actually happens."},{"en":"prepares the patient rather than replaces the examination","context":"In conclusion, people search because care is slow, costly or awkward to obtain, and good information genuinely helps them. The development is positive only where the Internet prepares the patient rather than replaces the examination."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#238","summary":"Why people seek medical advice online and whether this is a good development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2373","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Health","title":"Why older patients ignore exercise advice","prompt":"Many doctors recommend that older people should take regular exercise, but most patients do not follow this advice. What are the reasons for this? What can be done to solve this problem?","answer":"Doctors know that movement does more for an ageing body than most medication, yet the advice is rarely acted on. The reasons are practical and psychological rather than a lack of willingness, and the remedies have to remove the obstacles rather than repeat the instruction.\n\nFear is the first barrier. Older patients worry about falling, about pain and about making an existing condition worse, and told simply to exercise they have no idea what is safe, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">doing nothing feels like the cautious choice</strong>. Vague advice makes this worse: an instruction to be more active carries no starting point, no amount and no way of knowing whether it is working.\n\nCircumstances add to it. Many older people live with arthritis or breathlessness that makes the first weeks genuinely unpleasant, and the benefits arrive months later while the discomfort arrives immediately. Facilities are often unsuitable, since gyms are designed for the young and can be intimidating, and getting to one may be impossible without a car. Cost matters on a pension. There is also a belief, still common, that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slowing down is simply what age requires</strong>, and those who exercise alone find little to sustain the habit.\n\nThe solutions follow directly. Doctors should prescribe activity as precisely as medicine, naming the type, the amount and the progression, and refer patients to supervised classes rather than leaving them to arrange it. Programmes designed for older bodies — chair exercises, walking groups, swimming — work far better than general facilities, and free or subsidised places in community centres remove the cost. Group classes matter most, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the company is what brings people back</strong>, and families and local councils can help by making pavements, benches and safe routes available.\n\nIn conclusion, older patients ignore the advice because it is vague, frightening and hard to act on. Specific prescriptions, supervised group classes and facilities built for their age rather than borrowed from the young would change that.","phrases":[{"en":"doing nothing feels like the cautious choice","context":"Fear is the first barrier. Older patients worry about falling, about pain and about making an existing condition worse, and told simply to exercise they have no idea what is safe, so doing nothing feels like the cautious choice. Vague advice makes this worse: an instruction to be more active carries no starting point, no amount and no way of knowing whether it is working."},{"en":"slowing down is simply what age requires","context":"Circumstances add to it. Many older people live with arthritis or breathlessness that makes the first weeks genuinely unpleasant, and the benefits arrive months later while the discomfort arrives immediately. Facilities are often unsuitable, since gyms are designed for the young and can be intimidating, and getting to one may be impossible without a car. Cost matters on a pension. There is also a belief, still common, that slowing down is simply what age requires, and those who exercise alone find little to sustain the habit."},{"en":"the company is what brings people back","context":"The solutions follow directly. Doctors should prescribe activity as precisely as medicine, naming the type, the amount and the progression, and refer patients to supervised classes rather than leaving them to arrange it. Programmes designed for older bodies — chair exercises, walking groups, swimming — work far better than general facilities, and free or subsidised places in community centres remove the cost. Group classes matter most, because the company is what brings people back, and families and local councils can help by making pavements, benches and safe routes available."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#239","summary":"Why older people do not follow advice to exercise and what would help"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"A house or an apartment?","prompt":"Some people prefer to live in a house, while others feel that there are more advantages to living in an apartment. Are there more advantages than disadvantages of living in a house compared with living in an apartment?","answer":"Both houses and apartments can provide a comfortable home, but they suit different priorities. In my view, a house offers more space and privacy, while an apartment is usually more convenient and affordable. For many urban residents, the advantages of apartment living are greater.\n\nA house is attractive because it gives residents more control over their property. Families may have extra bedrooms, a garden and space for children or pets. Noise from neighbours is also less likely to be a problem. These benefits make a house especially suitable for larger families who value privacy and long-term stability.\n\nHowever, houses often cost more to buy, heat and maintain. They may also be located far from city centres, which increases commuting time. Apartments generally require less maintenance and are often close to jobs, shops and public transport. Modern buildings may also provide security, parking and shared facilities. For a single person or a small family, this can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce both cost and daily inconvenience</strong>.\n\nA further consideration is how long people expect to stay in one place. Young adults change jobs and cities frequently, and an apartment can be let or sold far more quickly, which makes it a sensible choice at the start of a career. A house, by contrast, is a commitment of ten or twenty years, and buyers who overestimate how settled they are may find that it is not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most economical choice over the long term</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, houses provide space and independence, but apartments offer practical advantages that are particularly important in crowded cities. The better choice depends on family size and income, although apartment living is likely to be more suitable for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a larger proportion of</strong> urban residents because it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes efficient use of limited space</strong>.","topic":"Housing","phrases":[{"en":"reduce both cost and daily inconvenience","context":"However, houses often cost more to buy, heat and maintain. They may also be located far from city centres, which increases commuting time. Apartments generally require less maintenance and are often close to jobs, shops and public transport. Modern buildings may also provide security, parking and shared facilities. For a single person or a small family, this can reduce both cost and daily inconvenience."},{"en":"the most economical choice over the long term","context":"A further consideration is how long people expect to stay in one place. Young adults change jobs and cities frequently, and an apartment can be let or sold far more quickly, which makes it a sensible choice at the start of a career. A house, by contrast, is a commitment of ten or twenty years, and buyers who overestimate how settled they are may find that it is not the most economical choice over the long term."},{"en":"a larger proportion of","context":"In conclusion, houses provide space and independence, but apartments offer practical advantages that are particularly important in crowded cities. The better choice depends on family size and income, although apartment living is likely to be more suitable for a larger proportion of urban residents because it makes efficient use of limited space."},{"en":"makes efficient use of limited space","context":"In conclusion, houses provide space and independence, but apartments offer practical advantages that are particularly important in crowded cities. The better choice depends on family size and income, although apartment living is likely to be more suitable for a larger proportion of urban residents because it makes efficient use of limited space."}],"subtype":"two-part","summary":"Living in a house compared with living in an apartment","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-014","_displayNo":"#240"},{"id":"writing-task-2-132","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Housing","title":"More people living alone","prompt":"In some countries, many more people are choosing to live alone nowadays than in the past. Do you think this is a positive or negative development?","answer":"In recent years it has become far more normal for people to live alone, particularly in large cities in the developed world. In my opinion, this trend brings genuine benefits at a personal and economic level, but it also carries a real social and financial cost.\n\nThe rise in one-person households can be seen as positive for both personal and economic reasons. On an individual level, people who live alone tend to become more independent and self-reliant than those who share a home with family, since there is no one else to cook, clean, pay the bills or manage the budget for them. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">From an economic perspective</strong>, the same trend creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">greater demand for housing</strong>, which benefits the construction industry, estate agents and the many businesses that rely on people furnishing a home of their own.\n\nHowever, the personal and economic arguments above can be viewed from the opposite angle. Rather than feeling more independent, people who live alone may instead experience loneliness, isolation and worry, since they miss out on the emotional support and daily conversation that family or flatmates would normally provide. They must also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">bear the weight of every household bill</strong> and decision without anyone to share it.\n\nThe same reversal applies to the economic argument: greater demand for housing tends to push up property prices and rents, so while the construction industry and estate agents may gain, the wider population, including people who live alone, ends up facing higher housing costs than before.\n\nIn conclusion, living alone offers genuine personal and economic benefits, but these come paired with a real emotional and financial cost. Whether the trend is ultimately positive probably depends on whether it is freely chosen: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independence that people actively want</strong> looks very different from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an isolation they were simply unable to avoid</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"From an economic perspective","context":"The rise in one-person households can be seen as positive for both personal and economic reasons. On an individual level, people who live alone tend to become more independent and self-reliant than those who share a home with family, since there is no one else to cook, clean, pay the bills or manage the budget for them. From an economic perspective, the same trend creates greater demand for housing, which benefits the construction industry, estate agents and the many businesses that rely on people furnishing a home of their own."},{"en":"greater demand for housing","context":"The rise in one-person households can be seen as positive for both personal and economic reasons. On an individual level, people who live alone tend to become more independent and self-reliant than those who share a home with family, since there is no one else to cook, clean, pay the bills or manage the budget for them. From an economic perspective, the same trend creates greater demand for housing, which benefits the construction industry, estate agents and the many businesses that rely on people furnishing a home of their own."},{"en":"bear the weight of every household bill","context":"However, the personal and economic arguments above can be viewed from the opposite angle. Rather than feeling more independent, people who live alone may instead experience loneliness, isolation and worry, since they miss out on the emotional support and daily conversation that family or flatmates would normally provide. They must also bear the weight of every household bill and decision without anyone to share it."},{"en":"independence that people actively want","context":"In conclusion, living alone offers genuine personal and economic benefits, but these come paired with a real emotional and financial cost. Whether the trend is ultimately positive probably depends on whether it is freely chosen: independence that people actively want looks very different from an isolation they were simply unable to avoid."},{"en":"an isolation they were simply unable to avoid","context":"In conclusion, living alone offers genuine personal and economic benefits, but these come paired with a real emotional and financial cost. Whether the trend is ultimately positive probably depends on whether it is freely chosen: independence that people actively want looks very different from an isolation they were simply unable to avoid."}],"subtype":"Positive / Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#241","summary":"The rise of one-person households"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2246","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Housing","title":"Should housing be provided free?","prompt":"Housing is a basic necessity for everyone. Therefore, the government should provide free housing for people who cannot afford to pay for it. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Shelter is as fundamental as food and medical care, and it is argued that governments should therefore house those who cannot house themselves. I largely agree, though I believe subsidised housing is a better instrument than housing provided at no cost at all.\n\nThe strongest argument is that homelessness is more expensive than housing. A person sleeping outside uses emergency services repeatedly, is admitted to hospital far more often and cannot hold a job without an address, and several cities that simply gave permanent housing to long-term rough sleepers found that the total public cost <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fell rather than rose</strong>. Housing is also the condition for everything else: children in temporary accommodation change schools repeatedly and fall behind, so the cost of not acting <strong class=\"key-phrase\">is paid by the next generation</strong>.\n\nThere is a market argument as well. In cities where private rents have risen far faster than wages, nurses, cleaners and bus drivers cannot afford to live where they work, and a city that cannot house the people who run it will eventually fail to function.\n\nMy reservation concerns the word free. Housing that costs nothing is expensive to maintain, tends to be built cheaply and can trap tenants, since taking a job may mean losing the home. Systems that work best charge rent capped as a proportion of income, so that a tenant contributes what they can and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keeps the gain from earning more</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that governments must house people who cannot house themselves, because the alternative costs more and damages children permanently. The provision should be secure and affordable rather than free, so that it supports people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without discouraging work</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"fell rather than rose","context":"The strongest argument is that homelessness is more expensive than housing. A person sleeping outside uses emergency services repeatedly, is admitted to hospital far more often and cannot hold a job without an address, and several cities that simply gave permanent housing to long-term rough sleepers found that the total public cost fell rather than rose. Housing is also the condition for everything else: children in temporary accommodation change schools repeatedly and fall behind, so the cost of not acting is paid by the next generation."},{"en":"is paid by the next generation","context":"The strongest argument is that homelessness is more expensive than housing. A person sleeping outside uses emergency services repeatedly, is admitted to hospital far more often and cannot hold a job without an address, and several cities that simply gave permanent housing to long-term rough sleepers found that the total public cost fell rather than rose. Housing is also the condition for everything else: children in temporary accommodation change schools repeatedly and fall behind, so the cost of not acting is paid by the next generation."},{"en":"keeps the gain from earning more","context":"My reservation concerns the word free. Housing that costs nothing is expensive to maintain, tends to be built cheaply and can trap tenants, since taking a job may mean losing the home. Systems that work best charge rent capped as a proportion of income, so that a tenant contributes what they can and keeps the gain from earning more."},{"en":"without discouraging work","context":"In conclusion, I agree that governments must house people who cannot house themselves, because the alternative costs more and damages children permanently. The provision should be secure and affordable rather than free, so that it supports people without discouraging work."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#242","summary":"Government provision of free housing for those who cannot afford it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2362","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Housing","title":"Homes without gardens","prompt":"With a growing population in cities, more and more people live in homes with small outdoor areas or none at all. Is this a positive or negative trend?","answer":"As cities grow upwards, the private garden is disappearing from ordinary housing. I regard the trend as negative for the people living in these homes, although the density that causes it brings real advantages to the city as a whole.\n\nThe case in its favour is worth setting out. Building flats rather than houses allows far more people to live close to work, shops and public transport, which shortens journeys, reduces car use and protects farmland from being covered. Small homes are also cheaper to heat and to maintain, and a household without a garden gains hours each month that would have gone on mowing and repairs. For many young workers, a small flat in the centre beats a house they cannot reach.\n\nThe losses are nonetheless serious. A private outdoor space is where small children play unsupervised, where families eat in summer and where people grow things, and none of these transfers easily to a public park several streets away. Access to greenery affects sleep, stress and recovery from illness, and the difference shows in health statistics between districts. Children in homes without gardens are less physically active, because going outside becomes an expedition rather than a decision. The pandemic demonstrated the cost sharply, when families confined to small flats suffered far more than those with a step outside.\n\nThere is also a fairness problem. Gardens have not vanished; they have become expensive, so outdoor space is quietly turning into <strong class=\"key-phrase\">something only wealthier households can buy</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, dense housing serves the city by shortening journeys and preserving land, and it is not the density that causes the harm. The loss of any private outdoor space damages health and childhood, so cities should require balconies, courtyards and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">parks within a few minutes of every door</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"something only wealthier households can buy","context":"There is also a fairness problem. Gardens have not vanished; they have become expensive, so outdoor space is quietly turning into something only wealthier households can buy."},{"en":"parks within a few minutes of every door","context":"In conclusion, dense housing serves the city by shortening journeys and preserving land, and it is not the density that causes the harm. The loss of any private outdoor space damages health and childhood, so cities should require balconies, courtyards and parks within a few minutes of every door."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#243","summary":"Whether living in homes with little or no outdoor space is a good trend"},{"id":"writing-task-2-031","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"English as a global language","prompt":"The advantages of the spread of English as a global language will continue\nto outweigh its disadvantages. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"English has become the default language of science, international business and diplomacy, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an increasing number of</strong> countries now teach it from primary school. Opinion is divided about whether this trend helps or harms the world. In my view the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, chiefly because a shared language lets people who would otherwise have no way to communicate trade and collaborate directly.\n\nThe practical benefits are considerable. A conference of scientists or engineers from a dozen countries can function efficiently only because there is one common working language, and multinational companies rely on English to move essential information between offices in different countries without the delay and expense of translation. Researchers who read English fluently also reach new findings years before a translation would ever appear.\n\nThe clearest cost is to smaller languages, not major national ones. Where a minority language coexists with a national or global one, it is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">language proficiency</strong> in the dominant tongue, not the local one, that helps a career, so parents increasingly speak the dominant language at home instead, even though nothing forces the choice. UNESCO estimates that a language disappears roughly every two weeks, and most of those still spoken are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on the verge of extinction</strong> because English and a handful of other dominant languages have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">made them commercially irrelevant</strong>.\n\nThere is a related worry about cultural diversity: because so much of the world’s news, film and academic publishing appears in English first, audiences everywhere absorb <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a disproportionate share of content</strong> shaped by British and American assumptions about which stories are worth telling and how they should be told. This is a genuine loss, and countries are right to keep investing in media and education in their own languages rather than treating English content as an adequate substitute. On balance, however, I still think the trade is worthwhile: the ability of people who share no other language to trade, research and simply talk to one another outweighs the cultural cost, provided that cost is actively managed rather than ignored.","phrases":[{"en":"an increasing number of","context":"English has become the default language of science, international business and diplomacy, and an increasing number of countries now teach it from primary school. Opinion is divided about whether this trend helps or harms the world. In my view the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, chiefly because a shared language lets people who would otherwise have no way to communicate trade and collaborate directly."},{"en":"language proficiency","context":"The clearest cost is to smaller languages, not major national ones. Where a minority language coexists with a national or global one, it is language proficiency in the dominant tongue, not the local one, that helps a career, so parents increasingly speak the dominant language at home instead, even though nothing forces the choice. UNESCO estimates that a language disappears roughly every two weeks, and most of those still spoken are on the verge of extinction because English and a handful of other dominant languages have made them commercially irrelevant."},{"en":"on the verge of extinction","context":"The clearest cost is to smaller languages, not major national ones. Where a minority language coexists with a national or global one, it is language proficiency in the dominant tongue, not the local one, that helps a career, so parents increasingly speak the dominant language at home instead, even though nothing forces the choice. UNESCO estimates that a language disappears roughly every two weeks, and most of those still spoken are on the verge of extinction because English and a handful of other dominant languages have made them commercially irrelevant."},{"en":"made them commercially irrelevant","context":"The clearest cost is to smaller languages, not major national ones. Where a minority language coexists with a national or global one, it is language proficiency in the dominant tongue, not the local one, that helps a career, so parents increasingly speak the dominant language at home instead, even though nothing forces the choice. UNESCO estimates that a language disappears roughly every two weeks, and most of those still spoken are on the verge of extinction because English and a handful of other dominant languages have made them commercially irrelevant."},{"en":"a disproportionate share of content","context":"There is a related worry about cultural diversity: because so much of the world’s news, film and academic publishing appears in English first, audiences everywhere absorb a disproportionate share of content shaped by British and American assumptions about which stories are worth telling and how they should be told. This is a genuine loss, and countries are right to keep investing in media and education in their own languages rather than treating English content as an adequate substitute. On balance, however, I still think the trade is worthwhile: the ability of people who share no other language to trade, research and simply talk to one another outweighs the cultural cost, provided that cost is actively managed rather than ignored."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#244","summary":"Advantages of the spread of English as a global language will continue to…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-040","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"Learning a language means learning a culture","prompt":"Some people argue that learning a second language necessarily involves learning about the culture of the countries where it is spoken. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people believe that it is impossible to learn a foreign language properly without also studying the culture of the countries in which it is spoken. I largely agree with this view, although the amount of cultural knowledge required depends on why the language is being learned.\n\nLanguage and culture are difficult to separate because meaning depends on context. Idioms, humour, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">levels of politeness and forms of address</strong> all reflect how a society thinks, and a learner who ignores them will produce sentences that are grammatically correct but socially wrong. A student who translates directly from Vietnamese into English, for example, may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sound abrupt or excessively formal</strong> without realising it. Cultural knowledge also aids memory, since vocabulary met in films, songs and real situations is retained far better than words <strong class=\"key-phrase\">memorised from a list</strong>.\n\nCulture also supplies the motivation that long-term study requires. Most people who reach a high level have developed a genuine interest in the literature, music or history of the language they are learning, and this is what carries them through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the difficult intermediate stage</strong>.\n\nI would qualify this in one respect. English is now used mainly between people who are not native speakers, so an engineer writing technical reports needs clarity and accuracy far more than familiarity with British or American customs. For such learners, cultural study is useful rather than essential.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that culture is an important part of language learning for anyone who wants to communicate naturally, but it matters much less for those who need a language purely as a professional tool.","phrases":[{"en":"levels of politeness and forms of address","context":"Language and culture are difficult to separate because meaning depends on context. Idioms, humour, levels of politeness and forms of address all reflect how a society thinks, and a learner who ignores them will produce sentences that are grammatically correct but socially wrong. A student who translates directly from Vietnamese into English, for example, may sound abrupt or excessively formal without realising it. Cultural knowledge also aids memory, since vocabulary met in films, songs and real situations is retained far better than words memorised from a list."},{"en":"sound abrupt or excessively formal","context":"Language and culture are difficult to separate because meaning depends on context. Idioms, humour, levels of politeness and forms of address all reflect how a society thinks, and a learner who ignores them will produce sentences that are grammatically correct but socially wrong. A student who translates directly from Vietnamese into English, for example, may sound abrupt or excessively formal without realising it. Cultural knowledge also aids memory, since vocabulary met in films, songs and real situations is retained far better than words memorised from a list."},{"en":"memorised from a list","context":"Language and culture are difficult to separate because meaning depends on context. Idioms, humour, levels of politeness and forms of address all reflect how a society thinks, and a learner who ignores them will produce sentences that are grammatically correct but socially wrong. A student who translates directly from Vietnamese into English, for example, may sound abrupt or excessively formal without realising it. Cultural knowledge also aids memory, since vocabulary met in films, songs and real situations is retained far better than words memorised from a list."},{"en":"the difficult intermediate stage","context":"Culture also supplies the motivation that long-term study requires. Most people who reach a high level have developed a genuine interest in the literature, music or history of the language they are learning, and this is what carries them through the difficult intermediate stage."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#245","summary":"Learning a language means learning a culture"},{"id":"writing-task-2-133","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"Should public money save dying languages?","prompt":"Several languages are in danger of extinction because they are spoken by very small numbers of people. Some people say that governments should spend public money on saving these languages, while others believe that would be a waste of money. Discuss both these views and give your opinion.","answer":"It is true that some minority languages may disappear in the near future. Although it can be argued that governments could save money by allowing this to happen, I believe that these languages should be protected and preserved.\n\nThere are several reasons why saving minority languages could be seen as a waste of money. Teaching a language to more people than currently speak it requires building facilities, training teachers and running <strong class=\"key-phrase\">years of sustained marketing</strong>, and that money might arguably serve the public better if it were spent on hospitals, schools or roads instead, especially when the language in question has only a few thousand remaining speakers. Running a country in a single language would also be simpler and cheaper, since ministries would no longer need to translate official documents, court proceedings and public signage for each minority group separately.\n\nDespite the above arguments, I believe that governments should try to preserve languages that are less widely spoken. A language is much more than simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a means of communication</strong>; it has a vital connection with the cultural identity of the people who speak it. If a language disappears, a whole way of life will disappear with it, and we will lose the rich cultural diversity that makes societies more interesting. By spending money to protect minority languages, governments can also preserve traditions, customs and behaviours that are part of a country&#x27;s history.\n\nIn conclusion, it may save money in the short term if we allow minority languages to disappear, but in the long term this would have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an extremely negative impact on</strong> our cultural heritage.","phrases":[{"en":"years of sustained marketing","context":"There are several reasons why saving minority languages could be seen as a waste of money. Teaching a language to more people than currently speak it requires building facilities, training teachers and running years of sustained marketing, and that money might arguably serve the public better if it were spent on hospitals, schools or roads instead, especially when the language in question has only a few thousand remaining speakers. Running a country in a single language would also be simpler and cheaper, since ministries would no longer need to translate official documents, court proceedings and public signage for each minority group separately."},{"en":"a means of communication","context":"Despite the above arguments, I believe that governments should try to preserve languages that are less widely spoken. A language is much more than simply a means of communication; it has a vital connection with the cultural identity of the people who speak it. If a language disappears, a whole way of life will disappear with it, and we will lose the rich cultural diversity that makes societies more interesting. By spending money to protect minority languages, governments can also preserve traditions, customs and behaviours that are part of a country's history."},{"en":"an extremely negative impact on","context":"In conclusion, it may save money in the short term if we allow minority languages to disappear, but in the long term this would have an extremely negative impact on our cultural heritage."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#246","summary":"Protecting endangered languages"},{"id":"writing-task-2-174","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"Will the skill of writing disappear?","prompt":"As the use of mobile phones and computers increases, fewer people write letters. Some people believe that the skill of writing will disappear completely. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"As mobile phones and computers have replaced handwritten letters, some people predict that the ability to write will eventually disappear. I disagree with this view, because technology has changed how we write rather than whether we write at all.\n\nThere is no doubt that certain forms of writing are dying out. Personal letters are now rare, handwriting is deteriorating because most people use a keyboard, and features such as voice messages and predictive text allow users to communicate with very little effort. Schools in several countries have already reduced the time spent teaching cursive script, and some have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removed it from the curriculum altogether</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, most people now write far more than any previous generation did. A typical office worker produces dozens of emails, reports and messages every day, and social media consists almost entirely of written text. The medium has changed, but the underlying skill of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">organising an idea and expressing it clearly</strong> is required far more often than it was when letters were posted once a month.\n\nThat skill also remains essential where it matters most. Contracts, medical records, academic papers, journalism and job applications all depend on precise writing, and employers regularly identify it as the ability they find <strong class=\"key-phrase\">most lacking in graduates</strong>. Voice technology cannot replace it, because a spoken sentence and a written argument are structured quite differently.\n\nIn conclusion, handwriting and traditional letters are certainly in decline, and that particular loss is worth regretting. I do not believe, however, that writing itself will disappear. If anything, digital communication has made the ability to write clearly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more valuable than before</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"removed it from the curriculum altogether","context":"There is no doubt that certain forms of writing are dying out. Personal letters are now rare, handwriting is deteriorating because most people use a keyboard, and features such as voice messages and predictive text allow users to communicate with very little effort. Schools in several countries have already reduced the time spent teaching cursive script, and some have removed it from the curriculum altogether."},{"en":"organising an idea and expressing it clearly","context":"Nevertheless, most people now write far more than any previous generation did. A typical office worker produces dozens of emails, reports and messages every day, and social media consists almost entirely of written text. The medium has changed, but the underlying skill of organising an idea and expressing it clearly is required far more often than it was when letters were posted once a month."},{"en":"most lacking in graduates","context":"That skill also remains essential where it matters most. Contracts, medical records, academic papers, journalism and job applications all depend on precise writing, and employers regularly identify it as the ability they find most lacking in graduates. Voice technology cannot replace it, because a spoken sentence and a written argument are structured quite differently."},{"en":"more valuable than before","context":"In conclusion, handwriting and traditional letters are certainly in decline, and that particular loss is worth regretting. I do not believe, however, that writing itself will disappear. If anything, digital communication has made the ability to write clearly more valuable than before."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#247","summary":"Will the skill of writing disappear?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2287","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"Must every child learn a foreign language?","prompt":"Because some children do not seem to have a natural ability to learn another language, schools should not force those children to study a foreign language. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Every class contains a few students who make almost no progress in a second language despite years of lessons. I disagree that they should be excused, because the argument rests on a false view of how languages are learned.\n\nThe case for excusing them is not absurd. Time spent failing at a subject is time taken from one where the same student might succeed, and repeated failure damages confidence in a way that spreads to other lessons. Where a child struggles with literacy in their first language, adding a second can reasonably be delayed.\n\nThe reasoning nevertheless fails at its foundation. Almost every human being acquires a language successfully as a child, which shows the capacity is general rather than exceptional. What differs is not ability but the method and the amount of exposure: classroom teaching of grammar for three hours a week suits some learners and defeats many others, and a student written off as untalented frequently makes rapid progress once immersed in the language or taught differently. Judging a twelve-year-old to have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no aptitude for something they have barely practised</strong> is a prediction dressed as a diagnosis.\n\nThe consequences of excusing them would also be unequal. Decisions of that kind fall disproportionately on children from poorer families, whose difficulty is usually a lack of exposure rather than of capacity, and removing them from language study <strong class=\"key-phrase\">closes doors they do not yet know exist</strong>, since languages affect university options and employment abroad. The wider benefits would be lost too: learning a second language improves understanding of one&#x27;s own, and it teaches that other ways of describing the world are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">different rather than wrong</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, no child should be released from language study on the basis of apparent talent. Where progress is poor the sensible response is to change the teaching, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the failure usually belongs to the method</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"no aptitude for something they have barely practised","context":"The reasoning nevertheless fails at its foundation. Almost every human being acquires a language successfully as a child, which shows the capacity is general rather than exceptional. What differs is not ability but the method and the amount of exposure: classroom teaching of grammar for three hours a week suits some learners and defeats many others, and a student written off as untalented frequently makes rapid progress once immersed in the language or taught differently. Judging a twelve-year-old to have no aptitude for something they have barely practised is a prediction dressed as a diagnosis."},{"en":"closes doors they do not yet know exist","context":"The consequences of excusing them would also be unequal. Decisions of that kind fall disproportionately on children from poorer families, whose difficulty is usually a lack of exposure rather than of capacity, and removing them from language study closes doors they do not yet know exist, since languages affect university options and employment abroad. The wider benefits would be lost too: learning a second language improves understanding of one's own, and it teaches that other ways of describing the world are different rather than wrong."},{"en":"different rather than wrong","context":"The consequences of excusing them would also be unequal. Decisions of that kind fall disproportionately on children from poorer families, whose difficulty is usually a lack of exposure rather than of capacity, and removing them from language study closes doors they do not yet know exist, since languages affect university options and employment abroad. The wider benefits would be lost too: learning a second language improves understanding of one's own, and it teaches that other ways of describing the world are different rather than wrong."},{"en":"the failure usually belongs to the method","context":"In conclusion, no child should be released from language study on the basis of apparent talent. Where progress is poor the sensible response is to change the teaching, since the failure usually belongs to the method."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#248","summary":"Whether children without aptitude should be excused from language study"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2350","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Language & Communication","title":"Are screens damaging literacy?","prompt":"There is a view that the widespread use of computers and mobile phones has contributed to a decline in reading and writing abilities among young people. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Teachers and employers regularly complain that young people cannot write a paragraph or read a long text. I partly agree that devices are involved, but the problem concerns stamina and register rather than literacy itself.\n\nYoung people today probably read and write more words than any earlier generation, since messaging, posts and comments are all text. What has changed is the shape of it. Screens train the eye to scan for the useful line and move on, which works for a notice and fails for an argument, so students struggle with anything requiring <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sustained attention on a single page</strong>. Writing has shifted in the same direction: quick exchanges reward brevity and speed, and formal structure is used so rarely that it is never practised. Predictive text and automatic correction remove the effort that once fixed spelling in the memory, and now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">errors are corrected without ever being noticed</strong>.\n\nThere are reasons for scepticism about the decline, however. Complaints about the writing of the young are centuries old. Measured literacy rates have risen, not fallen, and today&#x27;s students write for real readers rather than only for teachers, which usually improves clarity. Adapting language to the situation is a skill in itself, and a student who writes casually to friends and formally in an examination is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demonstrating range rather than ignorance</strong>.\n\nThe honest conclusion is that devices have changed the balance of practice. Reading many short fragments is not the same exercise as reading a chapter, and schools now have to teach deliberately what used to develop by itself: long reading, planning, and revising a draft.\n\nIn conclusion, phones and computers have not destroyed literacy, and young people use written language constantly. They have weakened concentration and formal writing, so the remedy is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practice at length rather than restrictions on technology</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"sustained attention on a single page","context":"Young people today probably read and write more words than any earlier generation, since messaging, posts and comments are all text. What has changed is the shape of it. Screens train the eye to scan for the useful line and move on, which works for a notice and fails for an argument, so students struggle with anything requiring sustained attention on a single page. Writing has shifted in the same direction: quick exchanges reward brevity and speed, and formal structure is used so rarely that it is never practised. Predictive text and automatic correction remove the effort that once fixed spelling in the memory, and now errors are corrected without ever being noticed."},{"en":"errors are corrected without ever being noticed","context":"Young people today probably read and write more words than any earlier generation, since messaging, posts and comments are all text. What has changed is the shape of it. Screens train the eye to scan for the useful line and move on, which works for a notice and fails for an argument, so students struggle with anything requiring sustained attention on a single page. Writing has shifted in the same direction: quick exchanges reward brevity and speed, and formal structure is used so rarely that it is never practised. Predictive text and automatic correction remove the effort that once fixed spelling in the memory, and now errors are corrected without ever being noticed."},{"en":"demonstrating range rather than ignorance","context":"There are reasons for scepticism about the decline, however. Complaints about the writing of the young are centuries old. Measured literacy rates have risen, not fallen, and today's students write for real readers rather than only for teachers, which usually improves clarity. Adapting language to the situation is a skill in itself, and a student who writes casually to friends and formally in an examination is demonstrating range rather than ignorance."},{"en":"practice at length rather than restrictions on technology","context":"In conclusion, phones and computers have not destroyed literacy, and young people use written language constantly. They have weakened concentration and formal writing, so the remedy is practice at length rather than restrictions on technology."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#249","summary":"Whether computers and phones have weakened young people's reading and writing"},{"id":"writing-task-2-025","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Reporting crime in detail","prompt":"Some people suggest that there should be restrictions on detailed descriptions of crimes in newspapers and on television. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some people believe that newspapers and television should not be allowed to describe crimes in detail. I largely agree, particularly where violent offences are concerned, although a complete restriction would be neither possible nor desirable.\n\nThe main argument for limits is that detailed reporting can encourage further offending. Descriptions of exactly how a fraud was carried out, or how a security system was defeated, amount to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">instructions that others can follow</strong>. There is also evidence of imitation in cases of extreme violence: research on mass shootings has repeatedly found that heavy coverage naming the attacker is followed by an increase in similar attacks, which is why several countries now advise the media not to publish the offender&#x27;s name.\n\nA second concern is the effect on the public. Graphic reporting distresses victims and their families, who are forced to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relive the event</strong> in public, and it distorts the audience&#x27;s sense of risk. Crime is reported far more often than it occurs, which is why people in many countries believe offending is rising when official figures show the opposite, and this fear <strong class=\"key-phrase\">falls most heavily on the elderly</strong>.\n\nHowever, restriction has limits. The press must be free to report what happens, since concealing crime would allow the authorities to hide their own failures, and the public has a right to know about genuine dangers in their area.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that gratuitous detail should be restricted, but reporting itself must remain free. The sensible test is whether a detail <strong class=\"key-phrase\">informs the public or merely entertains it</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"instructions that others can follow","context":"The main argument for limits is that detailed reporting can encourage further offending. Descriptions of exactly how a fraud was carried out, or how a security system was defeated, amount to instructions that others can follow. There is also evidence of imitation in cases of extreme violence: research on mass shootings has repeatedly found that heavy coverage naming the attacker is followed by an increase in similar attacks, which is why several countries now advise the media not to publish the offender's name."},{"en":"relive the event","context":"A second concern is the effect on the public. Graphic reporting distresses victims and their families, who are forced to relive the event in public, and it distorts the audience's sense of risk. Crime is reported far more often than it occurs, which is why people in many countries believe offending is rising when official figures show the opposite, and this fear falls most heavily on the elderly."},{"en":"falls most heavily on the elderly","context":"A second concern is the effect on the public. Graphic reporting distresses victims and their families, who are forced to relive the event in public, and it distorts the audience's sense of risk. Crime is reported far more often than it occurs, which is why people in many countries believe offending is rising when official figures show the opposite, and this fear falls most heavily on the elderly."},{"en":"informs the public or merely entertains it","context":"In conclusion, I agree that gratuitous detail should be restricted, but reporting itself must remain free. The sensible test is whether a detail informs the public or merely entertains it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#250","summary":"Reporting crime in detail"},{"id":"writing-task-2-045","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Is the influence of news media negative?","prompt":"Some people think that the news media has a great influence on people's lives today and that this is a negative development. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"There is no doubt that the news media shapes the way people see the world, and some argue that this influence has become harmful. I partly agree: the way news is now produced and consumed has created real problems, although the underlying activity remains valuable.\n\nThe negative effects are easy to identify. Because news organisations compete for attention, they emphasise conflict, crime and disaster, which leaves audiences with a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">distorted impression</strong> of how dangerous their society actually is. Surveys regularly show that people believe crime is rising when official figures show it falling. Online news has made this worse, since algorithms show each user the material they already agree with, and readers gradually come to regard those who disagree as dishonest rather than merely mistaken. The speed of publication also means that unverified claims <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spread widely before they can be corrected</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, the media performs a function that nothing else can replace. Investigative journalism has exposed corruption, unsafe products and abuses of power that would otherwise have remained hidden, and citizens cannot vote sensibly without reliable information about what their government is doing. Coverage of famines and natural disasters has also produced humanitarian assistance <strong class=\"key-phrase\">on an enormous scale</strong>.\n\nThe problem, therefore, is not influence itself but the commercial model that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewards outrage</strong>. Stronger rules on accuracy, together with greater public funding for independent broadcasters who do not depend on advertising revenue, would go a long way towards addressing it.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that current trends in news reporting are damaging, but the media&#x27;s central role in informing the public remains essential.","phrases":[{"en":"distorted impression","context":"The negative effects are easy to identify. Because news organisations compete for attention, they emphasise conflict, crime and disaster, which leaves audiences with a distorted impression of how dangerous their society actually is. Surveys regularly show that people believe crime is rising when official figures show it falling. Online news has made this worse, since algorithms show each user the material they already agree with, and readers gradually come to regard those who disagree as dishonest rather than merely mistaken. The speed of publication also means that unverified claims spread widely before they can be corrected."},{"en":"spread widely before they can be corrected","context":"The negative effects are easy to identify. Because news organisations compete for attention, they emphasise conflict, crime and disaster, which leaves audiences with a distorted impression of how dangerous their society actually is. Surveys regularly show that people believe crime is rising when official figures show it falling. Online news has made this worse, since algorithms show each user the material they already agree with, and readers gradually come to regard those who disagree as dishonest rather than merely mistaken. The speed of publication also means that unverified claims spread widely before they can be corrected."},{"en":"on an enormous scale","context":"Nevertheless, the media performs a function that nothing else can replace. Investigative journalism has exposed corruption, unsafe products and abuses of power that would otherwise have remained hidden, and citizens cannot vote sensibly without reliable information about what their government is doing. Coverage of famines and natural disasters has also produced humanitarian assistance on an enormous scale."},{"en":"rewards outrage","context":"The problem, therefore, is not influence itself but the commercial model that rewards outrage. Stronger rules on accuracy, together with greater public funding for independent broadcasters who do not depend on advertising revenue, would go a long way towards addressing it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#251","summary":"Is the influence of news media negative?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-049","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Global media and cultural diversity","prompt":"When international media (including movies, fashion shows, advertisements and other TV programmes) convey the same messages to the global audience, people argue that the expansion of international media has negative impacts on cultural diversity. What is your opinion?","answer":"International media gives people access to films, music and ideas from around the world. Although this can make popular culture more similar across countries, I do not think it must destroy cultural diversity. The outcome depends mainly on how local communities respond.\n\nThere is a genuine risk of cultural uniformity. Large media companies have the money to promote the same films, celebrities and consumer trends in many markets. Local artists may struggle to compete, and young audiences may start to see foreign lifestyles as more modern or attractive than their own. Over time, this can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weaken local traditions</strong> and reduce demand for regional languages, music and crafts.\n\nHowever, international media can also widen cultural understanding. Streaming platforms allow small producers to reach audiences far beyond their home countries, while viewers can discover stories that were previously unavailable to them. Governments and schools can support this positive effect by funding local content, teaching cultural history and ensuring that national broadcasters include regional voices. In this way, societies can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">engage with global culture</strong> without abandoning their identity.\n\n<strong class=\"key-phrase\">Cultural influence is also rarely one-way</strong>. South Korean pop music, for example, borrows the production techniques and marketing methods of the American entertainment industry, yet its lyrics, choreography and star system remain distinctly Korean, and the genre has pulled millions of new listeners towards Korean language and fashion rather than away from them. Absorbing outside methods does not have to mean losing what is local: a culture confident enough to blend in this way can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compete on more equal terms</strong> with the media companies that once threatened to drown it out.\n\nIn conclusion, global media can place pressure on cultural diversity, but cultural loss is not inevitable. If countries invest in local creative industries and people remain interested in their own heritage, international media can become a channel for exchange rather than a force for uniformity. The aim should be to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">preserve local identity while remaining open</strong> to outside influence.","phrases":[{"en":"weaken local traditions","context":"There is a genuine risk of cultural uniformity. Large media companies have the money to promote the same films, celebrities and consumer trends in many markets. Local artists may struggle to compete, and young audiences may start to see foreign lifestyles as more modern or attractive than their own. Over time, this can weaken local traditions and reduce demand for regional languages, music and crafts."},{"en":"engage with global culture","context":"However, international media can also widen cultural understanding. Streaming platforms allow small producers to reach audiences far beyond their home countries, while viewers can discover stories that were previously unavailable to them. Governments and schools can support this positive effect by funding local content, teaching cultural history and ensuring that national broadcasters include regional voices. In this way, societies can engage with global culture without abandoning their identity."},{"en":"Cultural influence is also rarely one-way","context":"Cultural influence is also rarely one-way. South Korean pop music, for example, borrows the production techniques and marketing methods of the American entertainment industry, yet its lyrics, choreography and star system remain distinctly Korean, and the genre has pulled millions of new listeners towards Korean language and fashion rather than away from them. Absorbing outside methods does not have to mean losing what is local: a culture confident enough to blend in this way can compete on more equal terms with the media companies that once threatened to drown it out."},{"en":"compete on more equal terms","context":"Cultural influence is also rarely one-way. South Korean pop music, for example, borrows the production techniques and marketing methods of the American entertainment industry, yet its lyrics, choreography and star system remain distinctly Korean, and the genre has pulled millions of new listeners towards Korean language and fashion rather than away from them. Absorbing outside methods does not have to mean losing what is local: a culture confident enough to blend in this way can compete on more equal terms with the media companies that once threatened to drown it out."},{"en":"preserve local identity while remaining open","context":"In conclusion, global media can place pressure on cultural diversity, but cultural loss is not inevitable. If countries invest in local creative industries and people remain interested in their own heritage, international media can become a channel for exchange rather than a force for uniformity. The aim should be to preserve local identity while remaining open to outside influence."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#252","summary":"When international media including movies, fashion shows, advertisements…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-135","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Why people prefer foreign films","prompt":"Many people prefer to watch foreign films rather than locally produced films. Why could this be? Should governments give more financial support to local film industries?","answer":"It is true that foreign films are more popular in many countries than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">domestically produced films</strong>. There are several reasons for this, and in my view governments should respond by subsidising the local film industry rather than leaving it to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">compete unaided</strong>.\n\nOne reason is simply budget. The established film industries of a few countries have huge sums available for staging action sequences, generating special effects and filming in spectacular locations, and Hollywood blockbusters such as Avatar or the James Bond films show what that money can buy: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their global appeal is undeniable</strong>.\n\nAnother reason why these big-budget films are so successful is that they often star the most famous actors and actresses, and they are made by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the most accomplished producers and directors</strong>. The poor quality, low-budget filmmaking in many countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suffers in comparison</strong>.\n\nIn my view, governments should support local film industries financially. In every country, there may be talented amateur film-makers who just need to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be given the opportunity to</strong> prove themselves. To compete with big-budget productions from overseas, these people need money to pay for film crews, actors and a host of other costs related to producing high-quality films. If governments did help with these costs, they would see an increase in employment in the film industry, income from film sales, and perhaps even <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a rise in tourist numbers</strong>. New Zealand, for example, has seen an increase in tourism related to the &#x27;Lord of the Rings&#x27; films, which were partly funded by government subsidies.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that increased financial support could help to raise the quality of locally made films and allow them to compete with the foreign productions that currently dominate the market.","phrases":[{"en":"domestically produced films","context":"It is true that foreign films are more popular in many countries than domestically produced films. There are several reasons for this, and in my view governments should respond by subsidising the local film industry rather than leaving it to compete unaided."},{"en":"compete unaided","context":"It is true that foreign films are more popular in many countries than domestically produced films. There are several reasons for this, and in my view governments should respond by subsidising the local film industry rather than leaving it to compete unaided."},{"en":"their global appeal is undeniable","context":"One reason is simply budget. The established film industries of a few countries have huge sums available for staging action sequences, generating special effects and filming in spectacular locations, and Hollywood blockbusters such as Avatar or the James Bond films show what that money can buy: their global appeal is undeniable."},{"en":"the most accomplished producers and directors","context":"Another reason why these big-budget films are so successful is that they often star the most famous actors and actresses, and they are made by the most accomplished producers and directors. The poor quality, low-budget filmmaking in many countries suffers in comparison."},{"en":"suffers in comparison","context":"Another reason why these big-budget films are so successful is that they often star the most famous actors and actresses, and they are made by the most accomplished producers and directors. The poor quality, low-budget filmmaking in many countries suffers in comparison."},{"en":"be given the opportunity to","context":"In my view, governments should support local film industries financially. In every country, there may be talented amateur film-makers who just need to be given the opportunity to prove themselves. To compete with big-budget productions from overseas, these people need money to pay for film crews, actors and a host of other costs related to producing high-quality films. If governments did help with these costs, they would see an increase in employment in the film industry, income from film sales, and perhaps even a rise in tourist numbers. New Zealand, for example, has seen an increase in tourism related to the 'Lord of the Rings' films, which were partly funded by government subsidies."},{"en":"a rise in tourist numbers","context":"In my view, governments should support local film industries financially. In every country, there may be talented amateur film-makers who just need to be given the opportunity to prove themselves. To compete with big-budget productions from overseas, these people need money to pay for film crews, actors and a host of other costs related to producing high-quality films. If governments did help with these costs, they would see an increase in employment in the film industry, income from film sales, and perhaps even a rise in tourist numbers. New Zealand, for example, has seen an increase in tourism related to the 'Lord of the Rings' films, which were partly funded by government subsidies."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#253","summary":"Foreign films and local film funding"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Should politicians' private lives be reported?","prompt":"Details of politicians' private lives should not be published in newspapers. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Politicians should not lose every right to privacy simply because they hold public office. Newspapers should publish private information only when it is clearly relevant to a politician&#x27;s duties, honesty or use of public resources.\n\nThere is a legitimate public interest in some personal matters. Undeclared financial relationships, conflicts of interest or behaviour that directly contradicts a public claim may affect trust and decision-making. Investigative reporting is therefore essential when private conduct reveals corruption, abuse of power or serious hypocrisy. In these cases, publication can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold powerful people accountable</strong>.\n\nHowever, details about family relationships, health or ordinary leisure activities are often published mainly to attract attention. Such stories can harm relatives who did not choose a public role and may discourage capable people from entering politics. Curiosity is not the same as public interest. Editors should be able to explain how a private fact affects the politician&#x27;s work before releasing it.\n\nThe real difficulty is who applies that test. Editors compete for sales, and leaving the decision entirely to them has repeatedly failed: the British phone-hacking scandal revealed journalists intercepting private voicemail messages for stories with no bearing on anyone&#x27;s public duty. An independent regulator able to impose meaningful penalties, rather than a brief apology printed weeks later, would give the standard some force.\n\nIn conclusion, the press needs freedom to investigate those in power, but that freedom should be guided by relevance and proportionality. Private information should be published when it exposes conduct that affects public duty, not merely because it is sensational. This standard would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protect both accountability and personal dignity</strong>.","topic":"News & Media","phrases":[{"en":"hold powerful people accountable","context":"There is a legitimate public interest in some personal matters. Undeclared financial relationships, conflicts of interest or behaviour that directly contradicts a public claim may affect trust and decision-making. Investigative reporting is therefore essential when private conduct reveals corruption, abuse of power or serious hypocrisy. In these cases, publication can hold powerful people accountable."},{"en":"protect both accountability and personal dignity","context":"In conclusion, the press needs freedom to investigate those in power, but that freedom should be guided by relevance and proportionality. Private information should be published when it exposes conduct that affects public duty, not merely because it is sensational. This standard would protect both accountability and personal dignity."}],"subtype":"opinion","summary":"Whether politicians' private lives should be reported","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-136","_displayNo":"#254"},{"id":"writing-task-2-156","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Is radio out of date?","prompt":"Some people think that radio has become out of date and that there is no longer any need to listen to it for entertainment or news. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"With television, streaming services and podcasts widely available, some people believe that radio has become obsolete. I disagree, because radio possesses practical advantages that no other medium has replaced.\n\nThe argument that radio is outdated is easy to understand. Audiences for traditional stations have fallen, particularly among young people, who choose their own music rather than accept a presenter&#x27;s selection. Television provides images that radio cannot, and on-demand services allow listeners to hear exactly what they want at any time instead of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">waiting for a scheduled programme</strong>. Advertising revenue has moved online accordingly, and a number of long-established stations have closed.\n\nHowever, radio survives because it is the only medium that can be used while doing something else. Drivers, cooks, factory workers and runners cannot look at a screen, and for them audio is not an inferior option but the only possible one, which is why listening remains high during commuting hours. Radio is also extremely cheap to produce and receive, requires almost no bandwidth and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">works during power cuts and natural disasters</strong>, when television and internet services fail. In many rural regions it remains the main source of news.\n\nRadio has also adapted rather than declined. Podcasts are essentially radio distributed differently, and their audience has grown enormously, which suggests that demand for spoken audio is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increasing rather than disappearing</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that radio is out of date. Its audience has changed and its distribution has moved online, but the underlying medium remains uniquely convenient, cheap and reliable.","phrases":[{"en":"waiting for a scheduled programme","context":"The argument that radio is outdated is easy to understand. Audiences for traditional stations have fallen, particularly among young people, who choose their own music rather than accept a presenter's selection. Television provides images that radio cannot, and on-demand services allow listeners to hear exactly what they want at any time instead of waiting for a scheduled programme. Advertising revenue has moved online accordingly, and a number of long-established stations have closed."},{"en":"works during power cuts and natural disasters","context":"However, radio survives because it is the only medium that can be used while doing something else. Drivers, cooks, factory workers and runners cannot look at a screen, and for them audio is not an inferior option but the only possible one, which is why listening remains high during commuting hours. Radio is also extremely cheap to produce and receive, requires almost no bandwidth and works during power cuts and natural disasters, when television and internet services fail. In many rural regions it remains the main source of news."},{"en":"increasing rather than disappearing","context":"Radio has also adapted rather than declined. Podcasts are essentially radio distributed differently, and their audience has grown enormously, which suggests that demand for spoken audio is increasing rather than disappearing."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#255","summary":"Is radio out of date?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-161","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Why people distrust journalists","prompt":"We obtain most of our knowledge of the world from the news, yet many people no longer trust journalists. Why do people hold this view, and what qualities should a good journalist have?","answer":"Public confidence in journalism has fallen sharply in many countries. In my view there are two clear reasons for this, and they point directly to the qualities that a good journalist needs.\n\nThe first reason is commercial pressure. News organisations now earn money from the number of people who click on a story, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewards dramatic headlines rather than careful reporting</strong>. Articles are published before they have been checked, minor events are exaggerated, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">corrections appear quietly several days later</strong>. The second reason is bias. Most large outlets are owned by wealthy individuals or corporations with political interests, and readers have noticed that coverage of the same event can differ enormously from one newspaper to another. Social media has made this worse by allowing anyone to publish, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuine reporting now competes with rumour</strong>.\n\nThe qualities of a good journalist follow directly from these problems. The most important is accuracy: every claim should be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">verified through independent sources</strong> before publication, however attractive the story. The second is independence, meaning a willingness to print something that will displease an owner, an advertiser or a government. A good reporter must also be fair to the people they write about, giving them an opportunity to respond before an accusation appears in print. Courage matters too, since investigating corruption or organised crime carries real personal risk, and journalists in several countries have been imprisoned or killed for such work. Finally, a journalist must be able to explain complicated subjects such as economics or medicine clearly, without distorting them.\n\nIn conclusion, distrust of journalists is largely the result of commercial pressure and political bias. Restoring public confidence requires reporters who are accurate, independent, courageous and clear.","phrases":[{"en":"rewards dramatic headlines rather than careful reporting","context":"The first reason is commercial pressure. News organisations now earn money from the number of people who click on a story, which rewards dramatic headlines rather than careful reporting. Articles are published before they have been checked, minor events are exaggerated, and corrections appear quietly several days later. The second reason is bias. Most large outlets are owned by wealthy individuals or corporations with political interests, and readers have noticed that coverage of the same event can differ enormously from one newspaper to another. Social media has made this worse by allowing anyone to publish, so genuine reporting now competes with rumour."},{"en":"corrections appear quietly several days later","context":"The first reason is commercial pressure. News organisations now earn money from the number of people who click on a story, which rewards dramatic headlines rather than careful reporting. Articles are published before they have been checked, minor events are exaggerated, and corrections appear quietly several days later. The second reason is bias. Most large outlets are owned by wealthy individuals or corporations with political interests, and readers have noticed that coverage of the same event can differ enormously from one newspaper to another. Social media has made this worse by allowing anyone to publish, so genuine reporting now competes with rumour."},{"en":"genuine reporting now competes with rumour","context":"The first reason is commercial pressure. News organisations now earn money from the number of people who click on a story, which rewards dramatic headlines rather than careful reporting. Articles are published before they have been checked, minor events are exaggerated, and corrections appear quietly several days later. The second reason is bias. Most large outlets are owned by wealthy individuals or corporations with political interests, and readers have noticed that coverage of the same event can differ enormously from one newspaper to another. Social media has made this worse by allowing anyone to publish, so genuine reporting now competes with rumour."},{"en":"verified through independent sources","context":"The qualities of a good journalist follow directly from these problems. The most important is accuracy: every claim should be verified through independent sources before publication, however attractive the story. The second is independence, meaning a willingness to print something that will displease an owner, an advertiser or a government. A good reporter must also be fair to the people they write about, giving them an opportunity to respond before an accusation appears in print. Courage matters too, since investigating corruption or organised crime carries real personal risk, and journalists in several countries have been imprisoned or killed for such work. Finally, a journalist must be able to explain complicated subjects such as economics or medicine clearly, without distorting them."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Solutions","_displayNo":"#256","summary":"Why people distrust journalists"},{"id":"writing-task-2-169","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Will newspapers survive online news?","prompt":"Although more and more people read news on the Internet, newspapers will remain the most important source of news. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The Internet is beginning to rival newspapers as the best place to find information about what is happening in the world. I believe that this trend will continue, and the Internet will soon be just as important as the traditional press.\n\nOn the one hand, newspapers will probably remain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a vital source of information</strong> even in the Internet age. Not everyone wants to, or is able to, go online instead: older people and those in rural areas may have limited internet access, while plenty of readers with a perfectly good connection <strong class=\"key-phrase\">still prefer newsprint out of habit</strong>. Newspapers also carry a level of trust that many websites lack, since their stories pass through professional journalists and editors before publication, and for many readers there remains something satisfying about holding an actual paper rather than staring at a screen.\n\nHowever, the Internet is likely to become just as popular as newspapers, largely because it delivers news faster and follows readers everywhere, updating on a phone the moment a story breaks rather than the next morning. Online news is also more interactive: readers can share articles, argue about them in comment sections, and post their own updates on platforms such as Twitter and YouTube, so that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the audience becomes part of the reporting</strong> rather than a passive recipient of it. Producing news digitally also avoids the paper, ink and delivery lorries that a print run requires, which makes it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the less wasteful option environmentally</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree with the view that newspapers will continue to be the main source of news, because I believe that the Internet will soon be equally important.","phrases":[{"en":"a vital source of information","context":"On the one hand, newspapers will probably remain a vital source of information even in the Internet age. Not everyone wants to, or is able to, go online instead: older people and those in rural areas may have limited internet access, while plenty of readers with a perfectly good connection still prefer newsprint out of habit. Newspapers also carry a level of trust that many websites lack, since their stories pass through professional journalists and editors before publication, and for many readers there remains something satisfying about holding an actual paper rather than staring at a screen."},{"en":"still prefer newsprint out of habit","context":"On the one hand, newspapers will probably remain a vital source of information even in the Internet age. Not everyone wants to, or is able to, go online instead: older people and those in rural areas may have limited internet access, while plenty of readers with a perfectly good connection still prefer newsprint out of habit. Newspapers also carry a level of trust that many websites lack, since their stories pass through professional journalists and editors before publication, and for many readers there remains something satisfying about holding an actual paper rather than staring at a screen."},{"en":"the audience becomes part of the reporting","context":"However, the Internet is likely to become just as popular as newspapers, largely because it delivers news faster and follows readers everywhere, updating on a phone the moment a story breaks rather than the next morning. Online news is also more interactive: readers can share articles, argue about them in comment sections, and post their own updates on platforms such as Twitter and YouTube, so that the audience becomes part of the reporting rather than a passive recipient of it. Producing news digitally also avoids the paper, ink and delivery lorries that a print run requires, which makes it the less wasteful option environmentally."},{"en":"the less wasteful option environmentally","context":"However, the Internet is likely to become just as popular as newspapers, largely because it delivers news faster and follows readers everywhere, updating on a phone the moment a story breaks rather than the next morning. Online news is also more interactive: readers can share articles, argue about them in comment sections, and post their own updates on platforms such as Twitter and YouTube, so that the audience becomes part of the reporting rather than a passive recipient of it. Producing news digitally also avoids the paper, ink and delivery lorries that a print run requires, which makes it the less wasteful option environmentally."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#257","summary":"Although more and more people read news on the Internet, newspapers will…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2204","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Newspapers versus other media","prompt":"Some people think that newspapers are the best way to learn about news. However, others believe that they can learn news more effectively through other media. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Although newspapers were once the main source of information about current events, most people now follow the news online, on television or through social media. I would argue that other media are faster and more convenient, but that newspapers still provide the most reliable and thorough coverage.\n\nThe advantages of newspapers concern depth and accuracy. Articles are researched over days or weeks, checked by editors and written by journalists who specialise in a particular field, so a newspaper report on an election or a court case is likely to be far more detailed and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more carefully verified than anything else available</strong>. Reading a paper also exposes people to stories they would never have chosen themselves, which gives a broader picture of what is happening in the world.\n\nOther forms of media, however, have obvious strengths. Television and news websites can report an earthquake or a political announcement within minutes, whereas a newspaper reader <strong class=\"key-phrase\">must wait until the following morning</strong>. Online sources are free, can be reached anywhere on a phone, and offer video, audio and live updates that print cannot match. Social media also allows ordinary people to report events directly from places where journalists are not present.\n\nIn my opinion the two are best used together. I rely on my phone for immediate headlines, but I turn to established newspapers, whether printed or online, when I want to understand why something has happened. The main danger of social media is that it spreads <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unverified claims</strong> extremely quickly, and professional journalism remains <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only effective check on this</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, other media are better for speed and access, but newspapers continue to offer the most trustworthy analysis.","phrases":[{"en":"more carefully verified than anything else available","context":"The advantages of newspapers concern depth and accuracy. Articles are researched over days or weeks, checked by editors and written by journalists who specialise in a particular field, so a newspaper report on an election or a court case is likely to be far more detailed and more carefully verified than anything else available. Reading a paper also exposes people to stories they would never have chosen themselves, which gives a broader picture of what is happening in the world."},{"en":"must wait until the following morning","context":"Other forms of media, however, have obvious strengths. Television and news websites can report an earthquake or a political announcement within minutes, whereas a newspaper reader must wait until the following morning. Online sources are free, can be reached anywhere on a phone, and offer video, audio and live updates that print cannot match. Social media also allows ordinary people to report events directly from places where journalists are not present."},{"en":"unverified claims","context":"In my opinion the two are best used together. I rely on my phone for immediate headlines, but I turn to established newspapers, whether printed or online, when I want to understand why something has happened. The main danger of social media is that it spreads unverified claims extremely quickly, and professional journalism remains the only effective check on this."},{"en":"the only effective check on this","context":"In my opinion the two are best used together. I rely on my phone for immediate headlines, but I turn to established newspapers, whether printed or online, when I want to understand why something has happened. The main danger of social media is that it spreads unverified claims extremely quickly, and professional journalism remains the only effective check on this."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#258","summary":"Newspapers versus other media"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2272","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Should violent films and games be banned?","prompt":"Films and computer games containing violence are popular. Some people say they have a negative effect on society and should be banned. Others say they are just harmless relaxation. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Violent films and games sell in enormous numbers, and opinion divides between those who blame them for real aggression and those who regard them as ordinary entertainment. My own position falls between the two, though closer to the second.\n\nThose who want a ban argue that repeated exposure changes attitudes. Watching violence presented as exciting and consequence-free may make aggression seem a normal response to conflict, and this concern is sharpest for children, whose sense of what is real is still forming. Games are held to be worse than films because the player performs the violence rather than watching it, and short-term studies do find <strong class=\"key-phrase\">measurably increased hostility</strong> immediately after play.\n\nThe opposing view points to what the wider evidence shows. Violent games have spread to almost every country over three decades while violent crime among young people has generally fallen, which is difficult to reconcile with a strong causal effect. Fiction has always dealt with violence, from Greek tragedy onwards, and audiences have consistently proved able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">distinguish a story from an instruction</strong>. There is also a strong argument about freedom, since banning material because a small minority may respond badly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">restricts everyone for the sake of a few</strong>.\n\nMy view is that a ban is both disproportionate and ineffective. The evidence supports a small short-term effect rather than a cause of serious violence, and prohibition simply moves material to unregulated channels where nobody checks who is watching.\n\nIn conclusion, these concerns should not be dismissed, particularly regarding young children. Age ratings enforced properly, together with parents knowing what their children play, address the real risk without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removing the choice from adults</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"measurably increased hostility","context":"Those who want a ban argue that repeated exposure changes attitudes. Watching violence presented as exciting and consequence-free may make aggression seem a normal response to conflict, and this concern is sharpest for children, whose sense of what is real is still forming. Games are held to be worse than films because the player performs the violence rather than watching it, and short-term studies do find measurably increased hostility immediately after play."},{"en":"distinguish a story from an instruction","context":"The opposing view points to what the wider evidence shows. Violent games have spread to almost every country over three decades while violent crime among young people has generally fallen, which is difficult to reconcile with a strong causal effect. Fiction has always dealt with violence, from Greek tragedy onwards, and audiences have consistently proved able to distinguish a story from an instruction. There is also a strong argument about freedom, since banning material because a small minority may respond badly restricts everyone for the sake of a few."},{"en":"restricts everyone for the sake of a few","context":"The opposing view points to what the wider evidence shows. Violent games have spread to almost every country over three decades while violent crime among young people has generally fallen, which is difficult to reconcile with a strong causal effect. Fiction has always dealt with violence, from Greek tragedy onwards, and audiences have consistently proved able to distinguish a story from an instruction. There is also a strong argument about freedom, since banning material because a small minority may respond badly restricts everyone for the sake of a few."},{"en":"removing the choice from adults","context":"In conclusion, these concerns should not be dismissed, particularly regarding young children. Age ratings enforced properly, together with parents knowing what their children play, address the real risk without removing the choice from adults."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#259","summary":"Whether violent films and computer games should be banned"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2301","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Is following the news a waste of time?","prompt":"Some people think the news has no connection to people's lives, so reading newspapers and watching television news is a waste of time. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"A growing number of people have stopped following the news, saying that it neither affects nor improves their lives. I disagree with that position, although the complaint behind it identifies something wrong with how news is currently produced.\n\nThe claim fails because the connection is usually indirect rather than absent. Decisions reported in the news — interest rates, tax changes, planning laws, public health rules — reach households within months, and someone who ignored them would be surprised by a mortgage payment or a school closure they could have anticipated. News also makes voting meaningful, since a citizen who knows nothing about a government&#x27;s record cannot judge it, and reporting is one of the few pressures that keeps officials and companies honest. There is a further point about understanding other people: a person who never encounters events beyond their own street mistakes their own experience for how the world works.\n\nThe complaint nevertheless has a foundation. Much of what is broadcast genuinely is irrelevant: celebrity stories, political arguments about nothing, and distant events reported without any explanation of why they matter. Coverage is also relentlessly negative and repetitive, and consuming it continuously produces anxiety without producing knowledge, which is why some readers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feel worse informed the more they watch</strong>.\n\nThe sensible response is to change the habit rather than abandon it. Reading a serious source once a day, choosing analysis over rolling coverage, and following the subjects that actually affect one&#x27;s work and family delivers the benefit without the exhaustion. It is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">continuous drip of alerts</strong> that wastes time, not information itself.\n\nIn conclusion, the news connects private lives to decisions made elsewhere, and ignoring it leaves people unprepared and easier to mislead. Much daily coverage is genuinely worthless, so the answer is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">read less and choose better</strong> rather than to stop.","phrases":[{"en":"feel worse informed the more they watch","context":"The complaint nevertheless has a foundation. Much of what is broadcast genuinely is irrelevant: celebrity stories, political arguments about nothing, and distant events reported without any explanation of why they matter. Coverage is also relentlessly negative and repetitive, and consuming it continuously produces anxiety without producing knowledge, which is why some readers feel worse informed the more they watch."},{"en":"continuous drip of alerts","context":"The sensible response is to change the habit rather than abandon it. Reading a serious source once a day, choosing analysis over rolling coverage, and following the subjects that actually affect one's work and family delivers the benefit without the exhaustion. It is the continuous drip of alerts that wastes time, not information itself."},{"en":"read less and choose better","context":"In conclusion, the news connects private lives to decisions made elsewhere, and ignoring it leaves people unprepared and easier to mislead. Much daily coverage is genuinely worthless, so the answer is to read less and choose better rather than to stop."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#260","summary":"Whether news is disconnected from ordinary life and not worth following"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2331","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Why the news is mostly bad","prompt":"News plays an important part in most people's lives. Why is news so important to people? Why is so much news dedicated to bad news? Should the news focus on good news instead?","answer":"Most people check the news before they have finished breakfast. It occupies that position because it tells us what has changed in a world we cannot see for ourselves, and it leans towards disaster for reasons that are partly commercial and partly human.\n\nNews matters because almost everything that affects a life is decided somewhere else. Interest rates, elections, war and epidemics all reach ordinary households, and knowing about them early allows people to act rather than react. It also makes citizenship possible, since a voter with no information is choosing blindly, and reporting is one of the few forces that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes powerful people uncomfortable</strong>. Beyond that, news gives a scattered population a shared set of events to discuss.\n\nBad news dominates for two reasons. The first is in us: human attention evolved to notice threats, so a report of danger is read while a report of steady improvement is scrolled past. The second is commercial, because outlets are paid by attention, and alarming headlines earn more of it. Bad events are also easier to report, since a bridge collapsing is a moment while a bridge standing safely for forty years is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a process without a date</strong>.\n\nReplacing this with good news would be a mistake, however. Reporting exists to warn and to hold power to account, and a diet of pleasant stories would leave the public unaware of what needs fixing. The honest correction is proportion rather than positivity: alongside each crisis, coverage should explain what is being done, how common the danger actually is, and what has improved, so that readers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">understand scale as well as drama</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, news is important because it connects private lives to distant decisions, and it favours bad news because both our attention and the market reward it. The answer is more context and better proportion, not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a cheerful substitute for reporting</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"makes powerful people uncomfortable","context":"News matters because almost everything that affects a life is decided somewhere else. Interest rates, elections, war and epidemics all reach ordinary households, and knowing about them early allows people to act rather than react. It also makes citizenship possible, since a voter with no information is choosing blindly, and reporting is one of the few forces that makes powerful people uncomfortable. Beyond that, news gives a scattered population a shared set of events to discuss."},{"en":"a process without a date","context":"Bad news dominates for two reasons. The first is in us: human attention evolved to notice threats, so a report of danger is read while a report of steady improvement is scrolled past. The second is commercial, because outlets are paid by attention, and alarming headlines earn more of it. Bad events are also easier to report, since a bridge collapsing is a moment while a bridge standing safely for forty years is a process without a date."},{"en":"understand scale as well as drama","context":"Replacing this with good news would be a mistake, however. Reporting exists to warn and to hold power to account, and a diet of pleasant stories would leave the public unaware of what needs fixing. The honest correction is proportion rather than positivity: alongside each crisis, coverage should explain what is being done, how common the danger actually is, and what has improved, so that readers understand scale as well as drama."},{"en":"a cheerful substitute for reporting","context":"In conclusion, news is important because it connects private lives to distant decisions, and it favours bad news because both our attention and the market reward it. The answer is more context and better proportion, not a cheerful substitute for reporting."}],"subtype":"Two-Part Question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#261","summary":"Why news matters, why it favours bad news, and whether it should change"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2349","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"News & Media","title":"Imported films or home-made ones?","prompt":"Some people believe that showing imported films and television programmes is beneficial for a country's culture, while others argue that countries should produce their own. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Streaming services have made foreign films available everywhere at once, and small national film industries are struggling against them. Both sides of this argument identify something real, and my own view is that the two are not alternatives.\n\nImported films bring genuine benefits. They show audiences how other people live, which reduces the suspicion that distance produces, and they carry ideas that a closed culture would take much longer to encounter. Viewers gain access to work of a technical quality that few countries can afford to match, and languages are learned painlessly from subtitles. Domestic film-makers also learn their craft by watching the best of it, which is why national industries usually improve after exposure rather than before it.\n\nThe opposing case is about balance rather than contact. When most of what a country watches is made elsewhere, children absorb foreign assumptions about family, success and beauty while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their own surroundings appear nowhere on screen</strong>, and local stories go untold because the audience has been trained to expect a different style. There is an economic argument too: money spent on imported programming leaves the country, and writers, actors and technicians lose the work that would keep the industry alive. Small languages suffer most, since a film in one is expensive to make and easy to skip.\n\nThe evidence suggests protection and openness can coexist. Countries that fund domestic production and require broadcasters to carry a share of local content have produced films that then export successfully, so support built the industry rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sheltering it from competition</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, imported films widen a culture and raise standards, and no country should shut them out. National production needs deliberate funding as well, because a country that never sees itself on screen loses the habit of telling its own stories.","phrases":[{"en":"their own surroundings appear nowhere on screen","context":"The opposing case is about balance rather than contact. When most of what a country watches is made elsewhere, children absorb foreign assumptions about family, success and beauty while their own surroundings appear nowhere on screen, and local stories go untold because the audience has been trained to expect a different style. There is an economic argument too: money spent on imported programming leaves the country, and writers, actors and technicians lose the work that would keep the industry alive. Small languages suffer most, since a film in one is expensive to make and easy to skip."},{"en":"sheltering it from competition","context":"The evidence suggests protection and openness can coexist. Countries that fund domestic production and require broadcasters to carry a share of local content have produced films that then export successfully, so support built the industry rather than sheltering it from competition."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#262","summary":"Whether foreign films benefit a culture or countries should make their own"},{"id":"writing-task-2-015","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Compulsory unpaid work for teenagers","prompt":"Some people think that all teenagers should be required to do unpaid work in their free time to help the local community. They believe this would benefit both the individual teenager and society as a whole. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Many young people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work on a volunteer basis</strong>, and this can only be beneficial for both the individual and society as a whole and encourage <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of shared civic responsibility</strong>. However, I do not agree that we should therefore force all teenagers to do unpaid work.\n\nMost young people are already under enough pressure with their studies, without being given the added responsibility of working in their spare time. School is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">just as demanding as a full-time job</strong>, and teachers expect their students to do <strong class=\"key-phrase\">homework and exam revision</strong> on top of attending lessons every day. When young people do have some free time, we should encourage them to enjoy it with their friends or to spend it doing sports and other leisure activities. They have many years of work ahead of them when they finish their studies.\n\nAt the same time, I do not believe that society has anything to gain from obliging young people to do unpaid work. In fact, I would argue that it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">goes against the values of</strong> a free and fair society to force a group of people to do something against their will. Doing this can only <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lead to resentment amongst young people</strong>, who would feel that they were being used, and parents, who would not want to be told how to raise their children. Currently, nobody is forced to volunteer, and this is surely the best system.\n\nIn conclusion, teenagers may choose to work for free and help others, but in my opinion we should not make this compulsory.","phrases":[{"en":"work on a volunteer basis","context":"Many young people work on a volunteer basis, and this can only be beneficial for both the individual and society as a whole and encourage a sense of shared civic responsibility. However, I do not agree that we should therefore force all teenagers to do unpaid work."},{"en":"a sense of shared civic responsibility","context":"Many young people work on a volunteer basis, and this can only be beneficial for both the individual and society as a whole and encourage a sense of shared civic responsibility. However, I do not agree that we should therefore force all teenagers to do unpaid work."},{"en":"just as demanding as a full-time job","context":"Most young people are already under enough pressure with their studies, without being given the added responsibility of working in their spare time. School is just as demanding as a full-time job, and teachers expect their students to do homework and exam revision on top of attending lessons every day. When young people do have some free time, we should encourage them to enjoy it with their friends or to spend it doing sports and other leisure activities. They have many years of work ahead of them when they finish their studies."},{"en":"homework and exam revision","context":"Most young people are already under enough pressure with their studies, without being given the added responsibility of working in their spare time. School is just as demanding as a full-time job, and teachers expect their students to do homework and exam revision on top of attending lessons every day. When young people do have some free time, we should encourage them to enjoy it with their friends or to spend it doing sports and other leisure activities. They have many years of work ahead of them when they finish their studies."},{"en":"goes against the values of","context":"At the same time, I do not believe that society has anything to gain from obliging young people to do unpaid work. In fact, I would argue that it goes against the values of a free and fair society to force a group of people to do something against their will. Doing this can only lead to resentment amongst young people, who would feel that they were being used, and parents, who would not want to be told how to raise their children. Currently, nobody is forced to volunteer, and this is surely the best system."},{"en":"lead to resentment amongst young people","context":"At the same time, I do not believe that society has anything to gain from obliging young people to do unpaid work. In fact, I would argue that it goes against the values of a free and fair society to force a group of people to do something against their will. Doing this can only lead to resentment amongst young people, who would feel that they were being used, and parents, who would not want to be told how to raise their children. Currently, nobody is forced to volunteer, and this is surely the best system."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#263","summary":"Compulsory community work for teenagers"},{"id":"writing-task-2-047","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Military service or community service?","prompt":"What are the benefits of requiring young people to serve in the army? Does participation in community work qualify as an alternative?","answer":"Some countries still require young people to complete a period of military service. In my opinion this brings certain genuine benefits, but community service would achieve most of the same aims without the drawbacks.\n\nThe advantages of military training are not difficult to identify. Recruits become considerably fitter, and they learn discipline, punctuality and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how to function under pressure</strong>, which many young people have never previously been required to do. There is a social benefit as well: conscription mixes young men from different regions and backgrounds who would otherwise never meet, which <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strengthens national cohesion</strong> in a way little else does. Employers frequently regard former conscripts as reliable, punctual and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well organised</strong>, and in some countries the experience is treated almost as a qualification in itself.\n\nThe objections, however, are serious. Military training is designed to prepare people for combat, and not everyone is suited to that environment; a significant number find the stress genuinely damaging. It also removes a year or two from a young person&#x27;s education or career at exactly the point when those years matter most.\n\nCommunity service seems to me a better instrument for the same purpose. Working in hospitals, care homes, conservation projects or disaster relief teaches responsibility and teamwork just as effectively, and it exposes young people to parts of society they would otherwise never see. It also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produces something of immediate value</strong>, whereas peacetime military training <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produces a readiness that is rarely used</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, military service genuinely does develop discipline and physical fitness, and I would not dismiss it. Community service, however, delivers comparable benefits at a far lower personal cost, and I would prefer it as the general requirement.","phrases":[{"en":"how to function under pressure","context":"The advantages of military training are not difficult to identify. Recruits become considerably fitter, and they learn discipline, punctuality and how to function under pressure, which many young people have never previously been required to do. There is a social benefit as well: conscription mixes young men from different regions and backgrounds who would otherwise never meet, which strengthens national cohesion in a way little else does. Employers frequently regard former conscripts as reliable, punctual and well organised, and in some countries the experience is treated almost as a qualification in itself."},{"en":"strengthens national cohesion","context":"The advantages of military training are not difficult to identify. Recruits become considerably fitter, and they learn discipline, punctuality and how to function under pressure, which many young people have never previously been required to do. There is a social benefit as well: conscription mixes young men from different regions and backgrounds who would otherwise never meet, which strengthens national cohesion in a way little else does. Employers frequently regard former conscripts as reliable, punctual and well organised, and in some countries the experience is treated almost as a qualification in itself."},{"en":"well organised","context":"The advantages of military training are not difficult to identify. Recruits become considerably fitter, and they learn discipline, punctuality and how to function under pressure, which many young people have never previously been required to do. There is a social benefit as well: conscription mixes young men from different regions and backgrounds who would otherwise never meet, which strengthens national cohesion in a way little else does. Employers frequently regard former conscripts as reliable, punctual and well organised, and in some countries the experience is treated almost as a qualification in itself."},{"en":"produces something of immediate value","context":"Community service seems to me a better instrument for the same purpose. Working in hospitals, care homes, conservation projects or disaster relief teaches responsibility and teamwork just as effectively, and it exposes young people to parts of society they would otherwise never see. It also produces something of immediate value, whereas peacetime military training produces a readiness that is rarely used."},{"en":"produces a readiness that is rarely used","context":"Community service seems to me a better instrument for the same purpose. Working in hospitals, care homes, conservation projects or disaster relief teaches responsibility and teamwork just as effectively, and it exposes young people to parts of society they would otherwise never see. It also produces something of immediate value, whereas peacetime military training produces a readiness that is rarely used."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#264","summary":"Military service or community service?"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"The teenage years or adult life?","prompt":"Some people think that the teenage years are the happiest time of most people's lives. Others think that adult life brings more happiness, in spite of greater responsibility. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Some people remember adolescence as the happiest period of life, while others prefer adulthood. I believe happiness depends more on personal circumstances than age, although adulthood often offers greater freedom and control.\n\nTeenagers may enjoy fewer financial responsibilities and more time with friends. School holidays, new experiences and strong social bonds can make this stage exciting. However, adolescence also involves academic pressure, insecurity and limited independence. Young people are often expected to make important choices before they fully understand themselves.\n\nAdults face work, bills and family duties, but they usually have more control over where they live, whom they meet and how they spend their time. They can build a career, form stable relationships and make decisions based on experience. For many people, this <strong class=\"key-phrase\">greater sense of agency</strong> outweighs the heavier responsibilities.\n\nIn my own view, the decisive difference is not the amount of pressure but the ability to escape it. A teenager who dislikes school or lives in a difficult household has almost no way out and must simply endure the situation for years. An unhappy adult can resign, move to another city or end a relationship, and even when those choices are painful they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove the sense of being trapped</strong>. A feeling of control is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the strongest predictors of life satisfaction</strong>, and it is largely unavailable before adulthood.\n\nIn conclusion, there is no universal happiest age. A supportive family, good health, meaningful relationships and a sense of purpose matter more than whether someone is a teenager or an adult. Nevertheless, I would choose adulthood because independence makes it easier to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shape life according to personal priorities</strong>.","topic":"Society","phrases":[{"en":"greater sense of agency","context":"Adults face work, bills and family duties, but they usually have more control over where they live, whom they meet and how they spend their time. They can build a career, form stable relationships and make decisions based on experience. For many people, this greater sense of agency outweighs the heavier responsibilities."},{"en":"remove the sense of being trapped","context":"In my own view, the decisive difference is not the amount of pressure but the ability to escape it. A teenager who dislikes school or lives in a difficult household has almost no way out and must simply endure the situation for years. An unhappy adult can resign, move to another city or end a relationship, and even when those choices are painful they remove the sense of being trapped. A feeling of control is one of the strongest predictors of life satisfaction, and it is largely unavailable before adulthood."},{"en":"one of the strongest predictors of life satisfaction","context":"In my own view, the decisive difference is not the amount of pressure but the ability to escape it. A teenager who dislikes school or lives in a difficult household has almost no way out and must simply endure the situation for years. An unhappy adult can resign, move to another city or end a relationship, and even when those choices are painful they remove the sense of being trapped. A feeling of control is one of the strongest predictors of life satisfaction, and it is largely unavailable before adulthood."},{"en":"shape life according to personal priorities","context":"In conclusion, there is no universal happiest age. A supportive family, good health, meaningful relationships and a sense of purpose matter more than whether someone is a teenager or an adult. Nevertheless, I would choose adulthood because independence makes it easier to shape life according to personal priorities."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Whether the teenage years or adult life bring more happiness","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-110","_displayNo":"#265"},{"id":"writing-task-2-120","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"What happiness is, and how to reach it","prompt":"Happiness is considered very important in life. Why is it difficult to define? What factors are important in achieving happiness?","answer":"It is no doubt true that the majority of people would like to be happy in their lives. While <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the personal nature of happiness</strong> makes it difficult to describe, there do seem to be some common needs that we all share with regard to experiencing or achieving happiness.\n\nHappiness is difficult to define because it means something different to each individual person. Nobody can fully understand or experience another person&#x27;s feelings, and we all have our own particular passions from which we take pleasure. Some people, for example, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">derive a sense of satisfaction from</strong> earning money or achieving success, whereas for others, health and family are much more important. At the same time, a range of other feelings, from excitement to peacefulness, may be associated with the idea of happiness, and the same person may therefore feel happy in a variety of different ways.\n\nAlthough it seems almost impossible to give <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a precise definition of happiness</strong>, most people would agree that there are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">some basic preconditions to achieving it</strong>. Firstly, it is hard for a person to be happy if he or she does not have a safe place to live and enough food to eat. Our basic survival needs must surely be met before we can lead a pleasant life. Secondly, the greatest joy in life is usually found in shared experiences with family and friends, and it is rare to find a person who is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">content to live in complete isolation</strong>. Other key factors could be individual freedom and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of purpose in life</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, happiness is difficult to define because it is particular to each individual, but I believe that our basic needs for shelter, food and company need to be fulfilled before we can experience it.","phrases":[{"en":"the personal nature of happiness","context":"It is no doubt true that the majority of people would like to be happy in their lives. While the personal nature of happiness makes it difficult to describe, there do seem to be some common needs that we all share with regard to experiencing or achieving happiness."},{"en":"derive a sense of satisfaction from","context":"Happiness is difficult to define because it means something different to each individual person. Nobody can fully understand or experience another person's feelings, and we all have our own particular passions from which we take pleasure. Some people, for example, derive a sense of satisfaction from earning money or achieving success, whereas for others, health and family are much more important. At the same time, a range of other feelings, from excitement to peacefulness, may be associated with the idea of happiness, and the same person may therefore feel happy in a variety of different ways."},{"en":"a precise definition of happiness","context":"Although it seems almost impossible to give a precise definition of happiness, most people would agree that there are some basic preconditions to achieving it. Firstly, it is hard for a person to be happy if he or she does not have a safe place to live and enough food to eat. Our basic survival needs must surely be met before we can lead a pleasant life. Secondly, the greatest joy in life is usually found in shared experiences with family and friends, and it is rare to find a person who is content to live in complete isolation. Other key factors could be individual freedom and a sense of purpose in life."},{"en":"some basic preconditions to achieving it","context":"Although it seems almost impossible to give a precise definition of happiness, most people would agree that there are some basic preconditions to achieving it. Firstly, it is hard for a person to be happy if he or she does not have a safe place to live and enough food to eat. Our basic survival needs must surely be met before we can lead a pleasant life. Secondly, the greatest joy in life is usually found in shared experiences with family and friends, and it is rare to find a person who is content to live in complete isolation. Other key factors could be individual freedom and a sense of purpose in life."},{"en":"content to live in complete isolation","context":"Although it seems almost impossible to give a precise definition of happiness, most people would agree that there are some basic preconditions to achieving it. Firstly, it is hard for a person to be happy if he or she does not have a safe place to live and enough food to eat. Our basic survival needs must surely be met before we can lead a pleasant life. Secondly, the greatest joy in life is usually found in shared experiences with family and friends, and it is rare to find a person who is content to live in complete isolation. Other key factors could be individual freedom and a sense of purpose in life."},{"en":"a sense of purpose in life","context":"Although it seems almost impossible to give a precise definition of happiness, most people would agree that there are some basic preconditions to achieving it. Firstly, it is hard for a person to be happy if he or she does not have a safe place to live and enough food to eat. Our basic survival needs must surely be met before we can lead a pleasant life. Secondly, the greatest joy in life is usually found in shared experiences with family and friends, and it is rare to find a person who is content to live in complete isolation. Other key factors could be individual freedom and a sense of purpose in life."}],"subtype":"Two-part question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#266","summary":"Defining and achieving happiness"},{"id":"writing-task-2-140","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"More dependent on each other, or more independent?","prompt":"Some people think that in the modern world we are more dependent on each other, while others think that people have become more independent. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have different views about whether we are more or less dependent on others nowadays. In my view, modern life forces us to be more independent than people were in the past.\n\nOne reason people give for growing interdependence is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cost of living</strong>, which has risen so sharply that young adults increasingly rely on their parents for help when buying a house; property prices are now so high that many people cannot <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pay a deposit and a mortgage</strong> without that support. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">Rising ambition compounds the pressure</strong>: as families want a better quality of life, both parents typically need to work full-time, which in turn makes them dependent on grandparents and paid babysitters for childcare that a single wage earner&#x27;s household would once have managed alone.\n\nHowever, I would agree with those who believe that people are more independent these days. In most countries, families are becoming <strong class=\"key-phrase\">smaller and more dispersed</strong>, which means that people cannot count on relatives as much as they used to. We also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have more freedom to</strong> travel and live far away from our home towns. For example, many students choose to study abroad instead of going to their local university, and this experience makes them more independent as they learn to live alone. Another factor in this growing independence is technology, which allows us to work alone and from any part of the world.\n\nIn conclusion, while there are some reasons to believe that people now depend on each other more, my own view is that we are more independent than ever.","phrases":[{"en":"the cost of living","context":"One reason people give for growing interdependence is the cost of living, which has risen so sharply that young adults increasingly rely on their parents for help when buying a house; property prices are now so high that many people cannot pay a deposit and a mortgage without that support. Rising ambition compounds the pressure: as families want a better quality of life, both parents typically need to work full-time, which in turn makes them dependent on grandparents and paid babysitters for childcare that a single wage earner's household would once have managed alone."},{"en":"pay a deposit and a mortgage","context":"One reason people give for growing interdependence is the cost of living, which has risen so sharply that young adults increasingly rely on their parents for help when buying a house; property prices are now so high that many people cannot pay a deposit and a mortgage without that support. Rising ambition compounds the pressure: as families want a better quality of life, both parents typically need to work full-time, which in turn makes them dependent on grandparents and paid babysitters for childcare that a single wage earner's household would once have managed alone."},{"en":"Rising ambition compounds the pressure","context":"One reason people give for growing interdependence is the cost of living, which has risen so sharply that young adults increasingly rely on their parents for help when buying a house; property prices are now so high that many people cannot pay a deposit and a mortgage without that support. Rising ambition compounds the pressure: as families want a better quality of life, both parents typically need to work full-time, which in turn makes them dependent on grandparents and paid babysitters for childcare that a single wage earner's household would once have managed alone."},{"en":"smaller and more dispersed","context":"However, I would agree with those who believe that people are more independent these days. In most countries, families are becoming smaller and more dispersed, which means that people cannot count on relatives as much as they used to. We also have more freedom to travel and live far away from our home towns. For example, many students choose to study abroad instead of going to their local university, and this experience makes them more independent as they learn to live alone. Another factor in this growing independence is technology, which allows us to work alone and from any part of the world."},{"en":"have more freedom to","context":"However, I would agree with those who believe that people are more independent these days. In most countries, families are becoming smaller and more dispersed, which means that people cannot count on relatives as much as they used to. We also have more freedom to travel and live far away from our home towns. For example, many students choose to study abroad instead of going to their local university, and this experience makes them more independent as they learn to live alone. Another factor in this growing independence is technology, which allows us to work alone and from any part of the world."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#267","summary":"Modern independence and interdependence"},{"id":"writing-task-2-141","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Older generations and their ideas about how to live","prompt":"The older generations tend to have very traditional ideas about how people should live, think and behave. However, some people believe that these ideas are not helpful in preparing younger generations for modern life. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this view?","answer":"It is true that many older people believe in traditional values that often seem incompatible with the needs of younger people. While I agree that some traditional ideas are outdated, I believe that others are still useful and should not be forgotten.\n\nOn the one hand, many of the ideas that elderly people have about life are becoming less relevant for younger people. In the past, for example, people were advised to learn a profession and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">find a secure job</strong> for life, but today&#x27;s workers expect much more variety and diversity from their careers. At the same time, the “rules’ around relationships <strong class=\"key-phrase\">are being eroded</strong> as young adults <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make their own choices</strong> about who and when to marry. But perhaps <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the greatest disparity between the generations</strong> can be seen in their attitudes towards gender roles. The traditional roles of men and women, as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">breadwinners and housewives</strong>, are no longer accepted as necessary or appropriate by most younger people.\n\nOn the other hand, some traditional views and values are certainly applicable to the modern world. For example, older generations attach great importance to working hard, doing one&#x27;s best, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking pride in one&#x27;s work</strong>, and these behaviours can surely benefit young people as they enter today&#x27;s competitive job market. Other characteristics that are perhaps seen as traditional are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">politeness and good manners</strong>. In our globalised world, young adults can expect to come into contact with people from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a huge variety of backgrounds</strong>, and it is more important than ever to treat others with respect. Finally, I believe that young people would lead happier lives if they had a more “old-fashioned’ sense of community and neighbourliness.\n\nIn conclusion, although the views of older people may sometimes seem unhelpful in today&#x27;s world, we should not dismiss all traditional ideas as irrelevant.","phrases":[{"en":"find a secure job","context":"On the one hand, many of the ideas that elderly people have about life are becoming less relevant for younger people. In the past, for example, people were advised to learn a profession and find a secure job for life, but today's workers expect much more variety and diversity from their careers. At the same time, the “rules’ around relationships are being eroded as young adults make their own choices about who and when to marry. But perhaps the greatest disparity between the generations can be seen in their attitudes towards gender roles. The traditional roles of men and women, as breadwinners and housewives, are no longer accepted as necessary or appropriate by most younger people."},{"en":"are being eroded","context":"On the one hand, many of the ideas that elderly people have about life are becoming less relevant for younger people. In the past, for example, people were advised to learn a profession and find a secure job for life, but today's workers expect much more variety and diversity from their careers. At the same time, the “rules’ around relationships are being eroded as young adults make their own choices about who and when to marry. But perhaps the greatest disparity between the generations can be seen in their attitudes towards gender roles. The traditional roles of men and women, as breadwinners and housewives, are no longer accepted as necessary or appropriate by most younger people."},{"en":"make their own choices","context":"On the one hand, many of the ideas that elderly people have about life are becoming less relevant for younger people. In the past, for example, people were advised to learn a profession and find a secure job for life, but today's workers expect much more variety and diversity from their careers. At the same time, the “rules’ around relationships are being eroded as young adults make their own choices about who and when to marry. But perhaps the greatest disparity between the generations can be seen in their attitudes towards gender roles. The traditional roles of men and women, as breadwinners and housewives, are no longer accepted as necessary or appropriate by most younger people."},{"en":"the greatest disparity between the generations","context":"On the one hand, many of the ideas that elderly people have about life are becoming less relevant for younger people. In the past, for example, people were advised to learn a profession and find a secure job for life, but today's workers expect much more variety and diversity from their careers. At the same time, the “rules’ around relationships are being eroded as young adults make their own choices about who and when to marry. But perhaps the greatest disparity between the generations can be seen in their attitudes towards gender roles. The traditional roles of men and women, as breadwinners and housewives, are no longer accepted as necessary or appropriate by most younger people."},{"en":"breadwinners and housewives","context":"On the one hand, many of the ideas that elderly people have about life are becoming less relevant for younger people. In the past, for example, people were advised to learn a profession and find a secure job for life, but today's workers expect much more variety and diversity from their careers. At the same time, the “rules’ around relationships are being eroded as young adults make their own choices about who and when to marry. But perhaps the greatest disparity between the generations can be seen in their attitudes towards gender roles. The traditional roles of men and women, as breadwinners and housewives, are no longer accepted as necessary or appropriate by most younger people."},{"en":"taking pride in one's work","context":"On the other hand, some traditional views and values are certainly applicable to the modern world. For example, older generations attach great importance to working hard, doing one's best, and taking pride in one's work, and these behaviours can surely benefit young people as they enter today's competitive job market. Other characteristics that are perhaps seen as traditional are politeness and good manners. In our globalised world, young adults can expect to come into contact with people from a huge variety of backgrounds, and it is more important than ever to treat others with respect. Finally, I believe that young people would lead happier lives if they had a more “old-fashioned’ sense of community and neighbourliness."},{"en":"politeness and good manners","context":"On the other hand, some traditional views and values are certainly applicable to the modern world. For example, older generations attach great importance to working hard, doing one's best, and taking pride in one's work, and these behaviours can surely benefit young people as they enter today's competitive job market. Other characteristics that are perhaps seen as traditional are politeness and good manners. In our globalised world, young adults can expect to come into contact with people from a huge variety of backgrounds, and it is more important than ever to treat others with respect. Finally, I believe that young people would lead happier lives if they had a more “old-fashioned’ sense of community and neighbourliness."},{"en":"a huge variety of backgrounds","context":"On the other hand, some traditional views and values are certainly applicable to the modern world. For example, older generations attach great importance to working hard, doing one's best, and taking pride in one's work, and these behaviours can surely benefit young people as they enter today's competitive job market. Other characteristics that are perhaps seen as traditional are politeness and good manners. In our globalised world, young adults can expect to come into contact with people from a huge variety of backgrounds, and it is more important than ever to treat others with respect. Finally, I believe that young people would lead happier lives if they had a more “old-fashioned’ sense of community and neighbourliness."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#268","summary":"Traditional values in modern life"},{"id":"writing-task-2-145","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Why people define happiness differently","prompt":"Discuss the reasons why people define happiness differently and how to\nobtain happiness?","answer":"People define happiness differently because they have different needs, values and life circumstances. For one person, happiness may mean professional success; for another, it may come from family life, health or personal freedom. In my view, people are most likely to become happy when their goals <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflect their own values rather than social pressure</strong>.\n\nAge and circumstance strongly influence what matters to an individual. A student may value friendship and opportunity, while a parent may place greater importance on security and time with children. Income also changes priorities. Someone struggling to pay for food or housing will naturally connect happiness with financial stability, whereas a wealthy person may seek meaning, relationships or creative achievement. These differences explain why there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no single formula for happiness</strong>.\n\nPersonality is equally important. Some people enjoy competition and public recognition, while others prefer a quiet and predictable life. Culture also shapes expectations about marriage, work and personal independence. Problems arise when people pursue goals mainly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">because those goals appear impressive to others</strong>. A high salary or prestigious title may bring little satisfaction if the daily work conflicts with a person&#x27;s interests or health.\n\nA practical route to happiness is to identify a small number of realistic priorities and invest time in them consistently. Close relationships, useful work, adequate rest and physical health provide a stronger foundation than constant comparison with other people. It is also helpful to accept that happiness is not a permanent emotional state. A good life can include difficulty while still offering <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a stable sense of purpose and contentment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, happiness varies because people differ in both circumstances and values. The best way to pursue it is to set realistic goals, protect important relationships and judge progress by personally meaningful standards.","phrases":[{"en":"reflect their own values rather than social pressure","context":"People define happiness differently because they have different needs, values and life circumstances. For one person, happiness may mean professional success; for another, it may come from family life, health or personal freedom. In my view, people are most likely to become happy when their goals reflect their own values rather than social pressure."},{"en":"no single formula for happiness","context":"Age and circumstance strongly influence what matters to an individual. A student may value friendship and opportunity, while a parent may place greater importance on security and time with children. Income also changes priorities. Someone struggling to pay for food or housing will naturally connect happiness with financial stability, whereas a wealthy person may seek meaning, relationships or creative achievement. These differences explain why there is no single formula for happiness."},{"en":"because those goals appear impressive to others","context":"Personality is equally important. Some people enjoy competition and public recognition, while others prefer a quiet and predictable life. Culture also shapes expectations about marriage, work and personal independence. Problems arise when people pursue goals mainly because those goals appear impressive to others. A high salary or prestigious title may bring little satisfaction if the daily work conflicts with a person's interests or health."},{"en":"a stable sense of purpose and contentment","context":"A practical route to happiness is to identify a small number of realistic priorities and invest time in them consistently. Close relationships, useful work, adequate rest and physical health provide a stronger foundation than constant comparison with other people. It is also helpful to accept that happiness is not a permanent emotional state. A good life can include difficulty while still offering a stable sense of purpose and contentment."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#269","summary":"Discuss the reasons why people define happiness differently and how…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-157","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Is it ever right to lie?","prompt":"Some people think that telling the truth is not always essential. It is necessary to tell lies sometimes. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Honesty is essential for trust, but I do not believe that every fact must be stated in every situation. Small lies can sometimes be justified when they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">prevent unnecessary harm</strong> and do not deceive someone about an important decision.\n\nThe main reason to value truth is that relationships and institutions depend on reliable information. People need accurate facts when they make choices about health, money, work or family matters. A person who lies for personal gain may damage others and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eventually lose credibility</strong>. For this reason, honesty should remain <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the normal rule rather than the exception</strong>.\n\nEven so, complete openness can occasionally be cruel or pointless. A gentle compliment may protect someone&#x27;s feelings, and a surprise party requires temporary secrecy. In more serious cases, a doctor might choose careful language when giving difficult news, although the patient should still receive the information needed to make decisions. The important test is whether the lie is intended to protect another person or merely to protect the liar. A harmless social lie should not be treated in the same way as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberate deception for personal benefit</strong>.\n\nThere is also a risk in allowing any exception at all: the reasoning used to justify a kind lie can just as easily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">excuse a self-interested one</strong>. A manager who quietly delays bad news to avoid alarming staff is using the same logic as someone hiding a gambling debt from their family. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The exception must therefore stay narrow</strong>, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">judged by its effect on the other person</strong> rather than the liar&#x27;s comfort.\n\nIn conclusion, telling the truth is normally the best policy because it creates trust and accountability. Nevertheless, limited exceptions are reasonable when the issue is minor, the intention is kind and no important right is affected. This approach <strong class=\"key-phrase\">balances honesty with compassion</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"prevent unnecessary harm","context":"Honesty is essential for trust, but I do not believe that every fact must be stated in every situation. Small lies can sometimes be justified when they prevent unnecessary harm and do not deceive someone about an important decision."},{"en":"eventually lose credibility","context":"The main reason to value truth is that relationships and institutions depend on reliable information. People need accurate facts when they make choices about health, money, work or family matters. A person who lies for personal gain may damage others and eventually lose credibility. For this reason, honesty should remain the normal rule rather than the exception."},{"en":"the normal rule rather than the exception","context":"The main reason to value truth is that relationships and institutions depend on reliable information. People need accurate facts when they make choices about health, money, work or family matters. A person who lies for personal gain may damage others and eventually lose credibility. For this reason, honesty should remain the normal rule rather than the exception."},{"en":"deliberate deception for personal benefit","context":"Even so, complete openness can occasionally be cruel or pointless. A gentle compliment may protect someone's feelings, and a surprise party requires temporary secrecy. In more serious cases, a doctor might choose careful language when giving difficult news, although the patient should still receive the information needed to make decisions. The important test is whether the lie is intended to protect another person or merely to protect the liar. A harmless social lie should not be treated in the same way as deliberate deception for personal benefit."},{"en":"excuse a self-interested one","context":"There is also a risk in allowing any exception at all: the reasoning used to justify a kind lie can just as easily excuse a self-interested one. A manager who quietly delays bad news to avoid alarming staff is using the same logic as someone hiding a gambling debt from their family. The exception must therefore stay narrow, judged by its effect on the other person rather than the liar's comfort."},{"en":"The exception must therefore stay narrow","context":"There is also a risk in allowing any exception at all: the reasoning used to justify a kind lie can just as easily excuse a self-interested one. A manager who quietly delays bad news to avoid alarming staff is using the same logic as someone hiding a gambling debt from their family. The exception must therefore stay narrow, judged by its effect on the other person rather than the liar's comfort."},{"en":"judged by its effect on the other person","context":"There is also a risk in allowing any exception at all: the reasoning used to justify a kind lie can just as easily excuse a self-interested one. A manager who quietly delays bad news to avoid alarming staff is using the same logic as someone hiding a gambling debt from their family. The exception must therefore stay narrow, judged by its effect on the other person rather than the liar's comfort."},{"en":"balances honesty with compassion","context":"In conclusion, telling the truth is normally the best policy because it creates trust and accountability. Nevertheless, limited exceptions are reasonable when the issue is minor, the intention is kind and no important right is affected. This approach balances honesty with compassion."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#270","summary":"People think that telling the truth is not always essential."},{"id":"writing-task-2-160","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Greed, selfishness and going back to old values","prompt":"Today, people are getting greedy and selfish. Some people argue that we should go back to the old days, and show respect for the family and community, so that we can make the world a better place to live in. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often said that people today are more selfish than previous generations, and that the remedy is to return to older habits of respect for family and community. I largely agree. Individual ambition has brought real benefits, but the weakening of family and neighbourhood ties has left many people materially better off and noticeably more alone.\n\nThe clearest effect is on family life. When every member of a household pursues a separate career and separate interests, the family becomes a set of overlapping schedules rather than a shared life, and the practical exchange it once depended on breaks down: grandparents no longer help with childcare, and adult children are less willing to look after elderly parents. The greater loss is harder to see, because families supply emotional support <strong class=\"key-phrase\">that no paid service can replace</strong>, and people who face illness, redundancy or bereavement without it recover far more slowly.\n\nCommunity life has weakened in the same way. Neighbours who have never spoken will not notice that an elderly resident has not opened her curtains for two days, and volunteering, local sports clubs and residents&#x27; associations all depend on people who are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willing to give time for no personal return</strong>. Selfishness has a commercial form too: a company that raises short-term profit by selling unnecessary products or quietly reducing quality treats its customers as a resource rather than as neighbours, and once that attitude becomes normal, the trust between strangers that makes a city workable begins to disappear.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that greater respect for family and community would improve modern life, although this does not require returning to the past. Keeping in regular contact with relatives, giving a few hours to a local organisation and knowing the people in one&#x27;s own street cost very little individually, yet together they rebuild <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of community</strong> and the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">social cohesion</strong> that competitive, individualistic societies steadily wear away.","phrases":[{"en":"that no paid service can replace","context":"The clearest effect is on family life. When every member of a household pursues a separate career and separate interests, the family becomes a set of overlapping schedules rather than a shared life, and the practical exchange it once depended on breaks down: grandparents no longer help with childcare, and adult children are less willing to look after elderly parents. The greater loss is harder to see, because families supply emotional support that no paid service can replace, and people who face illness, redundancy or bereavement without it recover far more slowly."},{"en":"willing to give time for no personal return","context":"Community life has weakened in the same way. Neighbours who have never spoken will not notice that an elderly resident has not opened her curtains for two days, and volunteering, local sports clubs and residents' associations all depend on people who are willing to give time for no personal return. Selfishness has a commercial form too: a company that raises short-term profit by selling unnecessary products or quietly reducing quality treats its customers as a resource rather than as neighbours, and once that attitude becomes normal, the trust between strangers that makes a city workable begins to disappear."},{"en":"a sense of community","context":"In conclusion, I agree that greater respect for family and community would improve modern life, although this does not require returning to the past. Keeping in regular contact with relatives, giving a few hours to a local organisation and knowing the people in one's own street cost very little individually, yet together they rebuild a sense of community and the social cohesion that competitive, individualistic societies steadily wear away."},{"en":"social cohesion","context":"In conclusion, I agree that greater respect for family and community would improve modern life, although this does not require returning to the past. Keeping in regular contact with relatives, giving a few hours to a local organisation and knowing the people in one's own street cost very little individually, yet together they rebuild a sense of community and the social cohesion that competitive, individualistic societies steadily wear away."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#271","summary":"Today, people are getting greedy and selfish."},{"id":"writing-task-2-2217","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Why people move home more often","prompt":"People used to live in the same city throughout their lives, but now they change where they live several times. What is the cause? Do you think it is a positive or negative trend?","answer":"A generation ago it was normal to be born, work and die in the same town. Today many people move several times, and I regard this as a broadly positive change despite the strain it places on communities.\n\nThe main cause is the way work has changed. Employment is no longer tied to a single local industry, and careers now advance by moving between firms rather than climbing within one, so a graduate may follow openings to three cities in a decade. Housing costs reinforce this: when young families are priced out of the city where they work, they relocate to a cheaper region and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accept a longer commute</strong>. Cheap transport and video calling have also made distance less painful, because a move no longer means losing contact with parents and old friends.\n\nThe clearest benefit is that people can go where their skills are actually wanted. A nurse or an engineer who is unemployed in one region can be in demand in another, and allowing that movement raises both individual incomes and national productivity. Moving also broadens people. Someone who has lived in three different places tends to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">hold their assumptions more loosely</strong>, having seen more than one way of organising ordinary life.\n\nThe cost falls on the places left behind and on the movers themselves. Towns that lose their young people are left with an ageing population and closing schools, while those who move repeatedly may find that they know their neighbours only slightly and have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no one to call on</strong> in a crisis.\n\nIn conclusion, mobility is driven mainly by where work and affordable housing are, and on balance it is positive, because the freedom to move is what allows people to build a life that suits them rather than one <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decided by birthplace</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"accept a longer commute","context":"The main cause is the way work has changed. Employment is no longer tied to a single local industry, and careers now advance by moving between firms rather than climbing within one, so a graduate may follow openings to three cities in a decade. Housing costs reinforce this: when young families are priced out of the city where they work, they relocate to a cheaper region and accept a longer commute. Cheap transport and video calling have also made distance less painful, because a move no longer means losing contact with parents and old friends."},{"en":"hold their assumptions more loosely","context":"The clearest benefit is that people can go where their skills are actually wanted. A nurse or an engineer who is unemployed in one region can be in demand in another, and allowing that movement raises both individual incomes and national productivity. Moving also broadens people. Someone who has lived in three different places tends to hold their assumptions more loosely, having seen more than one way of organising ordinary life."},{"en":"no one to call on","context":"The cost falls on the places left behind and on the movers themselves. Towns that lose their young people are left with an ageing population and closing schools, while those who move repeatedly may find that they know their neighbours only slightly and have no one to call on in a crisis."},{"en":"decided by birthplace","context":"In conclusion, mobility is driven mainly by where work and affordable housing are, and on balance it is positive, because the freedom to move is what allows people to build a life that suits them rather than one decided by birthplace."}],"subtype":"Causes / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#272","summary":"People changing where they live several times in a lifetime"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2226","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"The demand for instant results","prompt":"Nowadays more and more people want to get things done instantly, whether that is a service, information or a task. Why is this? Do you think this is a positive or negative development?","answer":"People now expect a delivery within hours, an answer within seconds and a reply to a message almost immediately. This expectation has been created by technology itself, and although speed brings genuine benefits, I regard the trend as negative overall.\n\nThe main cause is that a small number of companies have made instant service normal and everything else feel broken. Once a search engine returns results in a fraction of a second and a film starts playing the moment it is selected, a two-day wait for a bank transfer <strong class=\"key-phrase\">feels like an error rather than a process</strong>. Competition then spreads the standard, because a retailer offering delivery in three days simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">loses customers to a faster rival</strong>. Working life pushes in the same direction: employees are reachable at all hours, so a message sent in the evening is expected to be answered in the evening.\n\nThe advantages are real. Instant access to information saves an enormous amount of ordinary time, and services that once required a morning in a queue now take a minute, which matters most to people who cannot easily take time off work.\n\nMy concern is with what speed displaces. Work that is genuinely valuable — writing, research, learning a skill, recovering from illness — cannot be accelerated, and a population trained to expect immediate results becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unwilling to persist</strong> when something takes months. Decisions made quickly are also made badly, and the pressure to respond at once leaves people permanently on call and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">measurably more anxious</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the expectation of instant results comes from technology setting a standard that then spreads to everything else. I consider it a negative development, because the things that most improve a life are precisely the ones that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be hurried</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"feels like an error rather than a process","context":"The main cause is that a small number of companies have made instant service normal and everything else feel broken. Once a search engine returns results in a fraction of a second and a film starts playing the moment it is selected, a two-day wait for a bank transfer feels like an error rather than a process. Competition then spreads the standard, because a retailer offering delivery in three days simply loses customers to a faster rival. Working life pushes in the same direction: employees are reachable at all hours, so a message sent in the evening is expected to be answered in the evening."},{"en":"loses customers to a faster rival","context":"The main cause is that a small number of companies have made instant service normal and everything else feel broken. Once a search engine returns results in a fraction of a second and a film starts playing the moment it is selected, a two-day wait for a bank transfer feels like an error rather than a process. Competition then spreads the standard, because a retailer offering delivery in three days simply loses customers to a faster rival. Working life pushes in the same direction: employees are reachable at all hours, so a message sent in the evening is expected to be answered in the evening."},{"en":"unwilling to persist","context":"My concern is with what speed displaces. Work that is genuinely valuable — writing, research, learning a skill, recovering from illness — cannot be accelerated, and a population trained to expect immediate results becomes unwilling to persist when something takes months. Decisions made quickly are also made badly, and the pressure to respond at once leaves people permanently on call and measurably more anxious."},{"en":"measurably more anxious","context":"My concern is with what speed displaces. Work that is genuinely valuable — writing, research, learning a skill, recovering from illness — cannot be accelerated, and a population trained to expect immediate results becomes unwilling to persist when something takes months. Decisions made quickly are also made badly, and the pressure to respond at once leaves people permanently on call and measurably more anxious."},{"en":"cannot be hurried","context":"In conclusion, the expectation of instant results comes from technology setting a standard that then spreads to everything else. I consider it a negative development, because the things that most improve a life are precisely the ones that cannot be hurried."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#273","summary":"Why people increasingly expect things to be done instantly"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2262","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"The gap between generations","prompt":"Nowadays in many countries young people do not communicate with older people as much as they used to. Why is this happening? How could this situation be improved?","answer":"Contact between generations has weakened in many societies, and older people now report far fewer conversations with anyone under thirty. The causes are structural rather than personal, and they can be reduced by rebuilding the places where the two groups used to meet.\n\nThe main cause is that families no longer live together. Where three generations once shared a house or a street, young adults now move to cities for work while parents and grandparents remain behind, so contact becomes an occasional visit rather than a daily habit. Housing costs push in the same direction, since few young families can afford a home large enough for an older relative.\n\nA second cause is that daily life has separated by age. Schools, workplaces and retirement housing each contain a single generation, and much social contact has moved onto platforms that older people use least, so a young person may pass an entire week <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without speaking to anyone over fifty</strong>. Both sides then find conversation harder, and each assumes the other <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has nothing in common with them</strong>.\n\nThe remedies follow from the causes. Schools can arrange regular visits in which older residents talk about local history or listen to children read, which gives both a reason to meet rather than a duty. Housing policy matters as well: developments that place family homes and retirement flats around a shared garden produce contact <strong class=\"key-phrase\">as a by-product of ordinary life</strong>. Volunteering schemes that pair students with older neighbours for shopping or technology help work in the same way.\n\nIn conclusion, the distance between generations has grown because families have dispersed and daily life is now organised by age. It narrows whenever the two groups are given <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a shared task rather than an appointment</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"without speaking to anyone over fifty","context":"A second cause is that daily life has separated by age. Schools, workplaces and retirement housing each contain a single generation, and much social contact has moved onto platforms that older people use least, so a young person may pass an entire week without speaking to anyone over fifty. Both sides then find conversation harder, and each assumes the other has nothing in common with them."},{"en":"has nothing in common with them","context":"A second cause is that daily life has separated by age. Schools, workplaces and retirement housing each contain a single generation, and much social contact has moved onto platforms that older people use least, so a young person may pass an entire week without speaking to anyone over fifty. Both sides then find conversation harder, and each assumes the other has nothing in common with them."},{"en":"as a by-product of ordinary life","context":"The remedies follow from the causes. Schools can arrange regular visits in which older residents talk about local history or listen to children read, which gives both a reason to meet rather than a duty. Housing policy matters as well: developments that place family homes and retirement flats around a shared garden produce contact as a by-product of ordinary life. Volunteering schemes that pair students with older neighbours for shopping or technology help work in the same way."},{"en":"a shared task rather than an appointment","context":"In conclusion, the distance between generations has grown because families have dispersed and daily life is now organised by age. It narrows whenever the two groups are given a shared task rather than an appointment."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#274","summary":"Why young people communicate less with older people"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2265","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Is competitiveness a good quality?","prompt":"In many societies, competitiveness is regarded as an important personal quality. How does this affect individuals? Do you think this is a beneficial or harmful trend?","answer":"Many cultures treat the desire to outperform others as a virtue and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reward it from primary school onwards</strong>. The effects on individuals run in both directions, and I regard the trend as harmful once competition becomes the main measure of a person.\n\nThe positive effects are worth acknowledging. Competition supplies motivation that is difficult to generate any other way, since most people work harder when their performance is visible and compared. It also produces useful information: an athlete or a researcher discovers where they actually stand, which allows them to improve rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assume they are already good enough</strong>. Societies that reward achievement rather than family connections also allow talented people to rise from unpromising beginnings.\n\nThe harmful effects appear when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competitiveness becomes an identity</strong>. A person who measures their worth by ranking is anxious whenever they are not winning, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">since there is always somebody ahead</strong>, the anxiety is permanent. This shows in the mental health of students in the most competitive education systems, where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rates of exhaustion and depression are highest</strong>. Highly competitive individuals also cooperate poorly, because sharing an idea with a colleague means giving away an advantage, and most valuable work now depends on precisely that sharing.\n\nThere is a further cost. Competition rewards what can be measured, so people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">abandon work that matters but cannot be scored</strong>, and choose safe targets they are confident of hitting rather than difficult problems where they might <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fail in front of others</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, competitiveness raises effort and identifies talent, and a society without it would stagnate. The current emphasis is nonetheless harmful, because treating comparison as the measure of a person produces anxious individuals who cannot work together and who avoid the risks that real progress requires.","phrases":[{"en":"reward it from primary school onwards","context":"Many cultures treat the desire to outperform others as a virtue and reward it from primary school onwards. The effects on individuals run in both directions, and I regard the trend as harmful once competition becomes the main measure of a person."},{"en":"assume they are already good enough","context":"The positive effects are worth acknowledging. Competition supplies motivation that is difficult to generate any other way, since most people work harder when their performance is visible and compared. It also produces useful information: an athlete or a researcher discovers where they actually stand, which allows them to improve rather than assume they are already good enough. Societies that reward achievement rather than family connections also allow talented people to rise from unpromising beginnings."},{"en":"competitiveness becomes an identity","context":"The harmful effects appear when competitiveness becomes an identity. A person who measures their worth by ranking is anxious whenever they are not winning, and since there is always somebody ahead, the anxiety is permanent. This shows in the mental health of students in the most competitive education systems, where rates of exhaustion and depression are highest. Highly competitive individuals also cooperate poorly, because sharing an idea with a colleague means giving away an advantage, and most valuable work now depends on precisely that sharing."},{"en":"since there is always somebody ahead","context":"The harmful effects appear when competitiveness becomes an identity. A person who measures their worth by ranking is anxious whenever they are not winning, and since there is always somebody ahead, the anxiety is permanent. This shows in the mental health of students in the most competitive education systems, where rates of exhaustion and depression are highest. Highly competitive individuals also cooperate poorly, because sharing an idea with a colleague means giving away an advantage, and most valuable work now depends on precisely that sharing."},{"en":"rates of exhaustion and depression are highest","context":"The harmful effects appear when competitiveness becomes an identity. A person who measures their worth by ranking is anxious whenever they are not winning, and since there is always somebody ahead, the anxiety is permanent. This shows in the mental health of students in the most competitive education systems, where rates of exhaustion and depression are highest. Highly competitive individuals also cooperate poorly, because sharing an idea with a colleague means giving away an advantage, and most valuable work now depends on precisely that sharing."},{"en":"abandon work that matters but cannot be scored","context":"There is a further cost. Competition rewards what can be measured, so people abandon work that matters but cannot be scored, and choose safe targets they are confident of hitting rather than difficult problems where they might fail in front of others."},{"en":"fail in front of others","context":"There is a further cost. Competition rewards what can be measured, so people abandon work that matters but cannot be scored, and choose safe targets they are confident of hitting rather than difficult problems where they might fail in front of others."}],"subtype":"Effects / Beneficial or Harmful","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#275","summary":"How valuing competitiveness affects individuals"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2267","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Is life better than it used to be?","prompt":"People living in the 21st century have a better quality of life than people living in previous centuries. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that modern life, for all its conveniences, is more stressful and less satisfying than life in earlier times. I disagree with this view, because the improvements in the conditions that determine whether a life goes well have been extraordinary.\n\nThe most important change is that people survive. Global life expectancy has roughly doubled since 1900, and the fall in child mortality means that most parents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no longer expect to bury a child</strong>, which was a normal experience for previous generations. Diseases that once killed routinely are now prevented by vaccination, and injuries that were fatal are treated in an afternoon. Any comparison that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">overlooks this outweighs everything else</strong>.\n\nMaterial and social conditions have improved alongside. The share of the world living in extreme poverty has fallen sharply within a single lifetime, literacy is close to universal in most countries, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">work that once destroyed bodies by forty</strong> is now done by machines. Groups who were legally excluded for centuries — women, minorities, people with disabilities — have rights that would have been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unimaginable to their great-grandparents</strong>.\n\nThe honest objection is that some things have deteriorated. Anxiety and loneliness are widespread, communities are looser than they were, and previous centuries did not face the possibility of environmental collapse. These are serious and should not be dismissed.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree strongly with any suggestion that earlier centuries offered a better life. Modern societies have real problems of isolation and environmental risk, and solving them matters, but they are the difficulties of people who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expect to live into their eighties</strong> — a position almost nobody in history would have refused.","phrases":[{"en":"no longer expect to bury a child","context":"The most important change is that people survive. Global life expectancy has roughly doubled since 1900, and the fall in child mortality means that most parents no longer expect to bury a child, which was a normal experience for previous generations. Diseases that once killed routinely are now prevented by vaccination, and injuries that were fatal are treated in an afternoon. Any comparison that overlooks this outweighs everything else."},{"en":"overlooks this outweighs everything else","context":"The most important change is that people survive. Global life expectancy has roughly doubled since 1900, and the fall in child mortality means that most parents no longer expect to bury a child, which was a normal experience for previous generations. Diseases that once killed routinely are now prevented by vaccination, and injuries that were fatal are treated in an afternoon. Any comparison that overlooks this outweighs everything else."},{"en":"work that once destroyed bodies by forty","context":"Material and social conditions have improved alongside. The share of the world living in extreme poverty has fallen sharply within a single lifetime, literacy is close to universal in most countries, and work that once destroyed bodies by forty is now done by machines. Groups who were legally excluded for centuries — women, minorities, people with disabilities — have rights that would have been unimaginable to their great-grandparents."},{"en":"unimaginable to their great-grandparents","context":"Material and social conditions have improved alongside. The share of the world living in extreme poverty has fallen sharply within a single lifetime, literacy is close to universal in most countries, and work that once destroyed bodies by forty is now done by machines. Groups who were legally excluded for centuries — women, minorities, people with disabilities — have rights that would have been unimaginable to their great-grandparents."},{"en":"expect to live into their eighties","context":"In conclusion, I disagree strongly with any suggestion that earlier centuries offered a better life. Modern societies have real problems of isolation and environmental risk, and solving them matters, but they are the difficulties of people who expect to live into their eighties — a position almost nobody in history would have refused."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#276","summary":"Quality of life today compared with previous centuries"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2307","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"What actually produces success?","prompt":"Some people believe that success is the result of hard work and determination. Others think that there are more important factors, such as money and appearance. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Whether success is earned or inherited is one of the oldest arguments about how societies work, and both answers can be supported with plenty of examples. My own view is that effort decides what a person does with an opportunity, while money and appearance largely decide which opportunities arrive.\n\nThe case for effort is supported by what any career actually requires. Skill in medicine, music or business is built through years of practice that nobody enjoys, and people who begin with every advantage still fail without it. Determination also explains recovery, since almost every successful career contains a serious setback, and the difference between those who continue and those who stop is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">willingness to start again after a failure</strong>. Believing that effort matters is itself useful, because people who think outcomes are fixed stop trying.\n\nThe opposing view is uncomfortable but well evidenced. Wealth buys tutoring, unpaid internships, useful contacts and the ability to take risks without ruin, so two equally able children finish in very different places. Appearance also has measurable effects on hiring, promotion and pay, and connections open doors that no application will. Anyone who insists that success is purely earned has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">usually not looked closely at where they began</strong>.\n\nThe two combine rather than compete. Advantages of money and appearance move a person nearer the front of the queue, but they do not complete the work, and privileged people who do nothing are visible in every field. Equally, effort without opportunity is frequently wasted, which is why talented people in poor countries or poor districts <strong class=\"key-phrase\">achieve less than mediocre people elsewhere</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, hard work is necessary and explains why some people succeed while others with the same advantages do not. Money, contacts and appearance decide how far that work carries, so a fair society is one that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduces how much the starting point matters</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"willingness to start again after a failure","context":"The case for effort is supported by what any career actually requires. Skill in medicine, music or business is built through years of practice that nobody enjoys, and people who begin with every advantage still fail without it. Determination also explains recovery, since almost every successful career contains a serious setback, and the difference between those who continue and those who stop is willingness to start again after a failure. Believing that effort matters is itself useful, because people who think outcomes are fixed stop trying."},{"en":"usually not looked closely at where they began","context":"The opposing view is uncomfortable but well evidenced. Wealth buys tutoring, unpaid internships, useful contacts and the ability to take risks without ruin, so two equally able children finish in very different places. Appearance also has measurable effects on hiring, promotion and pay, and connections open doors that no application will. Anyone who insists that success is purely earned has usually not looked closely at where they began."},{"en":"achieve less than mediocre people elsewhere","context":"The two combine rather than compete. Advantages of money and appearance move a person nearer the front of the queue, but they do not complete the work, and privileged people who do nothing are visible in every field. Equally, effort without opportunity is frequently wasted, which is why talented people in poor countries or poor districts achieve less than mediocre people elsewhere."},{"en":"reduces how much the starting point matters","context":"In conclusion, hard work is necessary and explains why some people succeed while others with the same advantages do not. Money, contacts and appearance decide how far that work carries, so a fair society is one that reduces how much the starting point matters."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#277","summary":"Whether success comes from effort or from money and appearance"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2311","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Is ambition a good quality?","prompt":"Some people think that having ambition is a good quality. Is it important to be ambitious? Is it a positive or negative characteristic?","answer":"Ambition is praised in job interviews and warned against in old proverbs. It is genuinely important for anyone who wants to achieve something demanding, and it is a positive quality up to the point where the goal begins to matter more than the person pursuing it.\n\nAmbition matters because difficult things take years and offer little reward along the way. A surgeon studies for a decade, a novelist writes badly before writing well, and neither continues on enjoyment alone. What carries a person through the dull middle of any long project is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a picture of where the effort leads</strong>. Ambition also raises the target: people tend to achieve roughly what they aim at, so those who aim low finish low, and quiet ability without direction usually goes nowhere in particular.\n\nIt is a positive characteristic for the same reason. Ambitious individuals learn faster, because they seek out criticism instead of avoiding it, and societies depend on them, since medicine, engineering and the arts advance through people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unwilling to accept the current limit</strong>.\n\nThe quality turns harmful when it detaches from any purpose beyond status. A person who wants promotion rather than the work will take credit, hide mistakes and treat colleagues as obstacles. Ambition of that kind also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">makes satisfaction permanently unavailable</strong>, since each achievement resets the target, and the years spent chasing it are usually taken from health and family. Its worst form is measuring yourself entirely against other people.\n\nIn conclusion, ambition is important and largely positive, because sustained effort needs a destination. It becomes damaging when the goal is standing rather than substance, so the useful version is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ambition attached to the work itself</strong> rather than to the recognition it brings.","phrases":[{"en":"a picture of where the effort leads","context":"Ambition matters because difficult things take years and offer little reward along the way. A surgeon studies for a decade, a novelist writes badly before writing well, and neither continues on enjoyment alone. What carries a person through the dull middle of any long project is a picture of where the effort leads. Ambition also raises the target: people tend to achieve roughly what they aim at, so those who aim low finish low, and quiet ability without direction usually goes nowhere in particular."},{"en":"unwilling to accept the current limit","context":"It is a positive characteristic for the same reason. Ambitious individuals learn faster, because they seek out criticism instead of avoiding it, and societies depend on them, since medicine, engineering and the arts advance through people unwilling to accept the current limit."},{"en":"makes satisfaction permanently unavailable","context":"The quality turns harmful when it detaches from any purpose beyond status. A person who wants promotion rather than the work will take credit, hide mistakes and treat colleagues as obstacles. Ambition of that kind also makes satisfaction permanently unavailable, since each achievement resets the target, and the years spent chasing it are usually taken from health and family. Its worst form is measuring yourself entirely against other people."},{"en":"ambition attached to the work itself","context":"In conclusion, ambition is important and largely positive, because sustained effort needs a destination. It becomes damaging when the goal is standing rather than substance, so the useful version is ambition attached to the work itself rather than to the recognition it brings."}],"subtype":"Two-Part Question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#278","summary":"How much ambition matters for success and whether it is a positive trait"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2316","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Film or documents for learning about the past?","prompt":"It is better to learn about the way people lived in the past through films and video records than through written documents. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Documentaries and archive footage now reach far more people than any history book, and they clearly teach something a page cannot. I disagree that they are the better source overall, though the two work best together.\n\nFilm has genuine advantages. It shows what writing can only describe: how a street sounded, how quickly people walked, what a kitchen actually contained. Details of daily life that a diarist never bothered to record survive in the background of a recording, and viewers remember them because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">images stay in the mind longer than sentences</strong>. Film also reaches those who would never open a scholarly volume, and recorded interviews preserve the voices of people who left nothing written at all.\n\nIts weaknesses are equally clear. Recording covers barely a century, so anything earlier must be reconstructed by actors, and a costume drama tells us mainly what its own decade believed. Film is also shaped by whoever holds the camera: it selects, edits and adds music, and the result feels like evidence while being an argument. Written sources are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easier to check against one another</strong>, since a reader can compare accounts, weigh a letter against an official record and see where the writer had reason to lie.\n\nDocuments also carry what film cannot. Wills, tax records and court papers reveal what ordinary people owned and quarrelled over, and letters show what they thought, which no camera can film. History depends on that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">slow comparison of unglamorous material</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, film brings the past close and should be used widely, particularly for the recent period it genuinely covers. Written records remain the stronger foundation, because they reach further back and can be tested, and the sensible approach is to use film for texture and documents for evidence.","phrases":[{"en":"images stay in the mind longer than sentences","context":"Film has genuine advantages. It shows what writing can only describe: how a street sounded, how quickly people walked, what a kitchen actually contained. Details of daily life that a diarist never bothered to record survive in the background of a recording, and viewers remember them because images stay in the mind longer than sentences. Film also reaches those who would never open a scholarly volume, and recorded interviews preserve the voices of people who left nothing written at all."},{"en":"easier to check against one another","context":"Its weaknesses are equally clear. Recording covers barely a century, so anything earlier must be reconstructed by actors, and a costume drama tells us mainly what its own decade believed. Film is also shaped by whoever holds the camera: it selects, edits and adds music, and the result feels like evidence while being an argument. Written sources are easier to check against one another, since a reader can compare accounts, weigh a letter against an official record and see where the writer had reason to lie."},{"en":"slow comparison of unglamorous material","context":"Documents also carry what film cannot. Wills, tax records and court papers reveal what ordinary people owned and quarrelled over, and letters show what they thought, which no camera can film. History depends on that slow comparison of unglamorous material."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#279","summary":"Whether films and recordings teach history better than written documents"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2319","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"A growing population of children","prompt":"In some countries, the number of children aged 15 and younger is increasing dramatically. What are the current and future effects of this growing population?","answer":"In parts of Africa and South Asia, children now make up close to half the population. The immediate effects fall on services and family budgets, while the longer-term consequences depend almost entirely on what those children receive today.\n\nThe present effects are felt first in schools and clinics. Classrooms fill faster than they can be built, so teaching quality falls even where enrolment rises, and paediatric wards and vaccination programmes come under similar strain. Households feel it too: a family with six children spends everything it earns on food and school fees, which leaves nothing to save or invest, and older girls are often kept at home to look after the younger ones. Governments face the same squeeze at national level, since money spent on expanding basic services cannot go into roads or industry.\n\nThe future effects divide sharply. If these children are educated and healthy, the country receives what economists call a demographic dividend: a large working population supporting comparatively few dependents, which is the condition behind almost every rapid rise in national income. Several Asian economies grew precisely this way.\n\nIf the investment is not made, the same numbers become a burden. Millions of young adults will reach working age without qualifications and without jobs waiting for them, and large groups of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unemployed young men in crowded cities</strong> are associated with unrest and with heavy migration. The population will also age eventually, so a country that fails to build wealth while its workers are numerous will meet that stage poor.\n\nIn conclusion, a rapidly growing child population strains schools, health services and family finances now. Whether it later becomes an advantage or a crisis is decided by what is invested in those children before they grow up.","phrases":[{"en":"unemployed young men in crowded cities","context":"If the investment is not made, the same numbers become a burden. Millions of young adults will reach working age without qualifications and without jobs waiting for them, and large groups of unemployed young men in crowded cities are associated with unrest and with heavy migration. The population will also age eventually, so a country that fails to build wealth while its workers are numerous will meet that stage poor."}],"subtype":"Effects","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#280","summary":"The present and future effects of a rapidly rising number of children"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2366","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"What would make a society perfect?","prompt":"People have always imagined living in a perfect society, but there is no clear agreement on what that would look like. What do you think is the most important factor in creating a perfect society? How can this ideal be achieved?","answer":"Descriptions of the ideal society differ enormously, but most of them assume something they rarely state. In my view the essential factor is trust, and it is built through institutions that treat people equally rather than through any grand design.\n\nTrust deserves first place because everything else depends on it. Where people expect honesty, contracts are cheap to make, taxes are paid without heavy enforcement, strangers help each other and neighbours leave their children to play outside. Where trust is absent, the same society needs guards, lawyers, locks and bribes, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">wealth is spent protecting people from each other</strong>. Countries that score highly on trust also report the highest satisfaction, regardless of climate or size, which suggests it matters more than prosperity alone.\n\nTrust rests on two conditions. The first is that rules apply to everybody, since nothing destroys confidence faster than watching the powerful escape consequences that fall on ordinary people. The second is that inequality remains within limits people regard as fair, because a society divided into groups that share no schools, hospitals or streets <strong class=\"key-phrase\">loses the experience of having anything in common</strong>.\n\nAchieving this is slow work rather than a single reform. Courts and police must be independent enough to convict the influential, and corruption must be prosecuted visibly. Public services that everyone uses — schools, transport, health care — keep different groups in contact, and taxation must be seen to be collected fairly from those best able to pay. Education plays a part by teaching children to disagree without treating opponents as enemies.\n\nIn conclusion, no society will be perfect, and the attempt to design one has usually ended badly. A society where people can reasonably trust each other and the rules comes closest, and it is built by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enforcing fairness consistently over a long period</strong> rather than by any single change.","phrases":[{"en":"wealth is spent protecting people from each other","context":"Trust deserves first place because everything else depends on it. Where people expect honesty, contracts are cheap to make, taxes are paid without heavy enforcement, strangers help each other and neighbours leave their children to play outside. Where trust is absent, the same society needs guards, lawyers, locks and bribes, and wealth is spent protecting people from each other. Countries that score highly on trust also report the highest satisfaction, regardless of climate or size, which suggests it matters more than prosperity alone."},{"en":"loses the experience of having anything in common","context":"Trust rests on two conditions. The first is that rules apply to everybody, since nothing destroys confidence faster than watching the powerful escape consequences that fall on ordinary people. The second is that inequality remains within limits people regard as fair, because a society divided into groups that share no schools, hospitals or streets loses the experience of having anything in common."},{"en":"enforcing fairness consistently over a long period","context":"In conclusion, no society will be perfect, and the attempt to design one has usually ended badly. A society where people can reasonably trust each other and the rules comes closest, and it is built by enforcing fairness consistently over a long period rather than by any single change."}],"subtype":"Two-Part Question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#281","summary":"The most important element of an ideal society and how to move towards it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2368","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Society","title":"Why people are rarely at home","prompt":"Today, many people spend less and less time at home. What are the reasons for this? What are the effects on individuals and on society?","answer":"Homes in many cities now stand empty for most of the day. The reasons are largely economic, and the effects reach both individual health and the character of neighbourhoods.\n\nWork explains most of it. Housing costs have risen faster than wages, so households increasingly need two incomes and often more than one job each, and the same cost pushes families to live far from where they work, adding hours of commuting to an already long day. In many countries staying late is also expected regardless of output. Beyond work, life has moved outwards: shopping, eating, exercising and socialising all happen in commercial spaces rather than at home, and children&#x27;s activities are organised and located elsewhere. Small flats contribute as well, since a home too cramped to relax in becomes somewhere to sleep.\n\nThe effects on individuals are mostly negative. Long days away shorten sleep and leave little time for cooking, which shows in diet and weight. Family relationships suffer in a quieter way, since parents and children pass each other rather than eat together, and much of what children learn from adults happens in unhurried time that no longer exists. Eating and entertaining outside the home is also expensive, so households spend more precisely because they are at home less.\n\nSociety feels it too. Neighbourhoods where nobody is present during the day lose their informal supervision, so streets feel less safe and elderly residents go unchecked. Local shops close for lack of daytime customers, and community activity depends on volunteers who no longer have the hours. There is one encouraging countercurrent, since working from home has begun to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">return some daytime life to residential streets</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, people are absent from home mainly because of long hours and long journeys forced by the cost of housing. The consequences are worse health, thinner family life and neighbourhoods that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">empty out for most of the day</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"return some daytime life to residential streets","context":"Society feels it too. Neighbourhoods where nobody is present during the day lose their informal supervision, so streets feel less safe and elderly residents go unchecked. Local shops close for lack of daytime customers, and community activity depends on volunteers who no longer have the hours. There is one encouraging countercurrent, since working from home has begun to return some daytime life to residential streets."},{"en":"empty out for most of the day","context":"In conclusion, people are absent from home mainly because of long hours and long journeys forced by the cost of housing. The consequences are worse health, thinner family life and neighbourhoods that empty out for most of the day."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Effects","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#282","summary":"The reasons people spend less time at home and the effects of this"},{"id":"writing-task-2-034","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"The advantages and disadvantages of sport","prompt":"Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of playing sport and taking regular physical exercise, and give your own opinion.","answer":"Playing sport and taking regular exercise are widely encouraged, though both carry certain costs. In my view the advantages are considerably greater than the disadvantages.\n\nThe benefits to health are the clearest. Regular physical activity reduces the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes, stroke and several cancers, and it is one of the most effective treatments available for anxiety and mild depression. It also protects people in later life, since those who have exercised consistently <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retain mobility and independence</strong> far longer than those who have not.\n\nSport also develops qualities that transfer to other areas of life. Training for a competition teaches discipline and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the acceptance that improvement is gradual</strong>, while team sports require cooperation and the ability to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lose without resentment</strong>. For children in particular, a club provides a structured environment and adult supervision outside school hours, and it is one of the few settings where they meet people from different backgrounds on equal terms.\n\nThere are genuine drawbacks. Injuries are common, and contact sports such as rugby and boxing carry a risk of lasting damage, including to the brain. Serious training also demands time and money that a family may not have, and excessive competition can produce eating disorders or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an unhealthy obsession with results</strong>, especially among young athletes pushed too hard.\n\nIn conclusion, sport carries real risks of injury and, at a competitive level, considerable psychological pressure. For the great majority of people, however, these drawbacks are far outweighed by the benefits to physical health, mental well-being and social development, and they can be reduced further by sensible coaching.","phrases":[{"en":"retain mobility and independence","context":"The benefits to health are the clearest. Regular physical activity reduces the risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes, stroke and several cancers, and it is one of the most effective treatments available for anxiety and mild depression. It also protects people in later life, since those who have exercised consistently retain mobility and independence far longer than those who have not."},{"en":"the acceptance that improvement is gradual","context":"Sport also develops qualities that transfer to other areas of life. Training for a competition teaches discipline and the acceptance that improvement is gradual, while team sports require cooperation and the ability to lose without resentment. For children in particular, a club provides a structured environment and adult supervision outside school hours, and it is one of the few settings where they meet people from different backgrounds on equal terms."},{"en":"lose without resentment","context":"Sport also develops qualities that transfer to other areas of life. Training for a competition teaches discipline and the acceptance that improvement is gradual, while team sports require cooperation and the ability to lose without resentment. For children in particular, a club provides a structured environment and adult supervision outside school hours, and it is one of the few settings where they meet people from different backgrounds on equal terms."},{"en":"an unhealthy obsession with results","context":"There are genuine drawbacks. Injuries are common, and contact sports such as rugby and boxing carry a risk of lasting damage, including to the brain. Serious training also demands time and money that a family may not have, and excessive competition can produce eating disorders or an unhealthy obsession with results, especially among young athletes pushed too hard."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","_displayNo":"#283","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of sport"},{"id":"writing-task-2-105","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"The future of major sporting events","prompt":"International sporting events such as the Olympic Games are becoming increasingly expensive to host. Some people believe that they will gradually lose their importance. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"International sporting events such as the Olympic Games now cost host cities billions of dollars, and some people predict that they will gradually lose their importance. I disagree with this view, although I accept that the way such events are organised will have to change.\n\nThere are certainly reasons to be pessimistic. Recent Games have left several host cities with enormous debts and stadiums that stand empty afterwards, and a number of cities have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">withdrawn their bids after residents voted against them</strong>. Doping scandals and disputes over political influence have also damaged public confidence. Meanwhile, audiences can now watch elite sport of every kind at any time, so a single event no longer feels as rare as it once did.\n\nNevertheless, the underlying appeal remains extremely strong. The Olympic Games attract billions of viewers because they offer something no ordinary competition can: the world&#x27;s best athletes, in dozens of sports, competing under a national flag every four years. Broadcasters and sponsors <strong class=\"key-phrase\">continue to pay record sums for the rights</strong>, which is the clearest evidence that interest has not declined. These events also give minority sports such as fencing and rowing <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their only global audience</strong>.\n\nWhat is likely to change is the model rather than the event itself. Future competitions will probably use existing venues, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">be shared between several cities</strong> and be organised on a far more modest budget, as recent hosts have already begun to do.\n\nIn conclusion, I do not believe that major international sporting events will fade away. Their financial structure will have to become more realistic, but their appeal to audiences <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shows no sign of weakening</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"withdrawn their bids after residents voted against them","context":"There are certainly reasons to be pessimistic. Recent Games have left several host cities with enormous debts and stadiums that stand empty afterwards, and a number of cities have withdrawn their bids after residents voted against them. Doping scandals and disputes over political influence have also damaged public confidence. Meanwhile, audiences can now watch elite sport of every kind at any time, so a single event no longer feels as rare as it once did."},{"en":"continue to pay record sums for the rights","context":"Nevertheless, the underlying appeal remains extremely strong. The Olympic Games attract billions of viewers because they offer something no ordinary competition can: the world's best athletes, in dozens of sports, competing under a national flag every four years. Broadcasters and sponsors continue to pay record sums for the rights, which is the clearest evidence that interest has not declined. These events also give minority sports such as fencing and rowing their only global audience."},{"en":"their only global audience","context":"Nevertheless, the underlying appeal remains extremely strong. The Olympic Games attract billions of viewers because they offer something no ordinary competition can: the world's best athletes, in dozens of sports, competing under a national flag every four years. Broadcasters and sponsors continue to pay record sums for the rights, which is the clearest evidence that interest has not declined. These events also give minority sports such as fencing and rowing their only global audience."},{"en":"be shared between several cities","context":"What is likely to change is the model rather than the event itself. Future competitions will probably use existing venues, be shared between several cities and be organised on a far more modest budget, as recent hosts have already begun to do."},{"en":"shows no sign of weakening","context":"In conclusion, I do not believe that major international sporting events will fade away. Their financial structure will have to become more realistic, but their appeal to audiences shows no sign of weakening."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#284","summary":"The future of major sporting events"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Why athletes use banned substances","prompt":"In many professional sports, there is an increase in the number of athletes using banned substances to improve their performance. What are the causes of the phenomenon and what are some of the possible solutions?","answer":"The use of banned performance-enhancing drugs is increasing in some professional sports. The main causes are intense financial pressure, weak detection and a culture in which winning is valued above long-term health. Solutions must address both athletes and the organisations around them.\n\nElite competitors may believe that a small physical advantage can determine contracts, sponsorship and selection. If they suspect that rivals are already using drugs, they may feel forced to do the same. Coaches, doctors or managers can also encourage abuse when their own careers depend on results. This creates <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a powerful incentive to take hidden risks</strong>.\n\nSports bodies should use independent testing, share information internationally and impose penalties on support staff as well as athletes. Testing should be frequent and unpredictable, with samples stored for later analysis as technology improves. At the same time, athletes need confidential medical advice and clear education about long-term harm. Whistle-blowers should receive protection so that organised doping systems are easier to expose.\n\nThe financial pressure that drives athletes to dope in the first place also needs to be addressed directly. Sponsors and broadcasters could link the size of their contracts to a federation&#x27;s testing record rather than only to results, giving the sport itself a commercial reason to catch cheats rather than protect its stars. Funding for retired or injured athletes, whose careers can otherwise end overnight, would also reduce the desperation that pushes some competitors toward a short-lived advantage.\n\nIn conclusion, punishment alone will not solve the problem. Sport must reduce the pressure that rewards cheating and increase the chance that abuse is detected. A combination of independent oversight, education and protection for honest athletes can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">restore trust in competition</strong>.","topic":"Sport & Leisure","phrases":[{"en":"a powerful incentive to take hidden risks","context":"Elite competitors may believe that a small physical advantage can determine contracts, sponsorship and selection. If they suspect that rivals are already using drugs, they may feel forced to do the same. Coaches, doctors or managers can also encourage abuse when their own careers depend on results. This creates a powerful incentive to take hidden risks."},{"en":"restore trust in competition","context":"In conclusion, punishment alone will not solve the problem. Sport must reduce the pressure that rewards cheating and increase the chance that abuse is detected. A combination of independent oversight, education and protection for honest athletes can restore trust in competition."}],"subtype":"problem","summary":"Why athletes use banned substances, and what can be done","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-122","_displayNo":"#285"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Natural talent against training","prompt":"It is generally believed that some people are born with certain talents, for instance for sport or music, and others are not. However, it is sometimes claimed that any child can be taught to become a good sports person or musician. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Natural ability can give people an early advantage in music or sport, but high performance is rarely the result of talent alone. I believe most people can reach a strong level through good teaching and sustained practice, although not everyone will become world-class.\n\nSome physical and mental characteristics are partly inherited. Height may help a basketball player, while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sensitive ear can make musical training easier</strong>. Children also differ in coordination, memory and speed of learning. These differences explain why two beginners may progress at different rates under the same conditions.\n\nNevertheless, talent has limited value without disciplined practice. Skilled performers repeat difficult tasks, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">receive detailed feedback and gradually correct weaknesses</strong>. Access to coaching, equipment and family support is often more important than people realise. A moderately talented child with excellent training may therefore outperform a gifted child who lacks opportunity or motivation. Practice can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn potential into reliable performance</strong>.\n\nThe two views also interact in ways that are easy to overlook. A child judged to have no ear for music is offered fewer lessons and less encouragement, so the original judgement quietly produces the result it predicted. Youth sport shows the same effect: players born in the months just after the annual selection cut-off are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">heavily over-represented in professional academies</strong>, simply because they were bigger and stronger at the age of ten. Giving every child a long period of proper coaching therefore prevents <strong class=\"key-phrase\">early labels from limiting later development</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, people are not born with identical abilities, but talent should not be treated as a fixed limit. Schools and coaches should give learners time, expert guidance and realistic goals. This approach recognises natural differences while preserving the belief that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">ability can be developed</strong>.","topic":"Sport & Leisure","phrases":[{"en":"a sensitive ear can make musical training easier","context":"Some physical and mental characteristics are partly inherited. Height may help a basketball player, while a sensitive ear can make musical training easier. Children also differ in coordination, memory and speed of learning. These differences explain why two beginners may progress at different rates under the same conditions."},{"en":"receive detailed feedback and gradually correct weaknesses","context":"Nevertheless, talent has limited value without disciplined practice. Skilled performers repeat difficult tasks, receive detailed feedback and gradually correct weaknesses. Access to coaching, equipment and family support is often more important than people realise. A moderately talented child with excellent training may therefore outperform a gifted child who lacks opportunity or motivation. Practice can turn potential into reliable performance."},{"en":"turn potential into reliable performance","context":"Nevertheless, talent has limited value without disciplined practice. Skilled performers repeat difficult tasks, receive detailed feedback and gradually correct weaknesses. Access to coaching, equipment and family support is often more important than people realise. A moderately talented child with excellent training may therefore outperform a gifted child who lacks opportunity or motivation. Practice can turn potential into reliable performance."},{"en":"heavily over-represented in professional academies","context":"The two views also interact in ways that are easy to overlook. A child judged to have no ear for music is offered fewer lessons and less encouragement, so the original judgement quietly produces the result it predicted. Youth sport shows the same effect: players born in the months just after the annual selection cut-off are heavily over-represented in professional academies, simply because they were bigger and stronger at the age of ten. Giving every child a long period of proper coaching therefore prevents early labels from limiting later development."},{"en":"early labels from limiting later development","context":"The two views also interact in ways that are easy to overlook. A child judged to have no ear for music is offered fewer lessons and less encouragement, so the original judgement quietly produces the result it predicted. Youth sport shows the same effect: players born in the months just after the annual selection cut-off are heavily over-represented in professional academies, simply because they were bigger and stronger at the age of ten. Giving every child a long period of proper coaching therefore prevents early labels from limiting later development."},{"en":"ability can be developed","context":"In conclusion, people are not born with identical abilities, but talent should not be treated as a fixed limit. Schools and coaches should give learners time, expert guidance and realistic goals. This approach recognises natural differences while preserving the belief that ability can be developed."}],"subtype":"discuss","summary":"Natural talent compared with training and practice","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-126","_displayNo":"#286"},{"id":"writing-task-2-131","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Do hobbies have to be difficult to be enjoyable?","prompt":"Some people believe that hobbies need to be difficult to be enjoyable. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Some hobbies are relatively easy, while others present more of a challenge. Personally, I believe that both types of hobby can be fun, and I therefore disagree with the statement that hobbies need to be difficult in order to be enjoyable.\n\nOn the one hand, many people enjoy easy hobbies. One example of an activity that is easy for most people is swimming. This hobby requires very little equipment, it is simple to learn, and it is inexpensive. I remember learning to swim at my local swimming pool when I was a child, and it never felt like a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demanding or challenging experience</strong>. Another hobby that I find easy and fun is photography. In my opinion, anyone can take interesting pictures without knowing too much about <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the technicalities of operating a camera</strong>. Despite being straightforward, taking photos is a satisfying activity.\n\nOn the other hand, difficult hobbies can sometimes be more exciting. If an activity is more challenging, we might feel <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a greater sense of satisfaction</strong> when we manage to do it successfully. For example, film editing is a hobby that requires <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a high level of knowledge and expertise</strong>. In my case, it took me around two years before I became competent at this activity, but now I enjoy it much more than I did when I started. I believe that many hobbies give us more pleasure when we reach a higher level of performance because the results are better and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the feeling of achievement</strong> is greater.\n\nIn conclusion, simple hobbies can be fun and relaxing, but difficult hobbies can be equally pleasurable for different reasons.","phrases":[{"en":"demanding or challenging experience","context":"On the one hand, many people enjoy easy hobbies. One example of an activity that is easy for most people is swimming. This hobby requires very little equipment, it is simple to learn, and it is inexpensive. I remember learning to swim at my local swimming pool when I was a child, and it never felt like a demanding or challenging experience. Another hobby that I find easy and fun is photography. In my opinion, anyone can take interesting pictures without knowing too much about the technicalities of operating a camera. Despite being straightforward, taking photos is a satisfying activity."},{"en":"the technicalities of operating a camera","context":"On the one hand, many people enjoy easy hobbies. One example of an activity that is easy for most people is swimming. This hobby requires very little equipment, it is simple to learn, and it is inexpensive. I remember learning to swim at my local swimming pool when I was a child, and it never felt like a demanding or challenging experience. Another hobby that I find easy and fun is photography. In my opinion, anyone can take interesting pictures without knowing too much about the technicalities of operating a camera. Despite being straightforward, taking photos is a satisfying activity."},{"en":"a greater sense of satisfaction","context":"On the other hand, difficult hobbies can sometimes be more exciting. If an activity is more challenging, we might feel a greater sense of satisfaction when we manage to do it successfully. For example, film editing is a hobby that requires a high level of knowledge and expertise. In my case, it took me around two years before I became competent at this activity, but now I enjoy it much more than I did when I started. I believe that many hobbies give us more pleasure when we reach a higher level of performance because the results are better and the feeling of achievement is greater."},{"en":"a high level of knowledge and expertise","context":"On the other hand, difficult hobbies can sometimes be more exciting. If an activity is more challenging, we might feel a greater sense of satisfaction when we manage to do it successfully. For example, film editing is a hobby that requires a high level of knowledge and expertise. In my case, it took me around two years before I became competent at this activity, but now I enjoy it much more than I did when I started. I believe that many hobbies give us more pleasure when we reach a higher level of performance because the results are better and the feeling of achievement is greater."},{"en":"the feeling of achievement","context":"On the other hand, difficult hobbies can sometimes be more exciting. If an activity is more challenging, we might feel a greater sense of satisfaction when we manage to do it successfully. For example, film editing is a hobby that requires a high level of knowledge and expertise. In my case, it took me around two years before I became competent at this activity, but now I enjoy it much more than I did when I started. I believe that many hobbies give us more pleasure when we reach a higher level of performance because the results are better and the feeling of achievement is greater."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#287","summary":"Do difficult hobbies bring more enjoyment?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-166","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Do video games do more harm than good?","prompt":"Some people regard video games as harmless fun, or even as a useful educational tool. Others, however, believe that videos games are having an adverse effect on the people who play them. In your opinion, do the drawbacks of video games outweigh the benefits?","answer":"Many people, and children in particular, enjoy playing computer games. While I accept that these games can sometimes have a positive effect on the user, I believe that they are more likely to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have a harmful impact</strong>.\n\nOn the one hand, video games can be both entertaining and educational. Users, or gamers, are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transported into virtual worlds</strong> which are often more exciting and engaging than real-life pastimes. From an educational perspective, these games encourage imagination and creativity, as well as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">concentration, logical thinking and problem solving</strong>, all of which are useful skills outside the gaming context. Computer simulation games have also been shown to improve users’ motor skills and help to prepare them for real-world tasks, such as flying a plane.\n\nHowever, I would argue that these benefits are outweighed by the drawbacks. Gaming can be highly addictive because users are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">constantly given scores, new targets and frequent rewards</strong> to keep them playing. Many children now spend hours each day trying to progress through the levels of a game or to get a higher score than their friends. This type of addiction can have effects ranging from lack of sleep to problems at school, when <strong class=\"key-phrase\">homework is sacrificed for a few more hours</strong> on the computer or console. The rise in obesity in recent years has also been linked in part to the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sedentary lifestyle</strong> and lack of exercise that often accompany gaming addiction.\n\nIn conclusion, it seems to me that the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">potential dangers</strong> of video games are more significant than the possible benefits.","phrases":[{"en":"have a harmful impact","context":"Many people, and children in particular, enjoy playing computer games. While I accept that these games can sometimes have a positive effect on the user, I believe that they are more likely to have a harmful impact."},{"en":"transported into virtual worlds","context":"On the one hand, video games can be both entertaining and educational. Users, or gamers, are transported into virtual worlds which are often more exciting and engaging than real-life pastimes. From an educational perspective, these games encourage imagination and creativity, as well as concentration, logical thinking and problem solving, all of which are useful skills outside the gaming context. Computer simulation games have also been shown to improve users’ motor skills and help to prepare them for real-world tasks, such as flying a plane."},{"en":"concentration, logical thinking and problem solving","context":"On the one hand, video games can be both entertaining and educational. Users, or gamers, are transported into virtual worlds which are often more exciting and engaging than real-life pastimes. From an educational perspective, these games encourage imagination and creativity, as well as concentration, logical thinking and problem solving, all of which are useful skills outside the gaming context. Computer simulation games have also been shown to improve users’ motor skills and help to prepare them for real-world tasks, such as flying a plane."},{"en":"constantly given scores, new targets and frequent rewards","context":"However, I would argue that these benefits are outweighed by the drawbacks. Gaming can be highly addictive because users are constantly given scores, new targets and frequent rewards to keep them playing. Many children now spend hours each day trying to progress through the levels of a game or to get a higher score than their friends. This type of addiction can have effects ranging from lack of sleep to problems at school, when homework is sacrificed for a few more hours on the computer or console. The rise in obesity in recent years has also been linked in part to the sedentary lifestyle and lack of exercise that often accompany gaming addiction."},{"en":"homework is sacrificed for a few more hours","context":"However, I would argue that these benefits are outweighed by the drawbacks. Gaming can be highly addictive because users are constantly given scores, new targets and frequent rewards to keep them playing. Many children now spend hours each day trying to progress through the levels of a game or to get a higher score than their friends. This type of addiction can have effects ranging from lack of sleep to problems at school, when homework is sacrificed for a few more hours on the computer or console. The rise in obesity in recent years has also been linked in part to the sedentary lifestyle and lack of exercise that often accompany gaming addiction."},{"en":"sedentary lifestyle","context":"However, I would argue that these benefits are outweighed by the drawbacks. Gaming can be highly addictive because users are constantly given scores, new targets and frequent rewards to keep them playing. Many children now spend hours each day trying to progress through the levels of a game or to get a higher score than their friends. This type of addiction can have effects ranging from lack of sleep to problems at school, when homework is sacrificed for a few more hours on the computer or console. The rise in obesity in recent years has also been linked in part to the sedentary lifestyle and lack of exercise that often accompany gaming addiction."},{"en":"potential dangers","context":"In conclusion, it seems to me that the potential dangers of video games are more significant than the possible benefits."}],"subtype":"Advantages outweigh disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#288","summary":"The benefits and harms of video games"},{"id":"writing-task-2-189","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Funding national athletes","prompt":"Some people believe that national sports teams and individual athletes who represent their country should be funded by the government. Others think they should be funded by private businesses and sponsorship. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"When athletes compete for their country, the cost of training and supporting them can be met either by the state or by private sponsors. In my opinion a combination works best, but public funding should remain the foundation.\n\nThe argument for private money is a practical one. Governments have limited budgets, and every pound spent on elite sport is a pound not spent on hospitals or schools. Companies, by contrast, are willing to pay large sums because international competition brings them valuable publicity, and sports such as football and motor racing survive comfortably without any public support at all. Sponsorship also rewards success quickly, since backers move their money towards athletes who perform.\n\nThe weakness of this model is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sponsorship follows popularity rather than merit</strong>. A commercially attractive sport with a large television audience will attract more money than it needs, while archery, wrestling or the Paralympic disciplines may attract almost none. Talented competitors in minority sports would then be forced to abandon their careers, and a country&#x27;s representation would effectively be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">decided by advertisers</strong>. Sponsors can also withdraw suddenly, or exert influence over which events an athlete enters.\n\nFor this reason I believe the state should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">guarantee a basic level of support</strong>, distributed according to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sporting potential rather than commercial appeal</strong>. Private money can then supplement it, particularly for athletes who already earn well.\n\nIn conclusion, sponsorship is a useful addition, but only public funding can ensure that athletes are selected on ability and that less popular sports continue to exist.","phrases":[{"en":"sponsorship follows popularity rather than merit","context":"The weakness of this model is that sponsorship follows popularity rather than merit. A commercially attractive sport with a large television audience will attract more money than it needs, while archery, wrestling or the Paralympic disciplines may attract almost none. Talented competitors in minority sports would then be forced to abandon their careers, and a country's representation would effectively be decided by advertisers. Sponsors can also withdraw suddenly, or exert influence over which events an athlete enters."},{"en":"decided by advertisers","context":"The weakness of this model is that sponsorship follows popularity rather than merit. A commercially attractive sport with a large television audience will attract more money than it needs, while archery, wrestling or the Paralympic disciplines may attract almost none. Talented competitors in minority sports would then be forced to abandon their careers, and a country's representation would effectively be decided by advertisers. Sponsors can also withdraw suddenly, or exert influence over which events an athlete enters."},{"en":"guarantee a basic level of support","context":"For this reason I believe the state should guarantee a basic level of support, distributed according to sporting potential rather than commercial appeal. Private money can then supplement it, particularly for athletes who already earn well."},{"en":"sporting potential rather than commercial appeal","context":"For this reason I believe the state should guarantee a basic level of support, distributed according to sporting potential rather than commercial appeal. Private money can then supplement it, particularly for athletes who already earn well."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#289","summary":"Funding national athletes"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2211","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Should extreme sports be banned?","prompt":"Some people believe that extreme sports like skydiving and skiing are too dangerous and should therefore be prohibited. Do you agree or disagree with this belief?","answer":"Activities such as skydiving, skiing and rock climbing carry a real risk of serious injury, and some people believe they should be banned for that reason. I disagree, because adults are entitled to take calculated risks with their own lives.\n\nThe principal argument against prohibition is one of personal freedom. In a free society, individuals are allowed to make choices that may harm themselves, and we do not ban smoking, alcohol or motorcycling, even though these kill far more people every year than extreme sports do. Participants are usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">well informed about the dangers</strong>, undergo training and use specialised safety equipment. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">A ban would therefore be inconsistent</strong>, and it would also be very hard to enforce, since people would simply <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practise without supervision</strong>, which is considerably more dangerous.\n\nThere are positive reasons to allow such sports as well. They demand high levels of fitness, discipline and concentration, and many participants describe <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a sense of achievement</strong> that ordinary exercise does not provide. These sports also support a large industry of instructors, guides, equipment manufacturers and mountain resorts, on which many rural communities depend economically.\n\nI would accept that the risk should not <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fall on other people</strong>. Rescue operations are expensive and endanger the emergency services, so it is entirely reasonable to require participants to hold insurance, to use licensed instructors and to obey safety rules, and to close areas when conditions are genuinely hazardous.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that extreme sports should be prohibited. Regulation, training and compulsory insurance are a far more sensible response than a ban on activities that adults freely choose.","phrases":[{"en":"well informed about the dangers","context":"The principal argument against prohibition is one of personal freedom. In a free society, individuals are allowed to make choices that may harm themselves, and we do not ban smoking, alcohol or motorcycling, even though these kill far more people every year than extreme sports do. Participants are usually well informed about the dangers, undergo training and use specialised safety equipment. A ban would therefore be inconsistent, and it would also be very hard to enforce, since people would simply practise without supervision, which is considerably more dangerous."},{"en":"A ban would therefore be inconsistent","context":"The principal argument against prohibition is one of personal freedom. In a free society, individuals are allowed to make choices that may harm themselves, and we do not ban smoking, alcohol or motorcycling, even though these kill far more people every year than extreme sports do. Participants are usually well informed about the dangers, undergo training and use specialised safety equipment. A ban would therefore be inconsistent, and it would also be very hard to enforce, since people would simply practise without supervision, which is considerably more dangerous."},{"en":"practise without supervision","context":"The principal argument against prohibition is one of personal freedom. In a free society, individuals are allowed to make choices that may harm themselves, and we do not ban smoking, alcohol or motorcycling, even though these kill far more people every year than extreme sports do. Participants are usually well informed about the dangers, undergo training and use specialised safety equipment. A ban would therefore be inconsistent, and it would also be very hard to enforce, since people would simply practise without supervision, which is considerably more dangerous."},{"en":"a sense of achievement","context":"There are positive reasons to allow such sports as well. They demand high levels of fitness, discipline and concentration, and many participants describe a sense of achievement that ordinary exercise does not provide. These sports also support a large industry of instructors, guides, equipment manufacturers and mountain resorts, on which many rural communities depend economically."},{"en":"fall on other people","context":"I would accept that the risk should not fall on other people. Rescue operations are expensive and endanger the emergency services, so it is entirely reasonable to require participants to hold insurance, to use licensed instructors and to obey safety rules, and to close areas when conditions are genuinely hazardous."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#290","summary":"Should extreme sports be banned?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2212","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Elite training centres or public sports facilities","prompt":"Some countries achieve international success by building specialised facilities to train top athletes instead of providing sports facilities that everyone can use. Is it a positive or negative development?","answer":"Some governments concentrate their sports budgets on specialised training centres for a small number of elite competitors, rather than on facilities that the general public can use. In my opinion this is a negative development, although it does bring certain benefits.\n\nThe advantages are not difficult to identify. Success at the Olympic Games or a World Cup <strong class=\"key-phrase\">generates national pride and international recognition</strong>, and it can encourage young people to take up a sport when they see a compatriot winning. Elite athletes also require specialist coaching, sports science and equipment that ordinary facilities cannot possibly provide, so without dedicated centres a country will not compete <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the highest level</strong>.\n\nHowever, the costs of this approach are considerable. Elite programmes are extremely expensive and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">benefit a very small number of people</strong>, whereas the same money spent on local swimming pools, running tracks and sports halls would improve the health of an entire population. This matters because obesity, diabetes and heart disease are now among the largest <strong class=\"key-phrase\">burdens on public health budgets</strong>, and regular exercise is the cheapest way of reducing them. Spending public money on a handful of professional athletes while ordinary citizens have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nowhere affordable to exercise</strong> is difficult to justify.\n\nThere is also a flaw in the argument that elite success inspires participation. Studies following major sporting events have generally found little lasting increase in the number of people exercising, largely because those who are inspired discover that there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no accessible pool or pitch nearby</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I regard this as a negative trend. Governments should fund facilities that serve the whole community, and support elite athletes from a much smaller share of the budget.","phrases":[{"en":"generates national pride and international recognition","context":"The advantages are not difficult to identify. Success at the Olympic Games or a World Cup generates national pride and international recognition, and it can encourage young people to take up a sport when they see a compatriot winning. Elite athletes also require specialist coaching, sports science and equipment that ordinary facilities cannot possibly provide, so without dedicated centres a country will not compete at the highest level."},{"en":"at the highest level","context":"The advantages are not difficult to identify. Success at the Olympic Games or a World Cup generates national pride and international recognition, and it can encourage young people to take up a sport when they see a compatriot winning. Elite athletes also require specialist coaching, sports science and equipment that ordinary facilities cannot possibly provide, so without dedicated centres a country will not compete at the highest level."},{"en":"benefit a very small number of people","context":"However, the costs of this approach are considerable. Elite programmes are extremely expensive and benefit a very small number of people, whereas the same money spent on local swimming pools, running tracks and sports halls would improve the health of an entire population. This matters because obesity, diabetes and heart disease are now among the largest burdens on public health budgets, and regular exercise is the cheapest way of reducing them. Spending public money on a handful of professional athletes while ordinary citizens have nowhere affordable to exercise is difficult to justify."},{"en":"burdens on public health budgets","context":"However, the costs of this approach are considerable. Elite programmes are extremely expensive and benefit a very small number of people, whereas the same money spent on local swimming pools, running tracks and sports halls would improve the health of an entire population. This matters because obesity, diabetes and heart disease are now among the largest burdens on public health budgets, and regular exercise is the cheapest way of reducing them. Spending public money on a handful of professional athletes while ordinary citizens have nowhere affordable to exercise is difficult to justify."},{"en":"nowhere affordable to exercise","context":"However, the costs of this approach are considerable. Elite programmes are extremely expensive and benefit a very small number of people, whereas the same money spent on local swimming pools, running tracks and sports halls would improve the health of an entire population. This matters because obesity, diabetes and heart disease are now among the largest burdens on public health budgets, and regular exercise is the cheapest way of reducing them. Spending public money on a handful of professional athletes while ordinary citizens have nowhere affordable to exercise is difficult to justify."},{"en":"no accessible pool or pitch nearby","context":"There is also a flaw in the argument that elite success inspires participation. Studies following major sporting events have generally found little lasting increase in the number of people exercising, largely because those who are inspired discover that there is no accessible pool or pitch nearby."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#291","summary":"Elite training centres or public sports facilities"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2224","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Hosting international sporting events","prompt":"Some people think hosting major international sporting events brings a lot of advantages to a country, while others believe there are more disadvantages. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Countries compete fiercely for the right to stage events such as the Olympic Games or the World Cup, yet many host cities are still paying for them a decade later. I believe such events can be worthwhile, but only for countries that already need the infrastructure they require.\n\nSupporters point to benefits beyond the sport. Hosting forces a government to complete transport and housing projects that might otherwise be delayed for years, as Barcelona did in 1992 when the Olympics were used to reconnect the city to its own coastline. The weeks of global coverage also work as advertising that no tourist board could afford, and success by home athletes tends to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">raise participation in sport</strong> among children who watch it.\n\nCritics answer that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the costs are consistently understated</strong>. Bids are approved on the basis of optimistic budgets, and the final figure is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">routinely several times the original estimate</strong>, with the difference met from taxation. The stadiums themselves are the clearest evidence: purpose-built venues in cities without a team large enough to fill them stand empty afterwards while still costing money to maintain. Poorer residents may also be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">moved out of areas earmarked for development</strong>.\n\nMy own view is that the decisive question is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what the country would have built anyway</strong>. Where a host uses the deadline to deliver a metro line or housing that residents will use for fifty years, the expense is justified. Where it builds arenas that serve no purpose once the visitors leave, the money would have been better spent elsewhere.\n\nIn conclusion, hosting is neither automatically beneficial nor automatically wasteful. It repays a country that plans for the years after the event, and punishes one that plans only for <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the fortnight itself</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"raise participation in sport","context":"Supporters point to benefits beyond the sport. Hosting forces a government to complete transport and housing projects that might otherwise be delayed for years, as Barcelona did in 1992 when the Olympics were used to reconnect the city to its own coastline. The weeks of global coverage also work as advertising that no tourist board could afford, and success by home athletes tends to raise participation in sport among children who watch it."},{"en":"the costs are consistently understated","context":"Critics answer that the costs are consistently understated. Bids are approved on the basis of optimistic budgets, and the final figure is routinely several times the original estimate, with the difference met from taxation. The stadiums themselves are the clearest evidence: purpose-built venues in cities without a team large enough to fill them stand empty afterwards while still costing money to maintain. Poorer residents may also be moved out of areas earmarked for development."},{"en":"routinely several times the original estimate","context":"Critics answer that the costs are consistently understated. Bids are approved on the basis of optimistic budgets, and the final figure is routinely several times the original estimate, with the difference met from taxation. The stadiums themselves are the clearest evidence: purpose-built venues in cities without a team large enough to fill them stand empty afterwards while still costing money to maintain. Poorer residents may also be moved out of areas earmarked for development."},{"en":"moved out of areas earmarked for development","context":"Critics answer that the costs are consistently understated. Bids are approved on the basis of optimistic budgets, and the final figure is routinely several times the original estimate, with the difference met from taxation. The stadiums themselves are the clearest evidence: purpose-built venues in cities without a team large enough to fill them stand empty afterwards while still costing money to maintain. Poorer residents may also be moved out of areas earmarked for development."},{"en":"what the country would have built anyway","context":"My own view is that the decisive question is what the country would have built anyway. Where a host uses the deadline to deliver a metro line or housing that residents will use for fifty years, the expense is justified. Where it builds arenas that serve no purpose once the visitors leave, the money would have been better spent elsewhere."},{"en":"the fortnight itself","context":"In conclusion, hosting is neither automatically beneficial nor automatically wasteful. It repays a country that plans for the years after the event, and punishes one that plans only for the fortnight itself."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#292","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of hosting major sporting events"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2225","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"School sport or professional performance?","prompt":"Government money should be used to support children in school for sports rather than to support professional sports and arts that perform for the general public. To what extent do you agree?","answer":"Public budgets for sport and culture are limited, and it is sometimes argued that all of the money should go to school children rather than to professionals who perform for an audience. I agree that school provision deserves priority, but not that professional funding should be withdrawn entirely.\n\nThe case for schools is strongest on grounds of reach. Money spent on a stadium benefits the few thousand who attend, whereas money spent on school pitches, equipment and coaching reaches every child in the country, including those whose families could never pay for a club membership. The health argument reinforces this. Habits formed before the age of twelve tend to last, so a child who learns to swim or enjoys running is far less likely to become an inactive adult, and the saving to health services from that single change is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">difficult to overstate</strong>.\n\nSchool sport also does work that professional sport cannot. Losing a match and returning to training the following week teaches persistence, and a mixed-ability team teaches children to depend on people who are not like them. These lessons come from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taking part rather than watching</strong>.\n\nI would not go as far as the statement, however. Professional sport and the arts are what give children something to aim at, and a national orchestra or athletics squad also supports a wider network of teachers, coaches and technicians whose skills eventually reach schools. Removing that funding would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weaken the base as well as the top</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I largely agree that children in school should come first, because participation delivers more health and social value per pound than performance does. The right approach is to shift the balance decisively towards schools while keeping enough professional support to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give young people something to aspire to</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"difficult to overstate","context":"The case for schools is strongest on grounds of reach. Money spent on a stadium benefits the few thousand who attend, whereas money spent on school pitches, equipment and coaching reaches every child in the country, including those whose families could never pay for a club membership. The health argument reinforces this. Habits formed before the age of twelve tend to last, so a child who learns to swim or enjoys running is far less likely to become an inactive adult, and the saving to health services from that single change is difficult to overstate."},{"en":"taking part rather than watching","context":"School sport also does work that professional sport cannot. Losing a match and returning to training the following week teaches persistence, and a mixed-ability team teaches children to depend on people who are not like them. These lessons come from taking part rather than watching."},{"en":"weaken the base as well as the top","context":"I would not go as far as the statement, however. Professional sport and the arts are what give children something to aim at, and a national orchestra or athletics squad also supports a wider network of teachers, coaches and technicians whose skills eventually reach schools. Removing that funding would weaken the base as well as the top."},{"en":"give young people something to aspire to","context":"In conclusion, I largely agree that children in school should come first, because participation delivers more health and social value per pound than performance does. The right approach is to shift the balance decisively towards schools while keeping enough professional support to give young people something to aspire to."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#293","summary":"Public money for school sport rather than professional sport and the arts"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2294","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Sport as a business","prompt":"Nowadays, sport is a big business, with high earnings for professional sportspeople and the companies involved. Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"Television, sponsorship and merchandising have turned professional sport into an industry worth hundreds of billions. I regard the development as broadly positive for the standard of sport, though it has damaged the relationship between clubs and the people who support them.\n\nThe money has clearly improved what spectators watch. Athletes can train full-time with proper coaching, nutrition and medical care, which is why records fall in almost every discipline, and injuries that once ended careers are now treated successfully. Commercial income has also opened sport to people who could not previously afford to pursue it, since a talented child from a poor family can now be supported through an academy, and women&#x27;s competitions have grown rapidly on the same funding after decades of neglect. Broadcasting reaches audiences that would never have seen these events.\n\nThe costs fall on the sport itself. Wealth concentrates, so the same few clubs win repeatedly and competitions become predictable, and smaller clubs that formed the base of the game struggle or disappear. Fixtures are arranged for broadcasters rather than for supporters, with matches moved to inconvenient times and competitions restructured to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suit the audiences that pay the most</strong>. Ticket prices have risen beyond what many lifelong supporters can pay, and a club that was a local institution can be sold to owners with no connection to the place.\n\nThere are consequences for the athletes too. Pressure to perform and to appear constantly on television produces injuries, exhaustion and a measurable amount of doping, and young players are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">signed and discarded before they are twenty</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, commercial money has raised standards, widened access and made sport visible to millions, which is a genuine gain. It has also made competition less equal and pushed supporters aside, so the development is positive only where income is shared and clubs remain answerable to their communities.","phrases":[{"en":"suit the audiences that pay the most","context":"The costs fall on the sport itself. Wealth concentrates, so the same few clubs win repeatedly and competitions become predictable, and smaller clubs that formed the base of the game struggle or disappear. Fixtures are arranged for broadcasters rather than for supporters, with matches moved to inconvenient times and competitions restructured to suit the audiences that pay the most. Ticket prices have risen beyond what many lifelong supporters can pay, and a club that was a local institution can be sold to owners with no connection to the place."},{"en":"signed and discarded before they are twenty","context":"There are consequences for the athletes too. Pressure to perform and to appear constantly on television produces injuries, exhaustion and a measurable amount of doping, and young players are signed and discarded before they are twenty."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#294","summary":"Whether the huge earnings in professional sport are a good development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2303","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Competition and young athletes","prompt":"Some people believe that sports competitions are a source of emotional stress for young people, and that young people should therefore not take part in them. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Children in competitive sport are sometimes visibly distressed by defeat, and the pressure placed on them by adults can be severe. I agree that the stress is real, but I disagree that removing competition is the answer.\n\nThe concern is legitimate. Young athletes in demanding programmes train for hours daily, and anxiety, injury and eating disorders occur at measurable rates. Much of the pressure comes from adults: parents who shout from the touchline and coaches whose position depends on results teach a child that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their worth depends on the last result</strong>. Children develop at different rates, so early selection frequently rejects those who would have been better a few years later, and many able young people abandon sport entirely because of a bad experience before they were fourteen.\n\nRemoving competition would nonetheless cost more than it saved. Competing teaches how to lose, and a child who has never lost anything meets the first serious failure in adulthood with no experience of recovering from it. Competition also supplies the reason to practise, since improvement is dull without a test of whether it worked, and children themselves invent competitions in every playground, which suggests the instinct is not imposed. Sport is also one of the few areas where a young person can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">earn something entirely on their own effort</strong>.\n\nThe distinction that matters is between competition and the conditions surrounding it. What harms children is not the match but adult behaviour: excessive selection, year-round training, and treating a child&#x27;s performance as a family investment. Age-appropriate leagues, limits on training hours, coaches trained in child development and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">parents kept quiet on the touchline</strong> address the problem directly.\n\nIn conclusion, competitive sport can cause genuine stress, and the concern deserves attention rather than dismissal. Banning young people from competing would remove resilience, motivation and enjoyment, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reforming how adults run it</strong> is the better response.","phrases":[{"en":"their worth depends on the last result","context":"The concern is legitimate. Young athletes in demanding programmes train for hours daily, and anxiety, injury and eating disorders occur at measurable rates. Much of the pressure comes from adults: parents who shout from the touchline and coaches whose position depends on results teach a child that their worth depends on the last result. Children develop at different rates, so early selection frequently rejects those who would have been better a few years later, and many able young people abandon sport entirely because of a bad experience before they were fourteen."},{"en":"earn something entirely on their own effort","context":"Removing competition would nonetheless cost more than it saved. Competing teaches how to lose, and a child who has never lost anything meets the first serious failure in adulthood with no experience of recovering from it. Competition also supplies the reason to practise, since improvement is dull without a test of whether it worked, and children themselves invent competitions in every playground, which suggests the instinct is not imposed. Sport is also one of the few areas where a young person can earn something entirely on their own effort."},{"en":"parents kept quiet on the touchline","context":"The distinction that matters is between competition and the conditions surrounding it. What harms children is not the match but adult behaviour: excessive selection, year-round training, and treating a child's performance as a family investment. Age-appropriate leagues, limits on training hours, coaches trained in child development and parents kept quiet on the touchline address the problem directly."},{"en":"reforming how adults run it","context":"In conclusion, competitive sport can cause genuine stress, and the concern deserves attention rather than dismissal. Banning young people from competing would remove resilience, motivation and enjoyment, so reforming how adults run it is the better response."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#295","summary":"Whether young people should be kept out of sports competitions"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2328","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Games on handheld devices","prompt":"Entertainment such as electronic games on handheld devices is harmful to individuals and to the society they live in. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Phones have put an endless supply of games into every pocket, and the criticism that follows them is severe. I partly agree, because the harm lies less in the games themselves than in how these particular ones are built.\n\nThe defence is reasonable. Games demand attention, planning and quick judgement, and studies of regular players show improvements in spatial reasoning and reaction time. They are a cheap and harmless way to fill a commute, and multiplayer games sustain friendships across distance, which matters to people who are isolated. Treating them as worthless while accepting television as normal is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a double standard rather than an argument</strong>.\n\nThe concerns are still legitimate. Handheld games are designed by companies paid for attention, so they use daily rewards, streaks and near-misses borrowed directly from gambling. The purpose is to make stopping difficult, and for a minority it succeeds completely: schoolwork, sleep and exercise disappear. Many now include purchases dressed as chance, which introduces children to gambling before they can recognise it. The social cost is subtler, since hours absorbed in a screen are hours taken from somewhere else, most often from sleep, movement and the company of other people.\n\nThe distinction that matters is between playing and being kept playing. A game with an ending is entertainment; a game engineered never to finish is a product designed against its user. This points to remedies rather than bans: regulating purchases aimed at children, requiring companies to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disclose how the rewards are timed</strong>, and teaching families to set limits.\n\nIn conclusion, portable games are not harmful in themselves, and they give millions of people pleasure and connection. The commercial design behind many of them is harmful, and that is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what regulation should be aimed at</strong> rather than the games as a whole.","phrases":[{"en":"a double standard rather than an argument","context":"The defence is reasonable. Games demand attention, planning and quick judgement, and studies of regular players show improvements in spatial reasoning and reaction time. They are a cheap and harmless way to fill a commute, and multiplayer games sustain friendships across distance, which matters to people who are isolated. Treating them as worthless while accepting television as normal is a double standard rather than an argument."},{"en":"disclose how the rewards are timed","context":"The distinction that matters is between playing and being kept playing. A game with an ending is entertainment; a game engineered never to finish is a product designed against its user. This points to remedies rather than bans: regulating purchases aimed at children, requiring companies to disclose how the rewards are timed, and teaching families to set limits."},{"en":"what regulation should be aimed at","context":"In conclusion, portable games are not harmful in themselves, and they give millions of people pleasure and connection. The commercial design behind many of them is harmful, and that is what regulation should be aimed at rather than the games as a whole."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#296","summary":"Whether portable electronic games harm individuals and society"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2336","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Traditional games against modern ones","prompt":"Some people think traditional games are better than modern games in helping children develop their abilities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Skipping ropes and hide-and-seek have been replaced in most childhoods by consoles and phones. I partly agree that the older games develop a child more completely, but the advantage lies in how they are played rather than in their age.\n\nTraditional games teach through the conditions they impose. Children playing outside must agree the rules themselves, settle disputes without a referee and include whoever turns up, which is where negotiation and fairness are actually learned. The games involve running, climbing and balancing, so physical development happens without anyone calling it exercise. Simple equipment also forces invention, since a stick becomes several different things, and boredom between games <strong class=\"key-phrase\">pushes children into making something up</strong>.\n\nModern games are stronger than critics allow, however. Complex titles require planning, resource management and rapid decisions under pressure, and children who play together online must coordinate with people they cannot see, which is a genuine social skill. Games are also the entry point through which many children discover coding and design, and they include those whose disability or geography rules out the playground.\n\nThe real difference is the amount of freedom involved. A traditional game is shaped by the children playing it; a commercial game is shaped by designers who decide the goals, the rewards and when the next one arrives. That is why the modern version is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">easier to enjoy and harder to stop</strong>, and why it develops fewer of the abilities that come from <strong class=\"key-phrase\">having to organise the fun yourself</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, traditional games do more for physical health, social negotiation and imagination, so the preference for them is justified. Modern games train useful skills of their own, and the sensible aim is a childhood containing both rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nostalgia for one of them</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"pushes children into making something up","context":"Traditional games teach through the conditions they impose. Children playing outside must agree the rules themselves, settle disputes without a referee and include whoever turns up, which is where negotiation and fairness are actually learned. The games involve running, climbing and balancing, so physical development happens without anyone calling it exercise. Simple equipment also forces invention, since a stick becomes several different things, and boredom between games pushes children into making something up."},{"en":"easier to enjoy and harder to stop","context":"The real difference is the amount of freedom involved. A traditional game is shaped by the children playing it; a commercial game is shaped by designers who decide the goals, the rewards and when the next one arrives. That is why the modern version is easier to enjoy and harder to stop, and why it develops fewer of the abilities that come from having to organise the fun yourself."},{"en":"having to organise the fun yourself","context":"The real difference is the amount of freedom involved. A traditional game is shaped by the children playing it; a commercial game is shaped by designers who decide the goals, the rewards and when the next one arrives. That is why the modern version is easier to enjoy and harder to stop, and why it develops fewer of the abilities that come from having to organise the fun yourself."},{"en":"nostalgia for one of them","context":"In conclusion, traditional games do more for physical health, social negotiation and imagination, so the preference for them is justified. Modern games train useful skills of their own, and the sensible aim is a childhood containing both rather than nostalgia for one of them."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#297","summary":"Whether traditional games develop children's abilities better than modern games"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2339","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Team sports or individual sports?","prompt":"Some people think it is more beneficial to take part in team sports such as football, while others think individual sports such as swimming are better. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Football and swimming both keep a person fit, yet they teach quite different things. Each has a serious claim, and my own view is that the better choice depends on what a particular person needs rather than on the sports themselves.\n\nTeam sports offer lessons that cannot be learned alone. A player must read what teammates are doing, accept a role that may not be glamorous and keep going when a match is lost through somebody else&#x27;s mistake, which is closer to how most work and family life actually feels. The social pull is also practical: turning up matters because other people depend on it, so team players tend to keep exercising when individuals give up. For children who are shy or new to an area, a team supplies friendships that would otherwise take years.\n\nIndividual sports have their own strengths. Progress depends entirely on the person, so improvement is visible and honestly earned, and that produces a durable kind of confidence. There is no substitute to hide behind and no captain to blame, which builds self-discipline. They are also more flexible, since a swimmer or runner trains at any hour without organising twenty other people, and they can be continued into old age long after <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the body has stopped tolerating collisions</strong>. Children who dislike competition or find crowds difficult often thrive here after failing in a team.\n\nThe choice therefore depends on the individual. A child who avoids other people benefits from a team; one who is constantly compared with others benefits from a sport where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the only opponent is a previous time</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, team sports develop cooperation and keep people participating, while individual sports build discipline and last a lifetime. The greatest benefit comes from matching the sport to the person, and ideally from experiencing both.","phrases":[{"en":"the body has stopped tolerating collisions","context":"Individual sports have their own strengths. Progress depends entirely on the person, so improvement is visible and honestly earned, and that produces a durable kind of confidence. There is no substitute to hide behind and no captain to blame, which builds self-discipline. They are also more flexible, since a swimmer or runner trains at any hour without organising twenty other people, and they can be continued into old age long after the body has stopped tolerating collisions. Children who dislike competition or find crowds difficult often thrive here after failing in a team."},{"en":"the only opponent is a previous time","context":"The choice therefore depends on the individual. A child who avoids other people benefits from a team; one who is constantly compared with others benefits from a sport where the only opponent is a previous time."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#298","summary":"Whether taking part in team sports is more beneficial than individual sports"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2346","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Games instead of sport","prompt":"Nowadays, many children spend a great deal of time playing computer games and less time taking part in sport. Why is this the case? Is this a beneficial or harmful trend?","answer":"Children who once spent afternoons on a pitch now spend them on a console. Several practical changes explain the shift, and I regard it as harmful, chiefly because of what the body loses.\n\nAvailability is the first cause. A game is always ready, requires no equipment, no travel and no other players who happen to be free, whereas sport demands a pitch, a coach and a fixed time. Cities have made this worse by building over the open ground children used to play on, and parents who fear traffic and strangers now keep children indoors. Cost matters too, since organised sport carries fees that many families cannot meet, while a game already installed is free. The games themselves are also more compelling than they were. They are designed by companies whose income depends on time spent, so progress, rewards and friends inside the game make stopping difficult, and a child who loses at football feels it publicly while a game lets you try again immediately and privately.\n\nThere are benefits worth acknowledging: strategy games train planning, online play maintains friendships, and children who dislike competitive sport are no longer excluded from everything.\n\nThe costs are heavier. Physical activity in childhood determines bone strength, heart health and weight for the rest of life, and none of it can be recovered later. Sport also teaches losing, cooperation and the value of practice in a way that a screen cannot reproduce because nothing physical is at stake. Sitting for hours affects sleep and posture, and inactive children usually become inactive adults, which is why obesity and diabetes now appear at ages that were once unheard of.\n\nIn conclusion, games have grown because they are cheap, always available and skilfully designed, while sport has become harder to access. The trend is harmful, and the remedy lies in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">free local facilities and safe streets</strong> rather than in criticising children.","phrases":[{"en":"free local facilities and safe streets","context":"In conclusion, games have grown because they are cheap, always available and skilfully designed, while sport has become harder to access. The trend is harmful, and the remedy lies in free local facilities and safe streets rather than in criticising children."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Beneficial or Harmful","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#299","summary":"Why children play more computer games and less sport, and whether this is harmful"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2365","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Sport & Leisure","title":"Companies sponsoring sport","prompt":"Some businesses now use sports sponsorship as a form of advertising. While some believe this benefits the sports industry, others argue that it brings drawbacks. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Company names appear on shirts, stadiums and competitions at every level of sport. The money involved has transformed what athletes and clubs can do, and my own view is that the benefits are real but need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">firmer limits</strong> than most countries impose.\n\nThe argument for sponsorship is largely financial. Professional sport is expensive, and ticket sales alone may not cover stadiums, coaching and medical care. Sponsorship allows athletes in <strong class=\"key-phrase\">minority sports</strong> to train full-time rather than fitting practice around a job, and it can help fund broadcasts that reach audiences beyond the stadium. Money also reaches the bottom of the pyramid, since sponsors support <strong class=\"key-phrase\">youth academies</strong> and local clubs, and a child&#x27;s first team often exists because a local firm pays for the kit. Women&#x27;s sport in particular has grown on sponsorship after decades of neglect.\n\nThe drawbacks concern control and content. A sport dependent on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">commercial money</strong> begins to shape itself around it: matches are scheduled for foreign television audiences, breaks are inserted for advertising, and competitions are restructured to favour the clubs that attract sponsors, which widens the gap between rich clubs and everybody else. Sponsors can also withdraw suddenly, leaving clubs that planned around the income in serious trouble. The most serious objection is the nature of some sponsors, since gambling, alcohol and fast-food brands buy visibility with young audiences precisely because association with sport makes an unhealthy product look wholesome.\n\nThe reasonable position is therefore to keep the money and regulate the source. Restricting gambling and alcohol advertising in sport, and requiring a share of income to be passed down to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">grassroots clubs</strong>, addresses the main harms without removing the funding.\n\nIn conclusion, sponsorship pays for much of what makes modern sport possible and reaches far beyond <strong class=\"key-phrase\">elite competition</strong>. It distorts fixtures and promotes harmful products, so rules on who may sponsor and how the money is shared are what the industry now needs.","phrases":[{"en":"firmer limits","context":"Company names appear on shirts, stadiums and competitions at every level of sport. The money involved has transformed what athletes and clubs can do, and my own view is that the benefits are real but need firmer limits than most countries impose."},{"en":"minority sports","context":"The argument for sponsorship is largely financial. Professional sport is expensive, and ticket sales alone may not cover stadiums, coaching and medical care. Sponsorship allows athletes in minority sports to train full-time rather than fitting practice around a job, and it can help fund broadcasts that reach audiences beyond the stadium. Money also reaches the bottom of the pyramid, since sponsors support youth academies and local clubs, and a child's first team often exists because a local firm pays for the kit. Women's sport in particular has grown on sponsorship after decades of neglect."},{"en":"youth academies","context":"The argument for sponsorship is largely financial. Professional sport is expensive, and ticket sales alone may not cover stadiums, coaching and medical care. Sponsorship allows athletes in minority sports to train full-time rather than fitting practice around a job, and it can help fund broadcasts that reach audiences beyond the stadium. Money also reaches the bottom of the pyramid, since sponsors support youth academies and local clubs, and a child's first team often exists because a local firm pays for the kit. Women's sport in particular has grown on sponsorship after decades of neglect."},{"en":"commercial money","context":"The drawbacks concern control and content. A sport dependent on commercial money begins to shape itself around it: matches are scheduled for foreign television audiences, breaks are inserted for advertising, and competitions are restructured to favour the clubs that attract sponsors, which widens the gap between rich clubs and everybody else. Sponsors can also withdraw suddenly, leaving clubs that planned around the income in serious trouble. The most serious objection is the nature of some sponsors, since gambling, alcohol and fast-food brands buy visibility with young audiences precisely because association with sport makes an unhealthy product look wholesome."},{"en":"grassroots clubs","context":"The reasonable position is therefore to keep the money and regulate the source. Restricting gambling and alcohol advertising in sport, and requiring a share of income to be passed down to grassroots clubs, addresses the main harms without removing the funding."},{"en":"elite competition","context":"In conclusion, sponsorship pays for much of what makes modern sport possible and reaches far beyond elite competition. It distorts fixtures and promotes harmful products, so rules on who may sponsor and how the money is shared are what the industry now needs."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#300","summary":"Whether corporate sponsorship helps or harms the sports industry"},{"id":"writing-task-2-038","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Space research or living standards on Earth?","prompt":"Millions of dollars are spent on space research every year. Some people argue that the money should be spent on improving living standards on Earth. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Space research requires enormous public investment, while many people still lack adequate housing, healthcare and education. Although these social needs deserve priority, I do not agree that governments should abandon space programmes. A limited and carefully directed budget can produce direct practical benefits on Earth and support valuable long-term research.\n\nThe strongest argument against space spending is opportunity cost. Money used for a distant mission cannot be spent on hospitals, schools or clean water. In poorer countries, these services can save lives immediately, whereas the benefits of space exploration may seem uncertain or remote. Governments should therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">avoid prestige projects</strong> that consume large sums without a clear scientific or public purpose.\n\nHowever, space research is not simply an expensive adventure. Satellite technology <strong class=\"key-phrase\">supports weather forecasting, navigation, communications and disaster management</strong>. It helps farmers monitor crops, allows emergency services to track storms and gives scientists reliable evidence about climate change. Research carried out for space missions has also contributed to advances in materials, computing and medicine. These benefits are shared far beyond the space industry.\n\nSpace science is also a long-term investment. Satellites have recorded polar ice and sea temperatures for more than forty years, and a continuous record of that kind cannot be created once a crisis has already begun; the expertise and international cooperation behind such programmes take decades to build. Missions should still be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reviewed independently</strong>, so that governments can show what each one delivers when it competes with pressing social needs.\n\nIn conclusion, basic living standards should receive the largest share of public money, but this does not make space research wasteful. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">A balanced funding model</strong> can meet urgent needs while preserving a programme that delivers practical technology and scientific knowledge.","phrases":[{"en":"avoid prestige projects","context":"The strongest argument against space spending is opportunity cost. Money used for a distant mission cannot be spent on hospitals, schools or clean water. In poorer countries, these services can save lives immediately, whereas the benefits of space exploration may seem uncertain or remote. Governments should therefore avoid prestige projects that consume large sums without a clear scientific or public purpose."},{"en":"supports weather forecasting, navigation, communications and disaster management","context":"However, space research is not simply an expensive adventure. Satellite technology supports weather forecasting, navigation, communications and disaster management. It helps farmers monitor crops, allows emergency services to track storms and gives scientists reliable evidence about climate change. Research carried out for space missions has also contributed to advances in materials, computing and medicine. These benefits are shared far beyond the space industry."},{"en":"reviewed independently","context":"Space science is also a long-term investment. Satellites have recorded polar ice and sea temperatures for more than forty years, and a continuous record of that kind cannot be created once a crisis has already begun; the expertise and international cooperation behind such programmes take decades to build. Missions should still be reviewed independently, so that governments can show what each one delivers when it competes with pressing social needs."},{"en":"A balanced funding model","context":"In conclusion, basic living standards should receive the largest share of public money, but this does not make space research wasteful. A balanced funding model can meet urgent needs while preserving a programme that delivers practical technology and scientific knowledge."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#301","summary":"Millions of dollars are spent on space research every year."},{"id":"writing-task-2-128","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"The problems of mobile phones","prompt":"There are social, medical and technical problems associated with the use of mobile phones. What form do these problems take? Do the problems of using mobile phones outweigh the benefits?","answer":"Mobile phones create difficulties that are social, medical and technical in nature. In my opinion these problems are real but manageable, and they do not outweigh the considerable benefits that the technology provides.\n\nThe social problems are the most visible. People frequently attend to their phones rather than to those physically present, which weakens conversation within families, and constant availability means that work now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">intrudes into evenings and holidays</strong>. Children are exposed to bullying that follows them home, and online harassment is far harder to escape than the playground variety ever was.\n\nMedical concerns are more limited than often claimed. There is no reliable evidence that phone radiation causes cancer, but the effects on sleep are well established, since using a screen before bed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">delays the onset of sleep</strong>. Phone use while driving is a proven cause of fatal accidents, and repetitive strain in the hands and neck is now widespread. Technical problems include the loss of personal data through hacking, the theft of devices, and the environmental cost of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manufacturing and discarding</strong> hundreds of millions of handsets.\n\nAgainst these must be set benefits that were unimaginable a generation ago. A phone is a telephone, camera, map, bank, library and translator combined. It allows a farmer to check market prices, a traveller to navigate an unfamiliar city and anyone to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">summon emergency help immediately</strong>. In poorer countries, mobile banking has given millions their first access to financial services.\n\nIn conclusion, mobile phones create genuine social, medical and technical difficulties, but these arise mainly from how the devices are used. The advantages remain substantially greater.","phrases":[{"en":"intrudes into evenings and holidays","context":"The social problems are the most visible. People frequently attend to their phones rather than to those physically present, which weakens conversation within families, and constant availability means that work now intrudes into evenings and holidays. Children are exposed to bullying that follows them home, and online harassment is far harder to escape than the playground variety ever was."},{"en":"delays the onset of sleep","context":"Medical concerns are more limited than often claimed. There is no reliable evidence that phone radiation causes cancer, but the effects on sleep are well established, since using a screen before bed delays the onset of sleep. Phone use while driving is a proven cause of fatal accidents, and repetitive strain in the hands and neck is now widespread. Technical problems include the loss of personal data through hacking, the theft of devices, and the environmental cost of manufacturing and discarding hundreds of millions of handsets."},{"en":"manufacturing and discarding","context":"Medical concerns are more limited than often claimed. There is no reliable evidence that phone radiation causes cancer, but the effects on sleep are well established, since using a screen before bed delays the onset of sleep. Phone use while driving is a proven cause of fatal accidents, and repetitive strain in the hands and neck is now widespread. Technical problems include the loss of personal data through hacking, the theft of devices, and the environmental cost of manufacturing and discarding hundreds of millions of handsets."},{"en":"summon emergency help immediately","context":"Against these must be set benefits that were unimaginable a generation ago. A phone is a telephone, camera, map, bank, library and translator combined. It allows a farmer to check market prices, a traveller to navigate an unfamiliar city and anyone to summon emergency help immediately. In poorer countries, mobile banking has given millions their first access to financial services."}],"subtype":"Benefits & Drawbacks","_displayNo":"#302","summary":"The problems of mobile phones"},{"id":"writing-task-2-146","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Older inventions against recent ones","prompt":"Earlier technological development brought more changes to the life of ordinary people than recent technological development do. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Earlier inventions such as electricity, sanitation, the railway and the motor car transformed ordinary life on a remarkable scale. Nevertheless, I do not agree that recent technology has produced fewer changes. Digital systems have altered communication, work, commerce and access to knowledge in ways that are equally far-reaching.\n\nThe case for earlier technology is strong because it solved basic physical problems. Electricity extended productive hours, clean water reduced disease, and modern transport allowed people and goods to move quickly. These developments <strong class=\"key-phrase\">created the foundations of urban life</strong> and raised life expectancy. Their effect was immediate and visible across entire populations.\n\nRecent technology, however, has changed how people organise almost every daily activity. A smartphone now serves as a bank, map, camera, library and communication device. Remote work and online education allow services to cross national borders, while digital medical records and data analysis improve diagnosis. These changes may appear less dramatic than the arrival of electricity, but they have restructured the way information and decisions move through society.\n\nThe impact of recent innovation is also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">still unfolding</strong>, particularly in artificial intelligence, biotechnology and clean energy. Their long-term consequences may eventually be as visible as those of the railway or the electric grid.\n\nThe two periods are also connected: modern digital tools depend on earlier infrastructure. It is therefore unhelpful to rank one era as universally more important. In conclusion, earlier inventions met fundamental physical needs, while recent innovations have transformed coordination and knowledge. Both have brought <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changes of comparable depth</strong> in different areas of life.","phrases":[{"en":"created the foundations of urban life","context":"The case for earlier technology is strong because it solved basic physical problems. Electricity extended productive hours, clean water reduced disease, and modern transport allowed people and goods to move quickly. These developments created the foundations of urban life and raised life expectancy. Their effect was immediate and visible across entire populations."},{"en":"still unfolding","context":"The impact of recent innovation is also still unfolding, particularly in artificial intelligence, biotechnology and clean energy. Their long-term consequences may eventually be as visible as those of the railway or the electric grid."},{"en":"changes of comparable depth","context":"The two periods are also connected: modern digital tools depend on earlier infrastructure. It is therefore unhelpful to rank one era as universally more important. In conclusion, earlier inventions met fundamental physical needs, while recent innovations have transformed coordination and knowledge. Both have brought changes of comparable depth in different areas of life."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#303","summary":"Earlier technological development brought more changes to the life of…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-150","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Optimism about what science will do this century","prompt":"Many people are optimistic about the 21st century and expect scientists to make positive changes to the world. To what extent do you agree or disagree with their optimism? What changes would you like to see in the new century?","answer":"I am cautiously optimistic about the twenty-first century. Science has the potential to improve health, energy and living standards, but progress will only be positive if governments use new knowledge responsibly. The changes I would most like to see concern clean energy and equal access to healthcare.\n\nThere are good reasons for optimism. Medical research has already made many diseases easier to diagnose and treat, while digital technology allows knowledge to spread quickly. Further advances in vaccines, early screening and personalised treatment could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">extend healthy life expectancy</strong>. Science can also make farming more efficient and help cities use water and energy more carefully.\n\nHowever, scientific progress can create new risks. Automation may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remove jobs faster than workers can retrain</strong>, and poorly regulated data systems can threaten privacy. Environmental technology will also have limited value if countries continue to depend on fossil fuels. I would therefore like governments to invest heavily in renewable energy, public transport and energy-efficient housing. These measures would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduce emissions at source</strong> rather than simply deal with their consequences.\n\nA second priority should be affordable healthcare. Breakthroughs are of little value if only wealthy people can use them, and the gap is already visible: new cancer drugs and gene therapies are priced far beyond what most health systems can afford, and during the recent pandemic richer countries <strong class=\"key-phrase\">secured vaccine supplies many months before poorer ones</strong>. Public funding, international cooperation and fair pricing are needed so that essential treatment is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">available regardless of income</strong>.\n\nScientific progress alone will not guarantee a better century. New medicines, clean energy and more efficient farming can improve millions of lives, but access and regulation determine who benefits. I would particularly like to see research focused on affordable disease prevention and low-carbon infrastructure. These priorities could <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turn technical progress into widely shared human progress</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"extend healthy life expectancy","context":"There are good reasons for optimism. Medical research has already made many diseases easier to diagnose and treat, while digital technology allows knowledge to spread quickly. Further advances in vaccines, early screening and personalised treatment could extend healthy life expectancy. Science can also make farming more efficient and help cities use water and energy more carefully."},{"en":"remove jobs faster than workers can retrain","context":"However, scientific progress can create new risks. Automation may remove jobs faster than workers can retrain, and poorly regulated data systems can threaten privacy. Environmental technology will also have limited value if countries continue to depend on fossil fuels. I would therefore like governments to invest heavily in renewable energy, public transport and energy-efficient housing. These measures would reduce emissions at source rather than simply deal with their consequences."},{"en":"reduce emissions at source","context":"However, scientific progress can create new risks. Automation may remove jobs faster than workers can retrain, and poorly regulated data systems can threaten privacy. Environmental technology will also have limited value if countries continue to depend on fossil fuels. I would therefore like governments to invest heavily in renewable energy, public transport and energy-efficient housing. These measures would reduce emissions at source rather than simply deal with their consequences."},{"en":"secured vaccine supplies many months before poorer ones","context":"A second priority should be affordable healthcare. Breakthroughs are of little value if only wealthy people can use them, and the gap is already visible: new cancer drugs and gene therapies are priced far beyond what most health systems can afford, and during the recent pandemic richer countries secured vaccine supplies many months before poorer ones. Public funding, international cooperation and fair pricing are needed so that essential treatment is available regardless of income."},{"en":"available regardless of income","context":"A second priority should be affordable healthcare. Breakthroughs are of little value if only wealthy people can use them, and the gap is already visible: new cancer drugs and gene therapies are priced far beyond what most health systems can afford, and during the recent pandemic richer countries secured vaccine supplies many months before poorer ones. Public funding, international cooperation and fair pricing are needed so that essential treatment is available regardless of income."},{"en":"turn technical progress into widely shared human progress","context":"Scientific progress alone will not guarantee a better century. New medicines, clean energy and more efficient farming can improve millions of lives, but access and regulation determine who benefits. I would particularly like to see research focused on affordable disease prevention and low-carbon infrastructure. These priorities could turn technical progress into widely shared human progress."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#304","summary":"People are optimistic about the 21st century and expect scientists to make…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-168","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"What the arts tell us that science cannot","prompt":"The advance of science and technology has a significant impact on people, but the role of artists is also important, such as musicians, painters and writers. What can the arts tell us about life that science cannot?","answer":"Science explains how the natural world works, while the arts explore what human experience feels like. Both are important, but they answer different questions. Art can reveal emotion, identity and moral conflict in ways that scientific measurement alone cannot.\n\nA scientific study can describe the biological effects of grief, but a novel, painting or piece of music can help an audience understand the personal reality of loss. Art gives form to experiences that are difficult to measure, including love, fear, memory and belonging. Because people interpret a work differently, it can also encourage them to examine their own assumptions. In this sense, the arts provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">insight into the inner life of other people</strong>.\n\nThe arts also preserve the character of a society. Stories, songs and images record how communities understand family, authority, religion and historical change. Science may explain the materials used in an ancient object, but art and literature help us consider what that object meant to the people who made it. This cultural record allows later generations to see the world through a different set of values.\n\nFinally, art can explore moral questions before society reaches a clear answer. Films about artificial intelligence, genetic engineering or war do not provide technical solutions, but they can show the human consequences of a decision. This imaginative function is valuable because facts do not automatically tell us what we ought to do.\n\nIn conclusion, science offers reliable explanations and practical discoveries, whereas the arts deepen our understanding of emotion, culture and moral choice. They do not compete with science; they provide <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a complementary way of understanding life</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"insight into the inner life of other people","context":"A scientific study can describe the biological effects of grief, but a novel, painting or piece of music can help an audience understand the personal reality of loss. Art gives form to experiences that are difficult to measure, including love, fear, memory and belonging. Because people interpret a work differently, it can also encourage them to examine their own assumptions. In this sense, the arts provide insight into the inner life of other people."},{"en":"a complementary way of understanding life","context":"In conclusion, science offers reliable explanations and practical discoveries, whereas the arts deepen our understanding of emotion, culture and moral choice. They do not compete with science; they provide a complementary way of understanding life."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#305","summary":"Advance of science and technology has a significant impact on people, but…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-170","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Computers and children's development","prompt":"Some people believe that computers do not help children learn more effectively, and that using them has a negative effect on children's physical and mental development. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is sometimes claimed that computers do not improve children&#x27;s learning and that they damage physical and mental development. I disagree with the first part of this statement, although I accept that excessive screen use causes genuine harm.\n\nThe educational benefits are difficult to dismiss. Software can adapt to a child&#x27;s level, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">providing extra practice where it is needed</strong>, and immediate feedback helps learners <strong class=\"key-phrase\">correct mistakes before they become habits</strong>. Simulations allow students to observe chemical reactions or historical reconstructions that no classroom could provide, and children with dyslexia or impaired sight can use tools that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make written material accessible</strong> for the first time.\n\nThe concerns about development, however, deserve to be taken seriously. Children who spend many hours seated exercise less, and rates of obesity and poor posture have risen accordingly. Screen use before bed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">disrupts sleep</strong>, which affects concentration the following day. There is also reasonable evidence linking heavy social media use among adolescents, particularly girls, with anxiety and low self-esteem.\n\nThe important distinction is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">between purposeful and unlimited use</strong>. A child using educational software for an hour is in an entirely different situation from one scrolling through videos for five hours, and research on this subject is frequently criticised for confusing the two.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that computers fail to help children learn, since the evidence points clearly in the opposite direction. The real problem is the quantity and the type of screen time, and the sensible response is to regulate both rather than to avoid technology altogether.","phrases":[{"en":"providing extra practice where it is needed","context":"The educational benefits are difficult to dismiss. Software can adapt to a child's level, providing extra practice where it is needed, and immediate feedback helps learners correct mistakes before they become habits. Simulations allow students to observe chemical reactions or historical reconstructions that no classroom could provide, and children with dyslexia or impaired sight can use tools that make written material accessible for the first time."},{"en":"correct mistakes before they become habits","context":"The educational benefits are difficult to dismiss. Software can adapt to a child's level, providing extra practice where it is needed, and immediate feedback helps learners correct mistakes before they become habits. Simulations allow students to observe chemical reactions or historical reconstructions that no classroom could provide, and children with dyslexia or impaired sight can use tools that make written material accessible for the first time."},{"en":"make written material accessible","context":"The educational benefits are difficult to dismiss. Software can adapt to a child's level, providing extra practice where it is needed, and immediate feedback helps learners correct mistakes before they become habits. Simulations allow students to observe chemical reactions or historical reconstructions that no classroom could provide, and children with dyslexia or impaired sight can use tools that make written material accessible for the first time."},{"en":"disrupts sleep","context":"The concerns about development, however, deserve to be taken seriously. Children who spend many hours seated exercise less, and rates of obesity and poor posture have risen accordingly. Screen use before bed disrupts sleep, which affects concentration the following day. There is also reasonable evidence linking heavy social media use among adolescents, particularly girls, with anxiety and low self-esteem."},{"en":"between purposeful and unlimited use","context":"The important distinction is between purposeful and unlimited use. A child using educational software for an hour is in an entirely different situation from one scrolling through videos for five hours, and research on this subject is frequently criticised for confusing the two."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#306","summary":"Computers and children's development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-171","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Has technology given us more leisure time?","prompt":"Modern technology has given people more leisure time than in the past. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often claimed that machines and digital devices have freed people from work and given them more time to relax. I largely disagree with this view, because although technology has removed a great deal of physical labour, it has also created new demands that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consume the time it saves</strong>.\n\nThere is no doubt that technology has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">eliminated many hours of drudgery</strong>. Washing machines, dishwashers and vacuum cleaners have reduced housework from a full-time occupation to a few hours a week, which has been particularly important for women. In the workplace, computers perform in seconds calculations that once took clerks days, and online banking and shopping have removed the need for countless journeys. Measured purely in tasks completed per hour, we are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far more productive than our grandparents were</strong>.\n\nThe difficulty is that the time saved has not become free time. Employers have simply raised their expectations, so employees now produce far more within the same working day. Smartphones have also destroyed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the boundary between work and home</strong>, since emails arrive in the evening and at weekends and most people feel obliged to answer them. Much of what remains is absorbed by the devices themselves, because several hours a day now go to social media and streaming, which is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">distraction rather than genuine rest</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, technology has certainly made individual tasks quicker, but I disagree that it has given us more leisure. The hours it saves are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">immediately filled by heavier workloads</strong> and by the constant demands of the technology itself.","phrases":[{"en":"consume the time it saves","context":"It is often claimed that machines and digital devices have freed people from work and given them more time to relax. I largely disagree with this view, because although technology has removed a great deal of physical labour, it has also created new demands that consume the time it saves."},{"en":"eliminated many hours of drudgery","context":"There is no doubt that technology has eliminated many hours of drudgery. Washing machines, dishwashers and vacuum cleaners have reduced housework from a full-time occupation to a few hours a week, which has been particularly important for women. In the workplace, computers perform in seconds calculations that once took clerks days, and online banking and shopping have removed the need for countless journeys. Measured purely in tasks completed per hour, we are far more productive than our grandparents were."},{"en":"far more productive than our grandparents were","context":"There is no doubt that technology has eliminated many hours of drudgery. Washing machines, dishwashers and vacuum cleaners have reduced housework from a full-time occupation to a few hours a week, which has been particularly important for women. In the workplace, computers perform in seconds calculations that once took clerks days, and online banking and shopping have removed the need for countless journeys. Measured purely in tasks completed per hour, we are far more productive than our grandparents were."},{"en":"the boundary between work and home","context":"The difficulty is that the time saved has not become free time. Employers have simply raised their expectations, so employees now produce far more within the same working day. Smartphones have also destroyed the boundary between work and home, since emails arrive in the evening and at weekends and most people feel obliged to answer them. Much of what remains is absorbed by the devices themselves, because several hours a day now go to social media and streaming, which is distraction rather than genuine rest."},{"en":"distraction rather than genuine rest","context":"The difficulty is that the time saved has not become free time. Employers have simply raised their expectations, so employees now produce far more within the same working day. Smartphones have also destroyed the boundary between work and home, since emails arrive in the evening and at weekends and most people feel obliged to answer them. Much of what remains is absorbed by the devices themselves, because several hours a day now go to social media and streaming, which is distraction rather than genuine rest."},{"en":"immediately filled by heavier workloads","context":"In conclusion, technology has certainly made individual tasks quicker, but I disagree that it has given us more leisure. The hours it saves are immediately filled by heavier workloads and by the constant demands of the technology itself."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#307","summary":"Has technology given us more leisure time?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-173","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"The fast pace of modern life","prompt":"It is said that the fast pace of everyday life, resulting from the rapid development of telecommunications and travel, has negative effects on individuals, nations and the world as a whole. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often claimed that modern communications and cheap travel have made life faster, and that the consequences for individuals, nations and the planet are harmful. I partly agree: the pressures on individuals and the environment are real, but the picture is more mixed than this argument suggests.\n\nThe effects on individuals are the most convincing part of the case. Email and mobile phones have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removed the boundary between work and rest</strong>, so employees now answer messages in the evening and on holiday, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rates of stress-related illness have risen accordingly</strong>. Constant availability also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fragments attention</strong>, since people who are interrupted every few minutes find sustained concentration increasingly difficult.\n\nAt a global level the environmental argument is equally strong. Air travel is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">one of the fastest-growing sources of carbon emissions</strong>, and the expectation of rapid delivery has produced <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an enormous volume of freight and packaging</strong> that did not exist a generation ago.\n\nHowever, the same technologies have brought <strong class=\"key-phrase\">benefits that critics tend to overlook</strong>. Doctors can consult specialists on another continent within minutes, families separated by migration can see one another daily, and countries that were <strong class=\"key-phrase\">economically isolated</strong> now export to global markets. Emergency response to earthquakes and epidemics also depends entirely on the speed of modern communication, and thousands of lives are saved each year as a direct result.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that the pace of modern life damages individual well-being and the environment, and that both deserve serious attention. I do not accept, however, that the effects are simply negative, since the same developments have transformed healthcare, family contact and economic opportunity for the better.","phrases":[{"en":"removed the boundary between work and rest","context":"The effects on individuals are the most convincing part of the case. Email and mobile phones have removed the boundary between work and rest, so employees now answer messages in the evening and on holiday, and rates of stress-related illness have risen accordingly. Constant availability also fragments attention, since people who are interrupted every few minutes find sustained concentration increasingly difficult."},{"en":"rates of stress-related illness have risen accordingly","context":"The effects on individuals are the most convincing part of the case. Email and mobile phones have removed the boundary between work and rest, so employees now answer messages in the evening and on holiday, and rates of stress-related illness have risen accordingly. Constant availability also fragments attention, since people who are interrupted every few minutes find sustained concentration increasingly difficult."},{"en":"fragments attention","context":"The effects on individuals are the most convincing part of the case. Email and mobile phones have removed the boundary between work and rest, so employees now answer messages in the evening and on holiday, and rates of stress-related illness have risen accordingly. Constant availability also fragments attention, since people who are interrupted every few minutes find sustained concentration increasingly difficult."},{"en":"one of the fastest-growing sources of carbon emissions","context":"At a global level the environmental argument is equally strong. Air travel is one of the fastest-growing sources of carbon emissions, and the expectation of rapid delivery has produced an enormous volume of freight and packaging that did not exist a generation ago."},{"en":"an enormous volume of freight and packaging","context":"At a global level the environmental argument is equally strong. Air travel is one of the fastest-growing sources of carbon emissions, and the expectation of rapid delivery has produced an enormous volume of freight and packaging that did not exist a generation ago."},{"en":"benefits that critics tend to overlook","context":"However, the same technologies have brought benefits that critics tend to overlook. Doctors can consult specialists on another continent within minutes, families separated by migration can see one another daily, and countries that were economically isolated now export to global markets. Emergency response to earthquakes and epidemics also depends entirely on the speed of modern communication, and thousands of lives are saved each year as a direct result."},{"en":"economically isolated","context":"However, the same technologies have brought benefits that critics tend to overlook. Doctors can consult specialists on another continent within minutes, families separated by migration can see one another daily, and countries that were economically isolated now export to global markets. Emergency response to earthquakes and epidemics also depends entirely on the speed of modern communication, and thousands of lives are saved each year as a direct result."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#308","summary":"The fast pace of modern life"},{"id":"writing-task-2-175","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Has technology made our leisure less creative?","prompt":"The leisure industry is growing rapidly. The active role of modern technology in people's leisure lives nowadays has made them less creative. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Technology now occupies a large part of leisure time, leading some people to believe that it has reduced creativity. I disagree with this generalisation. Passive screen use can certainly become repetitive, but modern technology also gives ordinary people powerful tools to create, learn and share original work.\n\nThe negative argument is understandable. Recommendation systems often <strong class=\"key-phrase\">encourage users to watch similar videos for hours</strong>, and many games provide fixed goals rather than genuine freedom. When entertainment becomes automatic, people may spend less time reading, making things or inventing their own activities. This is mainly <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a problem of habit and design</strong>, not proof that all digital leisure is uncreative.\n\nIn contrast, affordable software allows people to edit films, compose music, draw, write and build games at home. Online courses teach techniques that were once available only through specialist schools, while creative communities provide feedback and collaboration. Even gaming can involve strategy, storytelling and the design of new worlds. Technology therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lowers the barrier between consuming and creating</strong>.\n\nEducation can improve the balance by teaching people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">how digital platforms compete for attention</strong>. When users understand recommendation systems and set limits on passive viewing, they have more time for demanding activities such as writing, design or music production. Schools and families can reinforce this habit by valuing finished creative projects, not simply hours spent online.\n\nIn conclusion, modern leisure can become passive when people use technology without purpose. However, the same devices can greatly expand creative opportunity. The important issue is whether users <strong class=\"key-phrase\">merely scroll through content</strong> or choose to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">use digital tools actively and deliberately</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"encourage users to watch similar videos for hours","context":"The negative argument is understandable. Recommendation systems often encourage users to watch similar videos for hours, and many games provide fixed goals rather than genuine freedom. When entertainment becomes automatic, people may spend less time reading, making things or inventing their own activities. This is mainly a problem of habit and design, not proof that all digital leisure is uncreative."},{"en":"a problem of habit and design","context":"The negative argument is understandable. Recommendation systems often encourage users to watch similar videos for hours, and many games provide fixed goals rather than genuine freedom. When entertainment becomes automatic, people may spend less time reading, making things or inventing their own activities. This is mainly a problem of habit and design, not proof that all digital leisure is uncreative."},{"en":"lowers the barrier between consuming and creating","context":"In contrast, affordable software allows people to edit films, compose music, draw, write and build games at home. Online courses teach techniques that were once available only through specialist schools, while creative communities provide feedback and collaboration. Even gaming can involve strategy, storytelling and the design of new worlds. Technology therefore lowers the barrier between consuming and creating."},{"en":"how digital platforms compete for attention","context":"Education can improve the balance by teaching people how digital platforms compete for attention. When users understand recommendation systems and set limits on passive viewing, they have more time for demanding activities such as writing, design or music production. Schools and families can reinforce this habit by valuing finished creative projects, not simply hours spent online."},{"en":"merely scroll through content","context":"In conclusion, modern leisure can become passive when people use technology without purpose. However, the same devices can greatly expand creative opportunity. The important issue is whether users merely scroll through content or choose to use digital tools actively and deliberately."},{"en":"use digital tools actively and deliberately","context":"In conclusion, modern leisure can become passive when people use technology without purpose. However, the same devices can greatly expand creative opportunity. The important issue is whether users merely scroll through content or choose to use digital tools actively and deliberately."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#309","summary":"Leisure industry is growing rapidly."},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"How technology has changed our relationships","prompt":"Nowadays the way many people interact with each other has changed because of technology. In what ways has technology affected the types of relationships people make? Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"Technology has changed both the number and nature of people&#x27;s relationships. It allows individuals to maintain contact across distance and to meet others with similar interests, but it can also make communication more superficial. Overall, the effect is positive when digital contact supports rather than replaces real interaction.\n\nMessaging, video calls and social networks make long-distance relationships easier to maintain. Families separated by work or migration can speak regularly, and professional teams can cooperate across countries. Online communities also help people with rare interests or health conditions find support. Technology has therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removed many barriers created by distance</strong>.\n\nThe weakness is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">convenience can reduce depth</strong>. Short messages often replace longer conversations, and people divide their attention between a screen and the person sitting in front of them. Social media also encourages comparison, since users measure their ordinary days against the edited version of life that their friends choose to post. These problems, though, come less from the technology itself than from habits of use.\n\nTechnology has also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expanded weak connections far more than close ones</strong>. Keeping in touch with former classmates or professional networks now costs almost nothing, whereas a close friendship is built by listening to someone at length when they are in difficulty, and no platform can do that on a user&#x27;s behalf. It is quite possible to have several hundred online contacts and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nobody to telephone at midnight</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, technology has widened the range of people we can reach while making it easier for that contact to remain shallow. On balance I regard the change as positive, provided that people set <strong class=\"key-phrase\">deliberate boundaries around online communication</strong> and use digital tools to arrange meetings and share real experiences rather than as a substitute for time spent together.","topic":"Technology & Science","phrases":[{"en":"removed many barriers created by distance","context":"Messaging, video calls and social networks make long-distance relationships easier to maintain. Families separated by work or migration can speak regularly, and professional teams can cooperate across countries. Online communities also help people with rare interests or health conditions find support. Technology has therefore removed many barriers created by distance."},{"en":"convenience can reduce depth","context":"The weakness is that convenience can reduce depth. Short messages often replace longer conversations, and people divide their attention between a screen and the person sitting in front of them. Social media also encourages comparison, since users measure their ordinary days against the edited version of life that their friends choose to post. These problems, though, come less from the technology itself than from habits of use."},{"en":"expanded weak connections far more than close ones","context":"Technology has also expanded weak connections far more than close ones. Keeping in touch with former classmates or professional networks now costs almost nothing, whereas a close friendship is built by listening to someone at length when they are in difficulty, and no platform can do that on a user's behalf. It is quite possible to have several hundred online contacts and nobody to telephone at midnight."},{"en":"nobody to telephone at midnight","context":"Technology has also expanded weak connections far more than close ones. Keeping in touch with former classmates or professional networks now costs almost nothing, whereas a close friendship is built by listening to someone at length when they are in difficulty, and no platform can do that on a user's behalf. It is quite possible to have several hundred online contacts and nobody to telephone at midnight."},{"en":"deliberate boundaries around online communication","context":"In conclusion, technology has widened the range of people we can reach while making it easier for that contact to remain shallow. On balance I regard the change as positive, provided that people set deliberate boundaries around online communication and use digital tools to arrange meetings and share real experiences rather than as a substitute for time spent together."}],"subtype":"positive","summary":"How technology has affected the relationships people make","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-176","_displayNo":"#310"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Giving companies your data in exchange for software","prompt":"Many people are now opting to provide technology companies with their personal data in exchange for access to software. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Many people exchange personal data for access to useful software. This arrangement offers convenience and lower prices, but it also creates serious privacy risks. In my view, the advantages outweigh the disadvantages only when users receive clear information and genuine control.\n\nData can improve a service. Navigation apps use location information to suggest routes, while streaming platforms recommend content based on previous choices. Advertising revenue also allows many tools to remain free. For users with limited budgets, this can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">expand access to valuable digital services</strong>.\n\nThe problem is that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">consent is often weak</strong>. Privacy policies are long, data may be shared with third parties and a breach can expose sensitive information. Users may also be profiled in ways that affect prices, employment or political messaging without their knowledge. Companies should collect only what is necessary, explain the purpose in plain language and offer a paid or less personalised alternative.\n\nRelying on companies to police themselves rarely works, since collecting more data is what makes many of these businesses more valuable and better at selling advertising. A firm that voluntarily limits what it collects earns less than a rival that does not, so left alone <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the incentive runs the wrong way</strong>. Independent enforcement, through <strong class=\"key-phrase\">audits and real fines for breaches</strong>, is needed to make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">data minimisation</strong> something companies must do, not merely something they might choose to do.\n\nIn conclusion, sharing some data can be a reasonable exchange, but only under strict conditions. Strong regulation, limited collection and easy deletion are needed to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give users meaningful control over their information</strong>. Without these safeguards, the convenience of free software does not justify the loss of privacy.","topic":"Technology & Science","phrases":[{"en":"expand access to valuable digital services","context":"Data can improve a service. Navigation apps use location information to suggest routes, while streaming platforms recommend content based on previous choices. Advertising revenue also allows many tools to remain free. For users with limited budgets, this can expand access to valuable digital services."},{"en":"consent is often weak","context":"The problem is that consent is often weak. Privacy policies are long, data may be shared with third parties and a breach can expose sensitive information. Users may also be profiled in ways that affect prices, employment or political messaging without their knowledge. Companies should collect only what is necessary, explain the purpose in plain language and offer a paid or less personalised alternative."},{"en":"the incentive runs the wrong way","context":"Relying on companies to police themselves rarely works, since collecting more data is what makes many of these businesses more valuable and better at selling advertising. A firm that voluntarily limits what it collects earns less than a rival that does not, so left alone the incentive runs the wrong way. Independent enforcement, through audits and real fines for breaches, is needed to make data minimisation something companies must do, not merely something they might choose to do."},{"en":"audits and real fines for breaches","context":"Relying on companies to police themselves rarely works, since collecting more data is what makes many of these businesses more valuable and better at selling advertising. A firm that voluntarily limits what it collects earns less than a rival that does not, so left alone the incentive runs the wrong way. Independent enforcement, through audits and real fines for breaches, is needed to make data minimisation something companies must do, not merely something they might choose to do."},{"en":"data minimisation","context":"Relying on companies to police themselves rarely works, since collecting more data is what makes many of these businesses more valuable and better at selling advertising. A firm that voluntarily limits what it collects earns less than a rival that does not, so left alone the incentive runs the wrong way. Independent enforcement, through audits and real fines for breaches, is needed to make data minimisation something companies must do, not merely something they might choose to do."},{"en":"give users meaningful control over their information","context":"In conclusion, sharing some data can be a reasonable exchange, but only under strict conditions. Strong regulation, limited collection and easy deletion are needed to give users meaningful control over their information. Without these safeguards, the convenience of free software does not justify the loss of privacy."}],"subtype":"outweigh","summary":"Giving personal data to technology companies in exchange for software","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-177","_displayNo":"#311"},{"id":"writing-task-2-182","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Did the Moon landing matter?","prompt":"The Moon landing is often described as one giant leap for mankind. Yet some people argue that this achievement made very little difference to ordinary people's daily lives. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"The first Moon landing is often described as one of humanity&#x27;s greatest achievements, yet some argue that it changed very little for ordinary people. In my opinion both claims contain some truth, but the achievement was more significant than its critics allow.\n\nThe sceptical position is easy to understand. The programme cost an enormous sum at a time when the United States faced poverty and racial conflict at home, and it was driven largely by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">competition with the Soviet Union</strong> rather than by science. No permanent base was established, nobody returned for half a century, and the daily life of an average family was not visibly altered by the fact that twelve men had walked on the Moon.\n\nThose who defend it point to consequences that were not obvious at the time. The programme required equipment that did not yet exist, and the effort to create it produced the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">miniaturised computing</strong> on which every modern device depends, along with advances in materials, medical monitoring and satellites. Satellite technology in particular now underpins weather forecasting, navigation and global communication, and few people realise how directly it descends from that programme.\n\nThere is also a less measurable effect. The landing demonstrated what a coordinated national effort could achieve, and photographs of the Earth taken from the Moon are widely credited with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strengthening the environmental movement</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the Moon landing did not transform daily life directly, and its motives were undeniably political. Its technological and cultural consequences, however, were substantial enough to justify calling it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine leap forward</strong> rather than an expensive gesture.","phrases":[{"en":"competition with the Soviet Union","context":"The sceptical position is easy to understand. The programme cost an enormous sum at a time when the United States faced poverty and racial conflict at home, and it was driven largely by competition with the Soviet Union rather than by science. No permanent base was established, nobody returned for half a century, and the daily life of an average family was not visibly altered by the fact that twelve men had walked on the Moon."},{"en":"miniaturised computing","context":"Those who defend it point to consequences that were not obvious at the time. The programme required equipment that did not yet exist, and the effort to create it produced the miniaturised computing on which every modern device depends, along with advances in materials, medical monitoring and satellites. Satellite technology in particular now underpins weather forecasting, navigation and global communication, and few people realise how directly it descends from that programme."},{"en":"strengthening the environmental movement","context":"There is also a less measurable effect. The landing demonstrated what a coordinated national effort could achieve, and photographs of the Earth taken from the Moon are widely credited with strengthening the environmental movement."},{"en":"a genuine leap forward","context":"In conclusion, the Moon landing did not transform daily life directly, and its motives were undeniably political. Its technological and cultural consequences, however, were substantial enough to justify calling it a genuine leap forward rather than an expensive gesture."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","_displayNo":"#312","summary":"Did the Moon landing matter?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-190","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Doing business without meeting face to face","prompt":"Nowadays people can carry out tasks such as shopping, banking and even business transactions without meeting each other face to face. What are the effects of this on individuals and on society?","answer":"Shopping, banking and business transactions can increasingly be completed without meeting another person face to face. This brings major <strong class=\"key-phrase\">practical benefits</strong>, but it can also weaken social contact and exclude people who cannot use digital services easily. In my view, society should develop these services while preserving <strong class=\"key-phrase\">suitable alternatives</strong>.\n\nFor individuals, convenience is the clearest advantage. A task that previously required travel and queuing may now take only a few minutes. This can be particularly useful for people with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mobility difficulties</strong>, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">caring responsibilities</strong> or homes far from a town centre. Comparing products and prices is also easier online. However, the benefit depends on access, confidence and reliable systems. A person who cannot resolve an account problem without using an unfamiliar app may find the new process less convenient than the old one. Remote transactions also create particular fraud risks when identities or payment details are misused.\n\nThe consequences for society are similarly mixed. Digital services can reduce some <strong class=\"key-phrase\">operating costs</strong> and allow small firms to reach customers beyond their local area. These opportunities may support competition and new kinds of employment. At the same time, closing branches and shops can remove both jobs and useful meeting places. Workers who need different skills may require training rather than simply being expected to adapt without assistance.\n\nThe best response is not to reject online transactions, but to design <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fair transition</strong>. Accessible interfaces, effective customer support and appropriate safeguards are essential. Where a service is important to everyday life, people should have a realistic way to use it even if a digital-only approach does not meet their needs.\n\nIn conclusion, less face-to-face contact can improve efficiency and widen opportunities, but the benefits are strongest when convenience is balanced with inclusion, security and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">continued human support</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"practical benefits","context":"Shopping, banking and business transactions can increasingly be completed without meeting another person face to face. This brings major practical benefits, but it can also weaken social contact and exclude people who cannot use digital services easily. In my view, society should develop these services while preserving suitable alternatives."},{"en":"suitable alternatives","context":"Shopping, banking and business transactions can increasingly be completed without meeting another person face to face. This brings major practical benefits, but it can also weaken social contact and exclude people who cannot use digital services easily. In my view, society should develop these services while preserving suitable alternatives."},{"en":"mobility difficulties","context":"For individuals, convenience is the clearest advantage. A task that previously required travel and queuing may now take only a few minutes. This can be particularly useful for people with mobility difficulties, caring responsibilities or homes far from a town centre. Comparing products and prices is also easier online. However, the benefit depends on access, confidence and reliable systems. A person who cannot resolve an account problem without using an unfamiliar app may find the new process less convenient than the old one. Remote transactions also create particular fraud risks when identities or payment details are misused."},{"en":"caring responsibilities","context":"For individuals, convenience is the clearest advantage. A task that previously required travel and queuing may now take only a few minutes. This can be particularly useful for people with mobility difficulties, caring responsibilities or homes far from a town centre. Comparing products and prices is also easier online. However, the benefit depends on access, confidence and reliable systems. A person who cannot resolve an account problem without using an unfamiliar app may find the new process less convenient than the old one. Remote transactions also create particular fraud risks when identities or payment details are misused."},{"en":"operating costs","context":"The consequences for society are similarly mixed. Digital services can reduce some operating costs and allow small firms to reach customers beyond their local area. These opportunities may support competition and new kinds of employment. At the same time, closing branches and shops can remove both jobs and useful meeting places. Workers who need different skills may require training rather than simply being expected to adapt without assistance."},{"en":"a fair transition","context":"The best response is not to reject online transactions, but to design a fair transition. Accessible interfaces, effective customer support and appropriate safeguards are essential. Where a service is important to everyday life, people should have a realistic way to use it even if a digital-only approach does not meet their needs."},{"en":"continued human support","context":"In conclusion, less face-to-face contact can improve efficiency and widen opportunities, but the benefits are strongest when convenience is balanced with inclusion, security and continued human support."}],"subtype":"Causes / Effects","_displayNo":"#313","summary":"Doing business without meeting face to face"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2201","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Is space exploration worth the money?","prompt":"Some people think that space exploration is a waste of time and money, while others believe that it is important for humankind. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Space programmes cost billions of dollars every year, and opinion is divided over whether this money is well spent. Some regard space exploration as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an expensive distraction</strong>, while others see it as essential to humanity&#x27;s future. I belong firmly to the second group.\n\nThe case against space exploration is easy to understand. The problems that governments face on Earth are urgent and enormous: poverty, disease, climate change and inadequate schools and hospitals. Critics argue that the sums involved in sending probes to distant planets would produce far greater returns if they were spent on vaccination programmes or clean water supplies. There is also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no guarantee of any benefit at all</strong>, since missions frequently fail and many discoveries interest only scientists.\n\nSupporters reply that the practical benefits are already substantial. Satellite technology, which came directly from the space programme, now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">underpins</strong> weather forecasting, global communications, navigation and the monitoring of deforestation and melting ice. Medical scanners, water purification systems and lightweight materials all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have their origins in</strong> space research. In the longer term, understanding other planets may be the only way to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protect the Earth from asteroid impacts</strong> or to secure resources once our own are exhausted.\n\nMy own view is that space exploration is a reasonable investment. The amounts involved are relatively small, since most space agencies receive <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a fraction of what governments spend on defence</strong>, so cancelling them would not solve poverty. Meanwhile the scientific returns have repeatedly proved <strong class=\"key-phrase\">far larger than anyone predicted</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, although the money could certainly be spent elsewhere, I believe space exploration is genuinely important for humankind.","phrases":[{"en":"an expensive distraction","context":"Space programmes cost billions of dollars every year, and opinion is divided over whether this money is well spent. Some regard space exploration as an expensive distraction, while others see it as essential to humanity's future. I belong firmly to the second group."},{"en":"no guarantee of any benefit at all","context":"The case against space exploration is easy to understand. The problems that governments face on Earth are urgent and enormous: poverty, disease, climate change and inadequate schools and hospitals. Critics argue that the sums involved in sending probes to distant planets would produce far greater returns if they were spent on vaccination programmes or clean water supplies. There is also no guarantee of any benefit at all, since missions frequently fail and many discoveries interest only scientists."},{"en":"underpins","context":"Supporters reply that the practical benefits are already substantial. Satellite technology, which came directly from the space programme, now underpins weather forecasting, global communications, navigation and the monitoring of deforestation and melting ice. Medical scanners, water purification systems and lightweight materials all have their origins in space research. In the longer term, understanding other planets may be the only way to protect the Earth from asteroid impacts or to secure resources once our own are exhausted."},{"en":"have their origins in","context":"Supporters reply that the practical benefits are already substantial. Satellite technology, which came directly from the space programme, now underpins weather forecasting, global communications, navigation and the monitoring of deforestation and melting ice. Medical scanners, water purification systems and lightweight materials all have their origins in space research. In the longer term, understanding other planets may be the only way to protect the Earth from asteroid impacts or to secure resources once our own are exhausted."},{"en":"protect the Earth from asteroid impacts","context":"Supporters reply that the practical benefits are already substantial. Satellite technology, which came directly from the space programme, now underpins weather forecasting, global communications, navigation and the monitoring of deforestation and melting ice. Medical scanners, water purification systems and lightweight materials all have their origins in space research. In the longer term, understanding other planets may be the only way to protect the Earth from asteroid impacts or to secure resources once our own are exhausted."},{"en":"a fraction of what governments spend on defence","context":"My own view is that space exploration is a reasonable investment. The amounts involved are relatively small, since most space agencies receive a fraction of what governments spend on defence, so cancelling them would not solve poverty. Meanwhile the scientific returns have repeatedly proved far larger than anyone predicted."},{"en":"far larger than anyone predicted","context":"My own view is that space exploration is a reasonable investment. The amounts involved are relatively small, since most space agencies receive a fraction of what governments spend on defence, so cancelling them would not solve poverty. Meanwhile the scientific returns have repeatedly proved far larger than anyone predicted."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#314","summary":"Is space exploration worth the money?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2215","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Are robots essential or dangerous?","prompt":"Some people think that robots are very important to humans' future development, while others think that they are dangerous and have negative effects on society. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Robots are already common in factories, hospitals and warehouses, and opinion is divided over whether they represent progress or a threat. In my view the technology itself is clearly valuable, and the real danger lies in how societies choose to manage its effects.\n\nThose who see robots as essential point to work that machines simply do better than people. A machine does not tire, lose concentration or need to breathe, so it can weld the same joint ten thousand times without variation, or inspect a damaged nuclear reactor where a human being would receive a fatal dose of radiation. Surgical robots allow instruments to move within a millimetre of a target, which shortens recovery times and reduces the risk of complications. Work of this kind is either beyond human capability or dangerous enough that we should welcome a machine taking it on.\n\nThe opposing argument is that automation <strong class=\"key-phrase\">destroys livelihoods faster than new ones appear</strong>. When a distribution centre installs robotic pickers, the jobs that disappear are exactly the ones that gave school leavers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a first foothold in employment</strong>, and a warehouse worker of fifty cannot easily <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retrain as a software engineer</strong>. There is also a legitimate worry about who owns the machines, because the gains from automation flow to the small number of firms that can afford it.\n\nI find the second concern serious but misdirected. Every objection above describes a failure of policy rather than a fault in the technology, and countries that fund retraining and tax productivity gains have absorbed automation without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mass displacement</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, robots will be central to future development because they extend what human beings can safely do. Whether that development benefits everyone depends on governments preparing for the transition rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaving workers to adjust alone</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"destroys livelihoods faster than new ones appear","context":"The opposing argument is that automation destroys livelihoods faster than new ones appear. When a distribution centre installs robotic pickers, the jobs that disappear are exactly the ones that gave school leavers a first foothold in employment, and a warehouse worker of fifty cannot easily retrain as a software engineer. There is also a legitimate worry about who owns the machines, because the gains from automation flow to the small number of firms that can afford it."},{"en":"a first foothold in employment","context":"The opposing argument is that automation destroys livelihoods faster than new ones appear. When a distribution centre installs robotic pickers, the jobs that disappear are exactly the ones that gave school leavers a first foothold in employment, and a warehouse worker of fifty cannot easily retrain as a software engineer. There is also a legitimate worry about who owns the machines, because the gains from automation flow to the small number of firms that can afford it."},{"en":"retrain as a software engineer","context":"The opposing argument is that automation destroys livelihoods faster than new ones appear. When a distribution centre installs robotic pickers, the jobs that disappear are exactly the ones that gave school leavers a first foothold in employment, and a warehouse worker of fifty cannot easily retrain as a software engineer. There is also a legitimate worry about who owns the machines, because the gains from automation flow to the small number of firms that can afford it."},{"en":"mass displacement","context":"I find the second concern serious but misdirected. Every objection above describes a failure of policy rather than a fault in the technology, and countries that fund retraining and tax productivity gains have absorbed automation without mass displacement."},{"en":"leaving workers to adjust alone","context":"In conclusion, robots will be central to future development because they extend what human beings can safely do. Whether that development benefits everyone depends on governments preparing for the transition rather than leaving workers to adjust alone."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#315","summary":"Robots and humanity's future development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2218","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Storing knowledge online","prompt":"In the past, knowledge was stored in books. Today, people store knowledge on the internet. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"Human knowledge was once held in printed volumes on library shelves; it now sits on servers and is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retrieved in seconds</strong>. In my view the advantages of this shift are decisive, although it has introduced problems that printed books never had.\n\nThe most important gain is access. A medical paper that would once have existed in a few hundred university libraries can now be read by a doctor in a rural clinic with a phone, and a student who cannot afford textbooks can study from free encyclopaedias and lecture recordings. Digital storage also allows knowledge to be corrected quickly. When a scientific finding is overturned, an online reference is updated within days, whereas a printed edition <strong class=\"key-phrase\">carries the error for years</strong> until the next reprint.\n\nSearchability matters just as much. Finding one statistic in a printed collection could take an afternoon; the same search now takes a moment, which frees researchers to spend their time thinking rather than hunting for material.\n\nThe drawbacks are real, however. Anyone can publish online, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accurate work sits beside deliberate falsehood</strong> with no obvious difference in appearance, and readers who have never been taught to check a source are easily misled. Digital records are also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less permanent than they look</strong>. Websites are taken down, formats become unreadable and whole archives can be lost when a company closes, while a book printed two centuries ago <strong class=\"key-phrase\">remains legible on the shelf</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the benefits clearly outweigh the costs, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">open access to reliable information</strong> changes far more lives than any library system ever could. The disadvantages are nonetheless worth taking seriously, and they argue for teaching people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evaluate what they read</strong> and for funding proper digital archives rather than for returning to print.","phrases":[{"en":"retrieved in seconds","context":"Human knowledge was once held in printed volumes on library shelves; it now sits on servers and is retrieved in seconds. In my view the advantages of this shift are decisive, although it has introduced problems that printed books never had."},{"en":"carries the error for years","context":"The most important gain is access. A medical paper that would once have existed in a few hundred university libraries can now be read by a doctor in a rural clinic with a phone, and a student who cannot afford textbooks can study from free encyclopaedias and lecture recordings. Digital storage also allows knowledge to be corrected quickly. When a scientific finding is overturned, an online reference is updated within days, whereas a printed edition carries the error for years until the next reprint."},{"en":"accurate work sits beside deliberate falsehood","context":"The drawbacks are real, however. Anyone can publish online, so accurate work sits beside deliberate falsehood with no obvious difference in appearance, and readers who have never been taught to check a source are easily misled. Digital records are also less permanent than they look. Websites are taken down, formats become unreadable and whole archives can be lost when a company closes, while a book printed two centuries ago remains legible on the shelf."},{"en":"less permanent than they look","context":"The drawbacks are real, however. Anyone can publish online, so accurate work sits beside deliberate falsehood with no obvious difference in appearance, and readers who have never been taught to check a source are easily misled. Digital records are also less permanent than they look. Websites are taken down, formats become unreadable and whole archives can be lost when a company closes, while a book printed two centuries ago remains legible on the shelf."},{"en":"remains legible on the shelf","context":"The drawbacks are real, however. Anyone can publish online, so accurate work sits beside deliberate falsehood with no obvious difference in appearance, and readers who have never been taught to check a source are easily misled. Digital records are also less permanent than they look. Websites are taken down, formats become unreadable and whole archives can be lost when a company closes, while a book printed two centuries ago remains legible on the shelf."},{"en":"open access to reliable information","context":"In conclusion, the benefits clearly outweigh the costs, because open access to reliable information changes far more lives than any library system ever could. The disadvantages are nonetheless worth taking seriously, and they argue for teaching people to evaluate what they read and for funding proper digital archives rather than for returning to print."},{"en":"evaluate what they read","context":"In conclusion, the benefits clearly outweigh the costs, because open access to reliable information changes far more lives than any library system ever could. The disadvantages are nonetheless worth taking seriously, and they argue for teaching people to evaluate what they read and for funding proper digital archives rather than for returning to print."}],"subtype":"Advantages & Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#316","summary":"Knowledge stored in books compared with on the internet"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2233","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Who benefits from communications technology?","prompt":"Some groups of people have benefited from modern communications technology but some people do not benefit at all. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Mobile networks and the internet are often described as universal, yet their advantages have reached some groups far more completely than others. I agree that the benefits are unevenly distributed, although the claim that certain people gain nothing at all goes too far.\n\nThe clearest winners are those who already had education and money. A professional who can work remotely, bank online and study from anywhere converts the technology directly into higher income and greater freedom. Businesses gain in the same way, since a small firm can now reach customers abroad without an office or a sales force, and a farmer with a phone can check prices before selling rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accepting whatever a trader offers</strong>.\n\nOthers are largely excluded. Elderly people who never used a computer at work face services that assume everyone can navigate an application, and as banks and government offices close their counters, those who cannot go online <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lose access to things they previously managed unaided</strong>. Households too poor for a connection are similarly cut off, and this now damages children directly, since homework and job applications both <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assume a device at home</strong>.\n\nI would qualify the statement in one respect. Even people who use no technology themselves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">benefit from it indirectly</strong>, because ambulances are dispatched faster, medicines are distributed more reliably and the goods in local shops arrive because of systems they never see. The gain is real but invisible to them.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that the benefits fall unequally, and that being unable to get online is now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a serious disadvantage rather than a preference</strong>. The remedy is not to slow the technology but to keep services reachable by other means and to treat a connection as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a basic utility rather than a luxury</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"accepting whatever a trader offers","context":"The clearest winners are those who already had education and money. A professional who can work remotely, bank online and study from anywhere converts the technology directly into higher income and greater freedom. Businesses gain in the same way, since a small firm can now reach customers abroad without an office or a sales force, and a farmer with a phone can check prices before selling rather than accepting whatever a trader offers."},{"en":"lose access to things they previously managed unaided","context":"Others are largely excluded. Elderly people who never used a computer at work face services that assume everyone can navigate an application, and as banks and government offices close their counters, those who cannot go online lose access to things they previously managed unaided. Households too poor for a connection are similarly cut off, and this now damages children directly, since homework and job applications both assume a device at home."},{"en":"assume a device at home","context":"Others are largely excluded. Elderly people who never used a computer at work face services that assume everyone can navigate an application, and as banks and government offices close their counters, those who cannot go online lose access to things they previously managed unaided. Households too poor for a connection are similarly cut off, and this now damages children directly, since homework and job applications both assume a device at home."},{"en":"benefit from it indirectly","context":"I would qualify the statement in one respect. Even people who use no technology themselves benefit from it indirectly, because ambulances are dispatched faster, medicines are distributed more reliably and the goods in local shops arrive because of systems they never see. The gain is real but invisible to them."},{"en":"a serious disadvantage rather than a preference","context":"In conclusion, I agree that the benefits fall unequally, and that being unable to get online is now a serious disadvantage rather than a preference. The remedy is not to slow the technology but to keep services reachable by other means and to treat a connection as a basic utility rather than a luxury."},{"en":"a basic utility rather than a luxury","context":"In conclusion, I agree that the benefits fall unequally, and that being unable to get online is now a serious disadvantage rather than a preference. The remedy is not to slow the technology but to keep services reachable by other means and to treat a connection as a basic utility rather than a luxury."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#317","summary":"Uneven benefits from modern communications technology"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2239","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Should we contact other worlds?","prompt":"Some scientists think that there are intelligent life forms on other planets and that a message should be sent to contact them. Other scientists think that it is a bad idea and would be dangerous. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Astronomers have identified thousands of planets capable of supporting life, which has revived an old argument about whether humanity should announce its presence. Both sides make serious points, and I favour the cautious position for reasons that are practical rather than fearful.\n\nThose in favour argue that contact would be the most significant discovery in history. Confirming that we are not alone would transform biology, and any civilisation capable of receiving a signal would almost certainly be older than ours, so what could be learned about energy, medicine or survival is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impossible to estimate in advance</strong>. There is also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an argument from consistency</strong>: we already listen for signals, and a universe in which everyone listens and nobody transmits <strong class=\"key-phrase\">produces silence by design</strong>.\n\nThe opposing view rests on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the asymmetry between us and anyone who replies</strong>. A civilisation able to cross interstellar distances would be so far ahead of us that we would have no way of protecting ourselves if its intentions were not friendly, and human history offers little comfort about what happens when a technologically advanced society meets a weaker one. Critics also object that a handful of researchers are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speaking on behalf of the entire planet</strong> <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without any mandate to do so</strong>.\n\nMy own view rests on that last point. The risk cannot be estimated, since we have no evidence at all about who might be listening, and a decision whose consequences fall on everyone should not be taken by a small group.\n\nIn conclusion, the scientific case for contact is genuine, and I would not oppose transmission permanently. It should follow an international agreement rather than an individual initiative, because this is one experiment that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be stopped once it has begun</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"impossible to estimate in advance","context":"Those in favour argue that contact would be the most significant discovery in history. Confirming that we are not alone would transform biology, and any civilisation capable of receiving a signal would almost certainly be older than ours, so what could be learned about energy, medicine or survival is impossible to estimate in advance. There is also an argument from consistency: we already listen for signals, and a universe in which everyone listens and nobody transmits produces silence by design."},{"en":"an argument from consistency","context":"Those in favour argue that contact would be the most significant discovery in history. Confirming that we are not alone would transform biology, and any civilisation capable of receiving a signal would almost certainly be older than ours, so what could be learned about energy, medicine or survival is impossible to estimate in advance. There is also an argument from consistency: we already listen for signals, and a universe in which everyone listens and nobody transmits produces silence by design."},{"en":"produces silence by design","context":"Those in favour argue that contact would be the most significant discovery in history. Confirming that we are not alone would transform biology, and any civilisation capable of receiving a signal would almost certainly be older than ours, so what could be learned about energy, medicine or survival is impossible to estimate in advance. There is also an argument from consistency: we already listen for signals, and a universe in which everyone listens and nobody transmits produces silence by design."},{"en":"the asymmetry between us and anyone who replies","context":"The opposing view rests on the asymmetry between us and anyone who replies. A civilisation able to cross interstellar distances would be so far ahead of us that we would have no way of protecting ourselves if its intentions were not friendly, and human history offers little comfort about what happens when a technologically advanced society meets a weaker one. Critics also object that a handful of researchers are speaking on behalf of the entire planet without any mandate to do so."},{"en":"speaking on behalf of the entire planet","context":"The opposing view rests on the asymmetry between us and anyone who replies. A civilisation able to cross interstellar distances would be so far ahead of us that we would have no way of protecting ourselves if its intentions were not friendly, and human history offers little comfort about what happens when a technologically advanced society meets a weaker one. Critics also object that a handful of researchers are speaking on behalf of the entire planet without any mandate to do so."},{"en":"without any mandate to do so","context":"The opposing view rests on the asymmetry between us and anyone who replies. A civilisation able to cross interstellar distances would be so far ahead of us that we would have no way of protecting ourselves if its intentions were not friendly, and human history offers little comfort about what happens when a technologically advanced society meets a weaker one. Critics also object that a handful of researchers are speaking on behalf of the entire planet without any mandate to do so."},{"en":"cannot be stopped once it has begun","context":"In conclusion, the scientific case for contact is genuine, and I would not oppose transmission permanently. It should follow an international agreement rather than an individual initiative, because this is one experiment that cannot be stopped once it has begun."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#318","summary":"Whether messages should be sent to intelligent life on other planets"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2250","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Are we losing face-to-face skills?","prompt":"Today more and more people are using mobile phones and computers. As a result, people are losing the ability to communicate face to face. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"It is often claimed that constant messaging is eroding our capacity to talk to each other in person. I partly agree: certain skills are genuinely weakening, but the broader claim that the ability is being lost overstates what the evidence shows.\n\nThere is something in the argument. Conversation in person requires reading a face, tolerating a pause and responding without time to think, and these are skills that improve only with practice. A teenager who conducts most of their social life through a screen gets less of that practice, and it shows in situations such as interviews, where young candidates increasingly report that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">speaking to a stranger feels unnatural</strong>. Devices also interrupt the conversations that do happen, since a phone placed on a table measurably reduces how much either person discloses.\n\nThe stronger objection is that most digital communication replaces no conversation at all. A message to a friend in another country substitutes for a letter or for silence, not for a meeting, and video calls have allowed families separated by migration to see each other in a way that was <strong class=\"key-phrase\">impossible a generation ago</strong>. Technology has also given a voice to people who find speaking difficult, including those with severe anxiety or autism, for whom writing is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not a lesser form of contact</strong>.\n\nMy own conclusion is that the skill is being redistributed rather than lost. People still talk at length to those they care about; what has declined is casual conversation with strangers, because the situations that once forced it have disappeared.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that certain conversational habits are weakening and that phones interrupt time spent together. The ability itself is not disappearing, and the remedy is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protect occasions for talking</strong> rather than to reject the technology.","phrases":[{"en":"speaking to a stranger feels unnatural","context":"There is something in the argument. Conversation in person requires reading a face, tolerating a pause and responding without time to think, and these are skills that improve only with practice. A teenager who conducts most of their social life through a screen gets less of that practice, and it shows in situations such as interviews, where young candidates increasingly report that speaking to a stranger feels unnatural. Devices also interrupt the conversations that do happen, since a phone placed on a table measurably reduces how much either person discloses."},{"en":"impossible a generation ago","context":"The stronger objection is that most digital communication replaces no conversation at all. A message to a friend in another country substitutes for a letter or for silence, not for a meeting, and video calls have allowed families separated by migration to see each other in a way that was impossible a generation ago. Technology has also given a voice to people who find speaking difficult, including those with severe anxiety or autism, for whom writing is not a lesser form of contact."},{"en":"not a lesser form of contact","context":"The stronger objection is that most digital communication replaces no conversation at all. A message to a friend in another country substitutes for a letter or for silence, not for a meeting, and video calls have allowed families separated by migration to see each other in a way that was impossible a generation ago. Technology has also given a voice to people who find speaking difficult, including those with severe anxiety or autism, for whom writing is not a lesser form of contact."},{"en":"protect occasions for talking","context":"In conclusion, I agree that certain conversational habits are weakening and that phones interrupt time spent together. The ability itself is not disappearing, and the remedy is to protect occasions for talking rather than to reject the technology."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#319","summary":"Whether phones and computers are eroding face-to-face communication"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2258","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Do printed books still matter?","prompt":"Some think that printed books are not necessary in the digital era, because all writing can be stored electronically. Others think printed books still play an important role. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Now that any text can be stored and read on a device, some people regard printed books as an expensive habit. Others argue that the physical object still does something a file cannot, and I find myself closer to the second position.\n\nThe argument against print is largely practical. A single device holds thousands of titles, weighs less than one hardback and can be carried anywhere, which matters greatly to students and to anyone living in a small flat. Digital text is also cheaper to produce and distribute, so a reader in a country with few bookshops can obtain a title immediately, and adjustable type makes reading possible for people with poor eyesight. Printing consumes paper, ink and transport, none of which a download <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requires at any stage</strong>.\n\nThose who defend print point to how reading actually works. Studies of comprehension consistently find that readers remember more from a printed page, partly because the physical position of text helps memory and partly because a book does not interrupt with notifications. A printed book is also permanent in a way that a licensed file is not, since a publisher can withdraw a digital edition from devices that have already paid for it, which has happened more than once.\n\nMy own view is that the two suit different purposes rather than competing. Reference material and casual reading belong on a screen, whereas anything that must be read closely or kept for decades is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">better held in print</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, digital storage has genuine advantages of cost and portability, and it has made reading possible for people who lacked access. Printed books remain necessary nonetheless, because they support deeper reading and because ownership of a physical copy <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cannot be revoked</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"requires at any stage","context":"The argument against print is largely practical. A single device holds thousands of titles, weighs less than one hardback and can be carried anywhere, which matters greatly to students and to anyone living in a small flat. Digital text is also cheaper to produce and distribute, so a reader in a country with few bookshops can obtain a title immediately, and adjustable type makes reading possible for people with poor eyesight. Printing consumes paper, ink and transport, none of which a download requires at any stage."},{"en":"better held in print","context":"My own view is that the two suit different purposes rather than competing. Reference material and casual reading belong on a screen, whereas anything that must be read closely or kept for decades is better held in print."},{"en":"cannot be revoked","context":"In conclusion, digital storage has genuine advantages of cost and portability, and it has made reading possible for people who lacked access. Printed books remain necessary nonetheless, because they support deeper reading and because ownership of a physical copy cannot be revoked."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#320","summary":"The role of printed books in the digital era"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2270","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Has technology made life complicated?","prompt":"Some people think the developments of technology make people's lives more complex, so we should make life simpler without using technology. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Modern life certainly feels busier than it once did, and some conclude that we should simplify it by using less technology. I agree that technology has added complexity, but I disagree entirely with the remedy proposed.\n\nThe observation itself is fair. Tasks that were once single actions now involve accounts, passwords and updates, and a person may manage a dozen applications simply to pay bills and arrange a journey. Technology has also removed the boundaries that used to structure a day, since messages arrive at any hour and work follows people home, so many feel that they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never entirely off duty</strong>. The pace of change adds to this, because skills learned once now have to be relearned repeatedly.\n\nThe proposed solution nonetheless collapses on inspection. Almost everything that makes contemporary life bearable rests on the technology being rejected: medicine that treats illnesses which were once fatal, the systems that put food in shops, and the networks that let families separated by migration speak daily. Abandoning technology would not produce a simpler life but a shorter and harder one, and the people who romanticise simplicity rarely propose <strong class=\"key-phrase\">giving up antibiotics or clean water</strong>.\n\nThe complexity is also better explained by how technology is used than by its existence. A phone interrupts because notifications are switched on, and work reaches people in the evening because employers expect it to, so both can be changed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">without discarding the device</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that technology has made daily life more complicated and more difficult to switch off from. The answer is to set limits on how it is used rather than to reject it, since removing it would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take the benefits along with the problems</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"never entirely off duty","context":"The observation itself is fair. Tasks that were once single actions now involve accounts, passwords and updates, and a person may manage a dozen applications simply to pay bills and arrange a journey. Technology has also removed the boundaries that used to structure a day, since messages arrive at any hour and work follows people home, so many feel that they are never entirely off duty. The pace of change adds to this, because skills learned once now have to be relearned repeatedly."},{"en":"giving up antibiotics or clean water","context":"The proposed solution nonetheless collapses on inspection. Almost everything that makes contemporary life bearable rests on the technology being rejected: medicine that treats illnesses which were once fatal, the systems that put food in shops, and the networks that let families separated by migration speak daily. Abandoning technology would not produce a simpler life but a shorter and harder one, and the people who romanticise simplicity rarely propose giving up antibiotics or clean water."},{"en":"without discarding the device","context":"The complexity is also better explained by how technology is used than by its existence. A phone interrupts because notifications are switched on, and work reaches people in the evening because employers expect it to, so both can be changed without discarding the device."},{"en":"take the benefits along with the problems","context":"In conclusion, I agree that technology has made daily life more complicated and more difficult to switch off from. The answer is to set limits on how it is used rather than to reject it, since removing it would take the benefits along with the problems."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#321","summary":"Whether life should be simplified by using less technology"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2293","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Who should control research?","prompt":"Many people believe that scientific research should be carried out and controlled by governments rather than by private companies. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"An increasing share of the world&#x27;s research is now funded by companies rather than states. I partly agree that governments should control it, but the answer depends on the kind of research rather than on a general principle.\n\nThe argument for public control is strongest where the incentives of business point the wrong way. A company must produce a return, so it works on problems with a paying market: treatments for wealthy patients rather than diseases of the poor, and products rather than the basic knowledge those products rest on. Firms also own their results, so findings are protected by patents instead of shared, and research that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">threatens the value of an existing product</strong> may never be published at all. Public funding is what supports work with no obvious application, which is where the largest discoveries have historically come from.\n\nPrivate research nonetheless does things governments do badly. Turning a discovery into something people can actually use requires enormous investment and a tolerance for failure that no minister enjoys explaining, and companies are far quicker at development, manufacture and distribution. They also respond to demand faster than a committee allocating grants, and public research has weaknesses of its own, since political priorities can shape findings as easily as commercial ones.\n\nThe arrangement that works is division rather than control. Governments should fund basic science, medicine for conditions with no market, and anything affecting public safety, while regulating private research strictly: registering trials in advance, requiring publication of negative results, and preventing a company from suppressing what it does not like.\n\nIn conclusion, government funding is essential for research that markets will not pay for and for knowledge that should belong to everyone. Private research delivers the practical results, so the sensible policy is public money for the questions and firm rules for the answers.","phrases":[{"en":"threatens the value of an existing product","context":"The argument for public control is strongest where the incentives of business point the wrong way. A company must produce a return, so it works on problems with a paying market: treatments for wealthy patients rather than diseases of the poor, and products rather than the basic knowledge those products rest on. Firms also own their results, so findings are protected by patents instead of shared, and research that threatens the value of an existing product may never be published at all. Public funding is what supports work with no obvious application, which is where the largest discoveries have historically come from."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#322","summary":"Whether governments rather than private firms should run scientific research"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2295","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Holidays in space","prompt":"Space travel has been possible for some time, and some people believe that space tourism could be developed in the future. Do you think this is a positive or negative development?","answer":"Several companies now sell brief flights beyond the atmosphere, and longer trips are promised within a decade. I regard the development as negative at present, though the reasons are practical rather than a hostility to space exploration.\n\nThere is a reasonable case in its favour. Paying passengers fund the engineering that reduces the cost of reaching orbit, and much of what makes satellites and scientific missions cheaper has been developed by companies whose income comes partly from tourism. The industry creates skilled employment and encourages young people into science, and the experience itself appears to change how travellers regard the planet, since almost every astronaut describes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">seeing the earth without borders</strong> as the strongest impression of the journey.\n\nThe objections are stronger. Each launch releases a large quantity of carbon and particles directly into the upper atmosphere, where the effect on the climate is worse than at ground level, and this is done for a journey lasting minutes. It is difficult to justify at a time when ordinary people are being asked to fly less. The cost is the second objection: tickets are priced beyond almost everyone, so the resources go towards <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an experience available only to the very wealthy</strong> while problems on the ground remain unfunded.\n\nThere are further difficulties. Orbit is already crowded with debris, and increasing traffic raises the risk of collisions that could damage the satellites weather forecasting and communication depend on. Safety regulation has not kept pace with the industry either, and a serious accident involving paying passengers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">would set the whole field back many years</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, space tourism advances useful technology and inspires people, and those benefits are real. Its environmental cost, extreme exclusivity and the pressure it places on orbit make it, for now, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a luxury the moment cannot justify</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"seeing the earth without borders","context":"There is a reasonable case in its favour. Paying passengers fund the engineering that reduces the cost of reaching orbit, and much of what makes satellites and scientific missions cheaper has been developed by companies whose income comes partly from tourism. The industry creates skilled employment and encourages young people into science, and the experience itself appears to change how travellers regard the planet, since almost every astronaut describes seeing the earth without borders as the strongest impression of the journey."},{"en":"an experience available only to the very wealthy","context":"The objections are stronger. Each launch releases a large quantity of carbon and particles directly into the upper atmosphere, where the effect on the climate is worse than at ground level, and this is done for a journey lasting minutes. It is difficult to justify at a time when ordinary people are being asked to fly less. The cost is the second objection: tickets are priced beyond almost everyone, so the resources go towards an experience available only to the very wealthy while problems on the ground remain unfunded."},{"en":"would set the whole field back many years","context":"There are further difficulties. Orbit is already crowded with debris, and increasing traffic raises the risk of collisions that could damage the satellites weather forecasting and communication depend on. Safety regulation has not kept pace with the industry either, and a serious accident involving paying passengers would set the whole field back many years."},{"en":"a luxury the moment cannot justify","context":"In conclusion, space tourism advances useful technology and inspires people, and those benefits are real. Its environmental cost, extreme exclusivity and the pressure it places on orbit make it, for now, a luxury the moment cannot justify."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#323","summary":"Whether the development of space tourism would be a good thing"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2310","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Is the Internet the greatest invention?","prompt":"There have been many inventions in human history, such as the wheel. Some people think the most important one is the Internet. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The Internet has reshaped work, learning and friendship within a single generation, which makes the claim that it leads all inventions tempting. I disagree, because a handful of older inventions did something the Internet has not: they made human life possible at its present scale.\n\nThe case for the Internet is strong on reach. It has given ordinary people access to almost all recorded knowledge, allowed a farmer to check a market price and a student in a village to attend a lecture, and it carries the science, banking and logistics that modern economies run on. It also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removed distance as a barrier to working together</strong>, which is why research now moves faster than at any earlier point.\n\nThe comparison still fails. Writing, agriculture, the printing press, antibiotics and electricity each altered what a human being could be. Farming allowed settlement and therefore cities; writing allowed one generation to speak to the next; antibiotics turned a scratch from a probable death into an inconvenience. Without electricity there is no Internet at all, and an invention that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depends on an earlier one</strong> can hardly outrank it.\n\nThere is a further difference in kind. Older inventions expanded what was available to humanity; the Internet mostly changes the speed and cost of moving what already exists. That is an enormous gain, but the world managed without it thirty years ago, whereas it could not manage a month without electricity or clean water. Its harms, from surveillance to the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">collapse of shared and reliable information</strong>, also complicate any simple verdict.\n\nIn conclusion, the Internet is arguably the most significant invention of recent decades and has changed daily life profoundly. Measured against inventions that made settlement, memory and survival itself possible, it belongs high on the list rather than at the top of it.","phrases":[{"en":"removed distance as a barrier to working together","context":"The case for the Internet is strong on reach. It has given ordinary people access to almost all recorded knowledge, allowed a farmer to check a market price and a student in a village to attend a lecture, and it carries the science, banking and logistics that modern economies run on. It also removed distance as a barrier to working together, which is why research now moves faster than at any earlier point."},{"en":"depends on an earlier one","context":"The comparison still fails. Writing, agriculture, the printing press, antibiotics and electricity each altered what a human being could be. Farming allowed settlement and therefore cities; writing allowed one generation to speak to the next; antibiotics turned a scratch from a probable death into an inconvenience. Without electricity there is no Internet at all, and an invention that depends on an earlier one can hardly outrank it."},{"en":"collapse of shared and reliable information","context":"There is a further difference in kind. Older inventions expanded what was available to humanity; the Internet mostly changes the speed and cost of moving what already exists. That is an enormous gain, but the world managed without it thirty years ago, whereas it could not manage a month without electricity or clean water. Its harms, from surveillance to the collapse of shared and reliable information, also complicate any simple verdict."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#324","summary":"Whether the Internet is the most important invention in human history"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2322","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"How technology changed children's free time","prompt":"New technologies have changed the way children spend their free time. Do the advantages of this trend outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"A generation ago children filled their afternoons with bicycles and board games; now most of that time belongs to screens. The change brings real gains, but I believe the disadvantages currently outweigh them.\n\nThe advantages should not be dismissed. A child with a tablet has access to explanations, languages and skills that no local library could supply, and many learn coding, drawing or editing entirely on their own. Games develop planning and quick decision-making, and online play keeps friendships alive across distance, which matters greatly to children who are isolated, disabled or living far from anyone their own age. Technology has also given quiet children a way of finding others who share their interests.\n\nThe costs, though, are physical, social and psychological at once. Time on a screen is time not spent moving, and childhood obesity and poor posture have risen alongside it. Sleep suffers, because devices are used late and delay it. Social skills develop through unsupervised play, where children argue, negotiate and repair things themselves, and a generation with less of that experience is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">less practised at handling ordinary friction</strong>. Social media adds a further layer, since children now compare themselves with an endless supply of edited lives, and rates of anxiety among teenagers have climbed accordingly.\n\nThe deepest problem is design. These products are built by companies whose income depends on attention, so they are engineered to be difficult to stop using. Adults struggle against them, and expecting a ten-year-old to succeed is an unreasonable demand disguised as a lesson in self-control.\n\nIn conclusion, technology has widened what children can learn and how they stay in touch, and no sensible parent would remove it entirely. The harm to sleep, activity and confidence is presently larger, so the answer lies in limits set by adults rather than by the child.","phrases":[{"en":"less practised at handling ordinary friction","context":"The costs, though, are physical, social and psychological at once. Time on a screen is time not spent moving, and childhood obesity and poor posture have risen alongside it. Sleep suffers, because devices are used late and delay it. Social skills develop through unsupervised play, where children argue, negotiate and repair things themselves, and a generation with less of that experience is less practised at handling ordinary friction. Social media adds a further layer, since children now compare themselves with an endless supply of edited lives, and rates of anxiety among teenagers have climbed accordingly."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#325","summary":"Whether the effect of new technology on children's leisure is mainly good"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2327","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Should research concentrate on health?","prompt":"Some people think scientific research should focus on solving world health problems. Others think there are more important issues. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Research budgets are finite, so deciding what scientists work on is a real choice rather than an abstract one. Health has an obvious claim on that money, but I believe climate and energy now have a stronger one.\n\nThe argument for health is immediate and human. Millions still die each year from malaria, tuberculosis and diarrhoeal disease, all of which are treatable, and the recent pandemic showed how quickly an unsolved medical problem can halt the world. Health research also pays for itself, since a healthy population works, learns and raises children, and a country cannot develop while its workers are sick. Ageing populations add further urgency, because dementia and cancer will otherwise consume an ever larger share of national spending.\n\nThe case for other priorities rests on sequence. Climate change is the clearest example: rising temperatures spread disease, ruin harvests and displace populations, so failing to address it creates health crises faster than medicine can cure them. Water, food security and clean energy work the same way, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the cause sits upstream of the symptom</strong>. There is also a practical point that many of the greatest medical advances, from antibiotics to imaging, came from research that was not aimed at any disease, which suggests that directing all science at health would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">close off the discoveries that make health possible</strong>.\n\nMy own view is that the division is artificial and that the sensible order runs from causes to consequences. Money should go first to problems that will generate many others if ignored, which today means energy and climate, while health research continues at a level that keeps existing progress moving.\n\nIn conclusion, world health deserves substantial and permanent funding, and no country should reduce it. Research aimed at <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the conditions that produce ill health</strong> protects more lives than treatment alone, so it deserves the first claim on new money.","phrases":[{"en":"the cause sits upstream of the symptom","context":"The case for other priorities rests on sequence. Climate change is the clearest example: rising temperatures spread disease, ruin harvests and displace populations, so failing to address it creates health crises faster than medicine can cure them. Water, food security and clean energy work the same way, since the cause sits upstream of the symptom. There is also a practical point that many of the greatest medical advances, from antibiotics to imaging, came from research that was not aimed at any disease, which suggests that directing all science at health would close off the discoveries that make health possible."},{"en":"close off the discoveries that make health possible","context":"The case for other priorities rests on sequence. Climate change is the clearest example: rising temperatures spread disease, ruin harvests and displace populations, so failing to address it creates health crises faster than medicine can cure them. Water, food security and clean energy work the same way, since the cause sits upstream of the symptom. There is also a practical point that many of the greatest medical advances, from antibiotics to imaging, came from research that was not aimed at any disease, which suggests that directing all science at health would close off the discoveries that make health possible."},{"en":"the conditions that produce ill health","context":"In conclusion, world health deserves substantial and permanent funding, and no country should reduce it. Research aimed at the conditions that produce ill health protects more lives than treatment alone, so it deserves the first claim on new money."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#326","summary":"Whether scientific research should focus on world health problems above other issues"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2330","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Putting personal details online","prompt":"Many people put their personal and private information, such as their address, phone number and card details, online. Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"Buying anything, booking anything or applying for anything now requires handing over details that were once kept private. The convenience is genuine, but I regard the development as negative because of how little control people retain afterwards.\n\nThe benefits explain why it happened. Stored details make a purchase take seconds rather than minutes, and an address held by a delivery company allows goods to arrive the next day. Verified identity also makes services safer in some respects, since banks can detect unusual activity and platforms can trace fraudulent sellers. For people who cannot easily travel, being able to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">arrange an entire life from a screen</strong> is not a small gain.\n\nThe dangers are structural rather than occasional. Once information is given, its owner cannot see where it goes: it is copied, sold to advertisers and stored on systems the customer never chose. Major breaches occur every year, and a leaked address, date of birth and card number are enough for identity theft, which victims spend months undoing. The damage is also permanent in a way physical theft is not, because a stolen password can be changed but a date of birth cannot.\n\nThere is a quieter cost as well. Details collected for one purpose are combined to build profiles that predict income, health and political leaning, and those profiles decide what prices people see and what jobs they are shown. Most users agreed to none of this in any meaningful sense, since consent means <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accepting terms nobody has time to read</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, sharing personal information online buys real convenience and some safety. It is on balance negative, because the risks fall on the individual while the benefits and the data <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accumulate with the companies collecting them</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"arrange an entire life from a screen","context":"The benefits explain why it happened. Stored details make a purchase take seconds rather than minutes, and an address held by a delivery company allows goods to arrive the next day. Verified identity also makes services safer in some respects, since banks can detect unusual activity and platforms can trace fraudulent sellers. For people who cannot easily travel, being able to arrange an entire life from a screen is not a small gain."},{"en":"accepting terms nobody has time to read","context":"There is a quieter cost as well. Details collected for one purpose are combined to build profiles that predict income, health and political leaning, and those profiles decide what prices people see and what jobs they are shown. Most users agreed to none of this in any meaningful sense, since consent means accepting terms nobody has time to read."},{"en":"accumulate with the companies collecting them","context":"In conclusion, sharing personal information online buys real convenience and some safety. It is on balance negative, because the risks fall on the individual while the benefits and the data accumulate with the companies collecting them."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#327","summary":"Whether sharing addresses, phone numbers and card details online is a good development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2356","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Getting older people online","prompt":"Mobile phones and the Internet offer many benefits for elderly people, yet they remain the group that uses these technologies the least. In what ways can these tools help older adults? What can be done to encourage them to use modern technology?","answer":"Older people have most to gain from technology that removes distance and effort, yet they are the least likely to use it. The benefits reach independence and health rather than convenience alone, and closing the gap requires teaching rather than persuasion.\n\nThe first benefit is contact. Video calls allow a grandparent to see grandchildren who live in another country, and messaging keeps friendships alive when walking to a friend&#x27;s house is no longer easy, which matters because loneliness in old age raises the risk of illness as sharply as smoking does. The second is health: appointments, prescriptions and consultations can be handled from home, and monitors can alert a family if something is wrong, so an older person <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stays in their own house for years longer</strong>. Practical independence follows, since banking, shopping and paying bills online spare a difficult journey, and access to news, courses and games gives structure to days that can otherwise be empty.\n\nThe reasons for low use explain what would help. Many older adults are not uninterested but afraid of making an irreversible mistake, and fear of fraud is entirely rational given how often they are targeted. Devices are designed for younger hands and eyes, with small text and constant updates that move the buttons they had just learned to find.\n\nEncouragement therefore has to be practical. Free classes in libraries and community centres, taught slowly and repeated, work far better than a leaflet, and older volunteers teaching their peers removes the embarrassment of asking. Manufacturers can offer simplified settings by default, and banks and health services should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">keep a telephone alternative open</strong> so that nobody is forced online before they are ready.\n\nIn conclusion, technology can keep older people connected, healthy and independent. Patient teaching, simpler devices and honest advice about fraud would do more than any campaign urging them to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">catch up with everybody else</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"stays in their own house for years longer","context":"The first benefit is contact. Video calls allow a grandparent to see grandchildren who live in another country, and messaging keeps friendships alive when walking to a friend's house is no longer easy, which matters because loneliness in old age raises the risk of illness as sharply as smoking does. The second is health: appointments, prescriptions and consultations can be handled from home, and monitors can alert a family if something is wrong, so an older person stays in their own house for years longer. Practical independence follows, since banking, shopping and paying bills online spare a difficult journey, and access to news, courses and games gives structure to days that can otherwise be empty."},{"en":"keep a telephone alternative open","context":"Encouragement therefore has to be practical. Free classes in libraries and community centres, taught slowly and repeated, work far better than a leaflet, and older volunteers teaching their peers removes the embarrassment of asking. Manufacturers can offer simplified settings by default, and banks and health services should keep a telephone alternative open so that nobody is forced online before they are ready."},{"en":"catch up with everybody else","context":"In conclusion, technology can keep older people connected, healthy and independent. Patient teaching, simpler devices and honest advice about fraud would do more than any campaign urging them to catch up with everybody else."}],"subtype":"Two-Part Question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#328","summary":"How technology helps the elderly and how they can be encouraged to use it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2367","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Should scientists work across borders?","prompt":"Some people say that scientists around the world should work together on scientific research, but others worry that this may cause problems. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Research teams now routinely span several countries, and the results appear faster than any national effort could produce them. The worries about this are not imaginary, but I believe the benefits are far greater and the risks can be managed by restricting a narrow category of work.\n\nThe case for collaboration is practical. Modern science is expensive: particle accelerators, telescopes and genome projects cost more than most countries could justify alone, and sharing the bill makes them possible at all. Combining specialists also accelerates results, which the development of vaccines within a year demonstrated clearly, and problems such as climate, antibiotic resistance and pandemics do not stop at any border that a research budget respects. Poorer countries gain access to equipment and training they could not otherwise obtain, and results checked by teams in several places are more reliable, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">independent groups catch each other&#x27;s mistakes</strong>.\n\nThe concerns deserve serious answers. Some research has obvious military application, and openly shared work on viruses, weapons or artificial intelligence could be misused. Countries also compete economically, and a government that funded a discovery may reasonably object to a foreign firm profiting from it. Collaboration can be unequal too, with scientists in developing nations collecting data while institutions elsewhere take the credit, and there is a genuine risk that safety rules are avoided by moving the work somewhere permissive.\n\nThe answer is to sort research by risk rather than to close borders. The overwhelming majority — medicine, climate science, materials, agriculture — should be shared as widely as possible, while a small class of dual-use work is governed by common international standards and proper security.\n\nIn conclusion, scientific cooperation produces faster, cheaper and more trustworthy results, and the largest problems cannot be solved without it. The dangers are real but confined, so regulating the exceptions is wiser than restricting the whole.","phrases":[{"en":"independent groups catch each other's mistakes","context":"The case for collaboration is practical. Modern science is expensive: particle accelerators, telescopes and genome projects cost more than most countries could justify alone, and sharing the bill makes them possible at all. Combining specialists also accelerates results, which the development of vaccines within a year demonstrated clearly, and problems such as climate, antibiotic resistance and pandemics do not stop at any border that a research budget respects. Poorer countries gain access to equipment and training they could not otherwise obtain, and results checked by teams in several places are more reliable, since independent groups catch each other's mistakes."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#329","summary":"Whether international scientific collaboration is worth its risks"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2376","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Technology & Science","title":"Is much research a waste of money?","prompt":"Some people say that a lot of the scientific research conducted today is a waste of time and money. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Newspapers regularly report studies that appear trivial, and the sums involved are public money. I disagree with the complaint in general, though it identifies genuine problems in how research is currently organised.\n\nThe main reason for disagreeing is that useful research cannot be identified in advance. Work on bacterial defence mechanisms, undertaken out of curiosity, produced the gene-editing tools now used against inherited disease, and the mathematics behind encryption was developed by people who expected no application at all. Judging a project by whether its title sounds practical would have prevented most of the discoveries we now depend on. Failure is also part of the process, since an experiment that rules out an explanation saves everyone else from repeating it, even though nothing is announced.\n\nThe criticism nonetheless has substance. A great deal of research is never read, because academic careers depend on the number of papers published rather than on their value, which encourages small, safe studies split into as many articles as possible. Results that fail to confirm a hypothesis often go unpublished, so the same ground is covered repeatedly by teams unaware of each other. There is also a serious problem with reliability, since many findings in some fields cannot be reproduced, and money spent on work nobody can confirm is genuinely wasted.\n\nThese are arguments for reform rather than for cutting research. Funding longer projects, rewarding replication, requiring that all results be published and judging scientists on quality rather than quantity would remove most of the waste without touching the discovery.\n\nIn conclusion, the value of research cannot be predicted, and work that looks pointless has repeatedly turned out to matter enormously. The waste lies in the incentives placed on researchers, so the answer is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">change how science is funded and judged</strong> rather than to spend less on it.","phrases":[{"en":"change how science is funded and judged","context":"In conclusion, the value of research cannot be predicted, and work that looks pointless has repeatedly turned out to matter enormously. The waste lies in the incentives placed on researchers, so the answer is to change how science is funded and judged rather than to spend less on it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#330","summary":"Whether a large share of current scientific research is time and money wasted"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Why people travel more, and what they gain","prompt":"Today people are travelling more than ever before. Why is this the case? What are the benefits of travelling for the traveller?","answer":"People travel more than in the past because journeys have become cheaper, faster and easier to arrange. Higher incomes and flexible working patterns have also made travel possible for a wider group of people. Overall, this trend brings substantial personal and economic benefits.\n\nLow-cost airlines, online booking and improved transport networks have reduced both the price and difficulty of travel. Social media also exposes people to destinations that they might never have considered. In addition, many people now study, work or have relatives abroad, so international travel has become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">part of ordinary life</strong> rather than a rare luxury.\n\nTravel can broaden people&#x27;s understanding by placing them in unfamiliar environments. Visitors learn how others live, communicate and solve everyday problems. Tourism also creates employment in transport, accommodation and local services. When managed well, it can provide income for rural areas and help fund the protection of historic sites and natural attractions.\n\nHowever, these benefits depend on responsible behaviour. Overcrowding, pollution and rising housing costs can harm local communities. Governments should therefore control development, improve public transport and ensure that tourism revenue reaches residents. With sensible planning, travel can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">support cultural exchange and local prosperity</strong> without overwhelming destinations.\n\nTravel also builds a kind of practical confidence that a familiar routine at home rarely offers. Even a short trip forces travellers to solve unfamiliar problems alone, such as reading a foreign menu or rearranging a delayed connection, and most people manage far better than they expected. At the same time, employers and universities increasingly value international experience, treating it as evidence of independence. A well-planned trip can therefore <strong class=\"key-phrase\">combine personal enjoyment with practical learning</strong>, rather than serving only as a break from routine.","topic":"Tourism & Travel","phrases":[{"en":"part of ordinary life","context":"Low-cost airlines, online booking and improved transport networks have reduced both the price and difficulty of travel. Social media also exposes people to destinations that they might never have considered. In addition, many people now study, work or have relatives abroad, so international travel has become part of ordinary life rather than a rare luxury."},{"en":"support cultural exchange and local prosperity","context":"However, these benefits depend on responsible behaviour. Overcrowding, pollution and rising housing costs can harm local communities. Governments should therefore control development, improve public transport and ensure that tourism revenue reaches residents. With sensible planning, travel can support cultural exchange and local prosperity without overwhelming destinations."},{"en":"combine personal enjoyment with practical learning","context":"Travel also builds a kind of practical confidence that a familiar routine at home rarely offers. Even a short trip forces travellers to solve unfamiliar problems alone, such as reading a foreign menu or rearranging a delayed connection, and most people manage far better than they expected. At the same time, employers and universities increasingly value international experience, treating it as evidence of independence. A well-planned trip can therefore combine personal enjoyment with practical learning, rather than serving only as a break from routine."}],"subtype":"two-part","summary":"Why people travel more, and what travellers gain from it","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-087","_displayNo":"#331"},{"id":"writing-task-2-178","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"Should tourists pay more than local visitors?","prompt":"Foreign visitors should pay more than local visitors for cultural and historical attractions. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?","answer":"It is sometimes argued that tourists from overseas should be charged more than local residents to visit important sites and monuments. I completely disagree with this idea.\n\nThe argument in favour of higher prices for foreign tourists would be that cultural or historical attractions often depend on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">state subsidies</strong> to keep them going, which means that the resident population already pays money to these sites through the tax system. However, I believe this to be a very shortsighted view. Foreign tourists contribute to the economy of the host country with the money they spend on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a wide range of goods and services</strong>, including food, souvenirs, accommodation and travel. The governments and inhabitants of every country should be happy to subsidise important tourist sites and encourage people from the rest of the world to visit them.\n\nIf travellers realised that they would have to pay more to visit historical and cultural attractions in a particular nation, they would perhaps decide not to go to that country on holiday. To take the UK as an example, the tourism industry and many related jobs rely on visitors coming to the country to see places like Windsor Castle or Saint Paul&#x27;s Cathedral. These two sites charge the same price <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regardless of nationality</strong>, and this helps to promote the nation&#x27;s cultural heritage. If overseas tourists stopped coming due to higher prices, there would be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a risk of insufficient funding for the maintenance</strong> of these important buildings.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that every effort should be made to attract tourists from overseas, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">it would be counter productive</strong> to make them pay more than local residents.","phrases":[{"en":"state subsidies","context":"The argument in favour of higher prices for foreign tourists would be that cultural or historical attractions often depend on state subsidies to keep them going, which means that the resident population already pays money to these sites through the tax system. However, I believe this to be a very shortsighted view. Foreign tourists contribute to the economy of the host country with the money they spend on a wide range of goods and services, including food, souvenirs, accommodation and travel. The governments and inhabitants of every country should be happy to subsidise important tourist sites and encourage people from the rest of the world to visit them."},{"en":"a wide range of goods and services","context":"The argument in favour of higher prices for foreign tourists would be that cultural or historical attractions often depend on state subsidies to keep them going, which means that the resident population already pays money to these sites through the tax system. However, I believe this to be a very shortsighted view. Foreign tourists contribute to the economy of the host country with the money they spend on a wide range of goods and services, including food, souvenirs, accommodation and travel. The governments and inhabitants of every country should be happy to subsidise important tourist sites and encourage people from the rest of the world to visit them."},{"en":"regardless of nationality","context":"If travellers realised that they would have to pay more to visit historical and cultural attractions in a particular nation, they would perhaps decide not to go to that country on holiday. To take the UK as an example, the tourism industry and many related jobs rely on visitors coming to the country to see places like Windsor Castle or Saint Paul's Cathedral. These two sites charge the same price regardless of nationality, and this helps to promote the nation's cultural heritage. If overseas tourists stopped coming due to higher prices, there would be a risk of insufficient funding for the maintenance of these important buildings."},{"en":"a risk of insufficient funding for the maintenance","context":"If travellers realised that they would have to pay more to visit historical and cultural attractions in a particular nation, they would perhaps decide not to go to that country on holiday. To take the UK as an example, the tourism industry and many related jobs rely on visitors coming to the country to see places like Windsor Castle or Saint Paul's Cathedral. These two sites charge the same price regardless of nationality, and this helps to promote the nation's cultural heritage. If overseas tourists stopped coming due to higher prices, there would be a risk of insufficient funding for the maintenance of these important buildings."},{"en":"it would be counter productive","context":"In conclusion, I believe that every effort should be made to attract tourists from overseas, and it would be counter productive to make them pay more than local residents."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#332","summary":"Higher entrance fees for foreign tourists"},{"id":"writing-task-2-179","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Tourism & Travel","title":"Is tourism always a force for good?","prompt":"'Tourism is always a force for good which enables people of different countries to understand each other.' To what extent do you agree with this idea?","answer":"The role of tourism in the world today is widely debated, with some people claiming that it is indeed <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a force for international understanding</strong>. However, I personally do not entirely accept this, and I will explain why in this essay.\n\nFirstly, we must consider the damage that visitors do to the places they travel to see. When large numbers of western tourists reach relatively untouched regions, the roads, hotels and flights built to accommodate them bring waste, traffic and pressure on scarce water. We see this in the litter left on the trails around Machu Picchu in Peru, and in the erosion and disturbance to wildlife caused by safari vehicles in East Africa.\n\nThere are also numerous examples of <strong class=\"key-phrase\">tension between tourists and local cultures</strong>, ranging from drunken British tourists in Europe to American ‘spring breakers’ in Canadian or Latin American resorts. In these cases, tourism actually leads to resentment and distrust between nationalities, not better understanding.\n\nA further reason to be wary of this idea is the economic aspect. It is sometimes said that tourism improves international relations because it encourages the flow of money from wealthy to less developed countries. In reality, however, the wealth generated usually stays in the hands of private companies or local officials, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rarely drips down into the population</strong>. Many of us have visited less developed nations as tourists, and have witnessed at first hand the poverty that exists <strong class=\"key-phrase\">outside the confines of the resort hotels</strong>. We leave with the impression that our stay has contributed nothing to the lives of those we have observed.\n\nThis is not to say that tourism is entirely bad, of course. There are benefits in terms of improving local infrastructure such as roads and airports. But to say that it is &#x27;always a force for good&#x27; is to ignore the various problems it causes.","phrases":[{"en":"a force for international understanding","context":"The role of tourism in the world today is widely debated, with some people claiming that it is indeed a force for international understanding. However, I personally do not entirely accept this, and I will explain why in this essay."},{"en":"tension between tourists and local cultures","context":"There are also numerous examples of tension between tourists and local cultures, ranging from drunken British tourists in Europe to American ‘spring breakers’ in Canadian or Latin American resorts. In these cases, tourism actually leads to resentment and distrust between nationalities, not better understanding."},{"en":"rarely drips down into the population","context":"A further reason to be wary of this idea is the economic aspect. It is sometimes said that tourism improves international relations because it encourages the flow of money from wealthy to less developed countries. In reality, however, the wealth generated usually stays in the hands of private companies or local officials, and rarely drips down into the population. Many of us have visited less developed nations as tourists, and have witnessed at first hand the poverty that exists outside the confines of the resort hotels. We leave with the impression that our stay has contributed nothing to the lives of those we have observed."},{"en":"outside the confines of the resort hotels","context":"A further reason to be wary of this idea is the economic aspect. It is sometimes said that tourism improves international relations because it encourages the flow of money from wealthy to less developed countries. In reality, however, the wealth generated usually stays in the hands of private companies or local officials, and rarely drips down into the population. Many of us have visited less developed nations as tourists, and have witnessed at first hand the poverty that exists outside the confines of the resort hotels. We leave with the impression that our stay has contributed nothing to the lives of those we have observed."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#333","summary":"‘Tourism is always a force for good which enables people of different…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-180","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Punishing drivers, or other routes to road safety","prompt":"Some people think that strict punishments for driving offences are the key to reducing traffic accidents. Others, however, believe that other measures would be more effective in improving road safety. Discuss both these views and give your own opinion.","answer":"People have differing views with regard to the question of how to make our roads safer. In my view, both punishments and a range of other measures can be used together to promote better driving habits. On the one hand, strict punishments can certainly help to encourage people to drive more safely. Penalties for dangerous drivers can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">act as a deterrent</strong>, meaning that people avoid <strong class=\"key-phrase\">repeating the same offence</strong>. There are various types of driving penalty, such as small fines, licence suspension, driver awareness courses, and even prison sentences. The aim of these punishments is to show dangerous drivers that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">their actions have negative consequences</strong>. As a result, we would hope that drivers become <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more disciplined and alert</strong>, and that they follow the rules more carefully.\n\nSeveral other approaches, however, do not rely on punishment at all. Education is probably the most important: a driver who has never been taught to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">anticipate danger</strong> will not necessarily drive more carefully simply because a fine is possible, so schools and extended driving tests could teach hazard awareness <strong class=\"key-phrase\">before bad habits form</strong>, rather than punishing them once the damage is done. Road design can reinforce the same goal. Speed bumps, road bends and warning signs physically slow traffic down in places where drivers might otherwise take risks, and speed cameras extend that effect to stretches of road where no officer is watching.\n\nA different kind of solution is to reduce the number of car journeys that take place at all. When governments and local councils invest in reliable, affordable public transport, they give people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a genuine alternative to driving</strong>, and a road with fewer cars on it is safer for everyone, regardless of how carefully any individual driver behaves.\n\nIn conclusion, while punishment plays a useful role in deterring dangerous drivers, I believe it works best alongside education, safer road design and investment in transport that reduces the need to drive in the first place.","phrases":[{"en":"act as a deterrent","context":"People have differing views with regard to the question of how to make our roads safer. In my view, both punishments and a range of other measures can be used together to promote better driving habits. On the one hand, strict punishments can certainly help to encourage people to drive more safely. Penalties for dangerous drivers can act as a deterrent, meaning that people avoid repeating the same offence. There are various types of driving penalty, such as small fines, licence suspension, driver awareness courses, and even prison sentences. The aim of these punishments is to show dangerous drivers that their actions have negative consequences. As a result, we would hope that drivers become more disciplined and alert, and that they follow the rules more carefully."},{"en":"repeating the same offence","context":"People have differing views with regard to the question of how to make our roads safer. In my view, both punishments and a range of other measures can be used together to promote better driving habits. On the one hand, strict punishments can certainly help to encourage people to drive more safely. Penalties for dangerous drivers can act as a deterrent, meaning that people avoid repeating the same offence. There are various types of driving penalty, such as small fines, licence suspension, driver awareness courses, and even prison sentences. The aim of these punishments is to show dangerous drivers that their actions have negative consequences. As a result, we would hope that drivers become more disciplined and alert, and that they follow the rules more carefully."},{"en":"their actions have negative consequences","context":"People have differing views with regard to the question of how to make our roads safer. In my view, both punishments and a range of other measures can be used together to promote better driving habits. On the one hand, strict punishments can certainly help to encourage people to drive more safely. Penalties for dangerous drivers can act as a deterrent, meaning that people avoid repeating the same offence. There are various types of driving penalty, such as small fines, licence suspension, driver awareness courses, and even prison sentences. The aim of these punishments is to show dangerous drivers that their actions have negative consequences. As a result, we would hope that drivers become more disciplined and alert, and that they follow the rules more carefully."},{"en":"more disciplined and alert","context":"People have differing views with regard to the question of how to make our roads safer. In my view, both punishments and a range of other measures can be used together to promote better driving habits. On the one hand, strict punishments can certainly help to encourage people to drive more safely. Penalties for dangerous drivers can act as a deterrent, meaning that people avoid repeating the same offence. There are various types of driving penalty, such as small fines, licence suspension, driver awareness courses, and even prison sentences. The aim of these punishments is to show dangerous drivers that their actions have negative consequences. As a result, we would hope that drivers become more disciplined and alert, and that they follow the rules more carefully."},{"en":"anticipate danger","context":"Several other approaches, however, do not rely on punishment at all. Education is probably the most important: a driver who has never been taught to anticipate danger will not necessarily drive more carefully simply because a fine is possible, so schools and extended driving tests could teach hazard awareness before bad habits form, rather than punishing them once the damage is done. Road design can reinforce the same goal. Speed bumps, road bends and warning signs physically slow traffic down in places where drivers might otherwise take risks, and speed cameras extend that effect to stretches of road where no officer is watching."},{"en":"before bad habits form","context":"Several other approaches, however, do not rely on punishment at all. Education is probably the most important: a driver who has never been taught to anticipate danger will not necessarily drive more carefully simply because a fine is possible, so schools and extended driving tests could teach hazard awareness before bad habits form, rather than punishing them once the damage is done. Road design can reinforce the same goal. Speed bumps, road bends and warning signs physically slow traffic down in places where drivers might otherwise take risks, and speed cameras extend that effect to stretches of road where no officer is watching."},{"en":"a genuine alternative to driving","context":"A different kind of solution is to reduce the number of car journeys that take place at all. When governments and local councils invest in reliable, affordable public transport, they give people a genuine alternative to driving, and a road with fewer cars on it is safer for everyone, regardless of how carefully any individual driver behaves."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#334","summary":"Road safety: punishment or prevention"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2227","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Raising the legal driving age","prompt":"Some people think that the best way to improve road safety is to increase the minimum legal age for driving a car or motorbike. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Young drivers are involved in a disproportionate share of serious accidents, and one proposed remedy is simply to make them wait longer for a licence. I agree that raising the age would save lives, but I do not accept that it is the best measure available.\n\nThere is a sound reason why age matters. The part of the brain that weighs risk against reward continues developing into the mid-twenties, which is why a seventeen-year-old is more likely to overtake on a blind bend or drive at speed with friends in the car. Raising the minimum age by two years would remove the least experienced and most impulsive drivers from the road at the point when they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">most likely to misjudge a situation</strong>, and the accident figures would fall accordingly.\n\nThe objection is that age is a crude substitute for what actually causes crashes, which is inexperience. A twenty-year-old in their first month of driving is little safer than an eighteen-year-old in their first month, so delaying the licence <strong class=\"key-phrase\">postpones the risk rather than removing it</strong>. There is also a cost that falls unevenly: in rural areas without buses, losing two years of independent travel can mean losing access to work and education.\n\nMeasures aimed directly at inexperience appear to work better. Graduated licensing, which restricts night driving and the number of young passengers during the first year, has cut fatalities substantially where it has been introduced, and it does so without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shutting anyone out of employment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I partly agree, since a higher age would reduce deaths. It is nonetheless a blunt instrument, and a graduated licence combined with stricter testing addresses the same problem while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imposing a far smaller cost</strong> on young people who need to travel.","phrases":[{"en":"most likely to misjudge a situation","context":"There is a sound reason why age matters. The part of the brain that weighs risk against reward continues developing into the mid-twenties, which is why a seventeen-year-old is more likely to overtake on a blind bend or drive at speed with friends in the car. Raising the minimum age by two years would remove the least experienced and most impulsive drivers from the road at the point when they are most likely to misjudge a situation, and the accident figures would fall accordingly."},{"en":"postpones the risk rather than removing it","context":"The objection is that age is a crude substitute for what actually causes crashes, which is inexperience. A twenty-year-old in their first month of driving is little safer than an eighteen-year-old in their first month, so delaying the licence postpones the risk rather than removing it. There is also a cost that falls unevenly: in rural areas without buses, losing two years of independent travel can mean losing access to work and education."},{"en":"shutting anyone out of employment","context":"Measures aimed directly at inexperience appear to work better. Graduated licensing, which restricts night driving and the number of young passengers during the first year, has cut fatalities substantially where it has been introduced, and it does so without shutting anyone out of employment."},{"en":"imposing a far smaller cost","context":"In conclusion, I partly agree, since a higher age would reduce deaths. It is nonetheless a blunt instrument, and a graduated licence combined with stricter testing addresses the same problem while imposing a far smaller cost on young people who need to travel."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#335","summary":"Increasing the minimum driving age to improve road safety"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2256","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Cars or bicycles in cities?","prompt":"In some cities, people are choosing cars instead of bicycles, while in other cities riding bikes is replacing cars. Why is this the case? Which development do you think is better?","answer":"Cities are moving in opposite directions on transport: some are filling with cars while others are steadily returning to the bicycle. The difference is explained mainly by income and by planning decisions, and I believe the shift towards cycling is clearly the better development.\n\nThe move towards cars usually happens in rapidly developing economies. As incomes rise, a car becomes affordable and is treated as evidence of success, while cycling is associated with not being able to afford anything else. Distance reinforces this, since cities that grow outwards create journeys too long to cycle, and where roads have no protected lanes, riding among heavy traffic is genuinely <strong class=\"key-phrase\">too dangerous to be attractive</strong>.\n\nThe return to bicycles is largely a matter of deliberate policy. Cities such as Amsterdam and Copenhagen did not simply discover a cultural preference; they built separated lanes, secure parking and traffic rules that favour cyclists, and usage followed. Congestion helps, because in a dense city a bicycle is often faster door to door than a car, and awareness of air quality has <strong class=\"key-phrase\">changed how residents vote</strong>.\n\nCycling is the better direction for reasons that go beyond emissions. A city that moves people by bicycle needs far less space for each traveller, which frees streets for housing, trees and shops rather than parking. The health effect is also substantial, since a commute that includes exercise reduces heart disease measurably, and quieter streets are safer for children. Cars deliver comfort to the driver while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">imposing noise and danger on everyone else</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, the two trends reflect rising incomes on one side and investment in infrastructure on the other. Cycling is preferable, because it produces a city that is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">healthier and more pleasant to live in</strong> rather than merely easier to drive through.","phrases":[{"en":"too dangerous to be attractive","context":"The move towards cars usually happens in rapidly developing economies. As incomes rise, a car becomes affordable and is treated as evidence of success, while cycling is associated with not being able to afford anything else. Distance reinforces this, since cities that grow outwards create journeys too long to cycle, and where roads have no protected lanes, riding among heavy traffic is genuinely too dangerous to be attractive."},{"en":"changed how residents vote","context":"The return to bicycles is largely a matter of deliberate policy. Cities such as Amsterdam and Copenhagen did not simply discover a cultural preference; they built separated lanes, secure parking and traffic rules that favour cyclists, and usage followed. Congestion helps, because in a dense city a bicycle is often faster door to door than a car, and awareness of air quality has changed how residents vote."},{"en":"imposing noise and danger on everyone else","context":"Cycling is the better direction for reasons that go beyond emissions. A city that moves people by bicycle needs far less space for each traveller, which frees streets for housing, trees and shops rather than parking. The health effect is also substantial, since a commute that includes exercise reduces heart disease measurably, and quieter streets are safer for children. Cars deliver comfort to the driver while imposing noise and danger on everyone else."},{"en":"healthier and more pleasant to live in","context":"In conclusion, the two trends reflect rising incomes on one side and investment in infrastructure on the other. Cycling is preferable, because it produces a city that is healthier and more pleasant to live in rather than merely easier to drive through."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#336","summary":"Why some cities are shifting to cars and others to bicycles"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2263","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Rails or roads?","prompt":"Some people believe the government should spend money on building train and subway lines to reduce traffic congestion. Others think that building more and wider roads is the better solution. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Governments facing congestion must choose between investing in rail and expanding the road network. Both approaches have been tried extensively, and the evidence points strongly towards rail in large cities.\n\nThose who favour roads argue from flexibility and cost. A road serves cars, buses, lorries and emergency vehicles alike, and it can be built in stages wherever demand appears, whereas a metro line takes a decade and enormous capital before carrying a single passenger. Roads also reach places rail cannot justify, and for freight and for residents of low-density areas there is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">no practical alternative to driving</strong>.\n\nThe argument for rail rests on capacity. A single metro line can move as many people per hour as a dozen lanes of traffic, on a fraction of the land, which matters most in exactly the dense areas where congestion is worst. There is also a well-documented problem with the road solution: added capacity fills up. When a motorway is widened, journeys that were previously avoided become attractive, traffic grows to fill the new lanes, and within a few years the delay <strong class=\"key-phrase\">returns to where it started</strong>. Rail does not generate congestion in the same way.\n\nMy own view is that this asymmetry decides the question for cities. Widening roads treats the symptom and reliably recreates the problem, while a rail line removes cars permanently by offering a faster option, and it does so without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">demolishing the streets it is meant to serve</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, roads remain necessary for freight and for areas too thinly populated for rail. In congested cities, however, investment should go to trains and metros, because road building has repeatedly been shown to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">create the traffic it was intended to relieve</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"no practical alternative to driving","context":"Those who favour roads argue from flexibility and cost. A road serves cars, buses, lorries and emergency vehicles alike, and it can be built in stages wherever demand appears, whereas a metro line takes a decade and enormous capital before carrying a single passenger. Roads also reach places rail cannot justify, and for freight and for residents of low-density areas there is no practical alternative to driving."},{"en":"returns to where it started","context":"The argument for rail rests on capacity. A single metro line can move as many people per hour as a dozen lanes of traffic, on a fraction of the land, which matters most in exactly the dense areas where congestion is worst. There is also a well-documented problem with the road solution: added capacity fills up. When a motorway is widened, journeys that were previously avoided become attractive, traffic grows to fill the new lanes, and within a few years the delay returns to where it started. Rail does not generate congestion in the same way."},{"en":"demolishing the streets it is meant to serve","context":"My own view is that this asymmetry decides the question for cities. Widening roads treats the symptom and reliably recreates the problem, while a rail line removes cars permanently by offering a faster option, and it does so without demolishing the streets it is meant to serve."},{"en":"create the traffic it was intended to relieve","context":"In conclusion, roads remain necessary for freight and for areas too thinly populated for rail. In congested cities, however, investment should go to trains and metros, because road building has repeatedly been shown to create the traffic it was intended to relieve."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#337","summary":"Building rail lines compared with building more roads"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2285","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"A driving test every year","prompt":"The best way to make road transport safer is to require drivers to take a driving test every year. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Most drivers pass one examination in their late teens and are never assessed again, which is a strange arrangement for an activity that kills over a million people a year. I agree that periodic checks would help, but annual testing for everybody would be an expensive way to address the wrong problem.\n\nThe reasoning behind re-testing is sound. Skills decay and bad habits accumulate, so a driver of thirty years&#x27; experience may have forgotten rules that have since changed and acquired several dangerous routines. Eyesight and reaction times also decline gradually, and the person concerned is usually the last to notice. A test would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">correct faults before they cause an accident</strong> rather than after.\n\nThe objections are practical and substantial. Testing every licence holder each year would require an enormous administration, and the cost would fall on drivers, which penalises those who need a car to work. It is also poorly targeted, since most accidents are not caused by an inability to drive: speeding, alcohol, tiredness and phone use cause the majority, and all of them are committed by people who would pass any test on the day of the examination. A driver on their best behaviour for forty minutes proves very little about the other days.\n\nMeasures with stronger evidence behind them exist. Enforcement of speed limits by camera, serious penalties for drink-driving and phone use, graduated licences that restrict new drivers, safer road design and vehicle technology such as automatic braking have all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reduced deaths wherever they were introduced</strong>. Periodic checks still have a place, particularly eyesight tests and assessments for older drivers, but every few years rather than annually.\n\nIn conclusion, drivers should be re-checked from time to time, since skills and eyesight change. Annual testing would cost far more than it saved, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enforcement, road design and vehicle safety</strong> remain the better routes to safer roads.","phrases":[{"en":"correct faults before they cause an accident","context":"The reasoning behind re-testing is sound. Skills decay and bad habits accumulate, so a driver of thirty years' experience may have forgotten rules that have since changed and acquired several dangerous routines. Eyesight and reaction times also decline gradually, and the person concerned is usually the last to notice. A test would correct faults before they cause an accident rather than after."},{"en":"reduced deaths wherever they were introduced","context":"Measures with stronger evidence behind them exist. Enforcement of speed limits by camera, serious penalties for drink-driving and phone use, graduated licences that restrict new drivers, safer road design and vehicle technology such as automatic braking have all reduced deaths wherever they were introduced. Periodic checks still have a place, particularly eyesight tests and assessments for older drivers, but every few years rather than annually."},{"en":"enforcement, road design and vehicle safety","context":"In conclusion, drivers should be re-checked from time to time, since skills and eyesight change. Annual testing would cost far more than it saved, so enforcement, road design and vehicle safety remain the better routes to safer roads."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#338","summary":"Whether annual re-testing of drivers is the best way to improve road safety"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2288","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Should people be encouraged into cities?","prompt":"The best way to solve traffic and transport problems is to encourage people to live in cities rather than in suburbs or the countryside. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Suburban living generates far more travel per household than city living does, which makes concentrating the population look like an obvious remedy. I partly agree, since density genuinely reduces the need to travel, but calling it the best solution overstates what it can achieve.\n\nThe transport case for density is strong. A city dweller can walk to a shop, cycle to work or take a train that runs every few minutes, because enough passengers exist to justify the service, whereas a suburb of detached houses cannot support a bus route at all. Journeys are also shorter: the same trip that takes twenty minutes by car in a spread-out area takes five on foot in a dense one. Fewer vehicles means fewer emissions and accidents, and the land saved from roads and car parks can be used for housing.\n\nThe proposal has serious limits, however. People live in suburbs largely because city housing is expensive and cramped, so encouraging a move without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">building affordable family homes in the centre</strong> simply pushes prices higher and moves the problem. Cities also become congested in their own way, and a district that gains residents without gaining transport capacity <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exchanges long journeys for crowded ones</strong>. Not everyone can move in any case, since farming, tourism and much industry are located where they are, and emptying rural areas has consequences of its own.\n\nMore effective measures work on the journeys rather than the addresses. Building homes near stations, allowing shops and offices within residential districts, improving rural bus services and letting people work remotely for part of the week all <strong class=\"key-phrase\">shorten travel without requiring anybody to relocate</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, denser cities do reduce transport demand and deserve encouragement through planning and housing policy. Density alone will not solve the problem, so it belongs among several measures rather than ahead of them.","phrases":[{"en":"building affordable family homes in the centre","context":"The proposal has serious limits, however. People live in suburbs largely because city housing is expensive and cramped, so encouraging a move without building affordable family homes in the centre simply pushes prices higher and moves the problem. Cities also become congested in their own way, and a district that gains residents without gaining transport capacity exchanges long journeys for crowded ones. Not everyone can move in any case, since farming, tourism and much industry are located where they are, and emptying rural areas has consequences of its own."},{"en":"exchanges long journeys for crowded ones","context":"The proposal has serious limits, however. People live in suburbs largely because city housing is expensive and cramped, so encouraging a move without building affordable family homes in the centre simply pushes prices higher and moves the problem. Cities also become congested in their own way, and a district that gains residents without gaining transport capacity exchanges long journeys for crowded ones. Not everyone can move in any case, since farming, tourism and much industry are located where they are, and emptying rural areas has consequences of its own."},{"en":"shorten travel without requiring anybody to relocate","context":"More effective measures work on the journeys rather than the addresses. Building homes near stations, allowing shops and offices within residential districts, improving rural bus services and letting people work remotely for part of the week all shorten travel without requiring anybody to relocate."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#339","summary":"Whether concentrating the population in cities is the way to fix transport problems"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2291","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Why fares keep rising","prompt":"Nowadays, public transport prices are constantly increasing. Why is this happening? How can this problem be solved?","answer":"Bus and rail fares in many countries have risen faster than wages for years, which is a strange outcome for a service governments say they want people to use. The causes lie in how the service is funded, and the remedies must change that rather than simply capping the price.\n\nThe underlying cost is genuinely rising. Fuel, electricity and insurance have all become more expensive, staff wages have to keep pace with the labour market, and ageing trains, track and buses need replacement, which is paid for over years through fares. Falling passenger numbers make this worse, because a service with fewer travellers still costs the same to run, so the remaining passengers carry a larger share of a fixed bill. Where operators are private and hold a monopoly on a route, there is little pressure to keep prices down. Above all, governments have shifted the burden from taxation to the traveller, and each reduction in subsidy appears directly on the ticket.\n\nSeveral remedies are available. The most direct is to restore public funding, on the reasoning that transport produces benefits — less congestion, cleaner air, access to work — that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">passengers pay for but everybody enjoys</strong>. Money can also be raised from the beneficiaries: congestion charges, parking levies and taxes on the land whose value a new station creates. Simpler, cheaper fares tend to be self-financing, because they increase ridership, and countries that introduced flat monthly tickets saw both use and revenue rise.\n\nEfficiency matters too. Contracts open to competition, shared ticketing across operators and long-term investment plans reduce cost, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stop-and-start funding makes every project more expensive</strong>. Targeted discounts protect students, the elderly and low-paid workers without subsidising everyone.\n\nIn conclusion, fares rise because costs are increasing while public support is falling. Restoring subsidy, raising money from drivers and land value, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">simplifying fares to attract more passengers</strong> would keep transport affordable.","phrases":[{"en":"passengers pay for but everybody enjoys","context":"Several remedies are available. The most direct is to restore public funding, on the reasoning that transport produces benefits — less congestion, cleaner air, access to work — that passengers pay for but everybody enjoys. Money can also be raised from the beneficiaries: congestion charges, parking levies and taxes on the land whose value a new station creates. Simpler, cheaper fares tend to be self-financing, because they increase ridership, and countries that introduced flat monthly tickets saw both use and revenue rise."},{"en":"stop-and-start funding makes every project more expensive","context":"Efficiency matters too. Contracts open to competition, shared ticketing across operators and long-term investment plans reduce cost, since stop-and-start funding makes every project more expensive. Targeted discounts protect students, the elderly and low-paid workers without subsidising everyone."},{"en":"simplifying fares to attract more passengers","context":"In conclusion, fares rise because costs are increasing while public support is falling. Restoring subsidy, raising money from drivers and land value, and simplifying fares to attract more passengers would keep transport affordable."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#340","summary":"The reasons behind rising public transport fares and how to control them"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2298","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Living by car","prompt":"Many people depend on their cars for everyday domestic, social and working needs. However, unlimited use of cars causes a number of problems. What are some of these problems? What can be done to reduce car use?","answer":"In many countries the car has moved from a convenience to a requirement, and the costs of that arrangement have become difficult to ignore. The problems are environmental, spatial and social at once, and reducing dependence means changing what cities look like rather than lecturing drivers.\n\nPollution is the most familiar problem. Vehicles are a major source of the carbon dioxide driving climate change and of the fine particles that cause respiratory and heart disease in cities. Road deaths are the second, since traffic remains one of the leading causes of death among young people. Space is a less obvious cost: roads, junctions and parking consume a vast share of urban land that could hold housing or parks, and each additional lane fills with new traffic within a few years. There are social effects as well, because streets designed for vehicles are unpleasant to walk on, so children are driven rather than allowed to travel alone, and those without a car — the young, the old, the poor — find that the city has been built around a machine they do not own.\n\nReducing use requires making the alternatives genuinely better. Frequent, reliable public transport matters most, along with protected cycle lanes, since most people will not cycle beside heavy traffic. Planning is equally important: mixing homes, shops and schools within a district <strong class=\"key-phrase\">removes the journey rather than shortening it</strong>. Where driving remains attractive, congestion charges and parking prices work, provided the revenue improves the alternatives. Working from home for part of the week has already cut commuting substantially, and electric vehicles help with pollution but not with space or safety.\n\nIn conclusion, heavy car use damages health, consumes land and excludes those without a vehicle. Cutting it depends on building cities where the shortest and easiest journey is not by car.","phrases":[{"en":"removes the journey rather than shortening it","context":"Reducing use requires making the alternatives genuinely better. Frequent, reliable public transport matters most, along with protected cycle lanes, since most people will not cycle beside heavy traffic. Planning is equally important: mixing homes, shops and schools within a district removes the journey rather than shortening it. Where driving remains attractive, congestion charges and parking prices work, provided the revenue improves the alternatives. Working from home for part of the week has already cut commuting substantially, and electric vehicles help with pollution but not with space or safety."}],"subtype":"Problem / Solution","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#341","summary":"The problems caused by heavy car dependence and how to reduce it"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2300","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Days without cars in the centre","prompt":"Some cities have vehicle-free days when private cars, trucks and motorcycles are banned from the city centre, and people are encouraged to use public transport. Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?","answer":"A number of cities now close their centres to private vehicles on selected days, and the results are usually striking. I believe the advantages outweigh the disadvantages, mainly because of what such days demonstrate rather than what they achieve directly.\n\nThe immediate benefits are easy to measure. Air quality improves sharply within hours, and the reduction in noise changes the character of a street entirely. Roads become safe enough for children to cross alone and for cyclists who would not normally ride, and shops and cafes spread onto pavements that cars usually occupy. Public transport carries more passengers, which shows operators where extra capacity is needed.\n\nThe greater value is what residents learn. Most people cannot imagine their city without traffic until they experience it, and a single day changes that: streets that had been <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treated as space for moving vehicles</strong> are revealed as places where people would gather. Cities that later introduced permanent pedestrian zones frequently began with occasional closures, because the public opposition faded once the alternative had been seen.\n\nThe disadvantages are real but manageable. Traders fear losing customers, though evidence from pedestrianised streets suggests footfall usually rises. People with disabilities, and those who live outside the reach of public transport, are genuinely inconvenienced, and deliveries, taxis and emergency vehicles need arrangements. Traffic can also <strong class=\"key-phrase\">push itself into the surrounding streets</strong> rather than disappear, which shifts the pollution to residential areas. These objections point to how such days should be organised rather than against them: exemptions for disabled drivers, deliveries scheduled outside the closure, extra services on the day and enough notice for everybody to plan.\n\nIn conclusion, vehicle-free days deliver cleaner air and safer streets immediately, and they let a city <strong class=\"key-phrase\">see what a permanent change would feel like</strong>. With exemptions for those who need them, the benefits clearly outweigh the disruption.","phrases":[{"en":"treated as space for moving vehicles","context":"The greater value is what residents learn. Most people cannot imagine their city without traffic until they experience it, and a single day changes that: streets that had been treated as space for moving vehicles are revealed as places where people would gather. Cities that later introduced permanent pedestrian zones frequently began with occasional closures, because the public opposition faded once the alternative had been seen."},{"en":"push itself into the surrounding streets","context":"The disadvantages are real but manageable. Traders fear losing customers, though evidence from pedestrianised streets suggests footfall usually rises. People with disabilities, and those who live outside the reach of public transport, are genuinely inconvenienced, and deliveries, taxis and emergency vehicles need arrangements. Traffic can also push itself into the surrounding streets rather than disappear, which shifts the pollution to residential areas. These objections point to how such days should be organised rather than against them: exemptions for disabled drivers, deliveries scheduled outside the closure, extra services on the day and enough notice for everybody to plan."},{"en":"see what a permanent change would feel like","context":"In conclusion, vehicle-free days deliver cleaner air and safer streets immediately, and they let a city see what a permanent change would feel like. With exemptions for those who need them, the benefits clearly outweigh the disruption."}],"subtype":"Advantages / Disadvantages","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#342","summary":"Whether banning private vehicles from city centres on certain days is worthwhile"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2318","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Charging drivers to enter the city","prompt":"In some countries, the government has tried to reduce traffic by imposing a congestion charge during rush hour. Do you think this development is positive or negative?","answer":"Several cities now charge drivers for entering the centre at busy times, and the policy is unpopular with motorists everywhere it appears. I regard it as a positive development, provided the money raised is spent on the alternatives.\n\nThe main reason is that it works. Cities that have introduced such charges report immediate falls in traffic and faster journeys, because a price makes people ask whether a particular trip is necessary. Some travel earlier, some share a car, and many switch to the train, and the roads clear enough for buses, ambulances and delivery vehicles to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">move at a usable speed again</strong>. Cleaner air and fewer accidents follow directly.\n\nThere is a fairness argument as well. Driving into a crowded centre imposes costs on everybody else through delay, noise and pollution, and the driver pays none of them. A charge simply returns those costs to the person who creates them, which is the ordinary principle applied to every other kind of pollution.\n\nThe objections deserve an answer. A flat charge takes a larger share of a low income than a high one, and it can push shoppers towards out-of-town centres. Both problems are manageable rather than fatal: exemptions for disabled drivers and residents, discounts for low earners, and the visible reinvestment of revenue in buses and cycle routes turn the charge from a tax into an exchange. Where governments have collected the money and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">left the alternatives unimproved</strong>, resentment has been justified.\n\nIn conclusion, congestion charging reduces traffic quickly and asks drivers to pay for a cost they impose on others. It is a positive measure as long as the revenue is returned to public transport and the poorest travellers are protected.","phrases":[{"en":"move at a usable speed again","context":"The main reason is that it works. Cities that have introduced such charges report immediate falls in traffic and faster journeys, because a price makes people ask whether a particular trip is necessary. Some travel earlier, some share a car, and many switch to the train, and the roads clear enough for buses, ambulances and delivery vehicles to move at a usable speed again. Cleaner air and fewer accidents follow directly."},{"en":"left the alternatives unimproved","context":"The objections deserve an answer. A flat charge takes a larger share of a low income than a high one, and it can push shoppers towards out-of-town centres. Both problems are manageable rather than fatal: exemptions for disabled drivers and residents, discounts for low earners, and the visible reinvestment of revenue in buses and cycle routes turn the charge from a tax into an exchange. Where governments have collected the money and left the alternatives unimproved, resentment has been justified."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#343","summary":"Whether congestion charging during rush hour is a positive development"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2357","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Should cyclists pass a test?","prompt":"Some experts propose that cyclists should be required to pass a test before they are allowed to use public roads. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"As cycling has grown in cities, so have complaints about riders who ignore signals and ride on pavements. I disagree with compulsory testing, because it would prevent far more cycling than it would prevent danger, though training itself is valuable.\n\nThe argument for a test is reasonable in principle. Roads are shared, and a cyclist who does not understand junctions or signalling endangers pedestrians and themselves. Drivers must prove competence before being allowed out, and consistency suggests the same should apply to anyone using the same space. A test would also ensure that riders know the rules they are accused of breaking.\n\nThe objection is one of proportion. A bicycle weighs a few kilograms and travels at fifteen miles an hour; a car weighs well over a tonne and can travel at seventy. Testing exists because of the harm a vehicle can do to others, and cyclists <strong class=\"key-phrase\">injure almost nobody except themselves</strong>. Applying the same procedure to both ignores that difference entirely.\n\nThe practical consequences would be worse than the problem. A test costs money and time, and would stop exactly the people cycling should be encouraging: children, low-income workers and older riders. Cities gain enormously from more cyclists through cleaner air, less congestion and better public health, and any measure that reduces the number costs more in illness than it saves in accidents. Enforcement would also be impossible without licensing every bicycle, which no country has managed cheaply. What does work is free voluntary training, taught in schools, along with cycle lanes separated from traffic, since most conflict arises where riders and vehicles are forced to compete for the same narrow strip of road.\n\nIn conclusion, cyclists should certainly know the rules, and training deserves public funding. A compulsory test would deter cycling without making roads meaningfully safer, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">better infrastructure is the more sensible investment</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"injure almost nobody except themselves","context":"The objection is one of proportion. A bicycle weighs a few kilograms and travels at fifteen miles an hour; a car weighs well over a tonne and can travel at seventy. Testing exists because of the harm a vehicle can do to others, and cyclists injure almost nobody except themselves. Applying the same procedure to both ignores that difference entirely."},{"en":"better infrastructure is the more sensible investment","context":"In conclusion, cyclists should certainly know the rules, and training deserves public funding. A compulsory test would deter cycling without making roads meaningfully safer, so better infrastructure is the more sensible investment."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#344","summary":"Whether cyclists ought to be tested before using public roads"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2378","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Transport","title":"Free public transport around the clock","prompt":"Some people believe that the best way to reduce traffic congestion is for governments to provide free public transport 24 hours a day, seven days a week. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Removing fares entirely is an attractive proposal, and several cities have tried it. I agree that public transport should be cheap and frequent, but I disagree that making it free is the best way to reduce congestion.\n\nThe argument in its favour is straightforward. Price deters use, so removing it should move people out of cars, and free travel helps low-income households immediately, since transport is one of their largest costs. Buses would move faster without fare collection, and running services through the night would serve shift workers who currently have no alternative to driving. Cleaner air and fewer accidents would follow any real shift away from cars.\n\nThe evidence, however, suggests fares are not what keeps drivers in their cars. Cities that abolished them found that most new passengers had previously walked or cycled rather than driven, so congestion changed very little. What actually deters drivers is a service that is slow, infrequent or unreliable, and a driver choosing comfort and speed will not switch because the alternative is free. Free travel also removes the revenue that pays for the service, so unless the government replaces it in full, the result is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">crowded vehicles on routes that are gradually cut</strong> — the opposite of what was intended.\n\nMeasures that work address the road rather than the ticket. Congestion charging, higher parking prices, bus lanes and dedicated cycle routes make driving less attractive while making the alternative faster, and the revenue funds the improvement. Cheap fares belong in this package, especially for the young, the old and the poor, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">frequency and reliability matter more than price</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, free public transport is a generous policy with real social benefits and deserves consideration for that reason. As a cure for congestion it is ineffective, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">drivers respond to time rather than to fares</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"crowded vehicles on routes that are gradually cut","context":"The evidence, however, suggests fares are not what keeps drivers in their cars. Cities that abolished them found that most new passengers had previously walked or cycled rather than driven, so congestion changed very little. What actually deters drivers is a service that is slow, infrequent or unreliable, and a driver choosing comfort and speed will not switch because the alternative is free. Free travel also removes the revenue that pays for the service, so unless the government replaces it in full, the result is crowded vehicles on routes that are gradually cut — the opposite of what was intended."},{"en":"frequency and reliability matter more than price","context":"Measures that work address the road rather than the ticket. Congestion charging, higher parking prices, bus lanes and dedicated cycle routes make driving less attractive while making the alternative faster, and the revenue funds the improvement. Cheap fares belong in this package, especially for the young, the old and the poor, but frequency and reliability matter more than price."},{"en":"drivers respond to time rather than to fares","context":"In conclusion, free public transport is a generous policy with real social benefits and deserves consideration for that reason. As a cure for congestion it is ineffective, because drivers respond to time rather than to fares."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#345","summary":"Whether free public transport at all hours would solve congestion"},{"id":"writing-task-2-032","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"What makes a job satisfying?","prompt":"As most people spend a major part of adult life at the place of work, job satisfaction is an important part of individual well-being. What are the factors that contribute to job satisfaction? Are all the expectations for job satisfaction realistic?","answer":"Job satisfaction matters because adults spend a large part of their lives at work. It depends on several practical and psychological factors, but it is unrealistic to expect any job to provide constant happiness.\n\nAt the most basic level, employees need <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fair pay and reasonable job security</strong>. A salary should cover normal living costs, and workers should not feel that their position could disappear without warning. Working conditions also matter: <strong class=\"key-phrase\">manageable hours, respectful treatment and competent leadership</strong> can have a direct effect on morale. Beyond these basic needs, many people want a sense of purpose. They are more satisfied when they understand how their work contributes to a useful result and when their effort is recognised.\n\nAnother important factor is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">scope for professional growth</strong>. Employees are likely to remain motivated if they can learn new skills, take on greater responsibility and see <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a clear route to promotion</strong>. Relationships with colleagues are equally significant because a supportive team can make demanding work more enjoyable. In contrast, poor communication or persistent conflict can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make even a well-paid job difficult to tolerate</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, some expectations are unrealistic. No occupation can be interesting every day, and every workplace includes routine tasks, pressure and occasional disagreement. It is also unreasonable to expect an employer to provide personal fulfilment in every area of life. A healthy work-life balance usually depends on choices outside work as well as conditions inside it.\n\nIn conclusion, job satisfaction is built on fair treatment, meaningful work, development and supportive relationships. These are reasonable expectations, but employees should also accept that work will never be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">free from boredom or stress</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"fair pay and reasonable job security","context":"At the most basic level, employees need fair pay and reasonable job security. A salary should cover normal living costs, and workers should not feel that their position could disappear without warning. Working conditions also matter: manageable hours, respectful treatment and competent leadership can have a direct effect on morale. Beyond these basic needs, many people want a sense of purpose. They are more satisfied when they understand how their work contributes to a useful result and when their effort is recognised."},{"en":"manageable hours, respectful treatment and competent leadership","context":"At the most basic level, employees need fair pay and reasonable job security. A salary should cover normal living costs, and workers should not feel that their position could disappear without warning. Working conditions also matter: manageable hours, respectful treatment and competent leadership can have a direct effect on morale. Beyond these basic needs, many people want a sense of purpose. They are more satisfied when they understand how their work contributes to a useful result and when their effort is recognised."},{"en":"scope for professional growth","context":"Another important factor is scope for professional growth. Employees are likely to remain motivated if they can learn new skills, take on greater responsibility and see a clear route to promotion. Relationships with colleagues are equally significant because a supportive team can make demanding work more enjoyable. In contrast, poor communication or persistent conflict can make even a well-paid job difficult to tolerate."},{"en":"a clear route to promotion","context":"Another important factor is scope for professional growth. Employees are likely to remain motivated if they can learn new skills, take on greater responsibility and see a clear route to promotion. Relationships with colleagues are equally significant because a supportive team can make demanding work more enjoyable. In contrast, poor communication or persistent conflict can make even a well-paid job difficult to tolerate."},{"en":"make even a well-paid job difficult to tolerate","context":"Another important factor is scope for professional growth. Employees are likely to remain motivated if they can learn new skills, take on greater responsibility and see a clear route to promotion. Relationships with colleagues are equally significant because a supportive team can make demanding work more enjoyable. In contrast, poor communication or persistent conflict can make even a well-paid job difficult to tolerate."},{"en":"free from boredom or stress","context":"In conclusion, job satisfaction is built on fair treatment, meaningful work, development and supportive relationships. These are reasonable expectations, but employees should also accept that work will never be free from boredom or stress."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#346","summary":"As most people spend a major part of adult life at the place of work, job…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-064","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Sending new doctors and teachers to rural areas","prompt":"Good facilities can be found in cities but not in the countryside. Some people therefore argue that newly qualified doctors and teachers should be required to work in rural areas. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Rural areas in most countries have far fewer hospitals and schools than cities, and it is sometimes proposed that newly qualified doctors and teachers should be required to work there. I agree that rural communities need these professionals urgently, but I disagree that compulsion is the right way to achieve it.\n\nThe need is undeniable. A village without a doctor means that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treatable conditions become serious</strong>, and a school without qualified teachers condemns a generation of children to limited opportunities. Newly qualified professionals also gain genuinely from rural work: a doctor in a small clinic sees a far wider range of conditions and must make decisions independently, which develops competence more quickly than a junior post in a large hospital.\n\nCompulsion, however, tends to fail. Professionals forced into rural posts usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">count the days until they can leave</strong>, and unwilling staff provide poor care and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">depart the moment their term ends</strong>, so the community faces <strong class=\"key-phrase\">constant turnover instead of continuity</strong>. Skilled people may also emigrate rather than accept the requirement, which makes the shortage worse.\n\nThe better approach is to make rural work genuinely attractive. Higher salaries, subsidised housing, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">cancellation of student loans</strong>, guaranteed promotion afterwards and decent equipment have all been shown to work. Recruiting students from rural areas is particularly effective, since they already have family and friends outside the cities and are far more likely to return home and stay.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that rural communities deserve properly qualified doctors and teachers, and that the current imbalance is unacceptable. Incentives, however, will retain such people where obligation will not.","phrases":[{"en":"treatable conditions become serious","context":"The need is undeniable. A village without a doctor means that treatable conditions become serious, and a school without qualified teachers condemns a generation of children to limited opportunities. Newly qualified professionals also gain genuinely from rural work: a doctor in a small clinic sees a far wider range of conditions and must make decisions independently, which develops competence more quickly than a junior post in a large hospital."},{"en":"count the days until they can leave","context":"Compulsion, however, tends to fail. Professionals forced into rural posts usually count the days until they can leave, and unwilling staff provide poor care and depart the moment their term ends, so the community faces constant turnover instead of continuity. Skilled people may also emigrate rather than accept the requirement, which makes the shortage worse."},{"en":"depart the moment their term ends","context":"Compulsion, however, tends to fail. Professionals forced into rural posts usually count the days until they can leave, and unwilling staff provide poor care and depart the moment their term ends, so the community faces constant turnover instead of continuity. Skilled people may also emigrate rather than accept the requirement, which makes the shortage worse."},{"en":"constant turnover instead of continuity","context":"Compulsion, however, tends to fail. Professionals forced into rural posts usually count the days until they can leave, and unwilling staff provide poor care and depart the moment their term ends, so the community faces constant turnover instead of continuity. Skilled people may also emigrate rather than accept the requirement, which makes the shortage worse."},{"en":"cancellation of student loans","context":"The better approach is to make rural work genuinely attractive. Higher salaries, subsidised housing, cancellation of student loans, guaranteed promotion afterwards and decent equipment have all been shown to work. Recruiting students from rural areas is particularly effective, since they already have family and friends outside the cities and are far more likely to return home and stay."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#347","summary":"Sending new doctors and teachers to rural areas"},{"id":"writing-task-2-079","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"What really produces success?","prompt":"Some people who have been successful in life do not attribute their success to the theoretical knowledge they learned at university. In your opinion, what are the main factors that contribute to a person's achievement?","answer":"Many successful people say that they owe little to the theoretical knowledge they acquired at university. In my opinion academic study contributes more than they admit, but personal qualities and opportunity explain the greater part of achievement.\n\nThere is some truth in the sceptical view. University courses often teach models that are simplified or outdated, and much of the detail is forgotten within a few years. Several of the most successful entrepreneurs of the past generation left university without finishing, which suggests that formal qualifications are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not essential in every field</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, the value of higher education is easy to underestimate because it is indirect. A degree teaches people to structure an argument, evaluate evidence and work independently to a deadline, and these habits remain long after the content has faded. Certain professions are also impossible without it: nobody would accept a surgeon or a structural engineer who had <strong class=\"key-phrase\">learned informally</strong>.\n\nThe factors that matter most, however, are personal. Persistence appears repeatedly in accounts of successful careers, since most achievement follows a period of failure that others would not have tolerated. The ability to work with people is equally important, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">almost nothing significant is accomplished alone</strong>. Opportunity should not be ignored either: family wealth, contacts and country of birth determine what is realistically available, and much of what is called merit is partly luck.\n\nIn conclusion, university provides useful habits of thought rather than directly usable knowledge, which is why graduates often underestimate it. Persistence, cooperation and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">circumstance</strong> still account for a far larger share of what people achieve.","phrases":[{"en":"not essential in every field","context":"There is some truth in the sceptical view. University courses often teach models that are simplified or outdated, and much of the detail is forgotten within a few years. Several of the most successful entrepreneurs of the past generation left university without finishing, which suggests that formal qualifications are not essential in every field."},{"en":"learned informally","context":"Nevertheless, the value of higher education is easy to underestimate because it is indirect. A degree teaches people to structure an argument, evaluate evidence and work independently to a deadline, and these habits remain long after the content has faded. Certain professions are also impossible without it: nobody would accept a surgeon or a structural engineer who had learned informally."},{"en":"almost nothing significant is accomplished alone","context":"The factors that matter most, however, are personal. Persistence appears repeatedly in accounts of successful careers, since most achievement follows a period of failure that others would not have tolerated. The ability to work with people is equally important, because almost nothing significant is accomplished alone. Opportunity should not be ignored either: family wealth, contacts and country of birth determine what is realistically available, and much of what is called merit is partly luck."},{"en":"circumstance","context":"In conclusion, university provides useful habits of thought rather than directly usable knowledge, which is why graduates often underestimate it. Persistence, cooperation and circumstance still account for a far larger share of what people achieve."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#348","summary":"What really produces success?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-162","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Is a compulsory retirement age out of date?","prompt":"As populations age, some countries are considering abolishing compulsory retirement ages. Do you think a fixed retirement age is out of date, or should it be maintained?","answer":"As people live longer and remain healthy for longer, several countries are debating whether a fixed retirement age still makes sense. In my opinion compulsory retirement is out of date and should be replaced by more flexible arrangements.\n\nThe case for keeping a fixed age is not without merit. It allows employers to plan, and it <strong class=\"key-phrase\">opens positions for younger staff</strong> who would otherwise wait years for promotion. In physically demanding or safety-critical occupations, such as construction, surgery or flying aircraft, there is also a reasonable argument for setting a limit rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assessing each individual</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, the principle is difficult to defend. Forcing a competent employee to stop working on a particular birthday is a form of age discrimination, since it judges a person by their date of birth rather than by their performance. It is also wasteful, because experienced staff take decades of knowledge with them and many are dismissed while still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">at the height of their abilities</strong>. From a national perspective it is unsustainable, as a shrinking workforce cannot support a rapidly growing number of pensioners.\n\nThe sensible alternative is to remove the fixed age and rely instead on <strong class=\"key-phrase\">regular assessment of health and competence</strong>, combined with the option of reduced hours. Those who wish to leave at sixty should be free to do so, provided their pension allows it, and those who wish to continue should be able to.\n\nIn conclusion, a compulsory retirement age belongs to an era of shorter lives and larger young populations, and I believe it should now give way to individual choice.","phrases":[{"en":"opens positions for younger staff","context":"The case for keeping a fixed age is not without merit. It allows employers to plan, and it opens positions for younger staff who would otherwise wait years for promotion. In physically demanding or safety-critical occupations, such as construction, surgery or flying aircraft, there is also a reasonable argument for setting a limit rather than assessing each individual."},{"en":"assessing each individual","context":"The case for keeping a fixed age is not without merit. It allows employers to plan, and it opens positions for younger staff who would otherwise wait years for promotion. In physically demanding or safety-critical occupations, such as construction, surgery or flying aircraft, there is also a reasonable argument for setting a limit rather than assessing each individual."},{"en":"at the height of their abilities","context":"Nevertheless, the principle is difficult to defend. Forcing a competent employee to stop working on a particular birthday is a form of age discrimination, since it judges a person by their date of birth rather than by their performance. It is also wasteful, because experienced staff take decades of knowledge with them and many are dismissed while still at the height of their abilities. From a national perspective it is unsustainable, as a shrinking workforce cannot support a rapidly growing number of pensioners."},{"en":"regular assessment of health and competence","context":"The sensible alternative is to remove the fixed age and rely instead on regular assessment of health and competence, combined with the option of reduced hours. Those who wish to leave at sixty should be free to do so, provided their pension allows it, and those who wish to continue should be able to."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#349","summary":"Is a compulsory retirement age out of date?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-172","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Working from home: who benefits?","prompt":"Many employees may work at home with modern technology. Some people claim that it benefits only workers, but not employers. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"The spread of technologies such as the internet, video calls and instant messaging has made it far easier for employees to work from home. Some people claim that this arrangement benefits only the worker, while employers gain nothing in return. In my opinion, employers can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take full advantage of</strong> home working as well.\n\nHome working lets employers recruit far beyond people who can commute to a single office. Parents with young children, carers and people with disabilities are often highly capable but cannot accept a role that demands a long daily journey; once that barrier is removed, a company can hire the best person for the job rather than simply the closest one. This wider pool gives businesses access to skills and experience they would otherwise miss entirely.\n\nHome working can also raise motivation. Employees who control their own schedule can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">strike a balance between</strong> work and family life, and many report feeling more trusted and less anxious without a manager watching over their shoulder throughout the day. Motivated staff of this kind tend to produce better work and stay with a company for longer, which reduces the cost of recruitment.\n\nHome working is not without difficulties for employers, however. Managers cannot see directly whether staff are meeting deadlines, so performance has to be judged by results rather than by hours spent at a desk, which some managers find hard to trust. Team spirit can also suffer when colleagues rarely meet in person, and on-the-job training is far harder to deliver remotely than in a shared office, where a new employee can simply watch a colleague and ask questions.\n\nIn conclusion, employers as well as employees stand to gain from home working, through access to a wider range of talent and a more motivated workforce. To capture these benefits fully, however, companies need clear ways of measuring performance and a deliberate effort to keep teams connected.","phrases":[{"en":"take full advantage of","context":"The spread of technologies such as the internet, video calls and instant messaging has made it far easier for employees to work from home. Some people claim that this arrangement benefits only the worker, while employers gain nothing in return. In my opinion, employers can take full advantage of home working as well."},{"en":"strike a balance between","context":"Home working can also raise motivation. Employees who control their own schedule can strike a balance between work and family life, and many report feeling more trusted and less anxious without a manager watching over their shoulder throughout the day. Motivated staff of this kind tend to produce better work and stay with a company for longer, which reduces the cost of recruitment."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#350","summary":"Employees may work at home with modern technology."},{"id":"writing-task-2-183","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"One career for life, or several?","prompt":"Many people decide on a career path early in their lives and keep to it. This, they argue, leads to a more satisfying working life. To what extent do you agree with this view? What other things can people do in order to have a satisfying working life?","answer":"It is true that some people know from an early age what career they want to pursue, and they are happy to spend the rest of their lives in the same profession. While I accept that this may suit many people, I believe that others enjoy changing careers or seeking job satisfaction in different ways.\n\nOn the one hand, having <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a defined career path</strong> can certainly lead to a satisfying working life. Many people decide as young children what they want to do as adults, and it gives them a great sense of satisfaction to work towards their goals and gradually achieve them. For example, many children dream of becoming doctors, but <strong class=\"key-phrase\">to realise this ambition</strong> they need to gain the relevant qualifications and undertake years of training. In my experience, very few people who have qualified as doctors choose to change career because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">they find their work so rewarding</strong>, and because they have invested so much time and effort to reach their goal.\n\nOn the other hand, people find happiness in their working lives in different ways, and no single path suits everyone. Someone who has never had one particular ambition can still <strong class=\"key-phrase\">thrive by trying a variety of professions</strong>, since starting out on a completely new career path can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reinvigorating experience</strong> rather than a setback. For others, work is simply a means of earning money, and as long as their salary allows them to enjoy life outside work, changing jobs whenever a better offer appears causes them no distress. What ultimately determines satisfaction, for many people, is less the job title than the conditions surrounding it: a positive working atmosphere, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">enthusiastic colleagues</strong>, and an inspirational boss can make working life far more rewarding than the career itself, whatever profession a person happens to be in.\n\nIn conclusion, it can certainly be satisfying to pursue a particular career for the whole of one&#x27;s life, but this is by no means the only route to fulfilment.","phrases":[{"en":"a defined career path","context":"On the one hand, having a defined career path can certainly lead to a satisfying working life. Many people decide as young children what they want to do as adults, and it gives them a great sense of satisfaction to work towards their goals and gradually achieve them. For example, many children dream of becoming doctors, but to realise this ambition they need to gain the relevant qualifications and undertake years of training. In my experience, very few people who have qualified as doctors choose to change career because they find their work so rewarding, and because they have invested so much time and effort to reach their goal."},{"en":"to realise this ambition","context":"On the one hand, having a defined career path can certainly lead to a satisfying working life. Many people decide as young children what they want to do as adults, and it gives them a great sense of satisfaction to work towards their goals and gradually achieve them. For example, many children dream of becoming doctors, but to realise this ambition they need to gain the relevant qualifications and undertake years of training. In my experience, very few people who have qualified as doctors choose to change career because they find their work so rewarding, and because they have invested so much time and effort to reach their goal."},{"en":"they find their work so rewarding","context":"On the one hand, having a defined career path can certainly lead to a satisfying working life. Many people decide as young children what they want to do as adults, and it gives them a great sense of satisfaction to work towards their goals and gradually achieve them. For example, many children dream of becoming doctors, but to realise this ambition they need to gain the relevant qualifications and undertake years of training. In my experience, very few people who have qualified as doctors choose to change career because they find their work so rewarding, and because they have invested so much time and effort to reach their goal."},{"en":"thrive by trying a variety of professions","context":"On the other hand, people find happiness in their working lives in different ways, and no single path suits everyone. Someone who has never had one particular ambition can still thrive by trying a variety of professions, since starting out on a completely new career path can be a reinvigorating experience rather than a setback. For others, work is simply a means of earning money, and as long as their salary allows them to enjoy life outside work, changing jobs whenever a better offer appears causes them no distress. What ultimately determines satisfaction, for many people, is less the job title than the conditions surrounding it: a positive working atmosphere, enthusiastic colleagues, and an inspirational boss can make working life far more rewarding than the career itself, whatever profession a person happens to be in."},{"en":"a reinvigorating experience","context":"On the other hand, people find happiness in their working lives in different ways, and no single path suits everyone. Someone who has never had one particular ambition can still thrive by trying a variety of professions, since starting out on a completely new career path can be a reinvigorating experience rather than a setback. For others, work is simply a means of earning money, and as long as their salary allows them to enjoy life outside work, changing jobs whenever a better offer appears causes them no distress. What ultimately determines satisfaction, for many people, is less the job title than the conditions surrounding it: a positive working atmosphere, enthusiastic colleagues, and an inspirational boss can make working life far more rewarding than the career itself, whatever profession a person happens to be in."},{"en":"enthusiastic colleagues","context":"On the other hand, people find happiness in their working lives in different ways, and no single path suits everyone. Someone who has never had one particular ambition can still thrive by trying a variety of professions, since starting out on a completely new career path can be a reinvigorating experience rather than a setback. For others, work is simply a means of earning money, and as long as their salary allows them to enjoy life outside work, changing jobs whenever a better offer appears causes them no distress. What ultimately determines satisfaction, for many people, is less the job title than the conditions surrounding it: a positive working atmosphere, enthusiastic colleagues, and an inspirational boss can make working life far more rewarding than the career itself, whatever profession a person happens to be in."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#351","summary":"People decide on a career path early in their lives and keep to it."},{"id":"writing-task-2-184","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Is salary the most important thing about a job?","prompt":"When choosing a job, the salary is the most important consideration. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Many people choose their jobs based on the size of the salary offered. Personally, I disagree with the idea that money is the key consideration when deciding on a career, because I believe that other factors are equally important.\n\nOn the one hand, I agree that money is necessary in order for people to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meet their basic needs</strong>. For example, we all need money to pay for housing, food, bills, health care, and education. Most people consider it a priority to at least earn a salary that allows them to cover these needs and have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a reasonable quality of life</strong>. If people chose their jobs based on enjoyment or other non-financial factors, they might find it difficult to support themselves. Artists and musicians, for instance, are known for choosing a career path that they love, but that does not always provide them with enough money to live comfortably and raise a family.\n\nNevertheless, I believe that other considerations are just as important as what we earn in our jobs. Firstly, personal relationships and the atmosphere in a workplace are extremely important when choosing a job. Having a good manager or friendly colleagues, for example, can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">make a huge difference to</strong> workers’ levels of happiness and general quality of life. Secondly, many people&#x27;s feelings of job satisfaction come from their professional achievements, the skills they learn, and the position they reach, rather than the money they earn. Finally, some people choose a career because they want to help others and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">contribute something positive to</strong> society.\n\nIn conclusion, while salaries certainly affect people&#x27;s choice of profession, I do not believe that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">money outweighs all other motivators</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"meet their basic needs","context":"On the one hand, I agree that money is necessary in order for people to meet their basic needs. For example, we all need money to pay for housing, food, bills, health care, and education. Most people consider it a priority to at least earn a salary that allows them to cover these needs and have a reasonable quality of life. If people chose their jobs based on enjoyment or other non-financial factors, they might find it difficult to support themselves. Artists and musicians, for instance, are known for choosing a career path that they love, but that does not always provide them with enough money to live comfortably and raise a family."},{"en":"a reasonable quality of life","context":"On the one hand, I agree that money is necessary in order for people to meet their basic needs. For example, we all need money to pay for housing, food, bills, health care, and education. Most people consider it a priority to at least earn a salary that allows them to cover these needs and have a reasonable quality of life. If people chose their jobs based on enjoyment or other non-financial factors, they might find it difficult to support themselves. Artists and musicians, for instance, are known for choosing a career path that they love, but that does not always provide them with enough money to live comfortably and raise a family."},{"en":"make a huge difference to","context":"Nevertheless, I believe that other considerations are just as important as what we earn in our jobs. Firstly, personal relationships and the atmosphere in a workplace are extremely important when choosing a job. Having a good manager or friendly colleagues, for example, can make a huge difference to workers’ levels of happiness and general quality of life. Secondly, many people's feelings of job satisfaction come from their professional achievements, the skills they learn, and the position they reach, rather than the money they earn. Finally, some people choose a career because they want to help others and contribute something positive to society."},{"en":"contribute something positive to","context":"Nevertheless, I believe that other considerations are just as important as what we earn in our jobs. Firstly, personal relationships and the atmosphere in a workplace are extremely important when choosing a job. Having a good manager or friendly colleagues, for example, can make a huge difference to workers’ levels of happiness and general quality of life. Secondly, many people's feelings of job satisfaction come from their professional achievements, the skills they learn, and the position they reach, rather than the money they earn. Finally, some people choose a career because they want to help others and contribute something positive to society."},{"en":"money outweighs all other motivators","context":"In conclusion, while salaries certainly affect people's choice of profession, I do not believe that money outweighs all other motivators."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#352","summary":"Salary versus other job factors"},{"id":"writing-task-2-185","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Balancing work and personal life","prompt":"Many people today find it difficult to balance the demands of their work and personal life. What are the causes of this situation, and what can individuals and employers do to reduce the problem?","answer":"Finding a compromise between one&#x27;s job and one&#x27;s private life is probably harder than ever these days, especially if one has a family. There are several reasons for this, but also a number of measures we can take. Possibly the major factor is the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">increased workload</strong> that many people have to deal with. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The predominance of the service sector</strong> means that most people work in office-related jobs, and they find that employers expect them to stay longer or be available outside traditional office hours. On top of this, the rise of communication tools – ranging from smart phones to laptops and teleconferencing equipment – <strong class=\"key-phrase\">has accelerated the trend</strong>, because people are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">always contactable</strong>.\n\nThe economic recession has also made the labour market increasingly competitive, and so workers are under pressure to cooperate in this process, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fearing loss of promotion or even redundancy</strong> if they resist. However, there are various steps that can be taken to lessen the problem.\n\nIndividuals can protect some of this time themselves, for instance by agreeing clear hours with their employer during which they are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">genuinely free of work and uncontactable</strong>. Employers, for their part, could allow more flexible working hours, so that staff can step away during the day for important family events rather than requesting a full day off. Coaching in time management and stress control would also help, since many employees have never been taught how to structure <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a heavy workload</strong> rather than simply enduring it.\n\nIn summary, we can say that technology and economics are creating this situation, but also that employees and companies together can reduce the impact.","phrases":[{"en":"increased workload","context":"Finding a compromise between one's job and one's private life is probably harder than ever these days, especially if one has a family. There are several reasons for this, but also a number of measures we can take. Possibly the major factor is the increased workload that many people have to deal with. The predominance of the service sector means that most people work in office-related jobs, and they find that employers expect them to stay longer or be available outside traditional office hours. On top of this, the rise of communication tools – ranging from smart phones to laptops and teleconferencing equipment – has accelerated the trend, because people are always contactable."},{"en":"The predominance of the service sector","context":"Finding a compromise between one's job and one's private life is probably harder than ever these days, especially if one has a family. There are several reasons for this, but also a number of measures we can take. Possibly the major factor is the increased workload that many people have to deal with. The predominance of the service sector means that most people work in office-related jobs, and they find that employers expect them to stay longer or be available outside traditional office hours. On top of this, the rise of communication tools – ranging from smart phones to laptops and teleconferencing equipment – has accelerated the trend, because people are always contactable."},{"en":"has accelerated the trend","context":"Finding a compromise between one's job and one's private life is probably harder than ever these days, especially if one has a family. There are several reasons for this, but also a number of measures we can take. Possibly the major factor is the increased workload that many people have to deal with. The predominance of the service sector means that most people work in office-related jobs, and they find that employers expect them to stay longer or be available outside traditional office hours. On top of this, the rise of communication tools – ranging from smart phones to laptops and teleconferencing equipment – has accelerated the trend, because people are always contactable."},{"en":"always contactable","context":"Finding a compromise between one's job and one's private life is probably harder than ever these days, especially if one has a family. There are several reasons for this, but also a number of measures we can take. Possibly the major factor is the increased workload that many people have to deal with. The predominance of the service sector means that most people work in office-related jobs, and they find that employers expect them to stay longer or be available outside traditional office hours. On top of this, the rise of communication tools – ranging from smart phones to laptops and teleconferencing equipment – has accelerated the trend, because people are always contactable."},{"en":"fearing loss of promotion or even redundancy","context":"The economic recession has also made the labour market increasingly competitive, and so workers are under pressure to cooperate in this process, fearing loss of promotion or even redundancy if they resist. However, there are various steps that can be taken to lessen the problem."},{"en":"genuinely free of work and uncontactable","context":"Individuals can protect some of this time themselves, for instance by agreeing clear hours with their employer during which they are genuinely free of work and uncontactable. Employers, for their part, could allow more flexible working hours, so that staff can step away during the day for important family events rather than requesting a full day off. Coaching in time management and stress control would also help, since many employees have never been taught how to structure a heavy workload rather than simply enduring it."},{"en":"a heavy workload","context":"Individuals can protect some of this time themselves, for instance by agreeing clear hours with their employer during which they are genuinely free of work and uncontactable. Employers, for their part, could allow more flexible working hours, so that staff can step away during the day for important family events rather than requesting a full day off. Coaching in time management and stress control would also help, since many employees have never been taught how to structure a heavy workload rather than simply enduring it."}],"subtype":"Causes / Solutions","_displayNo":"#353","summary":"People today find it difficult to balance the demands of their work and…"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Uniforms at work","prompt":"Some companies have uniforms for their staff which must be worn at all times. Discuss the advantages and disadvantages of this.","answer":"Staff uniforms can strengthen a company&#x27;s identity and make employees easy to recognise, but they may also reduce comfort and personal choice. Their value depends on the type of work and the quality of the uniform.\n\nThe main advantage is clarity. In hospitals, hotels or transport services, customers can quickly identify the person who can help them. A uniform can also create a professional appearance and reduce pressure on employees to buy different work clothes. In hazardous jobs, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">standard clothing may provide essential protection</strong> and support consistent safety standards, since visible, well-fitted equipment is easier to inspect and enforce.\n\nThe disadvantages arise when a uniform is uncomfortable, unsuitable for the weather or <strong class=\"key-phrase\">designed without consulting staff</strong>. Employees may feel that the company ignores individual needs, especially in relation to religion, disability or body shape. <strong class=\"key-phrase\">The cost of cleaning and replacement</strong> can also be unfair if workers must pay it themselves.\n\nResponsibility for the uniform therefore matters. Employers should provide enough sets, pay for specialist cleaning when required, offer versions suitable for different climates, religions and physical needs, and review the design when duties or staff needs change rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaving it fixed for reasons of tradition alone</strong>. A policy is more acceptable when staff are consulted and the rules have a clear purpose. This can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">protect professional standards without creating unnecessary resentment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, uniforms are useful where identification, hygiene or safety matters. They are less necessary in roles with little public contact. A sensible policy should balance company image with comfort, dignity and practical needs.","topic":"Work & Employment","phrases":[{"en":"standard clothing may provide essential protection","context":"The main advantage is clarity. In hospitals, hotels or transport services, customers can quickly identify the person who can help them. A uniform can also create a professional appearance and reduce pressure on employees to buy different work clothes. In hazardous jobs, standard clothing may provide essential protection and support consistent safety standards, since visible, well-fitted equipment is easier to inspect and enforce."},{"en":"designed without consulting staff","context":"The disadvantages arise when a uniform is uncomfortable, unsuitable for the weather or designed without consulting staff. Employees may feel that the company ignores individual needs, especially in relation to religion, disability or body shape. The cost of cleaning and replacement can also be unfair if workers must pay it themselves."},{"en":"The cost of cleaning and replacement","context":"The disadvantages arise when a uniform is uncomfortable, unsuitable for the weather or designed without consulting staff. Employees may feel that the company ignores individual needs, especially in relation to religion, disability or body shape. The cost of cleaning and replacement can also be unfair if workers must pay it themselves."},{"en":"leaving it fixed for reasons of tradition alone","context":"Responsibility for the uniform therefore matters. Employers should provide enough sets, pay for specialist cleaning when required, offer versions suitable for different climates, religions and physical needs, and review the design when duties or staff needs change rather than leaving it fixed for reasons of tradition alone. A policy is more acceptable when staff are consulted and the rules have a clear purpose. This can protect professional standards without creating unnecessary resentment."},{"en":"protect professional standards without creating unnecessary resentment","context":"Responsibility for the uniform therefore matters. Employers should provide enough sets, pay for specialist cleaning when required, offer versions suitable for different climates, religions and physical needs, and review the design when duties or staff needs change rather than leaving it fixed for reasons of tradition alone. A policy is more acceptable when staff are consulted and the rules have a clear purpose. This can protect professional standards without creating unnecessary resentment."}],"subtype":"advdis","summary":"The advantages and disadvantages of staff uniforms","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-186","_displayNo":"#354"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Should companies reserve senior posts for women?","prompt":"Most high-level positions in companies are filled by men even though the workforce in many developed countries is more than 50 per cent female. Companies should be required to allocate a certain percentage of these positions to women. To what extent do you agree?","answer":"Men still occupy most senior positions in many companies, even where women form a large part of the workforce. Some people propose fixed quotas for women in leadership. I support temporary targets and transparent selection, but not automatic promotion based only on gender.\n\nThe case for intervention is strong. Senior appointments often depend on informal networks, previous opportunities and assumptions about who looks like a leader. Women may also receive fewer high-profile assignments or face career penalties for caring responsibilities. If companies simply wait for the imbalance to correct itself without setting any measurable goal, progress can remain extremely slow, sometimes across an entire career. Clear targets can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">force organisations to examine hidden barriers</strong> that a purely voluntary approach allows them to overlook indefinitely.\n\nRigid quotas, however, carry a cost of their own. When a fixed percentage is imposed, colleagues may assume that a woman was appointed to meet the figure rather than on her record, which weakens the authority she needs to do the job well. It is more effective to change the process that produces the shortlist: insisting that every list for a senior post contains qualified women, publishing promotion and pay data, and making sure that talented staff are given the high-profile projects from which future directors are chosen. Norway&#x27;s forty per cent board quota, for example, quickly raised the number of women on boards but left the executive ranks below them almost unchanged, which shows that a headline figure is a poor guide to whether a company <strong class=\"key-phrase\">reflects genuine equality of opportunity</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, companies should take active steps to correct unequal opportunity, but leadership decisions must remain evidence-based. Time-limited targets combined with transparent recruitment can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">improve representation without lowering standards</strong>.","topic":"Work & Employment","phrases":[{"en":"force organisations to examine hidden barriers","context":"The case for intervention is strong. Senior appointments often depend on informal networks, previous opportunities and assumptions about who looks like a leader. Women may also receive fewer high-profile assignments or face career penalties for caring responsibilities. If companies simply wait for the imbalance to correct itself without setting any measurable goal, progress can remain extremely slow, sometimes across an entire career. Clear targets can force organisations to examine hidden barriers that a purely voluntary approach allows them to overlook indefinitely."},{"en":"reflects genuine equality of opportunity","context":"Rigid quotas, however, carry a cost of their own. When a fixed percentage is imposed, colleagues may assume that a woman was appointed to meet the figure rather than on her record, which weakens the authority she needs to do the job well. It is more effective to change the process that produces the shortlist: insisting that every list for a senior post contains qualified women, publishing promotion and pay data, and making sure that talented staff are given the high-profile projects from which future directors are chosen. Norway's forty per cent board quota, for example, quickly raised the number of women on boards but left the executive ranks below them almost unchanged, which shows that a headline figure is a poor guide to whether a company reflects genuine equality of opportunity."},{"en":"improve representation without lowering standards","context":"In conclusion, companies should take active steps to correct unequal opportunity, but leadership decisions must remain evidence-based. Time-limited targets combined with transparent recruitment can improve representation without lowering standards."}],"subtype":"opinion","summary":"Requiring companies to reserve senior positions for women","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-187","_displayNo":"#355"},{"section":"writing-task-2","title":"Competing with younger people for the same jobs","prompt":"Nowadays more and more people have to compete with young people for the same jobs. What problems does this cause? What are some possible solutions?","answer":"Older workers increasingly compete with younger applicants for the same jobs. This can create longer unemployment, resentment and a loss of confidence for both groups. The best response is not to favour one age group, but to improve training and make recruitment more transparent.\n\nYoung applicants may struggle to gain their first experience because employers prefer candidates who can work immediately. Older applicants, meanwhile, may face assumptions that their skills are outdated or that they will be expensive to employ. When both groups are rejected repeatedly, the result can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long-term exclusion from the labour market</strong>.\n\nGovernments should fund practical retraining linked to real vacancies, while companies should describe the skills required rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">asking for a narrow age profile</strong>. Paid internships can help younger people enter a field, and short conversion courses can help experienced workers update digital or technical skills. Mixed-age teams should also be encouraged because they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">combine recent training with practical judgement</strong>.\n\nThere is also a direct financial case for retaining experienced staff rather than replacing them wholesale. Recruiting and training a new employee typically costs several months of salary before that person becomes fully productive, so losing a worker&#x27;s accumulated knowledge of clients, systems and established professional networks is expensive to replace, not merely inconvenient. A structured handover, in which an employee nearing retirement mentors a newer colleague for several months rather than leaving on a fixed date, protects this knowledge while still opening a genuine route into the profession for someone younger. Public employment services could support such arrangements directly, by funding the mentoring period itself rather than offering generic courses with little labour-market value.\n\nIn conclusion, age competition becomes harmful when employers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rely on stereotypes instead of evidence</strong>. Skills-based recruitment, accessible training and flexible career routes can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">give both generations a fair chance</strong> and reduce unnecessary conflict.","topic":"Work & Employment","phrases":[{"en":"long-term exclusion from the labour market","context":"Young applicants may struggle to gain their first experience because employers prefer candidates who can work immediately. Older applicants, meanwhile, may face assumptions that their skills are outdated or that they will be expensive to employ. When both groups are rejected repeatedly, the result can be long-term exclusion from the labour market."},{"en":"asking for a narrow age profile","context":"Governments should fund practical retraining linked to real vacancies, while companies should describe the skills required rather than asking for a narrow age profile. Paid internships can help younger people enter a field, and short conversion courses can help experienced workers update digital or technical skills. Mixed-age teams should also be encouraged because they combine recent training with practical judgement."},{"en":"combine recent training with practical judgement","context":"Governments should fund practical retraining linked to real vacancies, while companies should describe the skills required rather than asking for a narrow age profile. Paid internships can help younger people enter a field, and short conversion courses can help experienced workers update digital or technical skills. Mixed-age teams should also be encouraged because they combine recent training with practical judgement."},{"en":"rely on stereotypes instead of evidence","context":"In conclusion, age competition becomes harmful when employers rely on stereotypes instead of evidence. Skills-based recruitment, accessible training and flexible career routes can give both generations a fair chance and reduce unnecessary conflict."},{"en":"give both generations a fair chance","context":"In conclusion, age competition becomes harmful when employers rely on stereotypes instead of evidence. Skills-based recruitment, accessible training and flexible career routes can give both generations a fair chance and reduce unnecessary conflict."}],"subtype":"problem","summary":"Competition between older and younger people for the same jobs","visual":null,"id":"writing-task-2-188","_displayNo":"#356"},{"id":"writing-task-2-191","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Unpaid community work for young adults","prompt":"People tend to believe that all young adults should undertake a period of unpaid work for the good of the community. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Unpaid community work can give young adults useful experience and strengthen local services. However, I disagree with making it compulsory for everyone. Schools and governments should encourage participation and remove practical barriers, but the final choice should remain voluntary.\n\nVolunteering can be highly beneficial. Young people may learn to communicate with strangers, organise tasks and work as part of a team. A student who helps at a food bank or supports an environmental project also gains a clearer understanding of problems outside the classroom. These experiences can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">build practical skills and social awareness</strong> at the same time.\n\nThe difficulty with compulsory service is that unpaid work may affect young people unequally. Some students have caring duties, paid jobs or health problems, so a fixed requirement could place a heavier burden on them. Forced participation may also reduce motivation and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lead organisations to receive unwilling workers</strong>. A better system would offer several forms of service, flexible hours and help with travel costs. This would make volunteering <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accessible rather than punitive</strong>.\n\nCommunity organisations also need quality rather than reluctant attendance. Volunteers should receive a clear role, basic training and feedback so that their time produces a real benefit. Schools can recognise service through certificates or course credit without making it a legal duty. This approach <strong class=\"key-phrase\">rewards meaningful participation rather than mere compliance</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, community work should be strongly promoted but not imposed as a universal obligation. Young adults are more likely to contribute seriously when they understand the purpose of the work and can choose a suitable role. Voluntary programmes with good support would create <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lasting commitment to the community</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"build practical skills and social awareness","context":"Volunteering can be highly beneficial. Young people may learn to communicate with strangers, organise tasks and work as part of a team. A student who helps at a food bank or supports an environmental project also gains a clearer understanding of problems outside the classroom. These experiences can build practical skills and social awareness at the same time."},{"en":"lead organisations to receive unwilling workers","context":"The difficulty with compulsory service is that unpaid work may affect young people unequally. Some students have caring duties, paid jobs or health problems, so a fixed requirement could place a heavier burden on them. Forced participation may also reduce motivation and lead organisations to receive unwilling workers. A better system would offer several forms of service, flexible hours and help with travel costs. This would make volunteering accessible rather than punitive."},{"en":"accessible rather than punitive","context":"The difficulty with compulsory service is that unpaid work may affect young people unequally. Some students have caring duties, paid jobs or health problems, so a fixed requirement could place a heavier burden on them. Forced participation may also reduce motivation and lead organisations to receive unwilling workers. A better system would offer several forms of service, flexible hours and help with travel costs. This would make volunteering accessible rather than punitive."},{"en":"rewards meaningful participation rather than mere compliance","context":"Community organisations also need quality rather than reluctant attendance. Volunteers should receive a clear role, basic training and feedback so that their time produces a real benefit. Schools can recognise service through certificates or course credit without making it a legal duty. This approach rewards meaningful participation rather than mere compliance."},{"en":"lasting commitment to the community","context":"In conclusion, community work should be strongly promoted but not imposed as a universal obligation. Young adults are more likely to contribute seriously when they understand the purpose of the work and can choose a suitable role. Voluntary programmes with good support would create lasting commitment to the community."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#357","summary":"People tend to believe that all young adults should undertake a period of…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-192","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Should working hours be restricted?","prompt":"People tend to work longer hours nowadays. Working long hours has a negative effect on workers, their families and society, so working hours should be restricted. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"In many countries employees now work longer hours than they did a generation ago. I agree that this trend damages workers, families and society, and that legal limits on working time are justified.\n\nThe effects on the individual are well established. Employees who regularly work beyond about fifty hours a week show higher rates of heart disease, depression and stroke, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">exhaustion makes accidents far more likely</strong> in medicine, transport and construction. There is also strong evidence that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">productivity per hour falls sharply</strong> once people are tired, so the extra hours frequently produce very little additional work.\n\nFamilies suffer in a less measurable but equally serious way. A parent who leaves before their children wake and returns after they are asleep cannot provide guidance or attention, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">relationships between partners deteriorate</strong> when neither has any time left. Society loses too, because people who work constantly do not volunteer, coach a team or care for elderly relatives, and it is precisely these activities that hold communities together.\n\nThe usual objection is that limits reduce freedom and damage competitiveness. In practice, countries with strictly regulated hours, such as Germany and the Netherlands, are among the most productive in the world, which suggests <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the fear is misplaced</strong>. Individuals cannot solve this alone either, since an employee who refuses overtime while colleagues accept it risks their position.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that working hours should be restricted. The health, family and social costs are substantial, and the evidence indicates that shorter hours <strong class=\"key-phrase\">need not reduce output</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"exhaustion makes accidents far more likely","context":"The effects on the individual are well established. Employees who regularly work beyond about fifty hours a week show higher rates of heart disease, depression and stroke, and exhaustion makes accidents far more likely in medicine, transport and construction. There is also strong evidence that productivity per hour falls sharply once people are tired, so the extra hours frequently produce very little additional work."},{"en":"productivity per hour falls sharply","context":"The effects on the individual are well established. Employees who regularly work beyond about fifty hours a week show higher rates of heart disease, depression and stroke, and exhaustion makes accidents far more likely in medicine, transport and construction. There is also strong evidence that productivity per hour falls sharply once people are tired, so the extra hours frequently produce very little additional work."},{"en":"relationships between partners deteriorate","context":"Families suffer in a less measurable but equally serious way. A parent who leaves before their children wake and returns after they are asleep cannot provide guidance or attention, and relationships between partners deteriorate when neither has any time left. Society loses too, because people who work constantly do not volunteer, coach a team or care for elderly relatives, and it is precisely these activities that hold communities together."},{"en":"the fear is misplaced","context":"The usual objection is that limits reduce freedom and damage competitiveness. In practice, countries with strictly regulated hours, such as Germany and the Netherlands, are among the most productive in the world, which suggests the fear is misplaced. Individuals cannot solve this alone either, since an employee who refuses overtime while colleagues accept it risks their position."},{"en":"need not reduce output","context":"In conclusion, I agree that working hours should be restricted. The health, family and social costs are substantial, and the evidence indicates that shorter hours need not reduce output."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","_displayNo":"#358","summary":"Should working hours be restricted?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-193","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Is changing jobs periodically a good idea?","prompt":"Some people think that changing jobs periodically is good for a person's career. What is your opinion?","answer":"Some people believe that moving to a new employer every few years is a sensible career strategy. I largely agree, provided that the moves are purposeful rather than merely habitual.\n\nThere are clear reasons why periodic change benefits an individual. Pay rises are now usually larger when someone joins a new organisation than when they stay, because internal increases are constrained by salary structures that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">external offers are not</strong>. Changing employers also broadens experience: a person who has worked in three companies has seen three ways of solving the same problems, whereas someone who has stayed in one may <strong class=\"key-phrase\">mistake local habit for the only method</strong>. Moving is also often the only way to escape a role where promotion is blocked indefinitely by somebody who has no intention of leaving.\n\nThere are limits, however. Every move costs several months of reduced productivity while a person learns new systems and colleagues, and some professions reward the accumulated depth that only comes from staying. A history of very short appointments also makes recruiters cautious, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">training someone who leaves within a year</strong> is expensive.\n\nFor employers the trend is uncomfortable but not entirely negative. Constant turnover damages the knowledge held within a team and makes long projects harder to staff, yet new arrivals bring methods from elsewhere, and organisations that never recruit from outside tend to stagnate quietly.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that changing jobs periodically is generally good for a career, as long as each move <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adds something rather than repeating the last one</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"external offers are not","context":"There are clear reasons why periodic change benefits an individual. Pay rises are now usually larger when someone joins a new organisation than when they stay, because internal increases are constrained by salary structures that external offers are not. Changing employers also broadens experience: a person who has worked in three companies has seen three ways of solving the same problems, whereas someone who has stayed in one may mistake local habit for the only method. Moving is also often the only way to escape a role where promotion is blocked indefinitely by somebody who has no intention of leaving."},{"en":"mistake local habit for the only method","context":"There are clear reasons why periodic change benefits an individual. Pay rises are now usually larger when someone joins a new organisation than when they stay, because internal increases are constrained by salary structures that external offers are not. Changing employers also broadens experience: a person who has worked in three companies has seen three ways of solving the same problems, whereas someone who has stayed in one may mistake local habit for the only method. Moving is also often the only way to escape a role where promotion is blocked indefinitely by somebody who has no intention of leaving."},{"en":"training someone who leaves within a year","context":"There are limits, however. Every move costs several months of reduced productivity while a person learns new systems and colleagues, and some professions reward the accumulated depth that only comes from staying. A history of very short appointments also makes recruiters cautious, since training someone who leaves within a year is expensive."},{"en":"adds something rather than repeating the last one","context":"In conclusion, I agree that changing jobs periodically is generally good for a career, as long as each move adds something rather than repeating the last one."}],"subtype":"Opinion","_displayNo":"#359","summary":"Is changing jobs periodically a good idea?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-194","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Fathers at home while mothers work","prompt":"These days more fathers stay at home and take care of their children while mothers go out to work. What could be the reasons for this? Do you think it is a positive or a negative development?","answer":"It is true that men are increasingly likely to take on the role of househusband, while more women than ever are the breadwinners in their families. There could be several reasons for this, and I consider it to be a very positive trend.\n\nIn recent years, parents have had to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adapt to various changes</strong> in our societies. Equal rights movements have made great progress, and it is no longer remarkable for a woman to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">out-earn her partner</strong> after years spent gaining the same qualifications and workplace experience that were once reserved for men. It has also become socially acceptable for a man to stay at home and raise his children, rather than being seen as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">failing to provide for his family</strong>. At the same time, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the rising cost of living</strong>, especially childcare fees in large cities, means that once a nursery place costs almost as much as one partner earns, it often makes more financial sense for that parent, not automatically the mother, to stay at home instead. Couples therefore increasingly decide who works and who cares for the children based on income and preference rather than on tradition.\n\nIn my view, the changes described above should be seen as progress. We should be happy to live in a society in which men and women have equal opportunities, and in which women are not put under pressure to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sacrifice their careers</strong>. Equally, it seems only fair that men should be free to leave their jobs to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">assume childcare responsibilities</strong> if this is what they wish to do. Couples should be left to make their own decisions about which parental role each partner takes, according to their particular circumstances and needs.\n\nIn conclusion, the changing roles of men and women in the family are a result of wider changes in society, and I believe that these developments are desirable.","phrases":[{"en":"adapt to various changes","context":"In recent years, parents have had to adapt to various changes in our societies. Equal rights movements have made great progress, and it is no longer remarkable for a woman to out-earn her partner after years spent gaining the same qualifications and workplace experience that were once reserved for men. It has also become socially acceptable for a man to stay at home and raise his children, rather than being seen as failing to provide for his family. At the same time, the rising cost of living, especially childcare fees in large cities, means that once a nursery place costs almost as much as one partner earns, it often makes more financial sense for that parent, not automatically the mother, to stay at home instead. Couples therefore increasingly decide who works and who cares for the children based on income and preference rather than on tradition."},{"en":"out-earn her partner","context":"In recent years, parents have had to adapt to various changes in our societies. Equal rights movements have made great progress, and it is no longer remarkable for a woman to out-earn her partner after years spent gaining the same qualifications and workplace experience that were once reserved for men. It has also become socially acceptable for a man to stay at home and raise his children, rather than being seen as failing to provide for his family. At the same time, the rising cost of living, especially childcare fees in large cities, means that once a nursery place costs almost as much as one partner earns, it often makes more financial sense for that parent, not automatically the mother, to stay at home instead. Couples therefore increasingly decide who works and who cares for the children based on income and preference rather than on tradition."},{"en":"failing to provide for his family","context":"In recent years, parents have had to adapt to various changes in our societies. Equal rights movements have made great progress, and it is no longer remarkable for a woman to out-earn her partner after years spent gaining the same qualifications and workplace experience that were once reserved for men. It has also become socially acceptable for a man to stay at home and raise his children, rather than being seen as failing to provide for his family. At the same time, the rising cost of living, especially childcare fees in large cities, means that once a nursery place costs almost as much as one partner earns, it often makes more financial sense for that parent, not automatically the mother, to stay at home instead. Couples therefore increasingly decide who works and who cares for the children based on income and preference rather than on tradition."},{"en":"the rising cost of living","context":"In recent years, parents have had to adapt to various changes in our societies. Equal rights movements have made great progress, and it is no longer remarkable for a woman to out-earn her partner after years spent gaining the same qualifications and workplace experience that were once reserved for men. It has also become socially acceptable for a man to stay at home and raise his children, rather than being seen as failing to provide for his family. At the same time, the rising cost of living, especially childcare fees in large cities, means that once a nursery place costs almost as much as one partner earns, it often makes more financial sense for that parent, not automatically the mother, to stay at home instead. Couples therefore increasingly decide who works and who cares for the children based on income and preference rather than on tradition."},{"en":"sacrifice their careers","context":"In my view, the changes described above should be seen as progress. We should be happy to live in a society in which men and women have equal opportunities, and in which women are not put under pressure to sacrifice their careers. Equally, it seems only fair that men should be free to leave their jobs to assume childcare responsibilities if this is what they wish to do. Couples should be left to make their own decisions about which parental role each partner takes, according to their particular circumstances and needs."},{"en":"assume childcare responsibilities","context":"In my view, the changes described above should be seen as progress. We should be happy to live in a society in which men and women have equal opportunities, and in which women are not put under pressure to sacrifice their careers. Equally, it seems only fair that men should be free to leave their jobs to assume childcare responsibilities if this is what they wish to do. Couples should be left to make their own decisions about which parental role each partner takes, according to their particular circumstances and needs."}],"subtype":"Essay","_displayNo":"#360","summary":"These days more fathers stay at home and take care of their children while…"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2202","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Working in old age","prompt":"Some people think that it is a good idea for people to continue working in their old age. Do you agree or disagree?","answer":"In many countries people are living longer and healthier lives, and it is increasingly common for them to keep working beyond the traditional retirement age. I largely agree that this is a positive choice, provided that it remains a choice rather than a necessity.\n\nContinued employment brings clear benefits to the individual. Work provides structure, purpose and daily contact with other people, and the loss of these things often explains why some retired people <strong class=\"key-phrase\">become bored, isolated or depressed</strong>. There is also evidence that staying mentally and socially active helps to delay cognitive decline. The financial argument is equally important, because pensions in most countries are modest, and a few extra years of earnings can make <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the difference between comfort and hardship</strong>.\n\nSociety gains as well. Older employees possess <strong class=\"key-phrase\">experience and judgement that cannot be taught quickly</strong>, and they are usually the people who train younger colleagues. As birth rates fall, many economies simply cannot afford to lose millions of skilled workers at sixty-five, since a shrinking workforce has to support a growing number of pensioners.\n\nThere are, however, important conditions. Some jobs are physically demanding, and it would be unreasonable to expect a builder or a nurse to continue into their seventies. Older employees may also need reduced hours or a change of role. Most importantly, people who have worked all their lives should not be forced to continue simply because their pension is too small to live on.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that working in old age is a good idea for those who are able and willing, but it must be supported by flexible conditions and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">an adequate pension system</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"become bored, isolated or depressed","context":"Continued employment brings clear benefits to the individual. Work provides structure, purpose and daily contact with other people, and the loss of these things often explains why some retired people become bored, isolated or depressed. There is also evidence that staying mentally and socially active helps to delay cognitive decline. The financial argument is equally important, because pensions in most countries are modest, and a few extra years of earnings can make the difference between comfort and hardship."},{"en":"the difference between comfort and hardship","context":"Continued employment brings clear benefits to the individual. Work provides structure, purpose and daily contact with other people, and the loss of these things often explains why some retired people become bored, isolated or depressed. There is also evidence that staying mentally and socially active helps to delay cognitive decline. The financial argument is equally important, because pensions in most countries are modest, and a few extra years of earnings can make the difference between comfort and hardship."},{"en":"experience and judgement that cannot be taught quickly","context":"Society gains as well. Older employees possess experience and judgement that cannot be taught quickly, and they are usually the people who train younger colleagues. As birth rates fall, many economies simply cannot afford to lose millions of skilled workers at sixty-five, since a shrinking workforce has to support a growing number of pensioners."},{"en":"an adequate pension system","context":"In conclusion, I agree that working in old age is a good idea for those who are able and willing, but it must be supported by flexible conditions and an adequate pension system."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#361","summary":"Working in old age"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2210","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Young people changing careers","prompt":"A growing number of young people are changing their jobs and career paths. What are the reasons behind this phenomenon? Do the benefits of this trend outweigh the drawbacks?","answer":"It is increasingly common for young workers to move between employers, and even between professions, rather than remaining in a single career. This trend has two main causes, and in my view its advantages outweigh its disadvantages.\n\nThe first reason is economic. Permanent contracts have become rarer, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">whole industries can disappear within a decade</strong>, and pay rises are now often achieved by moving to a competitor rather than by waiting for promotion, so young people have little incentive to stay. The second reason is a change in expectations. Unlike their parents, most graduates today are looking for work that is interesting and personally meaningful, and they are willing to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">retrain</strong> when a job fails to provide this. Online courses and professional qualifications have made such transitions far easier than they were a generation ago.\n\nThe obvious drawback is instability. Workers who change roles frequently may lose pension contributions and other benefits, and some employers still treat a varied employment history as <strong class=\"key-phrase\">evidence of unreliability</strong>. Companies suffer too, since recruiting and training staff is expensive and constant <strong class=\"key-phrase\">turnover</strong> damages the knowledge held within a team.\n\nNevertheless, I believe the benefits are greater. Employees who have worked in several organisations acquire a wider range of skills and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">adapt more easily when technology reshapes their industry</strong>, which is exactly the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">flexibility a modern economy requires</strong>. They are also far more likely to find work that genuinely suits them, and people who enjoy their jobs are more productive and healthier than those who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stay in unsuitable positions out of fear</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, job mobility is driven by economic insecurity and by rising expectations, and its advantages clearly outweigh its drawbacks.","phrases":[{"en":"whole industries can disappear within a decade","context":"The first reason is economic. Permanent contracts have become rarer, whole industries can disappear within a decade, and pay rises are now often achieved by moving to a competitor rather than by waiting for promotion, so young people have little incentive to stay. The second reason is a change in expectations. Unlike their parents, most graduates today are looking for work that is interesting and personally meaningful, and they are willing to retrain when a job fails to provide this. Online courses and professional qualifications have made such transitions far easier than they were a generation ago."},{"en":"retrain","context":"The first reason is economic. Permanent contracts have become rarer, whole industries can disappear within a decade, and pay rises are now often achieved by moving to a competitor rather than by waiting for promotion, so young people have little incentive to stay. The second reason is a change in expectations. Unlike their parents, most graduates today are looking for work that is interesting and personally meaningful, and they are willing to retrain when a job fails to provide this. Online courses and professional qualifications have made such transitions far easier than they were a generation ago."},{"en":"evidence of unreliability","context":"The obvious drawback is instability. Workers who change roles frequently may lose pension contributions and other benefits, and some employers still treat a varied employment history as evidence of unreliability. Companies suffer too, since recruiting and training staff is expensive and constant turnover damages the knowledge held within a team."},{"en":"turnover","context":"The obvious drawback is instability. Workers who change roles frequently may lose pension contributions and other benefits, and some employers still treat a varied employment history as evidence of unreliability. Companies suffer too, since recruiting and training staff is expensive and constant turnover damages the knowledge held within a team."},{"en":"adapt more easily when technology reshapes their industry","context":"Nevertheless, I believe the benefits are greater. Employees who have worked in several organisations acquire a wider range of skills and adapt more easily when technology reshapes their industry, which is exactly the flexibility a modern economy requires. They are also far more likely to find work that genuinely suits them, and people who enjoy their jobs are more productive and healthier than those who stay in unsuitable positions out of fear."},{"en":"flexibility a modern economy requires","context":"Nevertheless, I believe the benefits are greater. Employees who have worked in several organisations acquire a wider range of skills and adapt more easily when technology reshapes their industry, which is exactly the flexibility a modern economy requires. They are also far more likely to find work that genuinely suits them, and people who enjoy their jobs are more productive and healthier than those who stay in unsuitable positions out of fear."},{"en":"stay in unsuitable positions out of fear","context":"Nevertheless, I believe the benefits are greater. Employees who have worked in several organisations acquire a wider range of skills and adapt more easily when technology reshapes their industry, which is exactly the flexibility a modern economy requires. They are also far more likely to find work that genuinely suits them, and people who enjoy their jobs are more productive and healthier than those who stay in unsuitable positions out of fear."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Benefits & Drawbacks","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#362","summary":"Young people changing careers"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2214","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"One career or several?","prompt":"Some people think that people who choose a job early and keep doing it are more likely to get a satisfying career life than those who frequently change jobs. Do you agree or disagree with this belief?","answer":"Some people believe that those who commit to a single occupation early in life end up more satisfied than those who move between jobs. I disagree with this view, because satisfaction depends on how well the work suits the individual rather than on how long it has been done.\n\nThere is some force in the argument for stability. A person who remains in one field <strong class=\"key-phrase\">accumulates deep expertise</strong>, builds a professional reputation and usually reaches a senior position with a good salary and a secure pension. Surgeons, lawyers and academics all require many years of continuous experience, and the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">sense of mastery</strong> this produces is a genuine source of satisfaction. Constant movement, by contrast, can leave someone <strong class=\"key-phrase\">permanently a beginner</strong>.\n\nNevertheless, this argument assumes that the original choice was a good one. Most people select a career at seventeen or eighteen, with almost no experience of the working world, and it would be surprising if they always chose correctly. Someone who discovers that a profession does not suit them, but who stays out of habit or fear, is likely to spend decades in quiet frustration. Changing direction is precisely what allows such a person to find work that matches their abilities and interests.\n\nThere is also a practical point. Industries now change so quickly that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">lifelong loyalty</strong> may not be rewarded. Workers with varied experience adapt more easily when their sector declines, whereas those with <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a single narrow specialism</strong> can find themselves <strong class=\"key-phrase\">unemployable in middle age</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I disagree that staying in one job produces a more satisfying career. What matters is finding work that genuinely suits the person, whether that takes one decision or several.","phrases":[{"en":"accumulates deep expertise","context":"There is some force in the argument for stability. A person who remains in one field accumulates deep expertise, builds a professional reputation and usually reaches a senior position with a good salary and a secure pension. Surgeons, lawyers and academics all require many years of continuous experience, and the sense of mastery this produces is a genuine source of satisfaction. Constant movement, by contrast, can leave someone permanently a beginner."},{"en":"sense of mastery","context":"There is some force in the argument for stability. A person who remains in one field accumulates deep expertise, builds a professional reputation and usually reaches a senior position with a good salary and a secure pension. Surgeons, lawyers and academics all require many years of continuous experience, and the sense of mastery this produces is a genuine source of satisfaction. Constant movement, by contrast, can leave someone permanently a beginner."},{"en":"permanently a beginner","context":"There is some force in the argument for stability. A person who remains in one field accumulates deep expertise, builds a professional reputation and usually reaches a senior position with a good salary and a secure pension. Surgeons, lawyers and academics all require many years of continuous experience, and the sense of mastery this produces is a genuine source of satisfaction. Constant movement, by contrast, can leave someone permanently a beginner."},{"en":"lifelong loyalty","context":"There is also a practical point. Industries now change so quickly that lifelong loyalty may not be rewarded. Workers with varied experience adapt more easily when their sector declines, whereas those with a single narrow specialism can find themselves unemployable in middle age."},{"en":"a single narrow specialism","context":"There is also a practical point. Industries now change so quickly that lifelong loyalty may not be rewarded. Workers with varied experience adapt more easily when their sector declines, whereas those with a single narrow specialism can find themselves unemployable in middle age."},{"en":"unemployable in middle age","context":"There is also a practical point. Industries now change so quickly that lifelong loyalty may not be rewarded. Workers with varied experience adapt more easily when their sector declines, whereas those with a single narrow specialism can find themselves unemployable in middle age."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#363","summary":"One career or several?"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2216","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Should social life come from work?","prompt":"Some people think a job not only provides income but also social life. Others think it is better to develop social life with people you do not work with. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","answer":"Most adults spend more waking hours with colleagues than with anyone else, which raises the question of whether friendships should grow out of work or be kept separate from it. Both arrangements have merit, although I believe a social life built entirely around the office carries real risks.\n\nThe case for workplace friendship is practical. Colleagues already share a routine, a location and a set of problems, so conversation starts easily in a way it rarely does among strangers. People who enjoy the company around them also cooperate more readily, ask for help sooner and stay in a job longer, which is why many employers deliberately fund social events. For someone who has moved to a new city for work, the office may be the only place where <strong class=\"key-phrase\">meeting people happens naturally</strong>.\n\nThe opposing view is that mixing the two makes both harder. A disagreement over a project becomes personal, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a promotion turns a friend into a manager</strong>, and confiding in a colleague about money or family can <strong class=\"key-phrase\">quietly damage a professional reputation</strong>. There is a further problem: if every friend is a coworker, redundancy removes an income and a social circle at the same time, which is precisely when support matters most.\n\nMy own position is that friendships at work are worth having but should not be the whole of one&#x27;s social life. Interests pursued outside the office — a sport, a language class, volunteering — put a person among people who <strong class=\"key-phrase\">have no stake in their career</strong>, and those relationships tend to be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">more honest for that reason</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, colleagues can become genuine friends and there is no reason to avoid it. The mistake is to rely on work for everything, because a life with only one source of company is a <strong class=\"key-phrase\">fragile arrangement</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"meeting people happens naturally","context":"The case for workplace friendship is practical. Colleagues already share a routine, a location and a set of problems, so conversation starts easily in a way it rarely does among strangers. People who enjoy the company around them also cooperate more readily, ask for help sooner and stay in a job longer, which is why many employers deliberately fund social events. For someone who has moved to a new city for work, the office may be the only place where meeting people happens naturally."},{"en":"a promotion turns a friend into a manager","context":"The opposing view is that mixing the two makes both harder. A disagreement over a project becomes personal, a promotion turns a friend into a manager, and confiding in a colleague about money or family can quietly damage a professional reputation. There is a further problem: if every friend is a coworker, redundancy removes an income and a social circle at the same time, which is precisely when support matters most."},{"en":"quietly damage a professional reputation","context":"The opposing view is that mixing the two makes both harder. A disagreement over a project becomes personal, a promotion turns a friend into a manager, and confiding in a colleague about money or family can quietly damage a professional reputation. There is a further problem: if every friend is a coworker, redundancy removes an income and a social circle at the same time, which is precisely when support matters most."},{"en":"have no stake in their career","context":"My own position is that friendships at work are worth having but should not be the whole of one's social life. Interests pursued outside the office — a sport, a language class, volunteering — put a person among people who have no stake in their career, and those relationships tend to be more honest for that reason."},{"en":"more honest for that reason","context":"My own position is that friendships at work are worth having but should not be the whole of one's social life. Interests pursued outside the office — a sport, a language class, volunteering — put a person among people who have no stake in their career, and those relationships tend to be more honest for that reason."},{"en":"fragile arrangement","context":"In conclusion, colleagues can become genuine friends and there is no reason to avoid it. The mistake is to rely on work for everything, because a life with only one source of company is a fragile arrangement."}],"subtype":"Discuss both views + opinion","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#364","summary":"Whether a job should also provide a social life"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2230","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Do social skills matter as much as qualifications?","prompt":"Most employers nowadays put increasing emphasis on social skills. Some people believe that social skills are important in addition to good qualifications for job success. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Job advertisements now routinely ask for communication and teamwork alongside technical qualifications. I agree that social skills have become genuinely important, though I would argue they matter for reasons that are often stated badly.\n\nThe practical reason is that almost no work is done alone any more. A software project, a hospital ward and a building site all depend on people handing information to each other accurately, and an engineer who cannot explain a risk to a manager is dangerous regardless of how good the calculation was. Most failures in organisations are failures of communication rather than of expertise, which is why employers have <strong class=\"key-phrase\">started screening for it deliberately</strong>.\n\nThe second reason is that technical knowledge dates quickly. A programming language learned at university may be obsolete within a decade, so employers increasingly hire for the ability to learn from colleagues, take criticism and work through disagreement, on the assumption that specific skills can be <strong class=\"key-phrase\">taught on the job</strong>.\n\nThere is a limit to this argument, however. Social confidence is not a substitute for competence, and organisations that promote the most persuasive rather than the most capable make expensive mistakes. There is also a fairness problem: interviews reward a particular manner of speaking, which tends to favour candidates from comfortable backgrounds and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">penalise those who were never coached</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, I agree that social skills matter alongside qualifications, because modern work is collaborative and knowledge dates faster than the ability to cooperate. They should nonetheless be judged as a genuine skill that can be taught and assessed, rather than as an impression formed in twenty minutes, so that employers reward <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what a candidate can actually do</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"started screening for it deliberately","context":"The practical reason is that almost no work is done alone any more. A software project, a hospital ward and a building site all depend on people handing information to each other accurately, and an engineer who cannot explain a risk to a manager is dangerous regardless of how good the calculation was. Most failures in organisations are failures of communication rather than of expertise, which is why employers have started screening for it deliberately."},{"en":"taught on the job","context":"The second reason is that technical knowledge dates quickly. A programming language learned at university may be obsolete within a decade, so employers increasingly hire for the ability to learn from colleagues, take criticism and work through disagreement, on the assumption that specific skills can be taught on the job."},{"en":"penalise those who were never coached","context":"There is a limit to this argument, however. Social confidence is not a substitute for competence, and organisations that promote the most persuasive rather than the most capable make expensive mistakes. There is also a fairness problem: interviews reward a particular manner of speaking, which tends to favour candidates from comfortable backgrounds and penalise those who were never coached."},{"en":"what a candidate can actually do","context":"In conclusion, I agree that social skills matter alongside qualifications, because modern work is collaborative and knowledge dates faster than the ability to cooperate. They should nonetheless be judged as a genuine skill that can be taught and assessed, rather than as an impression formed in twenty minutes, so that employers reward what a candidate can actually do."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#365","summary":"The growing emphasis employers place on social skills"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2235","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Laws that limit working hours","prompt":"Some countries have introduced laws to limit working hours for employees. Why are these laws introduced? Do you think they are a positive or negative development?","answer":"Several countries now cap the working week by law, usually at around forty-eight hours. These laws exist because individual employees cannot protect themselves, and I regard them as a clearly positive development.\n\nThe first reason for legislating is health. Research consistently links long hours to heart disease, depression and accidents, and the risk rises sharply beyond about fifty-five hours a week. Because the resulting illness is treated at public expense, governments have a direct financial interest in preventing it rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">paying for the consequences later</strong>. Tired workers are also dangerous to others, which is why drivers and surgeons were regulated first.\n\nThe second reason is that employees have little real choice. Where refusing overtime means losing a promotion or a contract, agreement is not genuine consent, and one worker accepting longer hours forces colleagues to match them. A legal ceiling removes that pressure by <strong class=\"key-phrase\">applying to everyone equally</strong>, which no individual negotiation can achieve.\n\nThe main objection is that such laws reduce output and restrict freedom. In practice the evidence points the other way: productivity per hour falls steeply once people are tired, so countries with shorter statutory weeks, such as Germany and the Netherlands, produce more per hour than countries with longer ones. The freedom argument also carries less weight than it appears to, since the law caps compulsory hours rather than <strong class=\"key-phrase\">forbidding anyone from working</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, these laws are introduced to protect health and to stop competition between employees driving hours upwards. They are positive, because they prevent a race that harms workers without <strong class=\"key-phrase\">producing more in the end</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"paying for the consequences later","context":"The first reason for legislating is health. Research consistently links long hours to heart disease, depression and accidents, and the risk rises sharply beyond about fifty-five hours a week. Because the resulting illness is treated at public expense, governments have a direct financial interest in preventing it rather than paying for the consequences later. Tired workers are also dangerous to others, which is why drivers and surgeons were regulated first."},{"en":"applying to everyone equally","context":"The second reason is that employees have little real choice. Where refusing overtime means losing a promotion or a contract, agreement is not genuine consent, and one worker accepting longer hours forces colleagues to match them. A legal ceiling removes that pressure by applying to everyone equally, which no individual negotiation can achieve."},{"en":"forbidding anyone from working","context":"The main objection is that such laws reduce output and restrict freedom. In practice the evidence points the other way: productivity per hour falls steeply once people are tired, so countries with shorter statutory weeks, such as Germany and the Netherlands, produce more per hour than countries with longer ones. The freedom argument also carries less weight than it appears to, since the law caps compulsory hours rather than forbidding anyone from working."},{"en":"producing more in the end","context":"In conclusion, these laws are introduced to protect health and to stop competition between employees driving hours upwards. They are positive, because they prevent a race that harms workers without producing more in the end."}],"subtype":"Reasons / Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#366","summary":"Why laws limiting working hours are introduced"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2271","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Banning age discrimination in hiring","prompt":"In some countries, it is illegal for companies to reject job applicants because of their age. Is this a positive or negative development?","answer":"Several countries now prohibit employers from turning down candidates on grounds of age. I regard this as a positive development, both for the individuals protected and for the economies that adopt it.\n\nThe strongest argument is fairness. Age says almost nothing about whether a particular person can do a particular job, and rejecting a candidate at fifty-five because of an assumption about energy or adaptability is a judgement about a category rather than about the applicant. The consequences are severe, because older workers who lose a job take far longer to find another and many leave the workforce permanently, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not by choice but by exhaustion</strong>.\n\nThere is also a practical case that will matter more as populations age. Countries with falling birth rates cannot afford to lose experienced workers a decade before they wish to retire, since the pensions and health care of an ageing society are paid for by those still in work. Older employees additionally bring judgement built over decades and lower turnover, and mixed-age teams consistently outperform uniform ones because they <strong class=\"key-phrase\">approach problems from different starting points</strong>.\n\nThe objection is that such laws are difficult to enforce, since an employer can always give another reason for a rejection, and that they may make firms reluctant to hire anyone they might struggle to dismiss. This has some force, but the same was said of laws on sex and disability discrimination, which changed behaviour substantially even though <strong class=\"key-phrase\">individual cases remained hard to prove</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, this is a positive development. It protects people at the point in life when unemployment does the most lasting damage, and it keeps skills in an economy that <strong class=\"key-phrase\">will need them for longer</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"not by choice but by exhaustion","context":"The strongest argument is fairness. Age says almost nothing about whether a particular person can do a particular job, and rejecting a candidate at fifty-five because of an assumption about energy or adaptability is a judgement about a category rather than about the applicant. The consequences are severe, because older workers who lose a job take far longer to find another and many leave the workforce permanently, not by choice but by exhaustion."},{"en":"approach problems from different starting points","context":"There is also a practical case that will matter more as populations age. Countries with falling birth rates cannot afford to lose experienced workers a decade before they wish to retire, since the pensions and health care of an ageing society are paid for by those still in work. Older employees additionally bring judgement built over decades and lower turnover, and mixed-age teams consistently outperform uniform ones because they approach problems from different starting points."},{"en":"individual cases remained hard to prove","context":"The objection is that such laws are difficult to enforce, since an employer can always give another reason for a rejection, and that they may make firms reluctant to hire anyone they might struggle to dismiss. This has some force, but the same was said of laws on sex and disability discrimination, which changed behaviour substantially even though individual cases remained hard to prove."},{"en":"will need them for longer","context":"In conclusion, this is a positive development. It protects people at the point in life when unemployment does the most lasting damage, and it keeps skills in an economy that will need them for longer."}],"subtype":"Positive or Negative","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#367","summary":"Laws making it illegal to reject applicants because of age"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2274","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Is work the most important thing in life?","prompt":"Some people think that work is the most important thing in people's life. Without the success of a career, life becomes meaningless. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Work takes up most of our waking hours and shapes how we see ourselves, so its importance is easy to overstate. A career matters a great deal, but I disagree that a life without professional success is empty.\n\nWork deserves much of the weight placed on it. A job supplies income, and poverty limits almost everything else a person might want to do. It also provides structure, colleagues and a sense of being useful, which is why unemployment damages health even when benefits cover the bills. People who are good at something and paid for it usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">take genuine pride in the work itself</strong>, and that pride is no small part of a life.\n\nThe claim collapses once success is treated as the source of meaning. Careers end. Illness, redundancy and retirement remove the job and leave the person, and anyone whose identity sat entirely inside a job title is left with nothing at that point. Judging a life by promotion also produces a strange verdict: a nurse who never rises above the ward, a parent who raises three children well and a volunteer who runs a local club for thirty years would all count as failures, which nobody actually believes.\n\nMeaning comes from a wider set of things: being loved, being trusted, learning, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">leaving something behind that outlasts you</strong>. Work can carry some of that, and so can family, friendship and the ordinary care people give one another. Societies that rank employment above everything else tend to produce <strong class=\"key-phrase\">long hours and lonely people</strong>, which is evidence against the belief rather than for it.\n\nIn conclusion, work is central to a good life because it provides money, purpose and self-respect. It is not the whole of it, and treating career success as the measure of a person leaves most lives, including successful ones, badly misjudged.","phrases":[{"en":"take genuine pride in the work itself","context":"Work deserves much of the weight placed on it. A job supplies income, and poverty limits almost everything else a person might want to do. It also provides structure, colleagues and a sense of being useful, which is why unemployment damages health even when benefits cover the bills. People who are good at something and paid for it usually take genuine pride in the work itself, and that pride is no small part of a life."},{"en":"leaving something behind that outlasts you","context":"Meaning comes from a wider set of things: being loved, being trusted, learning, and leaving something behind that outlasts you. Work can carry some of that, and so can family, friendship and the ordinary care people give one another. Societies that rank employment above everything else tend to produce long hours and lonely people, which is evidence against the belief rather than for it."},{"en":"long hours and lonely people","context":"Meaning comes from a wider set of things: being loved, being trusted, learning, and leaving something behind that outlasts you. Work can carry some of that, and so can family, friendship and the ordinary care people give one another. Societies that rank employment above everything else tend to produce long hours and lonely people, which is evidence against the belief rather than for it."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#368","summary":"Whether life is meaningless without a successful career"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2280","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Unemployment or a job you dislike?","prompt":"It is better for people to be unemployed than to be in a job they do not enjoy. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Advice to leave any job that makes you unhappy is now common, particularly among younger workers. I disagree with it as a general rule, although there are situations where walking away is clearly the right decision.\n\nUnemployment is far more damaging than the phrase suggests. It removes income first, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">financial fear quickly reaches the family</strong>, the housing and the ability to plan anything. It also removes structure, colleagues and the sense of contributing, which is why long spells out of work are linked to depression far more strongly than a disliked job is. Skills fade, and employers <strong class=\"key-phrase\">treat a long gap with suspicion</strong>, so each month out makes the next job harder to obtain.\n\nA job that is merely unenjoyable still supplies a good deal. It pays for the parts of life that do give pleasure, and most people find their satisfaction outside work in any case. It teaches things the worker did not expect to learn, and it is a far better position from which to look for something better, because <strong class=\"key-phrase\">searching from inside employment</strong> carries neither desperation nor a gap in the record.\n\nThere are limits to this. Work that damages health, involves abuse or requires dishonesty should be left, and where savings or family support exist, <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a short deliberate break to retrain</strong> can be a sound investment rather than an escape. The distinction is between leaving towards something and leaving away from something.\n\nIn conclusion, quitting is justified when a job harms a person or when a real plan is waiting. In the ordinary case of boredom or mild dislike, staying while looking is the wiser course, since unemployment usually costs more than the dissatisfaction it was meant to cure.","phrases":[{"en":"financial fear quickly reaches the family","context":"Unemployment is far more damaging than the phrase suggests. It removes income first, and financial fear quickly reaches the family, the housing and the ability to plan anything. It also removes structure, colleagues and the sense of contributing, which is why long spells out of work are linked to depression far more strongly than a disliked job is. Skills fade, and employers treat a long gap with suspicion, so each month out makes the next job harder to obtain."},{"en":"treat a long gap with suspicion","context":"Unemployment is far more damaging than the phrase suggests. It removes income first, and financial fear quickly reaches the family, the housing and the ability to plan anything. It also removes structure, colleagues and the sense of contributing, which is why long spells out of work are linked to depression far more strongly than a disliked job is. Skills fade, and employers treat a long gap with suspicion, so each month out makes the next job harder to obtain."},{"en":"searching from inside employment","context":"A job that is merely unenjoyable still supplies a good deal. It pays for the parts of life that do give pleasure, and most people find their satisfaction outside work in any case. It teaches things the worker did not expect to learn, and it is a far better position from which to look for something better, because searching from inside employment carries neither desperation nor a gap in the record."},{"en":"a short deliberate break to retrain","context":"There are limits to this. Work that damages health, involves abuse or requires dishonesty should be left, and where savings or family support exist, a short deliberate break to retrain can be a sound investment rather than an escape. The distinction is between leaving towards something and leaving away from something."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#369","summary":"Whether it is better to be unemployed than to work in a job you do not enjoy"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2282","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Time off work for new fathers","prompt":"All fathers should be entitled to time off work when their children are born. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Paid leave for mothers is now standard almost everywhere, while fathers in many countries receive a few days or nothing. I agree strongly that fathers should be entitled to leave, because the benefits reach the mother, the child and the workplace at once.\n\nThe most immediate reason concerns the mother. Recovery from birth takes weeks, and a woman managing that alone with a newborn is at real risk of exhaustion and depression. A partner at home shares the night feeds, the cooking and the visits, and rates of postnatal depression are measurably lower where fathers take leave. The relationship between the parents also benefits, since <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the load is shared instead of quietly resented</strong>.\n\nThe child gains as well. Fathers who care for an infant in the first weeks are more involved for years afterwards, and children with engaged fathers do better at school and show fewer behavioural difficulties. Caring for a baby is a skill learned by doing it, and a father excluded at the start <strong class=\"key-phrase\">never acquires the confidence to take over later</strong>. There is a strong equality argument too. Where only mothers take leave, employers come to regard young women as the risky hire, which is a substantial part of the pay gap. Leave taken by both parents removes that calculation and lets women return to work sooner.\n\nThe objection is cost, particularly for small firms losing a worker for weeks. That is a real difficulty, but it is met by funding the leave through the state rather than the employer, as several countries already do, and the cost is small against the <strong class=\"key-phrase\">savings in maternal illness and lost female earnings</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, paternity leave improves the mother&#x27;s health, the child&#x27;s development and equality at work. It should be a paid entitlement rather than a favour, and ideally reserved for the father so that it is actually taken.","phrases":[{"en":"the load is shared instead of quietly resented","context":"The most immediate reason concerns the mother. Recovery from birth takes weeks, and a woman managing that alone with a newborn is at real risk of exhaustion and depression. A partner at home shares the night feeds, the cooking and the visits, and rates of postnatal depression are measurably lower where fathers take leave. The relationship between the parents also benefits, since the load is shared instead of quietly resented."},{"en":"never acquires the confidence to take over later","context":"The child gains as well. Fathers who care for an infant in the first weeks are more involved for years afterwards, and children with engaged fathers do better at school and show fewer behavioural difficulties. Caring for a baby is a skill learned by doing it, and a father excluded at the start never acquires the confidence to take over later. There is a strong equality argument too. Where only mothers take leave, employers come to regard young women as the risky hire, which is a substantial part of the pay gap. Leave taken by both parents removes that calculation and lets women return to work sooner."},{"en":"savings in maternal illness and lost female earnings","context":"The objection is cost, particularly for small firms losing a worker for weeks. That is a real difficulty, but it is met by funding the leave through the state rather than the employer, as several countries already do, and the cost is small against the savings in maternal illness and lost female earnings."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#370","summary":"Whether all fathers should be entitled to leave when a child is born"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2284","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Should managers earn far more?","prompt":"Some people think it is a good thing for senior managers to have much higher salaries than the other workers in a company. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Chief executives in large firms now earn many times the salary of their average employee, and the multiple has grown steadily for decades. I agree that senior managers should be paid more, but not by the margins that have become normal.\n\nThere are respectable reasons for a substantial gap. Senior decisions carry consequences that ordinary roles do not: a single judgement about a market or an acquisition can determine whether thousands keep their jobs. The work also demands experience that takes decades to acquire, and the responsibility is personal, since a chief executive is dismissed when results fail while most employees are not. Competition is real too, because able managers are scarce and a firm that underpays them loses them to a competitor. A visible reward for reaching the top also encourages people to take on difficult roles.\n\nThe size of the current gap is much harder to defend. Executive pay has multiplied while wages below have barely moved, and no comparable change in the difficulty of the work explains it. The pay is often set by committees of other executives, so it reflects <strong class=\"key-phrase\">comparison with peers rather than performance</strong>, and it rises after failure as reliably as after success. Very large gaps also damage the organisation, since employees who see enormous rewards at the top and refused pay claims below stop believing that effort is fairly recognised, and companies with extreme differentials show higher staff turnover.\n\nThere is a wider effect, in that pay at the top has become a principal driver of inequality, and money paid to a handful of individuals is money <strong class=\"key-phrase\">not spent on wages, training or investment</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, senior managers carry heavier responsibility and should be paid accordingly. The present multiples reflect weak governance rather than value, so <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transparent limits linked to average pay</strong> would be a reasonable correction.","phrases":[{"en":"comparison with peers rather than performance","context":"The size of the current gap is much harder to defend. Executive pay has multiplied while wages below have barely moved, and no comparable change in the difficulty of the work explains it. The pay is often set by committees of other executives, so it reflects comparison with peers rather than performance, and it rises after failure as reliably as after success. Very large gaps also damage the organisation, since employees who see enormous rewards at the top and refused pay claims below stop believing that effort is fairly recognised, and companies with extreme differentials show higher staff turnover."},{"en":"not spent on wages, training or investment","context":"There is a wider effect, in that pay at the top has become a principal driver of inequality, and money paid to a handful of individuals is money not spent on wages, training or investment."},{"en":"transparent limits linked to average pay","context":"In conclusion, senior managers carry heavier responsibility and should be paid accordingly. The present multiples reflect weak governance rather than value, so transparent limits linked to average pay would be a reasonable correction."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#371","summary":"Whether large pay gaps between senior managers and other staff are justified"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2309","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Should employers care how staff dress?","prompt":"Some people believe that employers should not be concerned about the way their employees dress, but should be concerned about the quality of their work. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Offices that once required suits now accept jeans, and many employers say they judge output alone. I largely agree with that position, though appearance still carries weight in a limited set of jobs.\n\nOutput is the honest measure. A programmer writes the same code in a hooded top as in a jacket, and an accountant&#x27;s figures do not improve with a tie. Judging staff by clothing is also unreliable in a way that quietly damages a company, because it favours those who can afford expensive clothes and those whose background matches the manager&#x27;s, so capable people are overlooked for reasons unrelated to ability. Relaxed rules bring practical gains too: staff are more comfortable, save money, and firms that dropped their dress codes have found recruitment easier rather than standards lower.\n\nThere are exceptions, and they are not trivial. Where employees meet clients, patients or the public, dress is part of the service: a surgeon in a stained shirt or a lawyer in beachwear damages the trust a client needs to feel. Uniforms also serve identification and safety, which is why steel-capped boots and high-visibility jackets are <strong class=\"key-phrase\">requirements rather than preferences</strong>. In these cases the employer is protecting the customer, not imposing taste.\n\nThe sensible position is therefore a narrow standard rather than none. A company can reasonably ask for clean, safe clothing that suits the setting, and should <strong class=\"key-phrase\">explain the reason behind any rule it sets</strong>. What it should not do is police style, since a preference for formality is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a matter of habit, not of performance</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, quality of work is the proper measure and employers should concentrate on it. Dress codes are justified only where safety or a client relationship depends on them, and beyond that they achieve very little.","phrases":[{"en":"requirements rather than preferences","context":"There are exceptions, and they are not trivial. Where employees meet clients, patients or the public, dress is part of the service: a surgeon in a stained shirt or a lawyer in beachwear damages the trust a client needs to feel. Uniforms also serve identification and safety, which is why steel-capped boots and high-visibility jackets are requirements rather than preferences. In these cases the employer is protecting the customer, not imposing taste."},{"en":"explain the reason behind any rule it sets","context":"The sensible position is therefore a narrow standard rather than none. A company can reasonably ask for clean, safe clothing that suits the setting, and should explain the reason behind any rule it sets. What it should not do is police style, since a preference for formality is a matter of habit, not of performance."},{"en":"a matter of habit, not of performance","context":"The sensible position is therefore a narrow standard rather than none. A company can reasonably ask for clean, safe clothing that suits the setting, and should explain the reason behind any rule it sets. What it should not do is police style, since a preference for formality is a matter of habit, not of performance."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#372","summary":"Whether appearance at work matters or only the quality of the work"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2321","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Would younger leaders be better?","prompt":"Leaders and directors in organisations are normally older people. Some people think that having a younger leader would be better. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"Boardrooms and cabinets are dominated by people in their fifties and sixties, and the suggestion that younger leaders would do better is heard more often each year. I partly agree: age is a poor qualification in itself, but replacing it with youth would repeat the same mistake.\n\nThe case for younger leaders is strongest where the world is changing quickly. Someone who grew up with the technology their company sells understands its customers without translation, and younger leaders tend to be less attached to methods that once worked. They also have no reputation to protect from a change of direction, which makes difficult decisions easier, and they will personally live with the long-term consequences of choices about climate, debt and pensions, so they weigh them differently.\n\nExperience nonetheless earns its position. Judgement is largely a store of remembered failures, and a leader who has managed a recession, a scandal or a lost contract recognises the early signs. Much of leadership is also negotiation, and knowing which people to call and how far they can be pushed is <strong class=\"key-phrase\">knowledge that cannot be acquired quickly</strong>. Confidence without that store produces expensive errors, as several young founders have demonstrated.\n\nThe real objection to the present arrangement is not that leaders are old but that promotion often follows length of service rather than ability, so capable people wait for their turn while <strong class=\"key-phrase\">weak leaders remain in place until retirement</strong>. The answer is to select on evidence of judgement and to build teams of mixed ages, where the young supply energy and current knowledge and the experienced supply pattern recognition.\n\nIn conclusion, younger leaders would improve many organisations, particularly fast-moving ones. Youth is not a qualification either, and the sensible reform is to <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stop treating age as a proxy for competence</strong> in either direction.","phrases":[{"en":"knowledge that cannot be acquired quickly","context":"Experience nonetheless earns its position. Judgement is largely a store of remembered failures, and a leader who has managed a recession, a scandal or a lost contract recognises the early signs. Much of leadership is also negotiation, and knowing which people to call and how far they can be pushed is knowledge that cannot be acquired quickly. Confidence without that store produces expensive errors, as several young founders have demonstrated."},{"en":"weak leaders remain in place until retirement","context":"The real objection to the present arrangement is not that leaders are old but that promotion often follows length of service rather than ability, so capable people wait for their turn while weak leaders remain in place until retirement. The answer is to select on evidence of judgement and to build teams of mixed ages, where the young supply energy and current knowledge and the experienced supply pattern recognition."},{"en":"stop treating age as a proxy for competence","context":"In conclusion, younger leaders would improve many organisations, particularly fast-moving ones. Youth is not a qualification either, and the sensible reform is to stop treating age as a proxy for competence in either direction."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#373","summary":"Whether organisations would benefit from younger people in charge"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2345","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Why some people work harder","prompt":"In both education and employment, some people work harder than others. Why do some people work harder than others? Is working hard always a good thing?","answer":"In any classroom or office, effort varies far more than ability does. The reasons lie in circumstance and motivation rather than character, and hard work is valuable only while it remains attached to a purpose.\n\nNecessity is the first explanation. A student whose family has sacrificed to pay the fees, or an employee supporting relatives, works from a pressure that a comfortable colleague simply does not feel. Interest is the second and stronger one: people who find the subject absorbing put in hours that do not feel like effort, while those in the wrong course or job <strong class=\"key-phrase\">spend most of their energy on getting started</strong>. Upbringing matters too, since children raised where persistence was expected treat difficulty as normal rather than as a signal to stop.\n\nThere is also a question of belief. People work hard when they expect the effort to change something, which is why students who think ability is fixed give up quickly, and why employees in workplaces where promotion follows connections rather than results <strong class=\"key-phrase\">stop trying for reasons that are entirely rational</strong>.\n\nHard work is not always good, however. Beyond a certain point the returns fall sharply, and long hours produce exhaustion, poor judgement and errors that undo the extra time. Effort can also be misdirected, since somebody working twelve hours on the wrong task achieves less than a colleague who thinks first. The heaviest cost is personal, because health, family and friendship are what usually pay for the additional hours, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">nobody recovers that time later</strong>.\n\nIn conclusion, people work hard because of financial pressure, genuine interest, upbringing and a belief that effort matters. Hard work is admirable when it is aimed at something worthwhile and bounded by rest, and harmful once it becomes <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a habit that no longer serves any goal</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"spend most of their energy on getting started","context":"Necessity is the first explanation. A student whose family has sacrificed to pay the fees, or an employee supporting relatives, works from a pressure that a comfortable colleague simply does not feel. Interest is the second and stronger one: people who find the subject absorbing put in hours that do not feel like effort, while those in the wrong course or job spend most of their energy on getting started. Upbringing matters too, since children raised where persistence was expected treat difficulty as normal rather than as a signal to stop."},{"en":"stop trying for reasons that are entirely rational","context":"There is also a question of belief. People work hard when they expect the effort to change something, which is why students who think ability is fixed give up quickly, and why employees in workplaces where promotion follows connections rather than results stop trying for reasons that are entirely rational."},{"en":"nobody recovers that time later","context":"Hard work is not always good, however. Beyond a certain point the returns fall sharply, and long hours produce exhaustion, poor judgement and errors that undo the extra time. Effort can also be misdirected, since somebody working twelve hours on the wrong task achieves less than a colleague who thinks first. The heaviest cost is personal, because health, family and friendship are what usually pay for the additional hours, and nobody recovers that time later."},{"en":"a habit that no longer serves any goal","context":"In conclusion, people work hard because of financial pressure, genuine interest, upbringing and a belief that effort matters. Hard work is admirable when it is aimed at something worthwhile and bounded by rest, and harmful once it becomes a habit that no longer serves any goal."}],"subtype":"Two-Part Question","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#374","summary":"The reasons behind differences in effort and whether hard work is always good"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2375","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Follow your interest or choose a safe job?","prompt":"Some people believe that young people should be free to pursue the jobs they want, while others think they should choose jobs that are more practical for their future. Discuss both views and give your own opinion.","answer":"Few family arguments are as common as the one between a young person&#x27;s ambition and a parent&#x27;s sense of what is sensible. Both sides are arguing for the same person&#x27;s welfare, and my own view is that the disagreement is usually about timing rather than principle.\n\nThe argument for free choice rests on how work actually goes. Careers last forty years, and nobody sustains that in a field they find dull, so people pushed into safe professions frequently leave them exhausted in their thirties. Interest also produces skill, because someone absorbed in a subject practises far beyond what is required, and the difference between adequate and excellent is usually <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the hours nobody asked them to put in</strong>. Fields that once looked frivolous — games, sport, design — now employ millions, so the definition of a practical job keeps changing.\n\nThe practical view answers with what happens to those who fail. Some fields accept a tiny fraction of those who train for them, and the rest arrive at thirty with debt, no relevant experience and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a qualification that does not transfer</strong>. Parents also know that financial security shapes everything else, and that a stable income makes it possible to keep an interest alive outside work. In countries without a safety net, this is not timidity but arithmetic.\n\nThe sensible position combines them. Young people should choose their direction, since a life spent in work chosen by somebody else rarely goes well, but they should choose with honest information about earnings and competition, and should keep <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a qualification that can be used elsewhere</strong>. Testing an ambition seriously for a few years is very different from pursuing it indefinitely.\n\nIn conclusion, forcing young people into practical work produces capable but resentful adults, while ignoring the odds produces avoidable hardship. The answer is freedom exercised with realism and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">a route back if the first attempt fails</strong>.","phrases":[{"en":"the hours nobody asked them to put in","context":"The argument for free choice rests on how work actually goes. Careers last forty years, and nobody sustains that in a field they find dull, so people pushed into safe professions frequently leave them exhausted in their thirties. Interest also produces skill, because someone absorbed in a subject practises far beyond what is required, and the difference between adequate and excellent is usually the hours nobody asked them to put in. Fields that once looked frivolous — games, sport, design — now employ millions, so the definition of a practical job keeps changing."},{"en":"a qualification that does not transfer","context":"The practical view answers with what happens to those who fail. Some fields accept a tiny fraction of those who train for them, and the rest arrive at thirty with debt, no relevant experience and a qualification that does not transfer. Parents also know that financial security shapes everything else, and that a stable income makes it possible to keep an interest alive outside work. In countries without a safety net, this is not timidity but arithmetic."},{"en":"a qualification that can be used elsewhere","context":"The sensible position combines them. Young people should choose their direction, since a life spent in work chosen by somebody else rarely goes well, but they should choose with honest information about earnings and competition, and should keep a qualification that can be used elsewhere. Testing an ambition seriously for a few years is very different from pursuing it indefinitely."},{"en":"a route back if the first attempt fails","context":"In conclusion, forcing young people into practical work produces capable but resentful adults, while ignoring the odds produces avoidable hardship. The answer is freedom exercised with realism and a route back if the first attempt fails."}],"subtype":"Discuss Both Views","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#375","summary":"Whether young people should pursue the work they want or the practical option"},{"id":"writing-task-2-2380","section":"writing-task-2","topic":"Work & Employment","title":"Should doctors earn more than entertainers?","prompt":"Professional workers like doctors, nurses and teachers make a greater contribution to society, so they should be paid more than sports and entertainment personalities. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","answer":"A nurse earns in a year what a leading footballer earns in a morning, and most people find that ordering of rewards uncomfortable. I agree that essential professions are underpaid, but I disagree that the two groups can be compared in the way the statement assumes.\n\nThe case for paying professionals more is strong on its own terms. Doctors, nurses and teachers do work that a society cannot function without, and <strong class=\"key-phrase\">they train for years to do it</strong>. Their pay has fallen behind living costs in many countries, which is why hospitals and schools now <strong class=\"key-phrase\">struggle to fill posts</strong>, and a shortage of nurses costs far more than a pay rise would. There is also a moral objection to a scale of rewards in which entertainment is valued above care, since children learn from it what a society thinks is worth doing.\n\nThe comparison nevertheless misunderstands where the money comes from. A teacher is paid from taxation, and salaries are limited by what a country can raise and by how many teachers it employs. A footballer is paid by a club that sells tickets, shirts and broadcasting rights to millions of willing customers, so the salary reflects <strong class=\"key-phrase\">what an audience chooses to spend</strong> rather than any judgement about value. Nobody decided that entertainers should earn more; <strong class=\"key-phrase\">the difference emerges from scarcity</strong>, since thousands can be trained as accountants while very few can fill a stadium.\n\nThe practical conclusion is that professional pay should be raised on its own merits rather than by capping entertainers. Reducing an athlete&#x27;s income would <strong class=\"key-phrase\">transfer nothing to a hospital</strong>, and would simply move the talent abroad.\n\nIn conclusion, doctors, nurses and teachers contribute more to society than performers and deserve considerably better pay. The two salaries are set by entirely different mechanisms, so the argument for raising one is not an argument for lowering the other.","phrases":[{"en":"they train for years to do it","context":"The case for paying professionals more is strong on its own terms. Doctors, nurses and teachers do work that a society cannot function without, and they train for years to do it. Their pay has fallen behind living costs in many countries, which is why hospitals and schools now struggle to fill posts, and a shortage of nurses costs far more than a pay rise would. There is also a moral objection to a scale of rewards in which entertainment is valued above care, since children learn from it what a society thinks is worth doing."},{"en":"struggle to fill posts","context":"The case for paying professionals more is strong on its own terms. Doctors, nurses and teachers do work that a society cannot function without, and they train for years to do it. Their pay has fallen behind living costs in many countries, which is why hospitals and schools now struggle to fill posts, and a shortage of nurses costs far more than a pay rise would. There is also a moral objection to a scale of rewards in which entertainment is valued above care, since children learn from it what a society thinks is worth doing."},{"en":"what an audience chooses to spend","context":"The comparison nevertheless misunderstands where the money comes from. A teacher is paid from taxation, and salaries are limited by what a country can raise and by how many teachers it employs. A footballer is paid by a club that sells tickets, shirts and broadcasting rights to millions of willing customers, so the salary reflects what an audience chooses to spend rather than any judgement about value. Nobody decided that entertainers should earn more; the difference emerges from scarcity, since thousands can be trained as accountants while very few can fill a stadium."},{"en":"the difference emerges from scarcity","context":"The comparison nevertheless misunderstands where the money comes from. A teacher is paid from taxation, and salaries are limited by what a country can raise and by how many teachers it employs. A footballer is paid by a club that sells tickets, shirts and broadcasting rights to millions of willing customers, so the salary reflects what an audience chooses to spend rather than any judgement about value. Nobody decided that entertainers should earn more; the difference emerges from scarcity, since thousands can be trained as accountants while very few can fill a stadium."},{"en":"transfer nothing to a hospital","context":"The practical conclusion is that professional pay should be raised on its own merits rather than by capping entertainers. Reducing an athlete's income would transfer nothing to a hospital, and would simply move the talent abroad."}],"subtype":"Agree / Disagree","visual":null,"_displayNo":"#376","summary":"Whether essential professions deserve higher pay than sports and entertainment stars"}]);
